export default {
  2751: {
    source: "Что такое React Router?",
    question: "What is React Router?",
    shortAnswer: "React Router is a library for adding routing to React applications, letting the user move between pages without a full reload.",
    longAnswer: "React Router is a library that adds routing to React applications, providing smooth transitions between components. It lets developers build multi-page applications with dynamic content. React Router's core components, such as `<BrowserRouter>` and `<Route>`, control routes and decide which components render for the current URL. The tool improves the user experience, making interaction with a web application feel natural and similar to working with an ordinary website. A practical example is a profile page whose route depends on the user ID in the URL.",
    codeExample: "import { BrowserRouter as Router, Route, Switch } from 'react-router-dom';\nfunction App() {\nreturn (\n  <Router>\n    <Switch>\n      <Route path=\"/profile/:id\" component={ProfilePage} />\n      {/* Other routes */}\n    </Switch>\n  </Router>\n);\n}\nfunction ProfilePage({ match }) {\n  const userId = match.params.id;\n  return <div>Profile of the user with ID: {userId}</div>;\n}",
  },
  2752: {
    source: "Можете ли вы объяснить, что такое BrowserRouter и HashRouter?",
    question: "Can you explain what BrowserRouter and HashRouter are?",
    shortAnswer: "BrowserRouter and HashRouter are two routing methods in React Router. BrowserRouter uses real URLs, while HashRouter uses hash fragments (#).",
    longAnswer: "BrowserRouter and HashRouter are two ways to add routing to React applications. `BrowserRouter` uses real URLs, which means addresses can be changed in the browser's address bar and support SEO. For example, the URL `https://example.com/profile/1` will be correctly interpreted as the profile of the user with ID 1. `HashRouter`, by contrast, uses hash fragments (#) to change the URL, which allows the route to be manipulated without really changing the URL itself. It is less preferable because it does not support SEO and can be limiting when integrating with a server. The choice depends on the project's specific requirements, such as SEO support and server interaction.",
    codeExample: null,
  },
  2753: {
    source: "Как вы можете получить доступ к объекту истории в React Router?",
    question: "How can you access the history object in React Router?",
    shortAnswer: "The history object is accessed in React Router through `useHistory()` or `useNavigate()` in function components, which lets you manipulate the current route.",
    longAnswer: "In React Router, access to the history object is provided through `useHistory()` or `useNavigate()`.\n`useHistory()` gives access to the history object, which lets you change the current route — for example with `push()` to add a new route, or `goBack()` to return to the previous page.\n`useNavigate()` works similarly but returns a function for manipulating the route. These tools let you build dynamic, user-oriented applications that provide smooth and intuitive interaction.\nAn example is a \"Back\" button on a profile page that uses `goBack()` to return to the previous page.\n\n`useNavigate()` works similarly but returns a function for changing the route programmatically:",
    codeExample: "import { useHistory } from 'react-router-dom';\nfunction SomeComponent() {\n  const history = useHistory();\n  const navigateToProfile = () => {\n    history.push('/profile/1');\n  };\n  return <button onClick={navigateToProfile}>Go to profile</button>;\n}\n\nimport { useNavigate } from 'react-router-dom';\nfunction SomeComponent() {\n  const navigate = useNavigate();\n  const navigateToProfile = () => {\n    navigate('/profile/1');\n  };\n  return <button onClick={navigateToProfile}>Go to profile</button>;\n}",
  },
  2754: {
    source: "Какие основные компоненты предоставляет React Router DOM для создания маршрутов?",
    question: "Which core components does React Router DOM provide for creating routes?",
    shortAnswer: "React Router DOM provides key components such as `<BrowserRouter>`, `<Routes>`, `<Route>` and `<Link>`. `<BrowserRouter>` wraps the application to enable routing. `<Routes>` holds a set of routes, and `<Route>` determines which component renders when a particular URL matches. `<Link>` creates links that allow navigation without reloading the page.",
    longAnswer: "React Router DOM is a library for managing routing in React. Its core components:\n\n- `<BrowserRouter>`: The main wrapper that connects routing to the browser history. All routes must live inside this component.\n\n- `<Routes>`: A container for routes. It replaces `<Switch>` from older versions and defines the set of routes to check.\n\n- `<Route>`: Defines a path (`path`) and the associated component (`element`). If the current URL matches `path`, the given component renders.\n\n- `<Link>`: Replaces standard HTML links (`<a>`) for navigating between pages without a reload.\n\nThese components are used in most applications to build an SPA (Single Page Application), where moving between pages happens without a full page reload.",
    codeExample: "import { BrowserRouter } from 'react-router-dom';\nconst App = () => (\n  <BrowserRouter>\n    {/* Routes go here */}\n  </BrowserRouter>\n);\n\nimport { Routes, Route } from 'react-router-dom';\nconst AppRoutes = () => (\n  <Routes>\n    <Route path=\"/\" element={<Home />} />\n    <Route path=\"/about\" element={<About />} />\n  </Routes>\n);\n\nimport { Link } from 'react-router-dom';\nconst Navbar = () => (\n  <nav>\n    <Link to=\"/\">Home</Link>\n    <Link to=\"/about\">About</Link>\n  </nav>\n);",
  },
  2755: {
    source: "Чем отличаются компоненты <Route> и <Routes>?",
    question: "How do the <Route> and <Routes> components differ?",
    shortAnswer: "`<Route>` defines a single route and is tied to a specific component that renders when the URL matches. `<Routes>` is a container for `<Route>` and is responsible for finding the first matching route. Without `<Routes>`, `<Route>` components will not work correctly in newer versions of React Router.",
    longAnswer: "In React Router DOM, `<Route>` and `<Routes>` do different jobs:\n`<Route>`: This component describes a route: the path (`path`) and which component (`element`) renders on a match. It is the basic building block of routing.\n\n`<Routes>`: This is the container for all `<Route>` elements. In React Router 6 and above it replaces `<Switch>`. `<Routes>` checks routes top to bottom and renders the first one that matches. Without `<Routes>`, routes will not work.\n\nThe key difference:\n\n- `<Route>` defines a single route.\n\n- `<Routes>` organises checking them, in order to pick the right route for the current URL.",
    codeExample: "<Route path=\"/about\" element={<About />} />\n\n<Routes>\n  <Route path=\"/\" element={<Home />} />\n  <Route path=\"/about\" element={<About />} />\n</Routes>",
  },
  2756: {
    source: "Что произойдет, если два маршрута совпадают с текущим URL? Как это управляется?",
    question: "What happens if two routes match the current URL? How is that managed?",
    shortAnswer: "If two routes match, `<Routes>` picks the first one that fully matches the current URL. This is called an \"exact match\". You manage it by ordering routes from most specific to least specific.",
    longAnswer: "React Router DOM handles routes on a \"first match\" basis. This means `<Routes>` checks each `<Route>` top to bottom and picks the first whose `path` fully matches the current URL.\nFor example:\n\nIf the current URL is `/about/team`, `<Team />` renders, because that route is more specific.\nRoute ordering:\nSpecific routes go higher up.\nUsing wildcards (`*`):\nTo specify more general routes.\n\nThis approach gives you control over routing behaviour and prevents conflicts.",
    codeExample: "<Routes>\n  <Route path=\"/about/team\" element={<Team />} />\n  <Route path=\"/about\" element={<About />} />\n</Routes>\n\n<Route path=\"*\" element={<NotFound />} />",
  },
  2757: {
    source: "Что делает <Link> и чем он отличается от обычного <a> тега?",
    question: "What does <Link> do and how does it differ from a regular <a> tag?",
    shortAnswer: "`<Link>` from React Router is used for navigation inside a React application, preventing a full page reload. Unlike `<a>`, which refreshes the whole page, `<Link>` updates only the components that need it and preserves application state.",
    longAnswer: "In React applications, `<Link>` replaces the standard `<a>` tag for internal navigation. The main differences:\nPage reload:\n\n- `<a>` triggers a full page reload, because the browser goes to the server.\n\n- `<Link>` lets React Router intercept the transition and change only the content, which makes navigation instant.\n\nPreserving state:\n\n- With `<a>`, React state (for example data inside components) is reset.\n\n- `<Link>` preserves application state, because it works without a reload.\n\nExample:\n\nIn short, `<Link>` is used to create smooth, fast navigation inside a Single Page Application (SPA).",
    codeExample: "import { Link } from 'react-router-dom';\nconst Navigation = () => (\n  <nav>\n    <Link to=\"/\">Home</Link>\n    <Link to=\"/about\">About</Link>\n  </nav>\n);",
  },
  2758: {
    source: "Чем отличаются динамические сегменты маршрута и параметры запроса (query parameters)?",
    question: "How do dynamic route segments differ from query parameters?",
    shortAnswer: "A dynamic route segment is a part of the path written as `:paramName`, and it is required. Query parameters are passed in the string after `?` and are optional. Dynamic segments are part of the URL structure, while query parameters suit passing extra information.",
    longAnswer: "Dynamic route segments:\n\n- Are written in the route path using a colon (`:`).\n\n- Are a required part of the URL.\n\n- Are used to identify specific resources (for example a user ID).\n\nExample URL: `/user/123`. Here `123` is the dynamic segment `userId`.\nQuery parameters:\n\n- Are passed after `?` in the URL in `key=value` form.\n\n- Are not required and can be added to any route.\n\n- Are used to pass extra data, for example sorting or filters.\n\nThe key difference:\n\n- Dynamic segments are part of the path, included in the route definition.\n\n- Query parameters are extra information that does not affect the route.",
    codeExample: "<Route path=\"/user/:userId\" element={<User />} />\n\n/products?sort=price&order=asc",
  },
  2759: {
    source: "Как получить параметры маршрута внутри компонента?",
    question: "How do you get route parameters inside a component?",
    shortAnswer: "Route parameters can be read with the `useParams` hook, which returns an object whose keys match the dynamic segments. Query parameters can be extracted via `useSearchParams`, which gives access to the query string.",
    longAnswer: "React Router provides convenient hooks for working with route parameters:\nFor dynamic route segments: use the `useParams` hook:\n\nIf the route is `/user/:userId` and the URL is `/user/123`, then `userId` will be `123`.\nFor query parameters: use the `useSearchParams` hook:\n\nFor the URL `/products?sort=price`, `sort` will be `price`.\nThese hooks let you extract parameters efficiently and use them to render components or run logic.",
    codeExample: "import { useParams } from 'react-router-dom';\nconst User = () => {\n  const { userId } = useParams(); // Read the value of the userId segment\n  return <div>User ID: {userId}</div>;\n};\n\nimport { useSearchParams } from 'react-router-dom';\nconst Products = () => {\n  const [searchParams] = useSearchParams();\n  const sort = searchParams.get('sort'); // Read the value of the sort parameter\n  return <div>Sort by: {sort}</div>;\n};",
  },
  2760: {
    source: "Что делает useLocation, и какие данные он предоставляет?",
    question: "What does useLocation do, and what data does it provide?",
    shortAnswer: "`useLocation` is a React Router hook that returns an object with information about the current URL. It provides data such as the path (`pathname`), the query string (`search`) and the route state (`state`), if one was passed.",
    longAnswer: "The `useLocation` hook gives access to the current location in the application.\nThe returned object has the following properties:\n\n- `pathname`: A string representing the current path.\n\n- `search`: The query string, for example `?sort=price`.\n\n- `hash`: The URL fragment after `#`, for example `#section1`.\n\n- `state`: Extra data passed during navigation, for example through `Link` or `navigate`.\n\nExample usage:\n\n`useLocation` is often used to check the current path, render matching content, or work with passed data.",
    codeExample: "import { useLocation } from 'react-router-dom';\nconst CurrentLocation = () => {\n  const location = useLocation();\n  return (\n    <div>\n      <p>Pathname: {location.pathname}</p>\n      <p>Search: {location.search}</p>\n      <p>Hash: {location.hash}</p>\n      <p>State: {JSON.stringify(location.state)}</p>\n    </div>\n  );\n};",
  },
  2761: {
    source: "Как задать маршруты с вложенными компонентами (nested routes)?",
    question: "How do you define routes with nested components (nested routes)?",
    shortAnswer: "To create nested routes in React Router you use a route structure where one `<Route>` contains another. Child routes render inside the parent component using the `<Outlet>` component.",
    longAnswer: "Nested routes let you organise components into a hierarchy where child routes render inside the parent. This is achieved using the `<Outlet>` component to render the child routes.\nExample:\n\n- The parent component (`Layout`) holds shared content, for example a header and a footer.\n\n- `<Outlet>` marks the place where child routes (`/about`, `/contact`) will render.\n\nWhen navigating to `/about`, `<About />` renders inside `<Layout>`.",
    codeExample: "import { Routes, Route } from 'react-router-dom';\nconst App = () => (\n  <Routes>\n    <Route path=\"/\" element={<Layout />}>\n      <Route path=\"about\" element={<About />} />\n      <Route path=\"contact\" element={<Contact />} />\n    </Route>\n  </Routes>\n);\nconst Layout = () => (\n  <div>\n    <header>Header</header>\n    <Outlet /> {/* Child routes render here */}\n    <footer>Footer</footer>\n  </div>\n);",
  },
  2762: {
    source: "Что такое Outlet, и когда он используется?",
    question: "What is Outlet, and when is it used?",
    shortAnswer: "`Outlet` is a React Router component that renders a child route inside the parent component. It is used when routes are nested, to render child route content in a specified place.",
    longAnswer: "`Outlet` is the \"empty slot\" in a parent route's component where child routes render. It lets you build a hierarchical route structure.\nExample usage:\n\n- Defining the routes:\n\n- The parent component with `Outlet`:\n\nHow it works:\n\n- When navigating to `/dashboard`, `<Header>`, `<Dashboard />` and `<Footer>` render.\n\n- When navigating to `/settings`, `<Settings />` renders instead of `<Dashboard />`.\n\nWhen it is used:\n\n- With nested routes, when you need to add a shared shell (for example a header or footer).\n\n- To render dynamic content depending on the current child route.",
    codeExample: "<Routes>\n  <Route path=\"/\" element={<Layout />}>\n    <Route path=\"dashboard\" element={<Dashboard />} />\n    <Route path=\"settings\" element={<Settings />} />\n  </Route>\n</Routes>\n\nconst Layout = () => (\n  <div>\n    <header>Header</header>\n    <Outlet /> {/* Dashboard and Settings render here */}\n    <footer>Footer</footer>\n  </div>\n);",
  },
  2763: {
    source: "Как работают относительные и абсолютные пути в маршрутах?",
    question: "How do relative and absolute paths work in routes?",
    shortAnswer: "Absolute paths start with `/` and give the full path from the root. Relative paths depend on the current location (URL) and let you build routes relative to the current one.",
    longAnswer: "Absolute paths:\n\n- Start with `/`.\n\n- Give the exact location of the route regardless of the current path.\n\nRelative paths:\n\n- Are given relative to the current route.\n\n- Are convenient for nested routes.\n\nExample: for the current URL `/user/123`:\n\n- The absolute path `/settings` leads to `/settings`.\n\n- The relative path `settings` leads to `/user/123/settings`.\n\nUsing relative paths makes the code more flexible and easier to maintain when the route structure changes.",
    codeExample: "<Link to=\"/about\">About</Link> // Absolute path\n\n<Link to=\"profile\">Profile</Link> // Relative path",
  },
  2764: {
    source: "Как задать редирект в приложении?",
    question: "How do you set up a redirect in an application?",
    shortAnswer: "A redirect can be set up with the `<Navigate>` component for automatic redirection, or programmatically through the `useNavigate` hook.",
    longAnswer: "Redirecting with `<Navigate>`:\nUsed for automatic redirection, for example when a condition is checked.\n\nProgrammatic redirect through `useNavigate`:\nLets you redirect the user on a button press or in the course of running some logic.\n\nRedirects are useful for protecting routes, redirecting after an action, or moving users away from outdated pages.",
    codeExample: "import { Navigate } from 'react-router-dom';\nconst ProtectedRoute = ({ isAuthenticated }) => {\n  return isAuthenticated ? <Dashboard /> : <Navigate to=\"/login\" />;\n};\n\nimport { useNavigate } from 'react-router-dom';\nconst Login = () => {\n  const navigate = useNavigate();\n  const handleLogin = () => {\n    // Authentication logic\n    navigate('/dashboard');\n  };\n  return <button onClick={handleLogin}>Login</button>;\n};",
  },
  2765: {
    source: "Чем отличается replace от push при использовании useNavigate?",
    question: "How does replace differ from push when using useNavigate?",
    shortAnswer: "The `push` method adds a new route to the browser history, which lets you go back. The `replace` method replaces the current route without keeping it in history, which prevents returning to the previous page.",
    longAnswer: "The `useNavigate` hook provides two ways to navigate, differing in how they manipulate the browser history:\n\n- `push` (the default):\n\n- Adds a new route to the history stack.\n\n- The user can go back using the browser's \"Back\" button.\n\n- `replace`:\n\n- Replaces the current route.\n\n- Does not add it to the history stack, which rules out going back.\n\nWhen to use which:\n\n- `push`: For ordinary navigation, when the user may want to return to the previous page.\n\n- `replace`: For redirects, to rule out going back (for example after logging in).",
    codeExample: "navigate('/dashboard'); // Equivalent to push\n\nnavigate('/login', { replace: true });",
  },
  2766: {
    source: "Что такое маршруты-заглушки (fallback routes), и как их реализовать?",
    question: "What are fallback routes, and how do you implement them?",
    shortAnswer: "Fallback routes handle the case where the requested route does not exist. They are implemented with the `*` route and let you show an error page or redirect to the main route.",
    longAnswer: "Fallback routes are routes that fire when none of the previously defined routes matched the current URL. In React Router they are declared with the path `*`.\nExample implementation:\n\nIf the user navigates to a path that does not exist, for example `/unknown`, the `NotFound` component renders.\nUsing fallback routes improves the user experience, preventing blank pages and directing the user to useful information.",
    codeExample: "import { Routes, Route } from 'react-router-dom';\nconst App = () => (\n  <Routes>\n    <Route path=\"/\" element={<Home />} />\n    <Route path=\"/about\" element={<About />} />\n    <Route path=\"*\" element={<NotFound />} /> {/* Fallback route */}\n  </Routes>\n);\nconst NotFound = () => <h1>404: Page Not Found</h1>;",
  },
  2767: {
    source: "Как работает ленивый роутинг (lazy loading) в React Router DOM?",
    question: "How does lazy routing (lazy loading) work in React Router DOM?",
    shortAnswer: "Lazy routing loads route components only when they are needed. This is achieved using `React.lazy` combined with a dynamic import (`import()`), which reduces the initial bundle size.",
    longAnswer: "Lazy routing lets you load route components \"on demand\" instead of including them in the initial bundle. This is especially useful for large applications where not every page is needed right away.\nExample implementation:\nImport components lazily with `React.lazy`:\n\nUse `<Suspense>` to show a loading indicator:\n\nHow it works:\n\n- When the user navigates to the `/about` route, the `About` component is loaded dynamically.\n\n- This reduces the cost of the application's initial load.",
    codeExample: "import { lazy } from 'react';\n\nconst Home = lazy(() => import('./Home'));\nconst About = lazy(() => import('./About'));\n\nimport { Routes, Route } from 'react-router-dom';\nimport { Suspense } from 'react';\nconst App = () => (\n  <Suspense fallback={<div>Loading...</div>}>\n    <Routes>\n      <Route path=\"/\" element={<Home />} />\n      <Route path=\"/about\" element={<About />} />\n    </Routes>\n  </Suspense>\n);",
  },
  2768: {
    source: "Как использовать Suspense и lazy для загрузки компонентов маршрутов?",
    question: "How do you use Suspense and lazy to load route components?",
    shortAnswer: "`React.lazy` is used to import components lazily, and `Suspense` lets you show a loading indicator while the component loads.",
    longAnswer: "`React.lazy` and `Suspense` work together to make loading large components or pages easier. The core idea is to load components only when they are genuinely needed.\nSetting up `React.lazy`:\nUse the `React.lazy` function for a lazy import:\n\nUsing `Suspense`:\nWrap the routes or components in `<Suspense>` to handle the loading state:\n\nBenefits:\n\n- Components are fetched only when they are used.\n\n- The application's main page loads faster.\n\nExample with nested routes:\nIt works with nested routes too:\n\nSo `lazy` and `Suspense` let you control component loading, minimising the application's load time and improving the user experience.",
    codeExample: "const Profile = lazy(() => import('./Profile'));\n\nimport { Suspense } from 'react';\nconst App = () => (\n  <Suspense fallback={<div>Loading...</div>}>\n    <Routes>\n      <Route path=\"/profile\" element={<Profile />} />\n    </Routes>\n  </Suspense>\n);\n\nconst Dashboard = lazy(() => import('./Dashboard'));\nconst Settings = lazy(() => import('./Settings'));\nconst App = () => (\n  <Suspense fallback={<div>Loading...</div>}>\n    <Routes>\n      <Route path=\"/dashboard\" element={<Dashboard />}>\n        <Route path=\"settings\" element={<Settings />} />\n      </Route>\n    </Routes>\n  </Suspense>\n);",
  },
  2769: {
    source: "Что такое маршруты-защита (protected routes), и как их настроить?",
    question: "What are protected routes, and how do you set them up?",
    shortAnswer: "Protected routes let you restrict access to components or pages. They are implemented by checking a condition (for example authentication) and redirecting unauthorised users to another page.",
    longAnswer: "Protected routes are used to prevent access to pages unless certain conditions are met — for example, if the user is not signed in.\nExample implementation:\n\n- Create a guard component:\n\n- Use the guard component in the routes:\n\nBenefits:\n\n- Simplifies managing access to routes.\n\n- Improves security by ruling out access to restricted resources.",
    codeExample: "import { Navigate } from 'react-router-dom';\nconst ProtectedRoute = ({ isAuthenticated, children }) => {\n  return isAuthenticated ? children : <Navigate to=\"/login\" />;\n};\n\nimport { Routes, Route } from 'react-router-dom';\nconst App = () => {\n  const isAuthenticated = /* Authentication check logic */;\n  return (\n    <Routes>\n      <Route path=\"/dashboard\" element={\n        <ProtectedRoute isAuthenticated={isAuthenticated}>\n          <Dashboard />\n        </ProtectedRoute>\n      } />\n      <Route path=\"/login\" element={<Login />} />\n    </Routes>\n  );\n};",
  },
  2770: {
    source: "Как реализовать проверку прав доступа на маршрутах (authorization)?",
    question: "How do you implement access-rights checks on routes (authorization)?",
    shortAnswer: "An access-rights check (authorization) is added to routes where access is granted only for a matching role or permission. It is implemented by checking before the component renders.",
    longAnswer: "Access rights can be checked by adding a condition to a protected route. For example, using roles:\nA component for checking roles:\n\nAn example route with authorization:\n\nBenefits:\n\n- Makes it easy to manage access based on roles.\n\n- Provides flexibility when implementing complex security scenarios.",
    codeExample: "const RoleProtectedRoute = ({ userRole, allowedRoles, children }) => {\n  return allowedRoles.includes(userRole) ? children : <Navigate to=\"/unauthorized\" />;\n};\n\nconst App = () => {\n  const userRole = \"admin\"; // Example of the current role\n  return (\n    <Routes>\n      <Route path=\"/admin\" element={\n        <RoleProtectedRoute userRole={userRole} allowedRoles={['admin']}>\n          <AdminPanel />\n        </RoleProtectedRoute>\n      } />\n      <Route path=\"/unauthorized\" element={<Unauthorized />} />\n    </Routes>\n  );\n};",
  },
  2771: {
    source: "Как перехватить попытку смены маршрута (например, для отображения подтверждения)?",
    question: "How do you intercept an attempted route change (for example to show a confirmation)?",
    shortAnswer: "To intercept an attempted route change, React Router provides the `usePrompt` hook (or `useBlocker` in React Router 6), which lets you show a confirmation or run logic before the path changes.",
    longAnswer: "Intercepting attempted route changes can be implemented as follows:\nUsing the `useBlocker` hook:\n\nExample usage:\n\nExplanation:\n\n- If `isDirty` is `true`, an attempted route change triggers a confirmation.\n\n- If the user agrees, the transition continues.\n\nImportant: in recent versions of React Router, `useNavigate` is recommended for managing programmatic redirects, but `useBlocker` remains useful for confirmations.",
    codeExample: "import { useBlocker } from 'react-router-dom';\nconst useUnsavedChangesPrompt = (shouldBlock) => {\n  useBlocker(({ retry }) => {\n    if (window.confirm(\"You have unsaved changes. Leave the page?\")) {\n      retry(); // Continue the transition\n    }\n  }, shouldBlock);\n};\n\nconst FormPage = () => {\n  const [isDirty, setIsDirty] = useState(false);\n  useUnsavedChangesPrompt(isDirty);\n  const handleInputChange = () => setIsDirty(true);\n  return (\n    <form>\n      <input onChange={handleInputChange} />\n      <button type=\"submit\" onClick={() => setIsDirty(false)}>Save</button>\n    </form>\n  );\n};",
  },
  2772: {
    source: "Что такое createBrowserRouter и чем он отличается от использования <BrowserRouter>?",
    question: "What is createBrowserRouter and how does it differ from using <BrowserRouter>?",
    shortAnswer: "`<BrowserRouter>` provides basic routing with routes described declaratively in JSX. `createBrowserRouter` lets you describe routes programmatically, providing advanced capabilities such as data loaders, error handlers and route actions.",
    longAnswer: "`<BrowserRouter>`:\n\n- Works through JSX.\n\n- Routes are described declaratively:\n\n`createBrowserRouter`:\n\n- Lets you describe routes as a JavaScript object.\n\n- Supports loaders, actions and error handling.\n\nDifferences:\n\n- `createBrowserRouter` provides more capability for server rendering and managing route state.\n\n- `<BrowserRouter>` is simpler and suits applications without complex route logic.",
    codeExample: "import { BrowserRouter, Routes, Route } from 'react-router-dom';\nconst App = () => (\n  <BrowserRouter>\n    <Routes>\n      <Route path=\"/\" element={<Home />} />\n      <Route path=\"/about\" element={<About />} />\n    </Routes>\n  </BrowserRouter>\n);\n\nimport { createBrowserRouter, RouterProvider } from 'react-router-dom';\nconst router = createBrowserRouter([\n  {\n    path: \"/\",\n    element: <Home />,\n    errorElement: <NotFound />,\n    loader: fetchHomeData,\n  },\n  {\n    path: \"/about\",\n    element: <About />,\n  },\n]);\nconst App = () => <RouterProvider router={router} />;",
  },
  2773: {
    source: "Как настроить маршрутизацию на стороне сервера в приложении с использованием React Router DOM?",
    question: "How do you set up server-side routing in an application using React Router DOM?",
    shortAnswer: "For server-side routing you need to configure the server (for example Node.js with Express) so that it handles every request and points it at the React application's main HTML file. React Router on the client then takes over rendering components based on the path.",
    longAnswer: "Setting up routes on the server:\nUse Node.js and Express:\n\nWhy this matters:\n\n- The server returns the same HTML file for every request.\n\n- React Router interprets the URL and renders the matching component.\n\nFor server-side rendering (SSR):\n\n- Use libraries such as `react-router-dom/server` and `react-dom/server` to render components on the server.\n\nIn summary: setting up routes on the server provides deep-link support and prevents 404 errors when a page is accessed directly.",
    codeExample: "const express = require('express');\nconst path = require('path');\nconst app = express();\n// Serving static files\napp.use(express.static(path.join(__dirname, 'build')));\n// Handling every request\napp.get('*', (req, res) => {\n  res.sendFile(path.join(__dirname, 'build', 'index.html'));\n});\napp.listen(3000, () => console.log('Server running on port 3000'));\n\nimport { StaticRouter } from 'react-router-dom/server';\nconst html = renderToString(\n  <StaticRouter location={req.url}>\n    <App />\n  </StaticRouter>\n);",
  },
  2774: {
    source: "Как добавить обработку ошибок маршрутов (error handling routes)?",
    question: "How do you add route error handling?",
    shortAnswer: "Error handling in React Router is done through a route's `errorElement` property or through a global error handler at the router level. Errors can be displayed using an error component.",
    longAnswer: "Using the `errorElement` property:\nAdd `errorElement` to show an error page when a problem occurs.\n\nHandling loader errors:\nIf an error occurs in a data loader, `errorElement` is displayed automatically.\n\nBenefits:\n\n- Centralised error handling.\n\n- Better UX, showing clear messages instead of blank pages.",
    codeExample: "import { createBrowserRouter, RouterProvider } from 'react-router-dom';\nconst router = createBrowserRouter([\n  {\n    path: \"/\",\n    element: <Home />,\n    errorElement: <NotFound />,\n  },\n  {\n    path: \"/about\",\n    element: <About />,\n  },\n]);\nconst App = () => <RouterProvider router={router} />;\n\nconst loaderWithError = async () => {\n  throw new Error(\"Data loading error\");\n};\nconst router = createBrowserRouter([\n  {\n    path: \"/\",\n    element: <Home />,\n    loader: loaderWithError,\n    errorElement: <ErrorPage />,\n  },\n]);",
  },
  2775: {
    source: "Как интегрировать React Router DOM с системой управления состоянием, например, Redux?",
    question: "How do you integrate React Router DOM with a state management system, for example Redux?",
    shortAnswer: "Integrating React Router DOM with Redux lets you use Redux state to drive routing, for example for redirects based on the user's state. This is achieved through hooks such as `useSelector` and `useDispatch` inside route components.",
    longAnswer: "An example with an authentication check through Redux:\nCreate a Redux store holding user state:\n\nConnect Redux to the React application:\n\nUsing Redux state in routes:\nCheck the state before rendering protected routes:\n\nIntegrating with redirects:\nUse `useDispatch` to change routes programmatically:\n\nBenefits:\n\n- Centralises state management and routing.\n\n- Makes redirects based on global state convenient.",
    codeExample: "const initialState = { isAuthenticated: false };\nconst authReducer = (state = initialState, action) => {\n  switch (action.type) {\n    case 'LOGIN':\n      return { ...state, isAuthenticated: true };\n    case 'LOGOUT':\n      return { ...state, isAuthenticated: false };\n    default:\n      return state;\n  }\n};\n\nimport { Provider } from 'react-redux';\nimport { createStore } from 'redux';\nconst store = createStore(authReducer);\nconst App = () => (\n  <Provider store={store}>\n    <MainRouter />\n  </Provider>\n);\n\nimport { useSelector } from 'react-redux';\nimport { Navigate } from 'react-router-dom';\nconst ProtectedRoute = ({ children }) => {\n  const isAuthenticated = useSelector(state => state.isAuthenticated);\n  return isAuthenticated ? children : <Navigate to=\"/login\" />;\n};\n\nimport { useDispatch } from 'react-redux';\nimport { useNavigate } from 'react-router-dom';\nconst LoginPage = () => {\n  const dispatch = useDispatch();\n  const navigate = useNavigate();\n  const handleLogin = () => {\n    dispatch({ type: 'LOGIN' });\n    navigate('/dashboard');\n  };\n  return <button onClick={handleLogin}>Log in</button>;\n};",
  },
  2776: {
    source: "Как настроить маршруты с анимациями переходов?",
    question: "How do you set up routes with transition animations?",
    shortAnswer: "Transition animations between routes in React Router DOM are implemented using the `react-transition-group` library or frameworks such as Framer Motion. Route components are wrapped in an animated container.",
    longAnswer: "Installing `react-transition-group`:\n\nAn example with animation:\nSet up animated routes:\n\nAdd the CSS for the animation:\n\nUsing Framer Motion:\nA more flexible approach:\n\nIn summary: animating routes makes the application feel smoother and more interactive.",
    codeExample: "npm install react-transition-group\n\nimport { CSSTransition, TransitionGroup } from 'react-transition-group';\nimport { Routes, Route, useLocation } from 'react-router-dom';\nimport './animations.css';\nconst AnimatedRoutes = () => {\n  const location = useLocation();\n  return (\n    <TransitionGroup>\n      <CSSTransition key={location.key} classNames=\"fade\" timeout={300}>\n        <Routes location={location}>\n          <Route path=\"/\" element={<Home />} />\n          <Route path=\"/about\" element={<About />} />\n        </Routes>\n      </CSSTransition>\n    </TransitionGroup>\n  );\n};\n\n.fade-enter {\n  opacity: 0;\n}\n.fade-enter-active {\n  opacity: 1;\n  transition: opacity 300ms;\n}\n.fade-exit {\n  opacity: 1;\n}\n.fade-exit-active {\n  opacity: 0;\n  transition: opacity 300ms;\n}\n\nimport { motion } from 'framer-motion';\nconst AnimatedPage = ({ children }) => (\n  <motion.div\n    initial={{ opacity: 0 }}\n    animate={{ opacity: 1 }}\n    exit={{ opacity: 0 }}\n  >\n    {children}\n  </motion.div>\n);",
  },
  2777: {
    source: "Как React Router DOM обрабатывает неизвестные или некорректные URL?",
    question: "How does React Router DOM handle unknown or invalid URLs?",
    shortAnswer: "React Router DOM routes invalid URLs to an error-handling component, which is configured with a special route (`path=\"*\"` or `errorElement`).",
    longAnswer: "Using the fallback route `path=\"*\"`:\nAdd a route for unknown URLs:\n\nUsing `errorElement`:\nIn `createBrowserRouter` you can configure a global error handler:\n\nShowing a 404 page:\nCreate a component for the error:\n\nWhy this matters:\nHandling invalid routes improves UX by showing the user useful information instead of a blank page.",
    codeExample: "import { Routes, Route } from 'react-router-dom';\nconst App = () => (\n  <Routes>\n    <Route path=\"/\" element={<Home />} />\n    <Route path=\"*\" element={<NotFound />} />\n  </Routes>\n);\n\nconst router = createBrowserRouter([\n  {\n    path: \"/\",\n    element: <Home />,\n    errorElement: <NotFound />,\n  },\n  {\n    path: \"/about\",\n    element: <About />,\n  },\n]);\nconst App = () => <RouterProvider router={router} />;\n\nconst NotFound = () => <h1>Page not found</h1>;",
  },
  2778: {
    source: "Как передавать данные через loader в data маршрутах?",
    question: "How do you pass data through a loader in data routes?",
    shortAnswer: "`loader` in data routes is used to fetch data before the component renders. It returns data which is then available inside the component through the `useLoaderData` hook.",
    longAnswer: "Defining a `loader`:\nIt is a function that fetches data asynchronously.\n\nExample of using `loader`:\nDefine a route with a `loader`:\n\nReading the data in the component:\nUse the `useLoaderData` hook:\n\nError handling:\n\n- If the `loader` throws, `errorElement` takes over.\n\nBenefits:\n\n- Shorter render time, because the data is fetched before the component is shown.\n\n- Centralised handling of route data.",
    codeExample: "const fetchData = async () => {\n  const response = await fetch('/api/data');\n  if (!response.ok) throw new Error('Failed to load data');\n  return response.json();\n};\n\nimport { createBrowserRouter, RouterProvider } from 'react-router-dom';\nconst router = createBrowserRouter([\n  {\n    path: \"/\",\n    element: <Home />,\n    loader: fetchData,\n  },\n]);\nconst App = () => <RouterProvider router={router} />;\n\nimport { useLoaderData } from 'react-router-dom';\nconst Home = () => {\n  const data = useLoaderData();\n  return <div>{JSON.stringify(data)}</div>;\n};",
  },
  2779: {
    source: "Как работает action в data маршрутах, и зачем он нужен?",
    question: "How does action work in data routes, and what is it for?",
    shortAnswer: "`action` handles POST, PUT, DELETE and other requests for a route. It is used to perform operations such as submitting a form or updating data, and returns the operation's result, available in the component.",
    longAnswer: "Defining an `action`:\nIt is a function called on a POST or PUT request to the corresponding route.\n\nExample of using `action`:\nSet up a route with an `action`:\n\nHandling form submission:\nUse `<Form>` from React Router:\n\nReading the result:\nThe operation's result is available through `useActionData`.\n\nIn summary: `action` simplifies handling requests that change data, and integrates with routing.",
    codeExample: "const saveData = async ({ request }) => {\n  const formData = await request.formData();\n  const response = await fetch('/api/save', {\n    method: 'POST',\n    body: JSON.stringify(Object.fromEntries(formData)),\n    headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },\n  });\n  if (!response.ok) throw new Error('Failed to save data');\n  return response.json();\n};\n\nconst router = createBrowserRouter([\n  {\n    path: \"/form\",\n    element: <Form />,\n    action: saveData,\n  },\n]);\n\nimport { Form } from 'react-router-dom';\nconst FormComponent = () => (\n  <Form method=\"post\">\n    <input name=\"name\" type=\"text\" />\n    <button type=\"submit\">Submit</button>\n  </Form>\n);\n\nimport { useActionData } from 'react-router-dom';\nconst Form = () => {\n  const result = useActionData();\n  return result ? <p>Data saved</p> : null;\n};",
  },
  2780: {
    source: "Чем полезен useRouteError, и в каких ситуациях его использовать?",
    question: "What makes useRouteError useful, and in which situations should you use it?",
    shortAnswer: "`useRouteError` lets you read an error raised in a `loader` or `action` and show it to the user. It is used inside a route's `errorElement` to render custom error pages.",
    longAnswer: "How `useRouteError` works:\nAn error thrown in a `loader` or `action` is passed automatically to the route's `errorElement`.\nInside `errorElement`, the `useRouteError` hook provides the error object.\nExample usage:\nSet up a route with error handling:\n\nCreate a component to handle the error:\n\nHandling action errors:\nJust like with `loader`, errors from `action` are also handled through `errorElement`.\nBenefits:\n\n- Centralised error handling.\n\n- The ability to give the user clear error messages.\n\nIn summary: `useRouteError` improves the user experience by letting you show detailed information when errors occur.",
    codeExample: "const router = createBrowserRouter([\n  {\n    path: \"/\",\n    element: <Home />,\n    loader: async () => {\n      throw new Error(\"Data loading error\");\n    },\n    errorElement: <ErrorPage />,\n  },\n]);\n\nimport { useRouteError } from 'react-router-dom';\nconst ErrorPage = () => {\n  const error = useRouteError();\n  return (\n    <div>\n      <h1>Error!</h1>\n      <p>{error.message}</p>\n    </div>\n  );\n};",
  },
  2781: {
    source: "Как управлять заголовками страницы (title) через маршруты?",
    question: "How do you manage the page title through routes?",
    shortAnswer: "React Router DOM lets you manage the page title through `document.title` inside a route component. The `useEffect` hook is used to update the title depending on the route.",
    longAnswer: "Using `useEffect` to manage titles:\nIn the route component, change the page title on render:\n\nBenefits:\n\n- Dynamic title management for each route.\n\n- Easy to implement using React Router DOM.\n\nDrawbacks:\nIn some cases more code may be needed for complex routes, especially when using dynamic data.\nIn summary: managing titles through `useEffect` together with `useLocation` in React Router DOM provides both flexibility and simplicity.",
    codeExample: "import { useEffect } from 'react';\nimport { useLocation } from 'react-router-dom';\nconst Page = () => {\n  const location = useLocation();\n  useEffect(() => {\n    document.title = `Page Title - ${location.pathname}`;\n  }, [location.pathname]);\n  return <div>Page content</div>;\n};",
  },
  2782: {
    source: "Какие преимущества и недостатки у React Router DOM по сравнению с альтернативными библиотеками (например, Next.js маршрутизацией)?",
    question: "What are the advantages and disadvantages of React Router DOM compared with alternative libraries (for example Next.js routing)?",
    shortAnswer: "React Router DOM provides greater flexibility and integration with any React-based framework or library, whereas Next.js uses built-in routing and can offer server-side optimisations.",
    longAnswer: "Advantages of React Router DOM:\n\n- Flexibility: It can be integrated with any framework or library, and is not limited to React alone.\n\n- Configuration: It lets you set up routes through JSX and a component-based approach.\n\n- Dynamic state management: It supports Redux and other state management systems.\n\n- Performance: It lets you configure optimisation and caching on the client side.\n\nAdvantages of Next.js routing:\n\n- Server-side rendering (SSR): Gives a significant improvement in SEO and performance with static rendering.\n\n- Quick setup: Built-in routing lets you create routed applications quickly.\n\n- Application-level optimisation: Offers automatic generation of routes and of some components on the server.\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n- React Router DOM: Does not provide automatic server-side optimisation (SSR) and may need configuration to improve performance.\n\n- Next.js: Limits flexibility when integrating with non-Next components and libraries, since routes and components must be set up within the framework.\n\nUse in real scenarios:\n\n- For most projects React Router DOM is suitable, thanks to its flexibility and its ability to integrate with other libraries.\n\n- For applications with demanding SEO and performance requirements, Next.js may be the preferable choice.\n\nIn summary: the choice between React Router DOM and Next.js depends on the project's specific requirements, such as SEO, performance and integration flexibility.",
    codeExample: null,
  },
  2783: {
    source: "Как реагировать на изменение параметров маршрута (params) и обновлять данные?",
    question: "How do you react to a change in route parameters (params) and refresh the data?",
    shortAnswer: "When route parameters change, the component may not be recreated. To refresh the data, use a `watch` on `this.$route.params` or the `beforeRouteUpdate` hook. In those places you can request the data again. This approach guarantees the interface matches the current URL. It is standard practice in Vue applications.",
    longAnswer: "In Vue Router, when route parameters such as `id` change, the component often stays the same, so the creation hooks are not called.\nWhy the component is not recreated\nVue Router reuses the component if:\n\n- the same route is used\n\n- only the `params` change\n\nThis improves performance but requires refreshing the data manually.\nUsing watch\nThe simplest way is to watch the route parameters.\n\nSuitable if:\n\n- you need to react to a specific parameter\n\n- the logic is simple\n\nUsing beforeRouteUpdate\nFor more controlled behaviour, a navigation hook is used.\n\nAdvantages:\n\n- it is called before navigation completes\n\n- both the new and the old route are available\n\nPractical recommendations\n\n- Load the data in `created` on first entry\n\n- Refresh the data through `watch` or `beforeRouteUpdate`\n\n- Avoid logic that depends on the component being recreated\n\nConclusion\nTo react to a change in `params` you must track that change explicitly, because the component is not recreated automatically",
    codeExample: "watch: {\n  '$route.params.id'(newId, oldId) {\n    this.fetchData(newId);\n  }\n}\n\nbeforeRouteUpdate(to, from, next) {\n  this.fetchData(to.params.id);\n  next();\n}",
  },
  2784: {
    source: "Какие способы существуют для реагирования на смену динамического id в маршруте?",
    question: "What ways are there to react to a change of the dynamic id in a route?",
    shortAnswer: "A change of the dynamic `id` can be handled through a `watch` on `$route.params`, through `beforeRouteUpdate`, or through the `key` attribute on `<router-view>`. Each way has its own characteristics. `watch` and `beforeRouteUpdate` refresh the data without recreating the component. Using `key` causes the component to be recreated.",
    longAnswer: "A dynamic `id` in a route is a common scenario when displaying detail pages.\nWay 1. watch on params\nSuitable for refreshing data without recreating the component.\n\nPros:\n\n- a simple and clear approach\n\n- component state is preserved\n\nWay 2. beforeRouteUpdate\nUsed when you need control over navigation.\n\nPros:\n\n- access to both routes\n\n- the ability to cancel navigation\n\nWay 3. key on router-view\nYou can force the component to be recreated.\n\nCharacteristics:\n\n- the creation hooks are called\n\n- component state is reset\n\n- rarely used\n\nConclusion\nThe choice depends on whether component state needs to be preserved or the component recreated when the `id` changes.",
    codeExample: "watch: {\n  '$route.params.id'(id) {\n    this.loadData(id);\n  }\n}\n\nbeforeRouteUpdate(to, from, next) {\n  this.loadData(to.params.id);\n  next();\n}\n\n<router-view :key=\"$route.fullPath\" />",
  },
  2785: {
    source: "Для чего используется beforeRouteEnter?",
    question: "What is beforeRouteEnter used for?",
    shortAnswer: "`beforeRouteEnter` is used to run logic before the component is displayed. In this hook there is no access to `this` yet. It is often used to load data before entering the page. Once navigation completes, you can access the component instance through a callback. That makes the hook convenient for initialisation.",
    longAnswer: "`beforeRouteEnter` is a component route guard that is called before the component instance is created.\nThe peculiarity of accessing this\nIn `beforeRouteEnter`:\n\n- the component instance has not been created yet\n\n- `this` is unavailable\n\n- access to the component is possible through a callback\n\nExample:\n\nWhen to use beforeRouteEnter\nThis hook is suitable if:\n\n- you need to load data before rendering\n\n- it is important to avoid an intermediate state\n\n- control is required before entering the route\n\nDifference from beforeRouteUpdate\n\n- `beforeRouteEnter` — on first entry\n\n- `beforeRouteUpdate` — when params change\n\nPractical scenarios\n\n- Preloading data\n\n- Checking access rights\n\n- Logging entry to the page\n\nConclusion\n`beforeRouteEnter` is used to run logic before the component is created, and is useful when the data must be ready before the interface is displayed.",
    codeExample: "beforeRouteEnter(to, from, next) {\n  next(vm => {\n    vm.fetchData();\n  });\n}",
  },
  2786: {
    source: "Как реализовать проверку авторизации при переходе между маршрутами?",
    question: "How do you implement an authorization check when navigating between routes?",
    shortAnswer: "An authorization check is usually implemented through a global `beforeEach` guard. It checks for a token or an authentication state. If access is missing, it redirects to the login page. This approach centralises the security logic. It is the standard solution in Vue applications.",
    longAnswer: "Controlling access to routes is one of the most common tasks when using Vue Router.\nUsing the global beforeEach\nThe most widespread way is a global guard.\n\nUsing meta fields\nRoutes are marked with access flags.\n\nAdvantages of the approach\n\n- Centralised logic\n\n- Easy to maintain\n\n- Easy to scale\n\nAdditional measures\n\n- Checking role permissions\n\n- Refreshing the token\n\n- Handling the redirect after login\n\nConclusion\nAn authorization check during navigation is implemented through route guards, most often with a global `beforeEach`, which provides access control at the routing level.",
    codeExample: "router.beforeEach((to, from, next) => {\n  const isAuth = store.state.isAuthenticated;\n\n  if (to.meta.requiresAuth && !isAuth) {\n    next('/login');\n  } else {\n    next();\n  }\n});\n\n{\n  path: '/profile',\n  component: Profile,\n  meta: { requiresAuth: true }\n}",
  },
  2787: {
    source: "Как реализовать поддержку динамического base path в React-приложении?",
    question: "How do you implement support for a dynamic base path in a React application?",
    shortAnswer: "A dynamic base path lets a React application work correctly when it is deployed not at the domain root but in a subdirectory (for example /app/). To do this you need to configure the router and the bundler. In React Router, use the `basename` prop on the `` component. The basename value can come from environment variables or a configuration file. You also need to configure Webpack or another bundler by setting `publicPath`, so that static assets (JS, CSS) load from the correct path.",
    longAnswer: "Support for a dynamic base path is needed when your React application is deployed not at the domain root (for example `https://example.com`) but in a subdirectory (for example `https://example.com/my-app/`). This is common in microservice architectures where several applications live on one domain, or on shared hosting. Without correct configuration the application will look for assets and routes at the wrong paths, which leads to 404 errors and broken navigation.\n\nConfiguring React Router\n\nThe main tool for managing paths in React is the router. In React Router (v6) you can set the base path with the `basename` prop on the `<BrowserRouter>` or `<Router>` component. The value should be a string matching the subdirectory where your application lives. To make it dynamic, you can read the value from an environment variable or a configuration file loaded at build or run time.\n\nConfiguring the bundler (Webpack)\n\nFor static assets (JavaScript, CSS, images) to load correctly, you need to configure `publicPath` in your bundler. In Webpack this is done in the configuration. The `publicPath` value must match your application's base path. It can also be made dynamic using environment variables.\n\nIf you use Create React App, you can set the `PUBLIC_URL` environment variable before building. For example, `PUBLIC_URL=/my-app npm run build`. This automatically sets `publicPath` in Webpack and is used to prefix asset paths in the HTML.\n\nWorking with links and API requests\n\nWhen using a base path, all relative links to assets (for example images) and API endpoints must take that prefix into account. For links inside the application, use the `<Link>` component from React Router, which accounts for `basename` automatically. For API requests it is recommended to use absolute URLs or to create a base axios/fetch instance with the prefix set.\n\nConclusion: a dynamic base path is worth implementing when your React application is going to be deployed in a subdirectory. It ensures routing works correctly and assets load in environments such as microservices, corporate portals or shared hosting.",
    codeExample: "import { BrowserRouter, Routes, Route } from 'react-router-dom';\n\n// The base path value can come from an environment variable or config.\nconst BASE_PATH = process.env.REACT_APP_BASE_PATH || '/';\n\nfunction App() {\n  return (\n    <BrowserRouter basename={BASE_PATH}>\n      <Routes>\n        <Route path=\"/\" element={<HomePage />} />\n        <Route path=\"/about\" element={<AboutPage />} />\n      </Routes>\n    </BrowserRouter>\n  );\n}\n\n// webpack.config.js\nmodule.exports = {\n  output: {\n    publicPath: process.env.PUBLIC_PATH || '/',\n  },\n  // ... the rest of the configuration\n};\n\n// An example of configuring axios with a base URL\nimport axios from 'axios';\n\nconst api = axios.create({\n  baseURL: process.env.REACT_APP_API_BASE_URL || '/api',\n});\n\n// Every request through this instance will have the correct prefix.\napi.get('/users').then(response => console.log(response));",
  },
  2788: {
    source: "Что такое base URL в React Router и зачем он нужен?",
    question: "What is the base URL in React Router and what is it for?",
    shortAnswer: "The base URL in React Router is a prefix automatically added to every relative route in the application. It is needed when your React application is deployed not at the domain root (for example at `example.com/my-app/`) but in a subdirectory. Without the correct base URL, routing can break, because React Router will look for paths relative to the server root rather than your subdirectory. You set it with the `basename` prop on the `` component. This makes links and navigation work correctly in a production environment.",
    longAnswer: "In web development, especially when deploying single-page applications (SPAs), it often happens that the application is placed not in the domain's root directory but inside a subfolder. For example, it may be available at `https://company.com/dashboard/` or `https://github.com/username/project-name/` (for GitHub Pages). By default React Router assumes the application runs from the root (`/`). If that is not the case, all relative routes (for example `/users` or `/settings`) will be resolved relative to the server root, which leads to 404 errors or components rendering incorrectly.\n\nWhat is basename?\n\nThe `basename` prop on the `<BrowserRouter>` (or `<HashRouter>`) component lets you specify a base path for the whole application. Every route defined inside that router will automatically carry this prefix. This affects:\n\n- Link generation by the `<Link to=\"...\">` component.\n\n- Path matching in `<Route path=\"...\">`.\n\n- The behaviour of hooks such as `useNavigate()` and `useLocation()`.\n\nHow do you use it?\n\nYou set `basename` when creating the router. Here is a practical example:\n\nIn this example, if the user goes to `/admin/users`, React Router will correctly render the `UsersList` component, because it knows the base path is `/admin`.\n\nImportant details\n\n- Server configuration: setting `basename` in React Router does not replace configuring the server. Your web server (Nginx, Apache) or host (GitHub Pages, Netlify) must also be configured so that every request within `/your-base-path` is routed to your `index.html` (the SPA \"catch-all\" rule).\n\n- Absolute paths: if you use absolute URLs (starting with `/`) inside `basename`, they will be completed automatically. Relative paths (without a leading slash) work relative to the current URL.\n\n- Hash router: for `HashRouter` the principle is the same, but the hash fragment (`#`) is added after the base path.\n\nConclusion: use `basename` in React Router when your application is deployed in a subdirectory on the server. It is a necessary condition for routing and navigation to work correctly and for avoiding 404 errors in a production environment.",
    codeExample: "import { BrowserRouter, Routes, Route, Link } from 'react-router-dom';\n\nfunction App() {\n  // The application is deployed in the /admin subdirectory\n  return (\n    <BrowserRouter basename=\"/admin\">\n      <nav>\n        <Link to=\"/\">Dashboard</Link> // Actual path: /admin/\n        <Link to=\"/users\">Users</Link> // Actual path: /admin/users\n      </nav>\n      <Routes>\n        <Route path=\"/\" element={<Dashboard />} />\n        <Route path=\"/users\" element={<UsersList />} />\n      </Routes>\n    </BrowserRouter>\n  );\n}",
  },
  2789: {
    source: "Чем отличается клиентская маршрутизация в SPA от серверной навигации?",
    question: "How does client-side routing in an SPA differ from server navigation?",
    shortAnswer: "Client-side routing in an SPA handles transitions between pages in the browser without reloading the page, using JavaScript and the History API. Server navigation sends a request to the server, which returns new HTML, causing a full reload. Client-side routing is faster and creates a smooth user experience, but requires loading the whole application up front. Server navigation is simpler for SEO and works without JavaScript.",
    longAnswer: "The main difference\nClient-side routing in an SPA (Single Page Application) handles navigation in the browser without a full page reload. Server navigation, by contrast, sends a request to the server on every transition, and the server returns a new HTML document, causing a full reload.\nHow client-side routing works\nIn an SPA the application loads once, and then JavaScript intercepts clicks on links, changes the URL using the History API (pushState or replaceState) and updates the page content dynamically. This avoids the delays involved in loading resources again.\n\nHow server navigation works\nWith server navigation, every transition is a new HTTP request. The server handles the URL, generates HTML and sends it to the client. The browser fully reloads the page, resetting the application state.\n\nWhen to use which\nClient-side routing suits interactive applications where responsiveness and smooth transitions matter (for example dashboards, social networks). Server navigation is better for content sites where SEO optimisation and a fast first render matter (for example blogs, news portals).\nConclusion: the choice between client-side and server routing depends on requirements for performance, SEO and application complexity. For highly interactive SPAs, client-side routing is preferable; for content sites, server routing.",
    codeExample: "// An example with React Router\nimport { BrowserRouter, Routes, Route, Link } from 'react-router-dom';\n\nfunction App() {\n  return (\n    <BrowserRouter>\n      <nav>\n        <Link to=\"/\">Home</Link>\n        <Link to=\"/about\">About</Link>\n      </nav>\n      <Routes>\n        <Route path=\"/\" element={<Home />} />\n        <Route path=\"/about\" element={<About />} />\n      </Routes>\n    </BrowserRouter>\n  );\n}\n\n// An example of a server route in Express\napp.get('/about', (req, res) => {\n  res.send('<h1>About Page</h1><p>Server-rendered content</p>');\n});",
  },
  2790: {
    source: "В чем разница между react-router 5 и 6 версии?",
    question: "What is the difference between react-router version 5 and 6?",
    shortAnswer: "React Router v6 introduced several significant changes. The Switch component was replaced by Routes, and Route now uses element instead of component. Route order no longer matters, because v6 uses a ranking algorithm. The useHistory hook was replaced by useNavigate. v6 also added support for nested routes and relative links.",
    longAnswer: "The main differences between React Router v5 and v6\nReact Router v6 is a significant update that simplifies the API and makes routing more intuitive. The main change is replacing the Switch component with Routes, which automatically picks the best-matching route rather than the first match.\nChanges in route components\nIn v5, the Switch component was used to group Route elements, and route order mattered. In v6, Switch is replaced by Routes, and Route now takes an element prop instead of component or render. This lets you pass components directly:\n\nNavigation and hooks\nIn v5, the useHistory hook was used for programmatic navigation. In v6 it is replaced by useNavigate, which provides a more flexible API:\n\nNested routes and relative links\nReact Router v6 significantly improved support for nested routes. Child routes can now be defined directly inside the parent component using Outlet:\n\nConclusion\nReact Router v6 offers a cleaner, more predictable API, simplifies working with nested routes and improves performance. When migrating from v5 to v6 it is worth paying attention to replacing Switch with Routes, updating the navigation hooks, and refactoring nested routes to use Outlet.",
    codeExample: "// React Router v5\n<Switch>\n  <Route path=\"/home\" component={Home} />\n  <Route path=\"/about\" component={About} />\n</Switch>\n\n// React Router v6\n<Routes>\n  <Route path=\"/home\" element={<Home />} />\n  <Route path=\"/about\" element={<About />} />\n</Routes>\n\n// React Router v5\nconst history = useHistory();\nhistory.push('/home');\n\n// React Router v6\nconst navigate = useNavigate();\nnavigate('/home');\nnavigate(-1); // to go back\n\nfunction App() {\n  return (\n    <Routes>\n      <Route path=\"/dashboard\" element={<Dashboard />}>\n        <Route path=\"stats\" element={<Stats />} />\n        <Route path=\"settings\" element={<Settings />} />\n      </Route>\n    </Routes>\n  );\n}\n\nfunction Dashboard() {\n  return (\n    <div>\n      <h1>Dashboard</h1>\n      <Outlet /> {/* Child routes will render here */}\n    </div>\n  );\n}",
  },
  // ---- TypeScript ----
  1852: {
    source: "Что такое условные типы в TypeScript?",
    question: "What are conditional types in TypeScript?",
    shortAnswer: "Conditional types let you choose a type depending on a condition. The syntax resembles the ternary operator: `T extends U ? X : Y`.",
    longAnswer: "Conditional types let you compute a new type based on a logical condition. They work on this principle: if type `A` is compatible with type `B`, the result is one type, otherwise it is another. This is useful for generic data structures and functions whose types change depending on the input. Conditional types are most often used together with generics.\n\nIn the example below, the conditional type `IsString` returns `'yes'` when the given type is a string and `'no'` otherwise.",
    codeExample: "type IsString<T> = T extends string ? 'yes' : 'no';\ntype A = IsString<string>; // 'yes'\ntype B = IsString<number>; // 'no'",
  },
  1853: {
    source: "Что такое mapped types в TypeScript?",
    question: "What are mapped types in TypeScript?",
    shortAnswer: "Mapped types let you transform every property of an existing type in a single pass. This is handy for deriving a type from another one with all fields made required, optional or read-only.",
    longAnswer: "Mapped types take an existing type and apply a transformation to it through the `[P in keyof T]` syntax. This is how you create new types with changed modifiers (for example, adding `readonly` or `?` to every field). They are especially powerful in combination with `keyof` and conditional types — an object's structure can be changed dynamically without rewriting the types by hand.\n\nIn the example, the type `Readonly` makes every property of the given object read-only.",
    codeExample: "type Readonly<T> = {\n  readonly [P in keyof T]: T[P];\n};\ninterface User {\n  name: string;\n  age: number;\n}\nconst user: Readonly<User> = {\n  name: 'John',\n  age: 30,\n};\nuser.name = 'Doe'; // error: property `name` is read-only",
  },
  1854: {
    source: "Как работают шаблонные литеральные типы (template literal types) в TypeScript?",
    question: "How do template literal types work in TypeScript?",
    shortAnswer: "Template literal types construct new string types using template string syntax at the type level. They are useful for strictly typed routes, property names and other dynamic strings.",
    longAnswer: "Template literal types combine string literals and other types to create new string types. The syntax mirrors ordinary template literals in JavaScript, but it works at the type level. This is convenient for type-safe string patterns: URI routes, field names, commands with dynamic parts.\n\nIn the example, the type `Route` describes the allowed routes that start with `/users/` followed by any string.",
    codeExample: "type Route = `/users/${string}`;\n\nlet validRoute: Route = '/users/john'; // correct\nlet invalidRoute: Route = '/posts/john'; // error: the string does not match the pattern",
  },
  1855: {
    source: "Что такое Distributive Conditional Types в TypeScript?",
    question: "What are Distributive Conditional Types in TypeScript?",
    shortAnswer: "These are conditional types that are automatically distributed over each member of a union type when the checked type is a naked generic parameter.",
    longAnswer: "When a union is passed into the conditional type `T extends U ? X : Y` and `T` is a naked type parameter, TypeScript breaks the union apart into its individual members, applies the condition to each one and reassembles the result back into a union. This lets you transform complex types without duplicating code. Distributivity can be turned off by wrapping both sides in a tuple: `[T] extends [U] ? X : Y`.\n\nIn the example, the type `ToArray` creates a separate array for each type inside the union.",
    codeExample: "type ToArray<T> = T extends any ? T[] : never;\ntype A = ToArray<number>; // number[]\ntype B = ToArray<number | string>; // number[] | string[]",
  },
  1856: {
    source: "Что такое utility types в TypeScript?",
    question: "What are utility types in TypeScript?",
    shortAnswer: "Utility types are built-in helper types (`Partial`, `Pick`, `Omit`, `Required`, `Readonly`, `Record` and others) that transform existing types: extracting parts, changing modifiers, creating derived types.",
    longAnswer: "Utility types are built into TypeScript and spare you from writing type transformations by hand. The most common ones are: `Partial<T>` (all fields optional), `Required<T>` (all required), `Readonly<T>` (read-only), `Pick<T, K>` and `Omit<T, K>` (select or exclude fields), `Record<K, T>` (an object with the given keys and value type), `ReturnType<F>`, `Parameters<F>`. They make code more compact and easier to maintain.\n\nIn the example, `Partial<User>` makes every property of the `User` interface optional.",
    codeExample: "interface User {\n  name: string;\n  age: number;\n  email?: string;\n}\ntype PartialUser = Partial<User>; // every property becomes optional",
  },
  1857: {
    source: "Как работают рекурсивные типы в TypeScript?",
    question: "How do recursive types work in TypeScript?",
    shortAnswer: "Recursive types refer to themselves. They are needed to describe structures of arbitrary nesting depth — trees, linked lists, JSON.",
    longAnswer: "A recursive type mentions its own name in its definition. This is useful for data of unknown nesting depth: a tree, a graph, JSON. For example, you can describe a JSON value where an object contains fields each of which may again be a JSON value. TypeScript supports recursion in type aliases and interfaces, but with restrictions on types that expand infinitely.\n\nIn the example, the type `JSONValue` describes any valid JSON value, including nested objects and arrays.",
    codeExample: "type JSONValue = string | number | boolean | JSONObject | JSONArray;\ninterface JSONObject {\n  [key: string]: JSONValue;\n}\ntype JSONArray = JSONValue[];\nconst data: JSONValue = {\n  name: \"John\",\n  children: [\n    {\n      name: \"Jane\",\n    },\n  ],\n};",
  },
  1858: {
    source: "Что такое декоратор в TypeScript?",
    question: "What is a decorator in TypeScript?",
    shortAnswer: "A decorator is a special function that modifies a class, method, property or parameter. Decorators add metadata or change behaviour without touching the source code of the declaration.",
    longAnswer: "Decorators are declared with `@` and applied to classes and their members; they run when the class is defined, not when it is called. The kinds are: a class decorator (modifies the constructor), a method decorator (changes the method's behaviour), a property decorator (validation, logging) and a parameter decorator (checking or transforming arguments). Typical uses are validation (`@MinLength(5)`), logging, caching and authorization, as well as the Angular and NestJS frameworks.\n\nIn the example, the `log` decorator wraps a method and logs the arguments of every call.",
    codeExample: "function log(target: any, key: string, descriptor: PropertyDescriptor) {\n  const originalMethod = descriptor.value;\n  descriptor.value = function (...args: any[]) {\n    console.log(`Method ${key} was called with arguments: ${args}`);\n    return originalMethod.apply(this, args);\n  };\n  return descriptor;\n}\nclass Calculator {\n  @log\n  add(a: number, b: number) {\n    return a + b;\n  }\n}\nconst calc = new Calculator();\ncalc.add(2, 3); // In the console: \"Method add was called with arguments: 2,3\"",
  },
  1859: {
    source: "Для чего нужны миксины в TypeScript?",
    question: "What are mixins for in TypeScript?",
    shortAnswer: "Mixins let you reuse code across several classes by combining their functionality. They are an alternative to multiple inheritance, which TypeScript does not have.",
    longAnswer: "A mixin is a function that takes a base class and returns a new class with added properties and methods. By applying several such functions in sequence you can assemble a class out of independent \"pieces\" of behaviour without duplication. The helper type `Constructor` is normally used to type the base class.\n\nIn the example, `Timestamped` is a mixin that adds a `timestamp` property to any class passed to it.",
    codeExample: "type Constructor<T = {}> = new (...args: any[]) => T;\nfunction Timestamped<TBase extends Constructor>(Base: TBase) {\n  return class extends Base {\n    timestamp = Date.now();\n  };\n}\nclass User {\n  name: string;\n  constructor(name: string) {\n    this.name = name;\n  }\n}\nconst TimestampedUser = Timestamped(User);\nconst user = new TimestampedUser(\"Alice\");\nconsole.log(user.timestamp); // Prints the current time",
  },
  1860: {
    source: "Чем отличается абстрактный класс от интерфейса?",
    question: "How does an abstract class differ from an interface?",
    shortAnswer: "An abstract class cannot be instantiated directly, only inherited from, and it may contain method implementations and fields. An interface is a contract without implementation; a class can implement several interfaces at once.",
    longAnswer: "An abstract class is declared with the `abstract` keyword, may contain both abstract (bodyless) and implemented methods as well as fields and a constructor; you can inherit from only one such class. An interface describes only the signatures of properties and methods, exists only at compile time and does not appear in the compiled JavaScript; a class implements several interfaces through `implements`. The upshot: abstract classes suit shared template behaviour, interfaces suit describing a mandatory set of methods.",
    codeExample: "abstract class Animal {\n  abstract makeSound(): void; // Must be implemented\n  move() { console.log(\"Moving...\"); } // Already implemented\n}\nclass Dog extends Animal {\n  makeSound() { console.log(\"Woof!\"); }\n}\n\ninterface Flyable {\n  fly(): void;\n}\nclass Bird implements Flyable {\n  fly() { console.log(\"Flying!\"); }\n}",
  },
  1861: {
    source: "Как типизировать данные, чтобы избегать ошибок?",
    question: "How should you type data in order to avoid errors?",
    shortAnswer: "Describe the shape of objects with `type`/`interface`, use generics instead of `any`, narrow types with type guards, apply utility types for derived types and turn on strict mode (`strict: true` in `tsconfig.json`).",
    longAnswer: "Reliable typing comes down to several practices: describe the shape of objects with `type` or `interface`; factor shared behaviour out into generics instead of `any`; use type guards (`typeof`, `instanceof`, `value is T` predicates) to narrow `unknown` safely; apply utility types (`Partial`, `Readonly`, `Pick`) for derived types; and turn on the compiler's strict checks. This reduces the number of runtime errors and makes the code easier to maintain.\n\nThe example shows a basic type alias, a generic function, a custom type guard and derived types built with utility types.",
    codeExample: "type User = {\n  id: number;\n  name: string;\n  isAdmin?: boolean; // Optional field\n};\n\nfunction getFirst<T>(arr: T[]): T | undefined {\n  return arr[0];\n}\n\nfunction isString(value: unknown): value is string {\n  return typeof value === 'string';\n}\n\ntype PartialUser = Partial<User>; // All fields optional\ntype ReadonlyUser = Readonly<User>; // Read-only",
  },
  1862: {
    source: "Чем отличается тип unknown от типа any в TypeScript?",
    question: "How does the unknown type differ from the any type in TypeScript?",
    shortAnswer: "The `any` type lets a variable hold any value and lets you do anything with it, but it switches off type checking, which can lead to errors. The `unknown` type, by contrast, requires explicit type checks before use, which makes the code safer. Using unknown is recommended when the type of the data is not known in advance but type safety still has to be guaranteed.",
    longAnswer: "In TypeScript the `any` type gives you complete freedom when working with a variable, but all type protection is lost in the process. For example, if a value is assigned to a variable of type any, TypeScript will not check whether that variable's methods can be called or its properties used, which can lead to errors at runtime:\n\nThe `unknown` type is a safer alternative. Like any, it lets you assign any value to a variable, but as soon as you try to interact with that variable TypeScript demands explicit type checks. This protects the program from unexpected behaviour. For example:\n\nSo unknown is recommended in situations where the type of a value is not known in advance (for example, when working with user input or API data). This type helps you write safer code, unlike any, which removes all checks and can lead to errors in unexpected places.",
    codeExample: "let data: any = \"Hello\";\n// The error appears only at runtime, not at compile time\nconsole.log(data.toFixed()); // TypeError: data.toFixed is not a function\n\nlet value: unknown = \"Hello\";\n// TypeScript will not allow the method to be called without a type check\nconsole.log(value.toFixed()); // Compile-time error\nif (typeof value === \"number\") {\n  console.log(value.toFixed()); // Now this is safe\n}",
  },
  1863: {
    source: "В чем разница между интерфейсом (interface) и типом (type) в TypeScript?",
    question: "What is the difference between an interface and a type in TypeScript?",
    shortAnswer: "Interfaces and types in TypeScript are very similar — both are used to describe the structure of objects — but there are differences. Interfaces suit describing objects and classes better and can extend (inherit from) one another. Types are more universal: they can combine primitive types, form unions or intersections of other types, but they cannot be extended the way interfaces can.",
    longAnswer: "An interface and a type are two ways of declaring a data structure in TypeScript, and in most cases they can be used interchangeably. However, there are several key differences.\n\nInterfaces are made specifically for describing objects and classes. With them you can define which properties and methods an object must have. One advantage of interfaces is the ability to extend (inherit). One interface can inherit from another, adding new properties or methods to it:\n\nTypes are more flexible: they let you not only describe objects but also create unions or intersections of types and work with primitive values (strings, numbers and so on). For example, you can define that a variable may be either a string or a number:\n\nTypes, however, cannot extend one another the way interfaces do. Instead they can be combined with other types, which lets you build new types out of existing ones:\n\nTo sum up, interfaces are a better fit for describing complex data structures that need to be extended, especially in an object-oriented programming style. Types are more universal and can be used to describe any combination of data.",
    codeExample: "interface Person {\n  name: string;\n  age: number;\n}\ninterface Employee extends Person {\n  position: string;\n}\nconst employee: Employee = {\n  name: \"Alice\",\n  age: 30,\n  position: \"Developer\"\n};\n\ntype StringOrNumber = string | number;\nlet value: StringOrNumber = \"Hello\";\nvalue = 42; // Also valid\n\ntype Animal = { species: string };\ntype Dog = Animal & { breed: string };\nconst myDog: Dog = {\n  species: \"Canine\",\n  breed: \"Golden Retriever\"\n};",
  },
  1864: {
    source: "Что такое утверждение типа (type assertion) в TypeScript, и как оно используется?",
    question: "What is a type assertion in TypeScript, and how is it used?",
    shortAnswer: "A type assertion in TypeScript lets you tell the compiler that a variable has a particular type, even when TypeScript cannot determine that automatically. It is used when the developer is sure about the type of the data despite TypeScript's inference. A type assertion can be written with the `as` syntax or with angle brackets (`<>`), but it should be applied with care, because it switches off some of the compiler's checks.",
    longAnswer: "TypeScript uses a type inference system to determine the type of variables automatically from their value. In some cases, however, TypeScript may not have enough information to infer the correct type, and then it becomes necessary to state the type explicitly with a type assertion.\n\nFor example, when working with data received from an API, TypeScript may assign the type `unknown` or `any` if it does not know the exact shape of the data. In such situations a type assertion helps you specify the concrete data type, provided the developer knows what that type should be:\n\nThere are two syntaxes for a type assertion: using the as keyword or angle brackets (`<>`):\n\nIt is important to understand, however, that a type assertion does not change the actual value of the variable — it only tells the compiler how to treat it. If the type assertion is incorrect, it can lead to errors while the program is running:\n\nType assertions should be used with care. They are worth applying when you are sure TypeScript is wrong in its assumptions about a variable's type and you need to specify the correct type for the program to work properly.",
    codeExample: "let someValue: unknown = \"This is a string\";\n\n// We know this is a string, and we can state that with a type assertion\nlet stringLength: number = (someValue as string).length;\nconsole.log(stringLength); // 16\n\n// Using \"as\"\nlet value: any = \"Hello\";\nlet strLength: number = (value as string).length;\n\n// Using angle brackets\nlet anotherStrLength: number = (<string>value).length;\n\nlet numberValue: any = 123;\nconsole.log((numberValue as string).length); // Error at runtime, even though the compiler did not complain",
  },
  1865: {
    source: "Что такое пересечение типов (intersection types) в TypeScript, и как оно используется?",
    question: "What are intersection types in TypeScript, and how are they used?",
    shortAnswer: "An intersection type in TypeScript lets you combine several types into one, and the result includes all the properties of all the types. This is especially useful when you need to combine different data types or describe objects that must have several sets of properties at the same time. An intersection is created with the `&` operator and lets you build complex data structures flexibly.",
    longAnswer: "Intersection types are a way of combining two or more types into one. Unlike union types, which let a variable be one of several types, an intersection requires the variable to satisfy all of the combined types at once.\n\nIntersection types are created with the & operator, and the resulting type includes every property defined in each type. This is useful when you need to create complex objects or functions that must satisfy several sets of requirements.\n\nConsider an example with an intersection type describing an object that must be both a person and an employee:\n\nHere the type `Worker` includes all the properties of the types `Person` and `Employee`. The object `worker` must contain both a name and an age (from `Person`) and an employee id and a position (from `Employee`).\n\nIt is important to understand that if the combined types contradict each other — for example, if the same property has different types — TypeScript will produce an error. Consider an example:\n\nIntersection types are useful when you need to describe complex objects that have to satisfy several requirements at once. This lets you create strictly typed data and avoid errors when using objects that contain many properties.",
    codeExample: "interface Person {\n  name: string;\n  age: number;\n}\ninterface Employee {\n  employeeId: number;\n  position: string;\n}\n// An intersection of the types Person and Employee\ntype Worker = Person & Employee;\nconst worker: Worker = {\n  name: \"John\",\n  age: 35,\n  employeeId: 12345,\n  position: \"Developer\"\n};\n\ninterface Cat {\n  name: string;\n  purrs: boolean;\n}\ninterface Dog {\n  name: string;\n  barks: boolean;\n  purrs: number; // A contradiction\n}\n// TypeScript will produce an error, because the types of the purrs property differ\ntype Pet = Cat & Dog;",
  },
  1866: {
    source: "Что такое union-типы в TypeScript и зачем они нужны?",
    question: "What are union types in TypeScript and what are they for?",
    shortAnswer: "Union types let you state that a variable may hold values of different types. This is useful in situations where the data type can be flexible and change.",
    longAnswer: "Union types in TypeScript let a variable have several possible types. This means a variable can be, for example, either a string or a number depending on the situation. It is convenient for handling different variants of data in a single function or variable. Such types are used with the `|` operator.\n\nHere the variable `value` can be either a string or a number, which lets you encode different usage scenarios.",
    codeExample: "let value: string | number;\nvalue = 'Hello'; // correct\nvalue = 42; // also correct",
  },
  1867: {
    source: "Что такое generics в TypeScript?",
    question: "What are generics in TypeScript?",
    shortAnswer: "Generics let you create components that work with different data types. This is useful for building more universal and flexible functions or classes.",
    longAnswer: "Generics let you create functions, classes or interfaces that can work with various data types while keeping strict typing. This is especially useful for developing universal components. Instead of specifying a concrete type, a type variable is used, and it is supplied at the moment the function or class is used.\n\nExample:\n\nIn this example the function `identity` works with any data type passed to it as an argument, which makes it very flexible.",
    codeExample: "function identity<T>(arg: T): T {\n  return arg;\n}\nlet output1 = identity<string>('Hello'); // T = string\nlet output2 = identity<number>(42); // T = number",
  },
  1868: {
    source: "Что такое readonly свойства в TypeScript?",
    question: "What are readonly properties in TypeScript?",
    shortAnswer: "The `readonly` keyword lets you make a property of an object or a variable immutable. This means that once a value has been assigned, it cannot be changed.",
    longAnswer: "In TypeScript the `readonly` keyword lets you declare properties of objects or arrays immutable after they have been initialized. This is useful when you need to protect data from accidental changes later on. Applying this approach improves data integrity and helps avoid errors.\n\nExample:\n\nHere the property `name` is immutable, whereas `age` can be changed.",
    codeExample: "interface Person {\n  readonly name: string;\n  age: number;\n}\nconst person: Person = { name: 'John', age: 30 };\nperson.age = 31; // correct\nperson.name = 'Jack'; // error: a readonly property cannot be changed",
  },
  1869: {
    source: "Как работает type guards в TypeScript?",
    question: "How do type guards work in TypeScript?",
    shortAnswer: "Type guards are constructs that let you check data types while the program is running. This matters when working with union types, where you need to know the exact data type at a particular moment.",
    longAnswer: "Type guards help TypeScript determine the exact type of a variable while the program is running. They are especially useful when working with union types, where a variable may be one of several types. Type guards let you perform a type check with the `typeof` and `instanceof` operators, as well as with custom functions.\n\nExample:\n\nThis example uses `typeof` to determine whether the variable is a string or a number and handle it accordingly.",
    codeExample: "function printValue(value: string | number) {\n  if (typeof value === 'string') {\n    console.log(`String: ${value}`);\n  } else {\n    console.log(`Number: ${value}`);\n  }\n}",
  },
  1870: {
    source: "Что такое enum в TypeScript и как его использовать?",
    question: "What is an enum in TypeScript and how do you use it?",
    shortAnswer: "An enum is a special construct in TypeScript that lets you declare a set of named constants. This is useful for grouping related values such as statuses or operating modes.",
    longAnswer: "An enum (enumeration) is a way of defining a set of constants to which numeric or string values can be assigned. It is useful when you need to work with a set of predefined values, for example order statuses or access levels. TypeScript supports both numeric and string enums.\n\nExample:\n\nIn this example the enum `Direction` defines four directions, starting from the numeric value 1.",
    codeExample: "enum Direction {\n  Up = 1,\n  Down,\n  Left,\n  Right,\n}\nlet dir: Direction = Direction.Up;",
  },
  1871: {
    source: "Как работает strictNullChecks в TypeScript?",
    question: "How does strictNullChecks work in TypeScript?",
    shortAnswer: "The `strictNullChecks` flag in TypeScript makes checking for `null` and `undefined` mandatory, preventing the implicit use of those values in your code. This helps avoid the typical errors associated with undefined values.",
    longAnswer: "When the `strictNullChecks` flag is enabled, TypeScript does not allow variables to hold `null` or `undefined` by default unless those are stated explicitly in the type. This means that all checks for the presence of those values become mandatory, which helps avoid errors where functions or variables unexpectedly turn out to be undefined. This mode makes code safer.\n\nExample:",
    codeExample: "let name: string | null = null; // correct\nname = 'John'; // correct\nname = undefined; // error, unless stated in the type",
  },
  1872: {
    source: "Как работают модули в TypeScript?",
    question: "How do modules work in TypeScript?",
    shortAnswer: "Modules in TypeScript let you split code into independent parts and reuse it. This helps structure a project and avoid name conflicts.",
    longAnswer: "Modules are the foundation for organizing code in large projects. They let you import and export functions, classes and variables between files, which makes code easier to maintain and scale. TypeScript uses two kinds of modules: ES modules (`import`/`export`) and CommonJS modules. ES modules are supported by most modern browsers and runtimes.\n\nExample:",
    codeExample: "// user.ts\nexport class User {\n  constructor(public name: string) {}\n}\n// main.ts\nimport { User } from './user';\nconst user = new User('John');",
  },
  1873: {
    source: "В чем плюсы и минусы TS.",
    question: "What are the pros and cons of TS?",
    shortAnswer: "TypeScript adds static typing, which improves code quality and reduces errors. It does, however, require compilation and has a more complex setup. It suits large projects and team development.",
    longAnswer: "TypeScript is a superset of JavaScript with static typing.\n\nPros:\n- Static typing: catching errors at compile time\n- Autocompletion: better IDE support\n- Refactoring: safe code changes\n- Documentation: types as code documentation\n- Compatibility: full compatibility with JavaScript\n\nCons:\n- Compilation: requires an extra build step\n- Learning curve: additional concepts to learn\n- Setup: more complex project configuration\n- Bundle size: larger code size after compilation\n\nWhen to use it:\n- For large and complex projects\n- In development teams\n- When code reliability and maintainability matter",
    codeExample: "// A TypeScript example with types\ninterface User {\n  id: number;\n  name: string;\n  email: string;\n}\n\nfunction createUser(user: User): User {\n  return {\n    id: user.id,\n    name: user.name,\n    email: user.email\n  };\n}",
  },
  1874: {
    source: "Обязательно ли компилировать TS? ts-node утилита",
    question: "Is compiling TS mandatory? The ts-node utility",
    shortAnswer: "TypeScript must be compiled to JavaScript in order to run. ts-node lets you run TypeScript code without compiling it first, which is convenient for development but not for production.",
    longAnswer: "Compiling TypeScript is necessary because Node.js and browsers execute JavaScript, not TypeScript.\n\nThe compilation process:\n- Writing TypeScript code (`.ts` files)\n- Compiling with `tsc` (the TypeScript Compiler)\n- Getting JavaScript code (`.js` files)\n- Executing the JavaScript code\n\nts-node is a utility that:\n- Compiles TypeScript in memory\n- Executes the resulting JavaScript code\n- Does not create `.js` files on disk\n- Is convenient for development and testing\n\nDifferences from production:\n- Development: use ts-node for a quick start\n- Production: compile to JavaScript and run the `.js` files",
    codeExample: "# Installing ts-node\nnpm install -g ts-node\n\n# Running a TypeScript file\nts-node index.ts",
  },
  1875: {
    source: "Что будет при выводе Boolean(-1), Boolean(~-1) ?",
    question: "What will Boolean(-1) and Boolean(~-1) print?",
    shortAnswer: "`Boolean(-1)` will return `true`, because any non-zero number converts to `true`. `Boolean(~-1)` will return `false`, because `~-1` equals `0`, and zero converts to `false`.",
    longAnswer: "Conversion to Boolean in JavaScript follows the rules of \"truthy\" and \"falsy\" values.\n\nThe rules for converting numbers:\n- `0` → `false`\n- Any other number (including negative ones) → `true`\n- `NaN` → `false`\n\nThe bitwise NOT operation (`~`):\n- Inverts the bits of a number\n- `~x` is equivalent to `-(x + 1)`\n\nThe calculations:\n\nThe full example:",
    codeExample: "// Boolean(-1)\n-1 !== 0 // true\nBoolean(-1) // true\n\n// Computing ~-1\n~-1 = -(-1 + 1) = -(0) = 0\n\n// Boolean(0)\n0 === 0 // true\nBoolean(0) // false\n\nconsole.log(Boolean(-1));   // true\nconsole.log(Boolean(~-1));  // false\nconsole.log(~-1);           // 0",
  },
  1876: {
    source: "Почему удобно использовать утилитные типы вместо создания новых интерфейсов?",
    question: "Why is it convenient to use utility types instead of creating new interfaces?",
    shortAnswer: "Utility types are convenient because they save time, reduce the amount of code and provide type safety without duplication. They let you create derived types from existing ones, keeping them automatically in sync when things change. This makes code more maintainable and less error-prone.",
    longAnswer: "Utility types in TypeScript provide a powerful way of manipulating types without creating redundant interfaces.\n\nThe advantages of utility types:\n\n1. DRY (Don't Repeat Yourself):\n\n2. Automatic synchronization:\n\n3. Flexibility and composition:\n\nPopular utility types:\n\nPartial — all properties optional:\n\nRequired — all properties required:\n\nPick — selecting specific properties:\n\nOmit — excluding properties:\n\nReadonly — read-only properties:\n\nA practical example:",
    codeExample: "// Instead of this:\ninterface User {\n  id: number;\n  name: string;\n  email: string;\n  createdAt: Date;\n}\n\ninterface UserUpdate {\n  id?: number;\n  name?: string;\n  email?: string;\n  createdAt?: Date;\n}\n\n// Use this:\ninterface User {\n  id: number;\n  name: string;\n  email: string;\n  createdAt: Date;\n}\n\ntype UserUpdate = Partial<User>;\n\ninterface Product {\n  id: number;\n  name: string;\n  price: number;\n  category: string;\n}\n\n// When Product changes, ProductPreview updates automatically\ntype ProductPreview = Pick<Product, 'id' | 'name' | 'price'>;\n\n// An example of use\nfunction displayProduct(preview: ProductPreview) {\n  console.log(`${preview.name}: $${preview.price}`);\n}\n\n// Combining utility types\ntype ApiResponse<T> = {\n  data: T;\n  status: number;\n  message: string;\n};\n\ntype UserApiResponse = ApiResponse<User>;\ntype ProductApiResponse = ApiResponse<Product>;\n\n// A more complex composition\ntype Editable<T> = Partial<T> & { id: number };\ntype UserEditable = Editable<User>;\n\ntype PartialUser = Partial<User>;\n// { id?: number; name?: string; ... }\n\ntype RequiredUser = Required<PartialUser>;\n// { id: number; name: string; ... }\n\ntype UserPreview = Pick<User, 'id' | 'name'>;\n// { id: number; name: string }\n\ntype UserWithoutId = Omit<User, 'id'>;\n// { name: string; email: string; ... }\n\ntype ReadonlyUser = Readonly<User>;\n// { readonly id: number; readonly name: string; ... }\n\n// Forms often need partial data\ntype UserForm = Partial<Pick<User, 'name' | 'email'>>;\n\n// For API responses — a specific set of fields\ntype UserApi = Pick<User, 'id' | 'name'> & {\n  profileUrl: string;\n};\n\n// For internal logic — excluding sensitive data\ntype SafeUser = Omit<User, 'password' | 'token'>;",
  },
  1877: {
    source: "Для чего нужен оператор keyof и typeof в ts? Что выведет keyof от typeof?",
    question: "What are the keyof and typeof operators for in TS? What does keyof of typeof produce?",
    shortAnswer: "`typeof` in TypeScript (in a type context) is used to obtain the type of a variable or constant. `keyof` is used to obtain a type representing all the keys (property names) of another type. The combination `keyof typeof` first obtains the type of a value with `typeof` and then extracts the type of its keys with `keyof`. This lets you create a union type out of the property names of an object defined as a constant.",
    longAnswer: "These operators are the foundation for building dynamic, strictly typed constructs in TypeScript.\n\ntypeof (Type Query Operator)\nIn TypeScript `typeof` can be used in two contexts:\n- In a value position: as in JavaScript, it returns a string with the name of the variable's type at runtime.\n- In a type position: it extracts the type of a variable declared with `const`, `let` or `function`.\n\n`keyof` (Index Type Query Operator)\n`keyof` takes an object type and produces a union type of its keys.\n\nThe keyof typeof combination\nThis combination is extremely useful when you have a constant object and want to create a type out of its keys.\n\nConclusion: the `keyof` and `typeof` operators (and especially their combination) are powerful tools for deriving types from existing JavaScript code, which lets you write safer, more maintainable TypeScript while avoiding duplication.",
    codeExample: "const person = { name: 'Alice', age: 30 };\n\n// `typeof person` in a type context extracts the object type { name: string; age: number; }\ntype PersonType = typeof person;\n// Equivalent to: type PersonType = { name: string; age: number; }\n\ntype Person = { name: string; age: number };\ntype PersonKeys = keyof Person; // Result: \"name\" | \"age\"\n\n// Suppose we have a configuration object\nconst AppConfig = {\n  theme: 'dark',\n  language: 'en',\n  version: '1.0.0'\n} as const; // `as const` makes the properties readonly literals\n\n// 1. `typeof AppConfig` gives the type: { readonly theme: \"dark\"; readonly language: \"en\"; readonly version: \"1.0.0\"; }\n// 2. `keyof typeof AppConfig` takes the keys of that type.\ntype ConfigKey = keyof typeof AppConfig; // Result: \"theme\" | \"language\" | \"version\"\n\n// Now we can use this type for a function\nfunction getConfig(key: ConfigKey) {\n  return AppConfig[key];\n}\n\ngetConfig('theme'); // OK\ngetConfig('invalidKey'); // TS error: the argument is not assignable to the type \"theme\" | \"language\" | \"version\"",
  },
  1878: {
    source: "Где могут быть полезны mapped types?",
    question: "Where can mapped types be useful?",
    shortAnswer: "Mapped types are useful when you need to create a new type from an old one by applying the same transformation to every property of the source type. They are a perfect fit for tasks that require making all properties optional (`Partial`) or read-only (`Readonly`), or selecting a subset of properties by name. They are a powerful tool for avoiding routine code duplication when defining similar types.",
    longAnswer: "Mapped types let you iterate over the keys of an existing type and build a new type from them.\n\nSyntax and basic examples:\nThe syntax is `{ [P in K]: T }`, where `P` is each key from the union type `K` and `T` is the type the property is transformed into.\n- Creating utility types: TypeScript's standard utilities are implemented with mapped types.\n\n- Changing property modifiers: you can remove or add the `readonly` and `?` modifiers at the same time.\n\n- Changing property types: you can do more than copy — you can transform the type of every property.\n\nConclusion: mapped types are a tool for metaprogramming inside TypeScript's type system. They are indispensable for creating families of related types, ensuring consistency and reducing boilerplate, especially when working with state, configuration and DTOs (Data Transfer Objects).",
    codeExample: "// Make all properties optional\ntype MyPartial<T> = {\n  [P in keyof T]?: T[P];\n};\n\n// Make all properties read-only\ntype MyReadonly<T> = {\n  readonly [P in keyof T]: T[P];\n};\n\ninterface User {\n  name: string;\n  age: number;\n}\n\ntype PartialUser = MyPartial<User>; // { name?: string; age?: number; }\ntype ReadonlyUser = MyReadonly<User>; // { readonly name: string; readonly age: number; }\n\n// Remove optionality and readonly from all properties\ntype Concrete<T> = {\n  -readonly [P in keyof T]-?: T[P];\n};\n\n// Create a type where every property is a function returning void\ntype EventHandlers<T> = {\n  [P in keyof T as `on${Capitalize<string & P>}`]: () => void;\n};\n// For the type { name: string; age: number } we get:\n// { onName: () => void; onAge: () => void; }",
  },
  1879: {
    source: "Что такое структурная типизация в TypeScript?",
    question: "What is structural typing in TypeScript?",
    shortAnswer: "Structural typing is an approach in which type compatibility is determined by structure (fields and methods) rather than by explicit declarations or names. TypeScript checks that an object has all the required properties with the right types, regardless of which type was declared for that object.",
    longAnswer: "Structural typing in TypeScript\nStructural typing is a foundational concept of TypeScript's type system, and it differs from the nominal typing used in languages such as Java or C#.\n\nThe core concept\nUnder structural typing, two types are considered compatible if they have the same structure:\n\nComparison with nominal typing\nStructural typing:\n- Checks the shape of objects\n- Types with the same structure are compatible\n- Used in TypeScript, Go\n\nNominal typing:\n- Checks explicit type declarations\n- Requires explicit inheritance or implementation\n- Used in Java, C#\n\nExamples of structural typing\n\n1. Object compatibility\n\n2. Function compatibility\n\nThe advantages of structural typing\n\n- Flexibility: does not require explicit type declarations\n- Decomposition: lets you combine types easily\n- Practicality: the focus is on what an object can do, not what it is\n\nPotential problems\n\nUse in practice\nStructural typing is useful when working with external libraries:\n\nConclusion: structural typing makes TypeScript a flexible and practical language, letting you work with objects based on their actual structure rather than on formal type declarations.",
    codeExample: "interface Person {\n  name: string;\n  age: number;\n}\n\n// This object is compatible with Person, even though it is not declared as Person\nconst user = { name: \"Ivan\", age: 30 };\nconst person: Person = user; // OK - the structure matches\n\ninterface Point {\n  x: number;\n  y: number;\n}\n\n// An object with extra properties is compatible too\nconst point3D = { x: 1, y: 2, z: 3 };\nconst point: Point = point3D; // OK\n\nfunction printPoint(p: Point) {\n  console.log(p.x, p.y);\n}\n\nprintPoint(point3D); // OK - the Point structure is present\n\ntype Adder = (a: number, b: number) => number;\n\n// A function is compatible if its parameters and return value are compatible\nconst multiply: Adder = (x: number, y: number) => x * y; // OK\n\ninterface User {\n  id: number;\n  name: string;\n}\n\n// Both objects are structurally compatible with User\nconst correctUser = { id: 1, name: \"Ivan\" };\nconst wrongUser = { id: \"1\", name: \"Ivan\" }; // Error - id must be a number\n\n// But this problem is caught at compile time\n\n// We can use objects that structurally match\n// the expected types without an explicit type cast\nfetch('/api/users')\n  .then(res => res.json())\n  .then((users: User[]) => {\n    // TypeScript will check the structure of the data received\n  });",
  },
  1880: {
    source: "Что произойдёт, если передать объект с такой же структурой, как требуемый тип, но с другим именем типа?",
    question: "What happens if you pass an object with the same structure as the required type but with a different type name?",
    shortAnswer: "The object will be accepted without errors, because TypeScript uses structural typing. The compiler checks only the object's structure (the presence of the needed fields with the right types), not the type's name. If the structures match, the types are considered compatible.",
    longAnswer: "Compatibility of types with the same structure\nIn TypeScript type names play no part in compatibility checking — only the structure matters.\n\nA basic example\n\nTypes with an identical structure\nEven when the types are declared completely separately:\n\nDifferent types with overlapping structure\n\nPractical scenarios\n\n1. Working with an API\n\n2. Functions with parameters\n\nRestrictions on direct assignment\nTypeScript is stricter when assigning object literals directly:\n\nConclusion: TypeScript ignores type names entirely when checking compatibility. Only the structure matters — the presence of all required fields with compatible types. This makes the type system flexible and practical.",
    codeExample: "interface Person {\n  name: string;\n  age: number;\n}\n\nclass Employee {\n  name: string;\n  age: number;\n  salary: number;\n}\n\n// Employee is compatible with Person, because it contains all the needed fields\nconst person: Person = new Employee(); // OK\n\nfunction greet(p: Person) {\n  console.log(`Hello, ${p.name}`);\n}\n\ngreet(new Employee()); // OK - the structure matches\n\ninterface Car {\n  brand: string;\n  year: number;\n}\n\ninterface Vehicle {\n  brand: string;\n  year: number;\n}\n\nconst car: Car = { brand: \"Toyota\", year: 2020 };\nconst vehicle: Vehicle = car; // OK - the structures are identical\n\nfunction processCar(c: Car) { /* ... */ }\nprocessCar(vehicle); // OK\n\ninterface Animal {\n  name: string;\n  age: number;\n}\n\ninterface Pet {\n  name: string;\n  age: number;\n  owner: string;\n}\n\nconst animal: Animal = { name: \"Barsik\", age: 2 };\nconst pet: Pet = { name: \"Sharik\", age: 3, owner: \"Ivan\" };\n\n// Pet is compatible with Animal (it has all the needed fields)\nconst animalFromPet: Animal = pet; // OK\n\n// Animal is not compatible with Pet (the owner field is missing)\n// const petFromAnimal: Pet = animal; // Error!\n\n// A type from our own system\ninterface User {\n  id: number;\n  name: string;\n  email: string;\n}\n\n// Data from an API may have a different structure but still be compatible\nconst apiResponse = {\n  id: 1,\n  name: \"Ivan\",\n  email: \"ivan@example.com\",\n  createdAt: \"2023-01-01\"\n};\n\nconst user: User = apiResponse; // OK - all the required fields are there\n\ninterface Config {\n  timeout: number;\n  retries: number;\n}\n\nfunction setupAPI(config: Config) {\n  // ...\n}\n\n// Passing an object with extra properties\nsetupAPI({\n  timeout: 5000,\n  retries: 3,\n  baseURL: \"/api\" // An extra property - OK\n});\n\ninterface Point {\n  x: number;\n  y: number;\n}\n\n// Excess properties are not allowed on direct assignment\n// const p: Point = { x: 1, y: 2, z: 3 }; // Error!\n\n// But through a variable - OK\nconst point3D = { x: 1, y: 2, z: 3 };\nconst p: Point = point3D; // OK",
  },
  1881: {
    source: "Зачем применяются дженерики?",
    question: "What are generics used for?",
    shortAnswer: "Generics are used to create reusable components that work with different data types while preserving type safety. They let you parameterize types so that the same code can work with various types without losing type information at compile time.",
    longAnswer: "Using generics in TypeScript\nGenerics provide a way of creating components that can work with various types rather than with one concrete type.\n\nThe core problem without generics\nWithout generics we are forced to use `any`, which means losing type safety:\n\nThe solution with generics\n\nThe main usage scenarios\n\n1. Universal functions\n\n2. Universal interfaces\n\n3. Universal classes\n\nGeneric constraints\nYou can constrain the types a generic is allowed to work with:\n\nAdvanced scenarios\n\n1. Default generics\n\n2. Several type parameters\n\nConclusion: generics are essential for creating reusable, type-safe code. They let you write flexible components that preserve type information and provide compile-time checking.",
    codeExample: "// The problem: the type information is lost\nfunction identity(value: any): any {\n  return value;\n}\n\nconst result = identity(42); // The type of result is any, not number\n\n// The solution: the type is preserved\nfunction identity<T>(value: T): T {\n  return value;\n}\n\nconst num = identity(42);        // Type: number\nconst str = identity(\"hello\");   // Type: string\nconst bool = identity(true);     // Type: boolean\n\nfunction getFirst<T>(array: T[]): T | undefined {\n  return array[0];\n}\n\nconst numbers = getFirst([1, 2, 3]);     // Type: number | undefined\nconst strings = getFirst([\"a\", \"b\", \"c\"]); // Type: string | undefined\n\ninterface Response<T> {\n  data: T;\n  status: number;\n  message: string;\n}\n\nconst userResponse: Response<User> = {\n  data: { id: 1, name: \"Ivan\" },\n  status: 200,\n  message: \"OK\"\n};\n\nconst productResponse: Response<Product> = {\n  data: { id: 1, title: \"Phone\", price: 1000 },\n  status: 200,\n  message: \"OK\"\n};\n\nclass Stack<T> {\n  private items: T[] = [];\n\n  push(item: T): void {\n    this.items.push(item);\n  }\n\n  pop(): T | undefined {\n    return this.items.pop();\n  }\n}\n\nconst numberStack = new Stack<number>();\nnumberStack.push(1);\nnumberStack.push(2);\nconst num = numberStack.pop(); // Type: number | undefined\n\nconst stringStack = new Stack<string>();\nstringStack.push(\"hello\");\n\ninterface HasLength {\n  length: number;\n}\n\nfunction logLength<T extends HasLength>(item: T): void {\n  console.log(item.length);\n}\n\nlogLength(\"a string\");  // OK - a string has length\nlogLength([1, 2, 3]);   // OK - an array has length\n// logLength(42);       // Error - a number has no length\n\ninterface Pagination<T = any> {\n  data: T[];\n  page: number;\n  total: number;\n}\n\n// You can state the type explicitly or fall back to any\nconst usersPagination: Pagination<User> = { /* ... */ };\nconst defaultPagination: Pagination = { /* ... */ }; // T = any\n\nfunction pair<K, V>(key: K, value: V): [K, V] {\n  return [key, value];\n}\n\nconst stringNumber = pair(\"age\", 30);    // Type: [string, number]\nconst numberBoolean = pair(1, true);     // Type: [number, boolean]",
  },
  1882: {
    source: "В чём разница между any и never?",
    question: "What is the difference between any and never?",
    shortAnswer: "`any` represents any possible type and switches off type checking, while `never` represents the type of values that never occur. `any` is a supertype of all types, whereas `never` is a subtype of all types. `any` means \"any type\", while `never` means \"no type at all\".",
    longAnswer: "The difference between any and never\nThese two types represent opposite concepts in TypeScript's type system.\n\nThe any type\n`any` switches off type checking completely:\n\nWhen to use any:\n\n- When migrating from JavaScript\n- When working with dynamic data\n- Temporarily, when the type is unknown\n\nThe never type\n`never` represents values that never occur:\n\nThe key differences\n\n1. Type compatibility\n\n2. In unions and intersections\n\nPractical use\n\n1. Guarding against unhandled cases\n\n2. Filtering types\n\n3. Error handling\n\nWhen to use which\nUse any:\n- For a gradual migration from JavaScript\n- When working with third-party libraries that have no types\n- Temporarily, when a type is hard to express\n\nUse never:\n- For functions that always throw\n- For exhaustiveness checks\n- In conditional types, for filtering\n\nConclusion: `any` switches off the type system and should be used as little as possible, whereas `never` strengthens type safety by describing impossible states and guaranteeing that checks are exhaustive.",
    codeExample: "let variable: any = \"a string\";\nvariable = 42;          // OK\nvariable = true;        // OK\nvariable = [1, 2, 3];   // OK\n\n// No type checks at all\nvariable.nonExistentMethod(); // Fails only at runtime\n\n// A function that always throws\nfunction throwError(message: string): never {\n  throw new Error(message);\n}\n\n// A function with an infinite loop\nfunction infiniteLoop(): never {\n  while (true) {\n    // ...\n  }\n}\n\n// An exhaustiveness check\ntype Shape = \"circle\" | \"square\";\n\nfunction getArea(shape: Shape): number {\n  switch (shape) {\n    case \"circle\": return Math.PI;\n    case \"square\": return 1;\n    default: \n      const exhaustiveCheck: never = shape;\n      return exhaustiveCheck;\n  }\n}\n\n// any is compatible with any type\nlet a: any;\nlet b: number = a;      // OK\nlet c: string = a;      // OK\n\n// never is compatible only with never\nlet n: never;\n// let x: number = n;   // Error!\n// let y: string = n;   // Error!\nlet z: never = n;       // OK\n\n// In unions\ntype T1 = string | any;     // any\ntype T2 = string | never;   // string\n\n// In intersections  \ntype T3 = string & any;     // any\ntype T4 = string & never;   // never\n\ntype Action = \"CREATE\" | \"UPDATE\" | \"DELETE\";\n\nfunction handleAction(action: Action) {\n  switch (action) {\n    case \"CREATE\": /* ... */ break;\n    case \"UPDATE\": /* ... */ break;\n    // case \"DELETE\": we forgot to handle this one\n    default:\n      const unexpected: never = action;\n      throw new Error(`Unhandled action: ${unexpected}`);\n  }\n}\n\n// Removes null and undefined from a type\ntype NonNullable<T> = T extends null | undefined ? never : T;\n\ntype A = NonNullable<string | null>;        // string\ntype B = NonNullable<number | undefined>;   // number\ntype C = NonNullable<boolean | null | undefined>; // boolean\n\nfunction processData(data: string): string | never {\n  if (!data) {\n    throw new Error(\"The data is required\");\n  }\n  return data.toUpperCase();\n}\n\nconst result = processData(\"\"); // Throws an error - the type is never",
  },
  1883: {
    source: "Можно ли создать собственный тип ошибки (custom error type)? Как?",
    question: "Can you create your own custom error type? How?",
    shortAnswer: "Yes, you can create your own error type. To do so you create a class that inherits from the built-in `Error` class and add the properties and methods you need. In the constructor you must call `super()` and set `Object.setPrototypeOf(this, CustomError.prototype)` so that `instanceof` works correctly.",
    longAnswer: "Creating custom error types\nYour own error types let you create specialized exceptions that carry additional information.\n\nA basic custom error class\n\nSpecialized errors with additional properties\n\nUsing custom errors\n\nHandling custom errors\n\nAn error hierarchy\nYou can build complex error hierarchies:\n\nPractical recommendations\n\n1. Always set the prototype\n\n2. Use the right name\n\n3. Add an error code\n\nConclusion: creating custom error types lets you structure exception handling, add contextual information and build a clear error hierarchy for better error management in your application.",
    codeExample: "class CustomError extends Error {\n  constructor(message: string) {\n    super(message);\n    \n    // Important for instanceof to work correctly\n    Object.setPrototypeOf(this, CustomError.prototype);\n    \n    // Preserve the call stack\n    if (Error.captureStackTrace) {\n      Error.captureStackTrace(this, CustomError);\n    }\n    \n    this.name = \"CustomError\";\n  }\n}\n\nclass ValidationError extends Error {\n  public readonly field: string;\n  public readonly value: any;\n\n  constructor(field: string, value: any, message?: string) {\n    super(message || `Validation error on the field ${field}`);\n    \n    this.name = \"ValidationError\";\n    this.field = field;\n    this.value = value;\n    \n    Object.setPrototypeOf(this, ValidationError.prototype);\n  }\n}\n\nclass NetworkError extends Error {\n  public readonly statusCode: number;\n  public readonly url: string;\n\n  constructor(statusCode: number, url: string, message?: string) {\n    super(message || `Network error ${statusCode} for ${url}`);\n    \n    this.name = \"NetworkError\";\n    this.statusCode = statusCode;\n    this.url = url;\n    \n    Object.setPrototypeOf(this, NetworkError.prototype);\n  }\n}\n\nfunction validateUser(user: any): void {\n  if (!user.name) {\n    throw new ValidationError(\"name\", user.name, \"The name is required\");\n  }\n  \n  if (user.age < 0) {\n    throw new ValidationError(\"age\", user.age, \"The age cannot be negative\");\n  }\n}\n\nfunction fetchUser(id: number): Promise<User> {\n  return fetch(`/api/users/${id}`)\n    .then(response => {\n      if (!response.ok) {\n        throw new NetworkError(response.status, `/api/users/${id}`);\n      }\n      return response.json();\n    });\n}\n\ntry {\n  validateUser({ age: -1 });\n} catch (error) {\n  if (error instanceof ValidationError) {\n    console.error(`Error on the field ${error.field}:`, error.message);\n    // Error on the field age: The age cannot be negative\n  } else {\n    console.error(\"Unknown error:\", error);\n  }\n}\n\nabstract class AppError extends Error {\n  public abstract readonly code: string;\n  \n  constructor(message: string) {\n    super(message);\n    this.name = this.constructor.name;\n    Object.setPrototypeOf(this, new.target.prototype);\n  }\n}\n\nclass DatabaseError extends AppError {\n  public readonly code = \"DB_ERROR\";\n  public readonly query: string;\n\n  constructor(query: string, message: string) {\n    super(message);\n    this.query = query;\n  }\n}\n\nclass UniqueConstraintError extends DatabaseError {\n  public readonly code = \"UNIQUE_CONSTRAINT\";\n  public readonly constraint: string;\n\n  constructor(constraint: string, query: string) {\n    super(query, `Unique constraint violated: ${constraint}`);\n    this.constraint = constraint;\n  }\n}\n\n// Without this instanceof will not work correctly\nObject.setPrototypeOf(this, CustomError.prototype);\n\nthis.name = this.constructor.name; // Sets the name automatically\n\nclass BusinessError extends Error {\n  public readonly code: string;\n  \n  constructor(code: string, message: string) {\n    super(message);\n    this.code = code;\n    this.name = \"BusinessError\";\n    Object.setPrototypeOf(this, BusinessError.prototype);\n  }\n}",
  },
  1884: {
    source: "Как работает TypeScript в браузере?",
    question: "How does TypeScript work in the browser?",
    shortAnswer: "TypeScript cannot run directly in the browser, because browsers understand only JavaScript. TypeScript is compiled (transformed) into ordinary JavaScript by a special tool — the TypeScript compiler. This process removes all TypeScript types and constructs, leaving pure JavaScript code. The resulting JavaScript can then be executed in any browser, just like ordinary JavaScript code.",
    longAnswer: "TypeScript is a superset of JavaScript that adds static typing and other features, but browsers can only execute JavaScript code. That is why TypeScript has to be transformed into JavaScript before it is used in the browser.\n\nHow TypeScript works in the browser\n\n1. Compiling TypeScript to JavaScript\nTypeScript code goes through the TypeScript compiler (tsc), which:\n- Checks types for errors\n- Removes type annotations and TypeScript-specific constructs\n- Generates equivalent JavaScript code\n\nAn example of TypeScript code:\n\nAfter compilation we get JavaScript:\n\n2. Compilation tools\nThe TypeScript Compiler (tsc)\n- The official compiler from Microsoft\n- Can be run from the command line or as part of a bundler\n\nBabel with TypeScript plugins\n- An alternative way of transpiling\n- Often used in modern builds\n\n3. Configuring compilation\nThe `tsconfig.json` file controls the compilation process:\n\n4. Integration into the development process\nDevelopment mode:\n- Hot reload with automatic recompilation\n- Source maps for debugging\n\nProduction mode:\n- Minification and optimization\n- Removal of comments and unnecessary whitespace\n\nA practical workflow example\n\nAfter compilation into `app.js`:\n\nKey aspects\n\nThe advantages of compilation\n\n- Catching errors at compile time — typical errors are found before the code runs in the browser\n- Compatibility with older browsers — compiling down to older ES versions\n- Optimization — removing unused code and types\n\nLimitations\n\n- Compilation time — an extra step in the development process\n- Build size — types are removed, but the compiler may add polyfills\n\nConclusion\nTypeScript should be used when you need static typing and early error detection, but it is important to remember that the browser only receives JavaScript code after compilation. For small projects, or when maximum client-side performance matters, using plain JavaScript may be worth considering.",
    codeExample: "function greet(name: string): string {\n    return `Hello, ${name}!`;\n}\n\nfunction greet(name) {\n    return `Hello, ${name}!`;\n}\n\n{\n  \"compilerOptions\": {\n    \"target\": \"ES5\",\n    \"module\": \"ES6\",\n    \"outDir\": \"./dist\",\n    \"strict\": true\n  }\n}\n\n// The original TypeScript (app.ts)\ninterface User {\n  id: number;\n  name: string;\n}\n\nfunction getUserInfo(user: User): string {\n  return `User ${user.name} has ID: ${user.id}`;\n}\n\nfunction getUserInfo(user) {\n  return \"User \" + user.name + \" has ID: \" + user.id;\n}",
  },
  1885: {
    source: "Что такое TypeScript и зачем он используется во frontend-разработке?",
    question: "What is TypeScript and why is it used in frontend development?",
    shortAnswer: "TypeScript is a layer on top of JavaScript with support for static typing. It lets you find errors while you are still developing. TypeScript improves the readability and maintainability of code. It is especially useful in large projects. TypeScript code is compiled to ordinary JavaScript.",
    longAnswer: "TypeScript is a programming language that extends JavaScript with a type system.\n\nWhy TypeScript is needed\nTypeScript solves several key problems:\n- it reduces the number of runtime errors\n- it makes code more predictable\n- it improves autocompletion and navigation in the IDE\n\nHow TypeScript is used in frontend work\nIn frontend development TypeScript:\n- describes component props\n- types state and API responses\n- makes refactoring safer\n\nAn example of simple typing:\n\nWhen TypeScript is especially useful\n\n- large teams\n- long-lived projects\n- complex business logic\n- active refactoring\n\nLimitations\n\n- it takes time to learn\n- it increases the initial setup\n- types do not exist at runtime\n\nConclusion: TypeScript increases the reliability and scalability of frontend code, especially in large projects.",
    codeExample: "type User = {\n  id: number\n  name: string\n}",
  },
  1886: {
    source: "В каких случаях удобнее использовать interface, а в каких type?",
    question: "When is it more convenient to use interface, and when type?",
    shortAnswer: "`interface` is more convenient for describing objects and public contracts. `type` is more universal and suits unions, primitives and complex types. `interface` is easy to extend, `type` is easy to combine. In most cases the choice depends on the task and the project's style. The two tools are often used together.",
    longAnswer: "`interface` and `type` solve similar problems but have different strengths.\n\nWhen interface is more convenient\n`interface` is a better fit:\n- for describing objects\n- for public APIs\n- for component contracts\n\nIts characteristics:\n- it supports extension\n- it reads well\n- it is often used for React props\n\nWhen type is more convenient\n`type` is more universal and is used:\n- for union types\n- for primitives\n- for complex combinations\n\nThe key practical difference\n\n- `interface` — for structure\n- `type` — for composition\n\nHow it is usually done in practice\nIn many projects:\n- `interface` is used for objects\n- `type` — for everything else\n\nConclusion: `interface` and `type` do not compete but complement each other — it is important to understand what each is for and to use them deliberately.",
    codeExample: "interface User {\n  id: number\n  name: string\n}\n\ntype Status = 'loading' | 'success' | 'error'",
  },
  1887: {
    source: "Можно ли расширять interface и type, и в чём разница?",
    question: "Can interface and type be extended, and what is the difference?",
    shortAnswer: "`interface` can be extended with `extends` and with declaration merging. `type` is extended through intersections (`&`). `interface` is a better fit for public contracts and APIs. `type` is more flexible but does not support automatic merging of declarations. These differences matter when designing types.",
    longAnswer: "In TypeScript both `interface` and `type` can be extended, but the mechanisms and the consequences differ.\n\nExtending an interface\n`interface` supports:\n- `extends`\n- declaration merging\n\nAs a result `User` will contain both fields.\n\nThe advantages:\n- contracts are convenient to extend\n- it suits libraries\n- it scales well\n\nExtending a type\n`type` is extended through intersection types:\n\nIts characteristics:\n- there is no declaration merging\n- the composition is more explicit\n- it is better for complex types\n\nThe key differences\n\n- `interface` can be declared several times\n- `type` cannot\n- `type` supports unions (`|`)\n- `interface` does not\n\nThe practical choice\n\n- public contracts → `interface`\n- type composition → `type`\n\nConclusion: `interface` is better for extensible contracts, `type` for complex type logic and composition.",
    codeExample: "interface User {\n  id: number\n}\n\ninterface User {\n  name: string\n}\n\ntype User = {\n  id: number\n}\n\ntype ExtendedUser = User & {\n  name: string\n}",
  },
  1888: {
    source: "Что такое предикаты типов в TypeScript?",
    question: "What are type predicates in TypeScript?",
    shortAnswer: "Type predicates are functions that help TypeScript refine the type of a value. They return a `boolean` but have a special signature. Such functions are used in conditions. After the check TypeScript \"understands\" which type we are working with.",
    longAnswer: "TypeScript can narrow types automatically, but sometimes it needs a hint from the developer.\n\nDefinition\nA type predicate is a function with a return type of the form `value is Type`, which tells TypeScript that a value belongs to a particular type.\n\nAn example of a predicate\nConsider a check for a custom type:\n\nHow it works\nAfter such a function is used:\n- TypeScript trusts the result of the check\n- the variable's type is narrowed inside the block\n\nWhy this is needed\nType predicates are useful when you:\n- work with `unknown`\n- use union types\n- write reusable checks\n- validate data from an API\n\nThe difference from ordinary checks\nAn ordinary function:\n- returns a `boolean`\n- does not affect the type\n\nA type predicate:\n- returns a `boolean`\n- affects the type system\n\nA short conclusion\nType predicates let you control type narrowing manually and make code safer and more expressive. They are a powerful TypeScript tool for complex checks.",
    codeExample: "function isString(value: unknown): value is string {\n  return typeof value === 'string';\n}\n\nif (isString(data)) {\n  // data has the type string\n  data.toUpperCase();\n}",
  },
  1889: {
    source: "Какую типизацию имеет JavaScript и какую добавляет TypeScript?",
    question: "What kind of typing does JavaScript have, and what does TypeScript add?",
    shortAnswer: "JavaScript has dynamic, weak typing. Types are determined at runtime. TypeScript adds static typing at development time. This lets you find errors before the code runs. At runtime, meanwhile, it is still ordinary JavaScript.",
    longAnswer: "JavaScript and TypeScript differ not in their execution syntax but in their model of working with types.\n\nTyping in JavaScript\nJavaScript:\n- is dynamically typed\n- the type of a value can change\n- checks happen at runtime\n\nExample:\n\nType errors only show up when the code runs.\n\nWhat TypeScript adds\nTypeScript introduces:\n- static typing\n- type checking at compile time\n- type annotations\n- type inference\n\nExample:\n\nAn important point\nTypeScript:\n- does not change JavaScript's behaviour\n- does not exist at runtime\n- compiles to ordinary JavaScript\n\nAll types are removed during the build.\n\nThe practical benefit\nTypeScript:\n- reduces the number of bugs\n- improves autocompletion\n- makes code easier to maintain\n- makes refactoring safer\n\nConclusion\nJavaScript has dynamic typing, while TypeScript adds static type checking at development time without affecting how the code runs.",
    codeExample: "let value = 10;\nvalue = \"text\";\n\nlet value: number = 10;\n// value = \"text\"; // an error at development time",
  },
  1890: {
    source: "В чём основные преимущества TypeScript на больших проектах?",
    question: "What are the main advantages of TypeScript on large projects?",
    shortAnswer: "TypeScript helps manage the complexity of code through strict typing. It lets you find errors at development time rather than at runtime. Types improve the readability and self-documenting quality of code. Refactoring becomes safer. This matters especially when many developers work on a project.",
    longAnswer: "On large projects the main problem is not writing the code but maintaining it.\n\nControlling complexity\nTypeScript helps you:\n- describe the contracts between modules explicitly\n- restrict the allowed states of your data\n- reduce the number of implicit assumptions\n\nTypes become a form of documentation that is always up to date.\n\nEarly error detection\nErrors such as:\n- wrong arguments\n- missing fields\n- incorrect return values\n\nare caught:\n- in the IDE\n- during the build\n- before the application runs\n\nSafe refactoring\nWhen code changes, TypeScript:\n- highlights every place it is used\n- does not let you \"break\" a contract unnoticed\n- makes renaming and restructuring easier\n\nThis is critical in a codebase with thousands of files.\n\nBetter teamwork\nTypeScript:\n- sets common rules\n- reduces dependence on verbal agreements\n- makes onboarding new developers easier\n\nConclusion\nTypeScript is especially useful on large projects because it reduces complexity, makes code predictable and guards against errors as the system evolves.",
    codeExample: null,
  },
  1891: {
    source: "Какие недостатки TypeScript ты видишь на практике?",
    question: "What drawbacks of TypeScript do you see in practice?",
    shortAnswer: "TypeScript raises the barrier to entry and takes time to learn. It adds overhead when writing code. Sometimes types become too complex. There can also be discrepancies between the types and the real runtime behaviour. On small projects its benefit may not be obvious.",
    longAnswer: "Despite its advantages, TypeScript is not a free solution.\n\nIncreased complexity\nIn practice these problems arise:\n- complex generics\n- overloaded types\n- compiler errors that are hard to read\n\nThis can slow development down, especially for newcomers.\n\nDevelopment overhead\nTypeScript requires you to:\n- write types\n- keep them up to date\n- configure the setup (`tsconfig`)\n\nIn simple scenarios this looks excessive.\n\nA false sense of security\nIt is important to understand:\n- TypeScript does not check data at runtime\n- types may not match the real data\n- an API may return an unexpected result\n\nWithout runtime validation, errors are still possible.\n\nInfrastructure costs\nTypeScript:\n- increases build time\n- complicates tooling setup\n- adds a compilation step\n\nConclusion\nTypeScript has real drawbacks: complexity, overhead and the absence of runtime checks. Its value is not always realized and depends on the scale of the project.",
    codeExample: null,
  },
  1892: {
    source: "Имеет ли TypeScript собственную среду выполнения?",
    question: "Does TypeScript have its own runtime?",
    shortAnswer: "No, TypeScript does not have its own runtime. It is not executed directly. TypeScript is compiled to JavaScript, which is then executed in the browser or in Node.js. At runtime TypeScript disappears completely. All types are removed during compilation.",
    longAnswer: "TypeScript is often thought of as a separate language, but from the point of view of execution that is not the case.\n\nHow TypeScript works\nThe process looks like this:\n- The developer writes code in TypeScript\n- The TypeScript compiler checks the types\n- The code is compiled to JavaScript\n- Ordinary JavaScript is executed\n\nWhat happens to the types\nTypes:\n- exist only at development time\n- are used for checking and autocompletion\n- are removed completely during compilation\n\nExample:\n\nAfter compilation:\n\nWhy this matters\nIt follows that:\n- TypeScript cannot catch runtime errors\n- types cannot be checked while the code runs\n- validating data requires additional tools\n\nConclusion\nTypeScript has no runtime of its own and does not operate at runtime. It is a layer on top of JavaScript and exists only at development time.",
    codeExample: "function sum(a: number, b: number): number {\n  return a + b;\n}\n\nfunction sum(a, b) {\n  return a + b;\n}",
  },
  1893: {
    source: "Что такое any и почему его стараются избегать?",
    question: "What is any and why do people try to avoid it?",
    shortAnswer: "`any` switches off type checking for a value. TypeScript stops analysing which operations are allowed. This makes the code behave like ordinary JavaScript. Errors are no longer caught at development time. That is why people try to avoid `any` and use it only as a last resort.",
    longAnswer: "The `any` type is a deliberate \"emergency exit\" from TypeScript's type system.\n\nDefinition\nany is a type with which TypeScript permits any operation on a value without any checks at all.\n\nExample:\n\nTypeScript will not report errors even when the code is incorrect.\n\nWhy `any` is dangerous\nUsing `any`:\n- switches off type checking\n- breaks autocompletion\n- makes refactoring unsafe\n- hides real errors\n\nIn effect, a piece of code with `any` drops out of the type system.\n\nWhen `any` is used after all\n`any` may be justified:\n- when migrating from JavaScript\n- when working with third-party libraries that have no types\n- in prototypes and experiments\n\nEven in these cases people try to isolate it.\n\nAlternatives\nInstead of `any` people more often use:\n- `unknown`\n- strict union types\n- generics\n\nConclusion\n`any` kills TypeScript's advantages, so it is avoided. The less `any` there is in the code, the more reliable and predictable the type system becomes.",
    codeExample: "let value: any = 10;\nvalue.foo();\nvalue();\nvalue = \"text\";",
  },
  1894: {
    source: "Почему интерфейсы чаще рекомендуют использовать по умолчанию?",
    question: "Why are interfaces more often recommended as the default?",
    shortAnswer: "Interfaces are convenient for describing the shape of objects and they scale well. They support extension and declaration merging. This makes them handy for public contracts and libraries. In most cases interfaces read better. That is why they are often recommended as the default.",
    longAnswer: "TypeScript has two main ways of describing the structure of objects — `interface` and `type`.\n\nDefinition\nAn interface is a way of describing an object's contract, defining its shape and the fields it offers.\n\nThe practical advantages of interfaces\nInterfaces:\n- suit object structures better\n- are easy to extend with `extends`\n- support declaration merging\n- are convenient for public APIs\n\nAn example of extension:\n\nDeclaration merging\nIf an interface is declared several times:\n- TypeScript merges the declarations\n- this is useful for extending types that come from libraries\n\nWhen `type` is better\n`type` is preferable if:\n- you need union types\n- you need primitives\n- complex type composition is involved\n\nConclusion\nInterfaces are recommended as the default for describing objects and contracts. They scale better and are easier to maintain.",
    codeExample: "interface User {\n  id: number;\n}\n\ninterface Admin extends User {\n  role: string;\n}\n\ninterface Window {\n  customProp: string;\n}",
  },
  1895: {
    source: "Что произойдёт, если объявить интерфейс с одинаковым именем дважды?",
    question: "What happens if you declare an interface with the same name twice?",
    shortAnswer: "If you declare an interface with the same name twice, TypeScript merges them. All the fields from both declarations end up in the resulting interface. This is called declaration merging. This behaviour is specific to interfaces. It is not available for `type`.",
    longAnswer: "One of the unique features of interfaces is declaration merging.\n\nDefinition\nDeclaration merging is a mechanism by which several interface declarations with the same name are combined into one.\n\nExample:\n\nThe result:\n\nWhere this is used\nInterface merging is applied:\n- in types for browser APIs\n- when extending global objects\n- in the declarations of third-party libraries\n\nLimitations\nIt is important to remember:\n- the fields must not conflict in their types\n- `type` does not allow this\n- overuse hurts readability\n\nPossible risks\nUnintentional merging:\n- can complicate debugging\n- can make types non-obvious\n- can lead to hidden errors\n\nConclusion\nWhen an interface is declared again, TypeScript merges the declarations. This is a powerful but potentially dangerous mechanism that is worth using deliberately.",
    codeExample: "interface User {\n  id: number;\n}\n\ninterface User {\n  name: string;\n}\n\ninterface User {\n  id: number;\n  name: string;\n}",
  },
  1896: {
    source: "Как типизировать объект с разным набором полей у сущностей?",
    question: "How do you type an object whose entities have different sets of fields?",
    shortAnswer: "For objects with different sets of fields, union types are normally used. Each variant is described by its own type or interface. TypeScript can narrow types based on checks. This lets you work safely with different data structures. The approach makes code clear and protected from errors.",
    longAnswer: "In practice you often meet entities that share some fields but differ in the details.\n\nThe basic approach\nThe most common way is union types:\n\nHere:\n- `User` can be of different kinds\n- the set of fields depends on the role\n\nType narrowing\nTypeScript can determine the specific variant automatically:\n\nThis is called type narrowing.\n\nShared fields\nShared properties are often factored out:\n\nAlternatives\nDepending on the task you can use:\n- optional fields (worse for strict typing)\n- generics\n- mapped types\n\nConclusion\nUnion types let you describe objects with different sets of fields precisely. They are the main tool for modelling complex domain entities in TypeScript.",
    codeExample: "type User = Admin | Guest;\n\ninterface Admin {\n  role: \"admin\";\n  permissions: string[];\n}\n\ninterface Guest {\n  role: \"guest\";\n  expiresAt: Date;\n}\n\nfunction processUser(user: User) {\n  if (user.role === \"admin\") {\n    user.permissions; // available\n  }\n}\n\ninterface BaseUser {\n  id: number;\n}\n\ninterface Admin extends BaseUser {\n  role: \"admin\";\n}\n\ninterface Guest extends BaseUser {\n  role: \"guest\";\n}",
  },
  1897: {
    source: "Как бы ты описал общий тип для разных вариантов пользователя?",
    question: "How would you describe a common type for different variants of a user?",
    shortAnswer: "A common user type is conveniently described with a discriminated union. To do this you introduce a shared discriminator field. From it TypeScript understands which variant of the type the code is working with. This approach is safe and scales well. It is often used in real projects.",
    longAnswer: "When an entity has several clearly defined variants, a discriminated union is used.\n\nDefinition\nA discriminated union is a union type in which every variant has a shared field with a literal value.\n\nAn example of the description\n\nWhy this is convenient\nThis approach:\n- makes type narrowing easier\n- rules out invalid fields\n- improves readability\n- scales well\n\nAn example of use:\n\nWhy not optional fields\nOptional fields:\n- complicate the checks\n- allow invalid states\n- make the model less strict\n\nConclusion\nA discriminated union is the best way to describe a common type for the different variants of an entity. It makes the data model strict, readable and safe.",
    codeExample: "interface BaseUser {\n  id: number;\n  type: \"admin\" | \"user\";\n}\n\ninterface AdminUser extends BaseUser {\n  type: \"admin\";\n  permissions: string[];\n}\n\ninterface RegularUser extends BaseUser {\n  type: \"user\";\n  email: string;\n}\n\ntype User = AdminUser | RegularUser;\n\nfunction getUserInfo(user: User) {\n  if (user.type === \"admin\") {\n    user.permissions;\n  }\n}",
  },
  1898: {
    source: "JS или TypeScript — что выбрать и почему?",
    question: "JS or TypeScript — which should you choose and why?",
    shortAnswer: "TypeScript adds static typing on top of JavaScript and helps you find errors earlier. It improves the readability and maintainability of code, especially in large projects. JavaScript is simpler to start with but scales worse. Most production projects choose TypeScript. JavaScript remains appropriate for simple tasks.",
    longAnswer: "Choosing between JavaScript and TypeScript is a choice between speed of starting and durability of the project.\n\nWhat JavaScript gives you\nJavaScript:\n- is simple to use\n- requires no build step\n- suits small scripts and prototypes\n\nThe downsides:\n- errors are only found at runtime\n- a large codebase is harder to maintain\n\nWhat TypeScript adds\nTypeScript is a layer on top of JavaScript that:\n- introduces types\n- checks code at development time\n- improves autocompletion and navigation\n\nWhy TypeScript wins more often\n\n- fewer runtime errors\n- code is easier to read and maintain\n- it is easier to work as a team\n- refactoring is safer\n\nWhen JavaScript is still appropriate\n\n- small projects\n- quick prototypes\n- scripts without long-term maintenance\n\nConclusion\nTypeScript is almost always justified in long-lived projects. JavaScript is good for quick, simple solutions.",
    codeExample: "function sum(a: number, b: number): number {\n  return a + b\n}",
  },
  1899: {
    source: "Какие типы ошибок позволяет отлавливать TypeScript?",
    question: "What kinds of errors does TypeScript let you catch?",
    shortAnswer: "TypeScript catches type errors at compile time. It prevents the incorrect use of variables, functions and objects. It also helps avoid `undefined` and `null` errors. Many runtime errors turn into compile-time ones. This reduces the number of bugs in production.",
    longAnswer: "TypeScript is valuable because it detects errors before the code runs.\n\nThe main kinds of errors\nTypeScript helps uncover several classes of problem.\n- Type errors\n- passing a value of the wrong type\n- an incorrect argument type\n- a mismatched return value\n\n- Errors in object structure\n- missing required fields\n- typos in property names\n- the wrong shape of data\n\n- Problems with `null` and `undefined`\n- accessing fields without a check\n- forgotten optional fields\n\n- API contract errors\n- a mismatch with the server's response\n- working with a DTO incorrectly\n\nWhat TypeScript does not catch\n\n- logical errors\n- business-logic problems\n- errors that depend on data at runtime\n\nConclusion\nTypeScript does not replace tests, but it significantly reduces one class of technical errors. It is especially useful in large, actively evolving codebases.",
    codeExample: "type User = { id: number; name: string }\n\nconst user: User = { id: 1 } // error",
  },
  1900: {
    source: "Почему TypeScript улучшает читаемость и поддержку кода?",
    question: "Why does TypeScript improve the readability and maintainability of code?",
    shortAnswer: "TypeScript makes code clearer through explicit types and contracts. From the types you can see at once what data is expected and what a function returns. This reduces the need to read the implementation in order to understand the behaviour. The code becomes self-documenting. Maintenance and refactoring get easier.",
    longAnswer: "The maintainability of code depends directly on how easy it is to read and change.\n\nHow TypeScript improves readability\nTypeScript adds explicit expectations to the code:\n- argument types\n- return value types\n- the structure of objects\n\nThis lets you understand code without digging into the implementation.\n\nEven without the body of a function it is clear:\n- what is required as input\n- what will come out\n\nThe effect on maintenance and evolution\nTypeScript is especially useful when:\n- Working as a team\n- new developers get up to speed faster\n- there are fewer verbal agreements\n\n- Refactoring\n- the IDE shows broken places immediately\n- there are fewer hidden side effects\n\n- Evolving an API\n- contract changes are visible at once\n- it is harder to break code \"quietly\"\n\nTypeScript as documentation\nTypes effectively act as:\n- living documentation\n- a checkable contract\n- a source of autocompletion\n\nConclusion\nTypeScript makes code more explicit and predictable. This is especially valuable in large projects with a long life cycle.",
    codeExample: "function fetchUser(id: number): Promise<User> {\n  // the implementation is hidden\n}",
  },
  1901: {
    source: "В каких случаях разница между JS и TS минимальна?",
    question: "In which cases is the difference between JS and TS minimal?",
    shortAnswer: "The difference between JavaScript and TypeScript is minimal on small, simple projects. If the codebase is small, rarely changes and is written by a single developer, typing offers few advantages. TypeScript also barely helps in code without complex data structures. In such cases it can be excessive. It all depends on the scale and life span of the project.",
    longAnswer: "TypeScript is not a mandatory choice for every project.\n\nWhen TypeScript gives the least benefit\nThe difference between JS and TS is barely noticeable if:\n- The project is small\n- a handful of files\n- limited logic\n- a short life cycle\n\n- There are no complex data types\n- simple objects\n- minimal nesting\n- no complex API contracts\n\n- The code rarely changes\n- there is no active development\n- there is no frequent refactoring\n\nThe additional costs\nEven when the benefit is minimal, TypeScript still requires:\n- setting up the build\n- configuring types\n- maintaining the type descriptions\n\nWhen JS wins\n\n- quick prototypes\n- experiments\n- one-off scripts\n\nConclusion\nTypeScript is most effective where there is complexity and growth. In simple scenarios JavaScript remains a rational choice.",
    codeExample: null,
  },
  1902: {
    source: "Как типизировать функцию, которая получает значение объекта по ключу?",
    question: "How do you type a function that gets a value from an object by key?",
    shortAnswer: "The function is typed with generic parameters and `keyof`. The key is constrained to the object's keys, and the type of the return value depends on the key that was passed. This lets TypeScript infer the type precisely and prevents access to non-existent properties. The approach makes code safe and self-documenting.",
    longAnswer: "The task is to tie the type of the key to the type of the object's value.\n\nDefinition\n\n- `keyof T` — the union of all the keys of the object `T`.\n- `T[K]` — the type of the value at the key `K`.\n\nBasic typing of the function\n\nAn example of use\n\nWhat this approach gives you\n\n- You cannot pass a non-existent key.\n- The type of the result depends on the key.\n- Autocompletion works in the IDE.\n\nWhy not just string\n\nThe downsides:\n- There is no checking of keys.\n- Types are lost.\n- Runtime errors become possible.\n\nConclusion\nUsing `keyof` together with generic parameters is the standard way of accessing an object's properties type-safely in TypeScript.",
    codeExample: "function getValue<T, K extends keyof T>(obj: T, key: K): T[K] {\n  return obj[key];\n}\n\nconst user = {\n  id: 1,\n  name: \"Alex\",\n  active: true,\n};\n\nconst name = getValue(user, \"name\");     // string\nconst active = getValue(user, \"active\"); // boolean\n\nfunction bad(obj: any, key: string) {\n  return obj[key];\n}",
  },
  1903: {
    source: "Как сделать так, чтобы TypeScript ругался на несуществующий ключ объекта?",
    question: "How do you make TypeScript complain about a non-existent object key?",
    shortAnswer: "To make TypeScript complain about a non-existent key you need to constrain the keys with `keyof` and avoid the `string` or `any` types. If the key is typed as `keyof T`, the compiler will only allow access to properties that really exist on the object. Any attempt to pass an invalid key will raise an error during type checking. This is the main mechanism for type-safe access to objects.",
    longAnswer: "By default TypeScript can be too \"lenient\" if you give it a key type that is too general.\n\nDefinition\n`keyof` is a TypeScript operator that forms the union of all the valid keys of an object.\n\nThe problem without `keyof`\n\nWhat is wrong\n\n- TypeScript does not know whether such a key exists.\n- There is no error even for `get(obj, \"b\")`.\n- The type of the result becomes unsafe (`any` or `unknown`).\n\nThe correct approach with `keyof`\n\nThe behaviour\n\nUse with a concrete type\n\nAdditional strictness measures\n\n- Enable `noImplicitAny`.\n- Enable `strict`.\n- Avoid index signatures unless they are necessary.\n\nConclusion\nTo guarantee that TypeScript complains about non-existent keys, the key must always be typed through `keyof` rather than as an arbitrary string.",
    codeExample: "function get(obj: { a: number }, key: string) {\n  return obj[key];\n}\n\nfunction get<T, K extends keyof T>(obj: T, key: K): T[K] {\n  return obj[key];\n}\n\nconst user = { id: 1, name: \"Alex\" };\n\nget(user, \"id\");    // ok\nget(user, \"name\");  // ok\nget(user, \"age\");   // TypeScript error\n\ntype User = {\n  id: number;\n  name: string;\n};\n\nfunction getUserValue(user: User, key: keyof User) {\n  return user[key];\n}\n\n// weakens the checking\ntype Bad = {\n  [key: string]: string;\n};",
  },
  1904: {
    source: "Как ограничить generic ключами объекта (keyof)?",
    question: "How do you constrain a generic to an object's keys (keyof)?",
    shortAnswer: "A generic is constrained to an object's keys with the construct `K extends keyof T`. This guarantees that `K` can only be one of the keys of the object `T`. The approach lets you tie the type of the key to the type of the return value and get precise typing. It is the basic and most widespread pattern in TypeScript.",
    longAnswer: "Constraining a generic is a way of telling the compiler which values are allowed.\n\nDefinition\nA generic constraint is a restriction on a type parameter expressed with `extends`.\n\nA basic example\n\nWhat is happening here\n\n- `T` — the object type.\n- `K` — the key type.\n- `K extends keyof T` — the key can only be an existing one.\n- `T[K]` — the type of the value depends on the key.\n\nAn example of use\n\nWhy this is needed\n\n- Protection against typos.\n- Correct types without `as`.\n- Better autocompletion.\n- Safe universal utilities.\n\nAn extended example: writing a value\n\nThe behaviour\n\nConclusion\nConstraining a generic with `K extends keyof T` is a fundamental TypeScript technique for building strict, safe APIs when working with objects.",
    codeExample: "function getValue<T, K extends keyof T>(obj: T, key: K): T[K] {\n  return obj[key];\n}\n\nconst settings = {\n  theme: \"dark\",\n  retry: 3,\n};\n\ngetValue(settings, \"theme\"); // string\ngetValue(settings, \"retry\"); // number\ngetValue(settings, \"url\");   // error\n\nfunction setValue<T, K extends keyof T>(\n  obj: T,\n  key: K,\n  value: T[K]\n) {\n  obj[key] = value;\n}\n\nsetValue(settings, \"retry\", 5);     // ok\nsetValue(settings, \"retry\", \"5\");   // error",
  },
  1905: {
    source: "Почему типизация в TS удобнее, чем в чистом CSS?",
    question: "Why is typing in TS more convenient than in plain CSS?",
    shortAnswer: "TypeScript gives you autocompletion, compile-time checks and safe refactoring, none of which plain CSS has. Errors in the names of tokens, colours or variants are caught before the application runs. This is especially convenient in large projects and design systems. The result is styles that are more predictable and maintainable.",
    longAnswer: "Plain CSS is flexible but offers almost no protection against errors.\n\nDefinition\nDefinition: typing in TS is a way of restricting the allowed values and data structures in advance, so that errors are found during development rather than in the browser.\n\nWhat TypeScript adds on top of CSS\n\n- Autocompletion\n- the IDE suggests the available tokens, variants and themes.\n\n- Error checking\n- typos in names (`primaryy`) are caught immediately.\n\n- Safe refactoring\n- renaming a token updates every use of it.\n\n- Restricting values\n- you can state the allowed variants explicitly (`\"small\" | \"medium\" | \"large\"`).\n\nExample: constraints through TS\n\nIn plain CSS such errors would only show up visually.\n\nWhere this is especially useful\n\n- Design systems and UI kits\n- Theming (light/dark, brand themes)\n- Large teams and long-lived projects\n\nConclusion\nTyping makes styles safer, more convenient and more scalable, especially once CSS stops being \"just a couple of files\" and becomes part of the architecture.",
    codeExample: "type Size = \"sm\" | \"md\" | \"lg\";\n\nexport const button = {\n  sm: \"4px\",\n  md: \"8px\",\n  lg: \"12px\",\n} satisfies Record<Size, string>;",
  },
  1906: {
    source: "Какие сложности у тебя возникают при работе с TypeScript?",
    question: "What difficulties do you run into when working with TypeScript?",
    shortAnswer: "The main difficulties arise with generics, complex union types and type inference. Sometimes TS requires more code than you would like in order to express your intent. It can also be hard to type dynamic structures and third-party libraries. Over time these problems get solved with patterns and practice.",
    longAnswer: "TypeScript is powerful but not always obvious.\n\nCommon difficulties\n\n1. Generics\n\n- Signatures are hard to read\n- Errors look unwieldy\n- It is not always obvious where `<T>` is needed\n\n2. Union types and narrowing\n\n- You have to narrow the type explicitly\n- Without checks TS does not \"trust\" the developer\n\n3. Type inference\n\n- Sometimes TS infers too wide a type\n- You have to help it with `as const` or explicit annotations\n\n4. Third-party libraries\n\n- The types may be inaccurate\n- Sometimes you have to write a `d.ts`\n- The versions of the types and of the library can drift apart\n\nHow people usually cope with this\n\n- They break types down into simpler ones\n- They use utility types (`Pick`, `Omit`)\n- They are not afraid of explicit annotations\n\nConclusion\nDifficulties in TypeScript are a normal part of the work. The main thing is to understand why the compiler is complaining and to be able to translate business logic into comprehensible types.",
    codeExample: "function identity<T>(value: T): T {\n  return value;\n}\n\ntype Result = { ok: true; data: string } | { ok: false; error: Error };\n\nif (result.ok) {\n  // data is available\n}",
  },
  1907: {
    source: "Что такое декларативное слияние интерфейсов",
    question: "What is declarative merging of interfaces",
    shortAnswer: "Declarative merging of interfaces is TypeScript's ability to combine several declarations of the same interface into one. All the fields from the different declarations are gathered together automatically. This is used to extend types without rewriting them. It is most often applied when working with libraries and global types.",
    longAnswer: "Definition\nDeclaration merging is a TypeScript mechanism by which several declarations with the same name are combined into a single type.\n\nAn example with an interface\n\nThe result:\n- `User` has the fields `id` and `name`\n\nWhere this is used\n\n- Extending the types of third-party libraries\n- Adding fields to global interfaces\n- Describing an API that is \"grown\" piece by piece\n\nLimitations\n\n- It works only with `interface`\n- `type` cannot be merged this way\n- Conflicting types will produce an error\n\nAn example of practical use\n\nConclusion\nDeclarative merging of interfaces lets you extend types cleanly without changing the original source code, and it is an important part of the TypeScript ecosystem.",
    codeExample: "interface User {\n  id: number;\n}\n\ninterface User {\n  name: string;\n}\n\ninterface Window {\n  myAppVersion: string;\n}",
  },
  1908: {
    source: "Как с помощью Omit убрать поле из типа",
    question: "How do you remove a field from a type with Omit",
    shortAnswer: "`Omit` is a utility type that lets you create a new type by excluding one or more fields from an existing one. It does not change the original type but returns a new one. It is most often used when you need to pass an object without internal or sensitive fields. This makes types cleaner and safer.",
    longAnswer: "Definition\n`Omit` is a built-in TypeScript utility type that creates the type `T` without the properties `K`.\n\nA basic example\n\n`PublicUser` contains:\n- `id`\n- `name`\n\nRemoving several fields\n\nWhere this is used\n\n- DTOs for an API\n- Edit forms\n- Public data models\n- Removing internal fields (`createdAt`, `updatedAt`)\n\nAn important nuance\n`Omit`:\n- works only at the type level\n- does not remove the field at runtime\n\nConclusion\n`Omit` is a convenient, safe way of getting a derived type without the unneeded fields, with no need to duplicate the description of the structure.",
    codeExample: "type User = {\n  id: number;\n  name: string;\n  password: string;\n};\n\ntype PublicUser = Omit<User, 'password'>;\n\ntype SafeUser = Omit<User, 'password' | 'id'>;",
  },
  1909: {
    source: "Как с помощью Pick выбрать нужные поля",
    question: "How do you select the fields you need with Pick",
    shortAnswer: "`Pick` lets you create a new type that includes only the selected fields from an existing one. It is the opposite of `Omit`. It is useful when you need a \"narrow\" type for a specific scenario. The original type is not changed in the process.",
    longAnswer: "Definition\n`Pick` is a utility type that forms a type from `T` containing only the properties `K`.\n\nExample\n\nPractical cases\n\n- Lists and previews\n- Partial API responses\n- Optimizing component props\n- Explicitly restricting the available data\n\n`Pick` vs duplication\n\n- there is no need to describe the fields again\n- fewer discrepancies when the base type changes\n- better maintainability\n\nConclusion\n`Pick` helps you express explicitly which part of the data is really needed, and makes types more readable and more resilient to change.",
    codeExample: "type User = {\n  id: number;\n  name: string;\n  email: string;\n};\n\ntype UserPreview = Pick<User, 'id' | 'name'>;",
  },
  1910: {
    source: "Что такое Type Guard и зачем он нужен",
    question: "What is a Type Guard and what is it for",
    shortAnswer: "A Type Guard is a check that lets TypeScript determine the exact type of a value in a particular branch of code. It is used when working with union types, where the behaviour depends on the real type of the data. A Type Guard makes code safer and spares you unnecessary checks and type casts. It can be built in or custom.",
    longAnswer: "Definition\nA Type Guard is an expression or function that narrows the type of a value within a particular scope.\n\nBuilt-in Type Guards\n\n- `typeof`\n- `instanceof`\n- Checking for a property\n\nExample:\n\nA custom Type Guard\n\nUse:\n\nWhy they are needed\n\n- Safe work with union types\n- Readable code without `as`\n- Protection against runtime errors\n\nConclusion\nA Type Guard is TypeScript's key tool for safe logic when the type of a value is determined at runtime.",
    codeExample: "function print(value: string | number) {\n  if (typeof value === 'string') {\n    value.toUpperCase();\n  }\n}\n\ntype User = { name: string };\ntype Admin = { name: string; role: string };\n\nfunction isAdmin(user: User | Admin): user is Admin {\n  return 'role' in user;\n}\n\nif (isAdmin(u)) {\n  u.role;\n}",
  },
  1911: {
    source: "Как можно использовать интерфейсы в TypeScript для улучшения структуры данных?",
    question: "How can you use interfaces in TypeScript to improve your data structures?",
    shortAnswer: "Interfaces in TypeScript let you define the structure of your data, providing typing for objects and making code easier to maintain. They give you strict type checking and help guarantee that data conforms to the expected shape.",
    longAnswer: "In TypeScript interfaces are a powerful tool for ensuring data is strictly typed. Using interfaces helps guarantee that objects and data structures have the correct shape.\n- Defining the structure of an object:\n- An interface lets you specify the structure of an object, stating which fields are required and which are optional.\n- Example:\n\n- Using interfaces for arrays and collections:\n- Interfaces can also describe the types of the elements of arrays and collections.\n- Example:\n\n- Interfaces for functions:\n- Interfaces can be used to describe the structure of a function, stating the types of its parameters and its return value.\n- Example:\n\nConclusion: interfaces in TypeScript help ensure data is strictly typed and improve the structure of your code, making it clearer and less error-prone.",
    codeExample: "interface User {\n  name: string;\n  age: number;\n  email?: string; // an optional field\n}\n\nconst user: User = { name: 'Alice', age: 25 };\n\ninterface Product {\n  id: number;\n  name: string;\n}\n\nconst products: Product[] = [\n  { id: 1, name: 'Laptop' },\n  { id: 2, name: 'Phone' }\n];\n\ninterface SumFunction {\n  (a: number, b: number): number;\n}\n\nconst sum: SumFunction = (a, b) => a + b;",
  },
  1912: {
    source: "Какие сложности появляются при типизации данных, получаемых с сервера или внешних источников?",
    question: "What difficulties come up when typing data received from a server or from external sources?",
    shortAnswer: "The main difficulty is that TypeScript does not control data at runtime, and a server can send back anything. Types describe the client's expectations but do not guarantee the actual structure of the response. Data is often incomplete, contains `null`, has extra fields or differs from the documentation. This leads to runtime errors despite a correct compilation. That is why typing server data requires additional checks.",
    longAnswer: "TypeScript works only at compile time and cannot check the real data that arrives over the network. Because of this, several typical problems come up when working with a server.\n\nThe main difficulties of typing external data\n\n- No guarantees about the structure. The server may return an object that does not match the expected interface. TypeScript meanwhile considers the data valid if it has been cast to the required type.\n- Mismatched data types\n- a `number` may arrive as a string\n- a required field may be missing\n- a value may be `null` instead of an object\n\n- API evolution. Server contracts change over time, while the client's types can go stale. This is especially critical when there is no strict synchronization between teams.\n- Complex nested structures. The deeper the nesting, the higher the chance of discrepancies and the harder the types are to maintain.\n\nAn example of the problem\n\nConclusion\nTyping server data in TypeScript describes the client's expectations but does not guarantee that the data is correct. Additional runtime checks are necessary for reliability.",
    codeExample: "type User = {\n  id: number\n  name: string\n}\n\nconst user = response as User\n// TypeScript is happy, but the server may not have sent name",
  },
  1913: {
    source: "Как решается проблема несовпадения серверных данных и TypeScript-типов?",
    question: "How is the mismatch between server data and TypeScript types solved?",
    shortAnswer: "The problem is solved through explicit validation of the data at runtime. Validation schemas or manual checks are normally used. TypeScript types are supplemented with runtime checks to make sure the data matches expectations. Shared contracts between server and client also help. This reduces the number of hidden errors.",
    longAnswer: "A mismatch between types and real data cannot be solved by TypeScript alone. Additional approaches are used for this.\n\nThe main approaches\n\n- Runtime validation of the data. The data is checked as soon as it is received from the server. If the structure does not match, an error is thrown or fallback logic is applied.\n- Explicit transformation of the data. The server's response is converted into the required format by hand. This lets you control `null`, default values and types.\n- Contracts between server and client. These are used:\n- OpenAPI\n- GraphQL\n- shared types in a monorepo\n\n- Isolating the API layer. Working with raw data is moved into a separate layer, and only already-validated structures reach the rest of the code.\n\nAn example of the approach\n\nConclusion\nTypeScript types must be supplemented with runtime checks. Only a combination of compile-time and runtime approaches gives real reliability.",
    codeExample: "function parseUser(data: any): User {\n  if (typeof data.id !== 'number') {\n    throw new Error('Invalid user id')\n  }\n\n  return {\n    id: data.id,\n    name: String(data.name ?? '')\n  }\n}",
  },
  1914: {
    source: "Можно ли использовать опциональные поля для всех данных от сервера, и к чему это приводит?",
    question: "Can you use optional fields for all data from the server, and what does that lead to?",
    shortAnswer: "Technically you can make every field optional, but it degrades the quality of your types. The code stops protecting you from errors, because any field may be `undefined`. This leads to a large number of checks and complicates the logic. In the end TypeScript loses its value. This approach is considered an anti-pattern.",
    longAnswer: "Using optional fields looks like a simple solution to the problems with server data, but in practice it creates new difficulties.\n\nWhat happens when optional fields are used en masse\n\n- Loss of strict typing. The type no longer describes real expectations, only possible variants.\n- A growing number of checks in the code. In almost every place you have to write checks for `undefined`.\n- Hidden errors. The logic can break silently if a field is unexpectedly missing.\n- Reduced readability. It becomes unclear which fields are actually required for a component or function to work.\n\nAn example of the problem\n\nThe recommended approach\n\n- Make the fields without which the code does not work required\n- Handle undefined values at the API boundary\n- Use different types for \"raw\" and \"normalized\" data\n\nConclusion\nOptional fields are acceptable, but using them en masse destroys the benefit of TypeScript. It is better to validate the data explicitly and work with guaranteed structures.",
    codeExample: "type User = {\n  id?: number\n  name?: string\n}\n\n// you have to keep checking\nif (!user.id) {\n  return\n}",
  },
  1915: {
    source: "Какие задачи TypeScript помогает решать на этапе разработки?",
    question: "What problems does TypeScript help solve at development time?",
    shortAnswer: "TypeScript helps you find errors before the code runs. It adds strict typing on top of JavaScript. Types improve the readability and predictability of code. This matters especially in large, team-based projects.",
    longAnswer: "TypeScript solves a number of problems that are difficult or impossible to catch in plain JavaScript while the code is being written.\n\nDefinition\nTypeScript is a layer on top of JavaScript that adds static typing and compile-time checks.\n\nThe main problems TypeScript solves\nIn practice TypeScript is used for the following purposes:\n- Early error detection\n- Type mismatches\n- Incorrect function arguments\n- Accessing properties that do not exist\n\n- Increasing code reliability\n- Explicit contracts between parts of the application\n- Fewer hidden runtime errors\n\n- Improving readability\n- Types act as documentation\n- It is easier to understand which data is expected and returned\n\n- Supporting scaling\n- Code is easier to refactor\n- It is easier to work as a team\n\nExample\n\nConclusion\nTypeScript lets you move some errors from runtime to compile time. This makes development more predictable and safer, especially on large projects.",
    codeExample: "function sum(a: number, b: number): number {\n  return a + b;\n}",
  },
  1916: {
    source: "Что такое генерация кода на основе API-спецификаций и какие инструменты используются?",
    question: "What is code generation from API specifications and which tools are used?",
    shortAnswer: "Code generation from an API specification is the automatic creation of types, clients and requests based on OpenAPI or Swagger. It reduces the amount of hand-written code and lowers the risk of errors. OpenAPI Generator and Swagger Codegen are often used.",
    longAnswer: "Code generation is the process of creating client code from a description of an API.\n\nWhat an API specification is\nDefinition: an API specification is a formal description of the server's endpoints, parameters and responses.\n\nMost often these are used:\n- OpenAPI\n- Swagger\n\nWhat can be generated\n\n- TypeScript types\n- HTTP clients\n- request functions\n\nAn example of the result\n\nThe advantages\n\n- Frontend and backend stay in sync\n- Fewer errors\n- Faster development\n\nWhere it is applied\n\n- Large projects\n- Microservice architectures\n- Teams with a separate backend\n\nConclusion\nCode generation is especially useful when an API changes often and strict typing is required.",
    codeExample: "export interface User {\n  id: number;\n  name: string;\n}",
  },
  1917: {
    source: "Какие утилитарные типы TypeScript существуют?",
    question: "Which TypeScript utility types exist?",
    shortAnswer: "Utility types are built-in types for transforming other types. The ones used most often are `Partial`, `Required`, `Pick`, `Omit`, `Record` and `Readonly`. They help you create new types quickly without duplicating code.",
    longAnswer: "Utility types let you modify existing types and create derived types.\n\nThe main utility types\n\n- Partial. Makes all fields optional.\n\n- Required. Makes all fields required.\n- Pick. Selects specific fields.\n\n- Omit. Excludes fields.\n\n- Record. Creates a dictionary type.\n\n- Readonly. Makes fields immutable.\n\nWhere they are applied\n\n- API responses\n- forms\n- DTOs\n\nConclusion\nUtility types speed up work with types considerably and let you avoid duplication.",
    codeExample: "type User = { name: string; age: number };\ntype UpdateUser = Partial<User>;\n\ntype UserName = Pick<User, \"name\">;\n\ntype UserWithoutAge = Omit<User, \"age\">;\n\ntype Users = Record<string, User>;",
  },
  1918: {
    source: "Какие недостатки есть у enum в TypeScript?",
    question: "What drawbacks does enum have in TypeScript?",
    shortAnswer: "Enums increase bundle size, because they turn into an object in JavaScript. They are less flexible than union types and sometimes complicate typing. There are also unexpected quirks when working with numeric enums and reverse mapping. That is why modern TypeScript often prefers string union types.",
    longAnswer: "Enums in TypeScript are convenient but have a number of practical limitations.\n\n1) How an enum compiles\nDefinition: an enum is a type that compiles into a JavaScript object containing the values and sometimes a reverse mapping. Example:\n\nCompilation results in an object being created.\n\n2) Increased bundle size\nThe reason:\n- An enum creates an object.\n- A reverse mapping is sometimes added.\n- On large projects this increases the resulting code.\n\n3) Less convenient typing\nInstead of an enum people often use:\n\nThe advantages of a union:\n- No extra code in JS.\n- Easier to integrate with an API.\n\n4) Problems with numeric enums\nNumeric enums:\n- Allow non-obvious values.\n- Can lead to errors in comparisons.\n\nExample:\n\n5) When an enum is justified after all\n\n- When you need a set of constants.\n- When an explicit namespace matters.\n- In library code.\n\nConclusion: enums are convenient but can increase bundle size and are less flexible than union types, so they are applied deliberately.",
    codeExample: "enum Status {\n  Active,\n  Disabled\n}\n\ntype Status = \"active\" | \"disabled\";\n\nenum Role {\n  Admin = 1\n}\n\nlet r: Role = 999 as Role; // possible",
  },
  1919: {
    source: "Как получить тип store в TypeScript (например, через ReturnType)?",
    question: "How do you get the type of the store in TypeScript (for example, with ReturnType)?",
    shortAnswer: "The type of the `store` can be obtained by taking the return type of the function that creates it. `ReturnType` is used for this. The approach spares you from duplicating types by hand. It adapts to changes in the implementation automatically. This increases the reliability of your typing and reduces the number of errors.",
    longAnswer: "In TypeScript it is important to aim at inferring types from the code rather than describing them by hand.\n\nDefinition\nDefinition: ReturnType<T> is a TypeScript utility type that extracts the return type of the function `T`.\n\nAn example with a Redux store\n\nWhy do it this way\n\n- A single source of truth for types\n- The type updates automatically\n- Duplication is reduced\n\nWhere it is applied\n\n- Redux store\n- Configurations\n- Object factories\n- API clients\n\nTypical mistakes\n\n- Describing complex types by hand\n- Using `any` instead of inferring types\n\nConclusion\n`ReturnType` lets you obtain precise types straight from functions, making your code resilient to change.",
    codeExample: "export const createStore = () => {\n  return {\n    getState: () => ({ user: null }),\n    dispatch: (action: any) => {}\n  };\n};\n\nexport type Store = ReturnType<typeof createStore>;",
  },
  1920: {
    source: "В чём разница между Omit и Exclude?",
    question: "What is the difference between Omit and Exclude?",
    shortAnswer: "`Omit` is used to remove properties from an object type. `Exclude` is used to exclude members from a union type. Despite their similar names, they work on different kinds of thing. `Omit` is usually applied to DTOs and component props. `Exclude` is for narrowing sets of possible values.",
    longAnswer: "To apply `Omit` and `Exclude` correctly it is important to understand which level each type works on: the structure of an object, or a union of types.\n\nDefinition\nDefinition: Omit is a utility type that removes the given keys from an object type. Exclude is a utility type that excludes members from a union type.\n\nOmit: working with objects\n`Omit` is applied when you need to take an existing type and remove one or more fields from it.\n\nExample\n\nWhere it is used\n\n- The props of React components\n- DTOs for an API\n- Public representations of entities\n\nExclude: working with unions\n`Exclude` is used to remove individual variants from a union.\n\nExample\n\nWhere it is used\n\n- Statuses\n- Event types\n- Restricting the allowed values\n\nA common mistake\n\n- Trying to apply `Exclude` to an object type\n- Using `Omit` to work with a union\n\nConclusion\n`Omit` works with the keys of objects, while `Exclude` works with the members of a union type; the choice depends on the shape of the source type.",
    codeExample: "type User = {\n  id: string;\n  email: string;\n  password: string;\n};\n\ntype PublicUser = Omit<User, \"password\">;\n\ntype Status = \"loading\" | \"success\" | \"error\";\ntype StableStatus = Exclude<Status, \"loading\">;",
  },
  1921: {
    source: "Как получить тип возвращаемого значения функции из сторонней библиотеки с помощью TypeScript?",
    question: "How do you get the return type of a function from a third-party library with TypeScript?",
    shortAnswer: "The return type of a function can be obtained with `ReturnType` combined with `typeof`. If the function is asynchronous, `Awaited` is used in addition. This approach lets you reproduce a third-party library's types exactly. It protects you from drifting out of sync on updates. It is a basic practice when working with external SDKs and APIs.",
    longAnswer: "When working with third-party libraries it is important not to rewrite their types by hand but to extract them straight from the declarations.\n\nDefinition\nDefinition: typeof + ReturnType is a combination that lets you obtain the return type of a function from its declaration.\n\nA basic synchronous example\n\nThe asynchronous case\nIf the function returns a `Promise`, the type needs to be \"unwrapped\".\n\nExample\n\nWhy this is needed\n\n- No duplication of types\n- Changes are picked up automatically\n- Precise typing of complex structures\n\nTypical scenarios\n\n- API clients\n- SDKs of third-party services\n- Hooks and factories\n\nTypical mistakes\n\n- Describing return types by hand\n- Using `any` instead of inferring types\n- Ignoring `Awaited` for async functions\n\nConclusion\nThe combination of `typeof`, `ReturnType` and `Awaited` is the standard way of working safely with the types of third-party libraries.",
    codeExample: "import { createClient } from \"some-lib\";\n\ntype Client = ReturnType<typeof createClient>;\n\nimport { fetchUser } from \"api-lib\";\n\ntype User = Awaited<ReturnType<typeof fetchUser>>;",
  },
  1922: {
    source: "setTimeout и setInterval как стандарт JavaScript",
    question: "setTimeout and setInterval as a JavaScript standard",
    shortAnswer: "The setTimeout and setInterval functions are not part of the JavaScript language standard (ECMAScript). They are provided by the runtime environment, such as the browser or Node.js, through the Web API or other environment APIs. The ECMAScript specification contains no description of these functions. They are used for deferred or periodic execution of code, but their implementation and availability depend on the specific platform.",
    longAnswer: "Which standard they belong to\nThe `setTimeout` and `setInterval` functions are not part of the ECMAScript specification (the core JavaScript standard). They are defined in the environment's API:\n- In browsers: part of the Web API standardized by the WHATWG and the W3C (for example, in the HTML Living Standard specification).\n- In Node.js: part of the global object provided by the runtime.\n\nWhy this matters\nSince these functions are not part of the language core, their behaviour and availability can differ between environments. For example, the minimum delay, the timer's accuracy or additional parameters may depend on the implementation.\n\nAn example of use\n\nConclusion\n`setTimeout` and `setInterval` are widely available functions that are nevertheless not standardized in ECMAScript. They belong to the environment's API, which means they depend on the platform (browser, server and so on). Developers should keep this in mind when writing code for different runtimes.",
    codeExample: "// An example in the browser or Node.js\nconsole.log('Start');\nsetTimeout(() => {\n  console.log('Executed after 1 second');\n}, 1000);\n\nlet counter = 0;\nconst intervalId = setInterval(() => {\n  counter++;\n  console.log(`Interval tick ${counter}`);\n  if (counter >= 3) {\n    clearInterval(intervalId);\n    console.log('Interval stopped');\n  }\n}, 500);",
  },
  1923: {
    source: "Как реализуется расширение поведения без модификации существующего кода?",
    question: "How is behaviour extended without modifying existing code?",
    shortAnswer: "The 'Decorator' pattern lets you add new behaviour to objects dynamically without changing their source code. It wraps the original object in a decorator object that has the same interface and delegates calls to the wrapped object, adding its own logic before or after. This is useful when you need to extend a class's functionality but inheritance is not suitable because of an explosion of subclasses. The decorator follows the open/closed principle: classes are open for extension but closed for modification.",
    longAnswer: "The 'Decorator' pattern is a structural design pattern that lets you add new functionality to objects dynamically by wrapping them in special decorator objects. Instead of changing the original class (which violates the open/closed principle), you create a chain of wrappers, each of which adds its own behaviour.\n\nHow it works\nA decorator implements the same interface as the object it wraps. When a method is called, the decorator performs additional logic (before or after) and then delegates the call to the wrapped object. This lets you combine behaviour on the fly, creating flexible configurations.\n\nAn example in TypeScript\n\nWhere it is applied\n\n- In UI libraries, to add styles or behaviour to components (border, padding, for example).\n- In streaming APIs (Java I/O, .NET Streams), to add filters (buffering, compression).\n- In the middleware of web frameworks (Express.js, NestJS), to add logging and authentication.\n\nConclusion: use the 'Decorator' pattern when you need to add responsibilities to objects flexibly at runtime, avoiding a rigid inheritance hierarchy and keeping the code clean and extensible.",
    codeExample: "// The base component interface\ninterface Coffee {\n    cost(): number;\n    description(): string;\n}\n\n// A concrete component\nclass SimpleCoffee implements Coffee {\n    cost() { return 5; }\n    description() { return 'Simple coffee'; }\n}\n\n// The base decorator\nabstract class CoffeeDecorator implements Coffee {\n    constructor(protected coffee: Coffee) {}\n    cost() { return this.coffee.cost(); }\n    description() { return this.coffee.description(); }\n}\n\n// Concrete decorators\nclass MilkDecorator extends CoffeeDecorator {\n    cost() { return super.cost() + 2; }\n    description() { return super.description() + ', milk'; }\n}\n\nclass SugarDecorator extends CoffeeDecorator {\n    cost() { return super.cost() + 1; }\n    description() { return super.description() + ', sugar'; }\n}\n\n// Use\nlet myCoffee: Coffee = new SimpleCoffee();\nmyCoffee = new MilkDecorator(myCoffee);\nmyCoffee = new SugarDecorator(myCoffee);\nconsole.log(myCoffee.description()); // Simple coffee, milk, sugar\nconsole.log(myCoffee.cost()); // 8",
  },
  1924: {
    source: "Как дженерики реализованы в коллекциях?",
    question: "How are generics implemented in collections?",
    shortAnswer: "Generics in collections let you create data structures (lists, dictionaries, sets) that work with a particular type of element. This provides compile-time type checking, preventing errors when objects of an unsuitable type are added. For example, a List can contain only strings. This approach makes code safer and cleaner by removing the need for type casts.",
    longAnswer: "Generics in collections are a mechanism that lets you parameterize collection classes with the concrete data type they will work with. This means that instead of using collections in the style of \"raw\" types, which can store any objects, you create, for example, a list intended exclusively for strings or numbers.\n\nHow it is implemented\nAt the language level the compiler uses the technique of type erasure, or of preserving types, depending on the platform. In Java and C# generics provide compile-time type checking, but the type information is erased at runtime (in Java). In TypeScript types exist only at compile time in JavaScript.\n\nCode examples\nHere is what it looks like in practice in different languages:\n\nWhere and how it is applied\nGenerics in collections are used everywhere in modern development to:\n- Ensure type safety: the compiler catches type-mismatch errors before the program runs.\n- Remove the need for manual type casts, which makes code cleaner and faster.\n- Create reusable, flexible components: one collection implementation can work with any type.\n- Improve the readability and maintainability of code: from the signature it is immediately clear which data the collection works with.\n\nConclusion: using generics in collections is a mandatory practice for writing safe, maintainable and expressive code. They are worth applying whenever you need a data structure for storing elements of one particular type, in order to avoid runtime errors and explicit casts.",
    codeExample: "// Java: safe work with a list of strings\nList<String> names = new ArrayList<>();\nnames.add(\"Alice\");\n// names.add(42); // Compile error: incompatible types\nString first = names.get(0); // No cast required\n\n// C#: using a generic dictionary\nDictionary<int, string> users = new Dictionary<int, string>();\nusers.Add(1, \"Bob\");\n// users.Add(\"key\", \"value\"); // Compile error\nstring userName = users[1]; // Direct access with a typed key\n\n// TypeScript: a typed array\nconst numbers: Array<number> = [1, 2, 3];\nnumbers.push(4);\n// numbers.push(\"five\"); // Compile error\nconst doubled = numbers.map(n => n * 2); // The type of n is inferred as number",
  },
  1925: {
    source: "Что такое type safety?",
    question: "What is type safety?",
    shortAnswer: "Type safety is a property of a programming language that guarantees operations are only performed on data of permissible types. It prevents errors such as trying to add a number to a string. In languages with strict static typing (TypeScript or Java, for example) these checks happen at compile time, which helps find bugs earlier. In dynamic languages (JavaScript, for example) many checks are deferred until the program runs, which can lead to runtime errors.",
    longAnswer: "Type safety is a fundamental concept in programming which means that the language will not let you perform an operation on a value if that operation is not defined for its data type. The main goal is to prevent a whole class of errors that arise from unexpected behaviour of data — for example, trying to call a method that does not exist on an object, or performing an arithmetic operation on a string.\n\nHow it works\nThe language's type system controls the matching of types. This can be done at different stages:\n- Static type checking: the analysis happens before the program runs, at compile or lint time. Examples: TypeScript, Java, C#.\n- Dynamic type checking: the checks are performed while the program runs. Examples: Python, JavaScript, Ruby.\n\nStatically typed languages often provide a higher level of type safety, because many errors are caught before the code even runs.\n\nA practical example\nConsider the difference between JavaScript (dynamic typing) and TypeScript (static typing).\n\nWhere it is applied and why it is needed\nType safety is critically important in large projects and teams, because it:\n- Serves as a form of documentation, making function contracts explicit.\n- Lets tools (IDEs) provide intelligent autocompletion and code navigation.\n- Significantly reduces the number of errors caused by typos or incorrect API use, which are hard to catch in dynamic languages.\n- Makes refactoring easier — the compiler helps you find every place that needs updating.\n\nConclusion: type safety is a powerful mechanism for increasing the reliability and maintainability of code. It is worth applying in any project where stability matters, especially when working in a team or maintaining an application long term. Using languages or layers with static typing (TypeScript for the frontend, languages like Go or Rust for the backend) is an investment in code quality that pays for itself in reduced debugging time.",
    codeExample: "// JavaScript (no checking as you write)\nfunction calculateTotal(price, quantity) {\n    return price * quantity;\n}\n// This will only cause an error at runtime if a string is passed\nconsole.log(calculateTotal(\"10\", 5)); // \"10\" * 5 = 50 (implicit conversion, which may not be obvious)\nconsole.log(calculateTotal(\"ten\", 5)); // NaN (a runtime error)\n\n// TypeScript (static type checking)\nfunction calculateTotal(price: number, quantity: number): number {\n    return price * quantity;\n}\n// The TypeScript compiler will report an error immediately and refuse to compile the code:\n// calculateTotal(\"10\", 5); // An argument of type 'string' is not assignable to a parameter of type 'number'.\n// The correct call:\nconsole.log(calculateTotal(10, 5)); // 50",
  },
  1926: {
    source: "Когда имеет смысл выносить константы в отдельный модуль?",
    question: "When does it make sense to move constants into a separate module?",
    shortAnswer: "Constants are worth moving into a separate module when they are used in several places in the application and their values are logically related. This improves the readability of the code, because all the key values are gathered in one place. Changing a value requires an edit in only one module, which reduces the risk of errors. The approach also makes testing and refactoring easier.",
    longAnswer: "Moving constants into a separate module is a code-organization practice aimed at centralizing the immutable values used in different parts of an application. It is not merely a mechanical relocation of numbers and strings but a way of making code clearer, more manageable and less error-prone.\n\nWhen does it make sense?\n\n- Repeated use: if the same string, number or object is used in several modules, files or components.\n- Logical grouping: when the constants belong to a single subject area — for example, API settings (URL, keys, timeouts), status codes, error messages or UI configuration (colours, sizes).\n- Simpler changes: if a value may change in the future (for example, the base API URL when the environment changes), it is easier to update in one place.\n- Better readability: named constants (`MAX_RETRY_ATTEMPTS`, for example) are clearer than \"magic numbers\" (3, for example) scattered through the code.\n\nA practical example\nConsider an example in JavaScript/TypeScript. Instead of hard-coding values in different files, we create a `constants.js` or `config.ts` module.\n\nNow in any component or module of the application we import these constants:\n\nWhere it is applied\nThis approach is universal and is used in practically every programming paradigm and technology stack: frontend (React, Vue, Angular), backend (Node.js, Python, Java), and also in build configuration (Webpack) and environment settings.\n\nConclusion: move constants into a separate module when the values are used in several places, are logically grouped or may change. This is a key practice for creating clean, maintainable and scalable code, especially on medium and large projects.",
    codeExample: "// constants/api.js\nexport const API_BASE_URL = 'https://api.example.com/v1';\nexport const API_TIMEOUT = 30000; // 30 seconds\nexport const DEFAULT_PAGE_SIZE = 20;\n\n// constants/errors.js\nexport const ERROR_CODES = {\n  NOT_FOUND: 404,\n  UNAUTHORIZED: 401,\n  SERVER_ERROR: 500\n};\n\n// constants/ui.js\nexport const COLORS = {\n  PRIMARY: '#007bff',\n  SUCCESS: '#28a745',\n  DANGER: '#dc3545'\n};\n\n// userService.js\nimport { API_BASE_URL, DEFAULT_PAGE_SIZE } from '../constants/api';\n\nasync function fetchUsers(page = 1) {\n  const response = await fetch(\n    `${API_BASE_URL}/users?page=${page}&limit=${DEFAULT_PAGE_SIZE}`\n  );\n  return response.json();\n}",
  },
  1927: {
    source: "Почему важно явно указывать возвращаемый тип функции при работе с ORM?",
    question: "Why is it important to state a function's return type explicitly when working with an ORM?",
    shortAnswer: "Stating a function's return type explicitly when working with an ORM helps the TypeScript compiler check that the data you expect to get from the database is correct. It prevents runtime errors where, for example, a function is supposed to return an array of objects but returns a single object or null. It also improves autocompletion in the IDE and makes the code clearer for other developers by showing explicitly what the method returns.",
    longAnswer: "When using an ORM (Object-Relational Mapping) in TypeScript, stating a function's return type explicitly is not a mere formality but an important practice that directly affects the reliability and maintainability of the code. ORM libraries such as TypeORM, Prisma or Sequelize often return complex promises with generics, and without an explicit annotation TypeScript may infer the type as `any` or as something too general, which deprives you of the benefits of static typing.\n\nThe advantages of stating the type explicitly\n\n- Type safety: the compiler checks that you handle the query result correctly (for example, that you access fields that exist on the entity).\n- Error prevention: you see at once if you expect an array of users (`User[]`) while the method returns a single user (`User | null`).\n- Better DX (Developer Experience): the IDE provides accurate autocompletion for the fields of the returned object.\n- Documenting the code: the type serves as documentation that helps other developers understand what the function does.\n\nA practical example\nConsider an example with TypeORM and a `User` entity. Without an explicit type TypeScript cannot check operations on the result correctly.\n\nWhere this matters most\nExplicit types are critical for an ORM's repository methods (`find`, `findOne`, `save`), for custom queries with `QueryBuilder` and for defining relations between entities. In complex scenarios — for example when using `select` with partial fields, or joins — the return type may not be an entity but a special DTO or interface, which also has to be stated explicitly.\n\nConclusion: stating the return type explicitly when working with an ORM is a mandatory practice for creating reliable, maintainable and scalable backend applications in TypeScript. It minimizes errors caused by data mismatches and speeds development up considerably through good tooling support.",
    codeExample: "import { User } from './entity/User';\nimport { AppDataSource } from './data-source';\n\n// Without an explicit type: the type of result is inferred as any or Promise<any>\nasync function getUsersBad() {\n  return await AppDataSource.getRepository(User).find();\n}\n\n// With an explicit type: it is clear that an array of User is returned\nasync function getUsersGood(): Promise<User[]> {\n  return await AppDataSource.getRepository(User).find();\n}\n\n// Using the typed function\nasync function main() {\n  const users = await getUsersGood();\n  // The IDE will suggest the fields: users[0].id, users[0].name\n  users.forEach(user => console.log(user.name)); // Safe access\n}",
  },
  1928: {
    source: "Какие проблемы возникают при отсутствии TypeHint в функциях?",
    question: "What problems arise when functions have no type hints?",
    shortAnswer: "Without type annotations code becomes less clear, because it is not obvious which data types a function expects and what it returns. This increases the risk of runtime errors — passing a string instead of a number, for example. Refactoring also becomes harder, as does the work of static-analysis tools such as mypy or the IDE, which cannot warn you about a type mismatch. In the end the code becomes harder to maintain and scale.",
    longAnswer: "Type hints in functions are a way of stating explicitly the expected types of the arguments and of the return value. Their absence creates several key problems in software development.\n\nReduced readability and understanding of the code\nWhen you see a function without annotations, you cannot quickly understand what it does or which data it needs. You have to read the body of the function, the documentation (if there is any) or even run the code to work out the function's contract. This slows development down, especially in large teams or when working with someone else's code.\n\nMore errors at runtime\nMany errors caused by passing arguments of the wrong type are only discovered while the program is running. For example, a function that expects a number may receive a string and fail with a `TypeError` or — worse — silently produce an incorrect result.\n\nDifficulties with refactoring and with tooling\nModern IDEs and static analysers (mypy for Python, the TypeScript Compiler) rely heavily on type information. Without annotations they cannot:\n- Offer autocompletion (intellisense) for arguments and return values.\n- Detect potential errors before the code runs.\n- Safely perform automatic refactoring — renaming a function parameter, for example.\n\nA practical example with annotations\nHere is how the same function looks with type annotations:\n\nWhere this is applied\nType annotations are critically important in large projects, microservice architectures and library development, where a clear API contract prevents a great many integration errors. They are also the basis for generating documentation (with Sphinx, for example) and for writing more reliable tests.\n\nConclusion: using type annotations is especially useful in team development, when maintaining legacy code and in projects where reliability and predictability matter. They reduce the developer's cognitive load and let errors be found while the code is being written rather than in production.",
    codeExample: "def calculate_discount(price, discount_rate):\n    # Without annotations it is unclear: is price an int/float? Is discount_rate a float (0.1) or a string (\"10%\")?\n    return price * (1 - discount_rate)\n\n# The error may only show up at the call site\nresult = calculate_discount(100, \"0.1\")  # TypeError: can't multiply sequence by non-int of type 'str'\n\nfrom typing import Union\n\ndef calculate_discount(price: Union[int, float], discount_rate: float) -> float:\n    \"\"\"Returns the price after the discount has been applied.\"\"\"\n    return price * (1 - discount_rate)\n\n# Now the IDE and mypy will warn about the error\nresult = calculate_discount(100, \"0.1\")  # mypy: Argument 2 to \"calculate_discount\" has incompatible type \"str\"; expected \"float\"",
  },
  1929: {
    source: "Когда уместно использовать Union (или оператор |) в типах?",
    question: "When is it appropriate to use a Union (or the | operator) in types?",
    shortAnswer: "Union types in TypeScript let a variable, a function parameter or a return value belong to one of several types. This is useful when a value can be, for example, a string or a number, or when a function can return an object of a particular shape or null. Using a union increases the flexibility of the code while keeping compile-time type checking. TypeScript will require every possible variant of the type to be handled, which prevents runtime errors.",
    longAnswer: "Union types are a powerful construct of TypeScript's type system that lets a variable, a function parameter or a return value belong not to one type but to several. This reflects real scenarios where a value can take different forms depending on the context.\n\nThe core idea and the syntax\nThe syntax of a union type uses the vertical-bar operator `|` between the types. For example, `string | number` means that the value may be either a string or a number. This lets you describe the domain of possible values precisely without resorting to the `any` type, which switches off type checking.\n\nPractical use\nUnion types are often applied in the following situations:\n- Functions that work with different types of input: a formatting function, for example, that accepts both strings and numbers.\n- Handling values that may be absent: instead of returning only an object, a function may return `null` or `undefined` on error — the type of the result would be `SomeType | null`.\n- Modelling states: a data-loading state, for example, can be represented as a union: `Loading | Success | Error`.\n- Working with the DOM API: the `document.getElementById` property returns `HTMLElement | null`, because the element may not exist.\n\nA code example\nConsider a function that formats a value for display. It has to work with both numbers and strings:\n\nWithout a union type we would have to use `any`, which would deprive us of type checking and autocompletion.\n\nType narrowing\nThe key point when working with a union is narrowing the type. TypeScript will not let you perform an operation specific to one type until you have established which variant you are working with. The `typeof` and `instanceof` checks, equality checks or custom type guards are used for this.\n\nUnion types are a fundamental tool for creating flexible yet type-safe interfaces. They let you model a program's behaviour precisely without sacrificing strict typing.\n\nConclusion: use union types when a value, a parameter or a function's result can logically belong to one of several types. This is especially useful for handling optional values, modelling different states and working with external APIs where the data may come in different formats.",
    codeExample: "function formatValue(value: string | number): string {\n    // TypeScript forces us to check the type before any operations\n    if (typeof value === 'string') {\n        return value.toUpperCase();\n    } else {\n        // Here TypeScript knows that value is a number\n        return value.toFixed(2);\n    }\n}\n\nconsole.log(formatValue('hello')); // \"HELLO\"\nconsole.log(formatValue(42.123));  // \"42.12\"",
  },
  1930: {
    source: "Как правильно использовать аннотации типов для аргументов и возвращаемого значения?",
    question: "How do you use type annotations for arguments and for the return value correctly?",
    shortAnswer: "Type annotations in Python are written after a colon for arguments and after an arrow `->` for the return value. They do not affect how the code runs, but they help developers and tools like mypy understand the expected data types. For example, `def greet(name: str) -> str:`. For complex types use the `typing` module: `List[int]`, `Optional[str]`. Annotations make code self-documenting and easier to maintain.",
    longAnswer: "Type hints in Python are a way of stating explicitly the expected data types for a function's arguments and its return value. They were introduced in PEP 484 and serve primarily for static code analysis, better readability and documenting interfaces.\n\nThe basic syntax\nFor an argument the type is given after a colon; for the return value it comes after an arrow `->`, before the function's colon.\n\nUsing the typing module\nFor more complex structures such as lists, dictionaries, optional values or user-defined classes, the `typing` module is used.\n\nPractical use and tooling\nType annotations are especially useful in large projects and in team development. They let:\n- static-analysis tools (mypy, pyright, Pylance) check that types match and find potential errors before the code runs.\n- IDEs (VS Code, PyCharm) provide more accurate autocompletion and hints.\n- you write self-documenting code, where a function's contract (what it accepts and what it returns) is clear from its signature.\n\nIt is important to remember that Python remains a dynamically typed language — annotations do not affect execution at runtime (unless you use an additional library such as pydantic for validation).\n\nAn example with a user-defined type\n\nIn Python 3.10+ a more concise syntax with a vertical bar can be used instead of `Union[User, int]`: `User | int`.\n\nConclusion: type annotations are worth applying to every non-trivial function in projects where readability, maintainability and early error detection matter. They are practically mandatory in enterprise development and when creating libraries with clear APIs.",
    codeExample: "def add(a: int, b: int) -> int:\n    return a + b\n\n# Use with a type hint for a string\nmessage: str = \"Hello\"\nprint(add(5, 3))  # 8\n\nfrom typing import List, Optional, Dict\n\ndef process_items(items: List[int], config: Optional[Dict[str, str]] = None) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Processes a list of integers and an optional configuration dictionary.\"\"\"\n    if not items:\n        return False\n    # ... processing logic\n    return True\n\n# Calling the function\nresult = process_items([1, 2, 3], {\"mode\": \"fast\"})\n\nfrom typing import Union\n\nclass User:\n    def __init__(self, name: str):\n        self.name = name\n\ndef get_identifier(entity: Union[User, int]) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Accepts either a User object or an integer.\"\"\"\n    if isinstance(entity, User):\n        return entity.name\n    else:\n        return f\"ID-{entity}\"\n\n# Use\nuser = User(\"Alice\")\nprint(get_identifier(user))   # Alice\nprint(get_identifier(42))     # ID-42",
  },
  1931: {
    source: "Приходилось ли использовать TypeVar из модуля typing и в каких случаях?",
    question: "Have you had to use TypeVar from the typing module, and in which cases?",
    shortAnswer: "TypeVar is used to create generic types in Python. It lets you declare functions or classes that work with different types while preserving the relationship between them — for example, a function that returns an element of the same type as the argument it was given. This increases type safety and improves the hints in the IDE without restricting the flexibility of the code.",
    longAnswer: "TypeVar is a tool from the typing module for creating type variables, which let you define generic functions, methods and classes. It is useful when the logic of the code has to work with various types while type information still has to be preserved for static checking.\n\nThe main uses\nTypeVar is often used in the following scenarios:\n- Creating functions where the type of the argument and of the return value are related (for example, an identity function that returns the object it was given).\n- Defining generic classes such as containers (Box[T], for example) that can hold data of any type while still being type-checked.\n- Ensuring type safety in algorithms that abstract away from concrete types (comparison or sorting functions, for example).\n\nCode examples\nConsider a simple example of an identity function that returns the argument it was given:\n\nA more complex example is a generic Box class that holds a value of an arbitrary type:\n\nTypeVar also supports bounds and variance (covariant/contravariant), which lets you create stricter types. For example, constraining a type to numbers only:\n\nConclusion: TypeVar is worth applying when developing libraries, frameworks or any code that needs the flexibility to work with different types without losing the benefits of static typing. It is especially useful for creating reusable components such as containers, data-processing utilities or decorators.",
    codeExample: "from typing import TypeVar, List\n\nT = TypeVar('T')  # Declaring a type variable\n\ndef identity(value: T) -> T:\n    return value\n\n# Use\nresult_int: int = identity(42)        # The type T is inferred as int\nresult_str: str = identity(\"hello\")   # The type T is inferred as str\n\nfrom typing import TypeVar, Generic\n\nT = TypeVar('T')\n\nclass Box(Generic[T]):\n    def __init__(self, content: T) -> None:\n        self.content = content\n    \n    def get_content(self) -> T:\n        return self.content\n\n# Use\nbox_int: Box[int] = Box(100)\nbox_str: Box[str] = Box(\"text\")\nprint(box_int.get_content() + 5)   # Works, because content is an int\n\nfrom typing import TypeVar, Union\n\nNumeric = TypeVar('Numeric', int, float)  # T can only be int or float\n\ndef double(x: Numeric) -> Numeric:\n    return x * 2\n\nprint(double(10))     # OK\nprint(double(3.14))   # OK\n# double(\"string\")    # Type error: a string is not part of Numeric",
  },
  1932: {
    source: "Как используются дженерики в Python через typing?",
    question: "How are generics used in Python through typing?",
    shortAnswer: "Generics in Python let you create functions, classes or data structures that can work with different types while preserving type information for checks and hints. They are implemented through the `typing` module using `TypeVar` and generic classes such as `List[T]` or `Dict[K, V]`. This helps static analysers such as mypy check that types are used correctly without losing the flexibility of the code. For example, you can write a function that returns a list of elements of any type, as long as they are all the same.",
    longAnswer: "Generics in Python are a mechanism that lets you define functions, classes or data structures capable of working with various types while preserving information about the concrete type for static checks. They are implemented through the `typing` module, which introduces type annotations that support parametric polymorphism.\n\nThe main components of generics\nThe key elements for creating generics:\n- `TypeVar`: defines a type variable that can be replaced by a concrete type when used.\n- Generic classes: `List[T]` or `Dict[K, V]` from the `typing` module, for example, or user-defined classes inheriting from `Generic[T]`.\n- Annotations: stating generic types in the signatures of functions and classes.\n\nExamples of use\nConsider a simple example of a function that returns the first element of a list of any type:\n\nHere `T` is a type variable that ties the type of the list's elements to the type of the return value. When called with a `List[int]` the function returns an `int`.\n\nCreating a generic class\nYou can create your own classes parameterized by types. For example, a simple container:\n\nThe `Box` class can hold an object of any type, but when creating an instance we state the concrete type, which lets the static analyser check that operations are correct.\n\nWhere generics are applied\nGenerics are especially useful in the following scenarios:\n- Libraries and frameworks: in FastAPI, for example, for typing requests and responses, or in an ORM for typed database queries.\n- Data structures: implementing stacks, queues and trees that have to work with elements of different types.\n- Higher-order functions: functions that accept other functions (callbacks) with typed arguments and return values.\n- Data-processing utilities: functions for mapping, filtering and reducing collections.\n\nConstraints and advanced capabilities\nWith `TypeVar` you can set bounds or require the type to be of a particular class. For example:\n\nYou can also use `typing.Generic` with several parameters — for a dictionary, for example: `Dict[K, V]`.\n\nConclusion: generics in Python through the `typing` module are worth applying when you need to create flexible, reusable code that must remain type-safe. They are especially useful in libraries, frameworks and any project that uses static type checking (mypy, pyright), since they help avoid type errors and improve the readability of the code.",
    codeExample: "from typing import TypeVar, List\n\nT = TypeVar('T')  # Defining a type variable\n\ndef first_element(items: List[T]) -> T:\n    \"\"\"Returns the first element of the list.\"\"\"\n    return items[0]\n\n# Use\nnumbers: List[int] = [1, 2, 3]\nresult: int = first_element(numbers)  # mypy understands that result has the type int\nprint(result)  # 1\n\nfrom typing import Generic, TypeVar\n\nT = TypeVar('T')\n\nclass Box(Generic[T]):\n    def __init__(self, content: T) -> None:\n        self.content = content\n    \n    def get_content(self) -> T:\n        return self.content\n\n# Use\nbox_int: Box[int] = Box(42)\nvalue: int = box_int.get_content()  # The type of value is inferred as int\n\nbox_str: Box[str] = Box(\"hello\")\ntext: str = box_str.get_content()   # The type of text is inferred as str\n\nfrom typing import TypeVar, List\n\n# T must be a subtype of float or int (a numeric type)\nNumeric = TypeVar('Numeric', int, float)\n\ndef sum_values(values: List[Numeric]) -> Numeric:\n    return sum(values)\n\n# Correct\nsum_values([1, 2, 3])   # int\nsum_values([1.5, 2.5])  # float\n\n# A type error (if checked with mypy): str is not part of int/float\n# sum_values([\"a\", \"b\"])",
  },
  1933: {
    source: "Какие плюсы и минусы использования exception для бизнес-логики?",
    question: "What are the pros and cons of using exceptions for business logic?",
    shortAnswer: "Using exceptions for business logic has both pros and cons. The pros: they separate the normal flow from errors, making code cleaner, and they let you handle errors centrally at the upper levels. The cons: exceptions add a performance overhead and can hide logic, making it less explicit. For expected errors (invalid input, for example) it is better to use return values or special result objects.",
    longAnswer: "Exceptions are an error-handling mechanism that interrupts the normal flow of a program and transfers control to a special handler (a catch block). In the context of business logic, which implements the rules and operations of a domain, it is important to distinguish clearly when exceptions are appropriate and when they are not.\n\nThe advantages of using exceptions\n\n- Separation of concerns: the business-logic code stays clean, because error handling is moved to a higher level (to the controller or the presentation layer, for example).\n- Centralized handling: you can catch exceptions in one place, log them and turn them into responses for the user (HTTP 400, for example).\n- Enforced handling: in languages with checked exceptions (such as Java) the compiler forces the developer to handle the possible errors explicitly, which increases reliability.\n\nThe disadvantages of using exceptions\n\n- Performance: creating an exception and \"unwinding\" the stack is usually more expensive than returning a simple value. This can be critical in high-load loops.\n- Obscured control flow: exceptions can be used to control the normal flow (for example, to break out of nested loops), which is considered an anti-pattern because it hides intent.\n- Implicitness: it is not always obvious to the calling code which exceptions may be thrown, especially in languages without checked exceptions (such as JavaScript or Python).\n\nA practical example\nConsider a function that checks a user's balance before a withdrawal. Using an exception for an expected situation (insufficient funds) may be excessive.\n\nAn alternative approach is returning a result object that explicitly indicates success or failure, which can be more efficient and clearer for expected failures.\n\nConclusion: exceptions are worth using for handling genuinely exceptional, unexpected situations (a database failure, a lost network connection). For expected business-logic errors (invalid input, insufficient funds) it is preferable to use return values or special result types (Result/Option), since this makes the control flow explicit and reduces overhead.",
    codeExample: "// An example in TypeScript: using an exception for a business rule\nclass InsufficientFundsError extends Error {\n    constructor(public userId: number, public required: number) {\n        super(`User ${userId} lacks ${required} credits.`);\n    }\n}\n\nfunction withdraw(userId: number, amount: number): void {\n    const balance = getBalance(userId); // get the balance from the database\n    if (balance < amount) {\n        // A business error: insufficient funds\n        throw new InsufficientFundsError(userId, amount);\n    }\n    // ... withdrawal logic\n}\n\n// Handling at the top level (in an Express route, for example)\ntry {\n    withdraw(123, 1000);\n    res.status(200).json({ success: true });\n} catch (error) {\n    if (error instanceof InsufficientFundsError) {\n        res.status(400).json({ error: error.message });\n    } else {\n        // An unexpected error (a database problem, for example)\n        res.status(500).json({ error: 'Internal server error' });\n    }\n}\n\n// The alternative: returning a result without an exception\ninterface WithdrawalResult {\n    success: boolean;\n    errorMessage?: string;\n}\n\nfunction withdrawSafe(userId: number, amount: number): WithdrawalResult {\n    const balance = getBalance(userId);\n    if (balance < amount) {\n        return { success: false, errorMessage: 'Insufficient funds' };\n    }\n    // ... withdrawal logic\n    return { success: true };\n}\n\nconst result = withdrawSafe(123, 1000);\nif (!result.success) {\n    // Handle the expected business error\n    console.log(result.errorMessage);\n}",
  },
  1934: {
    source: "Какие практики используются для контроля качества кода (ESLint, Husky)?",
    question: "Which practices are used for code quality control (ESLint, Husky)?",
    shortAnswer: "For code quality control people often use ESLint for static analysis and Husky to automate checks through Git hooks. ESLint checks code against the configured style rules and for potential errors. Husky lets you run those checks (`npm run lint`, for example) automatically before a commit, preventing problematic code from reaching the repository. This forms a protective barrier and keeps the team disciplined.",
    longAnswer: "Code quality control is a set of practices aimed at keeping a codebase clean, consistent and reliable. The two key technologies for this in the JavaScript/TypeScript ecosystem are the ESLint linter and Husky, a tool for managing Git hooks.\n\nESLint: static code analysis\nESLint analyses your JavaScript or TypeScript code without executing it, looking for errors, style problems and discouraged constructs. It works from a set of rules that can be configured. Popular configurations such as `eslint:recommended` or the Airbnb style provide ready-made rule sets.\n\nESLint is applied during development: it can be run by hand, integrated into a code editor (VS Code, for example) to highlight problems in real time, or run as part of a CI/CD pipeline.\n\nHusky: automation through Git hooks\nHusky simplifies working with Git hooks — scripts that Git runs at particular moments (before a commit or before a push, for example). Husky lets you configure those hooks easily right inside the project, making the process standardized and mandatory for every developer.\n\nThe typical use is a pre-commit hook that runs linting and possibly formatting (Prettier) or unit tests. If the script exits with an error, the commit is aborted.\n\nTogether these tools create an automated quality-control pipeline. A developer trying to make a commit automatically triggers a code check. This prevents code that violates the team's conventions or contains obvious syntax errors from reaching the shared branch.\n\nConclusion: the practice of using ESLint with Husky is especially useful in team development for keeping code consistent and implementing the \"don't break master\" principle. It is worth applying on any project where more than one person works on the code, or which is meant to be maintained for a long time.",
    codeExample: "// An example .eslintrc.js configuration\nmodule.exports = {\n  env: {\n    browser: true,\n    es2021: true,\n  },\n  extends: ['eslint:recommended', 'plugin:@typescript-eslint/recommended'],\n  parser: '@typescript-eslint/parser',\n  plugins: ['@typescript-eslint'],\n  rules: {\n    'no-console': 'warn', // A warning when console.log is used\n    'semi': ['error', 'always'], // Requires a semicolon\n  },\n};\n\n// An example of the setup in package.json after installing Husky\n{\n  \"scripts\": {\n    \"lint\": \"eslint src/**/*.{js,ts}\",\n    \"prepare\": \"husky install\"\n  },\n  \"devDependencies\": {\n    \"eslint\": \"^8.0.0\",\n    \"husky\": \"^8.0.0\"\n  }\n}\n// Then the hook is created in the terminal:\n// npx husky add .husky/pre-commit \"npm run lint\"",
  },
  1935: {
    source: "Как использовать generics для создания кастомных типов?",
    question: "How do you use generics to create custom types?",
    shortAnswer: "Generics let you create components that work with various types rather than with one concrete type. You declare a type parameter (`<T>`, for example) in the definition of a type, interface or function. When using that type you can state the concrete type that will replace the parameter. This makes the code type-safe and reusable, avoiding duplicated logic for different data types.",
    longAnswer: "Generics are a powerful TypeScript tool for creating components that can work with various data types while preserving type information. Instead of hard-coding a type (number or string, for example), you use a type parameter that will be replaced by a concrete type when the component is used.\n\nThe basic syntax and creating custom types\nA type parameter is usually denoted by a single letter, `T` (for Type), for example. It can be used when defining a type, an interface or a class.\n\nGenerics in functions and interfaces\nGenerics are especially useful for functions that have to return a value of the same type as the argument, or for describing data structures such as arrays or promises.\n\nGeneric constraints\nSometimes you need to guarantee that a type parameter has certain properties. The `extends` keyword is used for this.\n\nPractical use\nGenerics are widely used in:\n- Libraries and frameworks: React (for typing props and state, for example), Redux Toolkit.\n- Data structures: creating typed queues, stacks and linked lists.\n- Working with APIs: typing server responses, where the structure of the data is known but can differ between endpoints.\n\nConclusion: use generics to create flexible, reusable and type-safe custom types, especially when a component's logic is the same for different data types but you want to preserve information about the concrete type at compile time.",
    codeExample: "// A generic type\ntype Container<T> = {\n  value: T;\n  id: number;\n};\n\n// Use with concrete types\nconst stringContainer: Container<string> = { value: \"Hello\", id: 1 };\nconst numberContainer: Container<number> = { value: 42, id: 2 };\n\n// A generic function\nfunction identity<T>(arg: T): T {\n  return arg;\n}\nconst output = identity<string>(\"myString\"); // The type of output is string\n\n// A generic interface for an API response\ninterface ApiResponse<T> {\n  data: T;\n  status: number;\n  message: string;\n}\n\nconst userResponse: ApiResponse<{ name: string; age: number }> = {\n  data: { name: \"Alice\", age: 30 },\n  status: 200,\n  message: \"OK\"\n};\n\ninterface HasLength {\n  length: number;\n}\n\nfunction logLength<T extends HasLength>(arg: T): void {\n  console.log(arg.length); // Safe, because T is guaranteed to have length\n}\n\nlogLength(\"text\"); // OK, a string has a length property\nlogLength([1, 2, 3]); // OK, an array has length\n// logLength(42); // Error: a number has no length property",
  },
  1936: {
    source: "Как реализовать Optional с помощью generics?",
    question: "How do you implement Optional with generics?",
    shortAnswer: "Optional is a container that may hold a value or be empty. With generics we parameterize the type of the value that can sit inside the Optional. This lets you create type-safe wrappers for any data type. For example, an Optional may contain a string or be empty. The implementation includes a private field for storing the value and methods for checking whether it is present and for extracting it.",
    longAnswer: "The Optional (or Maybe) concept is a design pattern meant to solve the problem of working with missing values (null) in a safer, more expressive way. Instead of returning null and risking a NullPointerException, a method returns a container that explicitly reports whether a value is present. Generics let you make this container universal, so that it can work with any data type while still being type-checked at compile time.\n\nThe main components of the implementation\nA basic implementation of Optional using generics usually includes the following elements:\n- A private field for storing a value of the generic type T.\n- A private constructor that takes a value (or null).\n- Static factory methods for creating instances: `of(value)` for non-null values and `empty()` for an empty Optional.\n- Methods for checking whether a value is present: `isPresent()`.\n- Methods for extracting the value safely: `get()` (throws an exception if there is no value), `orElse(defaultValue)`, `orElseGet(supplier)`.\n- Methods for functional transformations: `map()`, `flatMap()`, `filter()`.\n\nA code example in Java\nHere is a simplified implementation of Optional using generics:\n\nThis implementation shows how generics (`<T>`) let the Optional class work with any type. For example, you can create `Optional<String>`, `Optional<Integer>` and so on, and the compiler will check that the types match.\n\nWhere it is applied\nOptional is widely used in modern languages and frameworks to indicate explicitly that a value may be absent. In Java 8+ it is built into the standard library (`java.util.Optional`). In TypeScript/JavaScript a similar pattern is implemented with union types or libraries (fp-ts, for example). In functional languages (Haskell, Scala) it is a fundamental part of the type system.\n\nUsing Optional makes code more readable and safer, because it forces the developer to handle the case of a missing value explicitly instead of silently swallowing null.\n\nConclusion: implementing Optional with generics is a powerful way of creating a type-safe container for optional values. It is worth applying in the APIs of methods whose return value may be absent, in order to avoid null-related errors and make the method's contract more explicit.",
    codeExample: "public class Optional<T> {\n    private final T value; // The stored value of type T\n\n    // A private constructor\n    private Optional(T value) {\n        this.value = value;\n    }\n\n    // Create an Optional with a non-null value\n    public static <T> Optional<T> of(T value) {\n        if (value == null) {\n            throw new NullPointerException(\"Value cannot be null\");\n        }\n        return new Optional<>(value);\n    }\n\n    // Create an empty Optional\n    public static <T> Optional<T> empty() {\n        return new Optional<>(null);\n    }\n\n    // Check whether there is a value\n    public boolean isPresent() {\n        return value != null;\n    }\n\n    // Get the value (dangerous if empty)\n    public T get() {\n        if (value == null) {\n            throw new NoSuchElementException(\"No value present\");\n        }\n        return value;\n    }\n\n    // Return the value or a default\n    public T orElse(T other) {\n        return value != null ? value : other;\n    }\n}",
  },
  1937: {
    source: "Как избавиться от жесткой зависимости от конкретной реализации (например, singleton)?",
    question: "How do you get rid of a hard dependency on a concrete implementation (a singleton, for example)?",
    shortAnswer: "A hard dependency on a concrete implementation such as a Singleton can be removed by applying the dependency inversion principle (DIP) and dependency injection (DI). Instead of the class creating the dependency's instance itself, it should receive it from outside through a constructor, a method or a property. This lets you substitute the real implementation with a mock for testing, or with a different implementation depending on the context. The code thus becomes less coupled and more flexible.",
    longAnswer: "A hard dependency on a concrete class, especially on a Singleton, creates problems with testing and with the system's flexibility, because the code becomes tightly coupled to one implementation. The solution is to apply the dependency inversion principle (DIP), which states that high-level modules should not depend on low-level modules; both should depend on abstractions.\n\nThe main approach: Dependency Injection\nInstead of a class creating the dependency inside itself, it should receive it from outside. This can be done through the constructor (the most preferable way), through a setter or through an interface. Consider an example in TypeScript.\n\nWhere and how this is applied\nThis approach is widely used in modern frameworks (Angular, NestJS, Spring). Dependency-injection containers (IoC containers) manage the creation and wiring of objects automatically. In the example above `OrderService` now depends on the abstraction `ILogger` rather than on the concrete `Logger`. This lets you:\n- Test the class easily by substituting the dependencies with mock objects.\n- Change the implementation (for example, replace a console logger with a file logger or Sentry) without changing the code of `OrderService`.\n- Respect the single-responsibility principle (SRP) — the class is not in the business of creating its own dependencies.\n\nConclusion: apply dependency injection through abstractions (interfaces or abstract classes) everywhere the implementation may change or isolated testing is required. This is especially important for services that work with external systems (databases, APIs, logging).",
    codeExample: "// BAD: a hard dependency on a Singleton\nclass OrderService {\n    private logger = Logger.getInstance(); // Using the Singleton directly\n\n    processOrder() {\n        this.logger.log('Processing order...');\n        // ... logic\n    }\n}\n\n// GOOD: the dependency is injected through the constructor\ninterface ILogger {\n    log(message: string): void;\n}\n\nclass ConsoleLogger implements ILogger {\n    log(message: string) {\n        console.log(`[LOG]: ${message}`);\n    }\n}\n\nclass OrderService {\n    constructor(private logger: ILogger) {} // The dependency is supplied from outside\n\n    processOrder() {\n        this.logger.log('Processing order...');\n        // ... logic\n    }\n}\n\n// Use\nconst logger = new ConsoleLogger();\nconst orderService = new OrderService(logger);\norderService.processOrder();\n\n// For testing the implementation is easy to substitute\nclass MockLogger implements ILogger {\n    log(message: string) {\n        // Do nothing, or record into an array for assertions\n    }\n}\nconst testService = new OrderService(new MockLogger());",
  },
  1938: {
    source: "Как правильно управлять зависимостями сетевого слоя?",
    question: "How do you manage the dependencies of the network layer correctly?",
    shortAnswer: "Managing the dependencies of the network layer means designing it so that it is not tightly coupled to a concrete implementation. The main approach is to use abstractions (interfaces) for network clients together with dependency injection. This lets you substitute real HTTP requests with mocks for tests, change libraries (axios for fetch, for example) without rewriting the business logic, and manage configuration centrally (base URLs, headers). Such a layer becomes an independent module.",
    longAnswer: "Managing the dependencies of the network layer correctly is a key aspect of a modern application's architecture. It separates the code that performs HTTP requests from the rest of the business logic, making the system more modular, more testable and more adaptable to change.\n\nThe main principles\n\n- Abstraction: define an interface (or an abstract class) for the network client. The business logic should depend on that interface rather than on the concrete axios or fetch class.\n- Dependency injection (DI): the concrete implementation of the network client is \"injected\" from outside, usually through a constructor or method parameters.\n- Single responsibility: the network layer should deal only with communication: forming requests, handling responses and errors. Parsing data and business rules are the responsibility of other modules.\n\nA practical implementation\nConsider an example in TypeScript. First we define the abstraction, then the implementation, and then we show how to use it.\n\nThe advantages and the uses\nThis approach is applied for:\n- Unit testing: you can pass a mock `HttpClient` into `UserService` that returns fake data without making real network requests.\n- Easy replacement of libraries: to move from fetch to axios you need to create a new `AxiosHttpClient` class implementing the same interface and change one line where the dependencies are created.\n- Centralized management: the configuration (baseURL, authentication headers, interceptors) is encapsulated in one place — in the `HttpClient` implementation.\n\nConclusion: managing dependencies through abstractions and DI is worth applying in any project where network requests are a critical part. It is mandatory for medium and large applications, since it considerably simplifies testing, maintenance and the evolution of the code when external APIs or network libraries change.",
    codeExample: "// 1. The abstraction (interface) of the network client\ninterface HttpClient {\n  get<T>(url: string): Promise<T>;\n  post<T>(url: string, data: unknown): Promise<T>;\n  // ... other methods\n}\n\n// 2. A concrete implementation using fetch (or axios)\nclass FetchHttpClient implements HttpClient {\n  private baseURL: string;\n\n  constructor(baseURL: string) {\n    this.baseURL = baseURL;\n  }\n\n  async get<T>(url: string): Promise<T> {\n    const response = await fetch(`${this.baseURL}${url}`);\n    if (!response.ok) {\n      throw new Error(`HTTP error! status: ${response.status}`);\n    }\n    return response.json() as Promise<T>;\n  }\n  // ... the implementation of post and the other methods\n}\n\n// 3. The business-logic service, which depends on the abstraction\nclass UserService {\n  constructor(private httpClient: HttpClient) {}\n\n  async getUsers() {\n    // The business logic uses only the interface\n    return this.httpClient.get<User[]>('/users');\n  }\n}\n\n// 4. Composing (\"wiring up\") the dependencies\nconst httpClient = new FetchHttpClient('https://api.example.com');\nconst userService = new UserService(httpClient);\n\n// 5. Use\n// const users = await userService.getUsers();",
  },
  1939: {
    source: "Как обрабатывать отсутствующие поля в JSON?",
    question: "How do you handle missing fields in JSON?",
    shortAnswer: "When handling JSON with missing fields you need to allow for two main scenarios: when the field is absent entirely and when it has the value null. In strictly typed languages such as TypeScript you can use optional types or default values. In JavaScript you can use the optional chaining operator (?.) or the nullish coalescing operator (??) for safe access. It is always worth validating incoming data to avoid runtime errors.",
    longAnswer: "When working with JSON, especially from external sources, you cannot guarantee that every expected field is present. Handling missing data is a key part of building fault-tolerant applications.\n\nThe main approaches to handling it\n\n- Checking whether a field exists: before accessing a value you need to make sure the field exists on the object.\n- Using default values: if a field is absent or equal to null, a safe default should be substituted.\n- Validating the structure: using libraries to check that the JSON matches the expected schema (JSON Schema, Zod, Joi).\n\nExamples in JavaScript/TypeScript\nConsider a typical scenario of parsing a response from an API.\n\nValidation with libraries\nFor complex structures it is better to use validation libraries. For example, with Zod in TypeScript:\n\nConclusion: handling missing fields is a mandatory practice for preventing failures. Use safe-access operators and default values for simple cases, and validation schemas for complex data structures, especially when the JSON comes from unreliable sources.",
    codeExample: "// The original JSON, where the 'age' field may be absent\nconst jsonString = '{\"name\": \"Alice\"}';\nconst data = JSON.parse(jsonString);\n\n// UNSAFE: may throw an error if the field is absent\n// const userAge = data.age;\n\n// Safe access with the ?. and ?? operators\nconst userAge = data?.age ?? 25; // If age is missing or null, it will be 25\nconsole.log(`Age: ${userAge}`); // Output: Age: 25\n\n// An explicit check\nlet userName;\nif ('name' in data) {\n    userName = data.name;\n} else {\n    userName = 'Guest';\n}\n\n// Using destructuring with default values\nconst { name = 'Guest', age = 25, city = 'Unknown' } = data;\nconsole.log(name, age, city); // Alice 25 Unknown\n\nimport { z } from 'zod';\n\n// We define the schema of the expected data\nconst UserSchema = z.object({\n    name: z.string().default('Guest'),\n    age: z.number().optional().default(25),\n    email: z.string().email().optional(),\n});\n\n// We parse and validate the incoming data\ntry {\n    const safeUser = UserSchema.parse(incomingData);\n    // safeUser is guaranteed to have the name and age fields with their default values\n    console.log(safeUser.name);\n} catch (err) {\n    // Handling the validation error\n    console.error('Invalid data:', err);\n}",
  },
  1940: {
    source: "Что происходит, если не использовать await при вызове асинхронной функции?",
    question: "What happens if you do not use await when calling an asynchronous function?",
    shortAnswer: "If you do not use await when calling an asynchronous function, then instead of waiting for the result of the operation the function returns a Promise object in the pending state. This means the code carries on executing synchronously without waiting for the asynchronous task to finish, which can lead to an unresolved value being used. For example, if the fetchData function returns a Promise, then without await the variable receives the Promise itself rather than the data inside it, which will cause errors when you try to work with it as an ordinary object.",
    longAnswer: "Asynchronous functions in JavaScript, declared with the `async` keyword, always return a Promise object. The `await` keyword is used inside such functions to suspend execution until the Promise is fulfilled or rejected. If you call an asynchronous function without `await`, you get that Promise directly rather than its result.\n\nWhat happens without await\nWhen an asynchronous function is called without `await`, it starts executing but control returns to the calling code immediately. This means that subsequent operations can run before the asynchronous task has finished, which leads to a race condition or to data that is not ready being used.\n\nA code example\n\nWhere this is applied\nSometimes a deliberate call without `await` is useful — when you need to start several asynchronous operations in parallel to improve performance. For example, you can start several requests at once and then use `Promise.all` to wait for all the results.\n\nConclusion\nUse `await` to wait for the result of an asynchronous function when the code that follows depends on that result. If you need to perform operations in parallel or to manage the Promise by hand, you can omit `await`, but then the Promise must be handled explicitly with the `.then()` or `.catch()` methods.",
    codeExample: "async function fetchData() {\n    // Simulating an asynchronous operation, an API request for example\n    return new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(() => resolve('Data received'), 1000));\n}\n\nasync function process() {\n    // A call without await\n    const dataPromise = fetchData();\n    console.log(dataPromise); // Prints: Promise { <pending> }\n    // Trying to use the Promise as a string will cause an error\n    // console.log(dataPromise.toUpperCase()); // Error!\n    \n    // The correct call with await\n    const data = await fetchData();\n    console.log(data); // Prints: 'Data received' (after one second)\n}\n\nprocess();",
  },
  1941: {
    source: "Может ли enum реализовывать интерфейс?",
    question: "Can an enum implement an interface?",
    shortAnswer: "In TypeScript an enum can implement an interface. This lets you guarantee that every value of the enumeration conforms to a particular structure. For example, you can create an interface for an order status and then implement it through an enum, so that every value has code and description fields. This approach improves type safety and documents the code.",
    longAnswer: "In TypeScript enums are not merely a set of numeric or string constants. They can behave like objects at runtime and like types at compile time. One of the advanced capabilities is implementing interfaces with an enum. This lets you impose additional constraints on the structure of the enumeration's values, ensuring consistency and type safety.\n\nHow it works\nAn interface in TypeScript defines a contract — the set of properties and methods an object must have. An enum essentially creates an object whose keys are the names of the enumeration's members and whose values are the corresponding constants. If the enum's values are objects (for example, in the case of computed or string enums with object values), they can be checked against an interface.\n\nA code example\nConsider a practical example where we need to define order statuses with additional information.\n\nWhere this is applied\nImplementing interfaces through an enum is useful in the following scenarios:\n- Type-safe configurations: when a set of constants has to share a single structure (error codes with messages and HTTP statuses, for example).\n- Documentation and maintenance: the interface describes explicitly which data is associated with each enum value, which makes the code clearer.\n- Integration with type systems: it lets you use an enum in functions that expect parameters of a particular interface, strengthening the compiler's checks.\n\nIt is important to note that the syntax `enum MyEnum implements MyInterface` is not directly supported in TypeScript for plain numeric or string enums, because their values are primitives. However, by using objects as values (an enum with computed object values, for example) or by combining an enum with a namespace, you can achieve the same effect.\n\nConclusion: use enums with interfaces when you need a strictly typed set of related constants that must carry additional structured information — for statuses, error codes or configuration objects in an application, for example.",
    codeExample: "// We define the interface every status must implement\ninterface StatusInfo {\n  code: number;\n  description: string;\n}\n\n// We create an enum that implements this interface\nenum OrderStatus implements StatusInfo {\n  Pending = 10,\n  Processing = 20,\n  Shipped = 30,\n  Delivered = 40,\n  Cancelled = 99\n\n  // To implement the interface we would need to add methods or getters.\n  // But an enum cannot contain methods in its body directly.\n  // Instead we can use a namespace or a companion object.\n}\n\n// A more practical approach is to use a companion object.\nnamespace OrderStatus {\n  export function getInfo(status: OrderStatus): StatusInfo {\n    switch (status) {\n      case OrderStatus.Pending:\n        return { code: 10, description: \"The order is awaiting processing\" };\n      case OrderStatus.Processing:\n        return { code: 20, description: \"The order is being assembled\" };\n      // ... the remaining cases\n      default:\n        return { code: 0, description: \"Unknown status\" };\n    }\n  }\n}\n\n// Use\nconst status = OrderStatus.Pending;\nconst info = OrderStatus.getInfo(status);\nconsole.log(info.description); // \"The order is awaiting processing\"",
  },
  1942: {
    source: "Для чего нужны declaration files (.d.ts)?",
    question: "What are declaration files (.d.ts) for?",
    shortAnswer: "Declaration files (.d.ts) are needed to describe the types of existing JavaScript code, especially of external libraries. They let the TypeScript compiler perform type checking and provide autocompletion in the code editor when those libraries are used. Without them TypeScript would not know the structure of imported JS modules, and development would be less safe and less convenient. These files contain only declarations of types, interfaces and functions, not executable code.",
    longAnswer: "TypeScript declaration files (with the .d.ts extension) serve as a bridge between the statically typed world of TypeScript and the untyped or dynamically typed world of JavaScript. They let you describe the shape of existing JavaScript modules, libraries or global objects without rewriting their source code in TypeScript.\n\nThe main purpose and uses\nThe main task is to provide type safety and improve the development experience (IntelliSense) when using third-party libraries written in JavaScript. For example, when you install a library through npm, TypeScript looks for the corresponding .d.ts file in order to understand which functions, classes and objects it exports and what the types of their parameters and return values are.\n\nStructure and creation\nA .d.ts file contains only type declarations: interfaces, types, declarations of functions, classes and modules. There is no executable code in them. They can be created by hand for your own projects, or you can use ready-made ones from the DefinitelyTyped repository (@types/*).\n\nPractical use\nIn a TypeScript project .d.ts files are usually picked up automatically if they are located in the @types folder or next to the source code. The tsc compiler includes them in the type-checking process. If a library has no official types, the community often publishes them under the @types namespace on npm.\n\nConclusion: .d.ts declaration files are critically important for the TypeScript ecosystem, because they let you use the huge legacy of JavaScript libraries safely and with full tooling support, providing type checking and autocompletion without any need to rewrite the code.",
    codeExample: "// An example declarations.d.ts file for a hypothetical library\n\ndeclare module 'my-js-library' {\n    export function calculateSum(a: number, b: number): number;\n    export const DEFAULT_TIMEOUT: number;\n    export interface Config {\n        retries: number;\n        logLevel: 'debug' | 'info';\n    }\n}",
  },
  1943: {
    source: "Как работает extends в TypeScript?",
    question: "How does extends work in TypeScript?",
    shortAnswer: "The extends keyword in TypeScript is used for inheritance. In classes it lets one class inherit the properties and methods of another. In interfaces it extends one interface with another, combining their descriptions. In generics extends acts as a constraint, stating that the type parameter must be compatible with the given type. It is the foundation for building hierarchies and reusing code.",
    longAnswer: "The `extends` keyword in TypeScript is a powerful tool for creating \"is a\" relationships between types, classes and even the parameters of generic types. It lets you build hierarchies, avoid code duplication and ensure strict typing in complex structures.\n\nClass inheritance\nWhen used with classes, `extends` implements classic OOP inheritance. The child class receives all the properties and methods of the parent (except the private ones) and can add its own or override existing ones with `super`.\n\nExtending interfaces\nFor interfaces `extends` means combining type descriptions. You can extend several interfaces separated by commas, creating a new type that includes all the parents' properties.\n\nGeneric constraints\nIn generic types `extends` sets a constraint, guaranteeing that the given type will have a particular structure. This lets you access properties safely inside a generic function or class.\n\nConclusion: use `extends` to create logical hierarchies of types and classes when you need to express an \"is a kind of\" relationship. It is the key mechanism for reusing code, ensuring type safety and modelling a domain in TypeScript.",
    codeExample: "class Animal {\n  constructor(public name: string) {}\n  move(distance: number = 0) {\n    console.log(`${this.name} moved ${distance}m.`);\n  }\n}\n\nclass Dog extends Animal {\n  bark() {\n    console.log('Woof! Woof!');\n  }\n  // Overriding the method\n  move(distance = 5) {\n    console.log('Running...');\n    super.move(distance); // Calling the parent's method\n  }\n}\n\nconst dog = new Dog('Rex');\ndog.bark(); // Woof! Woof!\ndog.move(); // Running...\n            // Rex moved 5m.\n\ninterface Named {\n  name: string;\n}\ninterface Aged {\n  age: number;\n}\n// The Person interface includes the properties of both Named and Aged\ninterface Person extends Named, Aged {\n  occupation: string;\n}\n\nconst user: Person = {\n  name: 'Alice',\n  age: 30,\n  occupation: 'Engineer'\n}; // Correct\n\n// The function accepts only objects that have a length property\nfunction logLength<T extends { length: number }>(item: T): void {\n  console.log(item.length);\n}\n\nlogLength('hello'); // 5 (a string has .length)\nlogLength([1, 2, 3]); // 3 (an array has .length)\n// logLength(42); // Error: a number has no .length",
  },
  1944: {
    source: "Что является результатом компиляции TypeScript?",
    question: "What is the result of compiling TypeScript?",
    shortAnswer: "The result of compiling TypeScript is pure JavaScript code. The TypeScript compiler (tsc) transforms .ts files into .js files, removing every TypeScript syntactic construct such as types and interfaces. This process can also include transpiling the code down to older versions of ECMAScript for browser compatibility. The end result is files that can run in any environment that supports JavaScript.",
    longAnswer: "TypeScript is a superset of JavaScript that adds static typing and other features absent from standard JS. Browsers and the Node.js runtime, however, do not understand TypeScript directly. That is why TypeScript code has to be transformed into standard JavaScript before it runs. This process is called compilation (or often transpilation).\n\nWhat happens during compilation?\nThe compiler's main task (tsc) is to remove all type annotations and TypeScript-specific constructs and leave valid JavaScript. For example, interfaces and type aliases disappear from the final code entirely, because they are used only during type checking.\n\nA code example\nConsider a simple TypeScript file (example.ts):\n\nAfter compiling with the command `tsc example.ts`, an example.js file will be created. Its contents (for the ES5 target, for example) will look like this:\n\nAs you can see, the type annotations and the declaration of the `User` type have been removed. The template-string syntax may have been converted into concatenation for compatibility with older standards.\n\nAdditional outputs of compilation\n\n- Declaration files (.d.ts): with the `--declaration` option the compiler generates .d.ts files containing only type information. They are useful for other projects that use your compiled code.\n- Source maps (.js.map): with the `--sourceMap` option .map files are created that link the compiled JS to the original TS for debugging.\n- Configuration: the process is controlled by the tsconfig.json file, where you can set the target JS version, the strictness of the checks, the output paths and much more.\n\nConclusion: the result of compiling TypeScript is executable JavaScript code, ready to run in the browser or in Node.js. This process is necessary in order to enjoy the benefits of static typing during development without losing compatibility with the entire JavaScript ecosystem.",
    codeExample: "// The original TypeScript code\ntype User = {\n  name: string;\n  age: number;\n};\n\nfunction greet(user: User): string {\n  return `Hello, ${user.name}!`;\n}\n\nconst john: User = { name: \"John\", age: 30 };\nconsole.log(greet(john));\n\n// The compiled JavaScript code\nfunction greet(user) {\n    return \"Hello, \" + user.name + \"!\";\n}\nvar john = { name: \"John\", age: 30 };\nconsole.log(greet(john));",
  },
  1945: {
    source: "Как задать опциональный generic?",
    question: "How do you declare an optional generic?",
    shortAnswer: "In TypeScript an optional generic parameter can be declared by giving it a default value. The syntax resembles default function parameters: `<T = DefaultType>`. This lets you use a generic type, and if it is not stated explicitly the default type is used. The approach makes components more flexible, because they can work both with a concrete type and with the default one when the user does not state it.",
    longAnswer: "Generics in TypeScript let you create components that can work with various data types while preserving type information. Sometimes we want a generic parameter to be optional — that is, if the user does not state it, a sensible default should be used. This is achieved with the syntax for assigning a default value to a generic parameter.\n\nThe syntax of an optional generic\nThe basic syntax looks like this: you declare the generic parameter and immediately assign it a default type with an equals sign.\n\nWhere this is applied\nOptional generics are especially useful in libraries and utilities where you want to provide flexibility but also to have safe default behaviour. For example:\n- In utility functions such as a `createStore` for state, where the default state may be an empty object.\n- In UI components (React, Vue), where props can be generic but have sensible defaults.\n- In decorators or factories that can work with different types but most often with one particular one.\n\nAn example with an interface and classes\nOptional generics can be used not only in functions but also in interfaces, types and classes.\n\nCombining with constraints\nYou can combine optional generics with constraints using the `extends` keyword. The default value must then satisfy the constraint.\n\nConclusion: use optional generics with a default type when you are creating universal constructs (functions, types, components) that should work \"out of the box\" without an explicit type while still allowing fine-tuning for advanced scenarios. This improves the developer experience by reducing boilerplate in simple cases.",
    codeExample: "// A function with an optional generic. The default type is string.\nfunction createArray<T = string>(length: number, value: T): T[] {\n  return Array(length).fill(value);\n}\n\n// Use with an explicit type (number).\nconst numArray = createArray<number>(3, 42); // Type: number[]\n\n// Use without stating the type — string will be used.\nconst strArray = createArray(3, \"hello\"); // Type: string[]\n\n// An API response interface with an optional generic for the data.\ninterface ApiResponse<TData = Record<string, unknown>> {\n  success: boolean;\n  data: TData;\n}\n\n// Use with a concrete type.\nconst userResponse: ApiResponse<{ name: string }> = {\n  success: true,\n  data: { name: \"Alice\" }\n};\n\n// Use without a type — data will be Record<string, unknown>.\nconst basicResponse: ApiResponse = {\n  success: false,\n  data: { errorCode: 404 }\n};\n\n// A generic with a constraint (it must be an object) and a default value.\nfunction mergeObjects<T extends object = {}>(obj1: T, obj2: T): T {\n  return { ...obj1, ...obj2 };\n}\n\n// Works with any object.\nconst result1 = mergeObjects({ a: 1 }, { b: 2 }); // Type: { a: number, b: number }\n\n// If no arguments are passed, the default type {} will not be useful,\n// but syntactically it is allowed.",
  },
  1946: {
    source: "Как валидировать данные на клиенте (например, через схемы)?",
    question: "How do you validate data on the client (with schemas, for example)?",
    shortAnswer: "Validating data on the client with schemas is the process of checking user input against predefined rules (schemas) before sending it to the server. Popular libraries such as Zod or Yup let you describe those rules declaratively. This gives the user instant feedback, reduces the load on the server and prevents incorrect data from being sent. The approach matters especially in registration and payment forms, or any input fields.",
    longAnswer: "Validating data on the client is a critically important stage in web development that ensures the information entered by the user is correct before it is sent to the server. Using validation schemas lets you structure this process, making it more reliable and easier to maintain. A schema is a set of rules describing the expected structure of and constraints on the data (a string of a particular length, a number within a range, a required field, for example).\n\nPopular libraries for schema validation\nThe most widespread tools are:\n- Zod: a library for TypeScript with an emphasis on type safety and inferring types from schemas.\n- Yup: a lightweight library, often used with Formik, with a simple and clear syntax.\n- Joi (more often on the server, but it can be used on the client too): a powerful library with a rich set of validators.\n\nHow it is applied\nValidation schemas are integrated into the form-handling process. When a field changes or the form is submitted, the data is checked against the schema. If validation fails, the user sees an error message next to the field. This gives instant feedback and prevents an incorrect request from being sent.\n\nA code example using Zod\n\nIn the example above `safeParse` returns an object that indicates success or failure safely, which is convenient for handling. The errors contain detailed information that can be shown to the user.\n\nThe advantages of the approach\n\n- A single source of truth: the validation rules are described in one place (the schema) rather than scattered through the code.\n- Type safety (TypeScript): libraries like Zod generate TypeScript types from the schemas automatically, which rules out type mismatches.\n- Reuse: the same schema can be used for validation on the client and, if needed, on the server.\n- Better UX: the user sees the errors immediately without waiting for the server's response.\n\nConclusion: using validation schemas on the client is a modern, effective approach that is worth applying in every form where the correctness of the input matters. It is especially useful in complex forms with many fields and interrelated rules, since it makes the validation code predictable and easy to test.",
    codeExample: "import { z } from 'zod';\n\n// 1. We define the schema for the registration form's data\nconst userSchema = z.object({\n  email: z.string().email('Invalid email'),\n  password: z.string().min(8, 'The password must be at least 8 characters'),\n  age: z.number().int().min(18, 'The age must be 18 or over').optional(),\n});\n\n// 2. The data entered by the user\nconst userInput = {\n  email: 'user@example', // Error: an invalid email\n  password: '123',        // Error: the password is too short\n};\n\n// 3. The validation attempt\nconst result = userSchema.safeParse(userInput);\n\nif (!result.success) {\n  // 4. Handling the errors\n  console.log(result.error.errors);\n  // Prints an array of objects describing the errors for each field\n  // This can be shown in the interface\n} else {\n  // The data is valid and can be sent to the server\n  const validData = result.data;\n  console.log('The data passed validation:', validData);\n}",
  },
  1947: {
    source: "Какие проблемы у default export?",
    question: "What problems does a default export have?",
    shortAnswer: "A default export creates a single \"main\" entity for a module, which can lead to renaming problems on import and degrades automatic refactoring. The importing side can give it any name, which reduces the clarity and consistency of the code. It also complicates re-exporting and the analysis of dependencies by tooling.",
    longAnswer: "A default export is ES6 module syntax that lets you export one \"main\" entity from a module. Although this seems convenient, the approach has several significant drawbacks that affect the maintainability and clarity of the code.\n\nProblems with renaming and clarity\nWhen importing a default export, a developer can give it any name. This leads to inconsistency in the codebase, where the same component may be called differently in different files, which makes reading and searching harder.\n\nDifficulties with refactoring and analysis\nStatic-analysis tools (an IDE's rename feature, for example) work less well with default exports, because the link between the export and the import is less explicit. Re-exporting a default export through barrel files (index.js) also requires special syntax and is less intuitive.\n\nProblems with the dependency tree\nSome tools, such as Webpack, can apply more aggressive removal of unused code (tree shaking) to named exports, because they are listed explicitly. With a default export, which is often one large object, this can work less effectively.\n\nConclusion\nUsing a default export is justified when a module really does represent a single logical entity — a class or a React component in its own file, for example — and you want to emphasize that. For libraries, utilities and large projects, however, named exports are preferable, since they improve clarity, simplify refactoring and encourage better tree shaking.",
    codeExample: "// module.js\nexport default function MyComponent() {}\n\n// fileA.js\nimport Component from './module'; // The name 'Component'\n\n// fileB.js\nimport MySuperComponent from './module'; // The name 'MySuperComponent'\n\n// The re-export problem\nexport { default as MyComponent } from './MyComponent'; // Special syntax\n// With a named export it would be simpler:\n// export { MyComponent } from './MyComponent';",
  },
  1948: {
    source: "Как JSON-копирование влияет на TypeScript?",
    question: "How does JSON copying affect TypeScript?",
    shortAnswer: "Copying an object with JSON.stringify and JSON.parse creates a deep copy but loses all TypeScript type information. Every property becomes of type any or unknown unless you state it explicitly. Class methods, symbols, undefined values and circular references are also lost. It is a simple way of deep-copying simple data, but a dangerous one for complex objects with methods.",
    longAnswer: "In TypeScript types exist only at compile time and are \"erased\" when the code runs. The `JSON.stringify()` and `JSON.parse()` methods work with values at runtime, taking no account of TypeScript's type system.\n\nWhat happens to the types?\nAfter deserialization TypeScript sees the object as an ordinary object literal. If you do not provide an explicit type annotation or type assertion, the compiler will infer a wide type based on the structure of the JSON, which can lead to typing errors.\n\nA practical example and what is lost\nConsider a class and copying it:\n\nHow do you preserve the typing?\nSo that TypeScript understands the type correctly after copying, you need to state it explicitly:\n- Use a type assertion: `const copy = JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(orig)) as User;` (careful, this can be unsafe).\n- Create a constructor or factory method that takes an object and returns an instance of the class.\n- Use serialization/deserialization libraries with type support (class-transformer, for example).\n\nConclusion\nJSON copying is a quick way of deep-copying simple data objects (POJOs), but it ignores TypeScript's type system entirely and loses meta-information (methods, prototypes). Use it for data without behaviour, and for complex objects with methods apply other cloning methods or restore the types explicitly after deserialization.",
    codeExample: "class User {\n  constructor(public name: string, public age: number) {}\n  greet() { return `Hello, ${this.name}`; }\n}\n\nconst original = new User('Alice', 30);\n// TypeScript knows that original is an instance of User\n\nconst jsonCopy: any = JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(original));\n// jsonCopy is now a plain object { name: 'Alice', age: 30 }\n// Type: any (we stated it explicitly, but unknown could have been used)\n// The greet() method is lost!\n// console.log(jsonCopy.greet()); // Runtime error: greet is not a function\n\n// Without an annotation TypeScript will infer the type { name: string; age: number; }\nconst inferredCopy = JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(original));\n// inferredCopy has the type { name: string; age: number; }, not User.",
  },
  1949: {
    source: "Что должно находиться в слое Shared?",
    question: "What should go in the Shared layer?",
    shortAnswer: "The Shared layer (or common layer) contains code that is used by several other layers of the application but does not belong directly to any of them. It usually holds shared utilities, constants, base types (DTOs, models), repository and service interfaces, and infrastructure-related code that does not depend on business logic. The main rule is that the Shared layer must not depend on outer layers such as the UI or the infrastructure, in order to avoid circular dependencies and ensure reuse.",
    longAnswer: "In a layered architecture such as Clean Architecture or Onion Architecture, the Shared layer (sometimes called Core or Domain) plays a key role in organizing dependencies. It serves as a central hub containing the elements common to several layers, which lets you avoid code duplication and violations of the Dependency Inversion principle.\n\nWhat usually lives in the Shared layer?\n\n- Shared data models (DTOs, Entities, Value Objects): base classes or interfaces describing the structure of the data that is passed between layers.\n- Repository and service interfaces: abstractions defining the contracts for working with data or external services. Their implementations will live in the infrastructure layer.\n- Utilities and helpers: shared functions such as validators, parsers and mathematical calculations that do not depend on any particular business logic.\n- Constants and enums: values common to the whole application — error codes, order statuses, for example.\n- Cross-cutting concerns: base implementations for logging, exception handling and caching, provided they are abstracted away from specific frameworks.\n\nAn example structure and code\nConsider a simple example in TypeScript for an e-commerce application. In the Shared layer we can define a repository interface and a base product model.\n\nRules and restrictions\nIt is critically important that the Shared layer does not depend on outer layers such as the UI (React components, for example), the infrastructure (databases, HTTP clients) or frameworks. This prevents circular dependencies and makes the code more testable and reusable. For example, the `IProductRepository` interface is declared in Shared, while its implementation `MongoProductRepository` lives in the infrastructure layer, which depends on Shared.\n\nConclusion: the Shared layer should be used to hold code that is common and independent of specific implementation details. This increases consistency, reduces duplication and makes the application easier to maintain, especially on large projects with a clear separation of responsibilities.",
    codeExample: "// shared/interfaces/IProductRepository.ts\nexport interface IProductRepository {\n  findById(id: string): Promise<Product | null>;\n  findAll(): Promise<Product[]>;\n  save(product: Product): Promise<void>;\n}\n\n// shared/models/Product.ts\nexport class Product {\n  constructor(\n    public id: string,\n    public name: string,\n    public price: number,\n    public category: string\n  ) {}\n\n  // A shared method that can be used in different layers\n  applyDiscount(percent: number): void {\n    if (percent > 0 && percent <= 100) {\n      this.price = this.price * (1 - percent / 100);\n    }\n  }\n}\n\n// shared/utils/validation.ts\nexport function isValidEmail(email: string): boolean {\n  const regex = /^[^\\s@]+@[^\\s@]+\\.[^\\s@]+$/;\n  return regex.test(email);\n}",
  },
  1950: {
    source: "В чем разница между ValueType и ReferenceType?",
    question: "What is the difference between a ValueType and a ReferenceType?",
    shortAnswer: "A ValueType stores its data directly in the variable, while a ReferenceType stores a reference to data in memory. Assigning a ValueType creates a copy of the value, whereas assigning a ReferenceType copies only the reference, and both variables then point at the same data. ValueTypes are usually placed on the stack, which makes operations on them fast, while ReferenceTypes are placed on the heap, which lets you work with large, dynamic data structures.",
    longAnswer: "In programming languages such as C#, Java or TypeScript, all data types fall into two main categories: value types (ValueType) and reference types (ReferenceType). This division determines how data is stored in memory and how it is passed between variables and methods.\n\nStorage in memory\nValue types store their data directly in the area of memory allocated for the variable. This is usually the stack — a fast, ordered area of memory managed automatically. Examples are the primitive types: int, bool, float, and also structures (struct in C#).\n\nReference types store in the variable not the data itself but the address (the reference) of the area of memory on the heap where that data lives. The heap is a dynamic area of memory managed by the garbage collector. Examples are classes, arrays and strings (in some languages).\n\nBehaviour on assignment and when passing\nThe key difference shows up when copying:\n- When a value-type variable is assigned to another variable, the value itself is copied. After that the two variables are completely independent.\n- When a reference-type variable is assigned, only the reference (the address) is copied. Both variables then point at the same object on the heap. Changes made through one variable will be visible through the other.\n\nAn example in C#\n\nWhere it is applied\nValue types are ideal for small, frequently used data where performance and copy semantics matter (the coordinates of a point, sums of money, for example). Reference types are used for large objects that can change dynamically and should be passed by reference for efficiency (lists of users, an application's configuration, for example).\n\nConclusion: use value types for simple, indivisible data where independent copying is needed. Use reference types for complex objects where shared state and efficient memory management are necessary.",
    codeExample: "// A ValueType example (a structure)\nint a = 10;\nint b = a; // The value 10 is copied\nb = 20;\nConsole.WriteLine(a); // Output: 10 (a has not changed)\n\n// A ReferenceType example (a class)\nclass Person { public string Name; }\nPerson p1 = new Person { Name = \"Alice\" };\nPerson p2 = p1; // The reference is copied, p2 points at the same object\np2.Name = \"Bob\";\nConsole.WriteLine(p1.Name); // Output: Bob (the object was changed through p2)",
  },
  1951: {
    source: "Чем generics лучше использования object?",
    question: "Why are generics better than using object?",
    shortAnswer: "Generics let you create components that work with different types while preserving type information. Using object erases that information, making the code less type-safe. With generics the compiler can check types at compile time, preventing errors. It also improves autocompletion in the IDE and the documentation of the code. Generics make code more expressive and reusable without losing type safety.",
    longAnswer: "Generics are a mechanism that lets you create functions, classes or interfaces that can work with various data types while preserving information about the concrete type. The key difference from using the `object` type lies in preserving type safety and type information throughout the life cycle of the code.\n\nThe problem with using object\nThe `object` type in TypeScript represents any non-primitive value (everything except number, string, boolean, symbol, null and undefined). When a function accepts or returns `object`, the information about that object's concrete structure is lost. This forces you to use type assertions or runtime checks, which weakens static type checking.\n\nThe solution with generics\nGenerics let you parameterize the type, creating a \"template\" that will be filled in with a concrete type at the call site. The compiler tracks that type, providing compile-time checking.\n\nThe key advantages of generics over object\n\n- Type safety: the compiler prevents operations on incompatible types, reducing runtime errors.\n- Preserved type information: no type casting is required, and the code becomes cleaner and more reliable.\n- Reusability: one generic component can work with many types, avoiding code duplication.\n- Better IDE support: development tools provide accurate autocompletion and hints based on the concrete type.\n- Stricter contracts: you can impose constraints on generic parameters (`T extends SomeInterface`, for example), guaranteeing that particular properties or methods are present.\n\nPractical use\nGenerics are widely used in libraries and frameworks. For example, in React for typing component props and state, in data structures (maps, sets), when working with promises (`Promise<T>`) or in utility functions such as `pick` and `omit`.\n\nConclusion: generics should be applied whenever you need to create a reusable component that has to work with different types while preserving full type safety. Using `object` is justified only in rare cases where the type really is unknown or unimportant (when working with untyped external libraries, for example), and even then `unknown` is often the better choice.",
    codeExample: "// An example with object: type safety is lost\nfunction getFirstElement(arr: object[]): object {\n    return arr[0];\n}\nconst numbers = [1, 2, 3];\nconst first = getFirstElement(numbers); // The type of first is object\n// console.log(first.toFixed(1)); // Compile error: the property toFixed does not exist on the type object\n// A type cast is required: (first as number).toFixed(1)\n\n// The same example with generics\nfunction getFirstElementGeneric<T>(arr: T[]): T {\n    return arr[0];\n}\nconst numbers = [1, 2, 3];\nconst firstNum = getFirstElementGeneric(numbers); // The type of firstNum is inferred as number\nconsole.log(firstNum.toFixed(1)); // OK, the compiler knows this is a number\n\nconst strings = [\"a\", \"b\", \"c\"];\nconst firstStr = getFirstElementGeneric(strings); // The type of firstStr is string\nconsole.log(firstStr.toUpperCase()); // OK\n\n// An example with a constraint\ninterface HasId {\n    id: number;\n}\nfunction getById<T extends HasId>(items: T[], id: number): T | undefined {\n    return items.find(item => item.id === id);\n}\n// The compiler guarantees that the array's elements have an id property.",
  },
  1952: {
    source: "Какие проблемы возникают при работе с null?",
    question: "What problems arise when working with null?",
    shortAnswer: "Null represents the absence of a value, but using it often leads to runtime errors such as NullPointerException. It violates the principles of type safety, because a variable can be either an object or null. The code becomes overloaded with null checks, which makes it harder to read and maintain. Some languages, Kotlin for example, have introduced nullable types to solve this problem.",
    longAnswer: "The concept of null was introduced in programming to denote the absence of a value for a reference or pointer. Although this seems convenient, its widespread use gives rise to a number of serious problems that can crash applications and make debugging difficult.\n\nThe main problems with null\n\n- Runtime errors (NullPointerException/NPE): the most frequent problem is trying to call a method or access a property on a null reference, which crashes the program immediately.\n- Violation of type safety: if a language lets any reference be null, then the type system cannot guarantee that a variable contains a valid object, which weakens static analysis.\n- Code polluted with checks: developers are forced to add checks constantly (if (obj != null)), which increases the volume of code, makes it less readable and can mask logical errors.\n- Ambiguous meaning: null can mean different things — \"the value was not found\", \"the value has not been initialized yet\" or \"the value is deliberately absent\" — which makes code harder to understand.\n\nA code example in Java\n\nApproaches to a solution\nModern languages and paradigms offer alternatives. For example, using the Optional pattern (Java, TypeScript) or built-in nullable types (Kotlin, TypeScript with strictNullChecks). These approaches force the developer to handle the case of a missing value explicitly at the type level, which prevents many errors.\n\nConclusion: problems with null are a classic source of errors. Using language features (Optional, nullable types) or strict code conventions helps make a program more predictable and robust. Knowing about these problems matters especially when designing APIs and the public methods of libraries.",
    codeExample: "public class UserService {\n    public String getUserName(User user) {\n        // A potential problem: if user == null, the getProfile() call will fail.\n        Profile profile = user.getProfile();\n        return profile.getName(); // And here too, if profile == null.\n    }\n    // Safer but cluttered code:\n    public String getUserNameSafe(User user) {\n        if (user != null) {\n            Profile profile = user.getProfile();\n            if (profile != null) {\n                return profile.getName();\n            }\n        }\n        return \"Unknown\";\n    }\n}",
  },
  1953: {
    source: "Используется ли Swagger для взаимодействия между сервисами?",
    question: "Is Swagger used for communication between services?",
    shortAnswer: "Swagger (OpenAPI) is a tool for describing, documenting and testing RESTful APIs. By itself it is not used for the actual communication between services, but it provides an API specification from which client code, server stubs and documentation can be generated automatically. That specification serves as a contract that makes integrating services easier. Developers use the generated code for API calls, which makes the interaction more reliable and less error-prone.",
    longAnswer: "Swagger, now more often called OpenAPI, is a specification for describing RESTful APIs in JSON or YAML format. Its main purpose is to create a machine-readable API contract that describes in detail all the endpoints, request parameters, response formats, data models and authentication methods.\n\nHow does Swagger help with communication between services?\nAlthough Swagger is not itself a transport protocol (like HTTP or gRPC), it plays a key role in inter-service communication in a microservice architecture. The OpenAPI specification serves as the single source of truth for an API. When service A has to call service B, service B's team provides an up-to-date OpenAPI specification. Service A's team can use that specification to generate client code automatically in the programming language they need. The generated code encapsulates all the details of HTTP requests and of serializing and deserializing data, which simplifies and speeds up integration considerably.\n\nA practical example of use\nConsider a scenario where a user service (`UserService`) provides an API for getting information about a user. Its OpenAPI specification might look like this:\n\nAn order service (`OrderService`) that needs the user's data can use a tool such as `openapi-generator` to create a client for `UserService` in TypeScript:\n\nThis approach removes the need to write HTTP clients and JSON parsing by hand, reducing the chance of errors caused by mismatched types or formats.\n\nWhere else is OpenAPI applied?\n\n - Documentation: tools such as Swagger UI or ReDoc create interactive documentation from the specification automatically.\n - Mocking and testing: from the specification you can generate a mock server for testing clients before the backend is implemented.\n - Validation: middleware (in Express.js, for example) can use the specification to validate incoming requests.\n - Server code generation: you can generate the skeleton of the server code (controllers, models), which speeds development up.\n\nConclusion: Swagger (OpenAPI) is not a transport mechanism for communication, but it is critically important as a design and automation tool. It is worth applying in projects with several services in order to ensure clear contracts, reduce manual work and increase the reliability of integrations.",
    codeExample: "openapi: 3.0.3\ninfo:\n  title: User Service API\n  version: 1.0.0\npaths:\n  /users/{userId}:\n    get:\n      summary: Get user by ID\n      parameters:\n        - name: userId\n          in: path\n          required: true\n          schema:\n            type: integer\n      responses:\n        '200':\n          description: OK\n          content:\n            application/json:\n              schema:\n                $ref: '#/components/schemas/User'\ncomponents:\n  schemas:\n    User:\n      type: object\n      properties:\n        id:\n          type: integer\n        name:\n          type: string\n        email:\n          type: string\n\n// The generated client code (an example)\nimport { UserApi } from './generated-client';\n\nconst userApi = new UserApi();\nasync function getOrderWithUser(orderId: number, userId: number) {\n  try {\n    const user = await userApi.getUserById(userId); // A call through the generated client\n    console.log(`Order ${orderId} belongs to ${user.name}`);\n  } catch (error) {\n    console.error('Failed to fetch user:', error);\n  }\n}",
  },
  1954: {
    source: "Можно ли добавлять реализацию методов в интерфейс и зачем?",
    question: "Can you add method implementations to an interface, and why would you?",
    shortAnswer: "In the classic understanding an interface contains only method signatures without their implementations. In modern languages such as Java (from version 8) and C# (from 8.0), however, method implementations can be added to interfaces using the 'default' keyword (Java) or with modifiers (C#). This lets you extend interfaces by adding new functionality without breaking the existing code of the classes that implement them. The approach helps APIs evolve and reduces the need for abstract classes that exist only to provide a shared implementation.",
    longAnswer: "Traditionally an interface in object-oriented programming defines a contract — the set of methods a class must implement — without providing any implementation. This provides polymorphism and abstraction, letting different classes share a common interface but have different behaviour.\n\nThe evolution of interfaces\nAs programming languages developed, a need for more flexible constructs appeared. For example, when a new method was added to an interface, every existing class implementing it would have to provide an implementation, which broke backward compatibility. To solve this problem, default methods were introduced in Java 8, and in C# 8.0 methods with an implementation in interfaces (using modifiers such as an explicit `virtual` and `override`).\n\nHow it works\nA default method in an interface has a body and is marked with the `default` keyword (in Java) or can be declared as `virtual` with an implementation (in C#). A class implementing the interface can use that default implementation or override it if different behaviour is required.\n\nPractical use\nThis capability is especially useful when developing libraries and frameworks, where interfaces can evolve without breaking existing code. For example, in the Java Collections Framework the `stream()` and `forEach()` methods were added to the collection interfaces as default methods, which made it possible to introduce functional capabilities without requiring changes in all the implementing classes.\n\nIt is important, however, not to abuse this capability and turn interfaces into something resembling abstract classes. Interfaces should still define a contract first and foremost, and any shared logic should be minimal and non-functional (helper methods, for example).\n\nConclusion: adding method implementations to interfaces is a powerful tool for ensuring backward compatibility and improving an API gradually. It is worth applying when you need to extend an interface's functionality without breaking existing implementations, but the main business logic is better left in classes.",
    codeExample: "// An example in Java\ninterface Vehicle {\n    void start(); // An abstract method\n    \n    default void honk() { // A default method\n        System.out.println(\"Beep beep!\");\n    }\n}\n\nclass Car implements Vehicle {\n    @Override\n    public void start() {\n        System.out.println(\"Car started\");\n    }\n    // The honk() method is inherited with its default implementation\n}",
  },
  1955: {
    source: "Что такое dependency injection?",
    question: "What is dependency injection?",
    shortAnswer: "Dependency injection (DI) is a design pattern in which an object's dependencies (services or other objects, for example) are supplied to it from outside rather than created inside the object itself. This makes code more flexible and easy to test, because the dependencies can be replaced with mocks. DI is often implemented through the constructor, through properties or through methods. It is a key part of many modern frameworks such as Angular, Spring and NestJS.",
    longAnswer: "Dependency injection (DI) is a design pattern that implements the principle of Inversion of Control (IoC). Its core idea is that an object should not create its own dependencies but receive them ready-made from outside. This separates the creation of objects from their use, which makes the system more modular and easier to test.\n\nHow it works\nInstead of a class creating instances of the services it needs, those dependencies are \"injected\" into it through the constructor, through properties (setters) or through methods. Constructor injection is the most widespread and the recommended way, since it makes the dependencies explicit and guarantees that the object is created in a valid state.\n\nAn example without DI and with DI\nConsider an example in TypeScript. Without DI the `UserService` class creates the repository instance itself, which creates tight coupling and complicates testing.\n\nWith DI the dependency is supplied from outside:\n\nDI containers\nIn large applications creating and passing dependencies by hand becomes cumbersome. DI containers (or IoC containers) are used to automate this process. The container knows how to create objects and resolve their dependencies, often from configuration or metadata (decorators in TypeScript, for example).\n\nWhere and why it is applied\n\n - Testing: it is easy to substitute a real dependency (a database, for example) with a mock or stub.\n - Flexibility and maintenance: replacing a dependency's implementation (changing the library for sending email, for example) requires changes in only one place — the container's configuration.\n - Following the SOLID principles: DI directly supports the Dependency Inversion Principle.\n - Architecture: it is the foundation of many modern frameworks (Angular, Spring, ASP.NET Core, NestJS).\n\nConclusion: dependency injection is worth applying in projects of medium and large complexity where testability, architectural flexibility and ease of maintenance matter. It is especially useful in server applications and large client-side SPAs.",
    codeExample: "// WITHOUT DI: bad practice\nclass UserService {\n    private userRepository: UserRepository;\n    constructor() {\n        this.userRepository = new UserRepository(); // The dependency is created inside\n    }\n    getUser(id: number) {\n        return this.userRepository.find(id);\n    }\n}\n\n// WITH DI: good practice\nclass UserService {\n    constructor(private userRepository: UserRepository) {}\n    // The dependency is injected through the constructor\n    getUser(id: number) {\n        return this.userRepository.find(id);\n    }\n}\n\n// Somewhere in the application's code we create the dependencies and \"assemble\" the object\nconst repo = new UserRepository();\nconst service = new UserService(repo); // We inject the dependency\n\n// An example of using the Injectable decorator in NestJS\nimport { Injectable } from '@nestjs/common';\nimport { UserRepository } from './user.repository';\n\n@Injectable() // The decorator marks the class as managed by the container\nclass UserService {\n    constructor(private userRepository: UserRepository) {}\n}\n\n// The NestJS container will create UserRepository automatically and inject it into UserService.",
  },
  1956: {
    source: "Когда следует применять паттерн Strategy?",
    question: "When should the Strategy pattern be applied?",
    shortAnswer: "The Strategy pattern should be applied when you have several related algorithms or variants of behaviour and need to choose between them while the program runs. It is useful when a class contains a lot of conditional statements for selecting behaviour. The pattern encapsulates each algorithm in its own class, making them interchangeable. This makes it easy to add new algorithms without changing the existing client code. Strategy helps you observe SOLID principles such as open/closed and single responsibility.",
    longAnswer: "The Strategy pattern is a behavioural design pattern that defines a family of algorithms, encapsulates each of them and makes them interchangeable. This lets you choose the algorithm you need while the program is running rather than at compile time.\n\nThe core idea and its uses\nThe main goal is to separate the algorithm from the object that uses it. This is especially useful in systems where an object's behaviour has to change dynamically depending on the context or on user input. For example, a navigation application may use different route-building algorithms (walking, driving, public transport) that can be switched at the user's wish.\n\nA practical example\nConsider a simple example of a payment system that supports different methods (CreditCard, PayPal, Crypto). Instead of piling conditional statements into the order class, we factor out strategies.\n\nThe key advantages\n\n- Getting rid of conditional statements: it removes long switch/case or if/else blocks.\n- Flexibility and extensibility: new algorithms are added easily without modifying the existing context code.\n- Observing SOLID: each strategy has a single responsibility, and the context is open for extension.\n- Testability: strategies can be tested in isolation.\n\nConclusion: the Strategy pattern is worth applying when a system contains several variations of one algorithm or behaviour that can change depending on external conditions, and you want to keep the code clean, flexible and easy to maintain. It is a perfect fit for scenarios where rules or logic are added or changed often.",
    codeExample: "// The strategy interface\ninterface PaymentStrategy {\n    pay(amount: number): void;\n}\n\n// Concrete strategies\nclass CreditCardPayment implements PaymentStrategy {\n    pay(amount: number) {\n        console.log(`Paying ${amount} using Credit Card.`);\n        // Card processing logic\n    }\n}\n\nclass PayPalPayment implements PaymentStrategy {\n    pay(amount: number) {\n        console.log(`Paying ${amount} using PayPal.`);\n        // PayPal API logic\n    }\n}\n\n// The context that uses the strategy\nclass ShoppingCart {\n    private strategy: PaymentStrategy;\n\n    constructor(strategy: PaymentStrategy) {\n        this.strategy = strategy;\n    }\n\n    setPaymentStrategy(strategy: PaymentStrategy) {\n        this.strategy = strategy; // Changing the strategy dynamically\n    }\n\n    checkout(amount: number) {\n        this.strategy.pay(amount);\n    }\n}\n\n// Use\nconst cart = new ShoppingCart(new CreditCardPayment());\ncart.checkout(100); // Paying 100 using Credit Card.\n\ncart.setPaymentStrategy(new PayPalPayment());\ncart.checkout(200); // Paying 200 using PayPal.",
  },
  1957: {
    source: "Как обрабатывать отсутствие ключей в входных данных?",
    question: "How do you handle missing keys in input data?",
    shortAnswer: "Missing keys in data are a frequent problem, especially when working with APIs or user input. The main approaches include checking whether a key exists before accessing it, using the optional chaining (?.) and nullish coalescing (??) operators in JavaScript/TypeScript, and setting default values. It is also important to validate data at an early stage so as to avoid errors deep inside the code. These methods make code more reliable and predictable.",
    longAnswer: "When receiving data from external sources such as an API, configuration files or user forms, you can never be completely sure of its structure. A key you expect to find on an object may be absent, which leads to an error of the kind `Cannot read property '...' of undefined` and possibly to the application crashing. Handling missing keys is therefore a fundamental skill for building robust software.\n\nThe main handling strategies\nThere are several widespread approaches, which can be combined:\n- Checking whether a key exists: an explicit check with a condition before accessing nested properties.\n- Optional chaining (?.): a modern operator that lets you access the properties of deeply nested objects safely without raising an error if some intermediate property equals `null` or `undefined`.\n- Nullish coalescing (??): an operator that returns the right-hand operand only if the left-hand one equals `null` or `undefined`. It is ideal for setting default values.\n- Default parameters: in functions you can set default values for parameters, which is especially useful when destructuring objects.\n- Validating data on input: using validation libraries (Joi, Yup, Zod, for example) or writing your own checks to make sure the data matches the expected schema before using it in business logic.\n\nCode examples in JavaScript\nConsider a user object that may arrive from an API:\n\nWhere this is applied\nThese techniques are universal and used everywhere data is involved: frontend applications handling server responses; backend services parsing incoming HTTP requests; scripts that process configuration files. They matter especially in a microservice architecture, where services talk to one another and the format of the data can change.\n\nConclusion: handling missing keys is a mandatory practice for writing error-resistant code. Use optional chaining and default values for brevity and readability, and for critically important data supplement them with strict validation at the boundaries of the system.",
    codeExample: "// The input data: the object may be incomplete\nconst apiResponse = {\n    id: 123,\n    // The 'profile' property may be absent\n};\n\n// 1. The classic check (verbose but reliable)\nlet userName1 = 'Guest';\nif (apiResponse.profile && apiResponse.profile.name) {\n    userName1 = apiResponse.profile.name;\n}\n\n// 2. Using optional chaining and nullish coalescing (the modern way)\nconst userName2 = apiResponse?.profile?.name ?? 'Guest';\n\n// 3. Destructuring with default parameters\nfunction getUserInfo({ profile = {} } = {}) {\n    const { name = 'Guest' } = profile;\n    return name;\n}\nconst userName3 = getUserInfo(apiResponse);\n\nconsole.log(userName1, userName2, userName3); // All will print 'Guest'",
  },
  1958: {
    source: "Как компилятор понимает, что интерфейс является функциональным?",
    question: "How does the compiler know that an interface is functional?",
    shortAnswer: "The compiler considers an interface functional if it contains exactly one abstract method (a Single Abstract Method — SAM). Such an interface can be implemented with a lambda expression or a method reference. The @FunctionalInterface annotation helps the compiler check this condition at compile time, but it is not required in order to create a functional interface.",
    longAnswer: "In languages that support functional programming (Java from version 8, for example) the key notion is the functional interface. This is an interface that contains exactly one abstract method. It is precisely the presence of only one abstract method that lets the compiler \"understand\" that this interface can be implemented with a compact lambda expression rather than through a cumbersome anonymous class.\n\nThe criteria for a functional interface\nThe compiler checks the following conditions:\n- The interface must contain exactly one abstract method (a method without an implementation).\n- The interface may, however, contain any number of default methods with an implementation.\n- The interface may contain any number of static methods.\n- Methods inherited from the `Object` class (`toString`, `equals`, for example) are not counted as abstract.\n\nThe role of the @FunctionalInterface annotation\nThe `@FunctionalInterface` annotation is optional but extremely useful. It serves two main functions:\n- A compile-time check: if a developer marks an interface with this annotation but the interface does not satisfy the SAM criterion, the compiler reports an error. This prevents accidental changes that could break the functionality of lambda expressions.\n- Documenting the code: the annotation indicates the interface's purpose explicitly to other developers.\n\nCode examples\nConsider an example of a correct functional interface:\n\nAn example of an interface that is NOT functional (it will cause a compile error with the annotation):\n\nWhere functional interfaces are applied\nFunctional interfaces are widely used in modern Java code:\n- In the Streams API for the `filter`, `map` and `reduce` operations.\n- In concurrency with `ExecutorService` and `CompletableFuture`.\n- In event handling and in libraries such as JavaFX.\n- To create clean, declarative and reusable blocks of behaviour.\n\nConclusion: a functional interface should be used when you need to define a single action or predicate that will often be implemented through lambda expressions. It is the basis for writing concise, expressive code in the functional programming paradigm in Java.",
    codeExample: "// The annotation helps the compiler check the SAM condition\n@FunctionalInterface\ninterface StringProcessor {\n    // The single abstract method\n    String process(String input);\n    \n    // A default method — allowed\n    default void log(String msg) {\n        System.out.println(\"LOG: \" + msg);\n    }\n    \n    // A static method — allowed\n    static boolean isEmpty(String s) {\n        return s == null || s.trim().isEmpty();\n    }\n}\n\n// Use with a lambda\nStringProcessor toUpper = (str) -> str.toUpperCase();\nSystem.out.println(toUpper.process(\"hello\")); // Prints: HELLO\n\n@FunctionalInterface // COMPILE ERROR: several abstract methods\ninterface InvalidProcessor {\n    void methodA();\n    void methodB(); // The second abstract method violates the SAM condition\n}",
  },
  1959: {
    source: "Что такое реактивное программирование?",
    question: "What is reactive programming?",
    shortAnswer: "Reactive programming is a paradigm oriented towards working with streams of data and the automatic propagation of change. Instead of explicitly requesting new data, you declare how the system should react when it arrives. The key concepts are the Observable (an observable stream), the Observer and the operators for transforming them. It is especially useful for handling events, HTTP requests or user input, where data arrives asynchronously.",
    longAnswer: "Reactive programming is a style of programming in which the main focus is on streams of data and the automatic propagation of change. Instead of the imperative approach, where you tell the computer how to do things step by step, you describe declaratively the relationships between the various sources of data (the streams) and how the application should react to them.\n\nThe key concepts\nThe main building blocks are:\n- Observable: represents a stream of data or events that can be emitted over time. This may be a single value, many values or even an error.\n- Observer: an object that subscribes to an Observable and reacts to the data that arrives (next), to errors (error) or to the signal that the stream has finished (complete).\n- Operators: pure functions (such as map, filter and merge) that let you transform, combine and manipulate streams, creating new Observables.\n\nWhere is this applied?\nThis paradigm is a perfect fit for scenarios with asynchronous, event-driven data:\n- Handling user events (clicks, keyboard input).\n- Working with HTTP requests and WebSocket messages.\n- Managing state in complex frontend applications.\n- Processing data in real time, in dashboards or chats for example.\n\nA code example (RxJS)\nConsider a simple example of handling user input with a delay (debounce) for a search.\n\nIn this example we created a reactive stream out of input events. The `debounceTime` and `distinctUntilChanged` operators help optimize performance by reducing the number of useless search operations.\n\nConclusion\nReactive programming is worth applying when your application relies heavily on asynchronous events or streams of data. It makes code more declarative and composable and simplifies the handling of complex scenarios such as cancelling requests, merging streams or handling errors. For simple synchronous tasks, however, it can add unnecessary complexity.",
    codeExample: "import { fromEvent } from 'rxjs';\nimport { debounceTime, map, distinctUntilChanged } from 'rxjs/operators';\n\n// We get a reference to the input field\nconst searchInput = document.getElementById('search');\n\n// We create an Observable out of the input events\nconst searchStream = fromEvent(searchInput, 'input').pipe(\n  // We turn the event into the field's value\n  map(event => event.target.value),\n  // We wait 300 ms after the last input\n  debounceTime(300),\n  // We ignore consecutive repeated values\n  distinctUntilChanged()\n);\n\n// We subscribe to the stream\nconst subscription = searchStream.subscribe(searchTerm => {\n  console.log('Searching for:', searchTerm);\n  // Here an HTTP request could be sent\n});\n\n// Later you can unsubscribe: subscription.unsubscribe();",
  },
  1960: {
    source: "Для чего применяются Utility Types?",
    question: "What are Utility Types used for?",
    shortAnswer: "Utility Types are generic types built into TypeScript that simplify transforming existing types. They let you create new types from ones you already have — for example, making all fields optional (Partial) or selecting only the ones you need (Pick). This reduces code duplication and improves readability.",
    longAnswer: "What are Utility Types?\nUtility Types are a set of generic types built into TypeScript that let you transform existing types easily and flexibly. They spare you from describing a new interface by hand for every variation of your data, which is especially useful when working with APIs, forms or application state.\n\nThe main Utility Types\n\n- Partial<T> — makes all properties of the type T optional.\n- Required<T> — makes all properties required.\n- Readonly<T> — makes all properties read-only.\n- Pick<T, K> — selects only the given keys K from T.\n- Omit<T, K> — excludes the given keys K from T.\n- Record<K, T> — creates an object type with the keys K and values of type T.\n\nAn example of use\n\nWhere they are applied\nUtility Types are widely used in real projects: for describing partial updates (PATCH requests), creating simplified representations of data (for lists, for example), working with Redux reducers or with component state. They help preserve type safety without excessive code duplication.\n\nConclusion: Utility Types are a powerful TypeScript tool for flexible work with types. They are worth applying wherever you need to create a variation of an existing type without writing a new interface, which speeds up development and reduces the number of errors.",
    codeExample: "interface User {\n  id: number;\n  name: string;\n  email: string;\n}\n\n// A partial update of a user\nfunction updateUser(id: number, updates: Partial<User>) {\n  // updates may contain only name, only email or both\n}\n\n// Selecting only the name and email for display\ntype UserPreview = Pick<User, 'name' | 'email'>;\n\n// Excluding id from the form's data\ntype UserForm = Omit<User, 'id'>;",
  },
  1961: {
    source: "Какие Utility Types использовались на практике (Pick, Omit, Partial и др.)?",
    question: "Which Utility Types have you used in practice (Pick, Omit, Partial and others)?",
    shortAnswer: "Utility Types are generic types built into TypeScript that simplify manipulating types. For example, Pick creates a type with the selected keys from T, Omit excludes the given keys, and Partial makes all fields optional. They help avoid code duplication and make types more flexible.",
    longAnswer: "What are Utility Types?\nUtility Types are a set of generic types built into TypeScript that let you create new types from existing ones easily. They solve typical type-transformation tasks without any need to write complex conditional constructs by hand.\n\nThe main Utility Types\n\n- Pick<T, K> — selects from the type T only those properties whose keys are listed in K. Useful when you need to take a subset of an object's fields.\n- Omit<T, K> — excludes from the type T the properties with keys from K. Convenient for removing fields, for example when creating a type without an identifier.\n- Partial<T> — makes all properties of the type T optional. Often used for updating part of an object.\n- Required<T> — makes all properties required, the opposite of Partial.\n- Readonly<T> — makes all properties read-only.\n- Record<K, T> — creates an object type with keys from K and values of type T.\n\nExamples of use\n\nWhere they are applied\nUtility Types are used actively in real projects for creating the types of API responses, forms, Redux state and component props. For example, when working with forms Partial is often applied to the form data's type, and when filtering fields — Pick or Omit.\n\nConclusion: Utility Types are an indispensable tool for writing clean, safe TypeScript code. They are worth applying wherever flexible work with types is required, in order to avoid duplication and improve readability.",
    codeExample: "interface User {\n  id: number;\n  name: string;\n  email: string;\n  age: number;\n}\n\n// Pick: only the name and email\ntype UserContact = Pick<User, 'name' | 'email'>;\n\n// Omit: everything except id\ntype UserWithoutId = Omit<User, 'id'>;\n\n// Partial: all fields optional for an update\ntype UpdateUser = Partial<User>;\n\n// Record: a dictionary of users by id\ntype UserMap = Record<number, User>;",
  },
  1962: {
    source: "Какие сложности возникают с optional полями в TypeScript?",
    question: "What difficulties come up with optional fields in TypeScript?",
    shortAnswer: "Optional fields in TypeScript are marked with a ? and may contain the value undefined. The main difficulty is the need to check that a value is present before using it, otherwise TypeScript reports an error. This helps avoid runtime errors but requires additional checks or the use of operators such as optional chaining.",
    longAnswer: "What are optional fields?\nIn TypeScript optional fields are an object's properties that may be absent or equal to undefined. They are marked with a question mark after the field's name, for example: `name?: string`. This means the field's type is `string | undefined`, but with additional behaviour: when accessing such a field TypeScript requires a check for undefined.\n\nThe main difficulties\n\n- The need for a check: when accessing an optional field without a check TypeScript reports an error if strictNullChecks is enabled. For example, `obj.name.toUpperCase()` will cause an error, because name may be undefined.\n- Optional chaining: to simplify this work the `?.` operator is used, which lets you access nested properties safely: `obj?.name?.toUpperCase()`.\n- Typing functions: when passing optional fields into functions you need to bear in mind that they may be undefined and handle that accordingly.\n\nA code example\n\nConclusion\nOptional fields are useful for describing properties that are not required, but they require careful handling of undefined. Use optional chaining and default values to avoid errors and make your code safer.",
    codeExample: "interface User {\n  name?: string;\n  age: number;\n}\n\nfunction greet(user: User) {\n  // Error: Object is possibly 'undefined'\n  // console.log(user.name.toUpperCase());\n  \n  // Correct: a check or optional chaining\n  console.log(user.name?.toUpperCase() ?? 'Anonymous');\n}\n\ngreet({ age: 25 }); // Prints 'Anonymous'\ngreet({ name: 'Alice', age: 30 }); // Prints 'ALICE'",
  },
  1963: {
    source: "Как получить ключи объекта по типу значений (например, только string)?",
    question: "How do you get an object's keys by the type of their values (only string, for example)?",
    shortAnswer: "In TypeScript you can obtain the keys of an object whose values have a particular type by using conditional types and key remapping. You create a type that iterates over the keys and checks whether the type of the value matches the one you want. If it does, the key stays; otherwise it is excluded. This lets you type functions that work with a subset of an object's properties strictly.",
    longAnswer: "The concept of filtering keys by the type of their values\nTypeScript provides powerful tools for manipulating types at the type-system level. One common task is to obtain only those keys of an object whose values have a particular type — only strings, for example. This is achieved with conditional types and the key-remapping syntax in mapped types.\n\nHow it works\nThe core idea is to create a type that iterates over all the keys of the source object and checks whether the type of the value at that key is the one required. If it is, the key is included in the result; otherwise it is discarded. The `as` construct in a mapped type is used for this, which lets you transform keys.\n\nIn this example the type `KeysOfType` takes two parameters: `T`, the source object, and `V`, the value type you are looking for. Inside the mapped type we use `as` to include the key conditionally: if `T[K] extends V`, the key stays, otherwise it becomes `never`. We then cast the result to a type and extract its keys with `keyof`.\n\nPractical use\nThis approach is useful for creating strictly typed functions that work only with particular properties of an object. For example, a function that extracts all the string values from an object:\n\nThe return type guarantees that the function will return only those properties that have a string type.\n\nConclusion\nFiltering keys by the type of their values is a powerful TypeScript tool for creating type-safe utilities. It is especially useful when working with dynamic data where strict type checking at compile time is required, which reduces the chance of errors at runtime.",
    codeExample: "type KeysOfType<T, V> = {\n  [K in keyof T as T[K] extends V ? K : never]: T[K]\n} extends infer R ? keyof R : never;\n\n// An example of use\ninterface User {\n  name: string;\n  age: number;\n  email: string;\n  isAdmin: boolean;\n}\n\ntype StringKeys = KeysOfType<User, string>; // \"name\" | \"email\"\n\nfunction getStringValues<T extends Record<string, any>>(obj: T): Pick<T, KeysOfType<T, string>> {\n  const result: any = {};\n  for (const key in obj) {\n    if (typeof obj[key] === 'string') {\n      result[key] = obj[key];\n    }\n  }\n  return result;\n}\n\nconst user: User = { name: \"Alice\", age: 30, email: \"alice@example.com\", isAdmin: false };\nconst strings = getStringValues(user); // { name: \"Alice\", email: \"alice@example.com\" }",
  },
  1964: {
    source: "Почему не стоит делать as unknown as Type?",
    question: "Why should you avoid as unknown as Type?",
    shortAnswer: "The construct as unknown as Type lets you bypass TypeScript's type system, which can lead to runtime errors. It erases type information and makes code less predictable. Instead you should use proper type checks or assertions with type narrowing.",
    longAnswer: "Why as unknown as Type is bad practice\nThe `as unknown as Type` construct in TypeScript lets you force any value to any type, ignoring the type system entirely. It is like using `any`, but with a false sense of safety, since the resulting type is stated explicitly.\n\nHow it works\nFirst the value is cast to `unknown` — the safest type, which forbids any operation. Then it is immediately cast to the required type `Type`. The compiler does not check whether the source and target types are compatible, so the error may only show up at runtime.\n\nWhen it may be justified\nIn rare cases — for example, when working with external data whose type is guaranteed to be known but cannot be expressed in TypeScript. Even then, however, it is better to use schema validation (Zod or io-ts, for example).\n\nConclusion\nAvoid `as unknown as Type` in production code. Always aim at checking types at runtime, or use assertions with type narrowing, in order to preserve safety and readability.",
    codeExample: "const data: unknown = JSON.parse('{\"name\": \"Alice\"}');\n\n// A dangerous cast\nconst user = data as unknown as { name: string; age: number };\nconsole.log(user.age); // undefined, but TypeScript thinks this is a number\n\n// The correct approach — a check\nif (typeof data === 'object' && data !== null && 'name' in data) {\n  const safeUser = data as { name: string };\n  console.log(safeUser.name);\n}",
  },
  1965: {
    source: "Как обрабатывать неизвестный тип ошибки (unknown) в TypeScript?",
    question: "How do you handle an error of unknown type in TypeScript?",
    shortAnswer: "The unknown type in TypeScript represents a value whose type is not known. To handle an error of type unknown you first have to narrow its type with checks such as instanceof or typeof. This guarantees safe access to the error's properties. Without narrowing, the compiler will not let you perform operations on an unknown.",
    longAnswer: "What unknown is and what it is for\nIn TypeScript the `unknown` type is a type-safe alternative to `any`. It represents a value whose type is not known at the time the code is written. Unlike `any`, you cannot perform arbitrary operations on an `unknown` without first checking its type. This is especially useful when handling errors in a `catch` block, where the error has the type `unknown` by default (from TypeScript 4.0).\n\nHow do you handle an unknown error?\nTo work with an `unknown` you need to narrow its type. The most widespread way is to use an `instanceof Error` check. If the error is an instance of the `Error` class, you can safely access the `message`, `name` and `stack` properties. If the error's type is unknown, you can check whether it is an object with a `message` field.\n\nPractical recommendations\n\n- Always use `unknown` rather than `any` in a `catch` block, for better type safety.\n- Create a custom guard function to check the error's structure if it has a complex shape.\n- Avoid casting through `as any` — it reduces safety.\n\nConclusion\nHandling `unknown` errors in TypeScript requires explicit type narrowing, which makes code more predictable and protects against runtime errors. This approach is recommended in every TypeScript project for handling exceptions.",
    codeExample: "try {\n  // code that may throw an error\n} catch (error: unknown) {\n  if (error instanceof Error) {\n    console.log(error.message);\n  } else if (typeof error === 'string') {\n    console.log(error);\n  } else if (error && typeof error === 'object' && 'message' in error) {\n    console.log((error as { message: string }).message);\n  } else {\n    console.log('Unknown error');\n  }\n}",
  },
  1966: {
    source: "Что происходит с оператором \"+\" после компиляции?",
    question: "What happens to the \"+\" operator after compilation?",
    shortAnswer: "After TypeScript is compiled to JavaScript the \"+\" operator behaves like the ordinary addition operator in JavaScript. It can perform both numeric addition and string concatenation depending on the types of the operands. TypeScript checks types at compile time but does not change the operator's behaviour.",
    longAnswer: "The behaviour of the \"+\" operator after TypeScript is compiled\nTypeScript is a layer on top of JavaScript, and after compilation all the code becomes ordinary JavaScript. The \"+\" operator in TypeScript is not overloaded and does not change its behaviour — it corresponds exactly to JavaScript's addition operator.\n\nHow the \"+\" operator works in JavaScript\nIn JavaScript the \"+\" operator performs two functions: numeric addition and string concatenation. If at least one of the operands is a string, both operands are converted to strings and joined. If both operands are numbers, mathematical addition is performed.\n\nType checking at compile time\nTypeScript checks that the types of the operands are compatible with the addition operation. For example, if you try to add a number and an object, TypeScript will report a compile error, even though in JavaScript it might work (with conversion to a string).\n\nConclusion\nAfter TypeScript is compiled the \"+\" operator works exactly as it does in JavaScript. TypeScript merely adds static type checking at compile time, preventing potential errors caused by unexpected type conversion. This makes code more predictable and safer.",
    codeExample: "// Examples in TypeScript\nlet a: number = 5;\nlet b: number = 10;\nlet result1 = a + b; // 15 (numeric addition)\n\nlet c: string = \"Hello\";\nlet d: string = \" World\";\nlet result2 = c + d; // \"Hello World\" (concatenation)\n\nlet mixed = a + c; // \"5Hello\" (the number is converted to a string)\n\n// An error in TypeScript\nlet obj = { value: 10 };\nlet num = 5;\nlet result = num + obj; // Error: Object is possibly 'undefined', or incompatible types",
  },
  1967: {
    source: "Что такое sealed classes?",
    question: "What are sealed classes?",
    shortAnswer: "Sealed classes in Kotlin are classes that restrict the inheritance hierarchy, allowing subclasses to be created only within the same file. They are used to represent a fixed set of types — states or the results of operations, for example. This makes code safer and more predictable, especially when used in when expressions, where the compiler checks that every variant has been handled.",
    longAnswer: "What are sealed classes?\nSealed classes are a special kind of class in Kotlin that lets you create a restricted hierarchy of types. This means that all the possible subclasses of a sealed class are known at compile time and must be declared in the same file as the sealed class itself. That makes them ideal for modelling states, the results of operations or any other situation where the set of variants is fixed.\n\nHow does it work?\nSealed classes are declared with the `sealed` keyword. Subclasses can be declared either inside the body of the sealed class or at the top level of the same file. The main advantage is that when a `when` expression is used the compiler checks that every possible variant has been handled, which rules out errors.\n\nWhere is it applied?\nSealed classes are widely used in Android architecture (for representing UI states, for example), in functional programming (for algebraic data types) and in any scenario where you need to guarantee that every possible variant is handled. They are also useful when working with network requests, where the result may be a success, an error or still loading.\n\nConclusion\nSealed classes are worth applying when you need to create a type with a fixed set of subclasses, in order to ensure type safety and avoid errors when handling all the possible variants. This makes code more readable and more maintainable.",
    codeExample: "sealed class Result {\n    data class Success(val data: String) : Result()\n    data class Error(val message: String) : Result()\n    object Loading : Result()\n}\n\nfun handleResult(result: Result) {\n    when (result) {\n        is Result.Success -> println(\"Data: ${result.data}\")\n        is Result.Error -> println(\"Error: ${result.message}\")\n        Result.Loading -> println(\"Loading...\")\n    }\n}",
  },
  1968: {
    source: "Для чего используются protected и private-поля?",
    question: "What are protected and private fields used for?",
    shortAnswer: "Access modifiers control the visibility of a class's fields and methods. Private makes a class member accessible only inside the class itself, hiding the implementation details. Protected opens access to the class and its descendants, letting them override the behaviour. This is the foundation of encapsulation, protecting data from incorrect use.",
    longAnswer: "The purpose of access modifiers\nIn object-oriented programming access modifiers (private, protected, public) determine which parts of the code can reach a class's fields and methods. They implement the principle of encapsulation — hiding an object's internal state and providing a controlled interface.\n\nPrivate\nA field or method with the private modifier is accessible only inside the same class. This lets you hide implementation details that should not be changed from outside — an internal counter or temporary data, for example.\n\nHere balance is private, so external code cannot change it directly, only through the deposit and getBalance methods.\n\nProtected\nProtected widens the access: the class's members are visible inside the class itself and in descendant classes. This is convenient when you need to let subclasses use or override behaviour while hiding it from the outside world.\n\nThe makeSound method is accessible in Dog but not from an instance of Animal or from external code.\n\nConclusion\nPrivate and protected are key tools for building a reliable architecture. Private protects internal data from accidental change, while protected lets you extend classes flexibly through inheritance while keeping control of the interface. Using them is mandatory in any serious project in order to support encapsulation and reduce coupling in the code.",
    codeExample: "class BankAccount {\n  private balance: number;\n\n  constructor(initial: number) {\n    this.balance = initial;\n  }\n\n  public deposit(amount: number): void {\n    if (amount > 0) {\n      this.balance += amount;\n    }\n  }\n\n  public getBalance(): number {\n    return this.balance;\n  }\n}\n\nclass Animal {\n  protected name: string;\n\n  constructor(name: string) {\n    this.name = name;\n  }\n\n  protected makeSound(): void {\n    console.log('Some sound');\n  }\n}\n\nclass Dog extends Animal {\n  public bark(): void {\n    this.makeSound(); // accessible, because it is protected\n    console.log('Woof!');\n  }\n}",
  },
  1969: {
    source: "Как поддерживать консистентность UI между несколькими проектами?",
    question: "How do you keep the UI consistent across several projects?",
    shortAnswer: "To keep the UI consistent across projects people create a single component library or design system. The components live in a separate package (in a monorepo, for example) and are added as a dependency. This guarantees that every project uses the same interface elements, styles and behaviour.",
    longAnswer: "What UI consistency is and why it is needed\nUI consistency means that the user interface looks and behaves the same way in every part of an application or across a company's different projects. It improves the user experience, speeds up development and makes code easier to maintain.\n\nThe main approaches to maintaining consistency\n\n- A design system — a set of rules, tokens (colours, spacing, fonts) and components described in documentation.\n- A component library — implemented UI components (buttons, inputs, modals) that can be reused.\n- A monorepo — keeping the code of several projects and the shared library in one repository to make synchronization easier.\n\nAn example implementation in React\n\nHow this works in practice\nThe design team creates mockups in Figma using the shared tokens. Developers implement the components in the library, which is published as an npm package or linked through a workspace in the monorepo. When the design changes, only the library is updated, and all the projects receive the changes once the dependency is updated.\n\nConclusion\nUsing a design system and a component library is the standard way of maintaining UI consistency across several projects. It reduces code duplication, speeds up development and ensures a single user experience.",
    codeExample: "// packages/ui/src/Button.tsx\nimport React from 'react';\nimport './Button.css';\n\ninterface ButtonProps {\n  variant: 'primary' | 'secondary';\n  children: React.ReactNode;\n}\n\nexport const Button: React.FC<ButtonProps> = ({ variant, children }) => {\n  return <button className={`btn btn-${variant}`}>{children}</button>;\n};\n\n// packages/app1/src/App.tsx\nimport { Button } from '@company/ui';\n\nfunction App() {\n  return <Button variant=\"primary\">Click me</Button>;\n}",
  },
  1970: {
    source: "Для чего используются интерфейсы при подмене реализаций?",
    question: "What are interfaces used for when substituting implementations?",
    shortAnswer: "Interfaces let you separate the contract of a behaviour from its concrete implementation. This makes it possible to substitute implementations easily — for testing with mocks, for example, or for switching between different services. Instead of being tightly bound to a class, the code works through an abstraction, which makes the system flexible and extensible.",
    longAnswer: "Why are interfaces needed when substituting implementations?\nInterfaces in programming define a contract — the set of methods any suitable entity must implement. When code depends on an interface rather than on a concrete class, it becomes possible to substitute implementations without changing the code that uses them. This is the key principle of loose coupling.\n\nAn example in TypeScript\n\nWhere is this applied?\n\n- Testing: instead of a real database or API, mocks implementing the same interface are substituted.\n- Modularity: you can switch between different providers (cloud storage services, for example) without changing the business logic.\n- Dependency Injection: DI containers substitute the required implementations by interface automatically.\n\nConclusion\nInterfaces are a tool for building a flexible architecture. Use them everywhere the implementation may change: in services, repositories and adapters. This makes code testable, extensible and independent of particular libraries or external systems.",
    codeExample: "interface ILogger {\n  log(message: string): void;\n}\n\nclass ConsoleLogger implements ILogger {\n  log(message: string) {\n    console.log(message);\n  }\n}\n\nclass FileLogger implements ILogger {\n  log(message: string) {\n    // write to a file\n  }\n}\n\nclass App {\n  constructor(private logger: ILogger) {}\n  run() {\n    this.logger.log('App started');\n  }\n}\n\n// Substituting the implementation\nconst app = new App(new ConsoleLogger());\n// or\nconst app2 = new App(new FileLogger());",
  },
  1971: {
    source: "Модель — это класс?",
    question: "Is a model a class?",
    shortAnswer: "A model is not necessarily a class. A class is a template for creating objects in OOP, while a model is an abstract representation of data or business logic. A model can be implemented as a class, but it can also be a simple structure, an interface or even a function.",
    longAnswer: "What are a model and a class?\nIn programming the terms \"model\" and \"class\" are often confused, but they belong to different levels of abstraction. A class is a concrete construct in object-oriented languages (Java, Python, C++, for example) that defines the properties and methods for creating objects. A model is a broader notion describing the structure of an application's data or business logic, independently of the implementation language.\n\nExamples of the differences\nConsider the model of a user in a web application. It can be implemented as a class:\n\nBut the same model can be represented by a simple object or by an interface in TypeScript:\n\nIn functional programming a model may be a function returning data, or even a JSON schema.\n\nWhere it is applied\n\n- In the MVC architecture the model is the layer responsible for data and business logic, which may include classes but is not limited to them.\n- In databases a model describes the structure of tables and relations, which has no direct bearing on classes.\n- In machine learning a model is a mathematical function trained on data, not a class in OOP.\n\nConclusion: a model is a conceptual representation of data or logic, while a class is one way of implementing it. Understanding this difference helps you choose the right tools for designing applications.",
    codeExample: "class User {\n  constructor(name, email) {\n    this.name = name;\n    this.email = email;\n  }\n  getDisplayName() {\n    return this.name;\n  }\n}\n\ninterface UserModel {\n  name: string;\n  email: string;\n}",
  },
  1972: {
    source: "Как типизировать функцию через generic?",
    question: "How do you type a function with a generic?",
    shortAnswer: "A generic lets you create a function that works with different types while preserving the typing. The type is stated in angle brackets before the parameters. At the call site TypeScript infers the concrete type automatically. This spares you from duplicating code and makes functions flexible.",
    longAnswer: "What is a generic in a function?\nA generic is a TypeScript mechanism that lets you create components which work with various types without losing type information. Instead of writing a separate function for every type, you use a type parameter that is substituted at the call site.\n\nThe syntax\nThe type parameter is declared in angle brackets `<T>` before the function's parameter list. The letter `T` (for Type) is normally used, but any name is allowed.\n\nHow does it work?\nWhen the function is called, TypeScript analyses the argument it was given and infers the concrete type for `T` automatically. In the example above `num` will have the type `number` and `str` the type `string`. If you need to state the type explicitly, you can write `identity<number>(42)`.\n\nA practical example: a function for working with arrays\n\nHere `T[]` means an array of elements of type `T`. The function returns the first element or `undefined`, preserving the type.\n\nGeneric constraints\nSometimes you need to impose a constraint on the type in order to use particular properties. The `extends` keyword is applied for this:\n\nNow `T` can only be a type that has a `length` property.\n\nConclusion\nGenerics make functions universal and type-safe. They are worth applying wherever a function has to work with different types while still preserving strict typing — in utilities for working with arrays, promises or HTTP requests, for example.",
    codeExample: "function identity<T>(arg: T): T {\n  return arg;\n}\n\nconst num = identity(42);      // T = number\nconst str = identity('hello'); // T = string\n\nfunction firstElement<T>(arr: T[]): T | undefined {\n  return arr[0];\n}\n\nconst firstNum = firstElement([1, 2, 3]);      // number | undefined\nconst firstStr = firstElement(['a', 'b']);     // string | undefined\n\nfunction getLength<T extends { length: number }>(arg: T): number {\n  return arg.length;\n}\n\ngetLength('hello'); // 5\ngetLength([1, 2]);  // 2\ngetLength(42);      // Error: number has no length",
  },
  1973: {
    source: "Чем void отличается от never?",
    question: "How does void differ from never?",
    shortAnswer: "void means that a function returns undefined or null — that is, it finishes normally but without a useful value. never means that the function never finishes (for example, it throws an exception or enters an infinite loop). void is the type for functions that complete without returning anything; never is for ones that do not complete.",
    longAnswer: "The main difference between void and never\nIn TypeScript both types are used to describe the return values of functions, but they denote fundamentally different situations. `void` indicates that the function finishes normally but does not return any useful value (in fact it returns `undefined`). `never`, by contrast, says that the function never finishes — either it throws an exception or it enters an infinite loop.\n\nExamples of use\nConsider some simple examples:\n\nWhere they are applied\n\n- `void` is the standard type for functions that perform side effects (logging, changing the DOM, writing to a file) and do not return a value.\n- `never` is useful for error-handling functions, for infinite loops (in game engines, for example) or for exhaustiveness checks on types.\n\nConclusion\nUse `void` for ordinary functions that return nothing, and `never` when a function is guaranteed not to finish. This helps TypeScript analyse code more precisely and avoid errors related to unreachable execution paths.",
    codeExample: "function logMessage(msg: string): void {\n  console.log(msg);\n  // the function finishes but returns nothing\n}\n\nfunction throwError(msg: string): never {\n  throw new Error(msg);\n  // after throw, execution stops\n}\n\nfunction infiniteLoop(): never {\n  while (true) {\n    // an infinite loop, the function will never finish\n  }\n}",
  },
  1974: {
    source: "Какие ограничения TypeScript существуют относительно runtime?",
    question: "What limitations does TypeScript have with regard to runtime?",
    shortAnswer: "TypeScript is a static analyser that works only at compile time. All types, interfaces and annotations are removed when it is transpiled to JavaScript, so there is no typing at runtime at all. This means that type checks are not performed while the program runs, and type-related errors can only show up during operation.",
    longAnswer: "TypeScript's limitations at runtime\nTypeScript provides strict static typing, but it exists only at development and compile time. When the code is compiled to JavaScript, all the types are erased. This is the key limitation: TypeScript adds no runtime checks at all.\n\nAn example of type erasure\n\nAs you can see, the type annotations have been removed entirely. At runtime a string or an object can be passed in, and TypeScript cannot prevent it.\n\nThe practical consequences\n\n- There is no type checking at runtime — errors can only show up when the code runs.\n- Types cannot be used to validate data from external sources (APIs, user input).\n- There is no type reflection — you cannot obtain information about a type at runtime.\n\nHow to work around the limitations\nFor runtime validation people use third-party libraries such as Zod or io-ts, which create schemas that work at runtime too. You can also use custom checks with typeof or instanceof.\n\nConclusion\nTypeScript is useful for static analysis and for preventing errors at development time, but it does not replace runtime validation. For critical data from external sources, always use additional checks.",
    codeExample: "function add(a: number, b: number): number {\n  return a + b;\n}\n\n// After compilation to JavaScript:\nfunction add(a, b) {\n  return a + b;\n}",
  },
  1975: {
    source: "Почему interface нельзя проверить во время runtime?",
    question: "Why can't an interface be checked at runtime?",
    shortAnswer: "TypeScript is a layer on top of JavaScript that adds static typing. During compilation, however, TypeScript is transpiled to JavaScript and all type annotations, interfaces included, are removed. JavaScript has no built-in type system, so checking the existence or structure of an interface at runtime is impossible.",
    longAnswer: "Why do TypeScript interfaces not exist at runtime?\nTypeScript is a statically typed language that compiles to JavaScript. During compilation TypeScript removes all type annotations, interfaces included, in a process called type erasure. JavaScript, which TypeScript compiles into, is a dynamically typed language and has no built-in support for checking types at runtime.\n\nAn example of type erasure\nConsider a simple interface and a function:\n\nAfter compilation to JavaScript the code will look like this:\n\nAs you can see, the `User` interface has disappeared completely. JavaScript knows nothing of its existence.\n\nHow do you check an object's structure at runtime?\nIf you need to check whether an object matches a particular structure at runtime, you can use:\n- Custom type guards — functions that check the presence of properties and their types.\n- Validation libraries such as `zod`, `io-ts` or `yup`, which let you define schemas and validate data at runtime.\n\nAn example with a custom guard\n\nThis code works because we check the properties' actual types at runtime rather than relying on the interface.\n\nConclusion\nTypeScript interfaces are a tool for the developer that helps catch errors at compile time. They are not meant for validating data at runtime. If you need runtime validation, use guard functions or specialized libraries.",
    codeExample: "interface User {\n  name: string;\n  age: number;\n}\n\nfunction greet(user: User) {\n  console.log(`Hello, ${user.name}!`);\n}\n\nfunction greet(user) {\n  console.log(`Hello, ${user.name}!`);\n}\n\ninterface User {\n  name: string;\n  age: number;\n}\n\nfunction isUser(obj: any): obj is User {\n  return typeof obj.name === 'string' && typeof obj.age === 'number';\n}\n\nconst data = { name: 'Alice', age: 30 };\nif (isUser(data)) {\n  console.log('Data is a User');\n}",
  },
  1976: {
    source: "Как организуются runtime-проверки в TypeScript?",
    question: "How are runtime checks organized in TypeScript?",
    shortAnswer: "TypeScript provides no built-in runtime checks, because its type system works only at compile time. For checks while the code runs people use custom type guards, discriminated unions or libraries such as zod and io-ts. Type guards are functions that return a boolean and use the 'is' keyword to narrow the type.",
    longAnswer: "How does TypeScript handle types at runtime?\nTypeScript is a layer on top of JavaScript that adds static typing. After compilation to JavaScript, however, all the type information is removed. This means TypeScript provides no built-in mechanisms for checking types while the program runs. Developers have to implement such checks themselves.\n\nCustom type guards\nThe most widespread way is to create functions that check the structure of the data and return a boolean along with an indication of the type. Such functions are called type guards. They use the `is` keyword in their return type.\n\nDiscriminated unions\nAnother approach is to use discriminated unions. This is when every object in the union has a shared property (the discriminant) with a unique literal type. TypeScript can narrow the type based on the value of that property.\n\nLibraries for runtime validation\nFor more complex scenarios there are libraries such as `zod` and `io-ts`. They let you define data schemas and validate them at runtime, inferring the TypeScript types automatically.\n\nConclusion\nRuntime checks in TypeScript are necessary for validating data that comes from external sources (APIs, user input). Use type guards for simple cases, discriminated unions for working with unions of types, and libraries like zod for complex schemas. This increases the reliability of an application and prevents errors caused by incorrect data.",
    codeExample: "interface Cat {\n  meow(): void;\n}\ninterface Dog {\n  bark(): void;\n}\n\nfunction isCat(pet: Cat | Dog): pet is Cat {\n  return (pet as Cat).meow !== undefined;\n}\n\nfunction handlePet(pet: Cat | Dog) {\n  if (isCat(pet)) {\n    pet.meow(); // TypeScript knows that pet is a Cat\n  } else {\n    pet.bark(); // TypeScript knows that pet is a Dog\n  }\n}\n\ntype Shape =\n  | { kind: 'circle'; radius: number }\n  | { kind: 'square'; side: number };\n\nfunction area(shape: Shape): number {\n  switch (shape.kind) {\n    case 'circle':\n      return Math.PI * shape.radius ** 2;\n    case 'square':\n      return shape.side ** 2;\n  }\n}\n\nimport { z } from 'zod';\n\nconst UserSchema = z.object({\n  name: z.string(),\n  age: z.number().positive(),\n});\n\ntype User = z.infer<typeof UserSchema>;\n\nconst data = JSON.parse('{\"name\":\"Alice\",\"age\":30}');\nconst user = UserSchema.parse(data); // will throw an error if the data is invalid",
  },
  1977: {
    source: "Какие сущности TypeScript существуют только на этапе компиляции?",
    question: "Which TypeScript entities exist only at compile time?",
    shortAnswer: "The entities that exist only at compile time in TypeScript include interfaces, type aliases, generics, decorators (in the old syntax) and type annotations. They are used for static checking of the code and generate no JavaScript after compilation. The exception is enums, which are partly compiled into objects, although their type annotations do disappear.",
    longAnswer: "TypeScript entities that exist only at compile time\nTypeScript is a layer on top of JavaScript that adds static typing. Many TypeScript constructs exist exclusively at compile time and have no representation at runtime. This means that after transpilation to JavaScript they are removed entirely or turned into ordinary JS code without types.\n\nThe main entities that disappear after compilation\n\n- Interfaces — used to describe the structure of objects, but they generate no code.\n- Type aliases — like interfaces, they exist only for type checking.\n- Type annotations — every type statement (`: string`, for example) is removed.\n- Generics — the type parameters are erased, leaving only the logic without types.\n- Decorators (in the old syntax) — although they can affect runtime, their type annotations disappear.\n\nA code example\n\nAs you can see, the `User` interface and the generic `<T>` have disappeared entirely. This lets TypeScript be fully compatible with JavaScript without adding any execution overhead.\n\nThe exception: enums\nEnums are partly compiled into JavaScript objects, but their type annotations are removed as well. For example:\n\nSo the object itself exists at runtime, but the types associated with the enum disappear.\n\nConclusion\nUnderstanding which TypeScript entities exist only at compile time helps developers avoid errors caused by trying to use types at runtime. This knowledge is especially useful when writing libraries, or when working with reflection where types may be needed dynamically.",
    codeExample: "// The original TypeScript\ninterface User {\n  name: string;\n  age: number;\n}\n\nfunction greet<T>(user: T): void {\n  console.log(user);\n}\n\n// After compilation to JavaScript\nfunction greet(user) {\n  console.log(user);\n}\n\nenum Color { Red, Green, Blue }\n// Compiles into:\nvar Color;\n(function (Color) {\n  Color[Color[\"Red\"] = 0] = \"Red\";\n  // ...\n})(Color || (Color = {}));",
  },
  1978: {
    source: "Что такое высокая связанность классов и чем она опасна?",
    question: "What is high coupling between classes and why is it dangerous?",
    shortAnswer: "High coupling between classes means the classes depend heavily on one another. This is dangerous because a change in one class often requires changes in others, which makes maintenance and testing harder. Such code becomes fragile and difficult to extend.",
    longAnswer: "What is high coupling?\nTight coupling arises when classes depend directly on the concrete implementations of other classes. For example, an `OrderService` class may create an instance of `MySQLDatabase` inside itself instead of receiving it through the constructor. This binds the code rigidly to one particular database.\n\nWhy is this dangerous?\n\n- Difficulty of change: replacing the database, moving from MySQL to PostgreSQL, would require rewriting every class that uses `MySQLDatabase` directly.\n- Difficulty of testing: there is no way to substitute the real database with a mock in unit tests.\n- Reduced reusability: the class cannot be used in another project without carrying all its dependencies over.\n\nAn example of the problem\n\nThe solution: dependency inversion\nUse interfaces or abstractions so that classes depend on abstractions rather than on concrete implementations. Inject the dependencies through the constructor (dependency injection).\n\nConclusion: high coupling makes code fragile and hard to maintain. Apply the dependency inversion principle and dependency injection to create a flexible, testable architecture.",
    codeExample: "class MySQLDatabase {\n  save(data) { /* ... */ }\n}\n\nclass OrderService {\n  constructor() {\n    this.db = new MySQLDatabase(); // tight coupling\n  }\n  createOrder(order) {\n    this.db.save(order);\n  }\n}\n\nclass OrderService {\n  constructor(database) {\n    this.db = database; // loose coupling\n  }\n  createOrder(order) {\n    this.db.save(order);\n  }\n}\n\nconst db = new MySQLDatabase();\nconst service = new OrderService(db);",
  },
  1979: {
    source: "Что такое абстракция в контексте проектирования приложений?",
    question: "What is abstraction in the context of application design?",
    shortAnswer: "Abstraction is the principle of hiding complex implementation details and exposing only the interface that is needed. It lets you work with objects at a high level without going into their internal workings. For example, when calling a `sendEmail()` method we do not think about the SMTP or TCP protocols.",
    longAnswer: "What is abstraction?\nAbstraction is one of the fundamental principles of object-oriented programming and of application design. It consists in singling out the key characteristics of an object or process and ignoring the inessential details. Put simply, it is a way of reducing complexity by presenting a system as a set of comprehensible interfaces.\n\nHow does it work in practice?\nImagine you are using a television remote. You press the \"volume up\" button and the sound gets louder. You do not need to know exactly how the television processes the signal, which transistors are involved or how the digital-to-analogue converter works. The remote provides an abstraction — a simple interface for controlling a complex device.\n\nIn programming abstraction is implemented through classes, interfaces and abstract classes. For example:\n\nWhere is it applied?\n\n- In designing APIs and libraries — it hides internal complexity from developers.\n- In application architecture (a layered architecture, for example) — each layer abstracts away the details of the layers below.\n- In design patterns such as Factory, Strategy and Adapter.\n\nConclusion\nAbstraction helps you manage complexity, making code more modular, testable and comprehensible. It is worth applying wherever you need to separate \"what the system does\" from \"how it does it\" — this is the basis for creating flexible, maintainable applications.",
    codeExample: "// The abstraction: an interface for sending notifications\ninterface NotificationSender {\n    void send(String message);\n}\n\n// A concrete implementation for email\nclass EmailSender implements NotificationSender {\n    @Override\n    public void send(String message) {\n        // The complex logic of sending an email\n        System.out.println(\"Sending email: \" + message);\n    }\n}\n\n// A concrete implementation for SMS\nclass SMSSender implements NotificationSender {\n    @Override\n    public void send(String message) {\n        // The complex logic of sending an SMS\n        System.out.println(\"Sending SMS: \" + message);\n    }\n}\n\n// Using the abstraction\npublic class NotificationService {\n    private NotificationSender sender;\n    \n    public NotificationService(NotificationSender sender) {\n        this.sender = sender;\n    }\n    \n    public void notifyUser(String message) {\n        sender.send(message); // We do not know exactly how it is sent\n    }\n}",
  },
  1980: {
    source: "Как правильно описывать типы для сущностей приложения?",
    question: "How do you describe the types of an application's entities correctly?",
    shortAnswer: "The types of an application's entities are described with interfaces or type aliases in TypeScript. Interfaces are preferable for objects that may be extended, and type for unions and primitives. It is important to describe only the fields you need, avoiding redundancy, and to use optional properties (?) for data that is not required. This makes code self-documenting and prevents errors at compile time.",
    longAnswer: "The basics of typing entities\nWhen developing applications in TypeScript, describing the types of entities (a user, an order, a product, for example) correctly is key to ensuring type safety. It lets the compiler check that data is correct at development time, reducing the number of runtime errors. Types make code more readable and predictable, especially on large projects.\n\nInterfaces vs type aliases\nEntities are most often described with interfaces or type aliases. Interfaces support declaration merging and inheritance, which is convenient for extending entities. Type aliases are a better fit for creating complex types such as unions or tuples. For example:\n\nPractical recommendations\n\n- Use interfaces for objects that may be extended in the future.\n- Apply type for primitives, unions and types that do not need inheritance.\n- Add optional fields (?) for data that may be absent.\n- Avoid redundancy: do not duplicate types, use utilities such as Partial or Pick.\n\nAn example for an application entity\n\nConclusion: typing entities correctly increases the reliability of your code and makes it easier to maintain. Use interfaces for objects, type for simple or composite types, and always aim at minimalism when describing fields.",
    codeExample: "// An interface for a user\ninterface User {\n  id: number;\n  name: string;\n  email?: string; // an optional field\n}\n\n// A type alias for an order's status\ntype OrderStatus = 'pending' | 'shipped' | 'delivered';\n\n// The product entity\ninterface Product {\n  id: string;\n  title: string;\n  price: number;\n  description?: string;\n  category: Category; // another interface\n}\n\n// Use in a function\nfunction getProductPrice(product: Product): number {\n  return product.price;\n}",
  },
  1981: {
    source: "Почему TypeScript может выводить тип never?",
    question: "Why might TypeScript infer the type never?",
    shortAnswer: "The never type is inferred when a function never finishes (for example, it throws an exception or enters an infinite loop), or when every possible member of a union of types has been exhausted. It helps TypeScript guarantee that the code is correct and is used for exhaustiveness checks.",
    longAnswer: "What is the never type in TypeScript?\nThe `never` type represents values that can never occur. It is the bottom type in TypeScript's type hierarchy — that is, it can be assigned to any type, but nothing except `never` itself can be assigned to `never`. TypeScript infers `never` automatically in several key situations.\n\nWhen is never inferred?\n\n- Functions that never finish: if a function always throws an exception or enters an infinite loop, its return type is inferred as `never`.\n- Exhaustive checks: when using discriminated unions and conditional constructs, once every possible variant has been handled the remaining branch gets the type `never`.\n- Empty arrays with an explicit type: if an array is declared with the type `never[]`, it cannot contain elements.\n\nCode examples\n\nPractical use\nThe `never` type is used actively to ensure type safety when working with unions of types. It helps a developer not to miss handling a new variant when the type is extended. `never` is also applied in conditional types to filter or exclude particular types from a union.\n\nConclusion\nUnderstanding `never` is necessary for writing reliable TypeScript code, especially when working with complex types and discriminated unions. It is a powerful tool for static analysis that helps avoid errors at compile time.",
    codeExample: "// A function that throws an exception\nfunction throwError(message: string): never {\n  throw new Error(message);\n}\n\n// An infinite loop\nfunction infiniteLoop(): never {\n  while (true) {}\n}\n\n// An exhaustive check with a discriminated union\ntype Shape = \n  | { kind: 'circle'; radius: number }\n  | { kind: 'square'; side: number };\n\nfunction area(shape: Shape): number {\n  switch (shape.kind) {\n    case 'circle':\n      return Math.PI * shape.radius ** 2;\n    case 'square':\n      return shape.side ** 2;\n    default:\n      // shape has the type never here\n      const _exhaustive: never = shape;\n      return _exhaustive;\n  }\n}",
  },
  1982: {
    source: "Как правильно хранить строковые константы?",
    question: "How should string constants be stored?",
    shortAnswer: "String constants are better stored in a separate file or module, using objects or enums. This lets you avoid magic strings, makes changing values easier and prevents errors caused by typos. In JavaScript, for example, you can create an object with the constants and export it.",
    longAnswer: "Why store string constants separately?\nUsing string literals directly in code (so-called \"magic strings\") makes maintenance harder and increases the risk of errors. If the same string is used in several places, changing it would require a search and replace across the whole project. Moving the constants to a single place solves this problem.\n\nWays of storing them\n\n- An object with constants — a simple, clear way for JavaScript/TypeScript.\n- An enum — suitable for TypeScript and other strictly typed languages.\n- A separate file — the constants are grouped by meaning (api.js, messages.js, for example).\n\nAn example in JavaScript\n\nAn example in TypeScript with an enum\n\nConclusion\nStoring string constants in a centralized place improves readability, makes refactoring easier and reduces the chance of errors. This approach is recommended in any project where strings are repeated or may change.",
    codeExample: "// constants/api.js\nexport const API = {\n  BASE_URL: 'https://api.example.com',\n  ENDPOINT_USERS: '/users',\n  ENDPOINT_POSTS: '/posts'\n};\n\n// Use\nimport { API } from './constants/api';\nfetch(API.BASE_URL + API.ENDPOINT_USERS);\n\n// constants/status.ts\nexport enum OrderStatus {\n  PENDING = 'pending',\n  SHIPPED = 'shipped',\n  DELIVERED = 'delivered'\n}\n\n// Use\nif (order.status === OrderStatus.SHIPPED) { ... }",
  },
  1983: {
    source: "Чем Sequence отличается от Iterable в аннотациях типов?",
    question: "How does Sequence differ from Iterable in type annotations?",
    shortAnswer: "Iterable is the most general type for objects you can iterate over (with a for loop, for example). Sequence is a subtype of Iterable that additionally supports access by index and has a fixed length. Iterable covers generators, sets and files, while Sequence covers lists, tuples and strings. Using Sequence in an annotation guarantees that the object passed in can not only be iterated over but also indexed.",
    longAnswer: "The difference between Sequence and Iterable in Python type annotations\nIn Python's type system (the typing module) Iterable and Sequence represent different levels of abstraction for collections. Iterable is the most general contract: an object implementing the iteration protocol (the __iter__() method, or __getitem__() with consecutive indices). Sequence is a stricter contract, inheriting from Iterable, Reversible and Collection, which guarantees support for access by index (__getitem__ with an arbitrary integer index), for determining the length (__len__) and for iterating in reverse.\n\nThe key differences\n\n- Iterable: can be used in a for loop and passed into functions that expect an iterator. Examples: lists, tuples, strings, sets, dictionaries, generators, files.\n- Sequence: everything Iterable has, plus access by index (my_list[0], for example), slices and the .count() and .index() methods. Examples: list, tuple, str, range, bytes, bytearray.\n\nA code example\n\nWhen to use which\nUse Iterable if your function only iterates over the elements and does not need access by index or the length. This makes the function more flexible — it will be able to accept generators and other lazy sequences. Use Sequence when you need access by index, slices or the guarantee that the data can be iterated over more than once (a generator can be iterated over only once).\n\nConclusion: Sequence is a stricter, richer type than Iterable. The choice between them depends on which operations you plan to perform on the collection. For maximum flexibility use Iterable; for a guarantee of access by index use Sequence.",
    codeExample: "from typing import Iterable, Sequence\n\ndef process_iterable(data: Iterable[int]) -> None:\n    for item in data:\n        print(item)\n\ndef process_sequence(data: Sequence[int]) -> None:\n    # We can access it by index\n    first = data[0]\n    print(f\"First element: {first}\")\n    # We can get the length\n    print(f\"Length: {len(data)}\")\n\n# Examples of calls\nprocess_iterable([1, 2, 3])  # OK\nprocess_iterable((x for x in range(3)))  # OK, a generator\nprocess_sequence([1, 2, 3])  # OK\nprocess_sequence((1, 2, 3))  # OK, a tuple\n# process_sequence((x for x in range(3)))  # Type error: a generator is not a Sequence",
  },
  1984: {
    source: "Где хранится переменная-ссылка на объект класса и где сам объект?",
    question: "Where is the reference variable for a class object stored, and where is the object itself?",
    shortAnswer: "The reference variable for an object is stored on the stack, while the object itself lives on the heap. The stack is used to store primitive types and references to objects, because it is faster and has a strict order of memory allocation. The heap is intended for the dynamic allocation of memory for objects that may outlive the current function call.",
    longAnswer: "Where variables and objects are stored in memory\nIn programming languages such as Java, C# or JavaScript, memory is divided into two main areas: the stack and the heap. The stack is an area of memory that works on the LIFO (Last In, First Out) principle, where local variables and function calls are stored. The heap is an area for dynamic memory allocation, where objects and arrays are placed.\n\nHow this works in practice\nWhen you create an object of a class — `new MyClass()`, for example — the object itself is placed on the heap. The variable that refers to that object is stored on the stack (if it is a local variable) or on the heap (if it is a field of another object). The variable contains not the object itself but the address (the reference) of the area of memory on the heap where the object lives.\n\nWhy this matters\nUnderstanding this division helps you avoid errors related to memory leaks and incorrect management of references. For example, if a variable goes out of scope, the reference is removed from the stack, but the object on the heap may remain if other references to it exist. The garbage collector clears away such objects once no references to them are left.\n\nConclusion\nThis division lets you manage memory efficiently: the stack provides fast access to local variables, while the heap allows objects with arbitrary lifetimes to be placed flexibly. Knowing this concept is necessary for writing performant, safe code.",
    codeExample: "public class Example {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        MyClass obj = new MyClass(); // obj on the stack, the object on the heap\n        int number = 42; // number on the stack\n    }\n}",
  },
  1985: {
    source: "Для чего используется Zod и чем он отличается от ручной валидации данных?",
    question: "What is Zod used for and how does it differ from validating data by hand?",
    shortAnswer: "Zod is a library for describing data schemas declaratively and validating against them. Unlike checking by hand, Zod generates TypeScript types automatically, provides strict typing and simplifies error handling. This reduces the amount of boilerplate code and increases reliability.",
    longAnswer: "What is Zod and what is it for?\nZod is a library for creating data schemas and validating against them, oriented towards TypeScript. It lets you describe the expected structure of your data (an object with name and age fields, for example) and then check whether a given value conforms to it. The main advantage is automatic type inference: a Zod schema produces a correct TypeScript type, which rules out discrepancies between the type declaration and the validation logic.\n\nThe differences from validating by hand\nWhen validating by hand, a developer writes functions that check each field, handle errors and cast types manually. This leads to duplicated code and to errors. Zod, by contrast, offers a declarative approach: you describe the schema once, and the library takes care of checking, parsing and generating the types.\n\nA code example\n\nWhere it is applied\nZod is often used to validate API input, forms, configuration and any external data where type safety matters. It is especially useful in TypeScript projects where runtime errors have to be minimized.\n\nConclusion: Zod simplifies validation and makes code more readable and safer thanks to automatic type generation. It is worth applying in any TypeScript project where data from unreliable sources has to be checked.",
    codeExample: "import { z } from 'zod';\n\n// The schema\nconst UserSchema = z.object({\n  name: z.string().min(1, 'Name is required'),\n  age: z.number().int().positive(),\n});\n\n// The automatic type\ntype User = z.infer<typeof UserSchema>;\n\n// Validation\nconst result = UserSchema.safeParse({ name: 'Alice', age: 30 });\nif (!result.success) {\n  console.log(result.error.format());\n} else {\n  console.log(result.data); // strictly typed\n}",
  },
  1986: {
    source: "Как принудительно привести тип в TypeScript, если обычный as не позволяет?",
    question: "How do you force a type cast in TypeScript when a plain as will not allow it?",
    shortAnswer: "If a plain as does not work, use a double cast through unknown: value as unknown as TargetType. This bypasses the type-compatibility check, because unknown is compatible with any type. Apply it only when you are sure of the data's structure, otherwise you risk a runtime error.",
    longAnswer: "Forcing a type cast in TypeScript\nTypeScript checks type compatibility strictly when the `as` operator is used. If you try to cast a type that has no overlap with the target type, the compiler reports an error. For example, you cannot cast a `string` to a `number` directly.\n\nThe solution through unknown\nTo bypass this check, a double cast through `unknown` is used. First the value is cast to `unknown`, and then to the type you need. `unknown` is a supertype of all types, so such a cast is permitted.\n\nWhen this is useful\n\n- When working with data from external sources (an API, JSON) whose structure is known but which TypeScript cannot verify.\n- When migrating code from JavaScript, while the types are not yet fully described.\n- In rare cases where strict typing has to be bypassed for compatibility with libraries.\n\nThe risks and caveats\nA double cast switches off type checking, which can lead to runtime errors. Use it only when you are absolutely sure of the data. It is better to apply custom type guards or runtime checks.\n\nConclusion: a double cast through `unknown` is a powerful but dangerous tool. Apply it only to bypass TypeScript's strict restrictions in controlled scenarios, and prefer safe type checks.",
    codeExample: "const value: string = '123';\nconst num = value as unknown as number; // Works, but dangerous\nconsole.log(num); // '123' (a string, not a number)\n\nfunction isNumber(value: unknown): value is number {\n  return typeof value === 'number';\n}\n\nconst data: unknown = '123';\nif (isNumber(data)) {\n  console.log(data.toFixed(2)); // Safe\n}",
  },
  1987: {
    source: "Есть ли правила линтера, ограничивающие импорт не из индексных файлов?",
    question: "Are there linter rules restricting imports from anything other than index files?",
    shortAnswer: "Yes, there are ESLint rules such as no-restricted-imports or import/no-internal-modules that can restrict imports from anything other than index files. This helps keep the architecture clean and avoid deep dependencies. For example, the import/no-internal-modules rule forbids importing from internal modules, allowing only index files.",
    longAnswer: "What index files are and why use them\nIndex files (usually index.js or index.ts) serve as a module's entry point, exporting only its public API. This hides the internal structure and makes refactoring easier.\n\nLinter rules for controlling imports\nESLint with the eslint-plugin-import plugin provides several rules for restricting imports:\n- import/no-internal-modules — forbids importing from a module's internal files, allowing only index files.\n- no-restricted-imports — lets you specify a list of forbidden paths by hand.\n\nAn example configuration\n\nIn this example only imports from the index files inside the component folders are allowed.\n\nA practical example\n\nThe first import reaches the Button file directly; the second goes through the index file, which re-exports the component.\n\nConclusion\nUsing linter rules to restrict imports from non-index files helps maintain a modular architecture, makes refactoring easier and makes code more predictable. This is especially useful in large projects with many modules.",
    codeExample: "// .eslintrc.js\nmodule.exports = {\n  plugins: ['import'],\n  rules: {\n    'import/no-internal-modules': ['error', {\n      allow: ['src/components/*/index']\n    }]\n  }\n};\n\n// Forbidden:\nimport Button from './components/Button/Button';\n\n// Allowed:\nimport { Button } from './components/Button';",
  },
  1988: {
    source: "Для чего используются индексные файлы (barrel files) и зачем они нужны?",
    question: "What are index files (barrel files) used for and why are they needed?",
    shortAnswer: "Index files (usually index.js or index.ts) are used to group and re-export the modules of a folder. They simplify imports by letting you import everything you need from a single point rather than specifying the path to every file separately. This improves the readability of the code and reduces the number of import lines.",
    longAnswer: "What are index files (barrel files)?\nIndex files, often called barrel files, are files named index.js (or index.ts in TypeScript) that live in a folder and serve to re-export all the modules of that folder centrally. Instead of importing every component, utility or constant separately by its full path, you can import everything from a single index file.\n\nWhy are they needed?\nThe main goal is to simplify the imports in a project and make them cleaner. Without index files, imports can look cumbersome, especially when the folder structure is deep. Barrel files also help hide the internal structure of folders by providing a single interface for external code.\n\nAn example of use\nSuppose you have a components folder with several files:\n\nInside index.js you re-export everything:\n\nNow in another file you can import all the components in one line:\n\nInstead of writing three separate imports.\n\nWhen should they be applied?\nBarrel files are especially useful in large projects with many modules, where they improve the readability and maintainability of the code. They should be used in moderation, however, since excessive use can lead to circular dependencies or slow the build down by importing unused modules. In small projects, or when there are strict performance requirements, you can do without them.",
    codeExample: "components/\n  Button.js\n  Input.js\n  Modal.js\n  index.js\n\n// components/index.js\nexport { default as Button } from './Button';\nexport { default as Input } from './Input';\nexport { default as Modal } from './Modal';\n\n// App.js\nimport { Button, Input, Modal } from './components';",
  },
  1989: {
    source: "Приходилось ли вам использовать ts-ignore или явное приведение типов?",
    question: "Have you had to use ts-ignore or an explicit type cast?",
    shortAnswer: "ts-ignore is a comment that makes the TypeScript compiler ignore the next line. An explicit type cast (a type assertion) is a way of telling the compiler that you know the type better than it does. Both tools exist to bypass strict typing, but abusing them reduces the safety of your code. Use them only when you are sure of the type and TypeScript cannot infer it.",
    longAnswer: "What are ts-ignore and a type assertion?\n`ts-ignore` is the special comment `// @ts-ignore`, which switches off type checking for the next line. It is useful when you run into a type error caused by incompatible libraries or by temporary code. Using it often, however, can hide real problems.\n\nAn explicit type cast (a type assertion) is written with the syntax `as Type` or `<Type>`. It tells the compiler: \"I know this value has this type, trust me.\" This is safer than `ts-ignore`, since type checking still works for the rest of the code.\n\nExamples of use\n\nWhen to apply them\n\n- `ts-ignore` — only as a temporary solution, with a comment explaining why and a plan for fixing it.\n- A type assertion — when you know the type for certain (after a runtime check, for example) and the compiler cannot infer it.\n\nConclusion: use these tools deliberately, preferring a type assertion over ts-ignore. They help you integrate JavaScript code or work with dynamic data, but they should not become a habit for bypassing the type system.",
    codeExample: "// ts-ignore: we ignore the error if the library has no types\n// @ts-ignore\nconst result = someLegacyLibrary.doSomething();\n\n// A type assertion: we are sure the element exists\nconst input = document.getElementById('myInput') as HTMLInputElement;\ninput.value = 'Hello';",
  },
  1990: {
    source: "Следите ли за обновлениями TypeScript? Что нового появилось в TypeScript 5.5?",
    question: "Do you follow TypeScript releases? What is new in TypeScript 5.5?",
    shortAnswer: "TypeScript 5.5 brought several significant improvements. The main novelty is inferred type predicates, which improves type narrowing in functions. Control-flow analysis for constants has also been improved, support for isolatedDeclarations has been added for faster builds, and there are new capabilities for working with regular expressions.",
    longAnswer: "The main novelties of TypeScript 5.5\nTypeScript 5.5 is a release that significantly improves type inference and compilation performance. The key change is inferred type predicates, which lets TypeScript work out on its own when a function returns a type predicate, without the developer stating it explicitly.\n\nInferred Type Predicates\nPreviously, to narrow types in a function you had to state the return type explicitly as a type predicate. Now TypeScript can infer this automatically:\n\nImproved control flow for constants\nTypeScript 5.5 improved the analysis of constant expressions, which lets it determine types more precisely in complex conditions:\n\nIsolated Declarations\nThe new isolatedDeclarations compiler option lets you generate .d.ts files faster by processing each file independently. This is especially useful for large projects and monorepos.\n\nImprovements for regular expressions\nSupport for new flags has been added and the analysis of regular expressions improved, which helps avoid errors at compile time.\n\nConclusion: TypeScript 5.5 makes the language more convenient and more performant. Inferred type predicates simplify writing type-safe code, and the compilation-performance improvements matter for large projects. Following TypeScript releases is useful for every developer who works with the language.",
    codeExample: "function isString(value: unknown) {\n  return typeof value === \"string\";\n}\n// TypeScript 5.5 infers automatically: (value: unknown) => value is string\n\nfunction process(value: string | number) {\n  if (isString(value)) {\n    console.log(value.toUpperCase()); // value: string\n  }\n}\n\nconst config = { mode: \"development\" as const };\nif (config.mode === \"development\") {\n  // TypeScript now understands that config.mode is \"development\"\n}",
  },
  1991: {
    source: "Какое значение по умолчанию получает первый элемент числового enum, если значения не заданы явно?",
    question: "What default value does the first member of a numeric enum get if no values are stated explicitly?",
    shortAnswer: "The first member of a numeric enum gets the value 0 by default. If you do not state values explicitly, TypeScript assigns 0 to the first member automatically and gives every subsequent one the previous value plus 1. This is convenient for creating sequential constants without extra code.",
    longAnswer: "How numeric enums work in TypeScript\nNumeric enums in TypeScript are a way of defining a set of named constants with numeric values. If the values are not stated explicitly, the compiler assigns the value 0 to the first member automatically and gives every following one the previous value increased by 1. This behaviour is defined in the language specification and simplifies creating sequential enumerations.\n\nA code example\n\nIn this example Direction.Up gets the value 0 automatically, Down 1, Left 2 and Right 3. If you need to start from a different number, you can state the value for the first member explicitly, and the rest will continue the sequence.\n\nWhere this is applied\n\n- Defining states (order statuses, for example: New, Processing, Shipped).\n- Creating flags or options with numeric identifiers.\n- Simplifying code where the order of the members matters but the specific numbers do not.\n\nConclusion\nUse numeric enums without explicit values when you need simple sequential numbering of constants. This makes code readable and reduces the risk of errors when new members are added.",
    codeExample: "enum Direction {\n  Up,    // 0\n  Down,  // 1\n  Left,  // 2\n  Right  // 3\n}\n\nconsole.log(Direction.Up);    // 0\nconsole.log(Direction.Down);  // 1",
  },
  1992: {
    source: "Можно ли средствами TypeScript ограничить поле key в конфигурации колонок только ключами из переданных данных?",
    question: "Can TypeScript restrict the key field in a column configuration to the keys of the data that was passed in?",
    shortAnswer: "Yes, it can. Use generics with keyof to constrain the keys. For example, define a ColumnConfig type with the field key: keyof T. Then the function takes data of type T and an array of ColumnConfig. TypeScript will infer T automatically and check that key is only an existing key of the data.",
    longAnswer: "Restricting the keys in a column configuration with TypeScript\nTypeScript lets you type a column configuration strictly so that the `key` field can contain only the keys of the data that was passed in. This is achieved with generics and the `keyof` operator. The approach guarantees that you will not name a non-existent field, which prevents errors at compile time.\n\nHow it works\nCreate a generic type `ColumnConfig`, where `T` is the data type. The `key` field must be `keyof T`. Then a function that takes the data and the column configuration uses the generic to infer the data's type.\n\nUse in practice\nThis pattern is widely used in UI libraries (React Table, Ant Design Table, for example) to create type-safe tables. It is also useful when working with forms, where the form's fields have to match the keys of a data object.\n\nConclusion\nUse generics with `keyof` to constrain the keys in a column configuration. This increases the type safety of your code, reduces the number of errors and improves autocompletion in the IDE. It is especially useful in large projects with many tables and forms.",
    codeExample: "type ColumnConfig<T> = {\n  key: keyof T;\n  title: string;\n};\n\nfunction renderTable<T>(data: T[], columns: ColumnConfig<T>[]) {\n  // the implementation\n}\n\n// An example of use\nconst users = [\n  { id: 1, name: 'Alice', age: 30 },\n  { id: 2, name: 'Bob', age: 25 }\n];\n\nconst columns: ColumnConfig<typeof users[0]>[] = [\n  { key: 'id', title: 'ID' },\n  { key: 'name', title: 'Name' },\n  // { key: 'email', title: 'Email' } // Error: 'email' does not exist on the type\n];\n\nrenderTable(users, columns);",
  },
  1993: {
    source: "Как TypeScript помогает при работе с индексными типами и Record?",
    question: "How does TypeScript help when working with index types and Record?",
    shortAnswer: "Index types let you describe objects whose keys and values have particular types while the keys themselves are not known in advance. The Record utility creates an object type where every key from K has a value of type V. This is convenient for mappings, configurations and working with dynamic data, providing strict typing without losing flexibility.",
    longAnswer: "What are index types and Record?\nIn TypeScript index types let you describe objects whose keys may be arbitrary while the types of the keys and values are strictly defined. For example, the type `{ [key: string]: number }` means an object where all the keys are strings and the values are numbers. This is useful when the object's structure is not known in advance but the data types are fixed.\n\nThe Record utility\nRecord is a built-in utility type that simplifies creating such types. Writing `Record<K, V>` is equivalent to `{ [P in K]: V }`. K may be a union of string literals or the type `string`, `number` or `symbol`. This makes code more readable and reduces duplication.\n\nExamples of use\nConsider an example with an application's configuration:\n\nHere Record guarantees that the object contains only the given keys with the correct types. Another example is a mapping of statuses:\n\nA deeper understanding\nIndex types and Record are closely related to the `keyof` operator, which extracts the union of a type's keys. For example, `keyof ConfigKeys` would give `'apiUrl' | 'timeout' | 'retries'`. This lets you create generic functions that work with any object while preserving the typing.\n\nRecord can also be nested: `Record<string, Record<string, V>>` for two-dimensional structures. It is worth remembering, however, that Record does not support optional keys — for that you need `Partial<Record<K, V>>`.\n\nConclusion\nIndex types and Record are powerful tools for typing dynamic data structures. They are especially useful when working with configurations, mappings, API responses and any scenario where the keys are not fixed in advance but the types of the values are known. Using these constructs increases the reliability of your code and makes it easier to maintain.",
    codeExample: "type ConfigKeys = 'apiUrl' | 'timeout' | 'retries';\ntype Config = Record<ConfigKeys, string | number>;\n\nconst appConfig: Config = {\n  apiUrl: 'https://api.example.com',\n  timeout: 5000,\n  retries: 3\n};\n\n// Error: 'debug' is not part of ConfigKeys\n// appConfig.debug = true;\n\ntype Status = 'active' | 'inactive' | 'pending';\ntype StatusMessages = Record<Status, string>;\n\nconst messages: StatusMessages = {\n  active: 'User is active',\n  inactive: 'User is inactive',\n  pending: 'User is pending'\n};",
  },
  1994: {
    source: "Что такое принцип разделения интерфейсов (I в SOLID)?",
    question: "What is the Interface Segregation Principle (the I in SOLID)?",
    shortAnswer: "The Interface Segregation Principle (ISP) states that clients should not depend on interfaces they do not use. It is better to have several specialized interfaces than one general one. This reduces coupling in the code and makes it easier to maintain. For example, instead of a single Printer interface with print, scan and fax methods, it is better to create a separate interface for each function.",
    longAnswer: "What is the Interface Segregation Principle?\nThe Interface Segregation Principle (ISP) is the fourth SOLID principle, formulated by Robert Martin. It states that no client should depend on methods it does not use. In other words, it is better to have several narrowly specialized interfaces than one large, universal one.\n\nWhy does this matter?\nWhen an interface contains too many methods, the classes that implement it are forced to define even the methods they do not need. This leads to the following problems:\n- Coupling in the code increases — changes to the interface affect every implementing class.\n- Encapsulation is violated — clients gain access to functionality they do not require.\n- The readability and maintainability of the code decline.\n\nAn example of violating ISP\nImagine an interface for a multifunction device:\n\nIf a SimplePrinter class implements this interface, it will have to implement the scan and fax methods, which make no sense for a simple printer. This violates ISP.\n\nApplying ISP correctly\nLet us split the interface into several specialized ones:\n\nNow the SimplePrinter class implements only the Printer interface, while a MultiFunctionPrinter class can implement all three. This makes the system more flexible and easier to extend.\n\nConclusion\nThe Interface Segregation Principle helps you create loosely coupled, easily maintainable systems. It is worth applying when designing APIs, libraries and any abstractions where clients have different needs. By following ISP you reduce the number of unnecessary dependencies and make your code more resilient to change.",
    codeExample: "interface MultiFunctionPrinter {\n    void print(Document d);\n    void scan(Document d);\n    void fax(Document d);\n}\n\ninterface Printer {\n    void print(Document d);\n}\ninterface Scanner {\n    void scan(Document d);\n}\ninterface Fax {\n    void fax(Document d);\n}",
  },
  1995: {
    source: "Можно ли описать тип данных через понятие множества в математическом смысле?",
    question: "Can a data type be described through the mathematical notion of a set?",
    shortAnswer: "Yes, a data type can be described as a set of permissible values. For example, the boolean type is the set {true, false}. The number type in TypeScript is the set of all numbers. This way of looking at it helps you define strictly which operations are allowed and which values a variable may hold.",
    longAnswer: "The connection between data types and sets\nIn mathematics a set is a collection of distinct elements. In programming a data type determines which values a variable may hold and which operations on them are allowed. A data type can therefore be regarded as the set of all the possible values of that type.\n\nExamples\n\n- The type `boolean` is the set `{true, false}`.\n- The type `number` in TypeScript is the set of all numbers (floating-point ones).\n- The type `string` is the set of all strings.\n\nPractical use\nThis way of looking at it is useful when designing types and verifying the correctness of programs. For example, in TypeScript you can define a custom type as a union or an intersection of sets:\n\nConclusion\nUnderstanding types as sets helps you think about data rigorously, avoid errors and design more reliable systems. It is especially useful when working with static typing and complex types.",
    codeExample: "type Status = 'active' | 'inactive'; // a set of two strings\ntype Admin = User & { role: 'admin' }; // an intersection of sets",
  },
  1996: {
    source: "Почему добавление async меняет структуру возвращаемого типа функции в TypeScript?",
    question: "Why does adding async change the structure of a function's return type in TypeScript?",
    shortAnswer: "When a function is declared with async, TypeScript wraps its return value in a Promise automatically. Even if the function returns a number or a string, the return type becomes Promise<number> or Promise<string>. This happens because async functions always return a Promise, which is what lets you use await when calling them.",
    longAnswer: "How async changes the return type\nThe `async` keyword in TypeScript wraps a function's return value in a Promise automatically. This is fundamental behaviour that guarantees async functions always return a Promise, even when there is no explicit return of a Promise inside.\n\nAn example\n\nWithout async the same function would simply return a number. With async TypeScript infers the type as Promise<number> automatically.\n\nWhy this matters\n\n- It ensures uniformity: all async functions return a Promise, which simplifies working with asynchronous code.\n- It lets you use await when calling such functions.\n- It prevents errors where a developer forgets to wrap the result in a Promise.\n\nConclusion\nUse async when a function contains asynchronous operations (API requests, for example). This automatically makes the return type a Promise, which improves the readability and safety of the code.",
    codeExample: "async function getNumber(): Promise<number> {\n  return 42;\n}\n\n// The return type: Promise<number>\nconst result = getNumber(); // result: Promise<number>",
  },
  1997: {
    source: "Почему TypeScript позволяет выбрасывать через throw значения любого типа, включая примитивы?",
    question: "Why does TypeScript let you throw values of any type, primitives included?",
    shortAnswer: "TypeScript inherited from JavaScript the ability to throw values of any type, primitives included. This is because TypeScript is a superset of JavaScript and does not restrict the behaviour of throw. In practice, however, it is recommended to throw only instances of the Error class, since they carry a call stack and make debugging easier.",
    longAnswer: "Why does TypeScript let you throw values of any type?\nTypeScript is a strict superset of JavaScript, which means any valid JavaScript code is also valid TypeScript code. In JavaScript the `throw` operator can be used with any value: strings, numbers, objects, functions and so on. TypeScript preserves this flexibility in order not to break compatibility with existing code.\n\nExamples of throwing different types\n\nWhy can this be a problem?\nAlthough TypeScript lets you throw any value, this can complicate error handling. When using `catch` blocks the type of the caught value is `unknown` by default (from TypeScript 4.0), which requires an additional type check before use. If you throw only instances of `Error`, you can safely access the `message` and `stack` properties.\n\nRecommendations\n\n- Always throw instances of the `Error` class or its subclasses.\n- Use custom error classes for more precise handling.\n- Avoid throwing primitives, since this makes debugging harder.\n\nConclusion\nTypeScript preserves the ability to throw any type for compatibility with JavaScript, but in practice it is recommended to use only instances of `Error` in order to ensure predictable error handling and better debugging.",
    codeExample: "throw 'An error'; // a string\nthrow 42; // a number\nthrow { message: 'An error' }; // an object\nthrow new Error('An error'); // an instance of Error",
  },
  1998: {
    source: "Почему методы вроде JSON.parse и fetch возвращают any вместо unknown в TypeScript?",
    question: "Why do methods like JSON.parse and fetch return any rather than unknown in TypeScript?",
    shortAnswer: "The JSON.parse and fetch methods return any because of backward compatibility and historical reasons. When TypeScript first appeared, unknown did not yet exist, and any was the only way to describe an unknown type. Changing the return type to unknown would break millions of existing projects. The TypeScript developers therefore left any in place, but recommend stating the type by hand with generics or annotations.",
    longAnswer: "Why JSON.parse and fetch return any\nIn TypeScript the `JSON.parse` and `fetch` methods return the type `any` rather than `unknown` for historical reasons. When TypeScript first appeared (in 2012) the `unknown` type did not yet exist — it was only added in version 3.0 (2018). At the time `any` was the only way to describe a value whose type is not known at compile time.\n\nThe backward-compatibility problem\nChanging the return type from `any` to `unknown` would break millions of existing projects. The TypeScript developers decided to keep `any` for these methods so as not to break backward compatibility. They do, however, recommend that developers state the expected type by hand.\n\nHow to use them correctly\nRather than relying on `any`, it is better to state the type explicitly:\n\nConclusion\nUse explicit type annotations or generics when working with `JSON.parse` and `fetch` in order to preserve type safety. This will help you avoid runtime errors and make your code more predictable.",
    codeExample: "// Bad — returns any\nconst data = JSON.parse('{\"name\":\"John\"}');\nconsole.log(data.age); // No error, but age may not exist\n\n// Good — we state the type\ninterface User {\n  name: string;\n  age?: number;\n}\nconst user: User = JSON.parse('{\"name\":\"John\"}');\nconsole.log(user.age); // TypeScript will point out that age is optional\n\n// The same goes for fetch\nconst response = await fetch('/api/user');\nconst userData: User = await response.json();",
  },
  1999: {
    source: "Почему в TypeScript нет аналога throws из Java для обозначения исключений в сигнатуре функции?",
    question: "Why does TypeScript have no equivalent of Java's throws for declaring exceptions in a function's signature?",
    shortAnswer: "TypeScript has no equivalent of Java's throws because TypeScript follows JavaScript's philosophy, where exceptions are not part of the type system. In JavaScript errors can occur anywhere, and handling them with try/catch does not require a declaration in the signature. TypeScript focuses on the static typing of data rather than on controlling exceptions, which simplifies code and reduces coupling.",
    longAnswer: "Why does TypeScript have no checked exceptions?\nTypeScript, being a layer on top of JavaScript, inherits its dynamic nature of error handling. In JavaScript exceptions are not part of the type system — any function can throw any exception at any moment. TypeScript does not add checked exceptions (as in Java) because that would contradict the language's philosophy and complicate integration with existing JavaScript code.\n\nThe main reasons there is no throws in TypeScript\n\n- The dynamic nature of JavaScript: in JavaScript errors can arise not only explicitly (throw new Error()) but also implicitly (a ReferenceError when accessing a non-existent variable, for example). Declaring every possible exception in a signature is impossible.\n- Compatibility with the ecosystem: TypeScript has to be backward compatible with JavaScript. Adding checked exceptions would require changing every existing library and piece of code.\n- Simpler development: checked exceptions in Java often lead to cumbersome code with many try/catch blocks or throws clauses in signatures. TypeScript prefers a more concise approach.\n\nHow does TypeScript handle errors?\nTypeScript uses return types to indicate possible errors explicitly. For example, you can return an object with an error field or use discriminated unions:\n\nThis approach makes error handling explicit and type-safe without requiring checked exceptions.\n\nConclusion\nTypeScript does not include throws because of its dynamic foundation and its drive for simplicity. Instead it is recommended to use return types for explicit error handling, which makes code more predictable and easier to understand, especially on large projects.",
    codeExample: "type Result<T> = { success: true; data: T } | { success: false; error: string };\n\nfunction parseJSON(json: string): Result<any> {\n  try {\n    const data = JSON.parse(json);\n    return { success: true, data };\n  } catch (e) {\n    return { success: false, error: (e as Error).message };\n  }\n}\n\nconst result = parseJSON('invalid');\nif (result.success) {\n  console.log(result.data);\n} else {\n  console.error(result.error);\n}",
  },
  2000: {
    source: "Как создать интерфейс TypeScript на основе существующего, но без определённого поля?",
    question: "How do you create a TypeScript interface from an existing one but without a particular field?",
    shortAnswer: "To create an interface from an existing one but without a particular field, use the built-in utility type Omit. Omit creates a new type by excluding the given keys from the source type. For example, `Omit<User, 'password'>` will create a type without the password field. This lets you reuse existing types without duplicating them.",
    longAnswer: "What is Omit and what is it for?\nIn TypeScript a situation often arises where you need to create a new type from an existing one but exclude some fields. Instead of rewriting the whole interface by hand, you can use the built-in utility type `Omit`. It takes two parameters: the source type and a union of the keys that have to be excluded.\n\nAn example of use\nSuppose we have a user interface with a password that must not be sent to the client:\n\nHow does it work under the hood?\nThe `Omit` type is implemented as a combination of `Pick` and `Exclude`. It takes all the keys of the source type, excludes the given ones and then picks only the remaining fields. This lets you manipulate types flexibly without duplicating code.\n\nWhen is this useful?\nOmit is especially useful when working with APIs, where sensitive data has to be hidden, or when creating partial versions of objects for edit forms. It helps you maintain type safety and avoid errors when the source interface changes.\n\nConclusion: use Omit to create derived types with fields excluded when you need to reuse an existing structure with restrictions. This improves the maintainability of your code and reduces duplication.",
    codeExample: "interface User {\n  id: number;\n  name: string;\n  email: string;\n  password: string;\n}\n\n// We create a type without the password field\ntype PublicUser = Omit<User, 'password'>;\n\n// PublicUser now contains only id, name and email\nconst user: PublicUser = {\n  id: 1,\n  name: 'Alice',\n  email: 'alice@example.com'\n};",
  },
  2001: {
    source: "Зачем использовать import type в TypeScript и чем он отличается от обычного import?",
    question: "Why use import type in TypeScript, and how does it differ from an ordinary import?",
    shortAnswer: "import type is used to import only types, not values. Unlike an ordinary import, it generates no code in JavaScript at compile time, which reduces bundle size and improves performance. This is especially useful together with build tools such as Webpack, for better tree shaking.",
    longAnswer: "What is import type?\nIn TypeScript `import type` is syntax that lets you import only types (interfaces, types, classes as types) without including the corresponding values in the resulting JavaScript code. An ordinary `import` brings in both types and values, which can lead to unnecessary code in the bundle.\n\nThe main differences\n\n- Code generation: `import type` is removed entirely at compile time, whereas an ordinary import leaves code behind for the values.\n- Tree shaking: using `import type` helps build tools (Webpack, for example) optimize the bundle better by removing unused imports.\n- Safety: `import type` guarantees that you do not use the imported module as a value, which prevents accidental errors.\n\nA code example\n\nIn this example `import type { User }` will add no code to the JavaScript, while `import { greet }` will. If we had used only `import type` for `User`, the `greet` function would not have been available.\n\nWhen to apply it\nUse `import type` for every import that is needed only for types (interfaces, types, class types). This improves build performance and reduces bundle size, especially in large projects with many types.\n\nConclusion: `import type` is a tool for optimizing TypeScript projects that helps you avoid unnecessary code in JavaScript and improves tree shaking. Apply it everywhere you import only types, in order to make your code cleaner and more efficient.",
    codeExample: "// types.ts\nexport interface User {\n  name: string;\n  age: number;\n}\n\nexport function greet(user: User) {\n  console.log(`Hello, ${user.name}`);\n}\n\n// app.ts\n// An ordinary import — brings in both the type and the function\nimport { User, greet } from './types';\n\n// import type — brings in only the type\nimport type { User } from './types';\n\n// Using the type\nconst user: User = { name: 'Alice', age: 30 };\ngreet(user); // The greet function has to be imported separately",
  },
  2002: {
    source: "Что такое утилитарный тип Record в TypeScript?",
    question: "What is the Record utility type in TypeScript?",
    shortAnswer: "Record is a built-in utility type in TypeScript that creates an object type whose keys have the type K and whose values have the type V. It is convenient for creating dictionaries or mappings when you need to guarantee that every key of a particular set has a value of the same type. For example, `Record<string, number>` creates an object with string keys and numeric values.",
    longAnswer: "What is Record in TypeScript?\nRecord is one of TypeScript's built-in utility types, which lets you create typed objects with a fixed set of keys and a single value type. It is especially useful when you need to describe a data structure resembling a dictionary or associative array where all the values have the same type.\n\nThe syntax and its use\nRecord takes two parameters: the type of the keys (usually string, number or a union of strings) and the type of the values. For example:\n\nIf the keys are known in advance, you can use a union of types for strict typing:\n\nPractical use\nRecord is often applied for configurations, status mappings, form handling and any scenario requiring strict typing of objects with dynamic keys. It helps you avoid errors caused by incorrect value types or missing keys.\n\nConclusion\nRecord is a powerful tool for creating typed dictionaries in TypeScript. It is worth using when you need to guarantee that all of an object's values have one type and that the keys correspond to a particular set, which increases the reliability and readability of your code.",
    codeExample: "type UserRoles = Record<string, string>;\nconst roles: UserRoles = {\n  admin: 'full_access',\n  user: 'limited_access'\n};\n\ntype Role = 'admin' | 'user' | 'guest';\ntype RolePermissions = Record<Role, string[]>;\nconst permissions: RolePermissions = {\n  admin: ['read', 'write', 'delete'],\n  user: ['read'],\n  guest: []\n};",
  },
  2003: {
    source: "Что такое ReactFC и какие исторические проблемы с типизацией children были связаны с этим типом?",
    question: "What is React.FC and what historical problems with typing children were associated with this type?",
    shortAnswer: "React.FC (or React.FunctionComponent) is a type for functional components in React with TypeScript. Historically it added children to the props automatically, which led to problems: components that took no children accepted them anyway, and it also did not support generics. Nowadays it is recommended to state children explicitly or to use types without React.FC.",
    longAnswer: "What is React.FC?\nReact.FC (or React.FunctionComponent) is a type built into React for typing functional components with TypeScript. It simplifies declaring a component by adding children to the props automatically and returning JSX.Element.\n\nThe historical problems with typing children\nThe main problem with React.FC was that it added children to the props implicitly, even when the component was not supposed to accept them. This led to errors where a developer accidentally passed child elements to a component that did not handle them. For example:\n\nIn addition, React.FC did not support generics, which complicated creating reusable components with different prop types. It also did not allow defaultProps to be used with correct typing.\n\nThe modern approach\nFrom React 18 onwards it is recommended to avoid React.FC and state children explicitly in the props if they are needed. This makes code more predictable and flexible. An example:\n\nThis approach eliminates the implicit addition of children and lets you control the types better.\n\nConclusion\nReact.FC was useful for quick typing, but because of the problems with children and its limitations it is better to declare props explicitly. This matters especially on large projects, where precise types are critical for preventing errors.",
    codeExample: "// A component that expects no children\nconst Header: React.FC = () => <h1>Title</h1>;\n\n// No error arises, even though children are not wanted\n<Header>Some text</Header>;\n\n// Stating children explicitly\ninterface HeaderProps {\n  title: string;\n  children?: React.ReactNode;\n}\n\nconst Header = ({ title, children }: HeaderProps) => {\n  return (\n    <div>\n      <h1>{title}</h1>\n      {children}\n    </div>\n  );\n};",
  },
  2004: {
    source: "Что означает модификатор public перед полем в конструкторе класса?",
    question: "What does the public modifier before a field in a class constructor mean?",
    shortAnswer: "The public modifier before a constructor parameter in TypeScript creates and initializes a public class property with the same name automatically. This shortens the code by sparing you the manual declaration of the field and the assignment of the value. This syntax is called a parameter property and is available only in TypeScript, not in JavaScript.",
    longAnswer: "What a parameter property is in TypeScript\nWhen you write `constructor(public name: string)`, TypeScript creates a public `name` property on the class automatically and assigns the value it was given. This is equivalent to declaring the field by hand and assigning it in the constructor's body.\n\nA comparison\nWithout the syntactic sugar:\n\nWith a parameter property:\n\nBoth variants are identical after compilation. The `private`, `protected` and `readonly` modifiers work the same way.\n\nWhere it is applied\n\n- In classes with simple constructors, where fields need to be declared and initialized quickly.\n- In DI containers (Angular, NestJS, for example) for injecting dependencies through the constructor.\n\nConclusion\nUse parameter properties to reduce boilerplate when a class field corresponds directly to a constructor parameter. This improves readability and reduces the chance of errors in manual assignment.",
    codeExample: "class Person {\n  public name: string;\n  constructor(name: string) {\n    this.name = name;\n  }\n}\n\nclass Person {\n  constructor(public name: string) {}\n}",
  },
  2005: {
    source: "Как описать тип объекта с произвольными ключами и значениями в TypeScript с помощью Record?",
    question: "How do you describe the type of an object with arbitrary keys and values in TypeScript using Record?",
    shortAnswer: "Record is a built-in utility type in TypeScript that creates an object type whose keys have the type K and whose values have the type V. For example, `Record<string, number>` means an object with any string keys and numeric values. It is a convenient alternative to index signatures when you need to describe a dictionary or a map.",
    longAnswer: "What is Record in TypeScript?\nRecord is a built-in utility type that lets you describe an object with arbitrary keys of type K and values of type V. It is often used to create types representing dictionaries, maps or configurations where the keys are not known in advance but the values are all of the same type.\n\nThe syntax and examples\nThe basic syntax is `Record<KeyType, ValueType>`. KeyType is usually string, number or symbol, and ValueType is any type.\n\nA comparison with index signatures\nRecord is syntactic sugar over index signatures. Instead of `{ [key: string]: number }` you can write `Record<string, number>`. Record is more concise and readable, especially when working with union types of keys.\n\nPractical use\nRecord is often used to:\n- Describe configurations with dynamic keys\n- Create types for API responses where the keys are identifiers\n- Implement the \"registry\" or \"factory\" pattern\n\nConclusion\nRecord is a powerful, convenient tool for typing objects with arbitrary keys. It is worth applying when you need to describe a dictionary, a map or any structure where the keys are dynamic and the values are all of the same type. It makes code more readable and safer than hand-written index signatures.",
    codeExample: "// A simple example: a dictionary of strings\nconst dict: Record<string, string> = {\n  name: \"Alice\",\n  role: \"developer\"\n};\n\n// With numeric keys\nconst scores: Record<number, number> = {\n  1: 95,\n  2: 87\n};\n\n// With a union of keys\ntype Status = \"active\" | \"inactive\";\nconst users: Record<Status, number> = {\n  active: 10,\n  inactive: 3\n};\n\n// An index signature\ninterface StringNumberMap {\n  [key: string]: number;\n}\n\n// The equivalent through Record\ntype StringNumberMap = Record<string, number>;\n\n// An example: an application's configuration\ninterface AppConfig {\n  port: number;\n  debug: boolean;\n}\n\nconst config: Record<string, AppConfig> = {\n  production: { port: 8080, debug: false },\n  development: { port: 3000, debug: true }\n};",
  },
  2006: {
    source: "Как TypeScript помогает контролировать работу с null и переприсвоением типов?",
    question: "How does TypeScript help you control work with null and the reassignment of types?",
    shortAnswer: "With strictNullChecks enabled, TypeScript prevents null or undefined from being assigned to variables unless that is allowed explicitly. For working with values that may be null, union types (`string | null`, for example) and type guards are used. Control over reassigning types is achieved with readonly, const and strict typing, which prevents a variable's type from being changed by accident.",
    longAnswer: "How TypeScript helps you control null and the reassignment of types\nTypeScript provides powerful tools for working safely with null and undefined, and for controlling changes to variables' types. This matters especially for preventing common runtime errors caused by unexpected null values or by the unintended change of a data type.\n\nControlling null and undefined\nThe main mechanism is the strictNullChecks flag in tsconfig.json. When it is enabled, TypeScript does not allow null or undefined to be assigned to a variable unless its type explicitly permits those values. For example:\n\nTo allow null you have to use a union type:\n\nFor working safely with such variables, type guards are applied — a check for null, for example:\n\nYou can also use the optional chaining (?.) and nullish coalescing (??) operators:\n\nControlling the reassignment of types\nTypeScript prevents a variable's type from being changed by accident through strict typing. If a variable is declared with a particular type, a value of a different type cannot be assigned to it without an explicit cast:\n\nFor immutable variables the const keyword is used, which forbids reassignment:\n\nYou can also use the readonly modifier for the properties of objects or for arrays:\n\nConclusion\nWith strictNullChecks, union types, type guards and the readonly modifier, TypeScript provides strict control over null values and the reassignment of types. This makes code more predictable and safer, especially on large projects where such errors are hard to track down by hand.",
    codeExample: "let name: string = 'Alice';\nname = null; // Error: Type 'null' is not assignable to type 'string'\n\nlet name: string | null = 'Alice';\nname = null; // OK\n\nfunction greet(name: string | null) {\n  if (name !== null) {\n    console.log(name.toUpperCase()); // TypeScript knows that name is a string\n  }\n}\n\nconst city = user?.address?.city ?? 'Unknown';\n\nlet count: number = 5;\ncount = 'hello'; // Error: Type 'string' is not assignable to type 'number'\n\nconst pi: number = 3.14;\npi = 3.14159; // Error: Cannot assign to 'pi' because it is a constant\n\ninterface Config {\n  readonly apiUrl: string;\n}\nconst config: Config = { apiUrl: 'https://api.example.com' };\nconfig.apiUrl = 'new-url'; // Error: Cannot assign to 'apiUrl' because it is a read-only property",
  },
  2007: {
    source: "Что такое пересечение (&) и объединение (|) типов в TypeScript?",
    question: "What are intersection (&) and union (|) types in TypeScript?",
    shortAnswer: "An intersection type (&) creates a type that has to satisfy all the combined types at once. A union type (|) creates a type that may be one of the given types. Intersection is used to combine several types into one, union to state alternatives.",
    longAnswer: "What are intersection and union types?\nIn TypeScript intersection types and union types are powerful tools for working with types. An intersection, denoted by the `&` symbol, lets you create a new type that includes all the properties and methods of all the given types. A union, denoted by the `|` symbol, creates a type that may be one of the listed types.\n\nIntersection types (&)\nAn intersection type is used when an object has to conform to several types at once. For example, if you have a `User` type and an `Admin` type, the intersection `User & Admin` will contain all the properties of both.\n\nIn this example `SuperUser` must have every field from `User` and from `Admin`. If some field is missing, TypeScript reports an error.\n\nUnion types (|)\nA union type is used when a value may be one of several types. For example, a function may accept a string or a number.\n\nWhen working with a union type, TypeScript lets you access only the properties common to all the types. To reach the unique properties you have to use type narrowing.\n\nPractical use\nIntersection types are often used to combine interfaces — for example, when creating extended configurations or mixing functionality. Union types are useful for handling different data formats, such as API responses that may be successful or erroneous.\n\nConclusion\nIntersection and union types are fundamental TypeScript concepts that help you create flexible, safe types. Intersection suits combining several types into one; union suits working with alternative types. Use them to improve the readability and reliability of your code.",
    codeExample: "interface User {\n  name: string;\n  age: number;\n}\n\ninterface Admin {\n  role: string;\n  permissions: string[];\n}\n\ntype SuperUser = User & Admin;\n\nconst user: SuperUser = {\n  name: 'Alice',\n  age: 30,\n  role: 'admin',\n  permissions: ['read', 'write']\n};\n\nfunction printId(id: string | number) {\n  console.log('ID:', id);\n}\n\nprintId('abc'); // OK\nprintId(123);   // OK\n// printId(true); // Error: boolean is not part of the union",
  },
  2008: {
    source: "Как создать сервис в Angular и для чего он используется?",
    question: "How do you create a service in Angular and what is it used for?",
    shortAnswer: "A service in Angular is a class with the @Injectable decorator, used to move shared logic (working with an API, holding state, calculations) out of components. It is registered in the providers of a module or component and injected through the constructor. Services make code reusable and testable and follow the single-responsibility principle.",
    longAnswer: "A service in Angular is an ordinary TypeScript class marked with the `@Injectable` decorator. A service's main purpose is to move business logic, data handling and shared functions outside components, so that the components stay \"thin\" and deal only with presentation.\n\nCreating a service\nA service is created with the Angular CLI using the command `ng generate service my-service`. By hand you need to define a class with the `@Injectable` decorator and state at which level it will be available (`providedIn: 'root'` for a global singleton, for example).\n\nUse through Dependency Injection\nAngular uses a dependency injection mechanism. The service is registered with the providers (the root injector, a module or a component), after which it can be requested in the constructor of any component or other service.\n\nWhere it is applied\n\n- Working with HTTP (HttpClient) — API calls.\n- Shared state (a shopping cart, for example).\n- Logging, error handling, caching.\n- Exchanging data between unrelated components.\n\nConclusion: services in Angular are the main way of organizing logic that is not tied to a template. Using them makes code cleaner and simplifies testing and reuse. It is recommended to move any logic into services except direct work with the DOM or the handling of view events.",
    codeExample: "import { Injectable } from '@angular/core';\n\n@Injectable({\n  providedIn: 'root' // the service will be a singleton for the whole application\n})\nexport class DataService {\n  private data: string[] = [];\n\n  getData(): string[] {\n    return this.data;\n  }\n\n  addData(item: string): void {\n    this.data.push(item);\n  }\n}\n\nimport { Component } from '@angular/core';\nimport { DataService } from './data.service';\n\n@Component({\n  selector: 'app-example',\n  template: `<ul><li *ngFor=\"let item of items\">{{item}}</li></ul>`\n})\nexport class ExampleComponent {\n  items: string[];\n\n  constructor(private dataService: DataService) {\n    this.items = this.dataService.getData();\n  }\n}",
  },
  2009: {
    source: "Как сделать поля класса неизменяемыми? В каком месте класса можно инициализировать поле readonly?",
    question: "How do you make a class's fields immutable? Where in a class can a readonly field be initialized?",
    shortAnswer: "A class field is made immutable with the readonly modifier. Such a field can be initialized either at its declaration or in the class's constructor. After that its value cannot be changed. This helps protect data from accidental modification and makes code more predictable.",
    longAnswer: "What are readonly fields in TypeScript?\nThe `readonly` modifier in TypeScript lets you make a class property immutable once it has been initialized. This means that after a value has been assigned to the field, it cannot be changed. The approach is useful for creating configurations, constants or any data that must not change during an object's life.\n\nWhere can a readonly field be initialized?\nA readonly field can be initialized in only two places:\n- Directly at the field's declaration\n- In the class's constructor\n\nAn attempt to change a readonly field in any other method of the class will cause a compile error.\n\nA code example\n\nConclusion\nUse readonly for fields that have to stay unchanged after the object is created. This increases the reliability of your code and prevents accidental changes to important data, especially on large projects with many developers.",
    codeExample: "class Config {\n  readonly apiUrl: string = 'https://api.example.com'; // initialization at the declaration\n  readonly timeout: number;\n\n  constructor(timeout: number) {\n    this.timeout = timeout; // initialization in the constructor\n  }\n\n  updateTimeout(newTimeout: number): void {\n    // this.timeout = newTimeout; // Error! A readonly field cannot be changed\n  }\n}\n\nconst config = new Config(5000);\nconsole.log(config.apiUrl); // 'https://api.example.com'\nconsole.log(config.timeout); // 5000",
  },
  2010: {
    source: "Чем отличается шаблон «Фабрика» от «Фабричный метод»?",
    question: "How does the \"Factory\" pattern differ from \"Factory Method\"?",
    shortAnswer: "Factory Method is a pattern that defines an interface for creating an object but lets subclasses change the type of object that gets created. Factory (or Abstract Factory) is a pattern that provides an interface for creating families of related objects without specifying their concrete classes. The main difference: Factory Method uses inheritance and delegates the creation of the object to subclasses, whereas Factory uses composition and delegates creation to a whole factory object.",
    longAnswer: "An introduction to the patterns\nThe Factory Method and Factory patterns are often confused, although they solve different problems. Both are creational patterns, but they differ in their level of abstraction and in how they delegate the creation of objects.\n\nFactory Method\nThis pattern defines an interface for creating a single object but lets subclasses decide which concrete class to instantiate. It uses inheritance: the base class declares a factory method and the subclasses override it.\n\nFactory (Abstract Factory)\nThe Factory pattern provides an interface for creating families of related objects. It uses composition: the client receives a factory object and calls its methods to create products.\n\nThe key differences\n\n- The goal: Factory Method creates one object, Factory a family of objects.\n- The implementation: Factory Method uses inheritance and overriding a method; Factory uses composition and a separate factory class.\n- Flexibility: Factory is more flexible, since it lets you swap out whole families of products, but it is harder to implement.\n\nConclusion\nUse Factory Method when you need to delegate the creation of a single object to subclasses, and Factory when groups of related objects have to be created with a guarantee of their compatibility.",
    codeExample: "abstract class Document {\n    abstract createPage(): Page;\n    open() {\n        const page = this.createPage();\n        page.render();\n    }\n}\nclass Resume extends Document {\n    createPage(): Page {\n        return new SkillPage();\n    }\n}\n\ninterface GUIFactory {\n    createButton(): Button;\n    createCheckbox(): Checkbox;\n}\nclass WinFactory implements GUIFactory {\n    createButton(): Button { return new WinButton(); }\n    createCheckbox(): Checkbox { return new WinCheckbox(); }\n}",
  },
  2011: {
    source: "Что такое избыточная ответственность метода или класса (\"слишком много\" в одном методе/классе)?",
    question: "What is excessive responsibility in a method or class (\"too much\" in one method/class)?",
    shortAnswer: "Excessive responsibility is the situation where a method or class performs several unrelated tasks. It violates the single-responsibility principle (SRP), reduces readability and complicates testing and maintenance. Such code is hard to change without the risk of breaking other parts of the system.",
    longAnswer: "What is excessive responsibility?\nExcessive responsibility (a violation of the Single Responsibility Principle) arises when one class or method takes on the performance of several logically unrelated tasks. For example, a class may process data, save it to a database and send email notifications all at once. This makes code rigid and hard to understand and change.\n\nAn example of the problem\nConsider a class responsible both for calculating salaries and for sending a report:\n\nHere three different responsibilities are mixed into one class. If the report's format changes, you will have to change a class that is also responsible for calculating salaries — which increases the risk of errors.\n\nHow do you fix it?\nSplit the class into several, each with its own clear task:\n\nConclusion\nObserving the single-responsibility principle makes code more modular, testable and adaptable to change. Apply this approach whenever you notice that a class or method is starting to perform more than one logical task.",
    codeExample: "class EmployeeService {\n  calculateSalary(employee) {\n    // complex calculations\n  }\n  sendReport() {\n    // sending an email\n  }\n  saveToDatabase() {\n    // SQL queries\n  }\n}\n\nclass SalaryCalculator {\n  calculate(employee) { /* ... */ }\n}\nclass ReportSender {\n  send(report) { /* ... */ }\n}\nclass EmployeeRepository {\n  save(employee) { /* ... */ }\n}",
  },
  2012: {
    source: "Что такое namespace в TypeScript и для чего они используются?",
    question: "What is a namespace in TypeScript and what are they used for?",
    shortAnswer: "A namespace in TypeScript is a way of grouping code logically that helps avoid name conflicts in the global scope. Namespaces let you gather related functions, classes and interfaces under one name. They are used to organize code in large projects, especially when ES6 modules are not in use.",
    longAnswer: "What is a namespace in TypeScript?\nA namespace is a TypeScript construct that lets you group related entities (classes, interfaces, functions) under one name. This helps avoid polluting the global scope and name conflicts, especially on large projects.\n\nHow do namespaces work?\nNamespaces are created with the `namespace` keyword. Everything declared inside is private by default unless it is exported with `export`. To reach a namespace's members the syntax `NamespaceName.Member` is used.\n\nWhen should you use a namespace?\nNamespaces are useful in the following cases:\n- When you are working with the global context (in the browser without modules, for example).\n- To organize code in large projects where ES6 modules are not used.\n- To create libraries that will be included through `<script>` tags.\n\nThe difference from modules\nIn modern TypeScript it is recommended to use ES6 modules (import/export) rather than namespaces. Modules provide better isolation, support from build tools and more predictable behaviour. Namespaces remain relevant for backward compatibility and for specific scenarios.\n\nConclusion: a namespace is a tool for organizing code in TypeScript that helps avoid name conflicts. They are worth applying in projects where ES6 modules are unavailable, or for creating global libraries, but modern development prefers modules.",
    codeExample: "namespace Geometry {\n  export interface Point {\n    x: number;\n    y: number;\n  }\n\n  export function distance(p1: Point, p2: Point): number {\n    return Math.sqrt((p2.x - p1.x) ** 2 + (p2.y - p1.y) ** 2);\n  }\n}\n\nconst p1: Geometry.Point = { x: 0, y: 0 };\nconst p2: Geometry.Point = { x: 3, y: 4 };\nconsole.log(Geometry.distance(p1, p2)); // 5",
  },
  2013: {
    source: "Что делает ключевое слово infer в TypeScript?",
    question: "What does the infer keyword do in TypeScript?",
    shortAnswer: "The infer keyword is used in TypeScript's conditional types to declare a type variable that is inferred from the type being checked. It lets you extract and reuse parts of types — a function's return type or an array's element type, for example. Infer works only inside an extends with the ternary operator and cannot be used outside conditional types.",
    longAnswer: "What is infer in TypeScript?\nThe `infer` keyword is a powerful TypeScript tool that lets you declare type variables inside conditional types. It is used to capture and then use a part of the type being checked in the condition. Without `infer` you would not be able to extract inner types from complex structures dynamically.\n\nHow does it work?\nInfer is applied only in the construct `T extends U ? X : Y`, where `U` contains `infer`. When TypeScript checks whether `T` matches the pattern `U`, it infers a type for the variable declared through `infer` and substitutes it into the `X` branch.\n\nExamples of use\nConsider extracting an array's element type:\n\nAnother example is obtaining a function's return type:\n\nWhere is it applied?\nInfer is widely used in TypeScript's built-in utility types such as `ReturnType`, `Parameters`, `ConstructorParameters` and `InstanceType`. Developers also apply it to create their own advanced types — for extracting types from a Promise or from React components, for example.\n\nConclusion\nInfer is a key mechanism for working with conditional types in TypeScript, letting you extract and reuse parts of types dynamically. It is indispensable when creating flexible, type-safe abstractions, especially in libraries and frameworks where types have to be manipulated at a high level.",
    codeExample: "type ArrayElement<T> = T extends (infer U)[] ? U : never;\ntype Num = ArrayElement<number[]>; // number\ntype Str = ArrayElement<string[]>; // string\n\ntype ReturnType<T> = T extends (...args: any[]) => infer R ? R : never;\ntype Fn = (x: number) => string;\ntype Result = ReturnType<Fn>; // string",
  },
  // ---- end TypeScript ----
  // ---- HTML ----
  1: {
    source: "Как выглядит базовая структура HTML страницы?",
    question: "What does the basic structure of an HTML page look like?",
    shortAnswer: "The basic structure of an HTML page begins with `<!DOCTYPE html>` to declare the document type. Then comes the `<html>` tag, inside which there are two key sections: `<head>` (for meta information, styles and links to resources) and `<body>` (for the content the user sees). For example:\n\n`<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html>\n<head>\n<title>My first page</title>\n</head>\n<body>\n<h1>Hello, world!</h1>\n</body>\n</html>`",
    longAnswer: "HTML (HyperText Markup Language) is used to create the structure of web pages. Every HTML page begins with the document type declaration `<!DOCTYPE html>`, which tells the browser that the latest version of HTML (HTML5) is being used.\nThe page's structure is organized into tags:\n\n- `<html>`: the page's root element. All the HTML code it contains sits inside it.\n\n- `<head>`: the section for meta information. This is where the following go:\n\n- `<title>`: the page's title, shown in the browser tab.\n\n- `<meta>`: meta tags, for stating the character encoding, for example (`<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">`).\n\n- Linking styles (`<link>`) or scripts (`<script>`).\n\n- `<body>`: the section containing the page's visible content — text, images, buttons and so on.\n\nAn example of the basic structure:\n\n`<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">` sets the UTF-8 encoding so that text displays correctly.\n`<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0\">` helps adapt the page for mobile devices.\n\nCreating the page's basic structure is the first step when developing a web application. For example, if you want to build a business card or a landing page, you will start from the structure described above. It also serves as the foundation for adding styles (CSS) and logic (JavaScript).",
    codeExample: "<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html>\n  <head>\n    <meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n    <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0\">\n    <title>A basic HTML page</title>\n  </head>\n  <body>\n    <h1>Welcome!</h1>\n    <p>This is an example of the basic HTML structure.</p>\n  </body>\n</html>",
  },
  2: {
    source: "Что такое мета-теги?",
    question: "What are meta tags?",
    shortAnswer: "Meta tags are elements in the `<head>` of an HTML document that carry meta information — that is, data about the page itself. They are not shown to the user directly, but they affect indexing in search engines, the correct display of the page and interaction with social networks.\nFor example, `<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">` sets the encoding, while `<meta name=\"description\" content=\"A description of the page\">` gives search engines a description of the page.",
    longAnswer: "Meta tags (from the word \"meta information\") are special tags placed inside the `<head>` section of an HTML document that give browsers, search engines and other services information about the page.\nThe main kinds of meta tag:\n\n- The encoding:\n\nThis tag sets the character encoding so that text displays correctly. UTF-8 supports most languages and characters.\n\n- Adaptation for mobile devices:\n\nThis tag helps the page adapt to the size of the screen, especially on mobile devices.\n\n- A description of the page:\n\nUsed to improve SEO (search engine optimization). Search engines often show this description in their results.\n\n- Keywords:\n\nRarely used nowadays, since search engines rely more on the page's content.\n\n- The author:\n\nStates the page's author.\n\n- Social networks:\nFor the correct display of a link on social media, for example:\n\nThese tags are used by Facebook, Twitter and other platforms to build an attractive card when a link is posted.\n\nAn example of use:\n\nSEO: helps search engines index the page better.\nMobile devices: makes the site comfortable to view on different screens.\nSocial networks: improves how a link looks when it is posted.\nMeta tags matter at every stage of development, from building landing pages to complex web applications.",
    codeExample: "<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0\">\n\n<meta name=\"description\" content=\"This is a description of the page for search engines.\">\n\n<meta name=\"keywords\" content=\"HTML, meta tags, frontend\">\n\n<meta name=\"author\" content=\"The author's name\">\n\n<meta property=\"og:title\" content=\"The page's title\">\n<meta property=\"og:description\" content=\"A description for social networks\">\n<meta property=\"og:image\" content=\"link_to_image\">\n\n<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html>\n  <head>\n    <meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n    <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0\">\n    <meta name=\"description\" content=\"A tutorial page about meta tags.\">\n    <meta name=\"author\" content=\"John Smith\">\n    <title>Meta tags in HTML</title>\n  </head>\n  <body>\n    <h1>An example of using meta tags</h1>\n  </body>\n</html>",
  },
  3: {
    source: "Для чего используется тег <link>?",
    question: "What is the <link> tag used for?",
    shortAnswer: "The `<link>` tag is used to attach external resources to an HTML document. Most often it is applied to link CSS styles. The tag sits inside the `<head>` section and is not displayed on the page.\nFor example:\n\n`<link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"styles.css\">\n`\nThis code attaches the style file `styles.css` to the page.",
    longAnswer: "The `<link>` tag is an HTML element that lets you attach external resources to a web page. It is used only in the `<head>` section and is not displayed on the page itself.\nThe main attributes of the `<link>` tag:\n• `rel` (relationship): states the kind of relationship between the current document and the resource being attached.\n• `stylesheet` — for attaching CSS files.\n• `icon` — for specifying a favicon.\n• `href` (hyperlink reference): the path to the file being attached. This may be a relative or an absolute URL.\n• `type` (optional): the resource's MIME type, `text/css` for example.\nExamples of use:\n\n- Attaching a CSS file:\n\nThis is the most widespread use of the `<link>` tag. It links an external style file to the HTML document.\n\n- Specifying a favicon:\n\nThis tag tells the browser which icon to show on the page's tab.\n\n- Attaching other resources:\nAttaching fonts, for example:\n\nThe full example:\n\nThe `<link>` tag lets you separate a web page's structure from its styles. This is convenient for managing styles (CSS) and improves performance, since browsers can cache the attached files. For example, when developing a site, attaching fonts and CSS through `<link>` helps you change the appearance quickly without touching the HTML.",
    codeExample: "<link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"styles.css\">\n\n<link rel=\"icon\" href=\"favicon.ico\" type=\"image/x-icon\">\n\n<link href=\"https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Roboto&display=swap\" rel=\"stylesheet\">\n\n<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html>\n  <head>\n    <meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n    <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0\">\n    <title>An example with the link tag</title>\n    <link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"styles.css\">\n    <link rel=\"icon\" href=\"favicon.ico\" type=\"image/x-icon\">\n  </head>\n  <body>\n    <h1>An example of attaching resources</h1>\n  </body>\n</html>",
  },
  4: {
    source: "Что такое атрибут в html?",
    question: "What is an attribute in HTML?",
    shortAnswer: "An attribute in HTML is additional information that sets an element's properties. It is written inside the opening tag in the form name=\"value\".\nFor example, the href attribute on an <a> tag sets the link, while src on an <img> gives the path to an image. Attributes affect the appearance or functionality of elements, but their values are not visible on the page itself.\nFor example:\n\n`<a href=\"https://example.com\">Go to the site</a>`",
    longAnswer: "Attributes in HTML give elements additional information, helping you configure their properties or behaviour. Attributes are written in an element's opening tag and consist of a name and a value separated by an `=` sign.\nThe main principles:\n\n- Attributes are stated only in the opening tag.\n\n- Attribute values are enclosed in quotes (double or single).\n\n- A single element may have several attributes, which are listed separated by spaces.\n\nExamples of popular attributes:\n\n- `href` — for links:\n\nStates the address the link leads to.\n\n- `src` — for images:\n\nStates the path to the image.\n\n- `alt` — alternative text for an image:\n\nShown if the image fails to load.\n\n- `id` and `class` — for identification and styling:\n\n`id` must be unique on the page, while `class` is used to group elements.\n\n- `disabled` — a boolean attribute that makes an element inactive:\n\nAn example with several attributes:\n\n- `href`: the link's address.\n\n- `target=\"_blank\"`: opening in a new tab.\n\n- `title`: a tooltip on hover.\n\nPractical use:\nAttributes are used to control the behaviour and display of HTML elements. For example, you can configure a button so that it is inactive (the `disabled` attribute), add styles through `class` or attach external resources through `src` and `href`.\nA practical example:\n\nAttributes make elements flexible and functional, which lets you build complex, interactive web pages.",
    codeExample: "<a href=\"https://example.com\">Go</a>\n\n<img src=\"image.jpg\" alt=\"A description of the image\">\n\n<img src=\"image.jpg\" alt=\"A description of the image\">\n\n<div id=\"main\" class=\"container\"></div>\n\n<button disabled>A button</button>\n\n<a href=\"https://example.com\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Will open in a new window\">A link</a>\n\n<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html>\n  <head>\n    <title>An example of attributes</title>\n  </head>\n  <body>\n    <h1 id=\"main-title\" class=\"title\">A heading</h1>\n    <a href=\"https://example.com\" target=\"_blank\">Go to the site</a>\n    <img src=\"image.jpg\" alt=\"A picture\">\n  </body>\n</html>",
  },
  5: {
    source: "Как можно создать телефонную ссылку?",
    question: "How can you create a telephone link?",
    shortAnswer: "To create a telephone link you use the `<a>` tag with an `href` attribute whose value begins with `tel:`.\nFor example:\n\n`<a href=\"tel:+1234567890\">Call</a>`\nWhen the user taps this link, the device offers to place a call to the given number automatically. This is especially useful for mobile users.",
    longAnswer: "A telephone link in HTML is a hyperlink that uses the `tel:` scheme to specify a phone number. Such a link lets a call be initiated on click, provided the device supports that function (a smartphone, for example, or a computer with telephony software installed).\nHow a telephone link works:\n\n- The link's format:\nThe `href` attribute contains the phone number in the form `tel:number`. The number can be written with the country code so that it works universally.\nFor example:\n\n- Support for additional characters:\nYou can add characters:\n\n- Spaces, dashes and brackets are ignored by the device.\n\n- For pauses or additional actions, commas `,` (a pause) or semicolons `;` (wait) are used.\n\n- The link's text:\nThe text between the `<a>` tags is visible to the user and can be anything you like.\n\nThe full example:\n\nPractical use:\n\n- Mobile sites: convenient for users who want to contact a company quickly.\n\n- Customer support: on contact pages telephone links can be used to improve the user experience.\n\nTips:\n\n- Give the phone number in international format so that the link works on any device.\n\n- Make sure the link's text is clear and motivates the user to act (\"Call\", for example).\n\n- Telephone links improve a site's usability, especially for mobile users, and help you interact with your audience directly.",
    codeExample: "<a href=\"tel:+1234567890\">Call</a>\n\n<a href=\"tel:+1234567890,123\">Call with an extension</a>\n\n<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html>\n  <head>\n    <title>A telephone link</title>\n  </head>\n  <body>\n    <p>To contact us, tap:</p>\n    <a href=\"tel:+1234567890\">Call us</a>\n  </body>\n</html>",
  },
  6: {
    source: "Расскажите как работает браузер?",
    question: "Tell us how a browser works",
    shortAnswer: "A browser is a program that downloads HTML, CSS, JavaScript and other resources from a web server, interprets them and displays the page for the user. It works in stages:\n\n- Loading: fetching the resources (HTML, CSS, JS, images) over HTTP/HTTPS.\n\n- Parsing: the HTML is turned into a DOM tree and the CSS into a CSSOM.\n\n- Rendering: the DOM and CSSOM are combined into a render tree, which is used to display the elements on the screen.\n\n- JavaScript: scripts are executed, and they may change the page (updating the DOM, for example).\n\n- Painting: the browser calculates where the elements go (layout) and draws them (paint).",
    longAnswer: "A browser's work can be divided into several key stages, each of which plays its own part in creating and displaying a web page.\n\n- Loading the resources\n\nWhen you type a URL in the address bar:\n\n- The browser sends a request to the server over the HTTP/HTTPS protocols.\n\n- The server returns an HTML document.\n\n- The browser analyses the HTML, finds links to other resources (CSS, JavaScript, images) and loads them.\n\n- Parsing the HTML and creating the DOM\n\nThe HTML document is broken down into elements and nodes, from which the Document Object Model (DOM) is built — the page's hierarchical structure.\nAn example DOM for some HTML:\n\nThe DOM structure:\n\n- Parsing the CSS and creating the CSSOM\n\nThe loaded styles are turned into the CSS Object Model (CSSOM), which represents the elements' styles.\nFor example:\n\nThe CSSOM contains the rules for every element.\n\n- Creating the render tree\n\nThe DOM and CSSOM are combined into a render tree, which contains only the page's visible elements with their styles. Invisible elements (those with `display: none`, for example) are excluded.\n\n- Calculating the layout\n\nThe browser works out where on the screen the elements should go — the height and width of blocks, the position of text and images, for example.\n\n- Painting\n\nThe browser draws the elements on the screen taking their styles, colours and shadows into account.\n\n- Processing the JavaScript\n\nJavaScript is executed in an engine (V8 for Chrome, for example). Scripts can change the DOM or the CSSOM, which sometimes triggers a layout recalculation or a repaint.\nFor example:\n\n- Optimization through compositing\n\nModern browsers use compositing to separate layers (animations, for example) and process them independently, which speeds rendering up.\nThe browser's flow of work (simplified):\n\n- Receive the HTML → parse → DOM\n\n- Receive the CSS → parse → CSSOM\n\n- DOM + CSSOM → render tree → layout → paint\n\n- Display the page to the user\n\nAn example of the resources interacting:\nHTML:\n\nCSS (`styles.css`):\n\nJavaScript (`script.js`):\n\nOptimizing the browser's work:\n\n- Minimize the number of requests: combine CSS and JS files.\n\n- Use asynchronous loading: for JavaScript, add the `async` or `defer` attribute.\n\n- Minify your files: reduce the size of the resources.\n\n- Avoid blocking JavaScript and CSS: this speeds up the display of the page.\n\nA browser is a complex system that does an enormous amount of work in milliseconds in order to display a web page. Understanding how it works helps you build fast, optimized web applications.",
    codeExample: "<html>\n  <body>\n    <h1>Hello, world!</h1>\n  </body>\n</html>\n\n<html>\n └── <body>\n      └── <h1>\n           └── \"Hello, world!\"\n\nbody {\n  font-size: 16px;\n}\nh1 {\n  color: red;\n}\n\ndocument.querySelector(\"h1\").style.color = \"blue\";\n\n<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html>\n  <head>\n    <link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"styles.css\">\n  </head>\n  <body>\n    <h1>Hello, world!</h1>\n    <script src=\"script.js\"></script>\n  </body>\n</html>\n\nh1 {\n  color: red;\n}\n\ndocument.querySelector(\"h1\").textContent = \"Hello, JavaScript!\";",
  },
  7: {
    source: "Какие есть этапы рендеринга html страницы?",
    question: "What are the stages of rendering an HTML page?",
    shortAnswer: "- Parsing the HTML\n\n- The browser starts parsing the HTML document and building the DOM tree (Document Object Model), which represents the hierarchical structure of all the page's elements.\n\n- If the browser finds a `<script>`, it pauses parsing, loads and executes the script, which may change the DOM's structure.\n\n- Loading external resources\n\n- While parsing the HTML the browser discovers links to external resources (CSS, images, fonts, scripts) and starts loading them in parallel.\n\n- The CSS is loaded and parsed, forming the CSSOM (CSS Object Model).\n\n- Building the render tree\n\n- The DOM and CSSOM are combined to create the render tree, which is the structure of the elements to be displayed on the screen with their styles applied.\n\n- Calculating the geometry (Layout)\n\n- The browser calculates the size and position of every element on the page based on the render tree. This process is called layout or reflow.\n\n- Painting\n\n- The browser turns the render tree into pixels on the screen, drawing text, colours, images, shadows and other graphical elements.\n\n- Compositing\n\n- Some elements may be painted in separate layers to improve performance (animations or fixed elements, for example). At the compositing stage the browser combines those layers into a single image for display on the screen.\n\n- The user interface (UI)\n\n- The browser handles user events (clicks, scrolling, data entry and so on).\n\n- JavaScript can change the DOM, which may trigger the layout and painting stages again.",
    longAnswer: "- Parsing the HTML\n\n- The browser starts parsing the HTML document and building the DOM tree (Document Object Model), which represents the hierarchical structure of all the page's elements.\n\n- If the browser finds a `<script>`, it pauses parsing, loads and executes the script, which may change the DOM's structure.\n\n- Loading external resources\n\n- While parsing the HTML the browser discovers links to external resources (CSS, images, fonts, scripts) and starts loading them in parallel.\n\n- The CSS is loaded and parsed, forming the CSSOM (CSS Object Model).\n\n- Building the render tree\n\n- The DOM and CSSOM are combined to create the render tree, which is the structure of the elements to be displayed on the screen with their styles applied.\n\n- Calculating the geometry (Layout)\n\n- The browser calculates the size and position of every element on the page based on the render tree. This process is called layout or reflow.\n\n- Painting\n\n- The browser turns the render tree into pixels on the screen, drawing text, colours, images, shadows and other graphical elements.\n\n- Compositing\n\n- Some elements may be painted in separate layers to improve performance (animations or fixed elements, for example). At the compositing stage the browser combines those layers into a single image for display on the screen.\n\n- The user interface (UI)\n\n- The browser handles user events (clicks, scrolling, data entry and so on).\n\n- JavaScript can change the DOM, which may trigger the layout and painting stages again.",
    codeExample: null,
  },
  8: {
    source: "Какие есть виды подключения CSS в HTML?",
    question: "What ways are there of attaching CSS in HTML?",
    shortAnswer: "There are three main ways of attaching CSS to HTML: inline, internal and external. Inline CSS applies styles directly to an element using the `style` attribute. Internal CSS is placed inside a `<style>` tag in the `<head>` of the HTML document and applies to every element on the page. External CSS lives in a separate file with the `.css` extension, which is attached to the HTML through a `<link>` tag in the `<head>`, which lets the same styles be used on several pages.",
    longAnswer: "HTML has three main ways of attaching CSS, each with its own advantages and used in different situations.\nInline CSS: this method involves using the `style` attribute right inside an HTML element. For example:\n`<h1 style=\"color: blue;\">Hello, world!</h1>`\nInline CSS is convenient for applying styles to individual elements quickly, but it can lead to inefficient code if used too often.\nInternal CSS: the styles are defined inside a `<style>` tag placed in the document's `<head>`. For example:\n\nInternal CSS is convenient when you want the styles to apply to one page only. It is not suitable for large projects, however, since it requires duplicating the code on different pages.\nExternal CSS: this method means creating a separate CSS file, which is attached to the HTML document with a `<link>` tag. For example:\n\nExternal CSS is the most preferable method for large projects, since it lets you separate the HTML structure from the styles. This makes maintaining and changing styles easier, because it is enough to make a change in one place and it applies to every page that uses that file.\nEach of these methods can be useful depending on the context. In most cases external CSS is recommended for organizing code and improving its readability.",
    codeExample: "<head>\n  <style>\n    body {\n      background-color: lightgray;\n    }\n    h1 {\n      color: green;\n    }\n  </style>\n</head>\n\n<head><link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"styles.css\"></head>",
  },
  9: {
    source: "Что такое DOCTYPE и зачем он нужен?",
    question: "What is DOCTYPE and what is it for?",
    shortAnswer: "`<!DOCTYPE>` is an instruction to the browser telling it which mode it should process the HTML code in. Without it the browser may work in compatibility mode (quirks mode), which can make the page display incorrectly. Modern pages normally use `<!DOCTYPE html>` to switch on standards mode, which supports modern web technologies.\nFor example:\n\n`<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html>\n<head>\n<title>An example</title>\n</head>\n<body>\n<h1>Hello, world!</h1>\n</body>\n</html>`",
    longAnswer: "`<!DOCTYPE>` is an instruction to the browser telling it which version of the HTML specification to use when processing the page. It is not an HTML tag and is not displayed in the page's interface.\nWeb pages can be displayed differently depending on whether standards mode or compatibility mode is switched on:\n\n- Standards mode: the browser renders the page in accordance with modern HTML and CSS standards.\n\n- Compatibility mode (quirks mode): the browser imitates obsolete behaviour for old web pages.\n\nWhy does this matter?\nIf the `<!DOCTYPE>` declaration is absent, browsers may interpret the page's code incorrectly, especially when CSS is used. The size of blocks and the handling of tables, for example, may differ.\nFor example:\nA minimal HTML page:\n\nThe history of `<!DOCTYPE>`\nHTML 4.01:\nThe declaration was more complicated, since it included references to a DTD (Document Type Definition). For example:\n\nThis told the browser to use strict mode without obsolete elements.\nHTML5:\nThe simplified form `<!DOCTYPE html>`, since HTML5 no longer uses a DTD.\n\nHow does `<!DOCTYPE>` work?\nWhen the browser sees the `<!DOCTYPE>` declaration, it:\n\n- Switches to standards mode.\n\n- Interprets the HTML and CSS in accordance with modern specifications.\n\nIf `<!DOCTYPE>` is absent, the browser switches on compatibility mode, where obsolete behaviour is supported. This can lead to visual and functional errors.\n\nA practical example:\nWithout `<!DOCTYPE>`:\n\n- The browser may interpret the width of a block with `width: 100px;` as including its margin and border.\nWith `<!DOCTYPE>`:\n\n- The browser interprets only the block's content in accordance with the CSS box model.\n\n`<!DOCTYPE>` is needed so that the browser renders the page correctly in modern standards mode. It is a mandatory part of any HTML document and lays the foundation for your site to display and work properly.",
    codeExample: "<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n  <meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n  <title>A document</title>\n</head>\n<body>\n  <h1>An example page</h1>\n</body>\n</html>\n\n<!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC \"-//W3C//DTD HTML 4.01//EN\" \"http://www.w3.org/TR/html4/strict.dtd\">",
  },
  10: {
    source: "Какие есть виды списков в HTML?",
    question: "What kinds of list are there in HTML?",
    shortAnswer: "HTML has three main kinds of list: ordered lists (`<ol>`), unordered lists (`<ul>`) and definition lists (`<dl>`). Ordered lists use numbers or letters to mark each item, unordered ones use bullets, and definition lists contain \"term-description\" pairs.",
    longAnswer: "HTML provides three kinds of list for structuring data:\nAn ordered list (`<ol>`) — used when the order of the items matters. The list's items are enclosed in `<li>` tags, and the list as a whole in `<ol>`. By default the items are numbered with digits, but the style can be changed to Roman numerals or letters through the `type` attribute.\n\nAn unordered list (`<ul>`) — used when the order of the items does not matter. In this list the items are also enclosed in `<li>`, but bullets (dots, squares) are used instead of numbers.\n\nA definition list (`<dl>`) — used to create \"term-description\" pairs. The terms are enclosed in a `<dt>` tag and the descriptions in `<dd>`.\n\nThese lists are used to organize information on pages, improving their readability and accessibility.",
    codeExample: "<ol>\n  <li>The first item</li>\n  <li>The second item</li>\n  <li>The third item</li> \n</ol>\n\n<ul>  \n  <li>Item 1</li>  \n  <li>Item 2</li>  \n  <li>Item 3</li> \n</ul>\n\n<dl>  \n  <dt>HTML</dt>  \n  <dd>HyperText Markup Language</dd>  \n  <dt>CSS</dt>  \n  <dd>Cascading Style Sheets</dd> \n</dl>",
  },
  11: {
    source: "Как вставить видео в HTML?",
    question: "How do you embed video in HTML?",
    shortAnswer: "To embed video in HTML the `<video>` tag is used. Inside it the video's sources are given through `<source>` tags, and you can also add attributes such as `controls` to display the playback controls, `autoplay` for automatic playback and `loop` for repeated playback.",
    longAnswer: "HTML5 added support for video without any need for third-party plugins such as Flash. The `<video>` tag lets you embed video files in a page.\nIt supports several attributes for controlling the video's behaviour:\n\n- `controls` — shows the control panel (play, pause, seek).\n\n- `autoplay` — starts playing the video automatically when the page loads.\n\n- `loop` — makes the video play in a loop.\n\n- `muted` — turns the video's sound off by default.\n\nFor example:\n\nInside the `<video>` tag you can give several sources (`<source>`) in order to support different video formats in different browsers. If the browser does not support HTML5 video, the fallback text is displayed.",
    codeExample: "<video width=\"400\" controls>  \n  <source src=\"video.mp4\" type=\"video/mp4\">  \n  <source src=\"video.ogg\" type=\"video/ogg\">  Your browser does not support video. \n</video>",
  },
  12: {
    source: "Как вставить аудио в HTML?",
    question: "How do you embed audio in HTML?",
    shortAnswer: "To embed audio in HTML the `<audio>` tag is used. As with video, you can give several audio file formats through `<source>` tags, and the `controls`, `autoplay` and `loop` attributes control playback.",
    longAnswer: "The `<audio>` tag in HTML5 lets you embed audio files in a web page. As with video, you can use attributes to control the audio file's behaviour:\n\n- `controls` — adds a control panel for the user.\n\n- `autoplay` — starts playing the audio automatically.\n\n- `loop` — repeats the audio file in a loop.\n\n- `muted` — mutes the audio by default.\n\nFor example:\n\nThis code embeds an audio file with playback controls. As with video, several `<source>` elements let you support various audio formats for different browsers.",
    codeExample: "<audio controls>  \n  <source src=\"audio.mp3\" type=\"audio/mpeg\">  \n  <source src=\"audio.ogg\" type=\"audio/ogg\">  \n  Your browser does not support audio. \n</audio>",
  },
  13: {
    source: "Для чего используется тег <iframe>?",
    question: "What is the <iframe> tag used for?",
    shortAnswer: "The `<iframe>` tag is used to embed content from another web resource in a page — another site, a YouTube video or a map, for example. It creates a window inside the current page where the external resource is loaded. `<iframe>` is useful for integrating third-party content while preserving the page's main structure.",
    longAnswer: "The `<iframe>` tag (from \"inline frame\") lets you insert another document into a web page — the content of another web page, a video or a map, for example. This creates a window inside the current page where the external resource is loaded. It is used to embed videos from YouTube, maps from Google Maps, documents and other sites.\nFor example:\n\nThis code embeds the site `example.com` in your page. It is important to bear in mind that not all sites allow embedding through an `<iframe>` for security reasons (some sites use the `X-Frame-Options` header as protection, for example).\n\nThe `width` and `height` attributes set the window's dimensions.\n\nThe main advantage of using an `<iframe>` is the ability to integrate external content without changing the page's main code. You should be careful with it, however, because of the potential security vulnerabilities involved in loading external content.",
    codeExample: "<iframe src=\"https://www.example.com\" width=\"600\" height=\"400\"></iframe>",
  },
  14: {
    source: "Какой тег используется для создания ссылок в HTML?",
    question: "Which tag is used to create links in HTML?",
    shortAnswer: "To create links in HTML the `<a>` tag is used. It has an `href` attribute that sets the URL the link will lead to. Inside the `<a>` tag goes the text or other element the user can click to follow the link.",
    longAnswer: "The `<a>` tag (anchor) is used to create hyperlinks in HTML documents. This tag connects text or an element on the page to another document, file or web page. Its main attribute is `href`, which gives the path to the resource the link leads to.\nFor example:\n\nHere, when the text \"Go to the site\" is clicked, the browser navigates to the given URL. With the `target=\"_blank\"` attribute you can open the link in a new tab, and `rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"` is added to improve security when `target=\"_blank\"` is used.\nThe `<a>` tag can also be used to create anchor links within a single page. This is done by setting an identifier (`id`) on the element you want to link to and using `href` with that identifier.\nAn example of an anchor link:",
    codeExample: "<a href=\"https://www.example.com\">Go to the site</a>\n\n<a href=\"#section1\">Go to section 1</a> \n<div id=\"section1\">This is section 1</div>",
  },
  15: {
    source: "Что за тег <picture>? Чем он отличается от тега <img>?",
    question: "What is the <picture> tag? How does it differ from the <img> tag?",
    shortAnswer: "The `<picture>` tag lets you specify several image sources for different conditions, such as screen width or image format, so that the browser can pick the most suitable one. The `<img>` tag is used to insert a single image simply. The difference is that `<picture>` gives you more flexibility for responsive images.",
    longAnswer: "The `<picture>` tag in HTML is used to provide several versions of the same image, so that the browser can choose the most suitable one depending on conditions such as screen size or support for image formats. This is especially useful for responsive sites, when images of different size or quality are needed on different devices. Inside a `<picture>` tag there may be several `<source>` elements with `media` and `type` attributes that set the conditions for choosing an image. If none of the conditions is met, the image given in the `<img>` tag is displayed — and that tag is mandatory inside `<picture>`.\nFor example:\n\nHere, if the screen is wider than 800 pixels, the image `large.jpg` is loaded. If the screen is wider than 400 pixels but narrower than 800, `medium.jpg` is loaded. In all other cases `small.jpg` is used. The `<img>` tag, unlike `<picture>`, simply displays one image and cannot adapt to various conditions.\nUsing `<picture>` lets you improve a site's performance by loading smaller images on mobile devices and avoiding unnecessary network load.",
    codeExample: "<picture>  \n  <source media=\"(min-width: 800px)\" srcset=\"large.jpg\">  \n  <source media=\"(min-width: 400px)\" srcset=\"medium.jpg\">  \n  <img src=\"small.jpg\" alt=\"An example image\"> \n</picture>",
  },
  16: {
    source: "Что такое Canvas?",
    question: "What is Canvas?",
    shortAnswer: "`<canvas>` is an HTML tag used to draw 2D and 3D graphics through JavaScript. By itself `<canvas>` contains no graphics; a JavaScript API is used to create them. The element is useful for animations, games and the dynamic rendering of images, but it requires programming for every detail.",
    longAnswer: "`<canvas>` is an HTML5 tag that provides a space for drawing graphics with JavaScript. By itself `<canvas>` contains no content beyond the \"canvas\" you can draw on. JavaScript and its API are used for that, to render 2D or 3D graphics. With this API you can draw shapes, images and text, and even create animations.\nAn example of use:\n\nThis code creates a `<canvas>` element 500x500 pixels in size and draws a blue square on it with JavaScript. One of the advantages of `<canvas>` is its flexibility, but it requires much more effort than other graphical solutions, since every element is drawn programmatically and cannot be interactive without additional code.\n`<canvas>` is often used to create complex graphical interfaces such as games, data visualizations or interactive animations, where high performance and control over every pixel are required.",
    codeExample: "<canvas id=\"myCanvas\" width=\"500\" height=\"500\"></canvas> \n\n<script>  \n  const canvas = document.getElementById('myCanvas');  \n  const ctx = canvas.getContext('2d');  \n  ctx.fillStyle = 'blue';  \n  ctx.fillRect(50, 50, 100, 100); // We draw a blue square \n</script>",
  },
  17: {
    source: "Что такое SVG?",
    question: "What is SVG?",
    shortAnswer: "SVG (Scalable Vector Graphics) is a vector graphics format that lets you display images that scale without any loss of quality. SVG is used to create logos, icons, diagrams and other graphical elements that stay sharp at any resolution. SVG elements can be animated and controlled through CSS and JavaScript.",
    longAnswer: "SVG (Scalable Vector Graphics) is an XML format for creating two-dimensional vector graphics that is supported by every modern browser. Unlike raster graphics (PNG or JPEG, for example), SVG stores information about objects as geometric shapes (lines, circles, polygons), which lets images be scaled without any loss of quality. This makes SVG ideal for logos, icons and other graphical elements that have to look sharp on devices with different resolutions.\nAn example of use:\n\nThis code creates a red circle with a black outline. SVG supports not only simple shapes but also text, complex paths and transformations. SVG elements can also be styled and animated with CSS and controlled through JavaScript.\nSVG is used actively to create interactive elements on web pages, such as charts, diagrams or interactive maps. Unlike `<canvas>`, SVG is part of the DOM, so every element can be manipulated just like any other HTML element.",
    codeExample: "<svg width=\"100\" height=\"100\">  \n  <circle cx=\"50\" cy=\"50\" r=\"40\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"2\" fill=\"red\" /> \n</svg>",
  },
  18: {
    source: "С помощью каких тегов можно сверстать таблицу?",
    question: "Which tags can you use to lay out a table?",
    shortAnswer: "To create a table in HTML the tags `<table>`, `<tr>`, `<td>`, `<th>`, `<thead>`, `<tbody>` and `<tfoot>` are used. The `<table>` tag creates the table itself, `<tr>` a row, `<td>` a cell and `<th>` a header cell. Additional tags such as `<thead>`, `<tbody>` and `<tfoot>` let you divide the table into sections.",
    longAnswer: "Tables in HTML are created with several key tags:\n\n- `<table>` — the main tag, which wraps the whole table.\n\n- `<tr>` (table row) — defines a row of the table.\n\n- `<td>` (table data) — defines a cell containing data.\n\n- `<th>` (table header) — used for header cells in rows or columns; it is normally displayed in bold.\n\nAn example of a simple table:\n\nThis code creates a table with two columns: \"Name\" and \"Age\". In every row of the table the data goes in `<td>` cells and the headings in `<th>`.\nTo improve the table's structure you can also use additional tags:\n\n- `<thead>` — groups the rows containing the column headings.\n\n- `<tbody>` — groups the rows with the table's main content.\n\n- `<tfoot>` — groups the rows at the bottom of the table, normally for totals.\n\nAn example using all the tags:\n\nTables in HTML are used to present tabular data and should not be applied to align elements on a page. Using them properly helps structure data and makes it easier to take in.",
    codeExample: "<table>  \n  <tr>    \n    <th>Name</th>    \n    <th>Age</th>  \n  </tr>  \n  <tr>    \n    <td>Alice</td>    \n    <td>25</td>  \n  </tr>  \n  <tr>    \n    <td>Bob</td>    \n    <td>30</td>  \n  </tr> \n</table>\n\n<table>  \n  <thead>    \n    <tr>      \n      <th>Name</th>      \n      <th>Age</th>    \n    </tr>  \n  </thead>  \n  <tbody>    \n    <tr>      \n      <td>Alice</td>      \n      <td>25</td>    \n    </tr>    \n    <tr>      \n      <td>Bob</td>      \n      <td>30</td>    \n    </tr>  \n  </tbody>  \n  <tfoot>    \n    <tr>      \n      <td>Total</td>      \n      <td>55</td>    \n    </tr> \n  </tfoot>\n</table>",
  },
  19: {
    source: "Для чего нужен тег <head>?",
    question: "What is the <head> tag for?",
    shortAnswer: "The `<head>` tag is used to hold information about a web page that is not displayed on the screen directly. It includes meta tags, links to styles and scripts, the page's title (through the `<title>` tag) and other elements necessary for the site to work properly. Everything inside `<head>` is not displayed on the web page directly, but is used by the browser to process the page.",
    longAnswer: "The `<head>` tag is one of the mandatory elements of an HTML document, and it contains information that is invisible to users but important for browsers and search engines. Inside `<head>` you can place meta tags such as `<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">` for stating the encoding and `<meta name=\"description\" content=\"A description of the page\">` for SEO, links to style files (`<link>`) and scripts (`<script>`), and set the page's title through the `<title>` tag.\nAn example of use:\n\nThis code includes metadata about the page and a style file, attaches external JavaScript and sets the page title \"My page\", which is shown on the browser tab. Without a properly configured `<head>` a page may display or work incorrectly, and it will also perform poorly in search engines.",
    codeExample: "<head>  \n  <meta charset=\"UTF-8\">  \n  <meta name=\"description\" content=\"A description of the page\">  \n  <title>My page</title>  \n  <link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"styles.css\">  \n  <script src=\"script.js\"></script> \n</head>",
  },
  20: {
    source: "Какие основные различия между блочными и строчными элементами?",
    question: "What are the main differences between block and inline elements?",
    shortAnswer: "Block elements take up the whole width of the available space, starting on a new line (`<div>`, `<h1>`, `<p>`, for example), while inline elements take up only as much space as they need and sit on the same line as other elements (`<span>`, `<a>`, `<strong>`, for example). Block elements can contain both other block elements and inline ones, whereas inline elements normally contain only text or other inline elements.",
    longAnswer: "Block and inline elements have different display properties, which determines how they behave on a page. Block elements always start on a new line and take up the whole width of their parent container, even when their content takes up less space. `<div>`, `<h1>` and `<p>`, for example, are block elements. They are often used to create a page's main blocks.\nInline elements, by contrast, sit on the same line as other elements and take up only the space their content requires. Examples of inline elements are `<span>`, `<a>` and `<strong>`. They are often applied to style individual words or parts of text without disturbing the overall flow of the content.\nFor example:\n\nHere `<p>` is a block element that creates a new paragraph, while `<strong>` is an inline element that emphasizes the word \"important\" without interrupting the flow of the text.\nThe difference between these elements matters for building a page's layout properly and for using CSS to style them. Block elements, for example, are easy to control through width and height properties, whereas inline ones require special styles such as `display: block;` in order to change their behaviour.",
    codeExample: "<p>This is <strong>important</strong> text inside a paragraph.</p>",
  },
  21: {
    source: "Для чего нужен тег <noscript>?",
    question: "What is the <noscript> tag for?",
    shortAnswer: "The `<noscript>` tag displays content when the user has JavaScript disabled or their browser does not support scripts. This is useful for making a site accessible to all users, including those who cannot use JavaScript. Inside `<noscript>` you can place alternative versions of content or instructions for users.",
    longAnswer: "The `<noscript>` tag is an HTML tag that lets developers display a message or alternative content for users who have JavaScript disabled. In modern web applications JavaScript is often used to change a page's content dynamically, to work with APIs and to perform other interactive tasks. Not every user has JavaScript enabled, however, or uses a browser that supports scripts. In such cases `<noscript>` helps you provide important messages or basic functionality.\nAn example of use:\n\nThis code shows the user a message if their browser does not support JavaScript or it is disabled. The `<noscript>` tag is often used to display warnings, instructions or even simplified versions of content, so that the user can interact with the site even without script support.\nUsing the `<noscript>` tag improves a web page's accessibility and shows consideration for users whose JavaScript capabilities are disabled for whatever reason.",
    codeExample: "<noscript>  \n  <p>Your browser does not support JavaScript or it is disabled. Please enable JavaScript for the site to work fully.</p> \n</noscript>",
  },
  22: {
    source: "Для чего нужны data-атрибуты?",
    question: "What are data attributes for?",
    shortAnswer: "Data attributes are custom attributes beginning with `data-` that let you store additional data on HTML elements. That data can be used by JavaScript to process the elements on a page dynamically. Data attributes are convenient for passing data without any need to change the HTML structure or use hidden fields.",
    longAnswer: "Data attributes are a mechanism for storing custom data on HTML elements using attributes that begin with `data-` — `data-id`, `data-name` and so on. These attributes do not affect how the content is displayed, but they let you store information that can easily be read out with JavaScript and used to work with the page's elements.\nAn example of use:\n\nIn this example the button carries two data attributes: `data-product-id` and `data-category`. JavaScript can reach these values like this:\n\nData attributes are convenient for passing information from HTML into JavaScript without any need for additional elements or more complicated markup. They are used actively in dynamic interfaces, for managing events and for storing unique data associated with an element.",
    codeExample: "<button data-product-id=\"12345\" data-category=\"books\">Buy the book</button>\n\nconst button = document.querySelector('button'); \nconst productId = button.getAttribute('data-product-id'); \nconsole.log(productId); // 12345",
  },
  23: {
    source: "Как изменить стандартное поведение тега <a>, так чтобы ссылка открывалась в новой вкладке?",
    question: "How do you change the default behaviour of the <a> tag so that a link opens in a new tab?",
    shortAnswer: "For a link to open in a new tab you need to add the attribute `target=\"_blank\"` to the `<a>` tag. This is the standard way of telling the browser that the link should be opened in a new window or tab. For better security it is also recommended to add the attribute `rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"`.",
    longAnswer: "By default links created with the `<a>` tag open in the same tab. You can change this behaviour, however, by adding the attribute `target=\"_blank\"`. This attribute tells the browser to open the link in a new tab or window.\nFor example:\n\nWhen the user clicks this link, it opens in a new tab. Using `target=\"_blank\"` creates a potential security vulnerability, however, since the new tab can reach the `window.opener` object, which could in theory allow the original page to be manipulated. To prevent this, the attribute `rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"` is added:\n\nThis combination of attributes is the recommended practice for protecting sites and users.",
    codeExample: "<a href=\"https://example.com\" target=\"_blank\">Go to the site</a>\n\n<a href=\"https://example.com\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Go to the site</a>",
  },
  24: {
    source: "Что нужно сделать, чтобы отправить почту в HTML?",
    question: "What do you need to do to send mail in HTML?",
    shortAnswer: "To send an email through HTML you can use a link whose `href` attribute contains the `mailto:` protocol. This opens the user's mail application with a ready-made draft message. In the link you can give the address, the subject and the body of the message.",
    longAnswer: "HTML lets you create links for sending email using the `mailto:` protocol. When a user clicks such a link, their mail client (Outlook, Gmail or another) opens with the \"To\", \"Subject\" and even \"Body\" fields filled in beforehand (if you wish).\nAn example of a basic link:\n\nWhen the user clicks this link, their mail client opens with the \"To\" field filled in with `example@example.com` automatically.\nYou can also add a subject and a body:\n\nThis code will create a message with the subject \"Hello\" and the text \"The body of the message\". It is important to understand, however, that sending mail this way requires a mail client to be configured on the device. The method is not suitable for sending mail from the server, which normally requires server-side code or external services.",
    codeExample: "<a href=\"mailto:example@example.com\">Send a message</a>\n\n<a href=\"mailto:example@example.com?subject=Hello&body=The body of the message\">Send a message</a>",
  },
  25: {
    source: "Что такое атрибут alt у тега <img> и почему он важен?",
    question: "What is the alt attribute on the <img> tag and why does it matter?",
    shortAnswer: "The `alt` attribute on the `<img>` tag is used to provide a textual description of an image. This matters for users who cannot see the image — people with visual impairments, for example, or when the image fails to load. Search engines also use this text to index images, which improves SEO.",
    longAnswer: "The `alt` attribute (alternative text) on the `<img>` tag is meant to describe an image's content. This is especially useful for people with visual impairments who use screen readers to understand what a picture shows. If the image cannot be loaded, the text from the `alt` attribute is displayed in its place, which helps users understand the context.\nAn example of use:\n\nIn this example, if the image `logo.png` fails to load, users will see the text \"The logo of the XYZ company\". The `alt` attribute is also useful for SEO, since search engines analyse this text to determine what images contain, which can help the page be indexed.\nUsing the `alt` attribute properly is an important aspect of web accessibility that provides a better experience for every user and increases a site's visibility in search engines.",
    codeExample: "<img src=\"logo.png\" alt=\"The logo of the XYZ company\">",
  },
  26: {
    source: "Зачем для тега <script> добавляют атрибуты async и defer?",
    question: "Why are the async and defer attributes added to the <script> tag?",
    shortAnswer: "The async and defer attributes on the <script> tag are used to control how and when the browser loads and executes JavaScript. async loads the script asynchronously and executes it as soon as it has loaded, without waiting for the whole page to finish loading. defer also loads the script asynchronously but postpones its execution until the HTML document has loaded fully, which prevents the page's rendering from being blocked.",
    longAnswer: "When adding scripts to a page it is important to consider how they may affect performance and the user experience. By default, when the browser meets a `<script>` tag it pauses the page's rendering in order to load and execute the script, which can slow down the display of the content.\nThe `async` attribute lets the browser load the script asynchronously, which means that loading the script does not block the page's rendering. As soon as the script has loaded it is executed, even if the HTML document has not finished loading.\nFor example:\n\nThe `defer` attribute also loads the script asynchronously, but it postpones execution until the entire HTML document has loaded. This guarantees that the script runs only after the page has loaded fully.\nFor example:\n\nUsing these attributes lets you optimize loading time and improve the user experience, especially on pages with a lot of scripts or with heavy JavaScript frameworks.",
    codeExample: "<script src=\"script.js\" async></script>\n\n<script src=\"script.js\" defer></script>",
  },
  27: {
    source: "Какие есть виды подключения JavaScript к HTML?",
    question: "What ways are there of attaching JavaScript to HTML?",
    shortAnswer: "JavaScript can be attached to HTML in three main ways: inline, through an external file, and through tag attributes. Inline JavaScript is placed inside a `<script>` tag in the HTML document. An external JavaScript file is attached with the tag `<script src=\"file.js\">`. You can also use attributes such as `onclick` to handle events directly in the HTML.",
    longAnswer: "There are several ways of attaching JavaScript to HTML, and each has its own characteristics and advantages:\nInline JavaScript: this way means writing the JavaScript code directly inside the HTML document within a `<script>` tag. It is convenient for small scripts, since all the code is in one place.\n\nAn external JavaScript file: for more complex applications it is recommended to keep the JavaScript code in a separate file. This makes managing and reusing the code easier. To attach an external file, the `src` attribute on the `<script>` tag is used.\n\nEvent handlers in HTML: JavaScript can also be attached using attributes such as `onclick`, `onmouseover` and others. This lets JavaScript code be executed in response to events associated with HTML elements.\n\nEach of these ways has its advantages. Inline code is simple to use but is not suitable for large projects.\nExternal files help you keep things tidy, and event handlers let you add interactivity to a page's elements quickly. The right choice of approach depends on the size of the project and the structure of your code.",
    codeExample: "<script>\n  console.log(\"Hello, World!\");\n</script>\n\n<script src=\"script.js\"></script>\n\n<button onclick=\"alert('Hello!')\">Click me</button>",
  },
  28: {
    source: "Что такое доступность (accessibility)?",
    question: "What is accessibility?",
    shortAnswer: "Accessibility is the practice of building websites that every user can use, including people with disabilities. It involves improvements such as using proper headings, text for screen readers, colour contrast and keyboard navigation. The goal is to give every user access to the information and functionality, regardless of their abilities.",
    longAnswer: "Web accessibility (or a11y) is a set of methods and practices that let users with disabilities interact with a website effectively. Those disabilities may include problems with vision or hearing, or motor or cognitive impairments. For a site to be accessible, developers have to consider several aspects:\n\n- Text alternatives for images: the `alt` attribute on images, for example, matters for screen-reader users.\n\n- Keyboard navigation: every element on the site must be reachable without a mouse.\n\n- A proper heading structure: this helps screen readers and users understand and navigate the page better.\n\n- Colour contrast: text and interface elements must be easy to tell apart for people with colour blindness or other visual impairments.\n\nAccessibility is also governed by a number of standards such as the WCAG (Web Content Accessibility Guidelines). Observing those standards makes a site more inclusive and improves every user's interaction with the content.",
    codeExample: null,
  },
  29: {
    source: "SEO - для чего нужно, как достигается?",
    question: "SEO — what is it for and how is it achieved?",
    shortAnswer: "SEO (Search Engine Optimization) is a set of methods that help improve a site's position in search results. It is achieved through a proper site structure, the use of relevant keywords, optimizing images, increasing loading speed and ensuring accessibility. SEO helps attract more visitors by increasing visibility in search engines such as Google.",
    longAnswer: "SEO (search engine optimization) is the process of optimizing a website to improve its visibility in the organic (unpaid) results of search engines. Search engines such as Google index pages and determine their relevance with complex algorithms, taking into account factors such as the quality of the content, the site's structure and the user experience.\n\nThe main SEO methods include:\n\n- Keywords: it is important to use the queries users actually make in headings, meta descriptions and the page's text.\n\n- Quality content: regularly adding useful, original content helps improve your ranking in search engines.\n\n- Internal and external optimization: internal links, a proper heading structure (`<h1>`, `<h2>`), meta tags and descriptions all help improve a site's visibility.\n\n- Optimizing images: using compressed images and the `alt` attribute helps both performance and SEO.\n\n- Improving page loading speed: fast page loads matter both for SEO and for the user experience.\n\nSEO helps attract more organic traffic and improves a site's visibility and its ranking in search engines.",
    codeExample: null,
  },
  30: {
    source: "Какие теги были введены в HTML5?",
    question: "Which tags were introduced in HTML5?",
    shortAnswer: "HTML5 added many new semantic tags such as `<header>`, `<footer>`, `<article>` and `<section>`, which help improve the structure and readability of code. Tags for multimedia elements were also added, such as `<audio>` and `<video>`, and support for forms was improved with new attributes. These changes make HTML5 more powerful and more convenient for building modern websites.",
    longAnswer: "HTML5 brought many improvements to the HTML language, including new semantic tags, multimedia elements and form attributes, which make web development more intuitive and efficient. Here are some of the most significant changes:\nSemantic tags: HTML5 added tags that help describe a web page's structure better. For example:\n\n- `<header>`: used for the top part of a page or section.\n\n- `<footer>`: denotes the bottom part of a page or section.\n\n- `<article>`: for self-contained pieces of content such as articles or blog posts.\n\n- `<section>`: for logically related sections of a page.\n\n- `<nav>`: for navigation links. These tags improve accessibility, since they help both search engines and screen readers understand the page's structure better.\n\nMultimedia elements: HTML5 introduced the `<audio>` and `<video>` tags, which let you insert multimedia content into a page directly without third-party plugins.\n\nNew attributes for forms: HTML5 also improved forms by adding attributes such as `placeholder`, input types (`email`, `date`, `number`, for example) and the `<datalist>` element, which lets you create dropdown lists with suggested values.\nThese innovations make HTML5 a more powerful tool for building modern websites, improving semantics and accessibility and making the integration of multimedia content easier.",
    codeExample: "<audio controls>    \n  <source src=\"audio.mp3\" type=\"audio/mpeg\"> \n</audio> \n<video controls>    \n  <source src=\"video.mp4\" type=\"video/mp4\"> \n</video>",
  },
  31: {
    source: "Как работает перерендеринг элементов, когда используются атрибуты data- и как они могут влиять на производительность?",
    question: "How does the re-rendering of elements work when data- attributes are used, and how can they affect performance?",
    shortAnswer: "`data-` attributes do not cause a re-render by themselves, but if they change and are tied to a component's state, that can lead to the component being updated in React. Excessive use or frequent changes to `data-` attributes can hurt performance, since they can cause unnecessary repaints.",
    longAnswer: "`data-*` attributes in HTML let developers store custom data directly on elements. These attributes are often used in JavaScript to reach additional information associated with elements, which can be useful for changing the content or behaviour of web pages dynamically.\nWhen the values of these attributes are changed in JavaScript, that can cause the associated elements to re-render, especially in frameworks such as React. Depending on the implementation, the changes may lead to the virtual DOM being recomputed and synchronized with the real DOM, which can affect performance. The more elements have to be re-rendered, the more resources it takes, and this can slow the application down.\nTo optimize performance when using `data-*` attributes, the following is worth considering:\n\n- Minimize the number of changes: try to avoid changing attribute values often, especially in large lists of elements.\n\n- Use batching: combining several changes into a single update can reduce the number of re-renders.\n\n- Introduce memoization: in some cases memoization can be used to prevent unnecessary computation and renders.\n\nManaging the use of `data-*` attributes properly can therefore improve the performance of web applications considerably.",
    codeExample: null,
  },
  32: {
    source: "Как работает Shadow DOM? Какие преимущества он дает для разработки веб-компонентов?",
    question: "How does the Shadow DOM work? What advantages does it offer for developing web components?",
    shortAnswer: "The Shadow DOM creates an isolated DOM tree for a web component that is hidden from the rest of the document.\nThe advantages:\n\n- It isolates styles and markup.\n\n- It makes reuse easier.\n\n- It encapsulates logic.",
    longAnswer: "The Shadow DOM is a technology that lets you create a \"shadow\" DOM inside an element, isolating its content and styles from the main document. This lets developers create web components that can carry their own styles and elements without affecting other parts of the page. The Shadow DOM makes elements more independent and more reusable.\nWhen you create a Shadow DOM for an element — with the `attachShadow()` method, for example — a new context is created that does not inherit styles and scripts from the outer document. Here is an example of code demonstrating the creation of a Shadow DOM:\n\nThe advantages of the Shadow DOM:\n\n- Style isolation: styles inside the Shadow DOM do not conflict with global styles, which makes it ideal for developing components.\n\n- Encapsulation: everything inside the Shadow DOM is hidden from the outer environment, which reduces the chance of conflicts and errors.\n\n- Reusability: components with a Shadow DOM can easily be reused in different parts of an application, ensuring consistency and making maintenance easier.\n\nThe Shadow DOM is therefore a powerful tool for developing web components that provides encapsulation and protection from style conflicts.",
    codeExample: "class MyElement extends HTMLElement {    \n  constructor() {        \n    super();        \n    const shadow = this.attachShadow({ mode: 'open' });        \n    shadow.innerHTML = `            \n      <style>                \n        p {                    \n          color: blue;                \n        }            \n      </style>            \n      <p>Hello from Shadow DOM!</p>        \n    `;    \n  } \n} \n\ncustomElements.define('my-element', MyElement);",
  },
  33: {
    source: "Что такое Progressive Enhancement и Graceful Degradation? Как HTML может поддерживать оба подхода?",
    question: "What are Progressive Enhancement and Graceful Degradation? How can HTML support both approaches?",
    shortAnswer: "Progressive Enhancement and Graceful Degradation are two approaches to web development aimed at building adaptable, accessible web applications.\nProgressive Enhancement: start from a basic version and add improvements for modern browsers.\nGraceful Degradation: start from the full version and adapt it for older browsers.",
    longAnswer: "Progressive Enhancement and Graceful Degradation are the two main approaches to developing web applications, and they help ensure those applications are accessible and compatible with various devices and browsers.\n\n- Progressive Enhancement: this approach starts from basic functionality that is available on every device and browser. At the first stage a simple version of the application is built with HTML, which provides the main content and structure. Then styles are added with CSS and finally more complex features with JavaScript. This ensures accessibility for every user, regardless of their device or browser. For example, if you are building a form, it will work with basic HTML elements, while styles and validation with JavaScript are added for newer browsers.\n\n- Graceful Degradation: in this approach the full version of the application is built first using modern technologies such as CSS and JavaScript. The developer then works on making the application available to less advanced browsers or devices that may not support every feature. For example, if you are building an interactive element with JavaScript, you can provide a simple text alternative for users who cannot use JavaScript.\n\nHTML as the foundation:\nHTML supports both approaches by providing the basic structure and content. For example, you can use HTML to create a form that will work on every device. Then you can add CSS to improve its appearance and JavaScript to add interactivity. This lets developers build accessible, adaptable web applications that interact well with users regardless of their technology.\nThese approaches therefore help you create web applications that work well on every device and provide a better user experience.",
    codeExample: null,
  },
  34: {
    source: "Какие есть способы оптимизации загрузки изображений в HTML? Что такое форматы изображений WebP и AVIF?",
    question: "What ways are there of optimizing the loading of images in HTML? What are the WebP and AVIF image formats?",
    shortAnswer: "Ways of optimizing the loading of images in HTML:\n\n- Compressing images\n\n- Responsive sizes\n\n- Lazy loading\n\nImage formats:\n\n- WebP: high compression and quality; it supports both raster and animated images.\n\n- AVIF: even more efficient compression and better quality than WebP.",
    longAnswer: "Optimizing the loading of images is an important part of web development, since images can take a significant amount of time to load and have a strong effect on a page's performance. Here are some of the main methods of optimizing image loading in HTML:\nCompressing images: use tools to compress images without any loss of quality. This lets you reduce the file size and speed loading up. Formats such as JPEG and PNG can be compressed without any significant degradation in quality.\nResponsive image sizes: use the `srcset` and `sizes` attributes on the `<img>` tag to provide images of various sizes depending on the device. This lets smaller images be loaded on mobile devices and larger ones on desktops:\n\nLazy loading: use the `loading=\"lazy\"` attribute on the `<img>` tag to defer the loading of images that are outside the visible area of the screen. This helps save resources and speeds up the page's initial load:\n\nModern image formats: WebP and AVIF are modern formats that provide better compression than traditional formats such as JPEG and PNG.\nWebP: an image format from Google that supports both lossy and lossless compression. It gives you a smaller file size at a comparable image quality.\nAVIF: a new format based on the AV1 codec, which also offers excellent compression and support for HDR. It can produce even smaller files than WebP while preserving high quality.\nHere is an example of using the WebP format in HTML:\n\nThese optimization methods and modern image formats help improve the performance of web applications, reducing loading time and improving the user experience.",
    codeExample: "<img src=\"image-small.jpg\" srcset=\"image-medium.jpg 600w, image-large.jpg 1200w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 600px) 100vw, 50vw\" alt=\"Example image\">\n\n<img src=\"image.jpg\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Example image\">\n\n<picture>    \n  <source srcset=\"image.webp\" type=\"image/webp\">    \n  <img src=\"image.jpg\" alt=\"Example image\"> \n</picture>",
  },
  35: {
    source: "Что такое ARIA (Accessible Rich Internet Applications), и как правильно использовать атрибуты ARIA для улучшения доступности?",
    question: "What is ARIA (Accessible Rich Internet Applications), and how do you use ARIA attributes correctly to improve accessibility?",
    shortAnswer: "ARIA (Accessible Rich Internet Applications) is a set of attributes that helps developers improve the accessibility of complex web applications for people with disabilities. ARIA attributes can be added to HTML elements to describe their roles, states and properties for screen readers and other assistive technologies. For example, ARIA attributes can help establish that an element is a button or a heading, and provide information about its state (\"expanded\" or \"collapsed\", for example). Using ARIA properly makes content clearer and more accessible for every user.",
    longAnswer: "ARIA (Accessible Rich Internet Applications) is a specification developed to improve the accessibility of web applications, especially those that use dynamic interfaces and interactive elements such as buttons, tabs and dropdown lists. With ARIA attributes developers can provide additional information about the functionality and state of those elements, which makes it easier for users with disabilities to interact with web applications.\nThe key aspects of using ARIA:\nRoles (role): the `role` attribute defines an element's type and its function. For example, if you are building a custom element that should behave like a button, you can use `role=\"button\"`:\n\nStates (aria attributes): attributes such as `aria-expanded`, `aria-pressed` and `aria-hidden` help communicate the state of elements. For a dropdown menu, for example, you can use:\n\nValues (aria-label, aria-labelledby, aria-describedby): these attributes help provide an additional description of an element. For example:\n\nUse:\nIt is important to remember that ARIA attributes cannot replace semantic HTML. They should be used in addition to standard HTML elements, not instead of them. For example, rather than using a `div` with `role=\"button\"`, it is better to use a `<button>` element, which is already semantically correct.\nHere is an example of using ARIA properly in a form:\n\nApplying ARIA properly therefore helps make web applications more accessible, which matters for giving everyone equal access to online resources.",
    codeExample: "<div role=\"button\" tabindex=\"0\">Click me</div>\n\n<button aria-expanded=\"false\">Menu</button>\n\n<button aria-label=\"Close the window\">X</button>\n\n<form>    \n  <label for=\"username\">User name:</label>    \n  <input type=\"text\" id=\"username\" aria-required=\"true\" />    \n  <button type=\"submit\" aria-label=\"Submit the form\">Submit</button> \n</form>",
  },
  36: {
    source: "Как работают форматы ввода и валидация данных в HTML5? В чем различие между встроенной и кастомной валидацией?",
    question: "How do input formats and data validation work in HTML5? What is the difference between built-in and custom validation?",
    shortAnswer: "HTML5 provides various input formats such as `<input type=\"email\">`, `<input type=\"url\">` and `<input type=\"number\">`, which check the data entered against particular criteria automatically. Built-in validation happens automatically when a form is submitted, and the browser notifies the user of errors if the data does not match the format. Custom validation, by contrast, lets developers create their own rules for checking data with JavaScript, which gives more control and flexibility.",
    longAnswer: "HTML5 introduced many new input types and improved the mechanism for validating form data. This lets developers build more interactive, more convenient web applications with less time spent writing custom code.\nInput formats in HTML5:\nSome of the new input types include:\n\n- `<input type=\"email\">`: checks that the value entered has the form of an email address (user@example.com, for example).\n\n- `<input type=\"url\">`: checks that the value entered matches the form of a URL (https://example.com, for example).\n\n- `<input type=\"number\">`: restricts the input to numbers only and can include attributes for setting a range (`min` and `max`, for example).\n\nBuilt-in validation:\nHTML5 provides built-in form validation, which happens automatically on submission. If the data does not meet the requirements that have been set (an incorrect email format, for example), the browser shows an error message and prevents the form from being submitted. For example:\n\nIf the user enters an invalid email, the browser notifies them of it.\nCustom validation:\nCustom validation lets developers add their own rules for checking data. This is done with JavaScript and events such as `submit` or `input`. For example, you can create a check based on a particular condition:\n\nA comparison:\n\n- Built-in validation: an automatic check that the data matches the types you have set, with no need to write additional code.\n\n- Custom validation: lets you create more complex, more specific checks that the standard facilities cannot perform.\n\nBoth approaches can be used together to provide the best user experience and improve the quality of the data entered.",
    codeExample: "<form>    \n  <label for=\"email\">Email:</label>    \n  <input type=\"email\" id=\"email\" required>    \n  <button type=\"submit\">Submit</button> \n</form>\n\nconst form = document.querySelector('form'); \nform.addEventListener('submit', function(event) {    \n  const email = document.getElementById('email').value;    \n  if (!email.endsWith('@example.com')) {        \n    alert('The email must end with @example.com');        \n    event.preventDefault(); // prevents the form from being submitted    \n  } \n});",
  },
  37: {
    source: "Как работают Content Security Policy (CSP) заголовки и атрибуты вроде integrity в теге <script>?",
    question: "How do Content Security Policy (CSP) headers and attributes like integrity on the <script> tag work?",
    shortAnswer: "Content Security Policy (CSP) is a security mechanism that helps prevent various kinds of attack, such as XSS (Cross-Site Scripting), by restricting which resources may be loaded on a page. The CSP header defines where scripts, styles and other resources are allowed to be loaded from.\nThe `integrity` attribute on the `<script>` tag is used to check the integrity of the script being loaded, in order to make sure it has not been altered. If the script's hash does not match the given value, the browser refuses to load it.",
    longAnswer: "Content Security Policy (CSP) is a powerful security tool that lets web developers control which resources may be loaded and executed on their pages. CSP helps prevent Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) attacks and other security threats by creating policies for loading resources.\nAn example of using CSP:\nCSP is configured through the HTTP header `Content-Security-Policy`. Here is an example:\n\nThis header tells the browser to allow resources to be loaded only from the same origin (`'self'`) or from a trusted CDN. This limits an attacker's ability to load a malicious script.\nThe `integrity` attribute:\nThe `integrity` attribute is used on `<script>` and `<link>` tags to check the integrity of the resource being loaded. It contains a hash of the file's value, which must match the hash of the resource that was loaded. For example:\n\nIf the browser cannot verify that the script it loaded matches the given hash, it will not load it. This prevents altered or malicious code from being injected.\nCombining CSP and `integrity`:\nUsing CSP together with the `integrity` attribute creates a powerful layer of protection for web applications. It lets you not only control where resources may be loaded from but also verify their integrity before execution. This matters especially in modern web applications, where third-party libraries and frameworks are often used.\nUnderstanding and applying CSP and the `integrity` attribute properly therefore helps developers protect their applications from a great many common vulnerabilities.",
    codeExample: "Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' https://trusted.cdn.com;\n\n<script src=\"https://trusted.cdn.com/script.js\"        \n  integrity=\"sha384-oqVuAfXRKap7fdgcCY5Dn4Q3U8d6X7Ww1yq1GZ7E4j6Ic1ZrPy1X99AY2p3a8HnB\"                 \n  crossorigin=\"anonymous\">\n</script>",
  },
  38: {
    source: "Как можно улучшить безопасность HTML-страницы от CSRF, XSS-атак и других?",
    question: "How can you improve an HTML page's security against CSRF, XSS attacks and others?",
    shortAnswer: "HTML pages can be protected with various measures, such as using security headers (Content Security Policy, for example), validating data properly and guarding against XSS attacks. These measures help restrict access to resources and prevent malicious code from being injected. It is also important to observe the principles of secure coding and to use HTTPS to protect users' data. These approaches help minimize the security risks on a website.",
    longAnswer: "The security of web pages is a critically important aspect of development that demands attention at every level. There are many methods that can help increase the security of HTML pages and protect them from threats such as XSS (Cross-Site Scripting), CSRF (Cross-Site Request Forgery) and others.\nThe key aspects of HTML page security:\nContent Security Policy (CSP):\nCSP is a mechanism that lets web developers control which resources may be loaded and executed on their pages. Using the CSP header, for example, you can allow scripts to be loaded only from particular origins:\n\nValidating data:\nIt is important to check the data users enter in order to avoid malicious code being injected. The built-in form validation mechanisms in HTML5 and custom checks in JavaScript can help prevent XSS attacks:\n\nUsing HTTPS:\nEncrypting data with HTTPS protects the information passed between client and server and prevents attackers from intercepting it.\nSecurity headers:\nBesides CSP there are other headers, such as `X-Content-Type-Options`, `X-Frame-Options` and `X-XSS-Protection`, that help protect pages from various attacks.\nAuthentication and authorization:\nProtecting pages from unauthorized access through authentication mechanisms such as OAuth, and through authorization, helps restrict access to important resources.\nBy applying these measures developers can increase the security of HTML pages considerably and protect their users from potential threats.",
    codeExample: "Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' https://trusted.cdn.com;\n\nconst userInput = document.querySelector('#input').value; \nif (/<script>/.test(userInput)) {    \n  alert('Invalid input!'); \n}",
  },
  39: {
    source: "Что такое <template> и <slot>, как их использовать при создании кастомных элементов и динамических структур на странице?",
    question: "What are <template> and <slot>, and how do you use them when creating custom elements and dynamic structures on a page?",
    shortAnswer: "`<template>` is a special HTML element that lets you create fragments of code that are not displayed on the page until they are activated with JavaScript. The `<slot>` element is used in custom elements to define a place where content passed to the element can be inserted. These elements help developers create more flexible, more reusable components that can change their content dynamically depending on the context.",
    longAnswer: "HTML5 introduced the `<template>` and `<slot>` elements, which considerably simplify the creation of dynamic interfaces and custom elements. These tools help developers manage a page's content efficiently and create reusable components.\nThe `<template>` element:\nThis element is used to define a fragment of HTML that will not be displayed on the page until it is activated. It is useful for creating dynamic content without placing it in the DOM directly. For example:\n\nTo use this template you can get hold of it with JavaScript and clone it:\n\nThe `<slot>` element:\nThis element is used inside custom elements to mark the places where the user's content can be inserted. It lets you create flexible components that can accept different kinds of content. An example of a custom element using a slot:\n\nIn this example the content with the attribute `slot=\"header\"` will be inserted into the heading, and the rest of the content into the main paragraph.\nUse:\nUsing `<template>` and `<slot>` lets developers create powerful, reusable components that can be configured dynamically depending on the context. This makes development more efficient and simplifies the management of content on pages.\nThese tools therefore help you write cleaner, better organized and more efficient code, which makes them important for modern web applications.",
    codeExample: "<template id=\"myTemplate\">    \n  <div class=\"item\">A list item</div> \n</template>\n\nconst template = document.getElementById('myTemplate').content; \nconst clone = document.importNode(template, true); \ndocument.body.appendChild(clone);\n\n<template id=\"myComponent\">    \n  <div>        \n    <h1><slot name=\"header\"></slot></h1>        \n    <p><slot></slot></p>    \n  </div> \n</template> \n\n<my-component>    \n  <span slot=\"header\">A heading</span>    \n  This content will be inserted into the main slot. \n</my-component>",
  },
  40: {
    source: "Что делают метатеги <meta charset=\"UTF-8\">, <meta viewport>, <meta http-equiv>?",
    question: "What do the meta tags <meta charset=\"UTF-8\">, <meta viewport> and <meta http-equiv> do?",
    shortAnswer: "Meta tags are HTML elements that provide information about a page to the browser and to search engines. For example, `<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">` sets the character encoding, which matters for text to display correctly. `<meta name=\"viewport\">` is used to control how the page is displayed on mobile devices, letting the content adapt to the screen's dimensions. Meta tags such as `<meta http-equiv>` can set parameters related to HTTP headers, which also helps optimize the page.",
    longAnswer: "Meta tags play an important part in the optimization and correct display of web pages. They live in the `<head>` section of an HTML document and provide information that browsers, search engines and other clients can use.\nThe character encoding:\nUsing `<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">` sets the document's character encoding. UTF-8 is the most widespread encoding and supports a great many languages and characters, which ensures that text displays correctly.\n\nSettings for mobile devices:\nThe `<meta name=\"viewport\">` tag controls the page's dimensions and scale on mobile devices. This matters especially for responsive design. For example:\n`<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0\">`\nThis tells the browser that the page's width should match the device's width, and sets the initial scale.\nHTTP headers:\nMeta tags such as `<meta http-equiv=\"X-UA-Compatible\" content=\"IE=edge\">` can tell browsers how to process a page. This tag helps ensure compatibility with the latest HTML and CSS standards:\n\nSEO and social media:\nMeta tags are also used for SEO and social media. For example, `<meta name=\"description\" content=\"A description of the page\">` gives search engines a brief description of the page. Open Graph tags (`<meta property=\"og:title\">`, `<meta property=\"og:description\">`) help optimize the content when links are posted on social platforms.\nUsing the right meta tags not only improves a page's performance and accessibility but also helps SEO, which can increase a site's visibility in search engines. This makes them an important part of developing web pages, especially given modern standards and requirements.",
    codeExample: "<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n\n<meta http-equiv=\"X-UA-Compatible\" content=\"IE=edge\">",
  },
  41: {
    source: "Что такое microdata, и как она используется для структурирования данных на странице? В чем преимущества перед JSON-LD и RDFa?",
    question: "What is microdata, and how is it used to structure data on a page? What are its advantages over JSON-LD and RDFa?",
    shortAnswer: "Microdata is a format built into HTML that helps structure the data on a page so that search engines and other services can recognize and interpret it easily. Using attributes such as `itemprop` and `itemscope`, developers can describe entities — products or articles, for example. Unlike JSON-LD, which is stored separately from the main HTML code, microdata is added directly into the page's markup, which can be simpler to integrate but less flexible to change. RDFa is another alternative that can be integrated with any HTML element.",
    longAnswer: "Microdata is a technology built into HTML5 that is used to structure the data on a web page so that search engines and applications can understand its content better. It helps you describe entities such as people, products, events and places, and tie them to particular attributes and values that search engines can index.\nAn example of using microdata:\nImagine a web page that contains information about a book. With microdata we can describe the book's main properties:\n\nHere `itemscope` indicates that the element contains data about a book, while `itemprop` defines properties such as the name, the author and the publication date.\nThe advantages of microdata over JSON-LD and RDFa:\nMicrodata:\nIt is embedded directly in the HTML, which makes it easier to use in existing markup. This can be useful if a developer wants to integrate semantic data without adding a separate block of code, as JSON-LD requires.\nJSON-LD:\nJSON-LD is stored separately from the HTML and delivered as a script. It is more flexible for working with large volumes of structured data and easier to generate and change automatically.\nRDFa:\nThis is another way of adding semantic markup, and it integrates with any HTML element. RDFa can be more universal, since it does not depend on specific attributes the way microdata does.\nMicrodata is useful for embedding semantic data directly in HTML, but JSON-LD is considered more flexible and is supported by search engines such as Google. The choice between these methods depends on the complexity of the data and on the project's requirements.",
    codeExample: "<div itemscope itemtype=\"http://schema.org/Book\">   \n  <h2 itemprop=\"name\">The book's title</h2>   \n  <span itemprop=\"author\">The book's author</span>   \n  <span itemprop=\"datePublished\">2024</span> \n</div>",
  },
  42: {
    source: "Как работает HTML Custom Elements API, и как можно создать и зарегистрировать собственные элементы?",
    question: "How does the HTML Custom Elements API work, and how can you create and register your own elements?",
    shortAnswer: "The HTML Custom Elements API lets developers create their own HTML elements that behave like standard elements but can carry their own unique logic and styling. To create a custom element you have to define a class that inherits from `HTMLElement` and register it with `customElements.define()`. These elements can then be used on a page just like standard HTML tags. Custom Elements extend HTML's capabilities, making it more flexible for modern web applications.",
    longAnswer: "The HTML Custom Elements API is part of the Web Components specification, and it lets developers create their own HTML elements with custom logic and behaviour. Custom Elements let you define new kinds of element that can be reused anywhere in an application, improving the structure and modularity of the code.\nCreating a custom element:\nTo create a custom element you have to take three steps:\n\n- Create a class inheriting from `HTMLElement`:\n\n- Register the element with the browser using `customElements.define()`:\n\n- Use the custom element on the page:\n\nOnce registered, the `my-custom-element` element can be used anywhere in an HTML page, like an ordinary tag.\nThe advantages of custom elements:\n\n- Encapsulated logic: the element's behavioural logic is isolated entirely inside the class, which makes the code better organized and more modular.\n\n- Reuse: once you have created a custom element, it can be used repeatedly in different parts of the application, which reduces code duplication.\n\nCustom Elements are a tool for creating reusable components in web applications. They make HTML more flexible by letting developers define new elements with unique functions and behaviour.",
    codeExample: "class MyCustomElement extends HTMLElement {    \n  constructor() {        \n    super();        \n    this.innerHTML = \"<p>This is a custom element</p>\";    \n  } \n}\n\ncustomElements.define('my-custom-element', MyCustomElement);\n\n<my-custom-element></my-custom-element>",
  },
  43: {
    source: "Как управлять фокусом на странице с помощью атрибутов tabindex и методов, таких как focus()?",
    question: "How do you manage focus on a page with the tabindex attribute and methods such as focus()?",
    shortAnswer: "The `tabindex` attribute controls the order in which the elements on a page receive focus during keyboard navigation. Elements with a positive `tabindex` receive focus in ascending order of that number, elements with `tabindex=\"0\"` in the order they appear, and negative values make an element unreachable by keyboard focus. The `focus()` method in JavaScript lets you set focus on an element programmatically. This matters for ensuring accessibility and convenient control for users with special needs.",
    longAnswer: "Focus on a web page is the mechanism that determines which element is active and ready for the user to interact with. It matters for navigating a page by keyboard and for making web content accessible to people with disabilities.\nThe `tabindex` attribute:\nThe `tabindex` attribute controls the order in which the elements on a page will receive focus when the `Tab` key is used. The values of `tabindex` can be:\n\n- Positive values: elements with a positive `tabindex` receive focus first, in ascending order of the value. For example, an element with `tabindex=\"1\"` will receive focus before one with `tabindex=\"2\"`.\n\n- The value `0`: elements with `tabindex=\"0\"` take part in the standard focus navigation but have no priority.\n\n- Negative values: elements with a negative `tabindex`, such as `tabindex=\"-1\"`, cannot be focused by keyboard, but focus can be set on them programmatically with JavaScript.\n\nAn example of using `tabindex`:\n\nIn this example the focus order will be: Button 1, Button 2, Button 3, since `tabindex` determines the sequence.\nThe `focus()` method:\nIn JavaScript the `focus()` method lets you set focus on an element programmatically. This is useful for managing focus when the interface changes, or when you need to draw the user's attention to a particular element:\n\nAn example of using `focus()` to improve accessibility: when the user closes a modal window, focus can be returned to the element it was opened from:\n\nManaging focus with `tabindex` and methods such as `focus()` matters for ensuring accessibility and improving the user experience. This is especially critical for users who rely on the keyboard to navigate pages, such as people with disabilities.",
    codeExample: "<button tabindex=\"1\">Button 1</button> \n<button tabindex=\"3\">Button 3</button> \n<button tabindex=\"2\">Button 2</button>\n\ndocument.getElementById('myButton').focus();\n\nbutton.addEventListener('click', function() {\n  modal.close();    \n  button.focus(); // We return focus to the button after closing \n});",
  },
  44: {
    source: "Как работают fallback-механизмы в HTML5 для старых браузеров и какие техники следует применять для кроссбраузерной совместимости?",
    question: "How do fallback mechanisms work in HTML5 for older browsers, and which techniques should be used for cross-browser compatibility?",
    shortAnswer: "HTML5 offers new tags and features that older browsers do not always support. So that pages work properly in such browsers, fallback mechanisms are used — alternative content or tags that are picked up when the main functionality is unavailable. For video, for example, you can use an `<object>` element or a link to download the file if `<video>` is not supported. For cross-browser compatibility, polyfills and shims are also applied — scripts that add support for modern features to older browsers.",
    longAnswer: "Fallback mechanisms and cross-browser compatibility are important aspects of development that keep a web page's functionality available in different browsers, including older versions.\nAn example of a fallback for `<video>`:\n\nIn this example, if the browser does not support `<video>`, the user will see the text and a link for downloading the video.\nA fallback for CSS:\nIf a CSS property is not supported, you can use obsolete or alternative styles:\n\nCross-browser compatibility:\nTo achieve compatibility the following are often applied:\n\n- Polyfills: these are scripts that add support for modern features to older browsers. A polyfill for `fetch()` or `Promise`, for example.\n\n- Shims: similar to polyfills, but they replace unavailable functions with alternatives.\n\nAn example of a polyfill for `fetch()`: if the browser does not support `fetch()`, you can use a polyfill:\n\nCross-browser compatibility techniques:\n\n- Progressive Enhancement: basic functionality for everyone, enhanced functionality for newer browsers.\n\n- Graceful Degradation: features for modern browsers are developed first, and then fallback mechanisms are added for older ones.\n\n- Using feature detection: instead of checking the browser's version, specific capabilities are checked:\n\nUsing fallback mechanisms and polyfills matters for keeping web pages working on devices with differing support for modern standards. It helps make a site available to a greater number of users.",
    codeExample: "<video controls>  \n  <source src=\"video.mp4\" type=\"video/mp4\">  \n  <source src=\"video.ogv\" type=\"video/ogg\">  Your browser does not support the video tag.  \n  <a href=\"video.mp4\">Download the video</a> \n</video>\n\n.example {  \n  background: rgb(255, 0, 0); /* the old syntax */  \n  background: linear-gradient(red, yellow); /* the new syntax */ \n}\n\n<script src=\"https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/fetch/2.0.4/fetch.min.js\"></script>\n\nif ('querySelector' in document) {    \n  // code for modern browsers \n}",
  },
  45: {
    source: "Что такое CORS (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing), и как он относится к встроенным элементам типа <iframe>, <img>, и <script>?",
    question: "What is CORS (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing), and how does it relate to embedded elements such as <iframe>, <img> and <script>?",
    shortAnswer: "CORS is a mechanism that lets you restrict or permit the exchange of data between different domains. Browsers forbid requests to resources on another domain by default, for security. To permit such requests, the server has to send special headers. Elements such as `<iframe>`, `<img>` and `<script>` can load resources from other domains, but some operations — reaching the content of an `<iframe>` from another domain, for example — require CORS support.",
    longAnswer: "CORS (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing) is a web browser security policy that prevents cross-domain HTTP requests from being performed unless the server has permitted them explicitly. It exists to protect web applications from attacks such as CSRF (Cross-Site Request Forgery) and XSS (Cross-Site Scripting).\nHow CORS works:\nWhen the browser tries to fetch a resource from another domain, it sends a preflight request with the `OPTIONS` method in order to find out whether the server permits access. If the server does permit requests from another domain, it sends a response with the `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` header naming the permitted domain (or the `*` character, which means any domain).\nAn example of CORS headers:\n`Access-Control-Allow-Origin: https://example.com Access-Control-Allow-Methods: GET, POST`\nThese headers let the browser understand that requests from the domain `https://example.com` may be performed.\nCORS and embedded elements:\n\n- `<iframe>`: without CORS support, access to the content of an iframe from another domain will be blocked. This protects against manipulation of content loaded from third-party sources.\n\n- `<img>` and `<script>`: these tags can load resources from other domains without restriction, but access to the loaded data (the pixels of an image, for example) will be blocked if the server does not support CORS.\n\nAn example of using CORS with `fetch()`:\n\nCORS matters for the security of web applications, letting you control which domains may reach your resources. Developers need to configure the headers on the server correctly in order to permit or restrict access from other domains.",
    codeExample: "fetch('https://api.example.com/data', {  \n  method: 'GET',  \n  headers: {    \n    'Origin': 'https://mywebsite.com'  \n  } \n}) \n.then(response => response.json()) \n.then(data => console.log(data)) \n.catch(error => console.error('Error:', error));",
  },
  46: {
    source: "Как использовать атрибут srcset с тегом <img>?",
    question: "How do you use the srcset attribute with the <img> tag?",
    shortAnswer: "The `srcset` attribute on the `<img>` tag lets you specify several versions of an image with different resolutions or sizes. The browser chooses the most suitable version automatically depending on the screen's pixel density and width. Combined with the `loading=\"lazy\"` attribute you can use lazy loading of images to optimize the page's performance.",
    longAnswer: "The `srcset` attribute is used with `<img>` to give several versions of the same image, so that the browser can choose the most suitable one for the current device. The format of use may involve different image sizes (`image-1x.jpg 1x, image-2x.jpg 2x`, for example) or different widths (`image-small.jpg 500w, image-large.jpg 1000w`).\nDepending on the device, the browser loads the image with the resolution or size it needs, ensuring optimal performance.\nThe `loading=\"lazy\"` attribute lets the browser load images only when they come into the user's view. This reduces the page's loading time and the amount of data downloaded.\nAn example of use:\n\nIn this example the browser will choose a suitable image depending on the screen's width and load it when it becomes visible.",
    codeExample: "<img src=\"image-default.jpg\" \n    srcset=\"image-500w.jpg 500w, image-1000w.jpg 1000w\" \n    sizes=\"(max-width: 600px) 480px, 800px\"\n    loading=\"lazy\" \n    alt=\"A description of the image\">",
  },
  47: {
    source: "Как реализовать ленивую загрузку (lazy loading) элементов на странице с помощью атрибута loading? Какие есть альтернативные методы?",
    question: "How do you implement lazy loading of elements on a page with the loading attribute? What alternative methods are there?",
    shortAnswer: "Lazy loading is a technique that lets you defer the loading of images or frames until they appear in the visible area of the screen. The `loading=\"lazy\"` attribute on an `<img>` or `<iframe>` tag defers the element's loading automatically. This reduces the page's initial loading time and lowers data usage. Alternative methods include using the Intersection Observer API for more flexible control over when elements load.",
    longAnswer: "Lazy loading is a performance optimization method that lets resources be loaded only when the user needs them — that is, when they come into the visible area of the screen (the viewport). It is useful for pages with a lot of images or video, where loading every resource at once can slow the page's initial load down.\nThe `loading` attribute:\nHTML5 added native support for lazy loading on `<img>` and `<iframe>` elements through the `loading` attribute.\n\nWhen this attribute is used, the browser loads the image only when it is going to be in the visible area of the screen.\nThe advantages of `loading=\"lazy\"`:\n\n- Simplicity of use: adding the attribute requires minimal changes to the code.\n\n- Better performance: it speeds up the page's rendering, since it reduces the number of resources loaded.\n\n- Browser support: modern browsers support this attribute, which makes it available without third-party libraries.\n\nAlternative methods:\nThe Intersection Observer API: this API gives you more control over when elements should load. It tracks when an element comes into the visible area and triggers the loading of the resource.\n\nDeferred loading with JavaScript: you can defer loading by hand, changing the value of the `src` attribute on images or `<iframe>` elements only when they become visible.\nLazy loading helps reduce a page's loading time and save the user's data. Although the `loading=\"lazy\"` attribute is a convenient, simple solution, more complex scenarios can be implemented with the Intersection Observer for fine-tuning the loading process.",
    codeExample: "<img src=\"image.jpg\" alt=\"Lazy loaded image\" loading=\"lazy\">\n\nconst img = document.querySelector('img'); \nconst observer = new IntersectionObserver((entries, observer) => {  \n  entries.forEach(entry => {    \n    if (entry.isIntersecting) {      \n      entry.target.src = entry.target.dataset.src;      \n      observer.unobserve(entry.target);    \n    }  \n  }); \n}); \nobserver.observe(img);",
  },
  48: {
    source: "Как работает элемент <dialog> и API для управления модальными окнами? Какие особенности его использования?",
    question: "How does the <dialog> element and the API for managing modal windows work? What are the particulars of using it?",
    shortAnswer: "The `<dialog>` element lets you create built-in modal windows on a web page without any need for third-party libraries. It has the methods `.showModal()` for displaying the window in modal mode and `.close()` for closing it. One of its particulars is built-in support for modality and for trapping focus on the elements inside the dialog, which makes it convenient for creating interactive pop-up windows. It also supports attributes for configuring its appearance and behaviour.",
    longAnswer: "The `<dialog>` element appeared in HTML5 and is intended for creating modal or non-modal dialogs such as pop-up windows. Its key task is to display a window on top of the main content while managing the user's focus.\nThe element's main methods and attributes:\nThe `.showModal()` method: opens the window in modal mode, blocking interaction with the rest of the page's elements until it is closed.\n\nThe window becomes modal, and the user cannot interact with elements outside the dialog.\nThe `.close()` method: closes the dialog window.\n\nThe `open` attribute: if present, it makes the dialog visible without modality (non-modal mode).\n\nThe particulars of using it:\n\n- Focus: when the dialog is open in modal mode, focus stays inside it, which improves accessibility (a11y) and makes interaction with the interface more predictable for users.\n\n- Closing by default: if the user needs to close the dialog by pressing \"Esc\" or by clicking outside the window, that behaviour can be handled through events.\n\nExamples of use:\n\nThe `<dialog>` element lets you simplify the development of modal windows and improve their accessibility, and it supports modal behaviour without any complex libraries.",
    codeExample: "<dialog id=\"myDialog\">This is a modal dialog</dialog> \n\n<script> document.getElementById('myDialog').showModal();</script>\n\ndocument.getElementById('myDialog').close();\n\n<dialog open>This is an open dialog</dialog>\n\n<dialog id=\"myDialog\">  \n  <p>Example of dialog content.</p>  \n  <button onclick=\"this.closest('dialog').close()\">Close</button> \n</dialog> \n<button onclick=\"document.getElementById('myDialog').showModal()\">Open Dialog</button>",
  },
  49: {
    source: "Что такое HTML Imports и почему они были отклонены стандартом? Какие современные альтернативы существуют для организации модулей?",
    question: "What were HTML Imports and why were they rejected by the standard? What modern alternatives exist for organizing modules?",
    shortAnswer: "HTML Imports were an experimental technology that let you load and include HTML files in other documents using the `<link>` tag. They were rejected because of weak support and the existence of alternative standards such as JavaScript modules and Web Components. Modern alternatives include ES modules (import/export) and technologies based on templates and components, such as React and Web Components.",
    longAnswer: "HTML Imports were proposed as a way of including HTML code in other documents modularly. With the `<link>` tag you could load an external HTML file and include its content in a page:\n\nThis technology never gained wide acceptance, however, and was rejected for a number of reasons:\n\n- Limited browser support: HTML Imports were supported only in some browsers, such as Chrome, which made them unsuitable for cross-browser projects.\n\n- Performance difficulties: importing HTML files could increase loading time and make dependency management harder.\n\n- Better alternatives: the ECMAScript standard (ES6) offered more universal modules based on JavaScript, which handle the task of splitting and reusing code better.\n\nModern alternatives:\n\n- ES modules (JavaScript): this is the standardized way of organizing and loading code. With the `import`/`export` directives you can split code into modules that are easy to connect to one another.\n\n- Web Components: components based on the Web Components standard let you create custom elements containing HTML, CSS and JavaScript, which makes them an excellent alternative for building a modular interface.\n\nHTML Imports therefore failed to gain support because of the appearance of more capable standards such as ES modules and Web Components, which provide flexibility and power for building modular applications.",
    codeExample: "<link rel=\"import\" href=\"header.html\">\n\n// module.js \nexport const myFunction = () => { console.log(\"Hello, World!\"); }; \n// main.js \nimport { myFunction } from './module.js'; \nmyFunction();\n\nclass MyComponent extends HTMLElement {  \n  connectedCallback() {    \n    this.innerHTML = `<p>Custom component content</p>`;  \n  } \n} \ncustomElements.define('my-component', MyComponent);",
  },
  50: {
    source: "Какие существуют методы оптимизации производительности рендеринга HTML, такие как минимизация перерисовок и рефлоу?",
    question: "What methods exist for optimizing the performance of HTML rendering, such as minimizing repaints and reflows?",
    shortAnswer: "Optimizing rendering involves minimizing reflow operations (rebuilding the document's structure) and repaints (updating visual changes). This is achieved by reducing how often the DOM changes, by buffering changes through `requestAnimationFrame` and by using CSS transforms, which do not cause a reflow. It is also important to reduce the number of heavy operations such as changing the size and position of elements.",
    longAnswer: "The process of rendering HTML involves several stages: building the DOM tree and the render tree, computing styles and applying them, and the reflow and repaint processes.\n\n- Reflow: this is the process in which the browser recalculates the position and size of every element on the page when the DOM or the CSS changes. It is one of the most expensive operations.\n\n- Repaint: this is the process of updating the visible part of the page without changing its structure (changing a background colour, for example).\n\nFrequent reflows and repaints can slow a page down considerably, especially on mobile devices with low performance.\nOptimization methods:\n\n- Minimizing changes to the DOM: changing a large number of DOM elements one at a time causes a reflow and a repaint for every change. It is better to group the changes or to use DOM fragments to insert several elements at once.\n\n- Using CSS transforms: changes through `transform` and `opacity` normally do not cause a reflow, since they do not require the position of elements to be recalculated.\n\n- Buffering changes with `requestAnimationFrame`: this lets the browser perform the updates before the screen's next repaint, preventing frequent repaints.\n\n- Avoiding the reading of properties that cause a reflow: properties such as `offsetHeight` or `clientWidth` cause a reflow, since the browser has to recalculate their values. Try to cache the results instead, or read such properties less often.\n\nMinimizing reflows and repaints can therefore increase a page's performance considerably, especially when working with dynamic content and animations.",
    codeExample: "const fragment = document.createDocumentFragment(); \nfor (let i = 0; i < 100; i++) {  \n  const div = document.createElement('div');  \n  fragment.appendChild(div); \n} \ndocument.body.appendChild(fragment);\n\n.animate {  transform: translateX(100px); /* A fast animation without a reflow */ }\n\nlet element = document.getElementById('myElement'); \nrequestAnimationFrame(() => {  element.style.left = '100px'; });",
  },
  51: {
    source: "Как реализовать многоязычную поддержку (i18n) в HTML? Какие атрибуты и техники используются для этого?",
    question: "How do you implement multilingual support (i18n) in HTML? Which attributes and techniques are used for it?",
    shortAnswer: "For multilingual support in HTML the `lang` and `dir` attributes are used. The `lang` attribute states the language of the content (`lang=\"en\"` for English, for example), while `dir` states the direction of the text (`dir=\"rtl\"` for languages read from right to left, for example). To manage dynamic localization, JavaScript and libraries such as i18next are often used, which let you change the interface's language according to the user's preferences.",
    longAnswer: "Multilingual support (or internationalization, i18n) lets you display a web page's content in several languages, in order to make the site available to users from different countries. The main techniques involve using HTML attributes and third-party tools for changing the language dynamically.\nThe `lang` attribute: this attribute is used to state the language of an element's content or of the whole page. It helps search engines, and screen readers too, interpret the content correctly.\n\nThe `dir` attribute: sets the direction of the text. For Arabic or Hebrew, for example, `dir=\"rtl\"` (right-to-left) is used, which changes the direction of the text on the page.\n\nLocalization with JavaScript: to change the language dynamically according to the user's choice or the browser's settings, you can use libraries such as i18next. These libraries let you keep translations in JSON files and substitute the appropriate values into the page.\n\nFor more complex applications dynamic localization is often applied, where the language and text direction can change in real time based on the user's settings.",
    codeExample: "<html lang=\"en\">  \n  <body>    \n    <p lang=\"fr\">Bonjour!</p> <!-- This paragraph is in French -->  \n  </body> \n</html>\n\n<p dir=\"rtl\">هذا نص عربي</p> <!-- Text in Arabic -->\n\nimport i18next from 'i18next'; \ni18next.init({  \n  resources: {    \n    en: { translation: { \"welcome\": \"Welcome\" } },    \n    fr: { translation: { \"welcome\": \"Bienvenue\" } }  \n  },  \n  lng: \"en\", // The default language \n}); \ndocument.getElementById('welcome').innerText = i18next.t('welcome');",
  },
  52: {
    source: "Как использовать элемент <canvas> для рисования графики на стороне клиента? В каких сценариях это применимо?",
    question: "How do you use the <canvas> element to draw graphics on the client side? In which scenarios is this applicable?",
    shortAnswer: "The `<canvas>` element is used to draw graphics with JavaScript on a web page. With it you can create both simple shapes and complex animations and games. It has no built-in content, and every graphical element is drawn dynamically through methods such as `fillRect()` for drawing rectangles and `beginPath()` for creating lines and curves.",
    longAnswer: "The `<canvas>` element is an HTML5 element that provides a place for drawing with JavaScript. Unlike other HTML elements, `<canvas>` does not display content directly but is a container in which graphics can be drawn with scripts. The `CanvasRenderingContext2D` API is used to work with it, and it provides methods for drawing shapes, lines, text and images.\nAn example of simple use of `<canvas>`:\n\nWith the context's methods you can draw both simple shapes and more complex elements:\n\n- Rectangles: `fillRect(x, y, width, height)` draws a filled rectangle.\n\n- Lines and curves: `beginPath()` is used to create a path, after which lines can be added through `moveTo()` and `lineTo()`.\n\nAn example of drawing a circle:\n\nScenarios of use:\n\n- Games and animations: `<canvas>` is often used to create games, since it lets graphics be updated quickly and supports dynamic interaction.\n\n- Data visualization: it is also applied to create charts and diagrams.\n\n- Image editing: with `<canvas>` you can manipulate images (cropping, filters and so on).\n\nThe `<canvas>` element is a powerful tool for creating interactive graphics in the browser, providing a broad set of methods for working with 2D content.",
    codeExample: "<canvas id=\"myCanvas\" width=\"200\" height=\"200\"></canvas> \n<script>  \n  const canvas = document.getElementById('myCanvas');  \n  const ctx = canvas.getContext('2d');  // We draw a rectangle  \n  ctx.fillStyle = 'green';  \n  ctx.fillRect(10, 10, 150, 100); \n</script>\n\nctx.beginPath(); \nctx.arc(100, 100, 50, 0, Math.PI * 2); \nctx.stroke();",
  },
  53: {
    source: "Что такое HTML5 History API и как его использовать для управления навигацией без перезагрузки страницы?",
    question: "What is the HTML5 History API and how do you use it to manage navigation without reloading the page?",
    shortAnswer: "The HTML5 History API lets you change the URL in the browser without reloading the page, while preserving the navigation history. The API's main methods are `pushState()` and `replaceState()`, plus the `popstate` event. They let you change the page's state dynamically while updating the URL, which makes navigation in single-page applications (SPAs) smoother and more interactive.",
    longAnswer: "The HTML5 History API is a set of methods for managing the browser's navigation history without reloading the page. In traditional web applications every link you follow leads to a new page being loaded. With the History API you can change a page's content and URL dynamically without reloading it fully, which makes the interaction smoother, especially in single-page applications (SPAs).\nThe History API's main methods:\n\n- `history.pushState(state, title, url)`: adds a new entry to the browser's history with an updated URL. This method does not reload the page but lets you change the address bar.\n\n- `history.replaceState(state, title, url)`: replaces the current history entry without adding a new one, which is useful when you need to change the URL but not record a new step in the history.\n\n- `window.onpopstate`: this event fires when the user moves back or forward through the history. It can be used to update the page's content on such navigations.\n\nThe History API is used actively in modern frameworks such as React and Vue to manage routing in SPAs. This lets you make navigation more interactive and speed up the application's response.",
    codeExample: "history.pushState({page: 1}, \"Title\", \"/new-url\");\n\nhistory.replaceState({page: 2}, \"Title\", \"/another-url\");\n\nwindow.onpopstate = function(event) {  \n  console.log(\"location: \" + document.location + \", state: \" + JSON.stringify(event.state)); \n};",
  },
  54: {
    source: "Что такое Content Delivery Network (CDN) и как правильно подключать ресурсы HTML через CDN для повышения производительности?",
    question: "What is a Content Delivery Network (CDN) and how should you attach HTML resources through a CDN to improve performance?",
    shortAnswer: "A Content Delivery Network (CDN) is a network of servers located all over the world that hold copies of a website's static resources, such as images, CSS and JavaScript. Attaching resources through a CDN lets them be loaded from the nearest server, which speeds up the page's loading time and reduces the load on the main server. To attach them through a CDN, the URLs of the external resources are normally used, for example:\n\n`<script src=\"https://cdn.example.com/library.js\"></script>`",
    longAnswer: "A Content Delivery Network (CDN) is a distributed network of servers used to store and deliver content such as images, styles, scripts and video to users based on their geographical location. A CDN's main purpose is to improve a site's performance by reducing loading time, since the resources are loaded from the server nearest the user.\nThe advantages of using a CDN:\n\n- Faster page loads: since a CDN keeps copies of the files on servers all over the world, users can load the resources from the server closest to them, which reduces latency.\n\n- Less load on the main server: by distributing the traffic across many servers, the load on the origin server is reduced, which lets it handle more requests.\n\n- Reliability: if one server in the CDN fails, requests are redirected to the other available servers, which increases the site's resilience.\n\nAn example of attaching a resource through a CDN:\n\nIn this example the jQuery library is loaded from a CDN server, which improves the response time for users all over the world. Many popular libraries and frameworks, such as Bootstrap and React, are also served through CDNs, which makes attaching them easier.\nUsing a CDN properly:\n\n- Caching: a CDN supports the caching of resources, which lets users load them faster on repeat visits to the site.\n\n- A fallback: it is also important to provide a fallback in case the CDN is temporarily unavailable. For example, you can give a local path to the file if the load from the CDN fails:\n\nUsing a CDN improves the performance and reliability of sites considerably, especially when serving a global audience.",
    codeExample: "<!-- Attaching the jQuery library through a CDN --> \n<script src=\"https://code.jquery.com/jquery-3.6.0.min.js\"></script>\n\n<script src=\"https://cdn.example.com/library.js\"></script> <script>\n  if(!window.library){\n    document.write('<script src=\"/local/library.js\"><\\/script>');\n  }\n</script>",
  },
  55: {
    source: "Как интегрировать веб-шрифты (например, Google Fonts) в HTML и какие факторы влияют на производительность загрузки?",
    question: "How do you integrate web fonts (Google Fonts, for example) in HTML, and which factors affect loading performance?",
    shortAnswer: "Web fonts such as Google Fonts can be attached through a `<link>` element in the `<head>` section of your HTML, or you can use the CSS `@import`. Fonts can slow a page's rendering down, however, so it is important to optimize their loading. The main factors affecting performance include the number of font styles, their format and the loading strategy (`font-display: swap`, for example, to avoid blocking the rendering of text).",
    longAnswer: "Integrating web fonts (Google Fonts, for example) is a simple process that lets you use external fonts on your site. It is done through HTML and CSS. One of the most popular ways of attaching web fonts is using Google Fonts through a `<link>` tag in the HTML or the `@import` directive in CSS.\nAn example of integration through HTML:\n\nAn example through CSS:\n\nWhen using web fonts it is important to consider performance, since the browser has to load the fonts before it can start displaying the text. Here are several factors that affect loading speed:\n\n- The number of fonts and their styles: the more fonts and variations (weights or italics, for example), the more data has to be loaded.\n\n- The font format: support for various formats (WOFF, WOFF2, TTF) matters for cross-browser compatibility. WOFF2 is the more compressed, preferable format.\n\n- Loading strategies: using the `font-display: swap` property lets the browser display the text with a fallback font first and then replace it with the web font once it has loaded. This prevents empty space on the page while the font is loading.\n\nAn example of optimizing with `font-display`:\n\nIntegrating and optimizing fonts properly improves the user experience by speeding up page loads and preventing delays in the display of text.",
    codeExample: "<head>\n  <link\n    href=\"https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Roboto:wght@400;700&display=swap\"\n    rel=\"stylesheet\"\n  />\n</head>\n\n@import url(\"https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Roboto:wght@400;700&display=swap\");\n\n@font-face {\n  font-family: \"Roboto\";\n  src: url(\"roboto.woff2\") format(\"woff2\");\n  font-display: swap;\n}",
  },
  56: {
    source: "Что такое webmanifest и как он используется для создания Progressive Web Apps (PWA)?",
    question: "What is a webmanifest and how is it used to create Progressive Web Apps (PWAs)?",
    shortAnswer: "A webmanifest is a file in JSON format used to define a web application's metadata. It lets you configure the application's icons, name, theme and behaviour when it is added to a device's home screen. In a PWA the webmanifest helps make the web application resemble a native mobile app, improving the user experience.",
    longAnswer: "A webmanifest is a file used in Progressive Web Apps (PWAs) to describe an application's metadata. This file tells the browser and the device important information such as the application's name, its icons, its colour scheme and its display parameters when the application is installed on the user's device.\nThe manifest file is in JSON format and contains key fields such as:\n\n- `name`: the application's full name.\n\n- `short_name`: a short name, used on installation.\n\n- `icons`: a list of icons in different resolutions for display on the screen.\n\n- `start_url`: the URL the application starts from when it is opened.\n\n- `display`: determines how the application will look (`standalone`, for example, to launch in full screen without the browser interface).\n\n- `background_color` and `theme_color`: the background and theme colours used when the application is installed.\n\nAn example of a simple manifest:\n\nThis manifest lets users add the web application to their device's home screen, where it will behave like a native application, launching in its own window and with its own icons.\nIn the context of a PWA, the webmanifest is an important component that makes a web application available offline and lets users interact with it as though it were a native mobile app.",
    codeExample: "{  \n  \"name\": \"My Awesome App\",  \n  \"short_name\": \"AwesomeApp\",  \n  \"start_url\": \"/index.html\",  \n  \"display\": \"standalone\",  \n  \"background_color\": \"#ffffff\",  \n  \"theme_color\": \"#000000\",  \n  \"icons\": [    \n    {      \n      \"src\": \"/images/icon-192x192.png\",      \n      \"sizes\": \"192x192\",      \n      \"type\": \"image/png\"    \n    }  \n  ] \n}",
  },
  57: {
    source: "Как реализовать поддержку темной темы (dark mode) с помощью HTML и CSS? Какие метатеги и медиавыражения используются?",
    question: "How do you implement support for a dark theme (dark mode) with HTML and CSS? Which meta tags and media queries are used?",
    shortAnswer: "A dark theme can be implemented with the CSS media query `prefers-color-scheme`, which detects the user's preference as to the colour scheme (light or dark). The appropriate styles are applied depending on the browser's preference. You can also set up manual theme switching with JavaScript and CSS classes.",
    longAnswer: "Support for a dark theme in web applications lets users switch between the light and dark themes automatically based on their system settings. This is achieved with the CSS media query `prefers-color-scheme`. This media query checks which colour scheme the user's system prefers (light or dark) and applies the appropriate styles.\nAn example of using the media query for a dark theme:\n\nThis lets the dark theme be applied automatically when the user's device is set to a dark colour scheme.\nFor manual switching between themes with a button you can use JavaScript. For example, you can keep the theme's state in `localStorage` and change classes in the DOM dynamically.\nAn example with JavaScript for switching the theme by hand:\n\nWith this code you can save the user's choice and apply it on their next visit to the page.\nYou can also use meta tags such as `theme-color` to change the browser's colour scheme:\n\nThese meta tags configure the colour scheme of the browser's interface according to the theme. Support for a dark theme makes web applications more adaptive and improves the user experience.",
    codeExample: "/* The default style (the light theme) */ \nbody {  \n  background-color: white;  \n  color: black; \n} \n/* The dark theme */ \n@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {  \n  body {    \n    background-color: black;    \n    color: white;  \n  } \n}\n\nconst toggleTheme = () => {  \n  const theme = document.body.classList.toggle('dark') ? 'dark' : 'light';    \n  localStorage.setItem('theme', theme); \n}; \n  \ndocument.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => {  \n  const savedTheme = localStorage.getItem('theme');  \n  if (savedTheme) {    \n    document.body.classList.add(savedTheme);  \n  } \n});\n\n<meta name=\"theme-color\" content=\"#000000\" media=\"(prefers-color-scheme: dark)\"> \n<meta name=\"theme-color\" content=\"#ffffff\" media=\"(prefers-color-scheme: light)\">",
  },
  58: {
    source: "Какие способы оптимизации изображений на веб-странице вы знаете?",
    question: "What ways of optimizing images on a web page do you know?",
    shortAnswer: "Optimizing images involves several methods: compressing images (using the WebP, JPEG or PNG formats), loading images only as they are needed (lazy loading), choosing responsive images of different sizes for different devices, using a CDN for fast delivery, and adding the `alt` attribute to improve accessibility.",
    longAnswer: "Optimizing images is an important aspect of increasing a site's performance. Several key methods include:\nCompressing images: using image compression tools (TinyPNG, for example) reduces their size without any noticeable loss of quality. For web pages it is recommended to use modern image formats such as WebP, which provide better compression than traditional formats such as JPEG or PNG.\nResponsive images: you can provide images of different sizes for different screens using the `srcset` attribute:\n\nThis lets images matching the device's resolution be loaded, saving data.\nLazy loading: images are loaded only when they come into the user's view. This can be done with the `loading=\"lazy\"` attribute:\n\nUsing a CDN: keeping images on a Content Delivery Network (CDN) lets them be delivered faster, since the requests will be handled by the nearest servers.\nOptimizing through CSS and SVG: sometimes raster images can be replaced with vector ones (SVG), especially for icons and simple graphics.\nThese methods help reduce a page's loading time considerably and improve SEO.",
    codeExample: "<img src=\"small.jpg\" srcset=\"large.jpg 1024w, medium.jpg 640w, small.jpg 320w\" alt=\"Example Image\">\n\n<img src=\"image.jpg\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Example Image\">",
  },
  59: {
    source: "Как вы понимаете Web Accessibility (веб-доступность) и почему она важна?",
    question: "How do you understand web accessibility, and why does it matter?",
    shortAnswer: "Web accessibility means that websites and applications should be usable by people with various impairments, such as impairments of vision, hearing or mobility. It matters because it makes the internet available to every user, including those who use special devices such as screen readers.",
    longAnswer: "Web accessibility is the concept of building websites and applications that can be used by people with various impairments, including impairments of vision, hearing, mobility and cognitive function. The principles of web accessibility involve adapting interfaces to work with assistive technologies such as screen readers, and ensuring that every element of a web page is reachable by keyboard.\nWhy does this matter?\n\n- Inclusivity: around 15 % of the world's population has some form of disability. Making websites accessible makes the internet more inclusive for every user.\n\n- Legislation: many countries have laws and standards (the WCAG, for example) requiring the principles of web accessibility to be observed. Failing to meet those standards can have legal consequences.\n\n- Better performance: building an accessible site often improves overall performance and the user experience, since such sites tend to be better structured and more convenient to use.\n\nThe main accessibility practices:\n\n- Alternative text for images: adding `alt` text to images so that screen readers can describe them to people with visual impairments.\n\n- Focusable elements: make sure every interactive element is reachable by keyboard (buttons, links, for example).\n\n- Colour contrast: using sufficient contrast between text and background to improve readability.\n\nAn example of a simple accessibility improvement:\n\nAccessibility on the internet makes it convenient for every user, including people with impairments, and helps you create better, more inclusive products.",
    codeExample: "<img src=\"image.jpg\" alt=\"A description of the image\">",
  },
  60: {
    source: "Какие ключевые метрики используются для оценки производительности веб-сайта?",
    question: "Which key metrics are used to assess a website's performance?",
    shortAnswer: "The key metrics for assessing a website's performance include First Contentful Paint (FCP), which measures when content is first displayed; Largest Contentful Paint (LCP), which measures when the page's main content becomes visible; First Input Delay (FID), which shows how quickly the site responds to the user's first interaction; and Cumulative Layout Shift (CLS), which measures the stability of the page's layout. These metrics help you assess how quickly and how stably a website loads.",
    longAnswer: "To assess a website's performance, developers use several key metrics that help measure how quickly and efficiently a page loads and responds to the user's actions. Here are the main metrics that are applied most often:\n\n- First Contentful Paint (FCP): FCP measures the time at which the browser starts rendering the first element on the page (text, an image, a block). This matters because it shows when the user sees the first visual changes on the page. The faster the FCP, the better the user perceives the site's loading.\n\n- Largest Contentful Paint (LCP): LCP measures the time it takes for the page's main content to become visible (a large image or block of text, for example). A good LCP is an indicator that the important content loads quickly. The metric helps you understand how long the user has to wait before the main information appears.\n\n- First Input Delay (FID): FID measures the time from the user's first interaction (pressing a button, for example) to the moment the browser responds to that action. A high FID can be caused by heavy JavaScript operations blocking the interface. Improving FID makes a site more responsive.\n\n- Cumulative Layout Shift (CLS): CLS measures the stability of the page's layout as it loads. If elements move unexpectedly during loading (because fonts or images load late, for example), that creates a negative experience for the user. A good CLS shows that the page does not \"jump\" during rendering.\n\nAn example of CLS values:\n\nThese metrics form the basis for assessing the user experience and serve as reference points for improving a site's performance, which makes it faster and more convenient for users.",
    codeExample: "<style>\n  img { width: 100%; height: auto; } /* Stating the dimensions prevents jumps */ \n</style>",
  },
  61: {
    source: "Какие техники используются для критического CSS и ленивой загрузки стилей?",
    question: "Which techniques are used for critical CSS and the lazy loading of styles?",
    shortAnswer: "Critical CSS is the set of styles needed for the page's initial display, which are inlined into the HTML in order to avoid a loading delay. The remaining styles are loaded asynchronously. The lazy loading of styles involves using JavaScript to attach or load CSS conditionally, which lets styles be loaded as they are needed.",
    longAnswer: "Critical CSS consists of the styles needed to display the content that is \"above the fold\", and it is inlined directly into the HTML document in order to speed up the first paint.\nAfter that the remaining styles can be loaded asynchronously, using the `rel=\"preload\"` attribute or `media=\"print\"` followed by a change to `media=\"all\"`, so as to postpone their application until the page has loaded. This prevents the main page's painting from being blocked and, once loading is complete, lets the styles become active for every device.\nThe lazy loading of styles lets you attach CSS files as they are needed: with JavaScript, for example, to load styles when the user scrolls to a particular section, or using the `media` attribute to attach styles when a given condition is met.\nYou can also apply bundlers and tools such as `Webpack` and `critical`, which extract the critical styles automatically and simplify the optimization process.\nAn example of using lazy loading:\n\nIn this example the styles load asynchronously, since `media=\"print\"` initially prevents them from being applied to the screen display. Once loading is complete the attribute is changed to `media=\"all\"` and the styles start applying to every kind of device.",
    codeExample: "<link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"styles.css\" media=\"print\" onload=\"this.media='all'\">",
  },
  62: {
    source: "Как использовать PHP с функциями HTML5?",
    question: "How do you use PHP with HTML5 features?",
    shortAnswer: "PHP is used to generate HTML5 content dynamically, including creating multimedia elements, working with localStorage and handling forms. PHP can generate HTML5 elements such as forms and video or audio tags, and pass data into JavaScript for working with new HTML5 features such as local storage.",
    longAnswer: "PHP can work with HTML5 by generating dynamic content on the server side, which is then used to enhance the client side with HTML5's new capabilities. Here are several examples:\nCreating multimedia elements dynamically: PHP can generate video and audio tags for HTML5. For example:\n\nInteractivity with localStorage: PHP can pass data into JavaScript, which then uses the HTML5 API to save the data in `localStorage`. An example of PHP and JavaScript:\n\nHandling HTML5 forms: PHP handles forms with the new HTML5 fields (`email`, `tel`, for example), performing server-side validation and providing the results. For example:\n\nPHP works conveniently with HTML5's new capabilities, providing dynamic content generation and the handling of forms with additional features.",
    codeExample: "echo \"<video controls>\n         <source src='video.mp4' type='video/mp4'>\n         Your browser does not support the video tag.\n       </video>\";\n\necho \"<script>\n        localStorage.setItem('user', 'JohnDoe');\n        alert(localStorage.getItem('user'));\n      </script>\";\n\nif ($_SERVER['REQUEST_METHOD'] === 'POST') {\n      // Checking the form's data\n      if (filter_var($_POST['email'], FILTER_VALIDATE_EMAIL)) {\n          echo \"Email is valid!\";\n      } else {\n          echo \"Invalid email!\";\n      }\n}",
  },
  63: {
    source: "Какой механизм используют для встраивания одной веб страницы в другую?",
    question: "Which mechanism is used to embed one web page in another?",
    shortAnswer: "To embed web pages an iframe (inline frame) is used. This is an HTML element that creates a separate window inside a page for displaying another document. You can also use the components of frontend frameworks or server-side includes (SSI), but an iframe is the simplest and most universal way.",
    longAnswer: "An iframe (Inline Frame) is an HTML element that lets you embed one HTML page in another. It creates an independent viewport inside the parent page.\nThe iframe's main attributes: src (the address of the page being embedded), width and height (the dimensions), frameborder (the borders).\nAn iframe example:\n\nThe advantages of an iframe: complete isolation of styles and scripts, simplicity of use, support for cross-domain resources.\nThe disadvantages: problems with SEO, possible security problems, difficulty managing the content's dimensions.\nThe alternatives: React/Vue/Angular components for reusing UI, loading content with AJAX, Server Side Includes (SSI) for static sites.",
    codeExample: "<iframe src=\"https://example.com\" width=\"600\" height=\"400\"></iframe>",
  },
  64: {
    source: "Как сделать так, чтобы картинка подгружалась с сервера только тогда, когда пользователь до нее долистал?",
    question: "How do you make an image load from the server only once the user has scrolled down to it?",
    shortAnswer: "Lazy loading is used. To do this you add the attribute `loading=\"lazy\"` to the `<img>`, or use the Intersection Observer in JavaScript.",
    longAnswer: "The HTML attribute `loading=\"lazy\"` (supported in modern browsers):\n\nThe browser will load the image itself once it comes into the visible area.\nThe Intersection Observer API (if you need more control):\n\nIn the HTML markup:",
    codeExample: "<img src=\"image.jpg\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"An example\">\n\nconst img = document.querySelector('img.lazy');\nconst observer = new IntersectionObserver((entries) => {\n  entries.forEach(entry => {\n    if (entry.isIntersecting) {\n      entry.target.src = entry.target.dataset.src;\n      observer.unobserve(entry.target);\n    }\n  });\n});\nobserver.observe(img);\n\n<img data-src=\"real-image.jpg\" class=\"lazy\" alt=\"Lazy loading\">",
  },
  65: {
    source: "Расскажи все про оптимизацию загрузки сайтов",
    question: "Tell us everything about optimizing site loading",
    shortAnswer: "Optimizing site loading means the ways of making pages faster. The main methods are: compressing images, minifying CSS/JS, caching, lazy loading and using a CDN. The faster a site is, the better the user experience and the higher its position in search engines.",
    longAnswer: "Optimizing site loading involves many techniques aimed at reducing response time and improving performance.\n\n- Compressing images\nLarge images slow loading down. Use the WebP or AVIF formats, which provide high quality at a smaller size. Tools such as Squoosh or ImageOptim help you compress pictures without loss.\n\n- Minifying CSS and JavaScript\nRemoving whitespace and comments and shortening the code reduces the size of the files. Webpack, Vite or online tools such as UglifyJS are suitable for this.\n\n- Caching\nBrowsers can store static files (CSS, JS, images) so as not to download them again. Configure the HTTP headers `Cache-Control` and `Expires` on the server.\n\n- Lazy loading\nImages and other resources are loaded only when they come into the visible area of the screen. In HTML this is done through the `loading=\"lazy\"` attribute on `<img>`.\n\n- Using a CDN\nA Content Delivery Network (CDN) is a network of servers that deliver content to the user from the node nearest them. This speeds loading up, especially for a global audience.\n\n- Optimizing fonts\nAttach only the font weights you need and use `font-display: swap`, so that text is displayed immediately even if the font has not loaded yet.\n\nAn example of code for lazy loading:\n\nA script can replace `src` with `data-src` when the image appears in the viewport.",
    codeExample: "<img src=\"placeholder.jpg\" data-src=\"real-image.jpg\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"An example\">",
  },
  66: {
    source: "Как интегрировать карты в веб-приложение?",
    question: "How do you integrate maps into a web application?",
    shortAnswer: "To integrate maps, use the JavaScript API of a mapping service (Google Maps, Yandex Maps, Leaflet). Initialize the map in a component, add markers and event handlers.",
    longAnswer: "Popular solutions:\n\n- The Google Maps JavaScript API\n\n- The Yandex Maps API\n\n- Leaflet (open-source)\n\n- Mapbox GL JS\n\nBasic integration:\n\nIntegration with React:\nUse wrapper libraries:\n\n- `@react-google-maps/api`\n\n- `react-yandex-maps`\n\n- `react-leaflet`\n\nKey features:\n\n- Adding markers\n\n- Drawing shapes and zones\n\n- Geocoding addresses\n\n- Routing and directions\n\n- Custom map styles\n\nOptimization:\n\n- Lazy loading of maps\n\n- Clustering markers\n\n- Optimizing performance\n\n- Caching requests",
    codeExample: "<div id=\"map\" style=\"width: 100%; height: 400px;\"></div>\n\n<script>\n  const map = new google.maps.Map(document.getElementById('map'), {\n    center: { lat: 55.76, lng: 37.64 },\n    zoom: 10\n  });\n  \n  new google.maps.Marker({\n    position: { lat: 55.76, lng: 37.64 },\n    map: map,\n    title: 'Hello World!'\n  });\n</script>",
  },
  67: {
    source: "Какие действия триггерят reflow, repaint. И как можно избежать частого reflow?",
    question: "Which actions trigger a reflow and a repaint? And how can you avoid frequent reflows?",
    shortAnswer: "A reflow happens when a page's structure or the dimensions of elements change and the browser has to recalculate the layout. A repaint is triggered when the appearance changes (a colour, for example) while the dimensions stay the same. Frequent reflows slow a page down, so people try to minimize them — by grouping DOM changes together or using `classList` rather than changing styles one at a time, for example.",
    longAnswer: "What a reflow and a repaint are\n\n- A reflow is the recalculation of elements' geometry: dimensions, positions, structure. For example, when a block's width changes or a new element is added.\n\n- A repaint is the redrawing of the appearance without recalculating positions. For example, changing a background or a text colour.\n\nActions that cause a reflow\n\n- Changing an element's dimensions (`width`, `height`, `margin`, `padding`).\n\n- Adding or removing nodes in the DOM.\n\n- Using methods such as `offsetWidth` or `getComputedStyle` (they force the browser to compute the current state).\n\n- Changing fonts or the text content.\n\nHow to avoid frequent reflows\n\n- Group several changes together:\n\n- Use `classList.add/remove` rather than changing each style separately.\n\n- Work with an element outside the DOM (through a `documentFragment`) and then insert it whole.\n\n- Use `requestAnimationFrame` for animations.\n\nConclusion\nA reflow is an expensive operation, and it is worth minimizing when working with dynamic content. The less the browser has to recalculate the layout of elements, the faster the page works.",
    codeExample: "element.style.cssText = \"width: 100px; height: 100px; color: red;\";",
  },
  68: {
    source: "Что происходит в браузере после получения HTML-страницы от сервера?",
    question: "What happens in the browser after it receives the HTML page from the server?",
    shortAnswer: "After receiving the HTML the browser starts parsing it and building the `DOM`. As it parses, it finds resources (CSS, JS, images, fonts) and starts loading them. Once the CSS has loaded and been parsed, the `CSSOM` is built, and then the render tree is assembled from `DOM + CSSOM`. Next the browser calculates the dimensions and positions of the elements (layout) and draws them (paint), and then it may \"glue\" the layers together (composite). Scripts and styles can block some of these steps, which is why the first paint is postponed.",
    longAnswer: "After the browser has received the HTML it goes through several stages, and some of them run in parallel.\n1) Parsing the HTML and building the DOM\nThe browser reads the HTML as a stream and builds a tree of nodes.\n\n- What it does: tokenization → parsing → creating the `DOM`.\n\n- A side effect: as it reads the HTML the browser \"finds\" external resources:\n\n- `<link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"...\">`\n\n- `<script src=\"...\">`\n\n- `<img src=\"...\">`\n\n- fonts from `@font-face` and so on.\n\n2) Loading resources in parallel\nWhile the `DOM` is being built, the browser tries to fetch in advance whatever will be needed for the render.\n\n- The preload scanner: the browser can scan the HTML in advance and start downloads even before the DOM has been built fully.\n\n- Priority: CSS and critical resources normally get a higher priority than, say, \"decorative\" pictures.\n\n3) Building the CSSOM and applying the styles\nOnce the CSS has loaded, the browser parses it into a structure of rules.\n\n- Definition: the CSSOM is the CSS object model, a representation of the styles as a tree or set of structures convenient for computing the final styles.\n\n- Until the `CSSOM` is ready, the browser normally does not perform a full first paint (so as not to show the page without styles).\n\n4) Assembling the render tree\nNext the browser combines the information from the `DOM` and the `CSSOM`.\n\n- Definition: the render tree is the tree of elements that actually take part in the visual display (something with `display: none`, for example, does not get in).\n\n- At this step the \"final\" computed styles are worked out for the displayed nodes.\n\n5) Layout (calculating the geometry)\nThe browser determines where the elements will be and what size they will have.\n\n- computing dimensions, coordinates and line breaks\n\n- taking fonts, container sizes, flex/grid and so on into account\n\n6) Paint and composite\nThe browser draws the pixels and, where necessary, glues the layers together.\n\n- Paint: drawing text, backgrounds, borders, shadows and so on.\n\n- Composite: assembling the layers (with `transform` or `opacity`, for example) into the final picture on the screen.\n\nA small example: why this matters to a frontend developer\nIf you put a heavy `<script>` in the `<head>` without `defer`/`async`, it can stop the HTML being parsed and delay the appearance of the content:\n\nConclusion: after receiving the HTML the browser builds the `DOM`, loads and parses the CSS into the `CSSOM`, assembles the `render tree`, performs `layout` and then `paint`/`composite`. Understanding these stages helps you explain \"why the page is slow to show its content\".",
    codeExample: "<head>\n  <script src=\"/heavy.js\"></script>\n</head>",
  },
  69: {
    source: "Что такое критический путь рендеринга браузера?",
    question: "What is the browser's critical rendering path?",
    shortAnswer: "The critical rendering path is the sequence of steps and resources the browser needs in order to show a page to the user. It normally involves building the `DOM` and the `CSSOM`, creating the `render tree`, `layout` and `paint`. If there are blocking resources on the path (often CSS and synchronous JS), the browser postpones the first paint. Optimizing the critical path means reducing the volume and number of blocking resources and moving non-critical downloads later.",
    longAnswer: "The critical rendering path describes the \"minimum necessary chain\" without which the browser cannot paint the first screen.\nDefinition\nDefinition: the critical rendering path is the set of operations and dependencies (resources plus steps) that have to complete before the browser can perform its first paint.\nWhat the path usually consists of\nBefore the first paint the browser normally needs to:\n\n- Build the `DOM` from the HTML.\n\n- Load and parse the CSS → build the `CSSOM`.\n\n- Assemble the `render tree` from `DOM + CSSOM`.\n\n- Perform `layout` (the geometry).\n\n- Perform `paint` (the drawing), and sometimes `composite`.\n\nWhat most often \"blocks\" the critical path\n\n- CSS as render-blocking\n\n- Until the styles are ready, the browser is reluctant to draw the page \"bare\".\n\n- Synchronous JS in the wrong place\n\n- A `<script>` without `defer`/`async` can stop the HTML being parsed.\n\n- A script may request styles or dimensions (`getComputedStyle`, `offsetHeight`) and force the browser to \"catch up\" on its calculations synchronously.\n\nHow people normally optimize it (the idea, with no \"magic\")\n\n- They reduce and split the CSS:\n\n- the critical styles for the first screen come sooner,\n\n- the rest comes later.\n\n- They make scripts non-blocking:\n\n- `defer` for most scripts,\n\n- `async` for independent ones.\n\n- They postpone non-critical resources:\n\n- images below the first screen (`loading=\"lazy\"`),\n\n- analytics and widgets after the first render.\n\nA small example: `defer` as a typical optimization\n\n`defer` lets the browser carry on building the `DOM`, and the script runs later, once the HTML has been parsed.\nConclusion: the critical rendering path is what affects how quickly the first useful content appears. Optimizing it almost always comes down to reducing blocking CSS/JS and moving secondary things to \"after the first screen\".",
    codeExample: "<script src=\"/app.js\" defer></script>",
  },
  70: {
    source: "В каком порядке браузер строит DOM и CSSOM?",
    question: "In what order does the browser build the DOM and the CSSOM?",
    shortAnswer: "The `DOM` is built as the HTML is parsed — as a stream, from the top down. The `CSSOM` is built after the CSS files the browser finds in the HTML have been loaded and parsed. These processes can run in parallel: the DOM carries on being built while the CSS is downloading. To create the `render tree` and perform a correct first paint, however, the `CSSOM` normally has to be ready, which is why styles are considered render-blocking.",
    longAnswer: "It is important to separate \"building\" from \"being able to paint\".\n1) The DOM: as a stream, while reading the HTML\nThe browser reads the HTML and adds nodes to the `DOM` immediately.\n\n- the order: from the start of the document to the end\n\n- if a `<script>` without `defer`/`async` is met, the parsing of the DOM may stop until the script has run\n\n2) The CSSOM: after the CSS has loaded\nWhen the browser meets CSS, it starts downloading it and then parses it into the `CSSOM`.\n\n- Definition: the CSSOM is a structure containing the CSS rules and making it possible to compute the final styles.\n\n- CSS can be downloaded in parallel with the building of the DOM, but the CSSOM will appear only once the CSS has been received and parsed.\n\n3) The key dependency: the render tree requires both the DOM and the CSSOM\nEven if the `DOM` is already partly ready, the browser normally needs the `CSSOM` too for the visual tree.\n\n- The `render tree` is built from:\n\n- the DOM nodes that are displayed,\n\n- the computed styles based on the CSSOM\n\n4) What this means in practice\n\n- The `DOM` may be \"almost ready\" while painting is delayed because of the CSS.\n\n- Improperly attached styles (too many, too heavy, at the start) can shift `FCP`/`LCP`.\n\n- A script may depend on the CSS (measuring dimensions, for example), and then the browser will have to wait for the styles synchronously.\n\nA small example of a dependency on styles\n\nConclusion: the browser builds the `DOM` while parsing the HTML, and the `CSSOM` after the CSS has been loaded and parsed. For the visual assembly to be correct (render tree → layout → paint) both are normally needed, which is why CSS often affects the speed of the first render.",
    codeExample: "// requesting dimensions can force the browser to finish its style/layout calculations\nconst h = document.querySelector('.card').offsetHeight;",
  },
  71: {
    source: "Как происходит парсинг HTML и построение DOM-дерева?",
    question: "How does the parsing of HTML and the building of the DOM tree happen?",
    shortAnswer: "The browser reads the HTML document sequentially, character by character. It breaks the text into tokens and creates DOM nodes from them. Every HTML tag becomes a node of the tree. The result is a hierarchical structure reflecting the nesting of the page's elements.",
    longAnswer: "Parsing HTML is a fundamental process without which the browser cannot display a page.\nDefinition\nThe DOM (Document Object Model) is an object representation of an HTML document as a tree, which JavaScript can work with.\nThe stages of parsing HTML\n\n- Tokenization\nThe HTML code is broken down into tokens:\n\n- opening tags\n\n- closing tags\n\n- text nodes\n\n- Creating the nodes\nA corresponding DOM node is created for every token.\n\n- Forming the hierarchy\nThe nodes are added to the tree taking the nesting of the tags into account.\n\n- Error handling\nThe browser can correct invalid HTML, closing or rearranging tags automatically.\n\nAn example\nHTML:\n\nThe result:\n\n- `div` — the parent node\n\n- `p` — a child node\n\n- `Text` — a text node\n\nInteraction with JavaScript\nThe DOM tree is available through `document`, which lets you:\n\n- read elements\n\n- change the page's structure\n\n- add and remove nodes\n\nConclusion\nParsing HTML and building the DOM are the foundation of how a browser works. Understanding this process helps you work with the DOM deliberately and avoid inefficient operations.",
    codeExample: "<div>\n  <p>Text</p>\n</div>",
  },
  72: {
    source: "Какие атрибуты загрузки скриптов существуют в HTML?",
    question: "What script-loading attributes exist in HTML?",
    shortAnswer: "HTML provides the `async` and `defer` attributes for controlling the loading of scripts. By default scripts are loaded and executed synchronously, blocking the parsing of the HTML. The `async` attribute lets a script be loaded asynchronously and executed as soon as it has loaded. The `defer` attribute postpones a script's execution until the HTML has finished being parsed.",
    longAnswer: "The way scripts are attached directly affects a page's loading speed and whether it works correctly.\nDefinition\nScript-loading attributes are parameters of the `<script>` tag that determine when and how JavaScript code is loaded and executed.\nThe main loading options\n\n- Synchronous loading (the default)\n\n- The parsing of the HTML stops\n\n- The script is loaded and executed immediately\n\n- async\n\n- The script is loaded in parallel with the HTML\n\n- It is executed as soon as it has loaded\n\n- The order of execution between several `async` scripts is not guaranteed\n\n- defer\n\n- The script is loaded in parallel\n\n- It is executed after the HTML has been parsed fully\n\n- The order of execution is preserved\n\nAn example\n\n`analytics.js` will run as soon as it has loaded, while `app.js` will run after the DOM has been built.\nConclusion\nChoosing the right script-loading attributes helps speed a page's loading up and avoid errors related to accessing the DOM.",
    codeExample: "<script src=\"analytics.js\" async></script>\n<script src=\"app.js\" defer></script>",
  },
  73: {
    source: "Чем отличаются async и defer при подключении скриптов?",
    question: "How do async and defer differ when attaching scripts?",
    shortAnswer: "`async` and `defer` both let scripts be loaded without blocking the HTML, but they behave differently. `async` executes a script as soon as it has loaded, regardless of the DOM's state. `defer` executes a script only after the HTML has finished being parsed. `defer` also preserves the order in which scripts run, whereas `async` does not.",
    longAnswer: "Both attributes are used to optimize the loading of JavaScript, but they suit different tasks.\nThe main difference\n`async` is oriented towards independent scripts, while `defer` is for scripts that work with the DOM.\nThe behaviour of async\n\n- The script is loaded in parallel with the HTML\n\n- It is executed as soon as it has loaded\n\n- It may run before the DOM has finished being built\n\n- It does not guarantee the order of execution\n\nThe behaviour of defer\n\n- The script is loaded in parallel with the HTML\n\n- It is executed after the HTML has finished being parsed\n\n- The DOM is fully available\n\n- The order of execution is preserved\n\nAn example of use\n\n`vendor.js` will run before `app.js`, even if it loads later.\nPractical recommendations\n\n- `async` — analytics, trackers, independent scripts\n\n- `defer` — the application's main code, work with the DOM\n\nConclusion\nThe choice between `async` and `defer` depends on whether you need access to the DOM and whether the order in which the scripts run matters.",
    codeExample: "<script src=\"vendor.js\" defer></script>\n<script src=\"app.js\" defer></script>",
  },
  74: {
    source: "Как влияет синхронная загрузка скриптов на рендеринг страницы?",
    question: "How does the synchronous loading of scripts affect a page's rendering?",
    shortAnswer: "Synchronous scripts block the parsing of the HTML until they have been loaded and executed. Until the script has run, the browser cannot carry on building the DOM. This slows the page's first render down and can delay the display of the content. The more such scripts there are, the worse the perceived performance.",
    longAnswer: "By default every script in HTML is loaded synchronously unless special attributes are given.\nWhat synchronous loading means\nSynchronous loading means that the browser:\n\n- stops parsing the HTML\n\n- loads the JavaScript file\n\n- executes the code\n\n- and only then carries on processing the HTML\n\nWhy this blocks rendering\n\n- The parsing of the DOM stops\nUntil the script has run, the DOM tree cannot be built fully.\n\n- The render tree is blocked\nWithout the DOM and the CSSOM the browser cannot form the render tree.\n\n- The first display is delayed\nThe user sees a blank page, or partly loaded content, for longer.\n\nAn example\n\nThe text inside the `<p>` will not be processed until `heavy.js` has loaded and run.\nPractical recommendations\n\n- Use `defer` for the main code\n\n- Move critical scripts to the end of the HTML\n\n- Minify and combine JavaScript files\n\nConclusion\nSynchronous scripts have a strong effect on rendering speed and should be used only where they are genuinely necessary.",
    codeExample: "<script src=\"heavy.js\"></script>\n<p>The page's content</p>",
  },
  75: {
    source: "В какой момент браузер получает HTML и как формируются DOM и CSSOM?",
    question: "At what moment does the browser receive the HTML, and how are the DOM and CSSOM formed?",
    shortAnswer: "The browser receives the HTML after performing an HTTP request to the server. As soon as it has received it, it starts parsing the document. The DOM tree is formed during parsing. In parallel the browser loads the CSS and builds the CSSOM. These structures are used for the page's subsequent rendering.",
    longAnswer: "Once the network connection has been established, the browser starts receiving the data from which the page's visual representation is then formed.\nReceiving the HTML\nThe HTML arrives in response to an HTTP request. The browser does not wait for the whole document to load but starts processing it as the data arrives.\nForming the DOM\nThe DOM is created while the HTML is being parsed:\n\n- The HTML is taken apart token by token.\n\n- A DOM node is created for every element.\n\n- The nodes are linked into a tree structure.\n\nThe DOM represents the document's logical structure and is used by JavaScript to work with the page's elements.\nForming the CSSOM\nThe CSSOM is created separately from the CSS files and inline styles:\n\n- The browser loads all the CSS resources.\n\n- Every CSS file is parsed into an object model.\n\n- The CSSOM describes which styles apply to which elements.\n\nThe connection between the DOM and the CSSOM\nThe DOM and the CSSOM are combined to build the render tree, which is used when the page is painted. Without the CSSOM the browser cannot display elements correctly, which is why CSS blocks rendering.\nConclusion\nHTML is used to build the DOM and CSS to create the CSSOM. These structures are formed at an early stage of a page's loading and are the foundation for the subsequent rendering and for the work of JavaScript.",
    codeExample: null,
  },
  76: {
    source: "Чем DOM отличается от Shadow DOM?",
    question: "How does the DOM differ from the Shadow DOM?",
    shortAnswer: "The DOM is the overall tree of a page's elements, available to JavaScript and CSS. The Shadow DOM is an isolated subtree attached to a particular element. Styles and markup inside the Shadow DOM do not affect the rest of the page. This lets you create reusable, protected components. The Shadow DOM is used actively in Web Components.",
    longAnswer: "In the browser there are several levels of working with markup, and the Shadow DOM was introduced to solve the problems caused by the global nature of the DOM and CSS.\nThe ordinary DOM\nThe DOM is a single structure for the document:\n\n- every element is in one tree\n\n- CSS is global by default\n\n- JavaScript can reach any node\n\nThis is convenient, but in large applications it leads to style conflicts and unpredictable behaviour.\nThe Shadow DOM\nThe Shadow DOM creates an isolated subtree inside an element:\n\n- styles inside do not leak out\n\n- external styles do not affect the content of the Shadow DOM\n\n- the structure is hidden from a direct traversal of the DOM\n\nAn example of creating a Shadow DOM:\n\nThe main differences\nThe key differences can be put like this:\n\n- the DOM is global, the Shadow DOM is isolated\n\n- CSS in the DOM affects the whole page, in the Shadow DOM only the component\n\n- the Shadow DOM is intended for a component-based approach\n\nConclusion\nThe Shadow DOM solves the problem of isolating markup and styles. It is especially useful when creating reusable UI components and lies at the heart of Web Components.",
    codeExample: "const host = document.querySelector('#app')\nconst shadowRoot = host.attachShadow({ mode: 'open' })\n\nshadowRoot.innerHTML = `<p>Shadow content</p>`",
  },
  77: {
    source: "Что входит в базовую структуру HTML-документа?",
    question: "What goes into the basic structure of an HTML document?",
    shortAnswer: "The basic structure of an HTML document includes `<!DOCTYPE>`, the root element `<html>`, and the `<head>` and `<body>` sections.\n`<!DOCTYPE>` tells the browser that a modern HTML standard is being used.\nMetadata goes in `<head>`, and the page's content in `<body>`.\nWithout this structure the document may display incorrectly.",
    longAnswer: "An HTML document has a strict minimum structure that the browser needs in order to parse and render the page correctly.\nDefinition\nThe basic structure of an HTML document is the minimum set of elements that defines the document's type, its metadata and the content that is displayed.\nThe main structural elements\nBefore displaying the page the browser processes the following parts in sequence:\n\n- `<!DOCTYPE html>`\n\n- States that the document conforms to the HTML5 standard\n\n- Switches on standards mode for rendering\n\n- `<html>`\n\n- The root element of the whole document\n\n- Normally carries a `lang` attribute, `lang=\"ru\"` for example\n\n- `<head>`\n\n- Contains the housekeeping information\n\n- Includes:\n\n- `<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">`\n\n- `<title>`\n\n- `<meta name=\"viewport\">`\n\n- links to styles and other resources\n\n- `<body>`\n\n- Contains all the page's visual and interactive content\n\n- This is precisely the content the user sees\n\nAn example of the minimum structure\n\nConclusion\nThe basic HTML structure is the foundation of every web page. Without it the browser will not be able to interpret the document correctly, which directly affects display, accessibility and SEO.",
    codeExample: "<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"ru\">\n  <head>\n    <meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n    <title>The page's title</title>\n  </head>\n  <body>\n    <h1>The page's content</h1>\n  </body>\n</html>",
  },
  78: {
    source: "Что такое семантическая верстка и для чего она используется?",
    question: "What is semantic markup and what is it used for?",
    shortAnswer: "Semantic markup is the use of HTML tags according to their meaning.\nIt helps browsers and search engines understand a page's structure better.\nIt also improves accessibility for screen readers.\nInstead of generic `<div>` elements, tags such as `<header>`, `<main>` and `<article>` are used.",
    longAnswer: "HTML provides tags that describe not appearance but the meaning of the content. Semantic markup is built on using exactly such elements.\nDefinition\nSemantic markup is an approach to HTML markup in which elements are chosen on the basis of their meaningful role rather than their visual presentation.\nExamples of semantic tags\nIn practice semantics is realized through specialized HTML elements:\n\n- `<header>` — the header of a page or section\n\n- `<nav>` — navigation\n\n- `<main>` — the page's main content\n\n- `<section>` — a logical section\n\n- `<article>` — an independent block of content\n\n- `<footer>` — the footer of a page or section\n\nWhy semantics is better\nUsing semantic tags offers several advantages:\n\n- It improves the readability of the HTML code\n\n- It helps search engines understand the page's structure\n\n- It increases accessibility for screen readers\n\n- It makes the project easier to maintain and develop\n\nAn example\n\nConclusion\nSemantic markup makes an HTML document meaningful. This improves accessibility, SEO and the quality of code maintenance, so it should be used by default.",
    codeExample: "<main>\n  <article>\n    <header>\n      <h1>The article's title</h1>\n    </header>\n    <p>The article's text</p>\n  </article>\n</main>",
  },
  79: {
    source: "Как семантическая верстка влияет на SEO?",
    question: "How does semantic markup affect SEO?",
    shortAnswer: "Semantic markup helps search engines understand the structure and meaning of content better.\nSearch crawlers use HTML tags to determine the importance of a page's blocks.\nThe right tags improve indexing and can have a positive effect on ranking.\nSemantics by itself does not guarantee good SEO, but it is an important foundation.",
    longAnswer: "Search engines analyse a page's HTML code in order to understand what content it holds and what role that content plays.\nHow search engines use semantics\nWhen indexing a page, search crawlers pay attention to:\n\n- The document's structure\n\n- The hierarchy of headings\n\n- The types of content blocks\n\n- The main and supporting content\n\nSemantic tags give the search engine additional signals about the purpose of elements.\nExamples of the effect\n\n- `<main>` helps identify the page's main content\n\n- `<article>` indicates a self-contained unit of content\n\n- `<nav>` separates navigation from the main text\n\n- `<header>` and `<footer>` help mark out the housekeeping zones\n\nWhy this matters\nIf a page is laid out only with `<div>` elements, it is harder for a search engine to understand its structure.\nSemantic markup reduces the ambiguity of interpreting the content.\nAn example\n\nConclusion\nSemantic markup does not replace SEO optimization, but it creates the right foundation. It improves how search engines understand a page and increases the quality of indexing.",
    codeExample: "<article>\n  <h1>How caching works</h1>\n  <p>The article's main text</p>\n</article>",
  },
  80: {
    source: "Что такое base URL (base tag) и для чего он используется?",
    question: "What is a base URL (the base tag) and what is it used for?",
    shortAnswer: "`<base>` sets the base URL for every relative link on a page.\nIt is stated inside `<head>`.\nOnce it has been set, the browser uses it as the foundation for paths.\nThis makes working with links and resources easier.",
    longAnswer: "HTML has a special tag that affects how the browser interprets relative paths.\nDefinition\nThe base tag is an HTML tag that sets the base URL for every relative link in a document.\nHow the base tag works\nOnce `<base>` has been given, the browser:\n\n- Takes the stated URL as the base\n\n- Applies it to every relative link\n\n- Uses it for `<a>`, `<img>`, `<link>` and `<script>`\n\nFor example:\n\nThe actual URL will be:\n`https://example.com/app/page.html`\nWhere it is applied\n`<base>` is used:\n\n- In SPAs with nested routes\n\n- In projects with relative paths\n\n- When generating HTML on the server\n\nLimitations and risks\n\n- There can be only one `<base>` in a document\n\n- It can affect every link unexpectedly\n\n- It demands careful use\n\nConclusion\n`base` is a powerful but dangerous tool. It makes working with relative paths easier but requires strict control so as not to break navigation.",
    codeExample: "<head>\n  <base href=\"https://example.com/app/\">\n</head>\n\n<a href=\"page.html\"></a>",
  },
  81: {
    source: "Зачем использовать семантические теги с точки зрения доступности и поддержки кода?",
    question: "Why use semantic tags from the point of view of accessibility and code maintenance?",
    shortAnswer: "Semantic tags make a page's structure comprehensible not only to developers but also to assistive technologies. Screen readers use them to navigate the page. Such HTML is easier to read and maintain. Semantics reduces the number of workarounds and improves the quality of the code. It is an important foundation for accessible interfaces.",
    longAnswer: "Semantic tags describe the meaning of content, not merely its appearance.\nWhat semantic tags are\nSemantic tags are HTML elements that state the role of content explicitly.\nExamples:\n\n- `header` — the introductory part of a page or section\n\n- `nav` — navigation\n\n- `main` — the main content\n\n- `section` — a logical section\n\n- `article` — a self-contained block of content\n\n- `footer` — the footer\n\nThe effect on accessibility\nFrom the point of view of accessibility, semantics is critically important.\nThe advantages:\n\n- screen readers understand the page's structure\n\n- users can move between sections quickly\n\n- the need for `aria` attributes is reduced\n\nThe effect on code maintenance\nFor developers, semantic markup:\n\n- makes HTML self-documenting\n\n- makes reading and reviewing easier\n\n- reduces the risk of errors during later work\n\nFor example:\n\nConclusion\nSemantic tags improve the accessibility of interfaces and make code clearer and more maintainable.",
    codeExample: "<nav>\n  <!-- navigation -->\n</nav>\n<main>\n  <article>\n    <!-- the content -->\n  </article>\n</main>",
  },
  82: {
    source: "Какую роль семантическая вёрстка играет для поисковых систем?",
    question: "What part does semantic markup play for search engines?",
    shortAnswer: "Semantic markup helps search engines understand a page's structure and meaning better. Search crawlers use tags to identify the main content, the navigation and the secondary blocks. This improves the quality of indexing. Semantics does not guarantee a high ranking, but it creates the right foundation for SEO. It makes the processing of a page more predictable.",
    longAnswer: "Search engines analyse HTML code in order to determine which content is the most important.\nHow search engines use semantics\nSemantic tags help search crawlers:\n\n- identify the page's main content\n\n- tell navigation apart from the content\n\n- understand the hierarchy of sections\n\nFor example:\n\n- `main` points to the main content\n\n- `article` denotes a self-contained piece\n\n- `header` and `footer` set the context\n\nThe practical benefit for SEO\nSemantic markup:\n\n- improves the correctness of indexing\n\n- reduces the chance of the page being interpreted wrongly\n\n- makes working with microdata easier\n\nImportant:\n\n- semantics complements SEO but does not replace content and links\n\n- an incorrect structure can make the page harder to understand\n\nThe connection with accessibility\nSearch crawlers and screen readers analyse structure in similar ways, so improving accessibility often has a positive effect on SEO too.\nConclusion\nSemantic markup helps search engines interpret a page's structure and meaning correctly, improving the quality of indexing.",
    codeExample: null,
  },
  83: {
    source: "Какие браузеры необходимо поддерживать в современных frontend-проектах и почему?",
    question: "Which browsers do you need to support in modern frontend projects, and why?",
    shortAnswer: "Modern projects normally support the latest versions of Chrome, Edge, Firefox and Safari. These browsers cover the majority of users. Supporting older browsers increases the complexity and cost of development. The decision about support always depends on business requirements. The wider the support, the more restrictions there are in the code.",
    longAnswer: "The choice of which browsers to support directly affects a frontend application's architecture and the technologies it uses.\nThe basic set of browsers\nMost commercial projects support:\n\n- Chrome\n\n- Edge (Chromium)\n\n- Firefox\n\n- Safari\n\nThis is because of their high market share and their regular updates.\nWhy older browsers are not supported\nSupporting obsolete browsers:\n\n- restricts the use of modern APIs\n\n- requires polyfills and workarounds\n\n- complicates testing and maintenance\n\nSuch browsers are therefore supported only when the client's requirements are strict.\nHow the decision is made\nThe following are normally taken into account:\n\n- user analytics\n\n- the client's requirements\n\n- corporate standards\n\n- the cost of support\n\nTools for compatibility\nFor cross-browser work the following are applied:\n\n- transpilation through the bundler\n\n- polyfills for missing APIs\n\n- autoprefixing for CSS\n\nA short conclusion\nBrowser support is a compromise between reaching users and the complexity of development.",
    codeExample: null,
  },
  84: {
    source: "Что изменится, если заменить div на span с точки зрения отображения и отступов?",
    question: "What changes if you replace a div with a span, in terms of display and spacing?",
    shortAnswer: "`div` is a block element, while `span` is an inline one. `div` takes up the whole available width and starts on a new line. `span` sits inside the line and does not break the flow of the text. Vertical margins on a `span` do not work the way they do on a `div`. The replacement can change a page's appearance substantially.",
    longAnswer: "The difference between `div` and `span` is directly connected with their display type and their participation in the document's flow.\nDefinition\ndiv is a block element intended for structuring layout.\nspan is an inline element used to mark out part of a text or inline content.\nBehaviour in the document's flow\nWhen a `div` is replaced with a `span`, the following changes:\n\n- the element no longer starts on a new line\n\n- its width no longer stretches across the whole container\n\n- the element becomes part of a line of text\n\nWorking with margins\nFrom the point of view of margins:\n\n- on a `div` every margin works correctly\n\n- on a `span` the vertical `margin-top` and `margin-bottom` have no visual effect on the layout\n\n- horizontal margins on a `span` apply normally\n\nA practical example\n\nIn the second case the element will behave as part of the text, even with identical styles.\nA short conclusion\nReplacing a `div` with a `span` changes the element's layout model and can break a page's spacing and structure.",
    codeExample: "<div class=\"box\">Text</div>\n<span class=\"box\">Text</span>",
  },
  85: {
    source: "Чем принципиально отличается div от span?",
    question: "What is the fundamental difference between a div and a span?",
    shortAnswer: "`div` and `span` differ in their display type. `div` is a block element, `span` an inline one. `div` is used to build a page's structure. `span` is applied to mark out text locally or inline elements. Their behaviour in a layout is fundamentally different.",
    longAnswer: "Despite their outward simplicity, the difference between `div` and `span` is fundamental to HTML markup.\nThe main difference\nThe key difference lies in the value of the `display` property:\n\n- `div` has `display: block`\n\n- `span` has `display: inline`\n\nThe consequences of this difference\nBecause of this:\n\n- `div` always starts on a new line\n\n- `div` takes up the whole available width\n\n- `span` sits inside the line\n\n- `span` fits itself to its content\n\nThe purpose of the elements\nMost often:\n\n- `div` is used for containers, sections and layout\n\n- `span` is used to style part of a text or icons\n\nThe ability to change their behaviour\nBoth elements can be changed through CSS:\n\nThe elements' semantic purpose does not change as a result, however.\nA short conclusion\n`div` and `span` differ in their display type and their role in a layout, and the choice between them should be a deliberate one.",
    codeExample: "span {\n  display: block;\n}",
  },
  86: {
    source: "Почему поведение CSS и JavaScript может отличаться в разных браузерах?",
    question: "Why can the behaviour of CSS and JavaScript differ between browsers?",
    shortAnswer: "Different browsers use different engines. The implementation of standards can differ. Some APIs are supported incompletely or differently. The bugs of particular browsers also play a part. That is why the behaviour of CSS and JavaScript can differ.",
    longAnswer: "Discrepancies in browser behaviour are a normal part of frontend development.\nDifferent engines\nThe main browser engines:\n\n- Blink — Chrome, Edge\n\n- Gecko — Firefox\n\n- WebKit — Safari\n\nEvery engine implements the specifications in its own way.\nThe implementation of standards\nThe reasons for the differences:\n\n- incomplete support for standards\n\n- different interpretations of the specifications\n\n- experimental APIs\n\n- obsolete implementations\n\nCSS particulars\nIn CSS the differences show up in:\n\n- flexbox and grid\n\n- spacing and dimensions\n\n- default styles\n\nJavaScript particulars\nIn JS the differences involve:\n\n- support for new APIs\n\n- the handling of events\n\n- timings and optimizations\n\nA short conclusion\nCross-browser differences arise from different engines and implementations of the standards, so they have to be taken into account and tested.",
    codeExample: null,
  },
  87: {
    source: "Какие особенности чаще всего возникают при работе с Safari?",
    question: "Which particulars come up most often when working with Safari?",
    shortAnswer: "Safari often differs in the behaviour of its CSS and JavaScript compared with Chromium browsers. It has limitations and bugs in layout, events and the handling of APIs. Some modern capabilities appear later or work differently. This requires additional testing and workarounds. Safari remains a critical browser because of the iOS ecosystem.",
    longAnswer: "Safari is based on the WebKit engine, and its behaviour has a number of characteristic particulars that are important to bear in mind during development.\nCSS particulars\nWhen working with styles the following problems often come up:\n\n- unstable behaviour of flexbox and grid in complex layouts\n\n- differences in the calculation of `height`, `min-height` and `vh`\n\n- the particulars of how `position: sticky` works\n\n- differences in the default styles of elements\n\nJavaScript particulars\nIn JavaScript the things most often encountered are:\n\n- a delay in support for new APIs\n\n- differences in the behaviour of `Date`, `Intl` and `IntersectionObserver`\n\n- problems with `requestAnimationFrame`\n\n- WebKit's limitations in its optimizations\n\nMobile Safari\nParticularly many nuances are associated with iOS:\n\n- the address bar affects `100vh`\n\n- limited access to system APIs\n\n- strict rules for media autoplay\n\n- the particulars of handling touch events\n\nDebugging tools\nDebugging Safari is harder because:\n\n- the DevTools are less capable\n\n- remote debugging of iOS requires macOS\n\n- reproducing bugs can be unreliable\n\nPractical recommendations\nIn practice people normally:\n\n- test Safari separately\n\n- avoid edge-case CSS solutions\n\n- use proven polyfills\n\n- budget time for Safari fixes\n\nA short conclusion\nSafari demands separate attention because of the particulars of WebKit and iOS, and its behaviour has to be taken into account at the interface design stage.",
    codeExample: null,
  },
  88: {
    source: "Как семантическая разметка влияет на доступность интерфейса?",
    question: "How does semantic markup affect an interface's accessibility?",
    shortAnswer: "Semantic markup helps browsers and assistive technologies understand the purpose of a page's elements. Screen readers can announce the content and navigation correctly when the tags `header`, `nav`, `main`, `section` and `article` are used. This makes the interface accessible to users with impairments. Semantics also improves the document's structure and the maintainability of the code.",
    longAnswer: "Semantic markup is the use of HTML elements according to their meaning rather than merely their visual behaviour.\n1) What semantic markup is\nDefinition: semantic HTML is the use of HTML tags that reflect the meaning and role of a page's content.\nExamples:\n\n- `header` — the page's header\n\n- `nav` — navigation\n\n- `main` — the main content\n\n- `article` — a self-contained block of content\n\n- `footer` — the footer\n\n2) How this helps accessibility\nSemantics affects:\n\n- Screen readers:\n\n- they can recognize the page's sections\n\n- they let the user move between areas quickly\n\n- Keyboard navigation:\n\n- a correct structure makes moving around easier\n\n- The accessibility API:\n\n- the browser passes the correct roles to elements\n\n3) What happens without semantics\nIf you use only `div` elements:\n\n- The screen reader does not understand the structure.\n\n- Navigation becomes harder.\n\n- More ARIA attributes are required.\n\nFor example:\n\nis better than:\n\n4) Additional advantages\n\n- Better SEO.\n\n- Easier maintenance.\n\n- More readable code.\n\nConclusion: semantic markup directly affects accessibility, because it helps the browser and assistive technologies interpret a page's structure correctly.",
    codeExample: "<nav>\n  <a href=\"/\">Home</a>\n</nav>\n\n<div class=\"menu\">\n  <a href=\"/\">Home</a>\n</div>",
  },
  89: {
    source: "Как реализовать offline-first и recovery-сценарий при потере сети?",
    question: "How do you implement offline-first and a recovery scenario when the network is lost?",
    shortAnswer: "Offline-first is an architectural approach in which an application is designed to work locally first and foremost, with synchronization to the server happening when a network is available. To implement it, Service Workers are used to cache critical resources and intercept network requests. The user's data is stored locally in IndexedDB or localStorage. When the connection is restored, background tasks (the Background Sync API, for example) send the accumulated data to the server, providing the recovery scenario.",
    longAnswer: "Offline-first is a development paradigm that gives priority to the application working locally and treats the connection to the server as an optional enhancement. This is critically important for mobile applications and web applications that operate on an unreliable internet connection.\n\nThe key components of the implementation\n\n- The Service Worker: a script that runs in the browser's background. It acts as a proxy server, intercepting network requests. Its main task in offline-first is to serve requests from the cache when the network is unavailable.\n\n- Caching (the Cache API): used to store static resources (HTML, CSS, JS, images) and even API responses. Caching strategies (Cache-First, Network-First, for example) determine where to take the data from first.\n\n- Local data storage (IndexedDB): to save the user's data (forms, settings, the application's state), IndexedDB is used — a powerful client-side NoSQL database. It is preferable to localStorage for working with large volumes of structured data.\n\n- Background synchronization (the Background Sync API): lets you postpone sending data to the server until the network is restored. The user can perform an action offline, and the Service Worker will send it later, automatically.\n\nA practical example: saving a note offline\n\nConsider a simple notes application. When the network is lost, a new note has to be saved locally, and when the connection is restored it has to be sent to the server.\n\nThe recovery scenario and handling conflicts\n\nRecovery is not merely a matter of sending the accumulated data. You have to allow for:\n\n- A queue of operations: the data to be sent must be stored in the order it was created.\n\n- Handling conflicts: if the data on the server changed while the client was offline, you need a strategy for resolving conflicts (\"last one wins\" or a manual merge, for example).\n\n- Notifying the user: the interface should report the status of synchronization (\"Synchronizing...\", \"All data is up to date\").\n\nConclusion: the offline-first approach with a recovery scenario is worth applying in applications where continuity of operation is critical (mobile applications, tools for fieldwork, editors). It improves the user experience considerably, making the application resilient to network failures.",
    codeExample: "// 1. Registering the Service Worker in the main script\nif ('serviceWorker' in navigator) {\n  navigator.serviceWorker.register('/sw.js');\n}\n\n// 2. Saving a note in IndexedDB when the form is submitted\nasync function saveNote(noteData) {\n  // We check the online status\n  if (!navigator.onLine) {\n    // We save it to the local database\n    await saveToIndexedDB('pendingNotes', noteData);\n    // We register a task for background synchronization\n    if ('sync' in registration) {\n      await registration.sync.register('sync-notes');\n    }\n    alert('The note has been saved locally and will be sent later.');\n  } else {\n    // If we are online, we send it straight away\n    await sendToServer(noteData);\n  }\n}\n\n// 3. In the Service Worker (sw.js) we handle the background synchronization\nself.addEventListener('sync', event => {\n  if (event.tag === 'sync-notes') {\n    event.waitUntil(syncNotes()); // The function that sends the data from IndexedDB\n  }\n});\n\n// 4. The caching strategy for static files (Cache-First)\nself.addEventListener('fetch', event => {\n  event.respondWith(\n    caches.match(event.request)\n      .then(response => response || fetch(event.request))\n  );\n});",
  },
  90: {
    source: "Как решать проблемы изменения структуры данных в localStorage между версиями приложения?",
    question: "How do you solve the problems of changing the structure of data in localStorage between versions of an application?",
    shortAnswer: "To solve the problems of changing the structure of data in localStorage between versions of an application, you have to introduce a system for versioning the data schema. When the application starts you should check the stored version of the data and apply a sequence of migration functions to convert the old format into the new one. This lets users keep their data after the application has been updated, with nothing lost. The key steps are: storing the version, writing migrations, running them safely and rolling back on error.",
    longAnswer: "When developing client-side web applications that use `localStorage` to save state, user data or settings, sooner or later the need arises to change the structure of that data — to add a new field, rename an existing one or change the type of a value. If you simply release a new version of the application, the old data saved in users' browsers may become unreadable or cause errors, which spoils the user experience. A mechanism for updating the data smoothly is therefore needed.\nThe main strategy: versioning and migrations\nThe idea is to store a version number alongside the data (`dataVersion: 1`, for example). With every change to the structure that breaks backward compatibility, the version is incremented. When it starts, the application checks the currently stored version and, if it is lower than expected, applies migration functions in sequence that convert the data from the format of version N to the format of version N+1.\nA practical implementation\nConsider an example in JavaScript. Suppose we store an object of user settings. In version 1 we had a `userName` field, and in version 2 we want to split it into `firstName` and `lastName`.\n\nWhere it is applied and the important aspects\nThis approach is critically important for any application that stores state on the client and is meant to develop over the long term. It is applied not only with `localStorage` but also when working with IndexedDB, file formats or even a server API (although there the migrations normally happen on the database side). The key points for reliability:\n\n- Idempotent migrations: a migration can be applied several times without changing the result.\n- Error handling: if a migration fails, you need a rollback strategy (not overwriting the original data until the migration has completed successfully, for example).\n- Backups: before a migration you can save the raw data under a separate key in case something goes wrong.\n- Testing: migrations should be covered by tests with real data from old versions.\nConclusion: use data versioning and step-by-step migrations when your application develops and changes the structure of its client-side data. This ensures a trouble-free update for users and spares you from having to clear their localStorage forcibly with every release.",
    codeExample: "// A constant with the current version of the data schema in the application\nconst CURRENT_DATA_VERSION = 2;\n\n// A function for getting the data out of localStorage\nfunction getAppData() {\n  const rawData = localStorage.getItem('myAppData');\n  if (!rawData) {\n    // If there is no data, we return the defaults with the current version\n    return { dataVersion: CURRENT_DATA_VERSION, firstName: '', lastName: '' };\n  }\n  \n  let data = JSON.parse(rawData);\n  // If no version is stated, we treat it as obsolete (version 0, for example)\n  const storedVersion = data.dataVersion || 0;\n  \n  // We apply the migrations if the version is out of date\n  if (storedVersion < CURRENT_DATA_VERSION) {\n    data = runMigrations(data, storedVersion);\n  }\n  \n  return data;\n}\n\n// The collection of migration functions\nconst migrations = {\n  // The migration from version 0 to version 1 (if no version was stored)\n  0: (data) => {\n    // We add the dataVersion field\n    data.dataVersion = 1;\n    return data;\n  },\n  // The migration from version 1 to version 2\n  1: (data) => {\n    // We split userName into firstName and lastName\n    if (data.userName) {\n      const parts = data.userName.split(' ');\n      data.firstName = parts[0] || '';\n      data.lastName = parts.slice(1).join(' ') || '';\n      delete data.userName;\n    } else {\n      data.firstName = '';\n      data.lastName = '';\n    }\n    data.dataVersion = 2;\n    return data;\n  }\n};\n\nfunction runMigrations(data, fromVersion) {\n  let migratedData = { ...data };\n  for (let v = fromVersion; v < CURRENT_DATA_VERSION; v++) {\n    if (migrations[v]) {\n      migratedData = migrations[v](migratedData);\n    } else {\n      console.error(`Migration from version ${v} not found!`);\n      // In case of an error you can reset the data or throw an exception\n      break;\n    }\n  }\n  // We save the updated data back into storage\n  localStorage.setItem('myAppData', JSON.stringify(migratedData));\n  return migratedData;\n}",
  },
  91: {
    source: "Какие ограничения есть у браузера при работе с файловой системой через JavaScript?",
    question: "What limitations does the browser have when working with the file system through JavaScript?",
    shortAnswer: "JavaScript in the browser has no direct access to the user's file system, for security reasons. This prevents files from being read or written without the user's explicit permission. To work with files, special APIs such as the File System Access API are used, and they require interaction with the user (through a file-picker dialog, for example). You can also use limited storage (IndexedDB, for example) for the application's data, but not for arbitrary files on disk.",
    longAnswer: "Browsers execute JavaScript in a strict sandbox that isolates web pages from the operating system and the user's file system. This is a fundamental security principle that prevents malicious sites from stealing or damaging files.\n\nThe main limitations\n\n- No direct access to file paths: JavaScript cannot read or write files at arbitrary paths (`C:/Users/...`, for example).\n\n- An explicit user action is required: working with files is possible only after the user has interacted — through an `<input type=\"file\">` element or by invoking a file-picker dialog, for example.\n\n- Access only to the selected files: even after a selection, the script gains access only to the specific files that were chosen, not to the whole directory.\n\n- Limited persistent storage: to save data between sessions, APIs such as IndexedDB or localStorage are used, but they have quotas (usually a few megabytes) and do not provide access to the real file system.\n\nModern APIs for working with files\n\nWith the appearance of the File System Access API (formerly known as the Native File System API), developers gained the ability to ask the user for permission to access particular files or directories and to preserve that permission for later sessions.\n\nWhere it is applied\n\nLimited access to the file system is used in every web application that works with files: online editors (text, graphics), data-processing tools, IDEs in the browser (CodeSandbox, StackBlitz, for example). The safe model lets users control which files a site gains access to.\n\nConclusion: the limitations on access to the file system are a key element of browser security that protects users. For legitimate work with files you should use modern APIs, which ask for explicit permission and provide controlled access only to the selected resources.",
    codeExample: "// An example of a request to pick a file\nasync function pickFile() {\n  try {\n    // We open the file-picker dialog\n    [fileHandle] = await window.showOpenFilePicker();\n    // We get the file itself\n    const file = await fileHandle.getFile();\n    const contents = await file.text();\n    console.log(contents);\n  } catch (err) {\n    console.error('The user cancelled the selection, or an error occurred:', err);\n  }\n}\n\n// An example of writing to a file (permission to write is required)\nasync function saveFile(contents) {\n  const options = {\n    types: [{\n      description: 'Text files',\n      accept: {'text/plain': ['.txt']},\n    }],\n  };\n  const handle = await window.showSaveFilePicker(options);\n  const writable = await handle.createWritable();\n  await writable.write(contents);\n  await writable.close();\n}",
  },
  92: {
    source: "Почему при скачивании больших файлов через fetch возникает проблема переполнения памяти?",
    question: "Why does a memory overflow problem arise when downloading large files through fetch?",
    shortAnswer: "With an ordinary call to fetch().then(res => res.json()) or res.blob(), the browser loads the whole response into memory before handing it to your code. If a file weighs gigabytes, it is placed in RAM in its entirety, which can exhaust the available resources and crash the tab or the application. To avoid this you need to work with the response as a stream, processing the data in pieces as it arrives from the server rather than accumulating all of it at once.",
    longAnswer: "When you use `fetch` to get data, the browser buffers the whole response body in memory by default. This is convenient for small JSON responses or images, but it becomes a problem for large files such as video, archives or database dumps.\n\nWhy does the memory overflow happen?\n\nThe `response.json()`, `response.text()` and `response.blob()` methods read the entire response body and convert it into the corresponding format, keeping the result in JavaScript's memory in full. If the file is 2 GB, then before your code can process it the browser has to allocate at least 2 GB of RAM for that buffer. This can exceed the memory limits available to a browser tab and lead to an \"out of memory\" error, or simply to a freeze.\n\nThe solution: using the Streams API\n\nThe modern `fetch` API returns a `Response` object whose `body` property is a `ReadableStream`. This stream lets you read the data in chunks as it arrives from the server. You can process every chunk and free the memory immediately, without waiting for the whole file to load.\n\nA practical example\n\nHere is how you can download a large file and save it to the user's disk using streams, avoiding a memory overflow:\n\nWhere is this applied?\n\n- Streaming video or audio in web applications.\n\n- Loading and previewing large logs or CSV files.\n\n- Gradual processing of data from the server (the progressive loading of JSON, for example).\n\n- Client-side processing of files that do not fit into memory in their entirety (encryption, hashing).\n\nConclusion: use the Streams API through `response.body` when working with potentially large responses from the server, in order to process the data in pieces and avoid a memory overflow. This is critically important for building responsive web applications that work with large volumes of data.",
    codeExample: "async function downloadLargeFile(url, filename) {\n  // We request the resource\n  const response = await fetch(url);\n  // We get the stream out of the response body\n  const readableStream = response.body;\n  \n  // We create a stream for writing to the file through the File System Access API\n  // (in real code you need to check whether the browser supports it)\n  const fileStream = await createWritableStream(filename);\n  \n  // We copy the data from the response into the file as a stream\n  await readableStream.pipeTo(fileStream);\n  \n  console.log('The file has been saved!');\n}\n\n// A simplified function for the demonstration (in reality use showSaveFilePicker)\nasync function createWritableStream(filename) {\n  // For the example: we create a Blob object and a download link\n  // This is not streaming to disk but a demonstration of handling chunks.\n  const chunks = [];\n  const writer = {\n    write(chunk) {\n      // We process every chunk of data\n      chunks.push(chunk);\n      // Here you could, for example, send the chunk into a WebSocket\n      // or compute a hash on the fly\n      console.log(`Received ${chunk.byteLength} bytes`);\n    },\n    close() {\n      // At the end we assemble the file (for the example; in reality you should not do this)\n      const blob = new Blob(chunks);\n      const link = document.createElement('a');\n      link.href = URL.createObjectURL(blob);\n      link.download = filename;\n      link.click();\n    }\n  };\n  return writer;\n}",
  },
  93: {
    source: "Какие способы существуют для скачивания больших файлов в браузере без загрузки в память?",
    question: "What ways are there of downloading large files in the browser without loading them into memory?",
    shortAnswer: "The main way is to use the Streams API together with the Fetch API for processing data as a stream. This lets you read and process a file in pieces without loading it into memory in its entirety. You can also use the `download` attribute on a link or the `Content-Disposition: attachment` header so that the server serves the file for direct download. For very large files, Service Workers are sometimes used to cache parts of the file.",
    longAnswer: "When downloading large files in the browser the key task is to avoid loading the whole file into the user's RAM, which can lead to a loss of performance or a crashed tab. The traditional approach with `XMLHttpRequest` or a plain `fetch()` loads the whole response into memory as a Blob or a string, which is unacceptable for gigabyte-sized files. Modern APIs provide mechanisms for stream processing.\n\n1. Using the Fetch API with Streams\n\nThe Fetch API can return a response as a readable stream (`ReadableStream`). You can process the data as it arrives from the server — writing it straight to the user's disk, for example (if the browser supports the File System Access API), or performing stream processing.\n\n2. Downloading directly through a link or an iframe\n\nThe simplest and most memory-safe way is to make the browser treat the file as a download. This can be done by creating a link with the `download` attribute or by using an iframe. The file is saved straight into the downloads folder, bypassing JavaScript's memory.\n\nThis method works if the server sends the correct `Content-Disposition: attachment` header.\n\n3. The File System Access API (experimental)\n\nIn modern browsers (Chrome, Edge) you can use the File System Access API to create a file on the user's disk and stream data into it through Fetch. This lets you download files of any size, writing them to disk in pieces.\n\n4. Splitting into chunks (chunked download)\n\nIf the server supports byte ranges (the `Accept-Ranges: bytes` header), you can download a file in pieces using the `Range` header. Every piece can be processed separately and then combined on the client (with the File System Access API, for example).\n\nConclusion: for downloading large files without loading them into memory, it is preferable to use streaming APIs (Fetch + Streams) combined with the File System Access API for writing straight to disk. If maximum compatibility is required, the method of creating a link or an iframe will do, since it hands the task of downloading over to the browser.",
    codeExample: "async function downloadLargeFile(url) {\n  const response = await fetch(url);\n  const reader = response.body.getReader();\n  const chunks = [];\n\n  while (true) {\n    const { done, value } = await reader.read();\n    if (done) break;\n    // value is a Uint8Array (a chunk of data)\n    chunks.push(value);\n    // Here you could pass the chunks on, into another stream for example\n  }\n  // Combining the chunks in memory is not advisable for very large files\n  // It is better to use a WritableStream to write to disk\n}\n\n// Way 1: creating a temporary link\nconst link = document.createElement('a');\nlink.href = 'https://example.com/large-file.zip';\nlink.download = 'file.zip';\ndocument.body.appendChild(link);\nlink.click();\ndocument.body.removeChild(link);\n\n// Way 2: using an iframe\nconst iframe = document.createElement('iframe');\niframe.style.display = 'none';\niframe.src = 'https://example.com/large-file.zip';\ndocument.body.appendChild(iframe);\n// The file will start downloading\n\nasync function saveLargeFileStream(url, fileName) {\n  const response = await fetch(url);\n  const fileHandle = await window.showSaveFilePicker({\n    suggestedName: fileName\n  });\n  const writableStream = await fileHandle.createWritable();\n  await response.body.pipeTo(writableStream); // Streaming!\n  console.log('The file has been saved to disk.');\n}\n\nasync function downloadInChunks(url, chunkSize = 1024 * 1024) {\n  // First we find out the file's size\n  const headResponse = await fetch(url, { method: 'HEAD' });\n  const totalSize = parseInt(headResponse.headers.get('Content-Length'), 10);\n  \n  for (let start = 0; start < totalSize; start += chunkSize) {\n    const end = Math.min(start + chunkSize - 1, totalSize - 1);\n    const response = await fetch(url, {\n      headers: { 'Range': `bytes=${start}-${end}` }\n    });\n    const chunk = await response.arrayBuffer();\n    // Processing each chunk\n  }\n}",
  },
  94: {
    source: "Как браузер обрабатывает скачивание файлов (download через ссылку)?",
    question: "How does the browser handle the downloading of files (a download through a link)?",
    shortAnswer: "When the user clicks a link with the `download` attribute, the browser initiates a GET request to the given URL. If the server replies with the correct headers (`Content-Disposition: attachment`, for example), the browser interprets the response as a file to be saved rather than displayed. It then shows the standard dialog for saving the file to the user's local file system. This process bypasses the CORS security policy for same-origin links.",
    longAnswer: "Downloading files through links is a fundamental capability of the web that lets users save content to their devices. The mechanism is triggered when the user interacts with an HTML `<a>` element (an anchor) that has the `download` attribute set.\n\nHow it works\n\nThe process can be divided into several stages:\n\n- Initiating the request: clicking a link with `download` makes the browser perform an HTTP GET request to the URL given in the `href` attribute.\n\n- Handling the server's response: the HTTP header `Content-Disposition` plays the key part. If the server sends `Content-Disposition: attachment; filename=\"example.pdf\"`, the browser understands that the response body is a file to be saved rather than displayed in the current tab.\n\n- Saving the file: the browser receives the stream of data (binary or text) and, following its security policies, offers the user a choice of where to save it through a system dialog.\n\nA practical example\n\nConsider some simple HTML for creating a download link:\n\nIn the second example the server-side handler (in Node.js with Express, for example) has to set the correct headers:\n\nParticulars and limitations\n\nThe `download` attribute has limitations for security reasons. It will not work if the file is on another domain (cross-origin) and that domain's server does not send the corresponding CORS headers. In such cases the browser will simply navigate to the link rather than offering to download the file. To download data generated on the client (the contents of a text editor, for example), you can use the JavaScript API `URL.createObjectURL()` together with `Blob` objects.\n\nConclusion: the mechanism of downloading through links is simple and effective for serving static files or files generated on the server. It is worth applying when you need to give the user a simple way of saving a document, an image or an archive without requiring complex client-side logic.",
    codeExample: "<!-- A link to a static file on the same server -->\n<a href=\"/files/report.pdf\" download=\"my_report.pdf\">\n  Download the report (PDF)\n</a>\n\n<!-- A link that initiates a download through a server-side script -->\n<a href=\"/api/download?id=123\" download>Download the document</a>\n\n// An example in Node.js/Express\napp.get('/api/download', (req, res) => {\n  const filePath = path.resolve(__dirname, 'private', 'file123.zip');\n  // We set the header indicating a download\n  res.setHeader('Content-Disposition', 'attachment; filename=\"archive.zip\"');\n  res.sendFile(filePath); // We send the file\n});",
  },
  95: {
    source: "Какие подходы существуют для оптимизации работы с большими файлами на клиенте?",
    question: "What approaches exist for optimizing work with large files on the client?",
    shortAnswer: "The main approaches are reading files in pieces (chunking) through the File API, using streams for gradual processing, offloading heavy operations into Web Workers so as not to block the interface, and applying Blob to work with binary data without loading the whole file into memory. It is also important to use virtualization to display large lists of data from a file. These methods let you process gigabytes of data without any loss of performance.",
    longAnswer: "When working with large files (CSV, JSON or logs, for example) in the browser, loading the whole file into memory directly can freeze the interface or cause an \"out of memory\" error. Special techniques are therefore used that let you process the data gradually without burdening the main memory and the UI thread.\n\n1. Reading a file in pieces (chunking)\n\nWith the `File` and `Blob` APIs you can break a file into chunks and read them in sequence. The `slice()` method (or `blob.slice()`) creates a new Blob containing part of the original file. This lets you, for example, upload a large file to the server in pieces or process it line by line without loading it in its entirety.\n\n2. Stream processing (the Streams API)\n\nThe Streams API provides a more elegant way of processing data as it arrives. You can create a readable stream from a file and transform the data through a chain of transformations. This is especially useful for parsing large CSV or JSON files.\n\n3. Moving computation into Web Workers\n\nIf processing a file requires intensive computation (complex parsing or encryption, for example), it is worth moving it into a Web Worker. That will prevent the main thread and the user interface from being blocked.\n\n4. Virtualization for display\n\nIf thousands of rows of data have been extracted from a file, displaying them in the DOM can be slow. Virtualization libraries (react-window, for example) render only the visible elements, which speeds things up considerably.\n\nConclusion: the approaches described are worth applying when you work with files of tens of megabytes or more. Reading in pieces and streams suit sequential processing, Web Workers suit heavy computation, and virtualization suits displaying large volumes of data. This lets you build responsive web applications even when working with gigabytes of information.",
    codeExample: "const fileInput = document.getElementById('fileInput');\nfileInput.addEventListener('change', async (e) => {\n  const file = e.target.files[0];\n  const chunkSize = 1024 * 1024; // 1 MB\n  let offset = 0;\n\n  while (offset < file.size) {\n    const chunk = file.slice(offset, offset + chunkSize);\n    // Process the chunk (send it to the server or read its text, for example)\n    const text = await chunk.text();\n    console.log(`Read ${chunk.size} bytes`);\n    offset += chunkSize;\n  }\n});\n\nasync function processLargeFile(file) {\n  const stream = file.stream();\n  const reader = stream.getReader();\n\n  while (true) {\n    const { done, value } = await reader.read();\n    if (done) break;\n    // value is a Uint8Array with part of the data\n    // Processing the portion of data\n  }\n}\n\n// main.js\nconst worker = new Worker('file-worker.js');\nworker.postMessage({ file: largeFile });\nworker.onmessage = (e) => {\n  console.log('The result of the processing:', e.data);\n};\n\n// file-worker.js\nself.onmessage = async (e) => {\n  const file = e.data.file;\n  // The heavy processing of the file\n  const result = processFile(file);\n  self.postMessage(result);\n};",
  },
  96: {
    source: "Можно ли получить Cookie через JavaScript?",
    question: "Can you get a cookie through JavaScript?",
    shortAnswer: "Yes, you can get a cookie through JavaScript, but with limitations. The main way is to use the `document.cookie` property. It returns a string with every cookie available for the current origin (domain and path) that is not marked as HttpOnly. Cookies with the HttpOnly flag are protected from access through JavaScript and can only be sent over HTTP(S). This is an important security measure for protecting sensitive data such as session tokens.",
    longAnswer: "Web applications often use cookies to store small fragments of data on the client side, such as session identifiers or user settings. JavaScript provides an interface for interacting with that data through the `document.cookie` property.\n\nHow document.cookie works\n\nReading `document.cookie` returns a string containing every cookie available to the current page, in the form `name=value`, separated by semicolons. It is important to understand that access is restricted by the Same-Origin Policy. JavaScript can read and write only those cookies that belong to the current domain and path and that do not have the `HttpOnly` flag set.\n\nAn example of reading and writing a cookie\n\nSecurity and the HttpOnly flag\n\nThe `HttpOnly` flag is a critically important security attribute. When a cookie is marked HttpOnly (normally done by the server when setting the `Set-Cookie` header), the browser forbids any access to it through JavaScript. This protects the cookie from being stolen through cross-site scripting (XSS). So although JavaScript can read many cookies, the most important ones (session identifiers, for example) often remain unavailable.\n\nPractical use\n\n- Storing the user's preferences: an interface theme or language settings, for example, which can safely be kept in client-side cookies.\n\n- Tracking state: simple flags or tokens for analytics (taking regulatory requirements such as the GDPR into account).\n\n- Working with legacy systems: some old applications may rely on client-side cookies to pass data between pages.\n\nConclusion: use `document.cookie` to work with non-critical data on the client, but never keep confidential information in cookies that JavaScript can reach — use the HttpOnly flag and server-side sessions for that.",
    codeExample: "// Reading all the available cookies\nconst allCookies = document.cookie;\nconsole.log(allCookies); // Output: \"sessionId=abc123; theme=dark\"\n\n// Setting a new cookie\n// We give the name, the value and additional attributes such as the expiry (expires) or the path.\ndocument.cookie = \"username=JohnDoe; expires=Fri, 31 Dec 2024 23:59:59 GMT; path=/\";\n\n// After it has been set, document.cookie will contain the new cookie too.\nconsole.log(document.cookie);",
  },
  97: {
    source: "Как JavaScript взаимодействует с рендерингом браузера?",
    question: "How does JavaScript interact with the browser's rendering?",
    shortAnswer: "JavaScript runs on the browser's main thread, which is also responsible for rendering. Long synchronous operations block that thread, freezing the updating of the interface. The browser uses the event loop to schedule tasks: it executes JavaScript, then computes styles and layout and paints a frame. So as not to interfere with rendering, heavy tasks have to be broken into pieces or moved into Web Workers.",
    longAnswer: "The interaction between JavaScript and the browser's rendering is a key aspect of a web application's performance. Both processes (executing JS and updating the screen) happen on one thread, called the main thread. This means they cannot work at the same time.\nThe event loop and the rendering stages\nThe browser manages tasks with the event loop. After a block of JavaScript code has run, the browser may perform the rendering stages if necessary. The main stages are:\n\n- Style: recomputing the computed styles.\n- Layout (or reflow): calculating the geometry and position of elements.\n- Paint: filling in the pixels (rasterization).\n- Composite: assembling the layers for display.\nThe browser tries to render at 60 frames per second. If executing JavaScript takes longer than about 16 ms, a frame will be dropped, which leads to a \"stuttering\" interface.\nA practical example and the problem\nConsider code that processes a large array synchronously:\n\nWhile this loop is running, the browser cannot update an animation, handle clicks or repaint the page.\nWays of optimizing it\nTo avoid blocking, people use:\n\n- Breaking work into pieces (chunking) with `setTimeout` or `requestIdleCallback`.\n- Web Workers, to move computation into a separate thread.\n- Minimizing operations that cause the layout to be recomputed (layout thrashing). Reading and writing geometric properties in a single loop, for example, forces the browser to recompute the layout over and over.\nConclusion: understanding this interaction is critical for building smooth interfaces. JavaScript that runs for a long time has to yield control to the main thread so that the browser can render frames in good time. For background or complex computation it is preferable to use Web Workers.",
    codeExample: "// This function blocks the main thread\nfunction processLargeArray(data) {\n  for (let i = 0; i < data.length; i++) {\n    // Heavy computation\n    data[i] = Math.sqrt(data[i]) * Math.random();\n  }\n  return data;\n}\n\n// The call will freeze the interface while it runs\nconst result = processLargeArray(new Array(1000000).fill(10));\nconsole.log('Done'); // By this point the UI has not been updated",
  },
  98: {
    source: "Как установить и прочитать Cookie в браузере?",
    question: "How do you set and read a cookie in the browser?",
    shortAnswer: "Cookies are small strings of data that the server sends to the browser, which the browser stores and sends back with subsequent requests. You can set a cookie by assigning a string to the `document.cookie` property. You can read every cookie for the current domain by reading that same `document.cookie` property. It is important to bear in mind the cookie's parameters, such as the expiry (expires/max-age), the path and the HttpOnly flag, which forbids access from JavaScript.",
    longAnswer: "Cookies are a mechanism for storing small fragments of data on the client side, which the browser sends to the server automatically with every HTTP request. They are widely used to authenticate users, track sessions, store preferences (a theme or a language, for example) and target advertising.\nSetting a cookie\nTo set a cookie from JavaScript you assign a string to the `document.cookie` property. That string must contain the name, the value and additional attributes separated by semicolons. Important: the assignment does not overwrite all the existing cookies but only adds or updates the one you have named.\n\nReading cookies\nEvery cookie for the current domain is available as a single string through `document.cookie`. To get the value of a particular cookie, that string has to be parsed.\n\nThe key attributes and their use\n\n- expires and max-age: determine the cookie's lifetime. Without them the cookie will be a session cookie and will be deleted when the browser closes.\n- path: states the path on the server for which the cookie will be sent. The default is the current path.\n- domain: lets you state the domain for which the cookie is valid.\n- Secure: the cookie will be sent only over the secure HTTPS protocol.\n- HttpOnly: this attribute, set by the server, forbids access to the cookie from JavaScript, which protects against XSS attacks.\nCookies can also be set by the server using the HTTP header `Set-Cookie` in its response, which is the main method for authentication.\nConclusion: use cookies when you need to store data that has to be sent to the server automatically with every request — authentication tokens or session identifiers, for example. For storing large volumes of data that do not need to be sent to the server, `localStorage` or `sessionStorage` is preferable.",
    codeExample: "// Setting a simple cookie\ndocument.cookie = \"username=JohnDoe\";\n\n// Setting a cookie with parameters: the expiry, the path and the Secure flag\nconst expiryDate = new Date();\nexpiryDate.setDate(expiryDate.getDate() + 7); // A cookie for 7 days\ndocument.cookie = `sessionId=abc123; expires=${expiryDate.toUTCString()}; path=/; Secure`;\n\n// Getting every cookie as a string\nconst allCookies = document.cookie; // For example: \"username=JohnDoe; sessionId=abc123\"\n\n// A function for getting a value by name\nfunction getCookie(name) {\n  const cookies = document.cookie.split('; ');\n  for (let cookie of cookies) {\n    const [key, value] = cookie.split('=');\n    if (key === name) return value;\n  }\n  return null;\n}\n\nconsole.log(getCookie('username')); // Prints: JohnDoe",
  },
  99: {
    source: "Как requestAnimationFrame связан с Event Loop?",
    question: "How is requestAnimationFrame connected with the event loop?",
    shortAnswer: "requestAnimationFrame is a browser API that puts a callback into a special queue tied to the rendering cycle. The event loop manages the execution of tasks from different queues: macrotasks, microtasks and tasks related to painting. A requestAnimationFrame callback runs immediately before the stage of updating styles and layout (Layout/Paint) in the rendering cycle, which provides smooth animation synchronized with the screen's refresh rate.",
    longAnswer: "To understand the connection between `requestAnimationFrame` (rAF) and the event loop, you first have to recall that the event loop is a mechanism that continuously processes tasks from different queues. The event loop's main phases include executing macrotasks (`setTimeout`, for example), microtasks (promises, for example) and tasks related to rendering.\nWhere rAF sits in the event loop\nA callback passed into `requestAnimationFrame` does not go into the general macrotask queue. Instead the browser places it in a special animation-frame queue. That queue is processed at the \"update the rendering\" stage inside the event loop. This stage happens only when the browser is preparing to repaint the screen, normally synchronized with the monitor's refresh rate (60 Hz, for example).\nThe order of execution\nA typical cycle for a frame with animation looks like this:\n\n- All the microtasks run (promise handlers, for example).\n- One macrotask runs (a `setTimeout` callback, for example).\n- The render-update phase arrives. Here, if it is time to paint a frame:\n- All the callbacks scheduled through `requestAnimationFrame` run.\n- Style computation, layout and paint happen.\n\nA practical example\nConsider code that animates the movement of an element. Using rAF guarantees that the position is updated immediately before the browser paints, which minimizes jank.\n\nIn this example the `animate` function will be called by the browser at the optimal moment for animation. If we used `setInterval`, the calls could happen at unsuitable moments (when the browser is busy with other tasks, for example), which would lead to dropped frames or uneven animation.\nWhere this is applied\n`requestAnimationFrame` is the standard way of creating smooth animations in web applications. It is used in animation libraries, in game engines on Canvas or WebGL, and also for performing \"heavy\" computation that can be broken into frames so as not to block the main thread for long.\nConclusion: use `requestAnimationFrame` when you need smooth, performant animation or a visual update synchronized with the screen's refresh rate. It lets the browser integrate your code into its own rendering cycle, avoiding unnecessary computation and providing a better user experience.",
    codeExample: "const element = document.getElementById('box');\nlet position = 0;\n\nfunction animate() {\n  // We update the element's position\n  position += 2;\n  element.style.transform = `translateX(${position}px)`;\n\n  // We request the next animation frame\n  if (position < 200) {\n    requestAnimationFrame(animate);\n  }\n}\n\n// We start the animation\nrequestAnimationFrame(animate);",
  },
  100: {
    source: "Что произойдет, если убрать requestAnimationFrame из цикла рендеринга?",
    question: "What will happen if you remove requestAnimationFrame from the rendering cycle?",
    shortAnswer: "Without requestAnimationFrame the animation will run at arbitrary moments that are not synchronized with the browser's screen refresh rate. This can lead to jank, dropped frames and increased CPU consumption. The animation may run too fast or too slowly depending on the system's load. requestAnimationFrame guarantees that the update function is called before every repaint of the screen, which provides smoothness.",
    longAnswer: "requestAnimationFrame (rAF) is a browser API that lets you schedule a function to run before the screen's next repaint. Its main purpose is to synchronize animation code with the refresh rate of the device's display (usually 60 Hz, that is 60 frames per second).\n\nWhat happens without requestAnimationFrame?\n\nIf you use an ordinary `setInterval` or a recursive `setTimeout` for the animation loop, several problems arise:\n\n- No synchronization: timers are not tied to the browser's rendering cycle. Your function may be called in the middle of the rendering phase or when the browser is busy with other tasks (handling events or parsing, for example).\n\n- Dropped frames: if the function is called too often, the browser may not manage to paint the changes, which leads to jank.\n\n- CPU load in background tabs: timers carry on running even when the tab is inactive, wasting battery and processor resources.\n\n- Unpredictable speed: on different devices with different screen refresh rates (120 Hz, for example) the animation will run at a different speed.\n\nA code example: comparing the approaches\n\nHere is a simple example of animating the movement of an element. First, the problematic version with `setInterval`:\n\nAnd now the correct version with `requestAnimationFrame`:\n\nWhere is requestAnimationFrame applied?\n\nThis API is used not only for moving DOM elements. It is critically important for:\n\n- Canvas animations and games\n\n- Complex visualizations and charts\n\n- Smooth transitions and parallax effects\n\n- Your own animation libraries\n\nThe browser optimizes rAF calls automatically: it pauses them in inactive tabs, synchronizes them with VSync and combines several DOM changes into a single repaint.\n\nConclusion: always use requestAnimationFrame for any visual updates that have to happen smoothly and in sync with the screen's refresh rate. It provides optimal performance, saves energy and prevents visual artefacts. Timers (setInterval/setTimeout) are suitable only for tasks unrelated to rendering.",
    codeExample: "// BAD: using setInterval\nconst element = document.getElementById('box');\nlet pos = 0;\nconst intervalId = setInterval(() => {\n  pos += 5;\n  element.style.left = pos + 'px';\n  if (pos > 500) clearInterval(intervalId);\n}, 16); // ~60 frames/sec (1000/16)\n\n// GOOD: using requestAnimationFrame\nconst element = document.getElementById('box');\nlet pos = 0;\nfunction animate() {\n  pos += 5;\n  element.style.left = pos + 'px';\n  if (pos < 500) {\n    requestAnimationFrame(animate);\n  }\n}\nrequestAnimationFrame(animate);",
  },
  101: {
    source: "Что происходит при загрузке скрипта без async и defer?",
    question: "What happens when a script is loaded without async and defer?",
    shortAnswer: "When the browser meets a `<script>` tag without the `async` or `defer` attributes, it pauses the parsing of the HTML, loads the script file and executes it synchronously, and only then resumes parsing. This means the content after the script is not rendered until the script has finished its work, which can noticeably degrade the perceived loading speed.",
    longAnswer: "When the browser loads an HTML page, it parses its content in sequence, building the DOM tree. On meeting an ordinary `<script>` tag without the `async` or `defer` attributes, it stops the parsing process immediately. This is critical behaviour, because the script may contain commands that change the DOM (`document.write`, for example), and the browser has to ensure the tree is in a consistent state.\n\nThe sequence of events\n\n- The browser finds the tag `<script src=\"app.js\">`.\n\n- The parsing of the HTML is paused.\n\n- The browser starts loading the script file from the server (if `src` is given).\n\n- Once it has loaded fully, the script is executed synchronously.\n\n- Only after execution has finished does the parsing of the HTML resume.\n\nA practical example and the consequences\n\nConsider a simple HTML page:\n\nWhile the browser is loading and executing `heavy-script.js`, the user will see a blank screen. The page's content (`<h1>`, `<p>`) will not be painted until the script has finished. This leads to a poor user experience, especially on a slow connection.\n\nWhere this is applied and how to avoid the problems\n\nThis approach is necessary if it is critically important for the script to interact with DOM elements located immediately before it, or if the order in which several scripts run is strictly defined. In modern development, however, the following is recommended for optimizing performance:\n\n- Use the `defer` attribute for scripts that do not need to run immediately. They will be loaded in parallel with the parsing but will run only after the DOM has been built fully, before the `DOMContentLoaded` event.\n\n- Use the `async` attribute for independent scripts (analytics counters, for example), which can run at any moment after loading without blocking the parsing.\n\n- Place non-critical scripts before the closing `</body>` tag, so that the main content has time to be displayed.\n\nConclusion: ordinary script loading (without async/defer) blocks the parsing of the page, which can seriously degrade the perceived loading speed. Use it deliberately, only when a strict order of execution and immediate access to the DOM are required. In most cases `defer` is preferable for scripts that work with the DOM, and `async` for independent ones.",
    codeExample: "<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html>\n<head>\n    <title>An example</title>\n    <script src=\"heavy-script.js\"></script>\n</head>\n<body>\n    <h1>An important heading</h1>\n    <p>This text will appear with a delay.</p>\n</body>\n</html>",
  },
  102: {
    source: "Что такое DOM-дерево и как оно устроено?",
    question: "What is the DOM tree and how is it put together?",
    shortAnswer: "The DOM (Document Object Model) is a programming interface for HTML and XML documents that represents a document as a tree of objects. Every element, attribute and fragment of text becomes a node of that tree. The browser creates the DOM when a page loads, so that JavaScript can change the document's structure, style and content. Without the DOM, scripts would not be able to interact with a page's elements.",
    longAnswer: "The DOM (Document Object Model) is not a programming language but a standard, cross-platform interface that lets programs and scripts access and update a document's content, structure and style dynamically. When the browser loads an HTML page, it parses it and builds a tree structure in memory out of the elements, attributes and text — the DOM tree.\n\nThe structure of the DOM tree\n\nThe tree consists of nodes. The main kinds of node are:\n\n- The document (Document): the root node, representing the whole document.\n\n- Element nodes: the nodes corresponding to HTML tags (<div>, <p>, for example).\n\n- Text nodes: they contain the textual content inside elements.\n\n- Attribute nodes: although they are part of the DOM specification, in most modern APIs they are not represented as separate nodes in the tree but are properties of element nodes.\n\nThe nodes are linked by parent-child-sibling relationships, forming a hierarchy.\n\nAn example of HTML and the corresponding DOM\n\nConsider some simple HTML:\n\nThe DOM tree for this document will look roughly like this (in simplified form):\n\n- Document\n\n- ├── Element: <html>\n\n- │ ├── Element: <head>\n\n- │ │ └── Element: <title>\n\n- │ │ └── Text node: \"An example\"\n\n- │ └── Element: <body>\n\n- │ ├── Element: <h1>\n\n- │ │ └── Text node: \"A heading\"\n\n- │ └── Element: <p>\n\n- │ ├── Text node: \"A paragraph with \"\n\n- │ ├── Element: <strong>\n\n- │ │ └── Text node: \"bold\"\n\n- │ └── Text node: \" text.\"\n\nHow is the DOM used?\n\nJavaScript reaches the DOM through the global `document` object. With the methods of the DOM API (`getElementById`, `querySelector`, `createElement`, for example) you can find nodes, create new ones, change their properties and add them to or remove them from the tree. Any change to the DOM tree is reflected on the displayed page immediately (this process is called reflow and repaint).\n\nA practical code example\n\nConclusion: understanding the DOM tree is critically important for any client-side development in JavaScript, since it is the main way of making a web page interactive and dynamic. The DOM serves as the bridge between static HTML and a living, script-driven interface.",
    codeExample: "<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html>\n<head>\n    <title>An example</title>\n</head>\n<body>\n    <h1>A heading</h1>\n    <p>A paragraph with <strong>bold</strong> text.</p>\n</body>\n</html>\n\n// We find the element by its id\nconst header = document.getElementById('main-header');\n// We change its text content\nheader.textContent = 'A new heading';\n\n// We create a new element\nconst newParagraph = document.createElement('p');\nnewParagraph.textContent = 'This paragraph was added through JavaScript.';\n\n// We add it to the end of the document's body\ndocument.body.appendChild(newParagraph);\n\n// We change the element's style\nheader.style.color = 'blue';",
  },
  103: {
    source: "Что происходит при добавлении и удалении eventListener?",
    question: "What happens when you add and remove an event listener?",
    shortAnswer: "When you add an event listener, the handler function is registered for the given event on the element. When the event occurs, that function runs. Removing the event listener cancels the registration, and the function will no longer be called. It is important to remove listeners once they are no longer needed, especially on elements that are removed from the DOM, in order to avoid memory leaks.",
    longAnswer: "Event listeners are functions that \"listen\" for particular events (a click, a mouse hover, keyboard input) on DOM elements and run when those events occur. The mechanism of adding and removing them is key to the interactivity of web pages and to managing resources.\nAdding with addEventListener\nThe `addEventListener` method registers the handler function you pass for an event on the target element. You can add several handlers for the same event on the same element. The handler receives an event object (Event) containing useful information.\n\nRemoving with removeEventListener\nThe `removeEventListener` method removes a previously registered handler. For the removal to succeed you have to pass exactly the same arguments: the event type and a reference to the same function. Anonymous functions cannot be removed, so named functions are recommended.\n\nPractical use and important aspects\n\n- Managing memory: if an element is removed from the DOM but a listener on it remains, the function and the element may not be freed by the garbage collector (a memory leak). Always remove listeners before removing an element.\n- The options parameter: in modern JavaScript `addEventListener` takes a third parameter — an options object with settings such as `{ once: true }` (run the handler only once) or `{ passive: true }` (to improve scrolling performance).\n- The event phases: the third parameter may be a boolean `useCapture` value determining which phase (capture or bubble) the handler will be called in.\nConclusion: use `removeEventListener` for cleanup when an event handler is no longer needed (when a component is destroyed in an SPA framework, for example), in order to make the application's behaviour predictable and avoid memory leaks.",
    codeExample: "const button = document.getElementById('myButton');\nfunction handleClick(event) {\n    console.log('The button was clicked!', event.target);\n}\n// We add the listener\nbutton.addEventListener('click', handleClick);\n\n// We remove the listener\nbutton.removeEventListener('click', handleClick);\n// Now handleClick will no longer be called on a click.",
  },
  104: {
    source: "Какие библиотеки используются для карт (Leaflet, OpenLayers, Google Maps)?",
    question: "Which libraries are used for maps (Leaflet, OpenLayers, Google Maps)?",
    shortAnswer: "To embed maps in web applications, three main libraries are used most often: Leaflet, OpenLayers and the Google Maps API. Leaflet is a lightweight, easy-to-use open-source library, ideal for basic maps. OpenLayers is a more powerful, comprehensive solution for complex GIS tasks, also open-source. The Google Maps API is a proprietary service from Google that provides rich functionality but requires licensing for commercial use.",
    longAnswer: "In web development there are several key libraries for creating interactive maps, each with its own philosophy and area of application. The choice depends on the project's requirements: whether you need simplicity and speed, extended functionality for GIS, or integration with a popular service such as Google.\nLeaflet\nLeaflet is a lightweight (about 42 KB) open-source library that has become the standard for many projects thanks to its simplicity, good documentation and extensive community. It works excellently with tiled maps (OpenStreetMap, for example) and is easy to extend with plugins.\n\nOpenLayers\nOpenLayers is a powerful open-source library oriented towards complex cartographic tasks and GIS (geographic information systems). It supports an enormous number of data formats (vector tiles, KML, GeoJSON) and provides fine control over the display.\n\nThe Google Maps API\nThe Google Maps API is a proprietary service from Google that provides access to maps, streets, satellite imagery and a rich set of services (place search, routes). It requires an API key to be registered and, for intensive use, payment. Integration is simple but imposes licensing restrictions.\n\nConclusion: Leaflet is worth choosing for quickly introducing simple maps with open data. OpenLayers suits complex, customizable GIS applications. The Google Maps API is optimal when you need integration with Google's ecosystem (streets, business data) and a ready-made UI, and the project is prepared for the possible costs.",
    codeExample: "// An example of creating a simple map with Leaflet\nconst map = L.map('map').setView([51.505, -0.09], 13);\nL.tileLayer('https://{s}.tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png', {\n    attribution: '© OpenStreetMap contributors'\n}).addTo(map);\n// Adding a marker\nL.marker([51.5, -0.09]).addTo(map)\n    .bindPopup('An example marker.')\n    .openPopup();\n\n// An example of creating a map with OpenLayers\nimport Map from 'ol/Map';\nimport View from 'ol/View';\nimport TileLayer from 'ol/layer/Tile';\nimport OSM from 'ol/source/OSM';\n\nconst map = new Map({\n    target: 'map',\n    layers: [\n        new TileLayer({\n            source: new OSM()\n        })\n    ],\n    view: new View({\n        center: [0, 0],\n        zoom: 2\n    })\n});\n\n// An example of initializing a Google Maps map\nfunction initMap() {\n    const map = new google.maps.Map(document.getElementById(\"map\"), {\n        center: { lat: 51.505, lng: -0.09 },\n        zoom: 13,\n    });\n    new google.maps.Marker({\n        position: { lat: 51.5, lng: -0.09 },\n        map: map,\n        title: \"An example marker\"\n    });\n}",
  },
  105: {
    source: "В чем ограничения Canvas при отображении большого количества объектов?",
    question: "What are Canvas's limitations when displaying a large number of objects?",
    shortAnswer: "Canvas is a raster surface for drawing. The main limitation when working with a large number of objects is performance. Every object has to be redrawn every frame, which puts a heavy load on the CPU. There is no built-in system for culling invisible areas or optimizing repaints. For complex scenes it is better to use WebGL or SVG.",
    longAnswer: "HTML5 Canvas provides a low-level API for drawing graphics on the fly with JavaScript. Being essentially a raster canvas, however, it has a number of fundamental limitations when you try to display hundreds or thousands of dynamic objects.\nThe main performance limitations\n\n- No built-in DOM: unlike SVG, objects on a Canvas are not separate DOM elements. This means there is no automatic tracking of which object is where, and no events at the object level without additional logic.\n- A full repaint: whenever the scene changes (objects move), you normally have to clear the whole canvas or part of it and redraw every object again. This can become a bottleneck.\n- No hardware acceleration by default: standard 2D drawing (context2d) mostly uses the CPU. The canvas itself can be accelerated, but the drawing operations (fill, stroke) are not.\nPractical problems and an example\nImagine you are building a game with 10,000 particles. On every frame you have to update the position of every particle and draw it. The code might look like this:\n\nThis approach will quickly cause the frame rate to drop, because the browser performs thousands of drawing calls (arc, fill) on the CPU.\nWays of optimizing it and the alternatives\n\n- Using WebGL: for complex 2D/3D graphics, WebGL (through libraries such as Pixi.js or Three.js) uses the GPU, which increases performance dramatically.\n- Clipping: draw only the part of the scene that is visible in the viewport.\n- Combining objects: if objects are static, draw them once on an off-screen canvas and then copy the finished image.\n- Using sprite sheets to reduce the number of drawing calls.\nConclusion: pure Canvas 2D is poorly suited to displaying thousands of interactive, dynamically changing objects. For such tasks WebGL or a hybrid approach with optimized drawing is worth considering. Canvas is ideal for drawing images, simple animation or processing video, where the whole scene is rarely redrawn.",
    codeExample: "// A simplified example of an animation loop with particles\nconst particles = []; // An array of 10000 objects {x, y, vx, vy}\n\nfunction animate() {\n    // 1. Clearing the whole canvas\n    ctx.clearRect(0, 0, canvas.width, canvas.height);\n\n    // 2. Updating and drawing EVERY particle\n    for (let p of particles) {\n        p.x += p.vx;\n        p.y += p.vy;\n        ctx.beginPath();\n        ctx.arc(p.x, p.y, 2, 0, Math.PI * 2);\n        ctx.fill();\n    }\n    requestAnimationFrame(animate);\n}",
  },
  106: {
    source: "Какие подходы используются для работы с картами во фронтенде?",
    question: "What approaches are used for working with maps on the frontend?",
    shortAnswer: "For working with maps on the frontend, specialized JavaScript libraries are used most often. Leaflet is a lightweight, simple library for basic maps with tile support. Mapbox GL JS provides modern vector maps with high performance and customization. The Google Maps API is a popular commercial solution with extensive functionality, including search and routes. OpenLayers is a powerful library for complex GIS tasks with support for many data formats.",
    longAnswer: "Integrating maps into web interfaces has become the standard for a great many applications, from delivery services to analytics dashboards. The main task is to display geographic data, provide interactivity (zoom, panning, markers) and often to overlay your own layers of information. Ready-made libraries are used for this, which abstract away the complexities of working with tile servers, projections and rendering performance.\nPopular libraries\n\n- Leaflet: lightweight (about 42 KB) and easy to learn. Ideal for quickly introducing a basic map with markers, popups and vector layers (polygons, lines). It works with raster tiles from OpenStreetMap and other providers.\n- Mapbox GL JS: a modern library for vector maps. The maps are drawn on the client side using WebGL, which lets you style every element (roads, buildings) dynamically and animate transitions smoothly. It requires an access token.\n- The Google Maps JavaScript API: a commercial but very popular solution. It provides not only cartography but also ready-made services: Places (place search), Directions (route building) and Street View. Integration is simple but has limits and costs.\n- OpenLayers: a powerful library for professional GIS applications. It supports an enormous number of data sources (WMS, GeoJSON, KML) and complex operations on maps. Its barrier to entry is higher.\nA code example: a basic map with Leaflet\n\nThis code creates a map, adds a layer of OpenStreetMap tiles, a marker in the centre of Moscow and a simple polygon. Leaflet handles mouse and touch events for zooming and panning automatically.\nCriteria for choosing an approach\n\n- Leaflet: choose it for simple projects when you need lightness, open data (OSM) and minimal dependencies.\n- Mapbox GL JS: suits projects where custom map design, high performance with a large amount of dynamic data and a 3D view matter.\n- The Google Maps API: optimal if you need maps that users will recognize instantly, and integration with other Google services (search, routes, reviews).\n- OpenLayers: use it in complex analytical or enterprise systems where working with professional geodata and standards is necessary.\nConclusion: the choice of approach depends on the project's requirements: for a quick prototype or a simple map with markers, Leaflet will do; for creating a unique, high-performance cartographic interface, Mapbox GL JS; for using maps that are familiar to users with built-in services, the Google Maps API; and for complex GIS tasks, OpenLayers.",
    codeExample: "// 1. Attach Leaflet's CSS and JS in your HTML\n// 2. Initializing the map\nconst map = L.map('map-container').setView([55.751244, 37.618423], 10); // Moscow, zoom 10\n\n// 3. Adding a tile layer (we use OpenStreetMap)\nL.tileLayer('https://{s}.tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png', {\n    attribution: '© OpenStreetMap contributors'\n}).addTo(map);\n\n// 4. Adding a marker with a popup\nconst marker = L.marker([55.751244, 37.618423]).addTo(map);\nmarker.bindPopup(\"Kremlin<br>Moscow\").openPopup();\n\n// 5. Adding a simple polygon (a triangle)\nconst polygon = L.polygon([\n    [55.75, 37.60],\n    [55.75, 37.65],\n    [55.70, 37.62]\n]).addTo(map);\npolygon.bindPopup(\"I am a polygon.\");",
  },
  107: {
    source: "Как осуществляется работа с Figma в разработке интерфейсов?",
    question: "How is Figma used in interface development?",
    shortAnswer: "Figma is a cloud tool for interface design that lets designers create mockups, prototypes and design systems. Developers gain access to the mockups through shared links, where they can inspect elements: view styles (CSS properties), dimensions and spacing, and export assets (icons, images). To automate the process, plugins are often used that generate code or design-system tokens. This approach speeds up markup work and ensures the implementation matches the designer's intent.",
    longAnswer: "Figma has become the standard tool for designing user interfaces thanks to its collaborative, cloud-based nature. In the development process it serves as the single source of truth for visual design, prototyping and creating design systems.\nThe main stages of a developer's work with Figma\n\n- Access and familiarization: the designer grants access to the file through a link. The developer studies the structure of the mockups, the pages, the components and the state variants.\n- Inspecting elements: in inspect mode (the right-hand panel) you can select any layer and see its exact CSS properties: dimensions, spacing, colours, shadows, font parameters. This spares you from \"measuring\" pixels by hand.\n- Exporting assets: icons, illustrations and images can be exported in the formats (SVG, PNG, WebP) and resolutions you need, straight from the interface.\n- Working with the design system: if components and styles have been set up in Figma, the developer can see every variant of buttons, input fields and other elements, and also the tokens (variables) for colours, typography and spacing.\nIntegration into the development process\nFor deeper integration, Figma's plugins and API are used. Plugins can generate React components, CSS-in-JS objects or even whole themes for an application from the styles in Figma, for example. This reduces routine work and lowers the number of errors when transferring the design into code.\n\nFigma also lets you leave comments right on the mockup, which makes discussing details between team members easier without having to switch to other messengers.\nConclusion: working with Figma in interface development is a continuous process of inspecting, extracting data and communicating, which lets you translate a visual design into working code accurately and quickly, especially when there is a well-thought-out design system.",
    codeExample: "// An example: using colour tokens from Figma in CSS variables\n:root {\n  /* The colours taken from Figma's styles */\n  --color-primary: #4361ee;\n  --color-surface: #ffffff;\n  --text-primary: #2b2d42;\n}\n\n.button-primary {\n  background-color: var(--color-primary);\n  color: var(--color-surface);\n  padding: 12px 24px;\n  /* The padding values can match the spacing tokens from Figma */\n}",
  },
  108: {
    source: "Какие проблемы могут возникнуть при увеличении области нажатия у нескольких соседних кнопок?",
    question: "What problems can arise when you enlarge the hit area of several neighbouring buttons?",
    shortAnswer: "Enlarging the hit area of neighbouring buttons can make them overlap. A user pressing in the zone where they intersect may activate a button other than the one they wanted. This causes input errors and irritation and reduces the interface's accessibility, especially for people with motor impairments. It is important to design hit areas so that they do not intersect, providing clear boundaries between elements.",
    longAnswer: "A hit area is the invisible zone around a visual element (a button, for example) that responds to a click or a touch. Enlarging it improves accessibility, especially on mobile devices or for users with limited motor control. When several buttons are placed close together, however, enlarging their hit areas without regard to the boundaries creates serious problems.\nThe main problems\n\n- Unintended activation: the user may press in the zone where the areas overlap, and whichever button handled the event first will fire (this often depends on the order in the DOM or on z-index). This leads to errors and confusion.\n- Reduced usability: the interface becomes unpredictable, the user loses confidence in their actions, which increases the cognitive load and the time needed to complete a task.\n- Accessibility problems: for people using assistive technologies or with a tremor, clear boundaries between interactive elements are critically important. Overlapping areas make navigation practically impossible.\n- Difficulties in testing and maintenance: overlapping hit areas are hard to debug, since visually the buttons' boundaries may look correct while the logic of how they work is broken.\nA code example and the solution\nConsider an example of two neighbouring buttons with enlarged but overlapping hit areas.\n\nIn this example the `::after` pseudo-element extends each button's hit area by 10 pixels in every direction. If the buttons are placed right next to each other, those areas will intersect. The solution is to ensure a sufficient visual margin between the buttons that exceeds or equals the hit area's extension, or to design the hit areas so that they extend only in safe directions (for the left button, leftwards and up/down; for the right one, rightwards and up/down, for example).\nConclusion: enlarging the hit area is necessary to improve accessibility and convenience, but it should be done deliberately, avoiding overlap with neighbouring interactive elements. The key principle is to preserve a minimum safe distance between hit areas, or to use a logical separation of zones.",
    codeExample: "<style>\n  .btn {\n    padding: 10px 20px;\n    margin: 0; /* We removed the margins to make the problem obvious */\n    position: relative;\n  }\n  /* We enlarge the hit area with a pseudo-element */\n  .btn::after {\n    content: '';\n    position: absolute;\n    top: -10px;\n    bottom: -10px;\n    left: -10px;\n    right: -10px;\n    /* background: rgba(255,0,0,0.2); */ /* For visualization */\n  }\n</style>\n\n<button class=\"btn\" id=\"btn1\">Save</button>\n<button class=\"btn\" id=\"btn2\">Cancel</button>\n\n<script>\n  // On a click in the overlap zone only one button will fire\n  document.getElementById('btn1').addEventListener('click', () => {\n    console.log('\"Save\" was clicked');\n  });\n  document.getElementById('btn2').addEventListener('click', () => {\n    console.log('\"Cancel\" was clicked');\n  });\n</script>",
  },
  109: {
    source: "Как event используется в обработке касаний и что он содержит?",
    question: "How is the event used in handling touches, and what does it contain?",
    shortAnswer: "Touch events in JavaScript are represented by the TouchEvent object. It contains lists of touches (touches, targetTouches, changedTouches) that let you track several fingers at once. Every Touch object inside a list contains the coordinates (clientX, clientY), the touch's identifier and the target element. These events are necessary for building interactive interfaces on mobile devices, such as zooming, dragging or swipes.",
    longAnswer: "In web development for mobile devices and tablets, the standard mouse events (click, mousedown) are not well suited to handling multi-touch gestures. For that purpose JavaScript has a special kind of event — the TouchEvent. It is generated when a touch on a sensitive screen begins, moves and ends.\n\nThe contents of the TouchEvent object\n\nThe TouchEvent's key properties are three lists of `Touch` objects:\n\n- `touches`: the list of all the touches currently active on the screen.\n\n- `targetTouches`: the list of touches that began on the same target element as the current event.\n\n- `changedTouches`: the list of touches that changed and gave rise to this event (new fingers for `touchstart`, or removed ones for `touchend`, for example).\n\nThe Touch object\n\nEvery object in these lists represents a single touch by a finger and contains important data:\n\n- `identifier`: a unique numeric ID for tracking a particular finger throughout the gesture.\n\n- `target`: the DOM element the touch began on.\n\n- `clientX` / `clientY`: the touch's coordinates relative to the browser window (the viewport).\n\n- `pageX` / `pageY`: the coordinates relative to the whole document.\n\n- `screenX` / `screenY`: the coordinates relative to the device's screen.\n\n- `radiusX` / `radiusY`: the approximate dimensions of the touch area (for determining pressure, for example).\n\nThe event types and practical use\n\nThe main kinds of touch event:\n\n- `touchstart`: a finger has touched the screen.\n\n- `touchmove`: a finger is moving across the screen.\n\n- `touchend`: a finger has lifted off the screen.\n\n- `touchcancel`: the touch was interrupted by the system (an incoming call, for example).\n\nThese events are used to implement gestures: a swipe for navigation, pinch-to-zoom for scaling images, dragging interface elements.\n\nA code example\n\nA simple example of tracking a single touch in order to drag an element:\n\nConclusion: the TouchEvent object and its contents are the foundation for creating responsive, intuitive touch interfaces in mobile web applications. It is worth applying whenever you need precise control over multi-touch interactions that cannot be implemented through mouse events or standard CSS properties such as `touch-action`.",
    codeExample: "let draggable = document.getElementById('box');\nlet startX, startY, initialX, initialY;\n\n// Handling the start of the touch\ndraggable.addEventListener('touchstart', function(event) {\n  // We prevent the page from scrolling\n  event.preventDefault();\n  // We take the first touch from the list\n  let touch = event.touches[0];\n  // We record the starting coordinates of the touch and of the element\n  startX = touch.clientX;\n  startY = touch.clientY;\n  initialX = draggable.offsetLeft;\n  initialY = draggable.offsetTop;\n});\n\n// Handling the movement of the finger\ndraggable.addEventListener('touchmove', function(event) {\n  event.preventDefault();\n  let touch = event.touches[0];\n  // We compute the offset\n  let deltaX = touch.clientX - startX;\n  let deltaY = touch.clientY - startY;\n  // We apply the new position to the element\n  draggable.style.left = (initialX + deltaX) + 'px';\n  draggable.style.top = (initialY + deltaY) + 'px';\n});",
  },
  110: {
    source: "Как отследить появление клавиатуры?",
    question: "How do you detect the appearance of the keyboard?",
    shortAnswer: "On mobile devices the keyboard appears when an input field receives focus. This can be detected through a change in the dimensions of the visual viewport (the Visual Viewport API) or through the resize event on window. When the keyboard appears, the visual viewport shrinks in height. You can also listen for the focus and blur events on input elements in order to determine the moment indirectly.",
    longAnswer: "Detecting the appearance of the on-screen keyboard is a key task for building convenient mobile interfaces. When the user taps an input field, the system shows the keyboard, which changes the area in which content is viewed. There is no direct \"keyboardshow\" event on the web, so developers use indirect methods.\n\nThe main approaches to detection\n\nThe most reliable modern way is to use the Visual Viewport API. The visual viewport is the part of the window that is currently displayed on the screen (excluding the areas covered by the keyboard).\n\nAlternative and traditional methods\n\n- The resize event on window: in many mobile browsers the appearance of the keyboard triggers a resize event on the main window. This behaviour is not standardized, however, and can lead to false positives.\n\n- Tracking focus: you can listen for the focus and blur events on input fields. The keyboard's appearance normally follows focus immediately. This gives no information about exact dimensions but does indicate the context.\n\n- Checking the active element: combining document.activeElement (which points to the input field) with a change in the window's dimensions gives a more accurate picture.\n\nThese techniques should be applied in order to scroll to the active field automatically, change the layout (hiding the bottom navigation bar, for example) or switch the input into full-screen mode.\n\nConclusion: use the Visual Viewport API for the most accurate and modern detection of changes to the viewing area caused by the keyboard, especially when knowing the remaining space precisely is critical for adapting the UI.",
    codeExample: "// An example using the Visual Viewport API\nif (window.visualViewport) {\n  window.visualViewport.addEventListener('resize', function(event) {\n    const newHeight = event.target.height;\n    const windowHeight = window.innerHeight;\n    // If the visual viewport's height is considerably less than the window's height,\n    // the keyboard is probably open\n    if (newHeight < windowHeight * 0.7) {\n      console.log('The keyboard has probably appeared');\n      // Here you could, for example, scroll the input field into view\n    } else {\n      console.log('The keyboard is probably hidden');\n    }\n  });\n}",
  },
  111: {
    source: "В чем разница между синхронными и асинхронными HTTP-запросами?",
    question: "What is the difference between synchronous and asynchronous HTTP requests?",
    shortAnswer: "A synchronous request blocks the execution of the code until a response arrives from the server, freezing the user interface. An asynchronous request is sent and performed in the background, letting the main thread of code carry on working and handling other events. Once the server's response arrives, a special handler function (a callback) is called, which keeps the application responsive. Synchronous requests are considered obsolete and are hardly ever used in modern frontend work.",
    longAnswer: "HTTP requests are the basis of a web application's interaction with the server. By the way they are performed they fall into synchronous and asynchronous, and this difference directly affects performance and the user experience.\n\nSynchronous requests\n\nA synchronous (or blocking) request stops the script's execution at the place where it was called. The browser \"freezes\" — the user cannot scroll the page, press buttons or interact with the interface until the server sends a response. This approach was widespread in the early days of the web, but nowadays it is considered an anti-pattern because of its catastrophic effect on UX.\n\nAsynchronous requests\n\nAn asynchronous (non-blocking) request is sent and control returns to the main thread of execution immediately. The browser carries on handling user events, animation and other code. When the server replies, a predefined handler function (a callback) fires, a Promise resolves, or the code after the `await` keyword runs. This is the basis of AJAX technology and of modern SPA frameworks.\n\nWhere they are applied\n\n- Asynchronous requests are used everywhere: loading data for a news feed, submitting forms without reloading the page, search autocompletion, infinite scroll.\n\n- Synchronous requests have practically been banished from frontend development. In rare cases you may meet them in utility scripts or specific server environments where blocking is acceptable.\n\nConclusion: always use asynchronous HTTP requests in client-side JavaScript. It is a mandatory condition for building smooth, responsive web applications that do not make the user wait. The synchronous approach is an archaic method that leads to a \"hanging\" interface.",
    codeExample: "// An example of a synchronous request (DO NOT USE THIS IN PRACTICE)\nconst request = new XMLHttpRequest();\nrequest.open('GET', '/api/data', false); // The third parameter 'false' makes the request synchronous\nrequest.send();\n// The code here will not run until the response arrives\nconsole.log(request.responseText);\n\n// An example of an asynchronous request using the Fetch API and async/await\nasync function fetchData() {\n  try {\n    // The code is not blocked while the request is in flight\n    const response = await fetch('/api/data');\n    const data = await response.json();\n    console.log('The data has arrived:', data);\n    // We update the interface with the new data\n  } catch (error) {\n    console.error('Request error:', error);\n  }\n}\n// We call the function. The interface stays responsive.\nfetchData();\nconsole.log('This line will run straight away, without waiting for the response from the server.');",
  },
  112: {
    source: "Как preload и prefetch влияют на загрузку?",
    question: "How do preload and prefetch affect loading?",
    shortAnswer: "The preload and prefetch attributes are hints to the browser telling it how to load resources in advance. Preload tells the browser to load a critically important resource (a font or a script, for example) immediately, because it will be needed on the current page. Prefetch suggests that the browser load a resource for future navigation (for the next page, for example) in the background, when there is spare network bandwidth. This lets you speed up the display of the current page and make transitions between pages faster.",
    longAnswer: "The `preload` and `prefetch` attributes are mechanisms provided by modern browsers for optimizing the loading of resources. They let a developer tell the browser explicitly which files should be loaded in advance, before they are needed, which can improve a site's perceived performance considerably.\n\nPreload for critical resources\n\nThe `preload` attribute is used for resources that are critically important to the current page and will be used in the near future. The browser loads such a resource with a high priority without blocking the page's painting. This is especially useful for fonts, scripts or styles that are discovered late (through JavaScript, for example).\n\nIn this example we preload a font, stating the resource's type through the `as` attribute, which helps the browser set the priorities correctly and apply its security policies (CORS, for example).\n\nPrefetch for future navigation\n\nThe `prefetch` attribute is intended for resources that will be needed on later pages the user may navigate to. The browser loads such resources with a low priority when the network is idle, so as not to interfere with the loading of the current page.\n\nThis approach is often used in single-page applications (SPAs) to preload the code of sections the user may reach.\n\nPractical use and the differences\n\n- Preload is applied to the current page: for fonts, critical CSS/JS, images above the fold.\n\n- Prefetch is applied to future pages or actions: for loading the code of the next route in an SPA, or gallery images that may be needed.\n\n- The key difference is in the priority: `preload` is high, `prefetch` is low.\n\nConclusion: use `preload` to speed up the loading of critical resources on the current page, and `prefetch` to improve the speed of navigation between pages, especially in SPAs. These are simple but powerful tools for optimizing performance.",
    codeExample: "<link rel=\"preload\" href=\"critical-font.woff2\" as=\"font\" type=\"font/woff2\" crossorigin>\n\n<link rel=\"prefetch\" href=\"next-page-bundle.js\" as=\"script\">",
  },
  113: {
    source: "Что такое валидация и санитизация данных, и чем они отличаются?",
    question: "What are validation and sanitization of data, and how do they differ?",
    shortAnswer: "Validation checks whether data matches the rules you have set (the format of an email address, for example). Sanitization cleans or transforms data to remove dangerous elements (HTML tags, for example). Validation answers the question \"is the data correct?\", while sanitization answers \"is the data safe?\".",
    longAnswer: "The main concepts\nValidation and sanitization are two key processes in handling data, especially when working with user input. Validation checks whether the data meets particular criteria (the length of a string, the format of an email address, a range of numbers). Sanitization, by contrast, is aimed at removing or transforming potentially dangerous parts of the data, such as HTML tags or SQL injections.\nExamples and the differences\nImagine a registration form with a field for a user name. Validation might check that the name contains only letters and digits and that its length is between 3 and 20 characters. Sanitization will remove any HTML tags the user may have entered, in order to prevent XSS attacks.\n\nWhere it is applied\n\n- Web forms (registration, comments)\n- API endpoints (checking the input parameters)\n- Databases (protection against SQL injection)\nValidation is normally performed first, in order to filter out data that is obviously incorrect. Sanitization is applied afterwards, in order to make the data safe before it is stored or displayed.\nConclusion: validation and sanitization are complementary practices. Validation guarantees that the data has the expected format, while sanitization protects the system from malicious content. Use them together for reliable handling of user input.",
    codeExample: "// An example of validation in JavaScript\nfunction validateUsername(name) {\n  const regex = /^[a-zA-Z0-9]{3,20}$/;\n  return regex.test(name);\n}\n\n// An example of sanitization (removing HTML tags)\nfunction sanitizeInput(input) {\n  return input.replace(/<[^>]*>/g, '');\n}",
  },
  114: {
    source: "Как работает парсинг URL и зачем он нужен?",
    question: "How does the parsing of a URL work and what is it for?",
    shortAnswer: "Parsing a URL means taking an address apart into its constituent pieces: the protocol, the domain, the port, the path, the parameters and the anchor. It is needed to extract data from the address bar, for navigation and for handling requests. In JavaScript, for example, the URL object lets you get at these components easily.",
    longAnswer: "What is URL parsing?\nParsing a URL (Uniform Resource Locator) is the process of taking an address string apart into its logical components, such as the protocol, the host, the port, the path, the query parameters and the fragment. It is a fundamental operation in web development, since browsers and servers use URLs to identify resources and pass data.\nThe main components of a URL\n\n- The protocol (scheme) — http, https or ftp, for example.\n- The host — a domain name or an IP address.\n- The port — the port number (80 by default for HTTP, 443 for HTTPS).\n- The path — the path to the resource on the server.\n- The query parameters (the query string) — a set of key=value pairs, beginning with ?.\n- The fragment — the part after #, used for anchors on the page.\nAn example of parsing in JavaScript\n\nWhy is URL parsing needed?\nURL parsing is used to:\n\n- Extract data from the address bar (search parameters, for example).\n- Validate and normalize a URL before sending a request.\n- Navigate in single-page applications (SPAs) with React Router or Vue Router.\n- Create links with dynamic parameters.\nConclusion: URL parsing is a basic technique necessary for working with web addresses. It is applied in client-side and server-side applications for handling requests, routing and passing data. Understanding a URL's structure helps you avoid errors when working with HTTP and improves an application's security.",
    codeExample: "const url = new URL('https://example.com:8080/path/page?name=John&age=30#section');\nconsole.log(url.protocol); // 'https:'\nconsole.log(url.hostname); // 'example.com'\nconsole.log(url.port); // '8080'\nconsole.log(url.pathname); // '/path/page'\nconsole.log(url.search); // '?name=John&age=30'\nconsole.log(url.hash); // '#section'\n// Getting the parameters\nconsole.log(url.searchParams.get('name')); // 'John'",
  },
  115: {
    source: "Какие есть способы защиты от XSS?",
    question: "What ways are there of protecting against XSS?",
    shortAnswer: "XSS (cross-site scripting) is an attack in which an attacker injects a malicious script into a web page. The main means of protection are: escaping data on output, validating and sanitizing input, using a Content Security Policy (CSP), and setting the HttpOnly flag on cookies. It is also important to avoid dangerous functions such as innerHTML and to use safe template engines.",
    longAnswer: "What XSS is and why it matters\nXSS (Cross-Site Scripting) is a kind of attack in which an attacker injects malicious JavaScript code into a web page, which is then executed in the victim's browser. This can lead to cookies being stolen, redirection to phishing sites or the page's content being changed. Protection against XSS is critical for any web application that works with user data.\nThe main methods of protection\n\n- Output escaping: converting special characters (`<`, `>`, `&`, for example) into their HTML entities before they are inserted into the page. This prevents the data from being interpreted as code.\n- Validating and sanitizing input: checking and cleaning the data from the user on the server. Removing `<script>` tags, for example, or using a whitelist of allowed tags.\n- Content Security Policy (CSP): an HTTP header that restricts the sources of scripts, styles and other resources. `Content-Security-Policy: script-src 'self'`, for example, permits only scripts from the same domain to run.\n- The HttpOnly flag on cookies: setting the `HttpOnly` flag when creating a cookie prevents access to it through JavaScript, which protects session data from being stolen.\n- Avoiding dangerous functions: do not use `innerHTML`, `document.write()` or `eval()` with user data. Use `textContent` or safe template engines instead (React escapes its output automatically, for example).\nA code example\n\nConclusion\nProtection against XSS requires a comprehensive approach: escaping output, validating input, using CSP and safe APIs. These methods matter especially when developing applications with user-generated content (forms, comments, chats). Applying these practices reduces the risk of attacks and increases users' trust.",
    codeExample: "// Dangerous code (vulnerable to XSS)\nconst userInput = \"<script>alert('XSS')</script>\";\ndocument.getElementById('output').innerHTML = userInput;\n\n// Safe code (escaping)\nconst safeOutput = userInput.replace(/&/g, '&amp;').replace(/</g, '&lt;').replace(/>/g, '&gt;');\ndocument.getElementById('output').textContent = safeOutput;\n\n// An example of CSP in HTML\n// <meta http-equiv=\"Content-Security-Policy\" content=\"script-src 'self'\">",
  },
  116: {
    source: "Какие инструменты используются для анализа ошибок в браузере (например, Chrome DevTools)?",
    question: "Which tools are used to analyse errors in the browser (Chrome DevTools, for example)?",
    shortAnswer: "Chrome DevTools is a built-in set of tools for debugging web applications. It includes the console for viewing errors, the Network tab for analysing network requests, Sources for debugging JavaScript and Elements for inspecting the DOM. These tools help developers find and fix errors quickly.",
    longAnswer: "What is Chrome DevTools?\nChrome DevTools is a set of developer tools built into the Google Chrome browser. It lets you analyse and debug web pages, uncover JavaScript errors, optimize performance and study network requests. DevTools is indispensable to frontend developers when building and maintaining web applications.\nThe main tools for analysing errors\n\n- The Console — displays JavaScript errors, warnings and logs. It lets you execute code in real time.\n- Sources — provides access to the source code and lets you set breakpoints and step through the code.\n- Network — shows every network request, its status, loading time and headers. It helps you uncover problems with APIs or resources.\n- Elements — lets you inspect and edit HTML and CSS in real time, which is useful for checking styles.\nAn example of use\nSuppose the data on a page is not loading. Open DevTools (F12), go to the Network tab and reload the page. You will see a list of requests. If the request to the API is marked in red (status 404 or 500), that indicates an error on the server. An error message with details may appear in the console at the same time.\n\nIn the DevTools console you will see the error's text if the request failed. Using the Sources tab, you can set a breakpoint inside the .catch() and check the value of error.\nConclusion\nChrome DevTools is the main tool for debugging web applications. Knowing it is mandatory for any frontend developer, since it speeds up the finding and fixing of errors and also helps optimize performance.",
    codeExample: "// An example of code that may cause an error\nfetch('https://api.example.com/data')\n  .then(response => response.json())\n  .then(data => console.log(data))\n  .catch(error => console.error('Error:', error));",
  },
  117: {
    source: "Как тестировать поле ввода даты?",
    question: "How do you test a date input field?",
    shortAnswer: "Testing a date input field involves checking the correct formats (DD.MM.YYYY, for example), boundary values (29 February in a leap year), invalid dates (31 April) and special cases (past/future dates). It is also important to check the handling of an empty field, the entry of characters, and localization. Use both manual and automated testing with various scenarios.",
    longAnswer: "The main aspects of testing a date input field\nTesting a date input field requires checking a great many scenarios in order to be sure that the user's input is handled correctly. This includes validating the format, boundary values, error handling and conformity with the business logic.\nChecking the formats and validation\nMake sure the field accepts only the expected date formats (DD.MM.YYYY or YYYY-MM-DD, for example). Check entry with separators (dots, slashes, dashes) and without them. An example test:\n\nBoundary values and special cases\n\n- Check 29 February in a leap year and in a non-leap year.\n- Test 31 April, 30 February and other dates that do not exist.\n- Include a check of the minimum and maximum permissible date (not earlier than 1900, for example).\nError handling and UX\nMake sure that a clear error message is displayed when an incorrect date is entered. Check the behaviour with an empty field and with letters or special characters entered. It is also important to test localization (different date formats in different countries).\nAutomating the testing\nFor automation use tools such as Selenium or Cypress. An example test in Cypress:\n\nConclusion: testing a date input field is necessary for ensuring the application works correctly, preventing input errors and improving the user experience. It matters especially in systems involving booking, finance or scheduling.",
    codeExample: "// An example of checking the format in JavaScript\nfunction isValidDate(dateString) {\n  const regex = /^\\d{2}\\.\\d{2}\\.\\d{4}$/;\n  return regex.test(dateString);\n}\nconsole.log(isValidDate('31.12.2023')); // true\nconsole.log(isValidDate('2023-12-31')); // false\n\ncy.get('input[type=\"date\"]')\n  .type('2023-12-31')\n  .should('have.value', '2023-12-31');\ncy.get('input[type=\"date\"]')\n  .clear()\n  .type('invalid-date')\n  .should('have.class', 'error');",
  },
  118: {
    source: "Использовался ли logcat и для чего он нужен?",
    question: "Have you used logcat, and what is it for?",
    shortAnswer: "Logcat is a utility for viewing Android's system logs. It lets developers track messages from applications, errors and system events. It is used for debugging through Android Studio or the adb command line.",
    longAnswer: "What is logcat?\nLogcat is a command-line tool and a built-in feature of Android Studio that collects and displays system messages, including application logs, errors, warnings and debugging information. It is necessary for diagnosing problems in an application's operation, such as crashes, memory leaks or incorrect behaviour.\nHow do you use logcat?\nLogcat can be launched through Android Studio in the Logcat panel, or through the terminal with the command `adb logcat`. You can filter the messages by severity level (VERBOSE, DEBUG, INFO, WARN, ERROR) and by the tags that are set in the application's code.\nAn example of use in code\n\nConclusion\nLogcat is an indispensable tool for debugging Android applications, letting you find and fix errors quickly. It is worth using at every stage of development to monitor an application's behaviour.",
    codeExample: "import android.util.Log;\n\npublic class MainActivity extends AppCompatActivity {\n    private static final String TAG = \"MainActivity\";\n\n    @Override\n    protected void onCreate(Bundle savedInstanceState) {\n        super.onCreate(savedInstanceState);\n        setContentView(R.layout.activity_main);\n        \n        Log.d(TAG, \"Activity created\");\n        try {\n            // some code\n        } catch (Exception e) {\n            Log.e(TAG, \"Error occurred\", e);\n        }\n    }\n}",
  },
  119: {
    source: "Как тестировать приложения с учетом различий между iOS и Android?",
    question: "How do you test applications while allowing for the differences between iOS and Android?",
    shortAnswer: "Testing mobile applications requires allowing for the differences between the operating systems in navigation, permissions, notifications and performance. For iOS, Xcode and the simulator are used; for Android, Android Studio and the emulator. It is important to test on real devices because of the differences in screens, OS versions and hardware characteristics. The different approaches to memory management and power consumption are also taken into account.",
    longAnswer: "The main differences between iOS and Android in testing\nWhen testing mobile applications you have to allow for the fundamental differences between the iOS and Android platforms. iOS has a closed ecosystem with a limited number of devices, which makes compatibility testing easier but requires strict adherence to Apple's guidelines. Android, by contrast, is open and fragmented: many manufacturers, OS versions and screen sizes. This requires more extensive testing on different devices.\nTools and approaches\nFor iOS testing, Xcode with XCTest and the simulator are used, and the simulator does not emulate a real device's behaviour fully (sensors or performance, for example). For Android it is Android Studio with Espresso and the emulator, which imitates real conditions better. It is recommended to combine automated testing (UI tests, unit tests) with manual testing on real devices.\nExample code for a UI test on iOS (XCTest)\n\nExample code for a UI test on Android (Espresso)\n\nThe key aspects of testing\n\n- Navigation and gestures: iOS uses gestures (swipes, 3D Touch), Android uses buttons and gestures (Back, for example).\n- Permissions: iOS asks for permissions at the moment of use, Android at installation or while running.\n- Notifications: different APIs and behaviour (the grouping of notifications, for example).\n- Performance: iOS is optimized for particular hardware, Android requires testing on various chipsets.\nConclusion: testing mobile applications has to allow for the platforms' particulars, use specialized tools and always include tests on real devices in order to uncover problems that are invisible in simulators.",
    codeExample: "import XCTest\n\nclass LoginTests: XCTestCase {\n    let app = XCUIApplication()\n\n    override func setUp() {\n        super.setUp()\n        continueAfterFailure = false\n        app.launch()\n    }\n\n    func testLoginSuccess() {\n        let usernameField = app.textFields[\"username\"]\n        usernameField.tap()\n        usernameField.typeText(\"testuser\")\n\n        let passwordField = app.secureTextFields[\"password\"]\n        passwordField.tap()\n        passwordField.typeText(\"password123\")\n\n        app.buttons[\"login\"].tap()\n        XCTAssertTrue(app.staticTexts[\"Welcome\"].exists)\n    }\n}\n\nimport androidx.test.espresso.Espresso.onView\nimport androidx.test.espresso.action.ViewActions.*\nimport androidx.test.espresso.matcher.ViewMatchers.*\nimport androidx.test.ext.junit.rules.ActivityScenarioRule\nimport org.junit.Rule\nimport org.junit.Test\n\nclass LoginTest {\n    @get:Rule\n    val activityRule = ActivityScenarioRule(LoginActivity::class.java)\n\n    @Test\n    fun testLoginSuccess() {\n        onView(withId(R.id.username)).perform(typeText(\"testuser\"))\n        onView(withId(R.id.password)).perform(typeText(\"password123\"))\n        onView(withId(R.id.login_button)).perform(click())\n        onView(withText(\"Welcome\")).check(matches(isDisplayed()))\n    }\n}",
  },
  120: {
    source: "Какие инструменты используются для перехвата трафика (например, Charles)?",
    question: "Which tools are used to intercept traffic (Charles, for example)?",
    shortAnswer: "To intercept traffic, proxy tools such as Charles Proxy, Fiddler and Wireshark are used. They let you view, modify and analyse HTTP/HTTPS requests and responses. This helps debug APIs and check headers, cookies and request bodies. Charles is especially popular among mobile application developers.",
    longAnswer: "What is traffic interception and what is it for?\nIntercepting traffic is the process of monitoring and analysing the network requests between a client (a browser, a mobile application) and a server. It is necessary for debugging, security testing, optimizing performance and understanding how an API works.\nThe main tools\n\n- Charles Proxy — a popular tool for macOS and Windows. It lets you intercept HTTP/HTTPS traffic, modify requests and responses in real time, and emulate slow connections.\n- Fiddler — a powerful proxy for Windows that supports scripting and extended analysis.\n- Wireshark — a packet analyser for studying network protocols in depth; it works at a low level.\nAn example of using Charles Proxy\nSetting Charles up to intercept HTTPS traffic from a mobile device:\n\nOnce it is set up you can see every request, its headers, its body and the response status. When debugging a mobile application, for example, you can check what data is being sent to the server and what comes back in response.\nConclusion\nTraffic interception tools are indispensable for debugging client-server interaction, especially when developing mobile applications and web services. They help you find errors in an API quickly, check security and optimize requests.",
    codeExample: "1. Install Charles on your computer.\n2. Turn on SSL Proxying: Proxy -> SSL Proxying Settings.\n3. Add an entry for *.* (all hosts).\n4. Configure the proxy on the device: the computer's IP, port 8888.\n5. Install the Charles certificate on the device in order to decrypt HTTPS.\n6. Now every request is displayed in Charles.",
  },
  121: {
    source: "Какие ограничения есть у симуляторов?",
    question: "What limitations do simulators have?",
    shortAnswer: "Simulators cannot fully reproduce the hardware characteristics of real devices, such as processor performance or the behaviour of sensors or the battery. They also do not support some hardware-related APIs, the camera or GPS for example. This can mean that bugs related to the real environment go unnoticed. Simulators are therefore good for initial testing but do not replace tests on real devices.",
    longAnswer: "The limitations of simulators\nSimulators are software tools that imitate the behaviour of a real device or system, but they have a number of limitations that are important to bear in mind during development and testing. The main problem is that simulators cannot reproduce hardware characteristics accurately, such as processor speed, the amount of RAM or the behaviour of sensors (the accelerometer, the gyroscope). This means an application's performance in a simulator may differ greatly from a real device.\nExamples of the limitations\n\n- No support for real hardware APIs: the camera, GPS, Bluetooth or NFC, for example, may be unavailable or work incorrectly.\n- Differences in network behaviour: simulators often use the host's network, which does not reflect the real conditions of a mobile connection.\n- Limitations as to battery life: simulators do not emulate power consumption, so you cannot check how an application affects the battery charge.\nA practical example\nConsider testing an iOS application using the Xcode simulator. Code for working with the camera might look like this:\n\nIn the simulator this code will not be able to access the camera, which can hide errors in the video-handling logic. On a real device the camera will work, but problems with resolution or focus may arise that the simulator will not show.\nConclusion\nSimulators are useful for quick prototyping and for testing an application's logic, but to uncover problems related to hardware, performance and real conditions of use, testing on physical devices is necessary. Use simulators for the initial check, but always supplement them with tests on real hardware.",
    codeExample: "import UIKit\nimport AVFoundation\n\nclass CameraViewController: UIViewController {\n    var captureSession: AVCaptureSession?\n    \n    override func viewDidLoad() {\n        super.viewDidLoad()\n        // In the simulator AVCaptureDevice.default(for: .video) will return nil\n        guard let camera = AVCaptureDevice.default(for: .video) else {\n            print(\"The camera is unavailable in the simulator\")\n            return\n        }\n        // The further configuration of the session\n    }\n}",
  },
  122: {
    source: "Как проверять соответствие верстки макетам?",
    question: "How do you check that markup matches the mockups?",
    shortAnswer: "To check that markup matches the mockups, tools for overlaying the mockup on top of the page are used — PixelPerfect or PerfectPixel, for example. You can also compare screenshots in Figma or use the browser's built-in tools. It is important to check the spacing, font sizes, colours and the position of elements.",
    longAnswer: "The main methods of checking markup\nChecking that markup matches the mockups is the process of comparing the finished HTML/CSS code with the design mockup created in Figma, Sketch or Adobe XD. The main goal is to be sure that every element is positioned correctly and that the spacing, dimensions, colours and typography have been observed.\nTools for checking\n\n- PixelPerfect — a browser extension that lets you overlay an image of the mockup on top of the page and adjust its transparency.\n- PerfectPixel — a similar tool; it supports several layers and automatic alignment.\n- Figma Mirror — viewing the mockup on a mobile device in order to check responsiveness.\n- The browser's built-in tools — the element inspector for measuring spacing and dimensions.\nAn example of using PixelPerfect\n\nWhat to check\n\n- The spacing (margin/padding) between blocks\n- Font sizes and line heights\n- The colours of backgrounds, text and borders\n- The position of elements relative to one another\n- Responsiveness at different resolutions\nConclusion: using tools for pixel-perfect checking helps you spot discrepancies quickly and reduces the time spent on corrections. It is especially useful at the markup stage before handing the project over to the designer or the client.",
    codeExample: "// Install the PixelPerfect extension for Chrome\n// Open the page with your markup\n// Click the extension's icon and upload a screenshot of the mockup\n// Adjust the transparency and position in order to compare the layers",
  },
  123: {
    source: "Что такое Perfect Pixel и как он используется?",
    question: "What is Perfect Pixel and how is it used?",
    shortAnswer: "Perfect Pixel is an approach in which a web page's markup matches the design mockup exactly, to the pixel. It is used to achieve a high degree of accuracy in the visual presentation, especially in projects with strict design requirements. To do this, developers use tools for overlaying the mockup on top of the page and check the dimensions, spacing and fonts.",
    longAnswer: "What is Perfect Pixel?\nPerfect Pixel (or Pixel Perfect) is a markup method in which every element of a web page matches the design mockup (normally in PSD or Figma format) visually, to within a single pixel. This means the dimensions, spacing, colours, fonts and the position of elements all have to be identical to the mockup.\nHow is it used?\nThe developer opens the mockup in a graphics editor or uses a special browser extension (PixelPerfect or PerfectPixel, for example) that overlays a semi-transparent image of the mockup on top of the finished page. They then compare the elements and adjust the CSS properties until they match exactly.\nAn example of the approach\nSuppose that in the mockup a button is 120x40 pixels in size, with a left margin of 20px and a 14px Arial font. In code it will look like this:\n\nOnce the markup is done, the developer switches the extension on, overlays the mockup and checks whether the button matches the image. If it does not, they adjust the values.\nConclusion\nPerfect Pixel is useful in projects where the design is strictly prescribed and a high degree of accuracy is required — in landing pages or corporate sites, for example. It can slow development down, however, so it is worth applying selectively, when it is genuinely necessary.",
    codeExample: ".button {\n  width: 120px;\n  height: 40px;\n  margin-left: 20px;\n  font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\n  font-size: 14px;\n}",
  },
  124: {
    source: "Какие инструменты используются для проверки пиксель-перфекта?",
    question: "Which tools are used to check pixel perfection?",
    shortAnswer: "Pixel perfect is an approach in which the markup matches the design mockup exactly. To check it, browser extensions such as PixelPerfect or PerfectPixel are used, which overlay the mockup on top of the page. Tools such as Zeplin and Figma (comparison mode) are also applied, along with automated solutions such as Percy or Applitools.",
    longAnswer: "What pixel perfect is and why it is needed\nPixel perfect is an approach in frontend development in which the final web page matches the design mockup visually down to every pixel. This matters especially in projects with strict design requirements — in corporate sites or online shops, for example, where the brand has to look consistent.\nThe main tools for checking\n\n- Browser extensions: PerfectPixel (Chrome), PixelPerfect (Firefox) — they let you upload an image of the mockup and overlay it on the page with adjustable transparency.\n- Design tools: Figma, Zeplin, Avocode — they have built-in comparison modes or export with CSS properties.\n- Automated services: Percy, Applitools, Chromatic — they perform visual regression testing, comparing screenshots of pages against a reference.\nAn example of using the PerfectPixel extension\n\nWhen to apply pixel perfect\nThis approach is justified in projects with a fixed design and high demands for accuracy. For responsive or prototype sites, semantic markup without strict pixel control is enough. Conclusion: pixel perfect is a useful technique for a final check but is not always mandatory; use it when an exact match to the mockup is required.",
    codeExample: "// Install the PerfectPixel extension from the Chrome Web Store\n// Open the page and click the extension's icon\n// Upload the PNG mockup and adjust the transparency (usually 50%)\n// Compare the position of the elements: spacing, font sizes, colours\n// Edit the CSS in the browser's inspector if necessary",
  },
  125: {
    source: "Какие браузеры необходимо учитывать при тестировании?",
    question: "Which browsers do you need to take into account when testing?",
    shortAnswer: "When testing you need to take into account the browsers your target audience uses: Google Chrome, Mozilla Firefox, Safari, Microsoft Edge and possibly Opera. It is important to check the latest stable versions and also the previous one or two versions of each browser. For mobile devices the mobile versions of Safari and Chrome are added. The decision about supporting old browsers such as Internet Explorer is made on the basis of usage analytics.",
    longAnswer: "Which browsers to take into account when testing\nThe choice of browsers for testing depends on the target audience and on usage statistics. The main browsers normally included in testing are: Google Chrome, Mozilla Firefox, Apple Safari and Microsoft Edge. Opera may also be required and, in rare cases, Internet Explorer (if your audience uses it).\nThe criteria for choosing\n\n- Market share: use analytics data (Google Analytics, StatCounter) to determine which browsers are popular among your users.\n- Versions: test the latest stable version and the previous one or two versions of each browser.\n- Mobile browsers: take the mobile versions of Safari (iOS) and Chrome (Android) into account, and also built-in browsers.\n- Old browsers: if support for old versions is required (IE11, for example), include them in your testing, but this increases the cost.\nAn example approach\nFor a web application with a wide audience you can use the following list:\n\nIt is also recommended to use tools for emulating old browsers, such as BrowserStack or Sauce Labs.\nConclusion\nThe choice of browsers for testing should be based on data about your audience and on the project's requirements. The optimum is to test the latest versions of the popular browsers and the mobile versions, in order to ensure a good user experience.",
    codeExample: "Browsers for testing:\n- Chrome (the latest version)\n- Firefox (the latest version)\n- Safari (the latest version)\n- Edge (the latest version)\n- Mobile Safari (iOS)\n- Mobile Chrome (Android)",
  },
  126: {
    source: "Как тестировать адаптивность интерфейса?",
    question: "How do you test an interface's responsiveness?",
    shortAnswer: "An interface's responsiveness is tested by emulating devices in the browser, changing the window's dimensions and checking on real devices. The developer tools of Chrome and Firefox and online services are used. It is important to check that the content displays correctly, that the media queries work and that there is no horizontal scrolling.",
    longAnswer: "The main approaches to testing responsiveness\nTesting an interface's responsiveness is the process of checking how a web page displays and functions on various devices with different screen sizes, resolutions and orientations. The main goal is to be sure that the user interface stays convenient and functional on every device, from mobile phones to wide-format monitors.\nTools and methods\n\n- Emulating devices in the browser: the developer tools of Chrome and Firefox let you emulate various devices and change the screen resolution, the DPI and the orientation. This is a quick way of checking basic responsiveness.\n- Changing the browser window's dimensions: a simple but effective method is to change the browser window's width by hand in order to see how the interface responds to a change in size.\n- Real devices: testing on physical devices (smartphones, tablets, laptops) gives the most accurate results, since it takes the particulars of real browsers and operating systems into account.\n- Online services: services such as BrowserStack, Sauce Labs or LambdaTest provide access to real devices and browsers in the cloud for cross-browser testing.\nWhat to check\n\n- Media queries: be sure that the CSS media queries are applied correctly for the various resolutions.\n- No horizontal scrolling: the content must not extend beyond the screen horizontally.\n- The readability of text: the font size and line height have to be comfortable to read.\n- The behaviour of interactive elements: buttons, links and forms must be reachable by tapping on touch screens.\n- Images and media: images have to scale and must not be distorted.\nExample code for checking media queries\n\nThis example shows how media queries can be used to change the position of elements depending on the screen's width. When testing, it is important to check that each block of code is applied in the right range of resolutions.\nConclusion\nTesting responsiveness is necessary in order to ensure a good user experience on every device. Use a combination of emulation, manual testing and checks on real devices in order to uncover and fix problems before the product is released.",
    codeExample: "/* CSS media queries for different devices */\n@media (max-width: 768px) {\n  .container {\n    flex-direction: column;\n  }\n}\n\n@media (min-width: 769px) and (max-width: 1024px) {\n  .container {\n    flex-direction: row;\n    flex-wrap: wrap;\n  }\n}\n\n@media (min-width: 1025px) {\n  .container {\n    flex-direction: row;\n  }\n}",
  },
  127: {
    source: "Чем breakpoint отличается от rewrite?",
    question: "How does a breakpoint differ from a rewrite?",
    shortAnswer: "A breakpoint pauses the execution of code at the place you specify, letting you analyse the program's state step by step. A rewrite replaces the content of an HTTP response or request on the fly without changing the source code. A breakpoint is used to debug logic, while a rewrite is used to test various scenarios for the server's responses.",
    longAnswer: "The main difference\nA breakpoint and a rewrite are two different tools in the browser's DevTools that solve different problems. A breakpoint pauses the execution of JavaScript at a given line of code, letting you examine variables, the call stack and the application's state. A rewrite (or Overrides) lets you substitute the content of a network request or response without changing the source code on the server.\nWhen a breakpoint is used\nA breakpoint is applied for step-by-step debugging: you set a breakpoint in the code and execution stops. You can check the values of variables, run code in the console and step through. This is indispensable when hunting for logical errors, incorrect calculations or unexpected behaviour in functions.\n\nWhen a rewrite is used\nA rewrite lets you override the server's response for a particular URL. You can replace a JSON response, an HTML file or even an image. This is useful for testing different states of the UI without changing the backend: simulating a server error, an empty list or specific data, for example.\n\nThe key differences\n\n- A breakpoint works at the level of code execution, a rewrite at the level of network requests.\n- A breakpoint requires execution to stop, a rewrite happens transparently to the application.\n- A breakpoint helps debug logic, a rewrite helps test different data scenarios.\nConclusion: use a breakpoint to hunt for errors in your code, and a rewrite to check the interface's behaviour with various server responses without changing the backend.",
    codeExample: "// An example: debugging a function with a breakpoint\nfunction calculateTotal(items) {\n  let total = 0;\n  for (let item of items) {\n    total += item.price * item.quantity; // we set a breakpoint here\n  }\n  return total;\n}\n\n// An example: overriding an API response\n// In DevTools -> Network -> right-click the request -> Override content\n// We substitute the response with:\n{\n  \"users\": [],\n  \"error\": \"Service unavailable\"\n}",
  },
  128: {
    source: "Какие системные события нужно учитывать при тестировании (например, звонки, уведомления, потеря сети)?",
    question: "Which system events do you need to take into account when testing (calls, notifications, loss of network, for example)?",
    shortAnswer: "When testing mobile applications it is important to take system events into account, such as incoming calls, SMS messages, notifications, the loss or switching of the network, a change of screen orientation, a low battery charge and the device being locked. These events can interrupt an application's operation, cause data loss or leave it in an incorrect state. Testing should check how the application recovers from such interruptions and preserves the user's data.",
    longAnswer: "Why system events have to be taken into account\nSystem events are external influences on a mobile device that can interrupt an application's normal operation. Neglecting to test them leads to crashes, data loss and a poor user experience. For example, if an application does not handle an incoming call, it may freeze or lose the data that was entered.\nThe main kinds of system event\n\n- Incoming calls and SMS messages — they interrupt the current activity; the application has to pause and recover properly once the call has ended.\n- Notifications — push notifications or system alerts may cover the interface; it is important to check that the application does not lose its state.\n- Loss of network — going offline; the application has to store data locally and synchronize when the connection is restored.\n- A change of orientation — rotating the screen may cause the Activity (Android) or ViewController (iOS) to be recreated; the state has to be preserved.\n- A low battery charge — the system may restrict background processes; the application has to finish its operations properly.\n- The screen being locked — the application goes into the background; it is important to preserve progress and prevent memory leaks.\nAn example of testing on Android\n\nPractical recommendations\nSeparate test cases have to be written for every system event. Use emulators or real devices to simulate the events (through ADB commands for Android, for example). Automate the checks with frameworks such as Espresso or XCTest in order to guarantee stability with every build.\nConclusion: taking system events into account is mandatory for ensuring a mobile application's reliability. It prevents data loss, improves the user experience and increases the application's rating in the stores.",
    codeExample: "// An example of handling an incoming call in an Activity\n@Override\nprotected void onPause() {\n    super.onPause();\n    // We save the current state\n    saveState();\n}\n\n@Override\nprotected void onResume() {\n    super.onResume();\n    // We restore the state after the call\n    restoreState();\n}",
  },
  129: {
    source: "Как проверить корректность отображения интерфейса по макетам?",
    question: "How do you check that an interface is displayed correctly against the mockups?",
    shortAnswer: "To check an interface against the mockups, visual comparison (pixel perfect), tools for automated regression testing (Percy, Chromatic) and a manual review are used. It is important to check the spacing, the fonts, the colours and the behaviour at different resolutions. This helps avoid discrepancies between the design and the implementation.",
    longAnswer: "Checking an interface against the mockups\nThe correct display of an interface against the mockups is a key stage of development that guarantees the user sees exactly what the designer intended. Both manual and automated methods are applied for this.\nThe main approaches\n\n- Pixel-perfect comparison — overlaying a screenshot of the page on the mockup with some transparency in order to uncover discrepancies.\n- Visual regression testing — automatically comparing screenshots against reference ones with tools (Percy, Chromatic, Applitools).\n- A manual review — checking the spacing, fonts, colours and responsiveness by hand with the browser's developer tools.\nExample code for visual testing with Percy\n\nConclusion\nChecking an interface against the mockups regularly helps maintain the quality of the UI and prevents visual defects from accumulating. Automation is especially useful in projects where the design changes often.",
    codeExample: "// An example of integrating Percy with Cypress\ncy.visit('/page');\ncy.percySnapshot('Homepage'); // Takes a screenshot and compares it with the reference",
  },
  130: {
    source: "Какие инструменты можно использовать для проверки адаптивности интерфейса?",
    question: "Which tools can you use to check an interface's responsiveness?",
    shortAnswer: "To check an interface's responsiveness you can use the browsers' built-in developer tools (Chrome DevTools, Firefox Developer Tools), which let you emulate various devices and screen resolutions. There are also online services such as Responsinator and BrowserStack, and Lighthouse for an automated audit. In addition you can use physical devices and simulators for more accurate testing.",
    longAnswer: "Tools for checking an interface's responsiveness\nAn interface's responsiveness is a web page's ability to display correctly on various devices with different screen sizes. There are several categories of tool for checking it, from those built into the browser to specialized services.\nThe browser's developer tools\nThe most accessible way is to use the browser's developer tools. In Chrome DevTools, for example, there is a device emulation mode (Toggle Device Toolbar) that lets you pick a particular device or set an arbitrary resolution. You can check how media queries affect the layout and simulate touch events.\n\nOnline services and automated tools\n\n- Responsinator — a quick check on popular devices.\n- BrowserStack — lets you test on real devices and browsers in the cloud.\n- Lighthouse — a tool built into Chrome for auditing performance and accessibility, including a check of responsiveness.\nPhysical devices and simulators\nFor accurate testing it is recommended to use real smartphones and tablets. If you do not have them, the iOS (Xcode Simulator) and Android (Android Studio Emulator) simulators will do. They give a more realistic picture than emulation in the browser.\nConclusion\nUse a combination of the browser's tools for quick iterative checks, online services for cross-browser testing and physical devices for the final check. This will ensure the interface displays correctly on every target device.",
    codeExample: "/* An example of a CSS media query */\n@media (max-width: 768px) {\n  .container {\n    flex-direction: column;\n  }\n}",
  },
  131: {
    source: "В чем разница между JSON и XML?",
    question: "What is the difference between JSON and XML?",
    shortAnswer: "JSON (JavaScript Object Notation) is a lightweight format based on key-value pairs that is easy to read and parse. XML (eXtensible Markup Language) is a heavier markup language with tags and attributes that supports schemas and namespaces. JSON is normally simpler and faster for web APIs, whereas XML is more often used in complex documents and in systems with strict schemas.",
    longAnswer: "The main differences between JSON and XML\nJSON and XML are two popular formats for storing and transmitting structured data. JSON came out of JavaScript and became the standard for web APIs thanks to its simplicity. XML, by contrast, is an older, more powerful markup language that is widely applied in configuration files, documents and systems where strict validation matters.\nSyntax and readability\nJSON uses a minimalist syntax: objects in curly braces, arrays in square ones, the data represented as key-value pairs. XML uses opening and closing tags, which makes it more cumbersome. An example:\n\nData types and support\nJSON supports a limited set of types: strings, numbers, booleans, arrays, objects and null. XML has no built-in types — all the data is strings, which requires additional handling. JSON is easier to parse in programming languages, especially in JavaScript, where it can be turned into an object with `JSON.parse()`.\nUse\nJSON dominates in RESTful APIs, in configuration (package.json, for example) and in web applications. XML is often used in SOAP services, SVG graphics, RSS feeds and systems that require a strict schema (XSD).\nConclusion\nThe choice between JSON and XML depends on the task: for simple, fast exchanges of data on the web, JSON suits better, while for complex documents with validation and namespaces, XML does.",
    codeExample: "// JSON\n{\n  \"name\": \"Alice\",\n  \"age\": 30,\n  \"city\": \"New York\"\n}\n\n// XML\n<person>\n  <name>Alice</name>\n  <age>30</age>\n  <city>New York</city>\n</person>",
  },
  132: {
    source: "Как использовать DevTools для анализа верстки и сетевых запросов?",
    question: "How do you use DevTools to analyse markup and network requests?",
    shortAnswer: "DevTools are the browser's built-in tools for debugging. To analyse markup, use the Elements tab, where you can inspect and change the HTML/CSS in real time. For network requests, open the Network tab, where you can see every request, its status, its duration and its headers. This helps you find problems with display or with loading data quickly.",
    longAnswer: "The basics of working with DevTools\nDevTools (the developer tools) are a powerful set of tools built into modern browsers such as Chrome, Firefox and Edge. They let developers analyse, debug and optimize web pages. To open them, the F12 key or the combination Ctrl+Shift+I (Cmd+Option+I on a Mac) is normally used.\nAnalysing markup with the Elements tab\nThe Elements tab (or Inspector) shows the page's DOM tree and its CSS styles. You can:\n\n- Hover over elements in the tree in order to highlight them on the page.\n- Edit the HTML and CSS in real time — the changes are visible at once but are not saved.\n- Inspect the computed styles, the dimensions of blocks (the box model) and the CSS rules that have been applied.\nAn example: if an element has the wrong spacing, you can find it in Elements, look at the box model and change the margin or padding right there in the Styles panel.\nAnalysing network requests with the Network tab\nThe Network tab shows every HTTP request the page makes: loading the HTML, CSS, JavaScript and images, API requests and so on. Useful capabilities:\n\n- Viewing the response status (200, 404, 500 and so on).\n- The duration of every request (the time spent waiting, loading).\n- The request's and response's headers, including Content-Type and Cache-Control.\n- The response body (JSON or HTML, for example).\nAn example: if the page is not loading its data, open Network, find the request to the API and check its status and response body. If the status is 404, the URL is probably wrong.\nA practical example\n\nConclusion\nDevTools are an indispensable tool for every web developer. Use Elements for quickly debugging markup and Network for diagnosing problems with loading resources and with API requests. This speeds development up and helps you find errors without reloading the page.",
    codeExample: "// An example: checking that data loads through fetch\nfetch('https://api.example.com/data')\n  .then(response => response.json())\n  .then(data => console.log(data))\n  .catch(error => console.error('Error:', error));\n\n// In the DevTools Network tab you will see this request, its status and its duration.\n// If the request is not shown, check the filters (XHR, for example).",
  },
  133: {
    source: "Что такое тестирование совместимости?",
    question: "What is compatibility testing?",
    shortAnswer: "Compatibility testing is a kind of non-functional testing that checks how an application works in different environments: browsers, operating systems, devices and software versions. The goal is to be sure the product functions correctly for every user. A site, for example, should display the same way in Chrome, Firefox and Safari.",
    longAnswer: "What is compatibility testing?\nCompatibility testing is the process of checking that software works correctly in given combinations of hardware and software. It helps uncover problems that users may encounter because of differences in their environment.\nThe main aspects\n\n- Browsers: checking in Chrome, Firefox, Safari, Edge and others.\n- Operating systems: Windows, macOS, Linux, iOS, Android.\n- Devices: different screen resolutions, mobile phones, tablets.\n- Software versions: old and new versions of browsers and libraries.\nExample code for checking compatibility\n\nCode like this lets you adapt your styles according to the browser's capabilities.\nConclusion\nCompatibility testing is necessary in order to ensure an application works reliably on every target platform. It matters especially for web applications, where users have widely differing environments.",
    codeExample: "// Checking support for CSS Grid in the browser\nif (typeof CSS !== 'undefined' && CSS.supports('display', 'grid')) {\n  console.log('CSS Grid is supported');\n} else {\n  console.log('CSS Grid is not supported, use a fallback');\n}",
  },
  134: {
    source: "Что такое HTTP status code?",
    question: "What is an HTTP status code?",
    shortAnswer: "An HTTP status code is a three-digit number that the server sends in response to a request from a browser or another client. It shows whether the request succeeded, whether an error occurred or whether an additional action is required. The codes fall into categories: 1xx (informational), 2xx (success), 3xx (redirection), 4xx (client error) and 5xx (server error). For example, 200 means success, 404 means the page was not found, and 500 means an internal server error.",
    longAnswer: "What is an HTTP status code?\nAn HTTP status code is a three-digit integer that the server returns in response to a client's request (from a browser or a mobile application, for example). It is part of the first line of the HTTP response and reports the result of processing the request. The codes are standardized in the HTTP specification and fall into five classes, each of which begins with a particular digit.\nThe categories of status code\n\n- 1xx (Informational): informational codes indicating that the request has been accepted and is being processed. 101 Switching Protocols, for example, is used when upgrading to WebSocket.\n- 2xx (Success): success codes meaning that the request was successfully received, understood and processed. The most widespread is 200 OK.\n- 3xx (Redirection): redirection codes indicating that the client has to perform an additional action in order to complete the request. 301 Moved Permanently, for example, means the resource has moved to a new URL.\n- 4xx (Client Error): errors on the client's side, such as incorrect request syntax or a missing resource. For example, 404 Not Found means the page was not found; 401 Unauthorized means authorization is required.\n- 5xx (Server Error): errors on the server's side, when the server cannot fulfil a valid request. 500 Internal Server Error, for example, is an internal server error.\nAn example of use in code\nWhen developing web applications in Node.js with Express you can set the status codes explicitly:\n\nIn this example, if the user is not found, the server returns the code 404 with an error message. If the user exists, it returns 200 and the data.\nConclusion\nThe HTTP status code is a fundamental tool for understanding and debugging the interaction between client and server. Knowing the categories and the typical codes helps you diagnose problems in web applications quickly and handle responses correctly on the client side.",
    codeExample: "const express = require('express');\nconst app = express();\n\napp.get('/user/:id', (req, res) => {\n  const userId = req.params.id;\n  const user = findUserById(userId);\n  if (!user) {\n    return res.status(404).json({ error: 'User not found' });\n  }\n  res.status(200).json(user);\n});\n\napp.listen(3000);",
  },
  135: {
    source: "Какие факторы влияют на работу мобильного приложения?",
    question: "Which factors affect a mobile application's operation?",
    shortAnswer: "A mobile application's operation is affected by the device's hardware limitations, such as the processor, the memory and the battery. The network connection, the operating system's version and device fragmentation also matter. Optimizing the code, working with graphics and managing memory directly affect responsiveness and stability.",
    longAnswer: "The main factors affecting a mobile application's operation\nA mobile application's performance depends on a great many aspects, from the device's hardware characteristics to the quality of the network connection. Understanding these factors helps developers build more stable, faster applications.\nHardware limitations\n\n- The processor and the memory: different devices have different computing power and amounts of RAM. An application has to use the resources efficiently, avoiding memory leaks and excessive load on the CPU.\n- The battery: power consumption is critical for mobile devices. Background processes, frequent network requests and heavy graphics can drain a battery quickly.\n- The screen and graphics: the screen's resolution and the GPU's capabilities affect the rendering of the interface. Unoptimized images and animations can cause lag.\nSoftware and network factors\n\n- The operating system and its version: different versions of iOS and Android have different APIs and limitations. An application has to be compatible with a wide range of versions.\n- The network connection: network latency, packet loss and an unreliable connection affect the loading of data. Caching and the handling of network errors have to be implemented.\n- Device fragmentation: a great many models with different characteristics require thorough testing and adaptation of the interface.\nAn example of optimizing the loading of images\n\nIn this example the Glide library manages caching and the loading of images on a background thread automatically, which reduces the load on the UI and saves data.\nConclusion\nAllowing for hardware limitations, optimizing network interaction and testing on various devices are key to building a responsive, energy-efficient mobile application. Applying these practices matters especially when developing applications for the mass market with a wide range of devices.",
    codeExample: "// An example of caching images on Android using Glide\nImageView imageView = findViewById(R.id.image_view);\nString imageUrl = \"https://example.com/image.jpg\";\n\nGlide.with(this)\n    .load(imageUrl)\n    .placeholder(R.drawable.placeholder)\n    .error(R.drawable.error)\n    .diskCacheStrategy(DiskCacheStrategy.ALL)\n    .into(imageView);",
  },
  136: {
    source: "Как анализировать вкладки Network и Response?",
    question: "How do you analyse the Network and Response tabs?",
    shortAnswer: "The Network tab in DevTools shows every network request a page makes: loading times, statuses, headers. The Response tab displays the body of the server's response in various formats (JSON, HTML, an image). This helps you debug APIs, check data and optimize loading.",
    longAnswer: "What are the Network and Response tabs?\nThe Network tab in the browser's developer tools (DevTools) is a powerful tool for analysing all the HTTP requests a web page performs. It shows every request to the server, including static files (CSS, JS, images) and API calls. The Response tab is part of Network and displays the content the server returned for a particular request.\nHow to use Network for debugging\nOpen DevTools (F12 or Ctrl+Shift+I) and go to the Network tab. Reload the page or perform an action. You will see a list of requests. The main columns are:\n\n- Name — the name of the file or the request's URL\n- Status — the HTTP status (200, 404, 500 and so on)\n- Type — the kind of content (document, script, fetch)\n- Size — the size of the response\n- Time — the duration\nClick any request to see the details: Headers, Preview, Response (the response body) and Timing (the duration of the stages).\nAn example of analysing a response\nSuppose you make a request to an API and expect JSON. In the Response tab you will see:\n\nIf the response is empty or contains an error, you will notice it at once. You can also check the response headers — Content-Type: application/json, for example.\nA practical example with code\nSuppose you use fetch to get the data:\n\nOpen Network and find the request /api/users. Check the status (it should be 200), the type of response (JSON) and the response body. If the status is 404, check the URL. If it is 500, the problem is on the server.\nConclusion\nThe Network and Response tabs are an indispensable tool for debugging network interaction. They help you find problems with an API quickly, check that the data is correct and optimize the page's loading performance.",
    codeExample: "{\n  \"users\": [\n    {\"id\": 1, \"name\": \"Alice\"},\n    {\"id\": 2, \"name\": \"Bob\"}\n  ]\n}\n\nfetch('/api/users')\n  .then(response => response.json())\n  .then(data => console.log(data))\n  .catch(error => console.error('Error:', error));",
  },
  137: {
    source: "Как описать баг, связанный с версткой?",
    question: "How do you describe a bug related to markup?",
    shortAnswer: "Describe the bug by giving its title, the environment (browser, OS, screen resolution), the steps to reproduce it, and the actual and expected results. Attach a screenshot or a video. State the priority and the severity of the problem. A clear description helps the developer understand and fix the error quickly.",
    longAnswer: "The structure of a markup bug description\nFor a markup defect to be fixed effectively, the developer has to be given exhaustive information. The description should be clear, reproducible and contain every key detail.\nThe main components of a bug report\n\n- The title: briefly reflects the essence of the problem — \"The 'Submit' button extends beyond its container on mobile devices\", for example.\n- The environment: give the browser (Chrome 120, Firefox 121), the operating system (Windows 11, macOS 14), the screen resolution (375x812 for an iPhone X) and the device (iPhone 14, Samsung Galaxy S23).\n- The steps to reproduce: a numbered list of the actions that lead to the bug. For example: 1. Open the page /profile. 2. Click the \"Edit\" button. 3. Reduce the window's width to 375px.\n- The actual result: what really happens. For example: \"The 'Save' button is overlapped by the text of the input field\".\n- The expected result: how it should be. For example: \"The 'Save' button sits below the input field with 16px of spacing\".\n- Attachments: a screenshot or screen recording demonstrating the problem. You can add the HTML/CSS code if it helps localize the problem.\nAn example description\n\nConclusion: describing a markup bug properly saves the team's time, reduces the number of clarifying questions and speeds up the fixing of the defect. Always state the environment and attach visual evidence.",
    codeExample: "Title: The logo overlaps the menu in Safari on an iPad\nEnvironment: Safari 17, iPadOS 17, iPad Pro 11\" (resolution 834x1194)\nSteps to reproduce:\n1. Open the home page.\n2. Rotate the device into landscape orientation.\nActual result: The logo overlaps the first menu item.\nExpected result: The logo and the menu are displayed with 20px of spacing.\nPriority: High (it blocks navigation).",
  },
  138: {
    source: "Где передаются параметры в GET-запросе?",
    question: "Where are the parameters passed in a GET request?",
    shortAnswer: "The parameters in a GET request are passed in the query string (the URL) after the question mark (?). They have the form key=value and are separated by the & character. For example: /api/users?page=2&limit=10. This is the standard way of passing data in GET requests, used for filtering, sorting and pagination.",
    longAnswer: "How parameters are passed in a GET request\nIn an HTTP GET request the parameters are passed in the URL (Uniform Resource Locator) string after the question mark (?). This part of the URL is called the query string. The parameters have the form key=value, and several parameters are separated by the & character.\nAn example of the structure of a URL with parameters\nConsider the URL: `https://api.example.com/users?page=2&limit=10&sort=name`. Here:\n\n- `https://api.example.com/users` — the base path to the resource\n- `?` — the separator marking the start of the parameters\n- `page=2` — the first parameter (the page number)\n- `&` — the separator between parameters\n- `limit=10` — the second parameter (the number of items per page)\n- `sort=name` — the third parameter (sorting by name)\nA practical example in JavaScript\n\nThe particulars of passing parameters in a GET request\n\n- Visibility: the parameters are visible in the URL, so do not use GET to pass confidential data (passwords, tokens).\n- A length limit: a URL has a length limit (usually 2048 characters), so GET is not suitable for passing large volumes of data.\n- Caching: GET requests with parameters can be cached by browsers and proxy servers, which speeds up repeat requests.\n- Idempotence: GET requests must not change the server's state (only read data).\nConclusion\nThe parameters in a GET request are passed through the query string in the URL. This method is convenient for filtering, sorting and paginating data, but it is not suitable for passing sensitive information or large volumes of data. Use GET with parameters for safe, idempotent requests that read data.",
    codeExample: "// Composing a GET request with parameters\nconst baseUrl = 'https://api.example.com/users';\nconst params = {\n  page: 2,\n  limit: 10,\n  sort: 'name'\n};\n\n// Converting the object into a query string\nconst queryString = Object.entries(params)\n  .map(([key, value]) => `${encodeURIComponent(key)}=${encodeURIComponent(value)}`)\n  .join('&');\n\nconst fullUrl = `${baseUrl}?${queryString}`;\nconsole.log(fullUrl);\n// https://api.example.com/users?page=2&limit=10&sort=name\n\n// Performing the request\nfetch(fullUrl)\n  .then(response => response.json())\n  .then(data => console.log(data));",
  },
  139: {
    source: "Какую структуру имеет JSON?",
    question: "What structure does JSON have?",
    shortAnswer: "JSON (JavaScript Object Notation) is a text format for storing and transmitting data. It consists of \"key-value\" pairs, where the keys are strings in double quotes and the values may be strings, numbers, objects, arrays, booleans or null. The data is organized into objects (in curly braces) and arrays (in square brackets).",
    longAnswer: "The basics of JSON's structure\nJSON (JavaScript Object Notation) is a lightweight data-interchange format based on JavaScript's syntax. It is used to transmit structured data between server and client, and also to store configuration. JSON's structure consists of two main constructs: the object and the array.\nAn object\nAn object is an unordered set of \"key-value\" pairs enclosed in curly braces {}. The keys are always strings in double quotes, and the values may be of any permitted type. For example:\n\nAn array\nAn array is an ordered list of values enclosed in square brackets []. The values are separated by commas. For example:\n\nThe permitted value types\n\n- A string (in double quotes)\n- A number (an integer or a floating-point one)\n- A boolean (true or false)\n- null\n- An object\n- An array\nAn example of a complex structure\n\nIn this example the user object contains a string, a number, an array and null. The nesting can go to any depth.\nConclusion\nJSON is a simple, readable format that most programming languages parse easily. It is applied for APIs, configuration files and storing data, where universality and minimal size are required.",
    codeExample: "{\n  \"name\": \"Alice\",\n  \"age\": 30,\n  \"isStudent\": false\n}\n\n[\n  \"apple\",\n  \"banana\",\n  \"cherry\"\n]\n\n{\n  \"user\": {\n    \"id\": 1,\n    \"name\": \"Bob\",\n    \"hobbies\": [\"reading\", \"coding\"],\n    \"address\": null\n  },\n  \"active\": true\n}",
  },
  140: {
    source: "Для чего используется вкладка Application в DevTools?",
    question: "What is the Application tab in DevTools used for?",
    shortAnswer: "The Application tab in DevTools is used to inspect and manage all the data stored in the browser: local storage (localStorage), session storage (sessionStorage), cookies, the cache (Cache Storage) and IndexedDB, and also to manage Service Workers and PWA manifests. It is a key tool for debugging client-side storage and checking that a web application works correctly.",
    longAnswer: "The purpose of the Application tab\nThe Application tab in the browser's developer tools (DevTools) provides a centralized interface for inspecting, editing and deleting all the data a web application stores on the client side. It is necessary for debugging work with storage, checking that cookies are set correctly, analysing caching and managing Service Workers.\nThe main sections\n\n- Local Storage — data stored in the browser with no expiry, available within a single origin.\n- Session Storage — data that is deleted once the tab is closed.\n- Cookies — small fragments of data sent with every HTTP request, often used for sessions and tracking.\n- IndexedDB — a more complex client-side database for storing large volumes of structured data.\n- Cache Storage — part of the Cache API, used to cache resources (in Service Workers, for example).\n- Service Workers — managing the background scripts that intercept network requests and implement offline functionality.\nAn example of use\nSuppose you want to check whether an authorization token is being saved correctly in localStorage. You can open the Application tab, select Local Storage, find the key 'token' and see its value. You can also change or delete it right there in the interface for testing.\n\nConclusion\nThe Application tab is an indispensable tool for debugging client-side storage and managing a PWA's resources. It lets you check and modify data quickly, which speeds up the development and testing of web applications.",
    codeExample: "// An example of saving a token in localStorage\nlocalStorage.setItem('token', 'abc123');\n\n// Checking in DevTools: Application -> Local Storage -> your domain -> token = 'abc123'",
  },
  141: {
    source: "Для чего используется вкладка Sources в DevTools?",
    question: "What is the Sources tab in DevTools used for?",
    shortAnswer: "The Sources tab in Chrome DevTools is used to debug JavaScript code. You can set breakpoints, inspect and change the values of variables, step through the code and analyse the call stack. It is the main tool for finding and fixing errors in client-side code.",
    longAnswer: "The purpose of the Sources tab\nThe Sources tab in Chrome DevTools is a full-featured environment for debugging JavaScript, CSS and HTML. It lets a developer pause the execution of code at any moment, examine the application's state and step through scripts. It is an indispensable tool for understanding how code works and for hunting down bugs.\nThe main capabilities\n\n- Setting breakpoints: you can click a line number in the editor in order to set a breakpoint. When execution reaches that line, it pauses.\n- Stepping through: once execution has paused you can use the Step Over (F10), Step Into (F11) and Step Out (Shift+F11) buttons to run the code one line or one function at a time.\n- Inspecting variables: the Scope panel on the right displays every local, closure and global variable with its current value. You can change them while debugging.\n- Call Stack: shows the chain of function calls that led to the current breakpoint. This helps you understand where the function was called from.\n- Watch: lets you add expressions whose values will be computed and displayed at every step of the debugging.\nAn example of use\nSuppose you have a function that does not return the expected result:\n\nTo debug it, open the Sources tab, find the file with this code and click the line number of `let total = price + tax;`. Then run the code again (reload the page, for example). Execution will stop on that line. In the Scope panel you will see the values `price = 100` and `tax = 20`. By pressing Step Over you will move to `return total;` and see that `total = 120`. If the value were wrong, you would notice the problem at once.\nConclusion\nThe Sources tab is the main tool for debugging frontend code. It lets a developer not merely read logs but explore a program's execution interactively, which speeds up the finding and fixing of errors considerably. It is worth applying whenever the code's behaviour differs from what you expect, especially in complex scenarios involving asynchrony or a great many function calls.",
    codeExample: "function calculateTotal(price, tax) {\n  let total = price + tax;\n  return total;\n}\n\nlet result = calculateTotal(100, 20);\nconsole.log(result); // 120 is expected, but there may be an error",
  },
  142: {
    source: "Как реализовать debounce для поля поиска?",
    question: "How do you implement debounce for a search field?",
    shortAnswer: "Debounce is a technique that postpones the execution of a function until a certain time has passed since the last call. For a search field this means the request is sent only after the user has stopped typing. It reduces the number of requests to the server and improves performance.",
    longAnswer: "What debounce is and what it is for\nDebounce is a technique used to limit how often a function is called. It guarantees that the function will run only after a certain time has passed since the last call. This is especially useful for a search field, where every character typed may initiate a request to the server. Without debounce every key pressed would send a request, which leads to an unnecessary load and a poor user experience.\nHow do you implement debounce?\nImplementing debounce involves creating a function that takes another function and a delay. It returns a new function which, on every call, resets the timer and sets a new one. When the timer expires, the original function is called.\n\nWhere is debounce applied?\nDebounce is widely used in web development to handle events that can occur often, such as typing text, resizing the window or scrolling. It helps reduce the load on the browser and the server, improving performance.\nConclusion\nDebounce is a simple, effective way of optimizing frequent function calls. It is worth applying in any scenario where you need to react to user input or other frequent events, in order to avoid redundant computation and requests.",
    codeExample: "function debounce(func, delay) {\n  let timeoutId;\n  return function(...args) {\n    clearTimeout(timeoutId);\n    timeoutId = setTimeout(() => func.apply(this, args), delay);\n  };\n}\n\n// Use for a search field\nconst searchInput = document.getElementById('search');\nconst handleSearch = debounce((event) => {\n  console.log('Search query:', event.target.value);\n  // Sending the request to the server\n}, 300);\n\nsearchInput.addEventListener('input', handleSearch);",
  },
  143: {
    source: "Нужно ли отображать дочерние узлы при совпадении родителя?",
    question: "Should child nodes be displayed when the parent matches?",
    shortAnswer: "Yes, when a parent node matches, all its child nodes are normally displayed too, in order to preserve the integrity of the hierarchy. This lets the user see the full context of the element that was found. The exception is when only an exact match without the nested data is required.",
    longAnswer: "The general concept\nWhen searching in tree structures (the DOM tree, a file system or hierarchical menus, for example) the question often arises: should the child nodes be shown if the parent matched the search criterion? The answer depends on the context, but in most cases it is yes — the child nodes are displayed, in order to preserve the hierarchy and the context.\nWhy this matters\nIf you hide the child nodes when the parent matches, the user may lose their understanding of the structure. In a file manager, for example, when searching for the folder 'Documents' the user expects to see its contents rather than simply an empty folder. In interfaces with trees (a TreeView, for example) this is the standard behaviour.\nAn example in JavaScript\n\nThe exceptions\n\n- If an exact match without context is required (searching by ID, for example).\n- In large trees you can display only the parent with an indicator that it has children, for performance.\nConclusion\nDisplaying the child nodes when the parent matches improves the user experience and preserves the context. It is the standard practice in interfaces with hierarchical data, unless there are special requirements for the precision of the search.",
    codeExample: "// A function for searching a tree that displays the child nodes\nfunction searchTree(node, query) {\n  if (node.name.includes(query)) {\n    // We show the parent and all its children\n    node.visible = true;\n    node.children.forEach(child => showAllChildren(child));\n  } else {\n    // We search in the child nodes\n    node.children.forEach(child => searchTree(child, query));\n  }\n}\n\nfunction showAllChildren(node) {\n  node.visible = true;\n  node.children.forEach(child => showAllChildren(child));\n}",
  },
  144: {
    source: "Какие основные браузерные движки существуют?",
    question: "What are the main browser engines?",
    shortAnswer: "The main browser engines are: Blink (Chrome, Opera, Edge), WebKit (Safari) and Gecko (Firefox). There are also Trident (the old Internet Explorer) and its successor EdgeHTML (the old Edge). Every engine is responsible for painting HTML and CSS and for executing JavaScript.",
    longAnswer: "The main browser engines\nA browser engine is the software component responsible for loading, analysing and displaying web pages. It turns HTML, CSS and JavaScript into a visual representation on the screen. Knowing the engines helps developers understand why the same code may behave differently in different browsers.\nA list of the main engines\n\n- Blink — used in Google Chrome, Opera, Microsoft Edge (from 2020 onwards), Brave and other Chromium-based browsers. It is a fork of WebKit created by Google in 2013.\n- WebKit — used in Safari (Apple) and formerly in Chrome. Developed by Apple on the basis of KHTML.\n- Gecko — used in Firefox (Mozilla). Known for its strict support of web standards.\n- Trident — the old engine of Internet Explorer (IE4–IE11). No longer supported.\n- EdgeHTML — used in the old Microsoft Edge (until 2020). It has now been replaced by Blink.\nExample code for detecting the engine\n\nConclusion\nUnderstanding browser engines is necessary for testing and ensuring the compatibility of web applications. Different engines may interpret CSS properties or JavaScript APIs differently, so it is important to check that a site works in several browsers.",
    codeExample: "// Detecting the browser engine through navigator.userAgent\nconst userAgent = navigator.userAgent;\nif (userAgent.includes('Chrome') && !userAgent.includes('Edg')) {\n  console.log('Blink (Chrome)');\n} else if (userAgent.includes('Safari') && !userAgent.includes('Chrome')) {\n  console.log('WebKit (Safari)');\n} else if (userAgent.includes('Firefox')) {\n  console.log('Gecko (Firefox)');\n} else if (userAgent.includes('Edg')) {\n  console.log('Blink (Edge)');\n} else {\n  console.log('Unknown engine');\n}",
  },
  145: {
    source: "Что такое кроссбраузерность и почему возникают различия в отображении?",
    question: "What is cross-browser compatibility and why do differences in display arise?",
    shortAnswer: "Cross-browser compatibility is a site's ability to display correctly in every popular browser. The differences arise from different rendering engines (Blink, WebKit, Gecko), incomplete support for standards and obsolete browsers. To ensure compatibility, vendor prefixes, polyfills and progressive enhancement are used.",
    longAnswer: "What is cross-browser compatibility?\nCross-browser compatibility is the property of a website or application working correctly and displaying the same way in every modern browser, including Chrome, Firefox, Safari, Edge and Opera. It is an important aspect of web development, since users use different browsers and each of them may interpret the code in its own way.\nWhy do the differences arise?\nThe main reasons for differences in display:\n\n- Different rendering engines: Chrome and Edge use Blink, Firefox uses Gecko, Safari uses WebKit. Each engine handles CSS and JavaScript in its own way.\n- Incomplete support for standards: new HTML, CSS and JavaScript capabilities may not be implemented in every browser at the same time.\n- Obsolete browsers: old versions (Internet Explorer, for example) do not support modern technologies.\n- Vendor prefixes: some CSS properties require prefixes for particular browsers (`-webkit-` for Safari, for example).\nExample code with vendor prefixes\n\nHow do you ensure cross-browser compatibility?\nTo achieve cross-browser compatibility developers use:\n\n- Polyfills — scripts that add support for missing features (`fetch` for older browsers, for example).\n- CSS normalization — resetting the browser's default styles (Normalize.css, for example).\n- Progressive enhancement — creating a basic version that works everywhere and adding improvements for modern browsers.\n- Testing — checking in different browsers with tools such as BrowserStack.\nConclusion: cross-browser compatibility is necessary in order to give every user equal access to your content. Apply polyfills, prefixes and testing in order to minimize the differences and improve the user experience.",
    codeExample: ".box {\n  display: -webkit-flex; /* Safari */\n  display: flex; /* The standard */\n  -webkit-border-radius: 5px; /* Safari */\n  border-radius: 5px; /* The standard */\n}",
  },
  146: {
    source: "Что такое reflow и repaint?",
    question: "What are reflow and repaint?",
    shortAnswer: "Reflow is the process of recalculating the dimensions and positions of the elements on a page when the DOM or the CSS changes. Repaint is the updating of elements' appearance without any change to their geometry. A reflow always causes a repaint, but not the other way round. Frequent reflows reduce performance, so people try to minimize them.",
    longAnswer: "What are reflow and repaint?\nReflow (or layout) is the process in which the browser recalculates the geometry of elements: their dimensions, positions and spacing. It happens when the DOM changes, when CSS properties that affect dimensions change (width, height, margin, padding), or when the window is resized. Repaint is the process of updating the pixels on the screen without any change to the geometry — when a background or text colour changes, for example.\nHow are they connected?\nA reflow always causes a repaint, since after the geometry has been recalculated the appearance has to be updated. A repaint can happen without a reflow if only visual properties change (color, visibility, background-color).\nCode examples\n\nHow do you minimize reflows?\n\n- Change classes rather than individual styles.\n- Work with elements outside the flow (position: absolute/fixed).\n- Use a documentFragment for bulk changes to the DOM.\n- Avoid reading properties that cause a reflow often (offsetHeight, clientWidth).\nConclusion: understanding reflow and repaint helps you write fast web applications, especially with animations and dynamic content. Minimizing reflows is the key to a smooth interface.",
    codeExample: "// Causes a reflow and a repaint\ndocument.getElementById('box').style.width = '200px';\n\n// Causes only a repaint\ndocument.getElementById('box').style.backgroundColor = 'red';\n\n// Reading offsetHeight causes a reflow (a forced layout)\nconst height = document.getElementById('box').offsetHeight;",
  },
  147: {
    source: "Что делает getBoundingClientRect?",
    question: "What does getBoundingClientRect do?",
    shortAnswer: "The getBoundingClientRect method returns a DOMRect object with the element's coordinates relative to the browser window (the viewport). It contains the properties top, right, bottom, left, width and height. This is useful for determining whether an element is visible, for animations or for positioning tooltips.",
    longAnswer: "What is getBoundingClientRect?\nThe `getBoundingClientRect` method is called on a DOM element and returns a `DOMRect` object describing the element's size and its position relative to the visible area of the window (the viewport). The coordinates are measured from the top left corner of the viewport, not of the document as a whole.\nThe main properties of the returned object\n\n- `top` — the distance from the top edge of the viewport to the top edge of the element.\n- `bottom` — the distance from the top edge of the viewport to the bottom edge of the element.\n- `left` — the distance from the left edge of the viewport to the left edge of the element.\n- `right` — the distance from the left edge of the viewport to the right edge of the element.\n- `width` and `height` — the element's width and height, including its padding and border.\nAn example of use\n\nWhere it is applied\n\n- Checking whether an element is visible on the screen (for the lazy loading of images, for example).\n- Positioning pop-ups or tooltips relative to an element.\n- Animations that depend on an element's position (parallax, for example).\nConclusion\nThe `getBoundingClientRect` method is a simple, effective way of getting an element's exact coordinates in the viewport. It is indispensable for tasks involving the determination of visibility and positioning in dynamic interfaces.",
    codeExample: "const element = document.getElementById('myBox');\nconst rect = element.getBoundingClientRect();\nconsole.log(rect.top, rect.left, rect.width, rect.height);\n// For example: 100, 50, 200, 150",
  },
  148: {
    source: "Чем отличается transform от position при анимации?",
    question: "How does transform differ from position in animation?",
    shortAnswer: "Transform uses the GPU to move an element without changing its geometry, which triggers only compositing. Position changes an element's location through the top/left properties, which triggers a layout recalculation (reflow) and a repaint, loading the CPU. Transform works faster and more smoothly, especially in animation.",
    longAnswer: "The main difference between transform and position in animation\nThe `transform` property and the `top`/`left`/`right`/`bottom` properties (through `position`) affect the browser's rendering process differently. `transform` works at the compositing stage without touching layout and paint, which makes it more performant for animations.\nHow it works\nWhen `position` changes (`left: 100px`, for example) the browser runs the full cycle: recalculating the layout (reflow), then a repaint and only then compositing. This loads the CPU. `transform`, by contrast, moves the element on a separate layer using the GPU, bypassing layout and paint. This is especially noticeable with frequent changes (60fps).\nA code example\n\nWhen to use which\n\n- transform — for animations of movement, scaling and rotation. It provides smoothness and high performance.\n- position — for static positioning or infrequent changes, where performance is not critical.\nConclusion: for animations always prefer `transform`, since it uses the GPU and does not cause the layout to be recalculated, which is critical for the smoothness of interfaces.",
    codeExample: "/* A slow animation through position */\n.box {\n  position: relative;\n  animation: move 2s infinite;\n}\n@keyframes move {\n  from { left: 0; }\n  to { left: 200px; }\n}\n\n/* A fast animation through transform */\n.box {\n  animation: moveTransform 2s infinite;\n}\n@keyframes moveTransform {\n  from { transform: translateX(0); }\n  to { transform: translateX(200px); }\n}",
  },
  149: {
    source: "Что такое CSSOM и как он взаимодействует с DOM?",
    question: "What is the CSSOM and how does it interact with the DOM?",
    shortAnswer: "The CSSOM (CSS Object Model) is the object model of CSS, analogous to the DOM for HTML. The browser builds the CSSOM from the CSS rules and then combines it with the DOM into the render tree in order to paint the page. Without the CSSOM the browser does not know how to style elements, which is why building the CSSOM blocks rendering.",
    longAnswer: "What is the CSSOM?\nThe CSSOM (CSS Object Model) is an object representation of the CSS rules that the browser creates when a page loads. It works in the same way as the DOM but for styles: every CSSOM node contains information about selectors, properties and values. The browser builds the CSSOM from all the CSS files, the inline styles and the style attributes.\nHow does the CSSOM interact with the DOM?\nOnce the browser has built the DOM (the tree of HTML elements) and the CSSOM (the tree of styles), it combines them into the render tree. The render tree contains only the visible elements and their computed styles. An element with `display: none`, for example, does not get into the render tree, although it remains in the DOM. The render tree is then used for the layout stage (calculating the geometry) and paint (drawing the pixels).\nA code example\n\nIn this example the browser first parses the HTML and builds the DOM (the node div#app). Then it loads and parses the CSS, creating the CSSOM (the rule for #app). After that the render tree is formed: div#app with the colour blue and a size of 16px. If the CSS were blocked (by a slow server, for example), the rendering would be delayed until the CSSOM had been built fully.\nConclusion\nThe CSSOM is critically important for pages to display correctly: it determines every element's styles. Understanding its interaction with the DOM helps you optimize the critical rendering path — by minimizing blocking CSS resources to speed loading up, for example.",
    codeExample: "<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html>\n<head>\n  <link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"styles.css\">\n</head>\n<body>\n  <div id=\"app\">Hello</div>\n</body>\n</html>\n\n/* styles.css */\n#app { color: blue; font-size: 16px; }",
  },
  150: {
    source: "Когда происходит перерасчет layout?",
    question: "When does the layout get recalculated?",
    shortAnswer: "The layout is recalculated (a reflow) when elements' geometric properties change — width, height, position, spacing — or when elements are added to or removed from the DOM. A reflow is also triggered when the window is resized, when CSS pseudo-classes are activated (:hover, for example), when certain properties are read (offsetHeight, scrollTop) and when the content changes (text, for example). It is an expensive operation, so people try to minimize it.",
    longAnswer: "When does the layout get recalculated (a reflow)?\nRecalculating the layout (also called a reflow) is the rendering stage at which the browser computes the geometry (the dimensions and positions) of every element on the page. It happens every time a property that affects an element's size or position changes.\nThe main causes of a reflow:\n\n- A change to geometric CSS properties: width, height, margin, padding, border, top, left, display (switching to block/inline-block), position.\n- Adding or removing elements from the DOM.\n- A change to an element's content (replacing text or an image, for example).\n- The activation of CSS pseudo-classes such as :hover and :focus.\n- A change to the browser window's dimensions (resize).\n- Reading certain properties that force the browser to perform a reflow: offsetHeight, offsetWidth, scrollTop, scrollHeight, clientTop, getComputedStyle() and others.\nExample code that causes a reflow:\n\nHow do you minimize reflows?\n\n- Group style changes together (use classList.add or cssText).\n- Work with elements outside the flow (position: absolute/fixed).\n- Use a documentFragment for adding elements in bulk.\n- Avoid reading properties that cause a reflow inside loops.\nConclusion: understanding reflow matters for optimizing the performance of web pages, especially with animations and the dynamic updating of content. Minimizing layout recalculations speeds rendering up and improves the user experience.",
    codeExample: "const el = document.getElementById('box');\nel.style.width = '200px'; // a change of geometry -> a reflow\nconst height = el.offsetHeight; // a forced read -> a reflow\nel.style.height = '300px'; // one more reflow",
  },
  151: {
    source: "Какие теги блокируют рендеринг страницы?",
    question: "Which tags block a page's rendering?",
    shortAnswer: "Rendering is blocked by `<script>` without attributes and by `<link rel=\"stylesheet\">`. The browser stops building the DOM until it has loaded and executed the script or applied the styles. In React applications this topic matters when configuring `index.html`, SSR/SSG and lazy loading.",
    longAnswer: "What \"blocking rendering\" means\nThe browser builds the DOM in sequence, from the top down. If it meets a tag that requires a resource to be loaded or executed, it stops and waits. The page is not displayed until that resource has been processed.\n`<script>` — it blocks the parsing of the DOM\n\nThe difference between `async` and `defer`:\n\n- `async` — it runs at the moment it loads; the order is not guaranteed\n\n- `defer` — it runs after parsing, in the order of declaration\n\nReact applications (CRA, Vite) add `defer` to the bundle by default.\n`<link rel=\"stylesheet\">` — it blocks rendering\n\nCSS blocks rendering because the browser has to build the CSSOM before painting — otherwise the page would \"flash\" without styles (FOUC).\nWhat does NOT block rendering\n\nHow this looks in practice\n\nReact specifics\nIn React you rarely work with these tags directly, but the topic comes up in several contexts:",
    codeExample: "<!-- ❌ Blocks: the browser will stop parsing until it has loaded and executed this -->\n<script src=\"app.js\"></script>\n\n<!-- ✅ async: loads in parallel, runs as soon as it has loaded -->\n<script src=\"app.js\" async></script>\n\n<!-- ✅ defer: loads in parallel, runs after the DOM has been parsed fully -->\n<script src=\"app.js\" defer></script>\n\n<!-- ❌ Blocks: the browser will not draw a single pixel without the CSS -->\n<link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"styles.css\">\n\n<!-- ✅ Loading non-critical CSS without blocking -->\n<link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"print.css\" media=\"print\">\n<link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"heavy.css\" media=\"(min-width: 1200px)\">\n\n<!-- Images — they do not block, they load in parallel -->\n<img src=\"photo.jpg\">\n\n<!-- Fonts through font-face — they do not block parsing but can cause FOIT -->\n<link rel=\"preload\" href=\"font.woff2\" as=\"font\" crossorigin>\n\n<!-- preload/prefetch — merely hints to the browser, they do not block -->\n<link rel=\"preload\" href=\"chunk.js\" as=\"script\">\n<link rel=\"prefetch\" href=\"next-page.js\">\n\n<head>\n  <!-- ❌ Blocking CSS — fine for critical styles -->\n  <link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"critical.css\">\n\n  <!-- ✅ Non-critical CSS — loaded asynchronously with the onload trick -->\n  <link rel=\"preload\" href=\"non-critical.css\" as=\"style\"\n        onload=\"this.rel='stylesheet'\">\n</head>\n\n<body>\n  <!-- the content -->\n\n  <!-- ✅ Scripts at the end of body or with defer — the DOM is already built -->\n  <script src=\"app.js\" defer></script>\n</body>\n\n// Code splitting — Webpack/Vite will add defer automatically\nconst LazyPage = React.lazy(() => import('./Page'));\n\n// Next.js — built-in management of critical CSS\n// Components' styles are inlined into <head>, the rest is deferred\n\n// Vite/CRA index.html — the bundle always comes with defer\n// <script type=\"module\" src=\"/src/main.jsx\"></script>\n// type=\"module\" behaves like defer by default",
  },
  152: {
    source: "Что такое время до первого взаимодействия (TTI)?",
    question: "What is Time to Interactive (TTI)?",
    shortAnswer: "Time to Interactive (TTI) is a performance metric showing how many seconds after loading begins the page becomes fully interactive. It takes into account the time at which the content is displayed, the main scripts have run and the user can interact with the elements without delay. TTI helps you assess how quickly a user can start working with a page.",
    longAnswer: "What is Time to Interactive (TTI)?\nTime to Interactive (TTI) is a web page performance metric that measures the time from the start of navigation to the moment the page becomes fully interactive. Full interactivity means the content is displayed, the main scripts have been loaded and executed, and the user can interact with the elements (pressing buttons or filling in forms, for example) without any noticeable delay.\nHow is TTI measured?\nTTI is computed from two key moments:\n\n- The page has to be displayed visually (First Contentful Paint).\n- The browser's main thread has to be free of long tasks for at least 5 seconds.\nLong tasks are tasks that run for more than 50 ms and block interaction. TTI is recorded once the network and the main thread have stabilized.\nExample code for tracking TTI\nAlthough TTI is normally measured with tools such as Lighthouse, you can estimate it approximately with a Performance Observer:\n\nWhere is TTI applied?\nTTI is critically important for the user experience, especially on mobile devices and over slow connections. A low TTI means the user can start interacting quickly, which improves engagement and conversion. The metric is used in performance audits (Lighthouse, WebPageTest) and for optimizing the loading of scripts, reducing the size of JavaScript and eliminating long tasks.\nConclusion\nTTI helps developers assess how quickly a page becomes ready for interaction. Apply this metric to optimize the critical rendering path, especially on sites with a lot of JavaScript, in order to ensure a smooth user experience.",
    codeExample: "const observer = new PerformanceObserver((list) => {\n  const entries = list.getEntries();\n  entries.forEach(entry => {\n    if (entry.name === 'first-contentful-paint') {\n      console.log('FCP:', entry.startTime);\n    }\n  });\n});\nobserver.observe({ type: 'paint', buffered: true });\n\n// TTI requires more complex logic, usually through libraries",
  },
  153: {
    source: "Как браузер рендерит страницу от ввода URL до отображения?",
    question: "How does the browser render a page from the entry of a URL to its display?",
    shortAnswer: "The browser starts with a DNS request to get the server's IP address, then establishes a TCP connection and sends an HTTP request. Once it has received the HTML document, the parser builds the DOM tree, while the CSS and JavaScript load in parallel. The CSS forms the CSSOM, then the browser creates the render tree, computes the layout (the elements' geometry) and performs the paint (drawing the pixels).",
    longAnswer: "The overall rendering process\nWhen the user types a URL in the address bar, the browser begins a sequence of steps known as the critical rendering path. This process involves several stages, from resolving DNS to drawing pixels on the screen.\nThe main stages\n\n- The DNS request: the browser turns the domain name into the server's IP address.\n- The TCP connection: a connection to the server is established (normally over HTTPS).\n- The HTTP request: the browser sends a GET request to the server for the HTML document.\n- Parsing the HTML: the browser starts taking the HTML apart and building the DOM tree. On discovering external resources (CSS, JS) it initiates their loading.\n- Building the CSSOM: the CSS rules are turned into the CSS Object Model.\n- The render tree: combining the DOM and the CSSOM into a tree that contains only the visible elements.\n- Layout: computing the exact dimensions and positions of every element.\n- Paint: drawing the pixels on the screen.\nAn example with code\nConsider a simple HTML document:\n\nThe browser first loads the HTML, then finds the `<link>` and starts loading the CSS. The parsing of the HTML carries on, but rendering is blocked until the CSS has loaded fully. Once the CSS has arrived, the CSSOM is built, and then the render tree. Layout and paint follow. The JavaScript at the end is loaded and executed, which may change the DOM or the CSSOM and cause a recalculation.\nConclusion\nUnderstanding the critical rendering path helps you optimize the performance of web pages — by minimizing blocking resources and using asynchronous loading of scripts, for example.",
    codeExample: "<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html>\n<head>\n  <link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"styles.css\">\n</head>\n<body>\n  <div>Hello World</div>\n  <script src=\"app.js\"></script>\n</body>\n</html>",
  },
  154: {
    source: "Какие существуют метрики производительности загрузки страницы?",
    question: "What metrics exist for a page's loading performance?",
    shortAnswer: "The main metrics for a page's loading performance include First Contentful Paint (FCP), Largest Contentful Paint (LCP), First Input Delay (FID), Cumulative Layout Shift (CLS) and Time to Interactive (TTI). FCP measures the time until content is first displayed, LCP the loading time of the largest element, FID the delay on the first interaction, CLS the visual stability, and TTI the time until full interactivity. These metrics help you assess the user experience and optimize performance.",
    longAnswer: "Metrics for a page's loading performance\nA web page's loading performance is critically important for the user experience and for ranking in search engines. Standardized metrics are used to assess it, measuring various aspects of loading, from the first display of content to the stability of the layout. The main metrics are part of the Web Vitals set recommended by Google.\nThe main metrics\n\n- First Contentful Paint (FCP) — the time at which the browser first displays any content (text, an image, a canvas). A good value is less than 1.8 seconds.\n- Largest Contentful Paint (LCP) — the loading time of the largest visible element (an image, a video, a block of text). The target is less than 2.5 seconds.\n- First Input Delay (FID) — the delay between the user's first interaction (a click, a key press) and the browser's response. Good is less than 100 ms.\n- Cumulative Layout Shift (CLS) — a measure of visual stability that assesses unexpected shifts in the layout. A good value is less than 0.1.\n- Time to Interactive (TTI) — the time at which the page becomes fully interactive (ready for interaction). The target is less than 3.8 seconds.\nAn example of measuring with the Performance API\n\nUse\nThese metrics are used to monitor the real user experience (RUM) and for lab testing (Lighthouse). Optimizing for them involves compressing images, minifying JavaScript, using caching and preloading critical resources.\nConclusion: metrics for a page's loading performance are necessary for assessing a site's speed and stability objectively. Measuring and optimizing them regularly helps improve the user experience and raise conversion and SEO positions.",
    codeExample: "// Getting the metrics through a PerformanceObserver\nconst observer = new PerformanceObserver((list) => {\n  const entries = list.getEntries();\n  entries.forEach(entry => {\n    console.log(`${entry.name}: ${entry.startTime} ms`);\n  });\n});\nobserver.observe({ type: 'largest-contentful-paint', buffered: true });\nobserver.observe({ type: 'first-input', buffered: true });\nobserver.observe({ type: 'layout-shift', buffered: true });",
  },
  155: {
    source: "Какие этапы проходят HTML, CSS и JavaScript при загрузке страницы?",
    question: "Which stages do HTML, CSS and JavaScript go through when a page loads?",
    shortAnswer: "The browser loads the HTML and starts parsing it, building the DOM. When it meets CSS it builds the CSSOM, while JavaScript blocks parsing until it has run. The DOM and CSSOM are then combined into the render tree, after which layout (computing the geometry) and paint (drawing the pixels) happen.",
    longAnswer: "The stages of a page's loading\nWhen the browser receives an HTML document, it goes through several key stages in order to display the page. This process is called the critical rendering path. Understanding these stages helps developers optimize loading speed.\n1. Parsing the HTML and building the DOM\nThe browser starts parsing the HTML from the first byte. It turns the markup into the Document Object Model (DOM) — a tree of nodes in which every tag becomes an element. The parsing proceeds in sequence but can be paused.\n2. Loading and parsing the CSS (the CSSOM)\nWhen the browser meets a `<link>` or `<style>` tag, it starts loading the CSS. Parsing the CSS does not block the building of the DOM, but it does block rendering — the browser will not show the page until it has built the CSSOM (the CSS object model). The CSSOM is a tree of rules that are applied to elements.\n3. The effect of JavaScript\nJavaScript can block the parsing of the HTML. If a script is met without the `async` or `defer` attributes, the browser stops building the DOM, loads and executes the script, and only then carries on. This can slow loading down. The `defer` attribute postpones execution until parsing has finished, while `async` runs the script asynchronously without blocking the parsing.\n4. Building the render tree\nOnce the DOM and the CSSOM are ready, the browser combines them into the render tree. Only the visible elements get into it (`<head>` or elements with `display: none` are excluded, for example).\n5. Layout (reflow)\nAt this stage the browser computes the exact dimensions and positions of every element on the page. It is a resource-intensive process, especially with complex layouts.\n6. Paint\nThe final stage is turning the render tree into pixels on the screen. The browser draws the layers and applies colours, shadows and textures.\nExample code demonstrating the blocking\n\nIn this example the browser will first load the CSS, then stop at the script, execute it, and only then carry on parsing and rendering.\nConclusion\nUnderstanding the stages of loading helps developers optimize performance: put the CSS in `<head>` and the JavaScript at the end of `<body>` with the `async` or `defer` attributes, so as not to block rendering and to improve the user experience.",
    codeExample: "<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html>\n<head>\n  <link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"styles.css\"> <!-- blocks rendering -->\n  <script src=\"script.js\"></script> <!-- blocks the parsing of the HTML -->\n</head>\n<body>\n  <p>The text will appear once the script has loaded</p>\n</body>\n</html>",
  },
  156: {
    source: "В чем разница между переменными в CSS и переменными в SCSS?",
    question: "What is the difference between variables in CSS and variables in SCSS?",
    shortAnswer: "CSS variables (custom properties) work at the browser level, support inheritance and can be changed dynamically through JavaScript or media queries. SCSS variables are compiled into static values at build time and cannot be changed at runtime. CSS variables are available in the DOM and suit theming, while SCSS variables suit the convenience of development and the reuse of values.",
    longAnswer: "The main difference: when they are computed\nCSS variables (custom properties) are computed by the browser while the page is running, whereas SCSS variables are computed at the preprocessor's compilation stage. This key difference determines all the other characteristics.\nDynamism and inheritance\nCSS variables are inherited through the DOM and can be overridden in any selector, including media queries and pseudo-classes. SCSS variables have lexical scope (blocks of code) and do not respond to changes in the DOM.\n\nUse in JavaScript\nCSS variables can be read and changed through JS, which is convenient for dynamic themes or animations. SCSS variables are unavailable at runtime.\n\nConclusion\nUse CSS variables for dynamic styles, theming and responsiveness. SCSS variables suit static values that do not change at runtime, and improving the readability of code at the development stage.",
    codeExample: "/* SCSS */\n$primary: blue;\n.button { color: $primary; }\n.dark .button { $primary: white; /* Error: this will not override it */ }\n\n/* CSS */\n:root { --primary: blue; }\n.button { color: var(--primary); }\n.dark .button { --primary: white; /* Works */ }\n\n// Reading a CSS variable\ngetComputedStyle(element).getPropertyValue('--primary');\n// Changing it\nelement.style.setProperty('--primary', 'red');",
  },
  157: {
    source: "Можно ли отказаться от SCSS в современных проектах?",
    question: "Can you do without SCSS in modern projects?",
    shortAnswer: "Yes, you can do without SCSS in modern projects. Modern CSS supports variables (custom properties), nesting, calc() and other features that used to be available only through preprocessors. SCSS is still useful for mixins, loops and the advanced organization of code, however. The decision depends on the team and on the project's requirements.",
    longAnswer: "Can you do without SCSS in modern projects?\nYes, in most modern projects you can do without SCSS, since native CSS has evolved considerably. Many capabilities that used to be available only through preprocessors are now implemented in CSS itself. This does not mean SCSS is entirely obsolete, however — it still provides convenient tools for complex projects.\nWhat does modern CSS offer?\n\n- Variables (custom properties): `--primary-color: #333;` with support for being changed dynamically through JavaScript.\n- Nesting: native support for nested selectors (Chrome 120+, Safari 17.2+).\n- Functions: `calc()`, `min()`, `max()`, `clamp()` for flexible calculations.\n- Colour functions: `color-mix()`, `light-dark()`.\n- CSS modules: `@import` with support for modules, and `@layer` for managing the cascade.\nA comparison\nSCSS:\n\nModern CSS:\n\nWhen is SCSS still useful?\n\n- Mixins and functions: for reusing complex blocks of styles.\n- Loops and conditionals: for generating a great many classes (a grid, for example).\n- Managing colours: the `darken()` and `lighten()` functions are more convenient than `color-mix()`.\n- Old projects: migration may be expensive and unjustified.\nConclusion\nDoing without SCSS is justified in new projects with modern browsers, where performance and reducing dependencies matter. For complex systems with a lot of repeated code, however, or when support for old browsers is necessary, SCSS remains a useful tool. The decision should be based on the project's requirements and on the team's experience.",
    codeExample: "$primary: #3498db;\n.button {\n  background: $primary;\n  &:hover {\n    background: darken($primary, 10%);\n  }\n}\n\n:root {\n  --primary: #3498db;\n}\n.button {\n  background: var(--primary);\n}\n.button:hover {\n  background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--primary), black 10%);\n}",
  },
  158: {
    source: "Что такое публичный API модуля во фронтенде?",
    question: "What is a module's public API on the frontend?",
    shortAnswer: "A module's public API is the set of functions, classes or variables the module exports explicitly for other parts of the application to use. Everything that is not exported is considered private and unavailable from outside. This lets you hide the internal implementation and provide only the interface that is needed. In JavaScript, for example, export is used to declare the public API.",
    longAnswer: "What is a module's public API?\nA module's public API is the contract the module provides to external code. It determines which functions, classes, constants or types are available to be imported and used in other modules. The internal details of the implementation, such as helper functions or private variables, stay hidden. This is a key principle of encapsulation that helps keep code clean, reduce coupling and make refactoring easier.\nHow does it work in JavaScript?\nIn modern JavaScript (ES6+) a module's public API is defined with the `export` keyword. You can export individual entities or use `export default` for the main export. Everything that is not exported is private and unavailable from outside.\n\nIn another file you can import only what you need:\n\nWhy is this needed?\nA module's public API lets you:\n\n- Hide internal complexity and implementation details.\n- Reduce the risk of internal functions being used by accident.\n- Make testing and the replacement of modules easier.\n- Create clear boundaries between the parts of an application.\nThis matters especially in large projects, where modules are developed by different teams or reused in several places.\nConclusion\nA module's public API is the foundation of a modular architecture on the frontend. It helps you organize code and make it more predictable and easier to maintain. Use explicit exports in order to control exactly what is available from outside, and avoid exporting everything indiscriminately.",
    codeExample: "// math.js — a module with a public API\n\nexport function add(a, b) {\n  return a + b;\n}\n\nexport const PI = 3.14159;\n\n// A private function, not exported\nfunction helper() {\n  console.log('Internal helper');\n}\n\n// app.js\nimport { add, PI } from './math.js';\n\nconsole.log(add(2, 3)); // 5\nconsole.log(PI); // 3.14159",
  },
  159: {
    source: "Как SEO связано с HTML?",
    question: "How is SEO connected with HTML?",
    shortAnswer: "SEO (Search Engine Optimization) depends directly on HTML, since search engines analyse a page's structure and content. The proper use of semantic tags (`<header>`, `<article>`, `<footer>`, for example) helps crawlers understand the hierarchy of the content. Meta tags such as `<title>` and `<meta name=\"description\">` affect how the page appears in search results. Clean, valid HTML improves a site's indexing and ranking.",
    longAnswer: "The connection between SEO and HTML\nSEO (Search Engine Optimization) and HTML are inseparably connected, since HTML is the basis of every web page. Search engines such as Google use crawlers to scan HTML code in order to understand a page's content, its structure and its relevance to search queries. Good HTML directly affects how search crawlers interpret and rank a site.\nThe key HTML elements for SEO\n\n- Semantic tags: using the tags `<header>`, `<nav>`, `<main>`, `<article>`, `<section>` and `<footer>` helps search engines understand the document's logical structure. This improves indexing and can increase the chances of appearing in rich snippets.\n- Meta tags: the `<title>` tag sets the page's title, which is shown in search results. The `<meta name=\"description\">` meta tag provides a brief description that may also be shown in the snippet. Filling these tags in properly with keywords increases the click-through rate (CTR).\n- Headings (h1-h6): the `<h1>` tag should be unique to every page and contain the main topic. The hierarchy of headings (h1, h2, h3) helps search crawlers assess the importance of the content.\n- The alt attribute on images: the `alt` attribute on an `<img>` tag describes the image's content. This not only improves accessibility for users with disabilities but also lets search engines index the images, which can attract additional traffic.\n- Clean, valid code: the absence of errors in the HTML (unclosed tags, for example) makes the page easier to scan. Using a proper structure and avoiding duplicated content also has a positive effect on SEO.\nA code example\n\nConclusion: SEO and HTML work in tandem: competent semantic markup and the proper use of meta tags make a site more comprehensible to search engines, which improves its visibility and ranking. Applying these principles is mandatory for any site aiming at organic traffic.",
    codeExample: "<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n    <meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n    <title>How to bake the perfect bread | Recipes</title>\n    <meta name=\"description\" content=\"A step-by-step recipe for home-made bread with a crisp crust. Ingredients, baking time and the secrets of the dough.\">\n</head>\n<body>\n    <header>\n        <nav><!-- The navigation --></nav>\n    </header>\n    <main>\n        <article>\n            <h1>The perfect home-made bread</h1>\n            <section>\n                <h2>Ingredients</h2>\n                <!-- The list of ingredients -->\n            </section>\n            <section>\n                <h2>The step-by-step process</h2>\n                <img src=\"dough.jpg\" alt=\"Kneaded bread dough\">\n            </section>\n        </article>\n    </main>\n    <footer><!-- The footer --></footer>\n</body>\n</html>",
  },
  160: {
    source: "Все ли скрипты блокируют парсинг HTML?",
    question: "Do all scripts block the parsing of HTML?",
    shortAnswer: "Not all scripts block the parsing of HTML. Ordinary scripts without attributes block parsing until they have been loaded and executed fully. Scripts with the async or defer attributes do not block parsing, but they behave differently: async loads in parallel and runs as soon as it has loaded, while defer runs after parsing has finished. This lets you speed a page's loading up.",
    longAnswer: "How scripts affect the parsing of HTML\nWhen the browser meets a `<script>` tag without attributes, it stops parsing the HTML, loads the script, executes it, and only then carries on taking the document apart. This can slow the display of the page down, especially if the script is heavy or loads from a slow server.\nThe async and defer attributes\nThe `async` attribute lets a script be loaded in parallel with the parsing of the HTML. As soon as the script has loaded it runs, pausing the parsing. The `defer` attribute also loads the script in parallel but postpones its execution until the document has finished being parsed. Neither attribute blocks parsing, but `defer` guarantees the order in which scripts run, while `async` does not.\nA code example\n\nConclusion\nUse `defer` for scripts that have to run after the DOM has loaded fully, and `async` for independent scripts such as analytics. This improves performance and the user experience.",
    codeExample: "<!-- A blocking script -->\n<script src=\"script.js\"></script>\n\n<!-- Non-blocking async -->\n<script src=\"script.js\" async></script>\n\n<!-- Non-blocking defer -->\n<script src=\"script.js\" defer></script>",
  },
  161: {
    source: "Что такое Cumulative Layout Shift (CLS)?",
    question: "What is Cumulative Layout Shift (CLS)?",
    shortAnswer: "Cumulative Layout Shift (CLS) is a metric that measures how often and how far the elements on a page shift unexpectedly while it loads. A high CLS degrades the user experience, since the user may click the wrong button by accident or lose their place in the text. CLS is computed as the sum of all the unexpected shifts of visible elements multiplied by their share of the viewport.",
    longAnswer: "What is Cumulative Layout Shift (CLS)?\nCumulative Layout Shift (CLS) is one of the three main Web Vitals metrics, and it assesses a web page's visual stability. It measures how often and by how much interface elements shift unexpectedly while the page is loading. A low CLS means the user sees stable content, while a high one means the elements \"jump\", which can cause irritation and interaction errors.\nHow is CLS calculated?\nCLS is computed as the sum of all the unexpected shifts of visible elements. For every shift a score = impact fraction * distance fraction is calculated. The impact fraction is the share of the viewport affected by the shift, and the distance fraction is the distance the element moved relative to the viewport. The final CLS is the sum of all such scores over the page's lifetime.\nExamples and the causes of a high CLS\nThe main causes of a high CLS:\n\n- Images and video without explicit dimensions (width/height).\n- Dynamically loaded fonts that change the size of the text.\n- Advertising blocks inserted after the main content has loaded.\n- Animations that change the position of elements.\nA practical example\nConsider HTML code that causes a shift:\n\nIf the image has no dimensions set, the browser will display the text first and then, once the image has loaded, the text will shift downwards. To avoid this, the dimensions have to be stated explicitly:\n\nOr you can use CSS containers with a fixed aspect ratio.\nConclusion\nCLS is an important metric for ensuring a comfortable user experience. It should be optimized, especially on pages with dynamic content, images and advertising. It is recommended always to set the dimensions of media elements and to avoid inserting content after the main layout has loaded.",
    codeExample: "<div>\n  <img src=\"image.jpg\" alt=\"Example\">\n  <p>Some text</p>\n</div>\n\n<div>\n  <img src=\"image.jpg\" alt=\"Example\" width=\"400\" height=\"300\">\n  <p>Some text</p>\n</div>",
  },
  162: {
    source: "Что такое First Contentful Paint (FCP)?",
    question: "What is First Contentful Paint (FCP)?",
    shortAnswer: "First Contentful Paint (FCP) is a performance metric that measures the time from the start of a page's loading to the moment the browser displays the first piece of content — text or an image, for example. It helps you assess how quickly the user sees that the page is starting to load. A good FCP value is less than 1.8 seconds.",
    longAnswer: "What is First Contentful Paint (FCP)?\nFirst Contentful Paint (FCP) is one of the key performance metrics for web pages and is part of the Core Web Vitals. It measures the time in milliseconds from the moment the user starts navigating to the page (typing a URL or clicking a link, for example) to the moment the browser first paints any text or graphical content. That may be text, an image, an SVG, a canvas or even a non-empty `<div>` element. FCP does not take background images or fonts loaded through `@font-face` into account.\nHow is FCP measured?\nFCP is recorded by the browser at the moment the first content is painted in the viewport. Tools such as Lighthouse, PageSpeed Insights or the Performance API in the browser are used to measure it. An example of getting FCP through JavaScript:\n\nWhy does FCP matter?\nFCP directly affects the user's perception of loading speed. If the FCP is high (more than 2.5 seconds), the user may think the page is not working and leave. A low FCP (less than 1.8 seconds) creates a sense of fast loading. The metric is especially critical for content sites, online shops and news portals, where the first impression decides a great deal.\nHow do you improve FCP?\n\n- Minimize render-blocking CSS and JavaScript.\n- Use critical CSS (inline styles for the first screen).\n- Optimize images (compression, the modern WebP format).\n- Set up resource caching through a CDN.\n- Remove unused code and libraries.\nConclusion\nFCP is the basic metric for assessing a page's initial loading. Improving it directly raises the user experience and can have a positive effect on SEO and conversion. It is recommended to monitor FCP in real conditions regularly and to optimize the critical rendering paths.",
    codeExample: "new PerformanceObserver((list) => {\n  const entries = list.getEntries();\n  entries.forEach(entry => {\n    console.log('FCP:', entry.startTime, 'ms');\n  });\n}).observe({ type: 'paint', buffered: true });",
  },
  163: {
    source: "Может ли обращение к DOM через JS вызвать reflow?",
    question: "Can reaching the DOM through JS cause a reflow?",
    shortAnswer: "Yes, reaching the DOM through JavaScript can cause a reflow. A reflow is the process of recalculating the geometry of a page's elements. When you read or change properties that affect elements' dimensions or positions (offsetHeight, clientWidth, scrollTop, for example), the browser may perform a forced reflow in order to return up-to-date values. This can have a negative effect on performance, especially with frequent operations.",
    longAnswer: "What a reflow is and how it relates to the DOM\nA reflow (or layout) is the rendering stage at which the browser computes the dimensions and positions of every element. It happens when a page first loads, and also on any change that may affect the geometry: resizing the window, adding or removing elements, changing styles (width, height, margin, padding and so on).\nJavaScript can both read and change the DOM. Some read operations, such as getting offsetHeight, clientWidth, scrollTop or getComputedStyle, can force the browser to perform a reflow in order to return up-to-date values. This is called a forced synchronous reflow.\nExamples of operations that cause a reflow\n\n- Reading properties: offsetHeight, offsetWidth, clientHeight, clientWidth, scrollTop, scrollLeft, getBoundingClientRect, getComputedStyle.\n- Changing styles that affect the geometry: width, height, margin, padding, display, position, float and so on.\n- Adding or removing elements from the DOM.\n- Changing the content (textContent, for example), if it affects the dimensions.\nA practical example\n\nIn this example every access to offsetHeight and every change to width may cause a reflow. To minimize the number of reflows it is recommended to group the read and write operations together, or to use techniques such as reading all the values before making any changes.\nHow to avoid unnecessary reflows\n\n- Use classes to change styles rather than changing properties directly.\n- Work with elements outside the flow (with display: none, for example — then change them and show them afterwards).\n- Use a documentFragment for adding elements in bulk.\n- Cache the values that may cause a reflow if they do not change.\nConclusion: understanding reflow matters for optimizing the performance of web pages. Avoid frequent forced reflows, especially in animations or when working with large lists. Group your operations together and use modern approaches such as requestAnimationFrame to synchronize changes with the browser's rendering cycle.",
    codeExample: "const el = document.getElementById('myElement');\n// Reading offsetHeight causes a reflow\nconst height = el.offsetHeight;\n// Changing the width also causes a reflow\nel.style.width = '200px';\n// If you read after the change, there will be another reflow\nconst newHeight = el.offsetHeight;",
  },
  164: {
    source: "Что происходит при встрече браузером CSS и JavaScript во время парсинга HTML?",
    question: "What happens when the browser meets CSS and JavaScript while parsing HTML?",
    shortAnswer: "When the browser meets CSS (external or internal), it carries on parsing the HTML but pauses rendering until the CSS has loaded fully and the CSSOM has been built. On meeting ordinary JavaScript (without async/defer), the browser stops parsing the HTML entirely, loads and executes the script, and only then carries on. This is done because the script may change the DOM or the CSSOM.",
    longAnswer: "The effect of CSS and JavaScript on the parsing of HTML\nThe browser builds the DOM (Document Object Model) from the HTML and the CSSOM (CSS Object Model) from the CSS. These two trees are combined into the render tree in order to paint the page. CSS and JavaScript affect this process differently.\nCSS (render blocking)\nWhen the HTML parser meets a `<link rel=\"stylesheet\">` or `<style>` tag, it does not stop parsing the HTML but it does block rendering. The browser will not display the page until the CSSOM has been built fully. This is necessary in order to avoid FOUC (a Flash of Unstyled Content).\nJavaScript (parser blocking)\nWhen the parser meets an ordinary `<script>` tag (without the `async` or `defer` attributes), it stops parsing the HTML entirely. The browser loads the script (if it is external), executes it, and only then carries on parsing. This is because the script may change the DOM (through `document.write`) or reach the CSSOM.\nAn example\n\nIn this example the browser will first load the CSS, then stop at the script, load and execute it, and only then carry on parsing and painting.\nOptimization\n\n- For CSS: use `media` attributes to indicate that the styles are not critical to the initial rendering.\n- For JavaScript: use `async` (the script runs as soon as it has loaded, without blocking the parsing) or `defer` (the script runs once the parsing of the HTML has finished).\nConclusion: understanding render blocking and parser blocking is critically important for optimizing a page's loading speed (the Core Web Vitals). Use `async`/`defer` for scripts and minimize the critical CSS for a fast first display.",
    codeExample: "<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html>\n<head>\n  <link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"styles.css\"> <!-- Render blocking -->\n  <script src=\"script.js\"></script> <!-- Parser blocking -->\n</head>\n<body>\n  <p>Hello</p>\n</body>\n</html>",
  },
  165: {
    source: "Почему важно учитывать null при получении данных из localStorage?",
    question: "Why is it important to allow for null when getting data out of localStorage?",
    shortAnswer: "The getItem method returns null if the key does not exist in localStorage. If you do not check that value, the code may fail with an error when you try to call methods on null — JSON.parse(null), for example. Always check the result of getItem for null before using it.",
    longAnswer: "Why null matters when working with localStorage\nThe `localStorage.getItem(key)` method returns `null` if the given key is absent from storage. This is standard behaviour that has to be allowed for in order to avoid runtime errors.\nAn example of the problem\nConsider some typical code without a check:\n\nIf the key 'user' is absent, `getItem` will return `null`, and `JSON.parse(null)` will throw an exception.\nThe correct approach\nAlways check the result before using it:\n\nYou can also use the nullish coalescing operator (`??`) to supply a default value:\n\nConclusion\nChecking for null when getting data out of localStorage is mandatory for an application to work reliably, especially on the first run or after storage has been cleared.",
    codeExample: "const data = JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem('user'));\nconsole.log(data.name); // An error if the key is absent\n\nconst raw = localStorage.getItem('user');\nif (raw === null) {\n  console.log('The data was not found');\n} else {\n  const data = JSON.parse(raw);\n  console.log(data.name);\n}\n\nconst data = JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem('user') ?? '{}');",
  },
  166: {
    source: "Какие методы есть у localStorage?",
    question: "What methods does localStorage have?",
    shortAnswer: "localStorage provides methods for storing data in the browser. The main methods are: setItem(key, value) for saving, getItem(key) for retrieving, removeItem(key) for deleting a single entry, clear() for clearing the whole store, plus key(index) for access by index and the length property for getting the number of entries.",
    longAnswer: "The main methods of localStorage\nlocalStorage is part of the Web Storage API and lets you store data in the browser with no expiry. The data is preserved even after the tab or the browser has been closed. All the methods are synchronous and work only with strings.\nThe methods and their use\n\n- setItem(key, value) — saves a value under a key. If the key already exists, the value is overwritten.\n- getItem(key) — returns the value for a key, or null if the key is not found.\n- removeItem(key) — deletes the entry for a key.\n- clear() — deletes every entry from localStorage.\n- key(index) — returns the name of the key at an index (starting from 0).\n- length — a property showing the number of items stored.\nA code example\n\nImportant characteristics\n\n- Every value is converted to a string automatically. For objects, use JSON.stringify() and JSON.parse().\n- The store is limited in size (usually 5-10 MB per domain).\n- The data is available only within a single protocol and domain.\nConclusion: localStorage is convenient for storing user settings, tokens or cached data that has to survive between sessions. You should not store confidential information in it, however, since the data is reachable through JavaScript.",
    codeExample: "// We save the data\nlocalStorage.setItem('username', 'John');\nlocalStorage.setItem('theme', 'dark');\n\n// We get the data\nconst user = localStorage.getItem('username'); // 'John'\n\n// We delete a single entry\nlocalStorage.removeItem('theme');\n\n// We get the number of entries\nconsole.log(localStorage.length); // 1\n\n// Access by index\nconst firstKey = localStorage.key(0); // 'username'\n\n// We clear the whole store\nlocalStorage.clear();",
  },
  167: {
    source: "Как получить данные из localStorage?",
    question: "How do you get data out of localStorage?",
    shortAnswer: "localStorage lets you store data in the browser with no expiry. To retrieve data, the getItem() method is used, which takes a key and returns a string. If the key is not found, null is returned. The data is stored as strings, so objects have to be serialized with JSON.stringify() and parsed with JSON.parse().",
    longAnswer: "What localStorage is and how to work with it\nlocalStorage is part of the Web Storage API and provides the ability to store data in the user's browser. The data is preserved between sessions and has no expiry until it is deleted by hand or through JavaScript. Unlike cookies, localStorage is not sent to the server with every HTTP request, which makes it efficient for storing settings, tokens or cached data.\nThe getItem() method\nTo get data out of localStorage the `getItem(key)` method is used, where key is a string corresponding to the key the data was saved under. The method returns a string, or null if the key is absent. Since localStorage stores only strings, JSON.parse() has to be used in order to work with objects or arrays.\n\nPractical use\nlocalStorage is often used to store user settings (a theme, a language), the state of the interface (expanded menus) or temporary data that does not require server-side processing. For example, you can save the filter a user has selected on a page so that it is restored on reload.\nConclusion\nlocalStorage is a simple, fast way of storing data on the client, suitable for small volumes of information (up to 5-10 MB). Use it to cache settings or state, but avoid storing sensitive data, since it is reachable through JavaScript.",
    codeExample: "// Saving an object\nconst user = { name: 'Alice', age: 30 };\nlocalStorage.setItem('user', JSON.stringify(user));\n\n// Retrieving and parsing it\nconst storedUser = localStorage.getItem('user');\nif (storedUser) {\n  const parsedUser = JSON.parse(storedUser);\n  console.log(parsedUser.name); // Alice\n} else {\n  console.log('The key was not found');\n}",
  },
  168: {
    source: "Почему данные в localStorage нужно сериализовать?",
    question: "Why does data in localStorage have to be serialized?",
    shortAnswer: "localStorage can store only strings. If you try to save an object, an array or a number, they will be converted to a string automatically through the toString() method, which leads to data being lost or to an incorrect format. Data therefore has to be serialized to JSON with JSON.stringify() before saving, and deserialized with JSON.parse() when read.",
    longAnswer: "Why does localStorage require serialization?\nlocalStorage is a mechanism for storing data in the browser that works exclusively with strings. When you try to save a value that is not a string (an object, an array, a number or a boolean, for example), the browser calls the `toString()` method on that value automatically. For objects this results in the string `[object Object]` being stored, which makes the data useless.\nHow do you work with data properly?\nTo save and restore complex data structures correctly, JSON serialization has to be used. The `JSON.stringify()` method turns an object or array into a JSON string, and `JSON.parse()` restores the original structure.\n\nExamples of the problems without serialization\n\n- Saving a number: `localStorage.setItem('count', 42)` — the string '42' will be stored, but when read it will be a string rather than a number.\n- Saving an array: `localStorage.setItem('arr', [1,2,3])` — the string '1,2,3' will be stored, which is not valid JSON.\n- Saving an object: `localStorage.setItem('obj', {a:1})` — the string '[object Object]' will be stored.\nConclusion\nSerialization through JSON is a mandatory step when working with localStorage for anything other than plain strings. It guarantees the integrity and the correct type of the data when it is used later.",
    codeExample: "// Saving an object\nconst user = { name: 'Alice', age: 30 };\nlocalStorage.setItem('user', JSON.stringify(user));\n\n// Reading and restoring it\nconst storedUser = JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem('user'));\nconsole.log(storedUser.name); // 'Alice'",
  },
  169: {
    source: "Какие ошибки могут возникнуть при работе с localStorage?",
    question: "What errors can arise when working with localStorage?",
    shortAnswer: "The main errors when working with localStorage include exceeding the storage limit (usually 5-10 MB), which raises a QuotaExceededError exception. Security problems can also arise, such as XSS attacks, since the data is stored in plain form. In addition, localStorage does not support automatic synchronization between tabs, and the data may be lost when the browser's cache is cleared.",
    longAnswer: "The main errors when working with localStorage\nlocalStorage is a mechanism for storing data on the client side in the browser. Despite its simplicity it has a number of limitations and potential problems that are important to bear in mind during development.\nExceeding the storage limit\nMost browsers set a limit on the size of the data in localStorage (usually 5-10 MB per domain). When you try to save data beyond that limit, a `QuotaExceededError` exception is raised. This is especially relevant for applications that store large volumes of data, such as a cache of images or logs.\n\nSecurity problems\nThe data in localStorage is stored in plain form and is reachable by any JavaScript code on the page. This makes it vulnerable to XSS attacks. An attacker can gain access to sensitive information such as authentication tokens or user settings. Never store passwords, API keys or other confidential data in localStorage.\nNo synchronization between tabs\nChanges to localStorage in one tab are not reflected automatically in other tabs of the same domain. To synchronize them you have to use the `storage` event, which fires when the data changes in another tab.\n\nData loss when the cache is cleared\nThe user can clear the browser's data, localStorage included, at any moment. This will cause every piece of saved data to be lost. localStorage is therefore not suitable for storing critically important information that has to be preserved permanently.\nConclusion\nlocalStorage is convenient for storing small volumes of non-critical data, such as interface settings or temporary state. For storing sensitive information, or data that requires synchronization, however, it is better to use server-side solutions or safer mechanisms such as sessionStorage or IndexedDB.",
    codeExample: "try {\n  localStorage.setItem('key', 'largeData');\n} catch (e) {\n  if (e.name === 'QuotaExceededError') {\n    console.error('The localStorage limit has been exceeded');\n  }\n}\n\nwindow.addEventListener('storage', (event) => {\n  console.log('The data has changed:', event.key, event.newValue);\n});",
  },
  170: {
    source: "Как сохранить данные в localStorage?",
    question: "How do you save data in localStorage?",
    shortAnswer: "localStorage lets you store data in the browser with no expiry. To save, the method setItem('key', 'value') is used. The data is stored as strings, so objects have to be converted with JSON.stringify(). The data is reachable from any window of the same origin.",
    longAnswer: "What localStorage is and how to work with it\nlocalStorage is part of the Web Storage API and provides the ability to store key-value pairs in the browser. The data is preserved even after the tab or the browser has been closed and has no expiry. This is convenient for storing user settings, the state of the interface or caching small volumes of data.\nSaving data\nThe main method for writing is `setItem(key, value)`. Both parameters have to be strings. If you need to save an object or an array, use `JSON.stringify()` to convert it into a string.\n\nReading and deleting\nTo retrieve data use `getItem(key)`, which returns a string or `null` if the key is absent. To delete a single item use `removeItem(key)`; to clear everything, `clear()`.\n\nImportant characteristics\n\n- The data is stored as strings — any non-string types have to be converted explicitly.\n- The size of the store is limited (usually 5–10 MB per domain).\n- localStorage is synchronous — operations can block the main thread, so do not use it for large volumes of data.\n- The data is available only to the same protocol, domain and port (the same-origin policy).\nConclusion\nlocalStorage is a simple, effective way of storing small volumes of data on the client side. Use it to save user settings, the state of the interface or cached data that does not require synchronization with the server. For more complex scenarios (large volumes, working with files), consider IndexedDB or server-side storage.",
    codeExample: "// We save a string\nlocalStorage.setItem('username', 'Alice');\n\n// We save an object\nconst user = { name: 'Bob', age: 30 };\nlocalStorage.setItem('user', JSON.stringify(user));\n\n// We save an array\nconst colors = ['red', 'green', 'blue'];\nlocalStorage.setItem('colors', JSON.stringify(colors));\n\n// We read a string\nconst name = localStorage.getItem('username');\n\n// We read an object (not forgetting to parse it)\nconst savedUser = JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem('user'));\n\n// We delete a single item\nlocalStorage.removeItem('colors');\n\n// We clear the whole store\nlocalStorage.clear();",
  },
  171: {
    source: "Можно ли использовать XML и JSON в различных типах запросов?",
    question: "Can XML and JSON be used in various kinds of request?",
    shortAnswer: "Yes, XML and JSON can be used in various kinds of request, such as GET, POST, PUT and DELETE. JSON is more often applied in REST APIs because of its lightness and simplicity, while XML is used in SOAP and in configuration files. The choice depends on the project's requirements and on compatibility with other systems.",
    longAnswer: "Using XML and JSON in requests\nXML and JSON are formats for exchanging data that can be used in various kinds of HTTP request. JSON (JavaScript Object Notation) is more popular in modern web applications thanks to its compactness and its convenience for JavaScript. XML (eXtensible Markup Language) is often applied in enterprise systems and protocols such as SOAP.\nExamples of use\nIn a REST API, JSON is normally passed in the body of POST, PUT or PATCH requests, and it can also be part of the response to a GET request. XML can be used in the same way, but it requires additional processing on both the client and the server.\n\nConclusion\nXML and JSON can be used in any kind of request, but JSON is preferable for lightweight, fast applications, while XML suits systems that require a strict structure and extensibility — in SOAP services, for example.",
    codeExample: "// An example of JSON in a POST request\nfetch('/api/users', {\n  method: 'POST',\n  headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },\n  body: JSON.stringify({ name: 'John', age: 30 })\n});\n\n// An example of XML in a POST request (using SOAP)\nconst xml = '<soap:Envelope xmlns:soap=\"http://schemas.xmlsoap.org/soap/envelope/\"><soap:Body>' +\n  '<GetUser><id>123</id></GetUser></soap:Body></soap:Envelope>';\nfetch('/api/soap', {\n  method: 'POST',\n  headers: { 'Content-Type': 'text/xml' },\n  body: xml\n});",
  },
  172: {
    source: "Как используется Figma в процессе тестирования?",
    question: "How is Figma used in the testing process?",
    shortAnswer: "Figma is used by testers to check a finished interface against the mockups. In Figma you can measure spacing and the dimensions of elements and check colours and fonts. This helps you find discrepancies between the design and the implementation. Figma is also convenient for writing test cases based on the visual components.",
    longAnswer: "Figma's role in testing\nFigma is a tool for designing interfaces that is used actively by testers to check that markup matches the mockups. Unlike static images, Figma provides access to exact dimensions, spacing, colours and styles, which makes it indispensable for UI testing.\nHow testers work with Figma\n\n- Checking dimensions and spacing: with the \"Measure\" tool you can check whether the actual pixel values match the design.\n- Checking colours: Figma shows HEX, RGB and other colour codes, which lets you be sure the palette is accurate.\n- Analysing elements' states: the mockups often show hover, active and disabled states that have to be checked in the code.\n- Writing test cases: from Figma's components you can create checklists for regression testing.\nAn example of use\n\nConclusion\nFigma helps testers uncover visual defects accurately and quickly, especially at the acceptance-testing stage. Using Figma in the QA process raises the quality of the product and reduces the time spent communicating with designers.",
    codeExample: "// An example of checking spacing in the code (CSS) and in Figma\n// Figma: the gap between the button and the input field = 16px\n// In the code:\n.button {\n  margin-bottom: 16px; // it should match\n}",
  },
  173: {
    source: "Что делать, если в веб-приложении после нажатия кнопки ничего не происходит?",
    question: "What do you do if nothing happens in a web application after a button is pressed?",
    shortAnswer: "Open the browser's developer tools (F12). Check the console for JavaScript errors. Make sure an event handler has been attached to the button correctly. Check the Network tab in order to see whether requests are being sent to the server. If there are no errors, check whether the script is blocked or the event is being intercepted.",
    longAnswer: "Diagnosing the problem\nWhen nothing happens after a button is pressed, the first thing to do is open the browser's developer tools (usually the F12 key). This is the main tool for debugging web applications.\nChecking the console\nGo to the Console tab. This is where all the JavaScript errors, warnings and logs appear. If there is an error, it normally contains a description and the line number of the code. The error 'Uncaught TypeError: Cannot read properties of null', for example, indicates that you are trying to reach a DOM element that does not exist.\nChecking the event handler\nMake sure an event handler has been attached to the button. This can be done through the onclick attribute in the HTML or through addEventListener in JavaScript. An example of attaching one correctly:\n\nChecking the network requests\nIf the event handler is there but nothing happens, go to the Network tab. Press the button and see whether any new requests appear. If there are no requests, the problem is in the client-side code. If there is a request, check its status and the server's response.\nOther possible causes\n\n- The script failed to load because of an error elsewhere in the code\n- The event is being intercepted by another handler (event.stopPropagation)\n- The button is inside a form and the form is being submitted instead of the handler being called\n- A browser extension is blocking the script from running\nConclusion\nA systematic approach to debugging — checking the console, the event handlers and the network requests — lets you uncover most problems quickly. Use the developer tools as your main diagnostic instrument.",
    codeExample: "// HTML: <button id=\"myButton\">Click me</button>\n// JavaScript:\ndocument.getElementById('myButton').addEventListener('click', function() {\n  console.log('Button clicked!');\n  // Your code\n});",
  },
  174: {
    source: "Какие знания HTML и CSS необходимы тестировщику?",
    question: "What knowledge of HTML and CSS does a tester need?",
    shortAnswer: "A tester needs to understand the structure of an HTML document and be able to find elements by tag, class and ID. It is important to know the basics of CSS: selectors and the properties for working with dimensions, colour and positioning. This helps you describe bugs precisely and check markup.",
    longAnswer: "Why a tester needs HTML and CSS\nHTML and CSS are the basis of every web interface. A tester who understands these technologies can localize defects more precisely, find elements on a page faster and describe problems competently in a bug tracker. Without this knowledge it is hard to tell a markup error from a logical error in the application.\nThe main knowledge of HTML\n\n- The document's structure: `<html>`, `<head>`, `<body>`.\n- Semantic tags: `<header>`, `<main>`, `<footer>`, `<section>`.\n- Attributes: `id`, `class`, `href`, `src`, `alt`.\n- Forms: `<form>`, `<input>`, `<button>`, `<select>`.\nThe main knowledge of CSS\n\n- Selectors: by tag, by class, by ID, nested selectors.\n- Properties: `color`, `background`, `font-size`, `margin`, `padding`, `display`, `position`.\n- Units: px, em, rem, %, vw, vh.\n- Responsiveness: media queries (`@media`).\nAn example of use in testing\nSuppose a button cannot be clicked. The tester looks at the code:\n\nThey see the `disabled` attribute — that is not a markup bug but the application's logic. Or if an element is hidden:\n\nThe tester understands that the element is not displayed because of the CSS rather than because of an error.\nConclusion\nKnowing HTML and CSS at a basic level lets a tester find the causes of defects faster, describe them more precisely and work with developers more effectively. It is the mandatory minimum for working with web applications.",
    codeExample: "<button class=\"submit-btn\" disabled>Submit</button>\n\n<div style=\"display: none;\">A hidden block</div>",
  },
  175: {
    source: "Какие бывают классы HTTP-ответов (информационные, успешные, ошибки клиента, ошибки сервера)?",
    question: "What classes of HTTP response are there (informational, successful, client errors, server errors)?",
    shortAnswer: "HTTP responses fall into 5 classes: 1xx (informational) — the request has been accepted and processing continues; 2xx (successful) — the request was processed successfully; 3xx (redirection) — additional actions are required; 4xx (client errors) — the problem is on the client's side; 5xx (server errors) — the problem is on the server's side. Each class has its own range of codes — 200 OK, 404 Not Found, 500 Internal Server Error, for example.",
    longAnswer: "The classes of HTTP response\nHTTP statuses are grouped into five classes, each of which denotes a particular type of result for the processing of a request. The code's first digit indicates the class: 1xx, 2xx, 3xx, 4xx or 5xx. This is standardized in RFC 7231 and helps developers diagnose problems quickly.\n1xx — Informational\nThese codes mean that the request has been accepted and the server is still processing it. They are rarely used in everyday practice but matter for protocols such as WebSocket. An example: `100 Continue` — the client may carry on sending the request body.\n2xx — Successful\nThe request was processed successfully. The most widespread are: `200 OK` (standard success), `201 Created` (a resource was created), `204 No Content` (success with no response body).\n3xx — Redirection\nAdditional actions are required from the client, normally navigating to a different URL. Examples: `301 Moved Permanently` (the resource has moved permanently), `302 Found` (a temporary redirect), `304 Not Modified` (a cached response).\n4xx — Client errors\nThe problem is on the client's side: an invalid request, missing permissions or a missing resource. Common ones: `400 Bad Request` (incorrect syntax), `401 Unauthorized` (authentication is required), `403 Forbidden` (access is denied), `404 Not Found` (the resource was not found).\n5xx — Server errors\nThe server could not fulfil the request because of an internal problem. Examples: `500 Internal Server Error` (a general error), `502 Bad Gateway` (an invalid response from an upstream server), `503 Service Unavailable` (the server is temporarily unavailable).\nA code example\n\nThis example checks the class of the response: 2xx is success, 4xx a client error, 5xx a server error.\nConclusion\nKnowing the classes of HTTP status is necessary for handling responses correctly in web applications, debugging APIs and building reliable client-server interactions.",
    codeExample: "fetch('/api/data')\n  .then(response => {\n    if (response.status === 200) {\n      return response.json();\n    } else if (response.status === 404) {\n      console.error('Resource not found');\n    } else if (response.status >= 500) {\n      console.error('Server error');\n    }\n  });",
  },
  176: {
    source: "Какие правила синтаксиса JSON необходимо соблюдать?",
    question: "Which rules of JSON syntax have to be observed?",
    shortAnswer: "JSON requires strict syntax: keys and strings in double quotes, numbers without quotes, the boolean values true/false, and null. The data is separated by commas, objects go in curly braces and arrays in square brackets. Trailing commas and comments are not allowed.",
    longAnswer: "The main rules of JSON syntax\nJSON (JavaScript Object Notation) is a lightweight data-interchange format based on a subset of JavaScript. Its syntax is strict and requires the following rules to be observed:\n\n- Keys and strings are always enclosed in double quotes (`\"`). Single quotes are not allowed.\n- Numbers are written without quotes and may be integers or floating-point (`42`, `3.14`, for example).\n- The boolean values are `true` and `false` (without quotes).\n- Null is `null` (without quotes).\n- Objects are enclosed in curly braces `{}`, and the key-value pairs are separated by commas.\n- Arrays are enclosed in square brackets `[]`, and the elements are separated by commas.\n- Trailing commas (a comma after the last element) are forbidden.\n- Comments are not supported.\nAn example of valid JSON\n\nWhere it is applied\nJSON is used in APIs, configuration files, data storage and exchange between client and server. Observing the syntax is mandatory for parsing and serialization.\nConclusion: JSON is a simple, strict format that is easy to read and process. Its syntax rules ensure it is unambiguous and compatible between systems.",
    codeExample: "{\n  \"name\": \"Alice\",\n  \"age\": 30,\n  \"isStudent\": false,\n  \"hobbies\": [\"reading\", \"coding\"],\n  \"address\": null\n}",
  },
  177: {
    source: "Какие ошибки могут быть в JSON?",
    question: "What errors can there be in JSON?",
    shortAnswer: "Errors in JSON can be syntactic: extra commas, the wrong quotes (only double ones are allowed), missing quotes on keys, invalid data types (undefined, for example). Errors can also arise during parsing if the string does not match the format. Problems with escaping characters and with nesting are common.",
    longAnswer: "The main errors in JSON\nJSON (JavaScript Object Notation) is a text format for exchanging data, based on JavaScript's syntax. It is stricter, however: keys and strings have to be in double quotes, and values may only be of particular types (string, number, object, array, boolean, null).\nSyntactic errors\n\n- Extra commas: a comma after the last element in an object or array is not allowed. Example: `{\"a\": 1,}` — an error.\n- The wrong quotes: only double quotes (`\"`) are allowed. Single quotes or backticks will cause an error.\n- Missing quotes on keys: keys have to be strings in quotes. `{key: \"value\"}` is wrong; it has to be `{\"key\": \"value\"}`.\n- Invalid values: `undefined`, `NaN` and `Infinity` are not supported. Use `null`.\n- Escaping errors: special characters (a quote inside a string, for example) have to be escaped with a backslash: `\\\"`.\nCode examples\n\nConclusion\nKnowing the typical JSON errors helps you avoid problems when passing data between client and server, and when working with configuration files. Always check your JSON with a validator and use try-catch when parsing.",
    codeExample: "// Invalid JSON\nconst badJson = '{\"name\": \"John\", \"age\": undefined}';\n// Valid JSON\nconst goodJson = '{\"name\": \"John\", \"age\": null}';\n\n// Parsing with error handling\ntry {\n  const data = JSON.parse(badJson);\n} catch (e) {\n  console.error('Parsing error:', e.message);\n}",
  },
  178: {
    source: "Какие типы данных существуют в JSON?",
    question: "What data types exist in JSON?",
    shortAnswer: "JSON supports six data types: string, number, boolean, null, array and object. Strings are always in double quotes, numbers may be integers or floating-point, and arrays and objects can be nested. Other types, such as dates or undefined, are not supported directly.",
    longAnswer: "The main JSON data types\nJSON (JavaScript Object Notation) is a lightweight data-interchange format based on a subset of JavaScript's syntax. It supports exactly six data types, which let you represent practically any structured information.\n\n- String — a sequence of characters in double quotes; it supports escape sequences (`\\n`, `\\t`, for example). Example: \"Hello, world!\".\n- Number — an integer or a floating-point number, which may be negative. It does not support NaN, Infinity or leading zeros. Examples: 42, 3.14, -7.\n- Boolean — true or false.\n- Null — a special value denoting the absence of data. It is written as null.\n- Array — an ordered list of values enclosed in square brackets. The elements may be of any type, including other arrays and objects. Example: [1, \"two\", false].\n- Object — an unordered collection of key-value pairs enclosed in curly braces. The keys are always strings in double quotes. Example: {\"name\": \"Alice\", \"age\": 30}.\nAn example of use\n\nLimitations\nJSON does not support dates, functions, undefined, symbols or binary data. To pass dates, strings in ISO 8601 format are normally used. There is no support for comments either — that was done to keep parsing simple.\nConclusion\nKnowing JSON's data types is necessary for serializing and deserializing data correctly when exchanging it between client and server, and for working with configuration files and APIs.",
    codeExample: "{\n  \"user\": {\n    \"id\": 123,\n    \"name\": \"John Doe\",\n    \"active\": true,\n    \"roles\": [\"admin\", \"editor\"],\n    \"metadata\": null\n  }\n}",
  },
  179: {
    source: "Что такое PSI и как он используется?",
    question: "What is PSI and how is it used?",
    shortAnswer: "PSI (PageSpeed Insights) is a tool from Google for analysing the speed and performance of web pages. It provides metrics such as First Contentful Paint (FCP) and Largest Contentful Paint (LCP), along with recommendations for improvement. It is used to uncover bottlenecks in a site's loading and to improve the user experience.",
    longAnswer: "What is PSI?\nPSI (PageSpeed Insights) is a free tool from Google that analyses the performance of web pages on both mobile devices and desktops. It is based on data from the real user experience (CrUX) and on lab tests (Lighthouse). PSI's main purpose is to help developers understand how quickly their site loads and to give them specific advice on optimization.\nHow is it used?\nPSI is used to assess key performance metrics such as First Contentful Paint (FCP), Largest Contentful Paint (LCP), Cumulative Layout Shift (CLS) and others. These metrics affect ranking in Google's search results and users' satisfaction. The tool provides numerical scores (from 0 to 100) and detailed reports with recommendations.\nAn example of use\nSuppose you want to check your site's performance. You enter the URL in PSI and get a report. If the LCP exceeds 2.5 seconds, PSI will suggest optimizing the images or using caching. An example of code for optimizing an image:\n\nPSI also recommends minifying CSS and JavaScript, using compression and improving the server's response time.\nConclusion\nPSI is an indispensable tool for web developers who want to improve loading speed and the user experience. Using it regularly helps keep a site in line with modern performance and SEO standards.",
    codeExample: "<img src=\"image.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"A description\" />",
  },
  180: {
    source: "Как использовать HAR-файлы для анализа багов?",
    question: "How do you use HAR files to analyse bugs?",
    shortAnswer: "A HAR file (HTTP Archive) is a JSON log of all the browser's network requests. It helps developers analyse the loading of resources, server errors and bottlenecks. To analyse one, open the HAR in the developer tools or in a specialized service in order to see the timings, headers and statuses of the requests.",
    longAnswer: "What is a HAR file?\nHAR (HTTP Archive) is a file format that stores a complete record of all the network interactions between the browser and the server. It contains data about every request: the URL, the method, the headers, the duration, the size of the response and the status. HAR files are indispensable for reproducing and analysing bugs related to performance, the loading of resources or API errors.\nHow do you create a HAR file?\nIn Chromium-based browsers (Chrome, Edge), open the developer tools (F12), go to the Network tab, perform the actions that reproduce the bug, then right-click any request and choose 'Save all as HAR with content'. In Firefox it is the same: the Network tab → the 'Export HAR' button.\nAn example of analysing a HAR\nSuppose a user complains that a page takes a long time to load. Opening the HAR, you see a request for a large image with a timing of 5 seconds. This indicates that the image needs optimizing or that a CDN should be used. You can also find 404 or 500 errors that are not visible on the page.\n\nWhere should you apply it?\nHAR files are useful when debugging slow loading, API errors, caching problems and mismatched headers. They let developers and testers reproduce the environment exactly and find the root of the problem.\nConclusion: HAR files are a powerful tool for analysing network bugs and optimizing performance, especially when working with client-server applications.",
    codeExample: "// An example of a HAR's structure (simplified)\n{\n  \"log\": {\n    \"entries\": [\n      {\n        \"request\": {\n          \"url\": \"https://example.com/api/data\",\n          \"method\": \"GET\"\n        },\n        \"response\": {\n          \"status\": 200,\n          \"content\": {\n            \"size\": 1234\n          }\n        },\n        \"timings\": {\n          \"wait\": 300,\n          \"receive\": 50\n        }\n      }\n    ]\n  }\n}",
  },
  181: {
    source: "Как тестировать фильтры на сайте?",
    question: "How do you test the filters on a site?",
    shortAnswer: "Testing filters involves checking that each filter works correctly on its own, that their combinations work, and that boundary values are handled. It is important to check that the filters display the filtered data correctly and do not break other parts of the interface. It is also worth testing performance with a large amount of data.",
    longAnswer: "The main aspects of testing filters\nTesting the filters on a site is the process of checking that the mechanisms for selecting data by given criteria work correctly. It is necessary in order to ensure that the user's search for information is accurate and convenient. The main kinds of test include functional testing, boundary-value testing and integration testing.\nFunctional testing\nCheck that every filter works in isolation. If there is a price filter, for example, make sure that when the range from 100 to 200 is selected, only the products in that price segment are displayed.\n\nTesting combinations of filters\nCheck that several filters work together correctly. A filter by category and one by price, for example, should intersect rather than replace one another.\nBoundary values\nTest the extreme cases: empty results, the maximum and minimum values, and invalid input (negative numbers, for example).\nIntegration testing\nMake sure the filters do not break other components such as pagination or sorting. After a filter has been applied, for example, the pagination should recalculate the number of pages.\nConclusion: testing filters is necessary in order to ensure the user interface is reliable and convenient. It matters especially in online shops, catalogues and any system with a large volume of data, where the accuracy of filtering directly affects the user experience.",
    codeExample: "// An example of checking a price filter\nconst items = [\n  { name: 'A', price: 150 },\n  { name: 'B', price: 250 },\n  { name: 'C', price: 50 }\n];\nconst filterByPrice = (items, min, max) =>\n  items.filter(item => item.price >= min && item.price <= max);\nconsole.log(filterByPrice(items, 100, 200)); // [{ name: 'A', price: 150 }]",
  },
  182: {
    source: "Для чего используется вкладка Network в DevTools?",
    question: "What is the Network tab in DevTools used for?",
    shortAnswer: "The Network tab in DevTools is used to monitor all the network requests a page makes: loading the HTML, CSS, JavaScript and images, and API requests. It helps you analyse loading times, response statuses and file sizes and uncover bottlenecks. It is a key tool for optimizing performance and debugging network problems.",
    longAnswer: "The purpose of the Network tab\nThe Network tab in the browser's developer tools (DevTools) is intended for tracking and analysing all the network requests a web page performs. It lets a developer see which resources are loading, how long it takes and which errors arise. It is an indispensable tool for optimizing performance, debugging API integrations and understanding how an application behaves on the network.\nThe main capabilities\n\n- Viewing the requests: it displays every request (XHR, Fetch, images, scripts, styles) in chronological order.\n- The details of a request: clicking a request lets you see its Headers, Response body, Payload, Timing and cookies.\n- Filtering: you can filter the requests by type (XHR, JS, CSS, Img, Media, Font, Doc, WS) or by text in the URL.\n- Analysing performance: the Timing tab shows how long the DNS resolution, the connection, the TLS handshake, the sending of the request and the receiving of the response took.\n- Emulating conditions: you can simulate a slow connection (throttling) or disable the cache.\nAn example of use\nSuppose you are developing an application in React that loads data through an API. You want to check that the request is sent correctly and that the response arrives without errors. Open DevTools (F12), go to the Network tab and reload the page or perform the action that triggers the request. You will see the request to your API. Click it:\n\nIn Network you will see the response status (200 OK, for example), the headers (Content-Type, Authorization), the response body (JSON with the data) and the duration. If the status is 404 or 500, you will notice it at once and be able to fix it.\nConclusion\nThe Network tab is the main tool for debugging the network interaction of web applications. It helps you find problems with the loading of resources quickly, analyse performance and check that API requests are correct. Use it during development and testing in order to ensure your application works reliably.",
    codeExample: "// An example of code that makes a request\nfetch('https://api.example.com/users')\n  .then(response => response.json())\n  .then(data => console.log(data))\n  .catch(error => console.error('Error:', error));",
  },
  183: {
    source: "Для чего используется Qt?",
    question: "What is Qt used for?",
    shortAnswer: "Qt is a cross-platform framework for developing applications in C++. It is used to create graphical interfaces, desktop and mobile applications, and embedded systems. Qt provides a rich set of libraries for working with networking, databases, multimedia and graphics. Thanks to its modularity and its support for many platforms, Qt is popular in the industry for building complex interfaces.",
    longAnswer: "Qt is a powerful cross-platform framework written in C++ that lets you develop applications with a graphical interface (GUI) for various operating systems, including Windows, macOS, Linux, Android and iOS. It was created by the Qt Group and is widely used in industry, from desktop programs to embedded systems.\nQt's main capabilities\n\n- Cross-platform support: the same code can be compiled for different operating systems without changes.\n- A rich set of widgets: buttons, tables, trees, text fields and other interface elements.\n- Signals and slots: a mechanism for connecting events and handlers that simplifies development.\n- Modularity: Qt consists of many modules, such as QtWidgets, QtNetwork, QtSQL, QtMultimedia and others.\nAn example of a simple application in Qt\n\nThis code creates a window with a button. Qt handles the events and the drawing automatically.\nWhere is Qt applied?\n\n- Desktop applications: VLC Media Player, VirtualBox and Telegram Desktop, for example.\n- Embedded systems: Qt for MCU lets you run interfaces on microcontrollers.\n- Mobile applications: Qt supports Android and iOS.\n- Industry: control systems, medical equipment, automotive interfaces.\nConclusion: Qt is ideally suited to building cross-platform applications with a rich interface, especially when high performance and support for many platforms are required. It is worth choosing for projects where reliability and long-term support matter.",
    codeExample: "#include <QApplication>\n#include <QPushButton>\n\nint main(int argc, char *argv[]) {\n    QApplication app(argc, argv);\n    QPushButton button(\"Hello, Qt!\");\n    button.resize(200, 100);\n    button.show();\n    return app.exec();\n}",
  },
  184: {
    source: "Как проверяется соответствие реализации дизайн-макетам?",
    question: "How do you check that an implementation matches the design mockups?",
    shortAnswer: "Whether an implementation matches the design mockups is checked by visual comparison (pixel perfect) with tools such as Figma, Percy or Chromatic. Automated visual regression tests are also used. It is important to check not only the dimensions and colours but also the spacing, the typography and the behaviour at different resolutions.",
    longAnswer: "The process of checking against the design mockups\nChecking that an implementation matches the design mockups is a key stage of quality control for markup. It involves both manual and automated methods, in order to be sure that the interface looks and behaves the way the designer intended.\nManual checking (pixel perfect)\nThe developer or QA engineer overlays a screenshot of the finished page on the mockup in Figma or Photoshop, using an overlay mode. The following are checked:\n\n- The dimensions and spacing of elements\n- Colours and gradients\n- Typography (fonts, sizes, line heights)\n- The states of elements (hover, active, focus)\nAutomated checking (visual regression testing)\nTools such as Percy, Chromatic or Applitools take screenshots of components and compare them with reference images. When a difference is found, a notification is sent. An example of the configuration for Storybook with Chromatic:\n\nTools for checking\n\n- Figma — manual comparison through overlaying\n- Percy / Chromatic — automated visual regression testing\n- BrowserStack — checking on real devices\nConclusion: checking against the mockups regularly helps you avoid an accumulation of visual defects and speeds up sign-off with the designer. Automation is especially useful in large projects with frequent changes.",
    codeExample: "// .storybook/main.js\nmodule.exports = {\n  stories: ['../src/**/*.stories.@(js|jsx|ts|tsx)'],\n  addons: ['@storybook/addon-essentials'],\n  framework: '@storybook/react',\n};\n// Running it: npx chromatic --project-token=YOUR_TOKEN",
  },
  185: {
    source: "Как дебажить frontend-приложение в браузере?",
    question: "How do you debug a frontend application in the browser?",
    shortAnswer: "To debug a frontend application, the browser's built-in developer tools (DevTools) are used. The main capabilities are: inspecting and changing the HTML/CSS, setting breakpoints in JavaScript code, analysing network requests and profiling performance. DevTools can be opened with the F12 key or through the context menu.",
    longAnswer: "The basics of debugging in the browser\nThe developer tools (DevTools) are a set of facilities built into the browser that let you analyse and debug frontend applications. They are available in every modern browser (Chrome, Firefox, Edge, Safari) and are opened with the F12 key or through the context menu.\nThe key capabilities of DevTools\n\n- Elements (the inspector) — inspecting and editing the HTML structure and CSS styles in real time. It lets you experiment with your markup without reloading the page.\n- Console — the output of errors, warnings and logs. You can run arbitrary JavaScript code in order to test a hypothesis.\n- Sources — inspecting the source code, setting breakpoints, stepping through the code and viewing the values of variables.\n- Network — analysing every network request: loading time, headers, response body. It helps you diagnose problems with an API or with the loading of resources.\n- Performance — recording and analysing a page's performance: loading, rendering, the execution of scripts.\nAn example of using breakpoints\n\nWorking with source maps\nIf the code is minified or transpiled (from TypeScript or React, for example), source maps let you see the original source code rather than the compiled output. To get this, the generation of source maps has to be enabled in your bundler (Webpack, Vite).\nConclusion\nDevTools are an indispensable tool for every frontend developer. They let you find and fix errors in markup, logic and performance quickly without leaving the browser.",
    codeExample: "// The source code of the function that needs debugging\nfunction calculateTotal(price, tax) {\n  const taxAmount = price * tax;\n  const total = price + taxAmount;\n  return total;\n}\n\n// In the DevTools Sources tab we find this file,\n// and click the line number of const total = ... in order to set a breakpoint.\n// When the function is called, execution will stop on that line,\n// and you can look at the values of price, tax and taxAmount in the Scope panel.",
  },
  186: {
    source: "Как можно диагностировать медленную загрузку сайта без доступа к коду?",
    question: "How can you diagnose a slow-loading site without access to the code?",
    shortAnswer: "A slow-loading site can be diagnosed through the Network tab in the browser's developer tools. There you can see the loading time of every resource, the sizes of the files and the order in which they load. You can also use Lighthouse to audit performance and get recommendations. Analysing the waterfall chart helps you uncover blocking requests or images that are too large.",
    longAnswer: "Diagnosing a slow-loading site without access to the code\nTo diagnose a site's performance without access to the source code you can use the browser's built-in tools. The main one is the Network tab in Chrome DevTools, which shows every network request, its size and its duration.\nThe main diagnostic steps\n\n- Open DevTools (F12) and go to the Network tab.\n- Reload the page and study the waterfall chart — it shows the sequence in which the resources load.\n- Pay attention to long requests (red or orange bars) — they indicate slow resources.\n- Check the sizes of the images, scripts and styles — large files can slow loading down.\n- Use Lighthouse (the Audits tab) for an automatic performance audit.\nAn example of the analysis\nSuppose you see that loading the home page takes 5 seconds. In the waterfall you can see that a large JavaScript file (2 MB) loads at the start and blocks the rendering. This indicates that the script needs optimizing or should be loaded asynchronously.\n\nConclusion\nDiagnosing through the browser's tools lets you uncover the main performance problems quickly without access to the server. This is useful to frontend developers and QA engineers when optimizing the user experience.",
    codeExample: "// An example: analysing loading times through the Performance API (where available)\nperformance.getEntriesByType('resource').forEach(entry => {\n  console.log(entry.name, entry.duration);\n});",
  },
  187: {
    source: "Что смотреть во вкладке Network в DevTools?",
    question: "What should you look at in the Network tab in DevTools?",
    shortAnswer: "In the Network tab you can see every request a page makes: HTML, CSS, JS, images, API calls. It is useful to look at the response status (200, 404, 500), the loading time, the file size and the request and response headers. This helps you debug errors, optimize loading speed and check that the data being sent is correct.",
    longAnswer: "The main parts of the Network tab\nThe Network tab in the browser's developer tools shows every network request a web page performs. It is a key tool for debugging and optimizing performance.\nWhat is worth analysing\n\n- The status — the HTTP code: 200 (success), 404 (not found), 500 (a server error), for example. It lets you uncover problems with the loading of resources quickly.\n- The type — document, script, stylesheet, xhr/fetch, image and so on. It helps you understand which resources are loading.\n- The time (Time / Waterfall) — it shows how long each stage took: DNS, the connection, sending, waiting, receiving. Useful for finding bottlenecks.\n- The size — the size of the data transferred (compressed and uncompressed). Large files can slow loading down.\n- The headers — information about the request and the response: Content-Type, Cache-Control, Authorization and others. Important for debugging APIs and caching.\n- Payload / Preview / Response — the body of the request (for POST) and of the response. It lets you check which data is being sent and received.\nAn example of use\nSuppose a page is not loading an image. We open Network and find a request of type image with status 404. We look at the URL — the path may be wrong. Or we see that a request to the API returns 500 — which means the problem is on the server.\n\nIn Network you can see this request, its status, its headers and its response body.\nConclusion\nThe Network tab is an indispensable tool for debugging network interaction, checking that an API is correct, analysing loading performance and uncovering errors on the client's or the server's side.",
    codeExample: "// An example: checking an API response through fetch\nfetch('/api/users')\n  .then(response => {\n    if (!response.ok) {\n      console.error('Error:', response.status);\n    }\n    return response.json();\n  })\n  .then(data => console.log(data));",
  },
  188: {
    source: "Что такое design system?",
    question: "What is a design system?",
    shortAnswer: "A design system is a set of standards, rules and ready-made components for building consistent interfaces. It includes UI components, design tokens (colours, fonts), guidelines and documentation. Using a design system speeds development up, makes maintenance easier and ensures products are consistent. Examples: Material Design, Ant Design.",
    longAnswer: "What is a design system?\nA design system is a comprehensive set of standards, principles, rules and ready-made components that ensures user interfaces are consistent across all of a company's products. It is not merely a library of UI components but a coherent system including design tokens (colours, typography, spacing), interaction patterns, accessibility guidelines and documentation.\nThe main components\n\n- UI components: buttons, inputs, modal windows, tables and so on.\n- Design tokens: variables for colours, fonts and spacing (`--color-primary: #1890ff`, for example).\n- Patterns: recurring solutions for typical tasks (navigation, forms, filters).\n- Documentation: the rules of use, code examples, best practices.\nAn example implementation in React\n\nWhere it is applied\nA design system is used in large projects with several teams of developers and designers. It is critically important for products that require a single user experience (corporate portals, SaaS platforms, mobile applications, for example).\nConclusion: a design system is worth introducing when a company has several products or teams, in order to avoid fragmented interfaces, speed development up and make the code easier to maintain.",
    codeExample: "// An example of using design tokens in CSS-in-JS\nconst theme = {\n  colors: {\n    primary: '#1890ff',\n    background: '#f0f2f5',\n  },\n  spacing: (n) => `${n * 8}px`,\n};\n\n// A button component from the design system\nconst Button = styled.button`\n  background: ${props => props.theme.colors.primary};\n  padding: ${props => props.theme.spacing(2)} ${props => props.theme.spacing(4)};\n  border: none;\n  border-radius: 4px;\n  color: white;\n  cursor: pointer;\n`;",
  },
  189: {
    source: "Какие атрибуты есть у cookies?",
    question: "What attributes do cookies have?",
    shortAnswer: "The main cookie attributes are: Name, Value, Domain, Path, Expires/Max-Age, Secure, HttpOnly and SameSite. Name and Value set the name and the value. Domain and Path determine which URLs the cookie is sent for. Expires or Max-Age set its lifetime. Secure requires HTTPS. HttpOnly forbids access through JavaScript. SameSite restricts sending it in cross-site requests.",
    longAnswer: "The main cookie attributes\nCookies are small fragments of data that the server sends to the browser; the browser stores them and sends them back with every request to the server. A cookie's attributes control its behaviour, its security and its lifetime.\nThe list of attributes\n\n- Name — the cookie's name (required).\n- Value — the cookie's value (required).\n- Domain — the domain the cookie is valid for (example.com, for example).\n- Path — the URL path the cookie is sent for (/, for example).\n- Expires — the expiry date (in HTTP-date format).\n- Max-Age — the lifetime in seconds (it takes priority over Expires).\n- Secure — a flag indicating that the cookie must be transmitted only over HTTPS.\n- HttpOnly — a flag forbidding access to the cookie through JavaScript (document.cookie).\n- SameSite — restricts the sending of the cookie in cross-site requests (Strict, Lax, None).\nAn example of setting a cookie on the server (Node.js with Express)\n\nConclusion\nA cookie's attributes let you configure its behaviour precisely, from restricting its scope to increasing its security. Use Secure and HttpOnly to protect against interception and XSS attacks, and SameSite to prevent CSRF attacks.",
    codeExample: "res.cookie('sessionId', 'abc123', {\n  domain: '.example.com',\n  path: '/',\n  maxAge: 3600000, // 1 hour\n  secure: true,\n  httpOnly: true,\n  sameSite: 'strict'\n});",
  },
  190: {
    source: "Какие селекторы считаются наиболее стабильными?",
    question: "Which selectors are considered the most stable?",
    shortAnswer: "Selectors by class and by ID are considered the most stable, since they are less exposed to changes in the HTML structure. Selectors by tag and by attribute are less stable, while pseudo-classes and pseudo-elements can change depending on state. Using classes with meaningful names increases the stability and readability of your code.",
    longAnswer: "What is the stability of a selector?\nThe stability of a CSS selector means how likely it is that the style applied with it will not break when the HTML structure or the content changes. Stable selectors help you avoid unexpected changes to your markup and make the code easier to maintain.\nWhich selectors are considered the most stable?\n\n- Selectors by class (.class) — the most stable, since classes are normally assigned for a particular purpose and rarely change. They have medium specificity and are easy to override.\n- Selectors by ID (#id) — very stable, but they have high specificity, which can make overriding styles harder. They are used for unique elements.\n- Selectors by attribute ([attr]) — stable, provided the attributes do not change dynamically. They suit styling elements with particular properties.\n- Selectors by tag (element) — less stable, since tags can be reused in different contexts. Changing them may affect a great many elements.\n- Pseudo-classes (:hover, :focus) — they depend on an element's state and can change, so they are less stable for permanent styles.\nCode examples\n\nConclusion\nTo create stable, easily maintainable styles it is recommended to use selectors by class with meaningful names. This reduces the risk of breaking your markup when things change and makes working as a team easier.",
    codeExample: "/* A stable selector by class */\n.button {\n  background-color: blue;\n  color: white;\n}\n\n/* A less stable selector by tag */\nbutton {\n  border: none;\n  padding: 10px;\n}\n\n/* A selector by ID with high specificity */\n#submit-btn {\n  font-size: 16px;\n}",
  },
  191: {
    source: "Что такое JSON?",
    question: "What is JSON?",
    shortAnswer: "JSON (JavaScript Object Notation) is a lightweight data-interchange format based on the syntax of JavaScript objects. It uses \"key-value\" pairs and supports strings, numbers, arrays, objects, booleans and null. JSON is widely applied to pass data between server and client in web applications.",
    longAnswer: "What is JSON?\nJSON (JavaScript Object Notation) is a text format for storing and transmitting structured data. It is based on the syntax of JavaScript objects but is language-independent, which makes it universal for exchanging data between various systems.\nJSON's syntax\nJSON supports the following data types:\n\n- Strings (in double quotes)\n- Numbers (integers and floating-point)\n- Booleans (true/false)\n- null\n- Arrays (ordered lists in square brackets)\n- Objects (unordered collections of \"key-value\" pairs in curly braces)\nAn example of a JSON object:\n\nThe uses of JSON\nJSON is used in APIs to pass data between client and server, in configuration files, in databases (MongoDB, for example) and for serializing objects. It has replaced XML in many web applications thanks to its simplicity and readability.\nAn example of working with JSON in JavaScript\n\nConclusion: JSON is the standard format for exchanging data; it is simple to use, human-readable and supported by practically every programming language. It is worth applying wherever structured data has to be passed, especially in web development and microservice architectures.",
    codeExample: "{\n  \"name\": \"Alice\",\n  \"age\": 30,\n  \"isStudent\": false,\n  \"hobbies\": [\"reading\", \"coding\"],\n  \"address\": {\n    \"city\": \"New York\",\n    \"zip\": \"10001\"\n  }\n}\n\n// Converting an object into a JSON string\nconst user = { name: \"Bob\", age: 25 };\nconst jsonString = JSON.stringify(user);\nconsole.log(jsonString); // {\"name\":\"Bob\",\"age\":25}\n\n// Parsing a JSON string into an object\nconst parsedUser = JSON.parse(jsonString);\nconsole.log(parsedUser.name); // Bob",
  },
  192: {
    source: "Как тестировать форму оплаты банковской карты?",
    question: "How do you test a bank card payment form?",
    shortAnswer: "Testing a payment form involves checking the validation of the card number, the expiry date, the CVV and the cardholder's name. You have to check the handling of errors on incorrect data, support for different card types and the security of the data transfer. It is also important to test successful and unsuccessful payment scenarios through the payment gateway.",
    longAnswer: "The main aspects of testing a payment form\nTesting a bank card payment form requires attention to input validation, error handling and security. It is important to check that the form accepts the data correctly, displays errors and interacts with the payment gateway.\nValidating the fields\n\n- The card number: checking the length (16 digits for Visa/Mastercard), the format (groups of 4 digits) and the Luhn algorithm.\n- The expiry date: checking the MM/YY format and that the date has not passed.\n- The CVV: 3-4 digits, digits only.\n- The cardholder's name: letters, spaces and hyphens only.\nAn example test in JavaScript\n\nError handling\nCheck that the form shows clear error messages for every field — \"Invalid card number\" or \"The card has expired\", for example. It is also important to test scenarios with empty fields and with incorrect formats.\nSecurity\nMake sure the data is transmitted over HTTPS, is not saved in logs and is not displayed in the console. Use tokenization or PCI DSS compliant solutions.\nConclusion\nTesting a payment form is critical for security and UX. Apply automated tests for validation and manual ones for checking scenarios with the payment gateway.",
    codeExample: "function validateCardNumber(number) {\n  const cleaned = number.replace(/\\s/g, '');\n  if (!/^\\d{16}$/.test(cleaned)) return false;\n  let sum = 0;\n  for (let i = 0; i < cleaned.length; i++) {\n    let digit = parseInt(cleaned[i]);\n    if (i % 2 === 0) {\n      digit *= 2;\n      if (digit > 9) digit -= 9;\n    }\n    sum += digit;\n  }\n  return sum % 10 === 0;\n}",
  },
  193: {
    source: "Как улучшить UX формы оплаты?Какие способы оплаты можно добавить для улучшения пользовательского опыта?",
    question: "How do you improve the UX of a payment form? Which payment methods can you add to improve the user experience?",
    shortAnswer: "To improve the UX of a payment form it is worth adding popular payment methods such as credit cards, PayPal, Apple Pay, Google Pay and local methods. It is important to minimize the number of fields, to use autofill and to show security indicators. This reduces friction and increases conversion.",
    longAnswer: "Improving the UX of a payment form\nThe payment form is a critical stage of the sales funnel. Poor UX can lead to abandoned carts. The main goal is to make the process fast, safe and intuitive.\nThe choice of payment methods\nAdding a variety of payment methods makes things more convenient for users. It is recommended to include:\n\n- Credit and debit cards (Visa, Mastercard, Mir)\n- Electronic wallets (PayPal, WebMoney, Qiwi)\n- Mobile payments (Apple Pay, Google Pay, Samsung Pay)\n- Local methods (SBP, Alipay, iDEAL — depending on the region)\nA code example: a simple payment form with validation\n\nAdditional UX improvements\n\n- Autofill for the fields (autocomplete=\"cc-number\")\n- Real-time validation with errors highlighted\n- Security indicators (a padlock icon, an SSL certificate)\n- The ability to save a card for future purchases\n- Minimizing the number of steps (single-page checkout)\nConclusion: improving the UX of a payment form directly affects conversion and customer satisfaction. It is recommended to test different variants and analyse users' behaviour.",
    codeExample: "<form id=\"payment-form\">\n  <label for=\"card-number\">Card number</label>\n  <input type=\"text\" id=\"card-number\" placeholder=\"1234 5678 9012 3456\" required>\n  <label for=\"expiry\">Expiry date</label>\n  <input type=\"text\" id=\"expiry\" placeholder=\"MM/YY\" required>\n  <label for=\"cvv\">CVV</label>\n  <input type=\"text\" id=\"cvv\" placeholder=\"123\" required>\n  <button type=\"submit\">Pay</button>\n</form>",
  },
  194: {
    source: "Как повторно отправить запрос через DevTools?",
    question: "How do you resend a request through DevTools?",
    shortAnswer: "In the Network panel find the request you want. Right-click it and choose \"Replay XHR\" or \"Edit and Replay\". This lets you test an API quickly without reloading the page.",
    longAnswer: "Resending a request through DevTools\nThe browser's developer tools (DevTools) provide a convenient way of resending an HTTP request without having to reload the page or write code. This is especially useful when debugging an API, testing changes on the server or checking the responses to different parameters.\nHow to do it\n\n- Open DevTools (F12 or Ctrl+Shift+I).\n- Go to the Network tab.\n- Find the request you want in the list (you can filter by type — XHR, for example).\n- Right-click the request.\n- Choose Replay XHR — the request will be sent with the same headers and body.\n- To change the parameters, choose Edit and Replay — an editor opens where you can change the method, the URL, the headers or the request body.\nAn example of use\nSuppose you are testing a POST request that creates a user. After the first response you want to check how the server reacts to the same data being sent again. Instead of writing curl or a script, simply use Replay XHR in DevTools.\n\nAfter it has been sent you will see the request in Network. Right-click -> Replay XHR will send it again.\nConclusion\nThe ability to resend requests in DevTools speeds up the debugging and testing of APIs, letting you check changes quickly without additional tools. It is an indispensable skill for frontend and backend developers.",
    codeExample: "// An example of a request that can be replayed\nfetch('/api/users', {\n  method: 'POST',\n  headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },\n  body: JSON.stringify({ name: 'John' })\n});",
  },
  195: {
    source: "Для чего используется вкладка Fetch/XHR в DevTools?",
    question: "What is the Fetch/XHR tab in DevTools used for?",
    shortAnswer: "The Fetch/XHR tab in DevTools shows every network request the browser has sent. It lets you inspect the headers, the request and response bodies, the status codes and the durations. This helps you debug API errors, check the data being passed and analyse performance.",
    longAnswer: "The purpose of the Fetch/XHR tab\nThe Fetch/XHR tab in the browser's developer tools (DevTools) is intended for monitoring and debugging the asynchronous HTTP requests performed through the Fetch API or XMLHttpRequest (XHR). It is part of the Network panel and filters out everything except the requests initiated by JavaScript code, rather than by the loading of the page or its resources.\nHow it works\nWhen a web application sends a request to the server (to get data or submit a form, for example), the browser records it in its logs. The Fetch/XHR tab displays a list of such requests with the key information:\n\n- The request's URL\n- The method (GET, POST, PUT, DELETE and so on)\n- The response's status code (200, 404, 500 and others)\n- The type and size of the response\n- The duration\nClicking a particular request opens the details: the headers, the request and response bodies, the cookies and a timeline.\nAn example of use\nSuppose you are developing an application in React that loads a list of users through the Fetch API. If the data is not displayed, open DevTools (F12), go to the Network tab and select the Fetch/XHR filter. You will see the request to the API. Check the status code: if it is 200, the server replied successfully. Then look at the response body in the Response tab — the structure of the data may not match what you expect. If the status is 404, check that the URL is correct.\n\nIn DevTools you will see this request, its headers and its response, which will help you find the problem quickly.\nConclusion\nThe Fetch/XHR tab is an indispensable tool for debugging client-server interaction. It lets you uncover errors in requests quickly, check that the data is correct and optimize the performance of network operations.",
    codeExample: "// An example of code that may cause an error\nfetch('https://api.example.com/users')\n  .then(response => response.json())\n  .then(data => console.log(data))\n  .catch(error => console.error('Error:', error));",
  },
  196: {
    source: "Где применяется JSON?",
    question: "Where is JSON applied?",
    shortAnswer: "JSON (JavaScript Object Notation) is a lightweight data-interchange format based on the syntax of JavaScript objects. It is used to pass structured data between server and client in web applications, and also to store configuration. JSON is easy for both humans and machines to read, which makes it popular in REST APIs, in databases (MongoDB, for example) and in configuration files.",
    longAnswer: "JSON (JavaScript Object Notation) is a text format for exchanging data, based on the syntax of JavaScript objects. It was developed to represent simple data structures and associative arrays (objects). JSON is language-independent, which lets it be used in practically any programming environment.\nThe main areas where JSON is applied\n\n- Web APIs and REST services: JSON is the standard for passing data between a client (a browser, for example) and a server. Most modern APIs return their data in JSON format.\n- Configuration files: many tools and frameworks (Webpack, ESLint, npm, for example) use JSON to store a project's settings.\n- Storing data: NoSQL databases such as MongoDB store documents in a format close to JSON (BSON).\n- Exchanging data between services: in a microservice architecture JSON is often used to serialize the messages passed between components.\nAn example of using JSON in JavaScript\n\nAn example of using JSON in Python\n\nJSON supports only a few data types: strings, numbers, booleans, null, arrays and objects. This makes it simple but sufficient for most data-exchange tasks.\nConclusion: JSON is applied wherever a lightweight, readable, cross-platform format for passing or storing structured data is required. It is especially useful in web development, configuration and microservices, replacing more cumbersome formats such as XML.",
    codeExample: "// A JavaScript object\nconst user = {\n  name: \"Alice\",\n  age: 30,\n  isAdmin: false\n};\n\n// Serializing it into a JSON string\nconst jsonString = JSON.stringify(user);\nconsole.log(jsonString); // {\"name\":\"Alice\",\"age\":30,\"isAdmin\":false}\n\n// Parsing the JSON string back into an object\nconst parsedUser = JSON.parse(jsonString);\nconsole.log(parsedUser.name); // Alice\n\nimport json\n\n# A Python dictionary\ndata = {\n    \"name\": \"Bob\",\n    \"age\": 25,\n    \"skills\": [\"Python\", \"JSON\"]\n}\n\n# Serializing it into JSON\njson_data = json.dumps(data)\nprint(json_data)  # {\"name\": \"Bob\", \"age\": 25, \"skills\": [\"Python\", \"JSON\"]}\n\n# Parsing the JSON\nparsed_data = json.loads(json_data)\nprint(parsed_data[\"name\"])  # Bob",
  },
  197: {
    source: "Какие проверки ввода необходимо выполнять для текстовых полей?",
    question: "Which input checks have to be performed for text fields?",
    shortAnswer: "For text fields you have to check the length and the format (an email address or a phone number, for example), the absence of malicious characters (XSS, SQL injection), and sanitize the input. It is important to check the data both on the client and on the server. Use whitelists of permitted characters and avoid blacklists.",
    longAnswer: "The main input checks for text fields\nChecking input is a critically important stage of handling data that prevents a great many vulnerabilities, such as XSS, SQL injection and data corruption. It has to be performed both on the client side (for the user's convenience) and on the server side (for security).\nThe kinds of check\n\n- Checking the length: limiting the minimum and maximum number of characters in order to avoid buffer overflows or excessively long strings.\n- Checking the format: using regular expressions to check email addresses, phone numbers, URLs and other structured data.\n- Sanitization: removing or escaping dangerous characters such as `<script>` or `' OR 1=1 --`.\n- A whitelist of characters: permitting only particular characters (letters and digits only, for example) rather than trying to block every dangerous one.\nA code example in JavaScript (a server-side check)\n\nConclusion\nChecking input is mandatory for every text field, especially in web applications that work with user data. It protects against attacks and ensures the integrity of the data. Always use server-side validation as the main barrier, and client-side validation as an additional convenience.",
    codeExample: "function validateInput(input) {\n  // Checking the length\n  if (input.length < 1 || input.length > 100) {\n    return 'The length must be between 1 and 100 characters';\n  }\n  // Checking for dangerous characters (XSS)\n  const dangerous = /[<>\"'\\\\/]/g;\n  if (dangerous.test(input)) {\n    return 'Invalid characters';\n  }\n  // Escaping for safe output\n  const sanitized = input.replace(/&/g, '&amp;')\n                         .replace(/</g, '&lt;')\n                         .replace(/>/g, '&gt;');\n  return sanitized;\n}",
  },
  198: {
    source: "Что такое контрольная сумма номера банковской карты?",
    question: "What is the checksum of a bank card number?",
    shortAnswer: "The checksum of a bank card number is its last digit, computed with the Luhn algorithm. It is used to check that a card number is valid without contacting the bank. The algorithm works on the digits of the number: it doubles every second digit, sums all the digits, and if the sum is divisible by 10 the number is considered valid. This helps catch typos during entry.",
    longAnswer: "What is the checksum of a bank card number?\nThe checksum of a bank card number is the number's last digit, which is computed with the Luhn algorithm. This algorithm is used to check that a card number is valid without any need to contact the payment system or the bank. It helps catch accidental errors during entry, such as transposed digits or typos.\nHow does the Luhn algorithm work?\nThe Luhn algorithm is applied to the card number (normally 16 digits). The last digit is the check digit. The verification process involves the following steps:\n\n- Starting from the first digit on the left, double every second digit.\n- If the result of the doubling is greater than 9, subtract 9 (or add the digits of the number together).\n- Add up all the digits (including the ones that were not doubled).\n- If the total is divisible by 10 with no remainder, the number is valid.\nAn example for the number 4539 1488 0343 6467 (the checksum is 7):\n\nWhere is it applied?\nThe Luhn algorithm is used in payment systems (Visa, MasterCard) and also in other identifiers such as IMEIs and social security numbers. It is built into libraries for validating forms on sites and in mobile applications.\nConclusion: a checksum based on the Luhn algorithm is a simple, effective way of guarding against accidental entry errors. It is worth applying wherever card numbers or other identifiers have to be typed in by hand, in order to reduce the number of invalid transactions.",
    codeExample: "// We double every second digit (starting from the first):\n4*2=8, 3*2=6, 1*2=2, 8*2=16->1+6=7, 0*2=0, 4*2=8, 6*2=12->1+2=3, 6*2=12->1+2=3\n// We add up all the digits: 8+5+6+9+2+4+7+8+0+3+8+3+3+4+3+7 = 80\n// 80 is divisible by 10 -> the number is valid",
  },
  199: {
    source: "Какие инструменты браузера используются для диагностики проблем во фронтенде?",
    question: "Which browser tools are used to diagnose frontend problems?",
    shortAnswer: "The main browser tools for diagnosis are the Elements, Console, Sources, Network, Performance and Application tabs. Elements lets you inspect and edit the HTML/CSS. Console prints JavaScript errors and logs. Sources is used to debug code with breakpoints. Network shows every network request and its duration. Performance analyses a page's performance. Application manages storage, cookies and the cache.",
    longAnswer: "Browser tools for diagnosing frontend problems\nModern browsers provide a powerful set of built-in developer tools (DevTools) that help you uncover and fix problems in web applications. The main tabs include Elements, Console, Sources, Network, Performance and Application. Each of them solves specific diagnostic tasks.\nThe main tabs and their uses\n\n- Elements — inspecting and editing the DOM tree and the CSS styles in real time. It lets you check quickly how changes affect the appearance.\n- Console — printing JavaScript errors, warnings and logs. You can run arbitrary code for testing.\n- Sources — debugging JavaScript with breakpoints, stepping through, inspecting the call stack and the values of variables.\n- Network — monitoring every HTTP request, its status, duration, headers and body. It helps you find slow or failed requests.\n- Performance — recording and analysing a page's performance: loading, rendering, the execution of scripts. It lets you uncover bottlenecks.\n- Application — managing local storage, sessions, cookies, IndexedDB, the Service Worker cache and PWA manifests.\nAn example of use\nSuppose a page is loading slowly. Open the Network tab and reload the page. You will see every request and its duration. If some request takes a long time, check its size or the server's latency. Then go to Performance, press Record and repeat the action. The analysis will show which functions or which rendering take the most time.\n\nYou can also use breakpoints in Sources. If a function does not return the expected result, for example, set a breakpoint on its call and check the arguments.\nConclusion\nThe browser's tools are indispensable for quickly diagnosing and debugging frontend work. They let you find errors in your code, optimize performance and analyse network interaction without any additional libraries.",
    codeExample: "// An example of code for debugging in the Console\nconsole.log('A debugging message');\nconsole.error('Error: something went wrong');\nconsole.table([{name: 'Alice', age: 30}, {name: 'Bob', age: 25}]);",
  },
  200: {
    source: "Что такое HTTP-only Cookie?",
    question: "What is an HTTP-only cookie?",
    shortAnswer: "HTTP-only is a flag that forbids access to a cookie from JavaScript (document.cookie). Cookies with this flag are sent only with HTTP requests to the server. This protects session tokens from being stolen through XSS attacks. The flag is set by the server in the Set-Cookie header.",
    longAnswer: "What is an HTTP-only cookie?\nHTTP-only is a security attribute that can be added to an HTTP cookie. When a cookie is marked HTTP-only, the browser forbids access to it from client-side JavaScript (through `document.cookie`). Such cookies are sent by the browser automatically only with HTTP requests to the server that set them.\nWhy is this needed?\nThe main purpose is protection against cross-site scripting (XSS). If an attacker injects a malicious script into a page, they will not be able to read HTTP-only cookies — a session token, for example. This reduces the risk of session hijacking considerably.\nHow does it work?\nThe server sets the cookie with the `HttpOnly` flag in its response header:\n\nAfter that the browser stores the cookie, but JavaScript cannot read it:\n\nThe cookie is still sent with every request to the server automatically.\nWhere is it applied?\nHTTP-only cookies are widely used to store session identifiers, authentication tokens and other confidential data that should not be available to client-side code. It is standard practice in web development for increasing security.\nConclusion\nUse the HTTP-only flag for every cookie containing sensitive data, especially session tokens. It is a mandatory measure of protection against XSS attacks, but it is not a panacea — combine it with other security mechanisms such as Secure, SameSite and CSP.",
    codeExample: "Set-Cookie: sessionId=abc123; HttpOnly; Secure; SameSite=Strict\n\n// An attempt to read the cookie from JS\nconsole.log(document.cookie); // Will print an empty string, or the other cookies without HttpOnly",
  },
  201: {
    source: "Какие инструменты используются для Pixel Perfect?",
    question: "Which tools are used for Pixel Perfect work?",
    shortAnswer: "Pixel Perfect is an approach in which the markup matches the design mockup exactly. The main tools are browser extensions such as PerfectPixel (Chrome) and PixelPerfect (Firefox), along with the built-in developer tools. They let you overlay the mockup on the page and compare it pixel by pixel.",
    longAnswer: "What Pixel Perfect is and why it is needed\nPixel Perfect is a markup technique in which the final HTML/CSS code reproduces the design mockup (normally in PSD, Figma or Sketch format) as closely as possible. This matters for ensuring a consistent appearance on every device and browser, and for satisfying the requirements of the client or the designer.\nThe main tools\n\n- PerfectPixel (Chrome) — an extension that lets you upload an image of the mockup and overlay it on the page with adjustable transparency.\n- PixelPerfect (Firefox) — the equivalent for Firefox, with similar functionality.\n- The built-in developer tools — in Chrome DevTools you can use the \"Rendering\" panel and the \"Screenshot\" option for comparison.\n- Figma Mirror — for viewing the mockup on mobile devices in real time.\nAn example of using PerfectPixel\n\nPractical tips\n\n- Always check your markup at several screen resolutions.\n- Use CSS properties such as `box-sizing: border-box` in order to avoid discrepancies in dimensions.\n- For complex mockups, apply grids (CSS Grid or Flexbox) for precise positioning.\nConclusion: Pixel Perfect tools are necessary to frontend developers working in a team with designers, in order to minimize discrepancies between the mockup and the finished product. They are especially useful at the markup stage and when work is signed off.",
    codeExample: "// Install the PerfectPixel extension from the Chrome Web Store\n// Open the page and click the extension's icon\n// Upload an image of the mockup (design.png, for example)\n// Adjust the opacity for comparison\n// Use the arrow keys for precise positioning",
  },
  202: {
    source: "Что такое Pixel Perfect верстка?",
    question: "What is Pixel Perfect markup?",
    shortAnswer: "Pixel Perfect markup is an approach in which the finished HTML/CSS code matches the design mockup entirely, to the pixel. The developer uses tools to overlay the markup on the mockup and works until the dimensions, spacing, colours and fonts match perfectly. This demands attentiveness and skill with CSS.",
    longAnswer: "What is Pixel Perfect markup?\nPixel Perfect markup is a method of developing web interfaces in which the final HTML/CSS code is visually identical to the design mockup the designer provided. The main goal is to make every element (blocks, text, images) match to within a single pixel. This approach is often used in projects where a high degree of visual accuracy matters — in landing pages or corporate sites, for example.\nHow does it work?\nThe developer opens the mockup (normally in Figma, Sketch or Photoshop) and builds the page in the browser alongside it. For checking, special tools are used, such as browser extensions (PixelPerfect, for example) or plugins that overlay a semi-transparent image of the mockup on top of the markup. The developer then adjusts the CSS properties (width, height, margin, padding, font-size, color and so on) until they match exactly.\nA code example\nSuppose the mockup has a button 200x50 pixels in size, with 20px of spacing above it and a 16px Arial font. The markup will look like this:\n\nAfter that the developer checks whether the button matches the mockup and adjusts the values if necessary.\nWhen should you apply it?\nPixel Perfect markup is justified in projects with strict design requirements, where even a small deviation may be critical (branded sites, for example). In modern responsive and fluid layouts, however, this approach may be excessive, since priority is given to flexibility and ease of maintenance. Conclusion: use Pixel Perfect when accuracy matters more than development speed, but keep the trade-offs with responsiveness in mind.",
    codeExample: "<button class=\"btn\">Click me</button>\n\n.btn {\n  width: 200px;\n  height: 50px;\n  margin-top: 20px;\n  font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\n  font-size: 16px;\n  background-color: #007bff;\n  color: #fff;\n  border: none;\n  border-radius: 4px;\n}",
  },
  203: {
    source: "Как браузер работает с CSS?",
    question: "How does the browser work with CSS?",
    shortAnswer: "The browser loads the HTML and CSS and parses them into the DOM and the CSSOM. It then combines them into the render tree, computes the elements' geometry (layout) and draws the pixels on the screen (paint). This process is called the critical rendering path.",
    longAnswer: "How the browser handles CSS\nWhen the browser loads a web page, it processes the HTML and CSS in sequence. First the HTML is parsed, creating the DOM (Document Object Model). In parallel the CSS files are loaded and parsed, forming the CSSOM (CSS Object Model). These two trees are combined into the render tree, which contains only the visible elements.\nThe rendering stages\n\n- Parsing the CSS: the browser takes the CSS rules apart, resolves conflicts (the cascade and specificity) and builds the CSSOM.\n- Layout (reflow): the dimensions and positions of every element on the page are computed.\n- Paint: the browser draws the pixels, filling in colours, shadows, borders and so on.\n- Composite: the layers are combined into the final image.\nAn example\n\nThe browser will create a CSSOM node for .box, then compute its dimensions during layout, and fill it with red during paint.\nConclusion\nUnderstanding this process helps you optimize performance: avoid frequent reflows and repaints, and use properties that do not cause the layout to be recomputed (transform rather than changing top/left, for example).",
    codeExample: "/* CSS */\n.box {\n  width: 100px;\n  height: 100px;\n  background: red;\n}",
  },
  204: {
    source: "Какие ограничения по размеру есть у браузерных хранилищ?",
    question: "What size limits do the browser's storage mechanisms have?",
    shortAnswer: "localStorage and sessionStorage normally have a limit of about 5-10 MB per domain, depending on the browser. Cookies are limited to 4 KB per cookie and a maximum of 20-50 cookies per domain. These limits matter when choosing the right storage for a web application.",
    longAnswer: "The limits of the browser's storage mechanisms\nThe browser's storage mechanisms, such as localStorage, sessionStorage and cookies, have strict size limits set by the browser vendors. These limits affect which data can be stored on the client without a loss of performance or errors.\nlocalStorage and sessionStorage\nBoth use the same limits, normally from 5 to 10 MB per domain. In Chrome and Firefox, for example, the limit is 5 MB, while in Opera it is up to 10 MB. If you exceed the limit, the browser throws a QuotaExceededError exception. These stores suit caching data but not large files.\n\nCookies\nCookies have a far smaller limit: 4 KB per cookie and normally up to 20-50 cookies per domain (it depends on the browser). They are sent with every HTTP request, so their size is critical for performance. Cookies are better used only for authentication or identification, not for storing large amounts of data.\nConclusion\nThe choice of storage depends on the task: for small amounts of data (up to 4 KB) cookies will do; for medium amounts (up to 5-10 MB), localStorage or sessionStorage. For large volumes, use server-side databases or IndexedDB, which has limits of up to several hundred MB.",
    codeExample: "try {\n  localStorage.setItem('key', 'value');\n} catch (e) {\n  if (e.name === 'QuotaExceededError') {\n    console.log('The storage limit has been exceeded');\n  }\n}",
  },
  205: {
    source: "В каких случаях скрытый элемент остаётся интерактивным?",
    question: "In which cases does a hidden element stay interactive?",
    shortAnswer: "A hidden element stays interactive when the property visibility: hidden or opacity: 0 is used. In the first case the element is invisible but still takes up space and responds to clicks. In the second the element is completely transparent but also stays clickable. With display: none the element is removed from the flow and loses its interactivity.",
    longAnswer: "How hiding affects interactivity\nCSS has several ways of hiding an element, and their behaviour differs. If you want the element to stay interactive (responding to clicks or focus, for example), you have to use `visibility: hidden` or `opacity: 0`. With `display: none` the element is removed from the document's flow entirely and loses all interactivity.\nCode examples\n\nIn this example the element is invisible, but a click in its place will trigger the handler. `opacity: 0` works in the same way — the element is transparent but stays in the flow and responds to events.\nWhen this is useful\n\n- For creating appearance/disappearance animations without losing state.\n- For hiding elements that should remain available to screen readers (with care).\n- For implementing custom checkboxes or buttons, where a visually hidden element controls the behaviour.\nConclusion: use `visibility: hidden` or `opacity: 0` if you need to preserve the interactivity of a hidden element — for animations or accessibility, for example. For complete removal from the interface, apply `display: none`.",
    codeExample: "<div id=\"hidden\" style=\"visibility: hidden;\">Click me</div>\n<script>\n  document.getElementById('hidden').addEventListener('click', () => {\n    alert('The click worked!');\n  });\n</script>",
  },
  206: {
    source: "Что может блокировать построение DOM?",
    question: "What can block the building of the DOM?",
    shortAnswer: "The building of the DOM can be blocked by synchronous scripts, which run immediately on loading, and also by CSS files if they load before the scripts. The browser pauses the parsing of the HTML until it has loaded and executed the script or processed the CSS. This slows the display of the page down.",
    longAnswer: "What blocks the building of the DOM?\nBuilding the DOM (Document Object Model) is the process in which the browser parses the HTML and creates a tree structure of elements. Some resources can pause this process, delaying the painting of the page.\nThe main blockers\n\n- Synchronous scripts — `<script>` tags without the `async` or `defer` attributes. The browser stops parsing the HTML, loads the script, executes it, and only then carries on building the DOM.\n- CSS files — although CSS does not block the DOM directly by itself, it can block the execution of scripts that follow it. The browser waits for the CSS to load before running the script, which delays the DOM indirectly.\nAn example\n\nHow to avoid the blocking\n\n- Use `async` or `defer` for scripts so that they do not block the parsing.\n- Put your CSS in `<head>` and your scripts at the end of `<body>`.\n- Minimize the number of blocking resources.\nConclusion: understanding what blocks the DOM helps you optimize a page's loading, especially for improving First Contentful Paint (FCP) and the overall user experience.",
    codeExample: "<!-- A blocking script -->\n<script src=\"script.js\"></script>\n<!-- The parsing of the HTML is paused until script.js has loaded and run -->\n<div>This element will appear later</div>",
  },
  207: {
    source: "Как скрытие элемента влияет на DOM и поток документа?",
    question: "How does hiding an element affect the DOM and the document's flow?",
    shortAnswer: "The property display: none removes an element from the document's flow entirely; it takes up no space and is invisible. The property visibility: hidden makes an element invisible, but it carries on taking up space in the flow. Neither property removes the element from the DOM tree, but display: none excludes it from rendering.",
    longAnswer: "The effect on the DOM and the document's flow\nThe properties `display: none` and `visibility: hidden` affect an element's display and its place in the document differently. Neither removes the element from the DOM tree, but they change how it takes part in rendering.\ndisplay: none\nThe element is removed from the document's flow entirely. It takes up no space, and the neighbouring elements occupy its space. The element is invisible and takes no part in mouse or keyboard events.\n\nIn this example the second element is not displayed and takes up no space; the third element will come immediately after the first.\nvisibility: hidden\nThe element becomes invisible but carries on taking up space in the document's flow. It is not displayed, but its dimensions and position are preserved, affecting the placement of the other elements.\n\nHere the second element is not visible, but an empty space equal to its size remains between the first and the third.\nUse\n\n- `display: none` is used to hide an element completely when its space has to be freed up (in responsive design, for example).\n- `visibility: hidden` is applied when you need to hide an element but keep its space (for animations or as a placeholder, for example).\nConclusion: the choice between these properties depends on the task — whether the element's space in the document's flow has to be preserved or whether it should be removed from the visual presentation entirely.",
    codeExample: "<div>The first element</div>\n<div style=\"display: none\">A hidden element</div>\n<div>The third element</div>\n\n<div>The first element</div>\n<div style=\"visibility: hidden\">An invisible element</div>\n<div>The third element</div>",
  },
  208: {
    source: "Как скрытые элементы влияют на SEO?",
    question: "How do hidden elements affect SEO?",
    shortAnswer: "Hidden elements are generally not indexed by search engines if they are hidden completely with CSS (display: none). If an element is hidden but its content is still readable (through visibility: hidden, for example), however, search crawlers may index it. Using hidden elements to manipulate SEO can lead to penalties.",
    longAnswer: "The effect of hidden elements on SEO\nHidden elements on a web page can affect SEO differently depending on how they are hidden. Search engines such as Google aim to index content that is visible to users and may ignore hidden content or handle it differently.\nThe main ways of hiding elements\n\n- display: none — the element is removed from the document's flow entirely and is not displayed. Search crawlers normally do not index such content, since it is considered invisible to users.\n- visibility: hidden — the element stays in the flow but becomes invisible. Its content may be indexed, since it is still present in the DOM.\n- opacity: 0 — the element is transparent but takes up space. Search engines may index its content, since it is readable.\n- position: absolute; left: -9999px — the element is moved off the screen. This method is often used to hide text, but it can be judged an attempt to manipulate SEO.\nA code example\n\nRecommendations\nUse hidden elements only to improve the user experience — for modal windows or dropdown menus, for example. Do not try to hide keywords or links in order to manipulate your ranking, since that can lead to penalties from search engines.\nConclusion: hidden elements should be used with care, since their effect on SEO depends on the method of hiding. It is best to avoid hiding important content and to concentrate on creating good, visible content for users.",
    codeExample: "<!-- A hidden element with display: none -->\n<div style=\"display: none;\">This text will not be indexed</div>\n\n<!-- A hidden element with visibility: hidden -->\n<div style=\"visibility: hidden;\">This text may be indexed</div>\n\n<!-- A hidden element with opacity: 0 -->\n<div style=\"opacity: 0;\">This text may also be indexed</div>",
  },
  209: {
    source: "Что приходит браузеру в HTTP-ответе?",
    question: "What arrives at the browser in an HTTP response?",
    shortAnswer: "The browser receives an HTTP response consisting of a status line, headers and a body. The status line contains a code and a message — 200 OK, for example. The headers carry metadata such as Content-Type. The body contains the data that was requested — the page's HTML code, for example.",
    longAnswer: "The structure of an HTTP response\nWhen the browser sends a request to the server, the server returns an HTTP response. That response consists of three main parts: the status line, the headers and the body. The status line includes the protocol version, the status code (200, 404, for example) and a text message. The headers contain metadata such as the content type, the length of the body, caching and other parameters. The response body is the actual data the browser processes — HTML, JSON or an image, for example.\nAn example HTTP response\nConsider a simple example in which the server returns an HTML page. The response will have the status line `HTTP/1.1 200 OK`, headers such as `Content-Type: text/html`, and a body with the HTML code. Here is how it might look as text:\n\nThe key components\n\n- The status line: it indicates whether the request succeeded or failed. 200 OK, for example, means success; 404 Not Found means the resource was not found.\n- The headers: they carry additional information such as the data type (Content-Type), caching (Cache-Control) or cookies (Set-Cookie).\n- The body: it contains the payload the browser displays or processes. It may be empty — with a 204 No Content response, for example.\nPractical use\nUnderstanding the structure of an HTTP response matters for debugging network requests, configuring servers and building APIs. When developing a REST API, for example, the server returns JSON in the response body with the appropriate Content-Type header. Here is an example in Node.js using Express:\n\nIn this example the server returns status 200, a Content-Type header and JSON in the body.\nConclusion: the HTTP response is a fundamental element of web communication that you have to know in order to work with web technologies, debug and build server applications.",
    codeExample: "HTTP/1.1 200 OK\nContent-Type: text/html\nContent-Length: 123\n\n<html>\n<body>\n<h1>Hello, World!</h1>\n</body>\n</html>\n\nconst express = require('express');\nconst app = express();\n\napp.get('/api/data', (req, res) => {\n  res.status(200);\n  res.set('Content-Type', 'application/json');\n  res.json({ message: 'Success', data: [1, 2, 3] });\n});\n\napp.listen(3000);",
  },
  210: {
    source: "Какие HTML-теги считаются семантическими?",
    question: "Which HTML tags are considered semantic?",
    shortAnswer: "Semantic tags carry meaning, describing their content. Examples are `<header>`, `<nav>`, `<main>`, `<article>`, `<section>`, `<aside>` and `<footer>`. They improve accessibility for screen readers and help search engines understand a page's structure.",
    longAnswer: "What are semantic HTML tags?\nSemantic tags are HTML elements that describe their content and their role in the document's structure explicitly. Unlike the generic `<div>` or `<span>`, semantic tags carry meaning, which makes code more readable and comprehensible both to developers and to browsers, search engines and assistive technologies.\nThe main HTML5 semantic tags\n\n- `<header>` — the introductory part or navigation for a section.\n- `<nav>` — a block of navigation links.\n- `<main>` — the page's main content (unique to the page).\n- `<article>` — an independent, self-contained piece of content (an article, a post).\n- `<section>` — a thematic group of content, normally with a heading.\n- `<aside>` — content that is only indirectly related (a sidebar, a pull-quote).\n- `<footer>` — the concluding part of a section or page.\n- `<figure>` and `<figcaption>` — an illustration with a caption.\n- `<time>` — a date or a time.\n- `<mark>` — highlighted text (search results, for example).\nAn example of use\n\nWhy use semantic tags?\n\n- Accessibility: screen readers and other assistive technologies can interpret a page's structure better, helping users with disabilities.\n- SEO: search engines (Google, Yandex) take semantics into account for ranking and for displaying snippets.\n- Maintainability: the code becomes more readable and easier for a team of developers to maintain.\n- The future: semantic tags are part of the HTML5 standard, supported by every modern browser.\nConclusion: use semantic tags whenever you can, in order to improve accessibility, SEO and the readability of your code. They are especially useful in large projects and when working as a team.",
    codeExample: "<body>\n  <header>\n    <h1>My blog</h1>\n    <nav><a href=\"/\">Home</a></nav>\n  </header>\n  <main>\n    <article>\n      <h2>The article's title</h2>\n      <p>The article's text...</p>\n      <time datetime=\"2025-03-20\">20 March 2025</time>\n    </article>\n    <aside>\n      <p>Advertising or links</p>\n    </aside>\n  </main>\n  <footer>&copy; 2025</footer>\n</body>",
  },
  211: {
    source: "Для чего нужны header, section, article, footer?",
    question: "What are header, section, article and footer for?",
    shortAnswer: "The semantic tags header, section, article and footer are used to mark up a page's content logically. header defines the introductory part or the navigation, section groups thematically related content, article represents a self-contained block (a post or a news item, for example), and footer contains information about the author, a copyright notice or links. They improve the readability of code and help search engines and screen readers.",
    longAnswer: "The purpose of the semantic tags\nThe semantic tags `<header>`, `<section>`, `<article>` and `<footer>` were introduced in HTML5 in order to give a web page's structure real meaning rather than simply using the generic `<div>`. They help browsers, search engines and assistive technologies (screen readers, for example) interpret the content correctly.\nA description of each tag\n\n- <header> — represents the introductory part of a section or of the whole page. It normally contains a logo, a heading, navigation or a search box. It can be used several times on a page.\n- <section> — groups thematically related content. Every `<section>` should have a heading (`<h1>`–`<h6>`, for example). It is used for logical blocks such as chapters, tabs or groups of news items.\n- <article> — denotes a self-contained, independent fragment of content that could be distributed on its own (a blog article, a comment, a news item). An `<article>` may contain its own `<header>` and `<footer>`.\n- <footer> — contains information about the author, a copyright notice, links to a privacy policy or contact details. It is normally placed at the bottom of a section or page.\nAn example of use\n\nConclusion\nUsing semantic tags makes code more readable and improves SEO and accessibility. It is recommended to apply them whenever you can, instead of faceless `<div>` elements, in order to mark every block's purpose clearly.",
    codeExample: "<body>\n  <header>\n    <h1>My blog</h1>\n    <nav>...</nav>\n  </header>\n  <main>\n    <article>\n      <header>\n        <h2>The article's title</h2>\n        <time>2025-01-01</time>\n      </header>\n      <section>\n        <h3>Introduction</h3>\n        <p>The text of the introduction...</p>\n      </section>\n      <section>\n        <h3>The main part</h3>\n        <p>The details...</p>\n      </section>\n      <footer>\n        <p>Author: Ivan</p>\n      </footer>\n    </article>\n  </main>\n  <footer>\n    <p>&copy; 2025 The blog</p>\n  </footer>\n</body>",
  },
  212: {
    source: "Как скрытые элементы влияют на скринридеры?",
    question: "How do hidden elements affect screen readers?",
    shortAnswer: "Hidden elements can be perceived differently by screen readers. Using the CSS property `display: none` or the `hidden` attribute removes an element from the accessibility tree entirely, and the screen reader ignores it. The property `visibility: hidden` also hides an element both visually and from the screen reader. If an element is hidden with `opacity: 0` or `clip`, however, it may remain available to the screen reader, which can confuse the user. To hide something from screen readers semantically, the attribute `aria-hidden=\"true\"` is used.",
    longAnswer: "The effect of hidden elements on screen readers\nScreen readers rely on the accessibility tree, which the browser builds from the HTML code. The way an element is hidden directly affects whether it is included in that tree and therefore announced to the user.\nThe main ways of hiding and their effect\n\n- display: none and the hidden attribute: they remove the element from the accessibility tree entirely. The screen reader does not see or announce it. This is the right way to hide content that should not be available to anyone.\n- visibility: hidden: likewise, the element is hidden both from the visual presentation and from the accessibility tree.\n- opacity: 0 and clip: the element is visually invisible but remains in the accessibility tree. The screen reader may read it out, which can disorient the user, since they cannot see the element on the screen.\n- aria-hidden=\"true\": this attribute tells the screen reader explicitly to ignore the element, regardless of its visual state. It does not remove the element from the DOM but excludes it from the accessibility tree.\nA code example\n\nConclusion\nTo ensure accessibility it is important to choose the right way of hiding elements. Use `display: none` or `hidden` for content that should not be available to anyone. Apply `aria-hidden=\"true\"` for decorative elements or content that is visually present but carries no meaning for screen-reader users. Avoid using `opacity: 0` to hide content that should not be announced.",
    codeExample: "<!-- The element is hidden from everyone completely -->\n<div style=\"display: none;\">Nobody will see or hear this text</div>\n\n<!-- The element is visually hidden but available to a screen reader (bad practice) -->\n<div style=\"opacity: 0;\">This text will be heard but not seen</div>\n\n<!-- The element is visually visible but hidden from the screen reader -->\n<div aria-hidden=\"true\">This text will be seen but not heard</div>",
  },
  213: {
    source: "Что происходит при встрече тега <script> во время парсинга HTML?",
    question: "What happens when a <script> tag is met during the parsing of HTML?",
    shortAnswer: "When the HTML parser meets a `<script>` tag without the `async` or `defer` attributes, it stops building the DOM, loads the script's file and executes it, and only then resumes parsing. This is why such a script is called blocking: the content after it is not processed until it has finished running.",
    longAnswer: "The blocking behaviour of the script tag\nWhen the browser parses HTML and meets a `<script>` tag, it has to assume that the script may change the document — through `document.write`, for example — and therefore cannot carry on building the DOM in parallel. The parsing is paused, the script is loaded (if an external `src` is given) and executed, and only after that does the parser carry on from the place where it stopped. That is why a script in the `<head>` without attributes delays the appearance of the content.\n\nThe `async` and `defer` attributes change this behaviour: both let the file be downloaded in parallel with the parsing. `async` executes the script as soon as it has loaded, without guaranteeing the order; `defer` postpones execution until the HTML has been parsed fully, preserving the order of declaration.\n\nConclusion: for the main code that works with the DOM, use `defer`, and `async` for independent scripts such as analytics, so that the `<script>` tag does not block the building of the DOM.",
    codeExample: null,
  },
  214: {
    source: "Почему между inline-block появляются пробелы?",
    question: "Why do gaps appear between inline-block elements?",
    shortAnswer: "Gaps appear between inline-block elements because the browser interprets the spaces and line breaks in the HTML code as text nodes. Those spaces are displayed as ordinary spaces between the elements, creating visible gaps. To remove them you can use font-size: 0 on the parent, comments between the tags, or write the elements on one line.",
    longAnswer: "The cause of the gaps\nWhen you use display: inline-block on elements, the browser treats them as inline blocks. In HTML the spaces, tabs and line breaks between tags are counted as text nodes. Since inline-block elements behave like text, those spaces are displayed as visible gaps.\nAn example of the problem\n\nA gap about 4px wide will appear between the red and the blue square.\nWays of solving it\n\n- Remove the spaces from the markup: write the elements on one line without spaces.\n- Use comments: <!-- --> between the tags.\n- font-size: 0: set font-size: 0 on the parent and then state the font size explicitly for the child elements.\n- A negative margin: apply margin-right: -4px (it depends on the font size).\nAn example with font-size: 0\n\nThis method is often used in practice, since it requires no change to the HTML structure.\nConclusion\nGaps between inline-block elements are a consequence of how HTML handles whitespace. Understanding this helps you avoid unexpected gaps in your markup. It is recommended to use font-size: 0 on the parent or to write the elements on one line, for the sake of clean code.",
    codeExample: "<div>\n  <span style=\"display: inline-block; width: 50px; height: 50px; background: red;\"></span>\n  <span style=\"display: inline-block; width: 50px; height: 50px; background: blue;\"></span>\n</div>\n\n<div style=\"font-size: 0;\">\n  <span style=\"display: inline-block; width: 50px; height: 50px; background: red; font-size: 16px;\"></span>\n  <span style=\"display: inline-block; width: 50px; height: 50px; background: blue; font-size: 16px;\"></span>\n</div>",
  },
  215: {
    source: "Насколько оправдано использование inline-стилей?",
    question: "How justified is the use of inline styles?",
    shortAnswer: "Inline styles are styles set directly in the style attribute of an HTML element. They have a high priority, but their use is justified only in rare cases — for dynamic styles that depend on JavaScript, for example, or for quick prototyping. In production it is better to use CSS classes, since they improve readability, reusability and performance.",
    longAnswer: "What inline styles are and when they are applied\nInline styles are a way of setting CSS properties directly in the `style` attribute of an HTML element. For example: `<div style=\"color: red; font-size: 16px;\">Text</div>`. This approach lets you apply styles to a particular element quickly without creating separate CSS rules. Its use has both advantages and drawbacks, however.\nThe pros and cons of inline styles\n\n- Pros: a high priority (it overrides most other styles), convenience for dynamic styles (when changed through JavaScript, for example), simplicity for quick testing.\n- Cons: poor reusability (the styles are tied to a particular element), difficulty of maintenance (changes require every element to be edited), an increase in the size of the HTML, the impossibility of using pseudo-classes and media queries, reduced performance when they are changed frequently through the DOM.\nExamples of use\nHere is an example where inline styles may be justified — changing a colour dynamically depending on state:\n\nFor static styling, however, it is better to use classes:\n\nConclusion\nInline styles are worth applying only for dynamic changes that cannot be implemented through CSS classes, or in extremely small projects. In most cases external CSS files or CSS modules are preferable, for better maintainability and performance.",
    codeExample: "const button = document.getElementById('myButton');\nbutton.style.backgroundColor = isActive ? 'green' : 'red';\n\n<style>\n  .active { background-color: green; }\n  .inactive { background-color: red; }\n</style>\n<div class=\"active\">A button</div>",
  },
  216: {
    source: "Какой браузер используется чаще всего на Android?",
    question: "Which browser is used most often on Android?",
    shortAnswer: "On Android the browser used most often is Google Chrome. It is pre-installed on most devices and is tightly integrated with Google's services. Other popular browsers include Samsung Internet, Firefox and Opera.",
    longAnswer: "Popular browsers on Android\nOn the Android mobile platform the most widespread browser is Google Chrome. This is because Chrome is pre-installed on most Android devices, especially those certified by Google. It provides deep integration with Google's ecosystem and synchronizes bookmarks, passwords and history between devices.\nOther popular browsers\n\n- Samsung Internet — pre-installed on Samsung devices; it has a convenient interface and supports extensions.\n- Mozilla Firefox — it offers extended privacy settings and support for add-ons.\n- Opera — known for its built-in VPN and its data-saving mode.\nConclusion\nGoogle Chrome dominates on Android thanks to being pre-installed and integrated with Google's services, but other browsers also have their audience thanks to their unique features.",
    codeExample: null,
  },
  217: {
    source: "Какой браузер чаще всего используется на десктопе?",
    question: "Which browser is used most often on the desktop?",
    shortAnswer: "On the desktop the browser used most often is Google Chrome. Its market share is around 65-70 %. Other popular browsers are Safari, Firefox and Edge. The statistics can change over time.",
    longAnswer: "Which browser is used most often on the desktop?\nOn desktop computers the most popular browser is Google Chrome. According to various analytics services its market share is consistently around 65-70 %. This means that roughly two out of every three PC users open web pages in Chrome.\nWhy does Chrome lead?\n\n- Speed and performance: Chrome is known for loading pages quickly and handling JavaScript efficiently.\n- Extensions: the huge library of extensions in the Chrome Web Store lets you configure the browser for any task.\n- Integration with Google's services: convenient synchronization with Gmail, Google Drive, YouTube and other services.\n- Security: regular updates and built-in mechanisms for protection against malicious sites.\nOther popular browsers\n\n- Safari: it takes second place and is especially popular among macOS users.\n- Firefox: valued for its privacy and its open source code.\n- Microsoft Edge: it is gaining popularity thanks to its integration with Windows and its use of the Chromium engine.\nConclusion\nWhen developing web applications it is important to test them in Google Chrome first and foremost, since that will ensure they work correctly for most users. You should not forget the other browsers either, however, in order to guarantee compatibility with different platforms.",
    codeExample: null,
  },
  218: {
    source: "Нужно ли учитывать старые модели устройств?",
    question: "Do you need to take older device models into account?",
    shortAnswer: "Yes, older device models and browsers have to be taken into account if your target audience uses them. This ensures the application is accessible and works correctly for every user. Apply progressive enhancement or graceful degradation, so that the basic functionality works everywhere while modern capabilities are added for newer browsers.",
    longAnswer: "Why take older devices into account?\nSupporting older device models and browsers matters for making your web application accessible. If a significant part of your target audience uses obsolete browsers (Internet Explorer 11 or old versions of Safari on iOS, for example), ignoring them can lead to losing users and to lower conversion.\nApproaches to support\n\n- Progressive enhancement — a basic version that works on every device is built first, and then modern features are added for newer browsers.\n- Graceful degradation — the full version for modern browsers is developed first, and then it is made to work properly (possibly with a simplified interface) on older ones.\nA code example: checking support for CSS Grid\n\nIn this example modern browsers use Grid while older ones fall back to Flexbox.\nConclusion\nTaking older devices into account is necessary if your audience uses them actively. Apply progressive enhancement to ensure the basic functionality on every platform, and add modern capabilities as an enhancement for newer browsers.",
    codeExample: "@supports (display: grid) {\n  .container {\n    display: grid;\n    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;\n  }\n}\n\n/* A fallback for older browsers */\n.container {\n  display: flex;\n  flex-wrap: wrap;\n}\n\n.container > * {\n  flex: 1 1 50%;\n}",
  },
  219: {
    source: "Как проверить цвет через инструменты браузера?",
    question: "How do you check a colour through the browser's tools?",
    shortAnswer: "Open the developer tools (F12), go to the Elements tab and select the element. In the Styles panel find the CSS property color or background-color. Click the little coloured square next to the value in order to open the palette and see the exact colour.",
    longAnswer: "Checking a colour through the browser's tools\nThe browser's developer tools provide a convenient way of inspecting the colours used on a web page. This is necessary for precise markup, for debugging styles and for ensuring conformity with the design.\nThe main steps\n\n- Open the developer tools (F12 or Ctrl+Shift+I).\n- Go to the Elements (or Inspector) tab.\n- Select the element with the picker tool (the cursor icon) or by clicking the HTML code.\n- In the right-hand panel find the Styles (or Computed) tab.\n- Find the CSS property that sets the colour (color, background-color, border-color, for example).\n- Next to the colour's value (#ff0000, for example) there will be a little coloured square — click it.\nWorking with the palette\nOnce clicked, a colour palette opens where you can:\n\n- See the colour's exact value in HEX, RGB, HSL or other formats.\n- Change the colour by dragging the sliders or typing values.\n- Copy the colour's value to the clipboard.\nA code example\n\nIn the developer tools you will see these colours and be able to check them.\nConclusion\nUse the developer tools to check and adjust colours quickly in real time, which is especially useful when writing markup and debugging styles.",
    codeExample: "<div style=\"color: #3498db; background-color: #f1c40f;\">Some example text</div>",
  },
  220: {
    source: "Что такое HEX-цвет?",
    question: "What is a HEX colour?",
    shortAnswer: "A HEX colour is a hexadecimal representation of a colour, used in web design. It consists of a hash symbol (#) and six hexadecimal digits that define the intensity of the red, green and blue channels. #FF0000, for example, means red. HEX colours are widely applied in CSS to set the colours of elements.",
    longAnswer: "What is a HEX colour?\nA HEX colour is a way of representing a colour in the hexadecimal number system, widely used in web development and design. It is based on the RGB model, in which every colour is defined by three channels: red, green and blue. Each channel is encoded by two hexadecimal digits from 00 to FF, which corresponds to the decimal values from 0 to 255.\nThe structure of a HEX colour\nA HEX colour begins with a hash symbol (#), followed by six characters: the first two are the red channel, the next two the green and the last two the blue. #FF0000, for example, means the maximum intensity of red (255) and no green or blue — that is, pure red.\nExamples and use\nHEX colours are used in CSS to set the colours of backgrounds, text, borders and other elements. Here is an example:\n\nThere are also shortened three-character HEX codes — #F00, for example, is equivalent to #FF0000. This is convenient for simple colours.\nConclusion\nHEX colours are the standard, convenient way of specifying colours in web design, especially in CSS. They provide precise control over colour and are easy to read, which makes them indispensable to interface developers.",
    codeExample: "/* A red background */\nbackground-color: #FF0000;\n\n/* Green text */\ncolor: #00FF00;\n\n/* A blue border */\nborder: 2px solid #0000FF;",
  },
  221: {
    source: "Какой браузер используется по умолчанию на iOS?",
    question: "Which browser is used by default on iOS?",
    shortAnswer: "On iOS every browser, Chrome and Firefox included, is obliged to use Apple's WebKit engine. This means that the default and the only full-featured browser is Safari, since the others are merely shells over it. The user can install alternatives, but they have no rendering engines of their own.",
    longAnswer: "Which browser is used by default on iOS?\nOn iOS devices (iPhone, iPad) the default browser is Safari. It is a pre-installed application from Apple that uses the WebKit engine. It is important to understand that because of Apple's policy every third-party browser (Chrome, Firefox, Edge, for example) is also obliged to use WebKit rather than its own engine (Blink or Gecko). So technically every browser on iOS is a shell over Safari with a different interface and synchronization.\nWhy does this matter?\nThis restriction affects web development: if you test a site in Chrome on iOS, it renders the same way as in Safari. To check compatibility it is therefore enough to target Safari and WebKit. The user can change their default browser in the settings, but that will not change the engine.\nExample code for detecting the browser on iOS\n\nConclusion\nKnowing about this particular helps you avoid errors in cross-browser testing and in optimizing for Apple's mobile devices. Always check your sites in Safari on iOS, since it is the only browser with full access to the platform's features.",
    codeExample: "// Checking whether the browser is Safari on iOS\nconst isIOS = /iPad|iPhone|iPod/.test(navigator.userAgent);\nconst isSafari = /^((?!chrome|android).)*safari/i.test(navigator.userAgent);\nif (isIOS && isSafari) {\n  console.log('This is Safari on iOS');\n} else if (isIOS) {\n  console.log('A different browser on iOS (but it uses WebKit)');\n}",
  },
  222: {
    source: "Какие браузеры работают на одном движке?",
    question: "Which browsers run on the same engine?",
    shortAnswer: "Browsers can run on the same engine. Google Chrome, Opera, Brave, Microsoft Edge (from 2020) and Vivaldi, for example, use the Blink engine (a fork of WebKit). Safari uses WebKit. Firefox uses Gecko. This matters for cross-browser testing.",
    longAnswer: "What is a browser engine?\nA browser engine (a rendering engine) is the key component of a browser, responsible for parsing HTML and CSS and displaying a web page on the screen. Different browsers may use the same engine, which means their rendering behaves similarly, but it does not guarantee complete identity because of differences in the other components (the JavaScript engine, the security settings and so on).\nThe main engines and the browsers based on them\n\n- Blink (Google's fork of WebKit): Google Chrome, Microsoft Edge (Chromium-based), Opera, Brave, Vivaldi, Samsung Internet.\n- WebKit: Apple Safari, and every browser on iOS (because of Apple's requirements).\n- Gecko: Mozilla Firefox, Tor Browser.\n- Goanna (a fork of Gecko): Pale Moon, Basilisk.\nExample code for checking the engine\n\nWhy does this matter?\nKnowing the engine helps you predict how a page will be displayed in different browsers. CSS properties supported in Blink, for example, may not work in Gecko. This is critical when developing cross-browser applications.\nConclusion: understanding which engines are shared simplifies the testing and optimization of web applications, letting you concentrate on the key differences between the main engines (Blink, WebKit, Gecko).",
    codeExample: "// Detecting the browser engine through navigator.userAgent\nfunction getEngine() {\n  const ua = navigator.userAgent;\n  if (ua.includes('Gecko') && !ua.includes('WebKit')) return 'Gecko';\n  if (ua.includes('AppleWebKit') && !ua.includes('Chrome')) return 'WebKit';\n  if (ua.includes('AppleWebKit') && ua.includes('Chrome')) return 'Blink';\n  return 'Unknown';\n}\nconsole.log(getEngine());",
  },
  223: {
    source: "Что такое viewport?",
    question: "What is the viewport?",
    shortAnswer: "The viewport is the visible area of a web page on a device's screen. Without configuring it properly, mobile browsers may scale the page, making it awkward to use. The `<meta name=\"viewport\">` meta tag is used to control it. It is the foundation of responsive markup.",
    longAnswer: "What is the viewport?\nThe viewport is the rectangular area of the screen in which a web page is displayed. On a desktop it coincides with the browser window, while on mobile devices it may be smaller than the physical screen. Without an explicit viewport declaration, browsers on phones often try to show the whole page at once, scaling it down, which makes the text and the elements too small.\nWhy control the viewport?\nTo create responsive sites that display correctly on any device, the viewport's width has to be set equal to the device's screen width. This is achieved with a meta tag in the HTML. Without it, users will have to scale the page by hand, which degrades their experience.\nHow does it work?\nThe viewport meta tag tells the browser how to control the page's dimensions and scaling. The most widespread variant is:\n\nThe parameter `width=device-width` sets the viewport's width equal to the device's screen width. `initial-scale=1.0` sets the initial scale to 100 % (no zoom).\nAn example of use\nConsider a simple page without the viewport and with it:\n\nWithout the tag the text will be small on a phone and the user will have to zoom in. With the tag the text is displayed at its natural size.\nConclusion\nThe viewport is a critically important concept for responsive web design. Configuring it properly is mandatory for any site aimed at mobile devices, and it is the first step towards creating a convenient user interface.",
    codeExample: "<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0\">\n\n<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html>\n<head>\n  <!-- Without this tag a mobile browser will scale the page down -->\n  <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0\">\n</head>\n<body>\n  <p>Text that will be readable on a phone.</p>\n</body>\n</html>",
  },
  224: {
    source: "Как фиксировать отклонения от макета?",
    question: "How do you record deviations from the mockup?",
    shortAnswer: "Deviations from the mockup are recorded through visual comparison: a screenshot of the markup is overlaid on the mockup in an overlay mode (in the Pixel Perfect plugin, for example). Visual regression testing tools (Percy, Applitools) are also used, which compare screenshots automatically. It is important to document every discrepancy in a bug tracker with a screenshot and a description.",
    longAnswer: "How to record deviations from the mockup\nDeviations from the mockup are discrepancies between the implemented interface and the approved design. Recording them is critical for maintaining the product's quality and a consistent user experience. The main goal is to uncover discrepancies at the early stages of development, in order to avoid accumulating technical debt.\nMethods of recording\n\n- Manual visual comparison: use browser extensions (Pixel Perfect, PerfectPixel) to overlay a screenshot of the mockup on top of the markup. Adjust the transparency and check the spacing, the font sizes and the colours.\n- Automated visual regression testing: tools (Percy, Applitools, Chromatic) take screenshots of components and compare them with reference images. When a discrepancy is found, a report is generated with the changes highlighted.\n- Inspecting the code: use DevTools to check the CSS properties (padding, margin, font-size, color) and compare them with the values from the mockup (Figma, Sketch).\nExample code for an automatic check\n\nThe documentation process\n\n- Take a screenshot of the problematic area.\n- Give the exact location (the URL, the component, the screen resolution).\n- Describe the expected behaviour (from the mockup) and the actual behaviour.\n- Attach a link to the mockup in Figma or another tool.\n- Create a ticket in the bug tracker (Jira, Trello) with a priority.\nConclusion: recording deviations from the mockup is a mandatory part of the QA process. Manual methods suit spot checks, while automated tools save time in regression testing. Use a combination of approaches for maximum coverage.",
    codeExample: "// An example of using Percy for visual testing\nimport PercyScript from '@percy/script';\n\nPercyScript.run(async (page, percySnapshot) => {\n  await page.goto('http://localhost:3000');\n  // We take a snapshot of the page\n  await percySnapshot('Homepage');\n  // If there are discrepancies, Percy will mark them in its report\n});",
  },
  225: {
    source: "Как браузерные расширения могут влиять на работу приложения?",
    question: "How can browser extensions affect an application's behaviour?",
    shortAnswer: "Browser extensions can change the DOM, inject scripts, block resources or intercept requests. This can lead to unexpected behaviour in an application — errors in the JavaScript or changed styles, for example. It is important for developers to allow for this in their testing and to use code isolation.",
    longAnswer: "The effect of browser extensions on web applications\nBrowser extensions are programs that run in the browser's context and can interact with web pages. They can affect an application's behaviour in several ways, including changing the DOM, injecting additional scripts, blocking network requests or modifying the CSS. This can cause errors, reduced performance or even security problems.\nThe main mechanisms of influence\n\n- Changing the DOM: extensions can add, remove or change the elements on a page, which can break an application's logic, especially if it relies on a particular structure.\n- Injecting scripts: an extension's content scripts run in the page's context and can intercept events, change global objects or cause conflicts with the application's code.\n- Blocking resources: some extensions block advertising, trackers or scripts, which can leave necessary dependencies missing.\n- Changing styles: extensions can override CSS, which affects the interface's appearance and behaviour.\nA code example: how an extension can change a page\n\nHow to protect your application\nDevelopers can minimize the effect of extensions by using code isolation (the Shadow DOM, for example), by checking that the necessary elements are present before using them, and by testing in different environments. It is also important to avoid global variables and to use strict type checks.\nConclusion: browser extensions can affect an application's behaviour unpredictably, so it is important to test it in various conditions and to apply code-isolation practices in order to increase its robustness.",
    codeExample: "// An extension's content script that adds a button to the page\nconst button = document.createElement('button');\nbutton.textContent = 'Click me';\ndocument.body.appendChild(button);\n\n// This can break the application if it expects a particular number of elements",
  },
  226: {
    source: "Какие способы кросс-браузерного тестирования существуют?",
    question: "What ways of cross-browser testing are there?",
    shortAnswer: "Cross-browser testing involves checking by hand in different browsers, using automated tools (Selenium, Playwright, for example), using cloud testing services (BrowserStack, Sauce Labs) and using emulators/simulators. Polyfills and CSS prefixes are also applied to ensure compatibility. It is important to test on real devices and browser versions.",
    longAnswer: "The main ways of cross-browser testing\nCross-browser testing is necessary in order to check that a web application works the same way in various browsers (Chrome, Firefox, Safari, Edge and others) and their versions. It helps you uncover problems with display, functionality and performance.\nManual testing\nThe developer or tester opens the application in different browsers by hand and checks its behaviour. This method is simple but laborious when there are a great many combinations.\nAutomated testing\nTools are used that run the tests in different browsers automatically. Examples:\n\n- Selenium WebDriver — it lets you write tests in various languages (Java, Python, JavaScript) and run them in browsers.\n- Playwright — a modern tool from Microsoft that supports every major browser and has automatic waiting.\n- Cypress — oriented towards testing in a real browser, but it supports only Chrome-like browsers.\nAn example in Playwright:\n\nCloud services\nServices such as BrowserStack, Sauce Labs and LambdaTest provide access to real browsers and devices in the cloud. This lets you test on a great many configurations without installing local environments.\nEmulators and simulators\nFor mobile browsers, emulators (Android Studio, for example) or simulators (Xcode for iOS) are used. They help you check responsiveness and touch interaction.\nPolyfills and CSS prefixes\nTo support older browsers, polyfills (JavaScript code emulating modern APIs) and vendor prefixes in CSS (-webkit-, -moz-, for example) are applied. This is not a testing method but a way of reducing the number of problems.\nConclusion\nCross-browser testing should be combined: use automation for regression checks, cloud services to cover a great many configurations, and manual testing for complex scenarios. This matters especially for public web applications with a wide audience.",
    codeExample: "const { chromium } = require('playwright');\n(async () => {\n  const browser = await chromium.launch();\n  const page = await browser.newPage();\n  await page.goto('https://example.com');\n  const title = await page.title();\n  console.log(title);\n  await browser.close();\n})();",
  },
  227: {
    source: "Какие данные содержит HTTP Response?",
    question: "What data does an HTTP response contain?",
    shortAnswer: "An HTTP response consists of three main parts: the status line, the headers and the response body. The status line contains the protocol version, the status code and a text description. The headers carry metadata such as Content-Type or Cache-Control. The response body contains the data that was requested — an HTML page or JSON, for example.",
    longAnswer: "The structure of an HTTP response\nAn HTTP response is the server's reply to a client's request. It consists of three key components: the status line, the headers and the body. Each of them plays its own part in conveying information.\nThe status line\nThe response's first line contains the HTTP version, a numeric status code and a brief description. For example: `HTTP/1.1 200 OK`. The status code indicates the result of processing the request: 200 is success, 404 means not found, 500 an internal server error.\nThe headers\nThe headers carry metadata about the response. They may include the content type (`Content-Type`), the size (`Content-Length`), the caching policy (`Cache-Control`) and others. The headers are separated from the body by a blank line.\nThe response body\nThe body contains the actual data the client requested. It may be HTML, JSON, an image or any other resource. Not every response has a body — a response with the code 204 No Content, for example.\nAn example HTTP response\n\nIn this example the status line indicates success, the headers describe the type and size of the data, and the body contains a JSON object.\nConclusion\nUnderstanding the structure of an HTTP response is necessary for developing web applications, debugging network requests and configuring servers. It is the foundation of working with REST APIs and web technologies.",
    codeExample: "HTTP/1.1 200 OK\nContent-Type: application/json\nContent-Length: 27\n\n{\"message\": \"Hello, World!\"}",
  },
  228: {
    source: "Какие существуют CSP directives?",
    question: "What CSP directives are there?",
    shortAnswer: "CSP directives determine which resources may be loaded on a page. The main ones are: default-src (the base rule), script-src (scripts), style-src (styles), img-src (images), connect-src (network requests), font-src (fonts), frame-src (frames) and object-src (plugins). They help prevent XSS and injections.",
    longAnswer: "What are CSP directives?\nContent Security Policy (CSP) is a security mechanism that lets web developers control which resources may be loaded and executed on their pages. CSP directives are the rules stated in the Content-Security-Policy HTTP header or in a meta tag. They help prevent cross-site scripting (XSS) attacks and other injections.\nThe main directives\n\n- default-src — sets the base rule for every type of resource when no more specific directive is given.\n- script-src — defines the permitted sources for JavaScript.\n- style-src — controls the loading of CSS.\n- img-src — permits the sources for images.\n- connect-src — manages network requests (fetch, XMLHttpRequest).\n- font-src — sets the sources for fonts.\n- frame-src — defines which sources may be embedded in an iframe.\n- object-src — controls the loading of plugins (Flash, for example).\nAn example of use\n\nIn this example only the site's own resources are permitted, plus scripts from Google APIs, inline styles and images from any source.\nConclusion\nCSP directives are a powerful tool for increasing the security of web applications. They are worth applying in any project where protection against XSS and control over the resources that load matter.",
    codeExample: "Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' https://apis.google.com; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src *;",
  },
  229: {
    source: "Что такое hash в CSP?",
    question: "What is a hash in CSP?",
    shortAnswer: "A hash in CSP is a cryptographic hash (SHA-256, SHA-384 or SHA-512) of the contents of an inline script or style. It is added to the Content-Security-Policy header in order to permit the execution only of inline code whose hash matches. This lets you use inline scripts safely without abandoning CSP.",
    longAnswer: "What is a hash in CSP?\nContent Security Policy (CSP) is a security mechanism that helps prevent XSS attacks by restricting the sources from which resources may be loaded. Inline scripts (code right in the HTML) are hard to control, however, since they have no external source. To solve this problem CSP supports the use of hashes.\nA hash is a cryptographic hash (SHA-256, for example) of the contents of an inline script or style. You compute the hash of the script's exact text and add it to your CSP policy. When the page loads, the browser computes the hash of every inline script and compares it with the permitted ones. If the hash matches, the script runs; otherwise it is blocked.\nHow does it work in practice?\nSuppose you have an inline script:\n\nYou compute its SHA-256 hash (with an online tool or on the command line, for example). You get a string like 'sha256-abc123...'. You then add it to the CSP header:\n\nNow the browser will permit only that particular script to run. If someone tries to inject a different inline script, its hash will not match and it will be blocked.\nWhere is this applied?\nA hash in CSP is useful when you cannot do without inline scripts (for analytics or custom widgets, for example) and want to keep a strict security policy. It is an alternative to using a nonce (a one-off token).\nConclusion: use a hash in CSP when you need to permit particular inline scripts without compromising security. It is a reliable way of protecting yourself against XSS while keeping your markup flexible.",
    codeExample: "<script>alert('Hello');</script>\n\nContent-Security-Policy: script-src 'sha256-abc123...'",
  },
  230: {
    source: "Какие существуют методы защиты клиентской части приложения?",
    question: "What methods exist for protecting an application's client side?",
    shortAnswer: "Protecting the client side involves preventing XSS by escaping input, using a Content Security Policy (CSP) to restrict the sources of scripts, mandatory HTTPS for encrypting traffic, validating data on both the client and the server, and applying the same-origin policy to isolate resources.",
    longAnswer: "The main methods of protecting the client side\nThe security of an application's client side is aimed at protection against attacks such as cross-site scripting (XSS), request forgery (CSRF) and the interception of data. These methods help prevent the theft of sessions, the injection of malicious code and the leaking of confidential information.\nThe key approaches\n\n- Content Security Policy (CSP) — an HTTP header restricting the sources from which scripts, styles and other resources may be loaded. `Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'`, for example, blocks inline scripts.\n- Escaping input — converting special characters (`<` into `&lt;`, for example) in order to prevent XSS. In React this is done automatically through JSX.\n- HTTPS — encrypting the data between client and server, protecting against interception (man-in-the-middle).\n- The same-origin policy — restricting scripts' access to resources on another domain. CORS is used for cross-domain requests.\n- Validation and sanitization — checking the data on the client (with regular expressions, for example) and checking it again on the server, which is mandatory.\nA code example: CSP and escaping\n\nConclusion: a combination of CSP, escaping, HTTPS and validation creates multi-layered protection. These methods are mandatory for any web application that works with user data, especially in forms, comments and authentication.",
    codeExample: "// Setting CSP through a meta tag\n<meta http-equiv=\"Content-Security-Policy\" content=\"default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' https://trusted.cdn.com\">\n\n// Escaping input in JavaScript\nfunction escapeHTML(str) {\n  return str.replace(/&/g, '&amp;')\n            .replace(/</g, '&lt;')\n            .replace(/>/g, '&gt;')\n            .replace(/\"/g, '&quot;');\n}\n\n// Use in React (automatic)\nconst userInput = \"<script>alert('xss')</script>\";\nreturn <div>{userInput}</div>; // Safe",
  },
  231: {
    source: "Какие клиентские уязвимости встречаются во frontend?",
    question: "Which client-side vulnerabilities are met in frontend work?",
    shortAnswer: "The main client-side vulnerabilities are: XSS (cross-site scripting), CSRF (cross-site request forgery), clickjacking, the insecure storage of data (localStorage, cookies) and leaks through Referer headers. XSS lets a malicious script be injected; CSRF lets an action be performed on the user's behalf. The defences: escaping output, a Content Security Policy, SameSite cookies, the X-Frame-Options header.",
    longAnswer: "The main client-side vulnerabilities in frontend work\nClient-side vulnerabilities are security problems that arise in the user's browser. They can lead to the theft of data, the performance of unauthorized actions or the compromise of a session. The most widespread kinds are: XSS, CSRF, clickjacking and the insecure storage of data.\nXSS (Cross-Site Scripting)\nXSS lets an attacker inject malicious JavaScript code into a page, which is then executed in the victim's browser. There are three kinds: reflected, stored and DOM-based. An example of vulnerable code:\n\nThe defences: escaping all data before inserting it into HTML, using a Content Security Policy (CSP), avoiding innerHTML where possible.\nCSRF (Cross-Site Request Forgery)\nCSRF makes the victim's browser send a request to the target site on their behalf, using their cookies. An attacker might place a form on their own site that sends a POST request to change a password, for example. The defences: using CSRF tokens (unique to the session), checking the Origin/Referer header, setting the SameSite attribute on cookies (Strict or Lax).\nClickjacking\nClickjacking hides the target site in an invisible iframe while the user thinks they are clicking the elements of another site. The defences: setting the header X-Frame-Options: DENY or SAMEORIGIN, using a Content Security Policy with the frame-ancestors directive.\nThe insecure storage of data\nStoring sensitive data (tokens, passwords) in localStorage or sessionStorage makes it reachable by any script on the page. It is recommended to use httpOnly cookies for tokens, and localStorage only for non-critical data.\nConclusion\nKnowing the client-side vulnerabilities is necessary for writing secure code. Apply escaping, CSP, CSRF tokens and the proper storage of data in order to minimize the risks. These practices are mandatory for any frontend developer who works with user data.",
    codeExample: "// Vulnerable code: inserting the user's input directly into HTML\ndocument.getElementById('output').innerHTML = userInput;\n\n// The safe variant: using textContent\ndocument.getElementById('output').textContent = userInput;",
  },
  232: {
    source: "Какие угрозы решает CSP?",
    question: "Which threats does CSP address?",
    shortAnswer: "CSP (Content Security Policy) is an HTTP header that lets you control which resources (scripts, styles, images) may be loaded on a page. It prevents XSS attacks by blocking the execution of inline scripts and the loading of resources from untrusted sources. CSP is set through the Content-Security-Policy header and can be configured for different kinds of content.",
    longAnswer: "What CSP is and which threats it addresses\nContent Security Policy (CSP) is a security mechanism implemented through an HTTP header that lets web developers control which resources may be loaded and executed on their pages. CSP's main purpose is protection against cross-site scripting (XSS) attacks and other kinds of content injection.\nThe main threats CSP addresses:\n\n- XSS (Cross-Site Scripting) — attacks in which an attacker injects a malicious script into a page. CSP blocks the execution of inline scripts and of scripts from untrusted sources.\n- Clickjacking — attacks in which a user is tricked into clicking a hidden element. CSP can forbid the page from being embedded in an iframe.\n- Data injection — the injection of malicious data through forms or the URL. CSP restricts the sources from which data may be loaded.\nHow does CSP work?\nCSP is set through the `Content-Security-Policy` HTTP header and contains directives defining the permitted sources for various kinds of content. For example:\n\nIn this example:\n\n- `default-src 'self'` — permits every resource to be loaded only from the same domain.\n- `script-src 'self' https://trusted.cdn.com` — permits scripts only from your own domain and the named CDN.\n- `style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'` — permits inline styles (though this is not recommended for security).\nAn example of an XSS attack and protection with CSP\nSuppose a page has a vulnerability that lets a script be inserted:\n\nWithout CSP this script would run. With CSP, if the `script-src` directive does not permit inline scripts, the browser will block its execution and show an error in the console.\nConclusion\nCSP is a powerful tool for protecting web applications against XSS and other content-injection attacks. It should be applied on every production site, especially those that handle user input or use third-party scripts. Configuring CSP properly requires a balance between security and functionality, but even a basic configuration reduces the risks considerably.",
    codeExample: "Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' https://trusted.cdn.com; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline';\n\n<script>alert('XSS')</script>",
  },
  233: {
    source: "Что такое HTML?",
    question: "What is HTML?",
    shortAnswer: "HTML (HyperText Markup Language) is the standard markup language for creating web pages. It uses tags to define a document's structure and content — headings, paragraphs, links and images. Browsers interpret the HTML and display it as a visual page.",
    longAnswer: "What is HTML?\nHTML (HyperText Markup Language) is the fundamental markup language used to create and structure the content of web pages. It is not a programming language, since it contains no logic or algorithms; it serves to describe how text, images, links and other elements should be organized and displayed in the browser.\nThe main concepts\nHTML works on the basis of tags, which are enclosed in angle brackets. Tags normally come in pairs: an opening one and a closing one — `<p>` and `</p>`, for example. The content sits inside the tags. Tags' attributes let you set additional properties such as links (`href`) or identifiers (`id`).\nAn example of a simple HTML page\n\nIn this example `<!DOCTYPE html>` declares the document's type, `<html>` is the root element, `<head>` contains the metadata (the tab's title, for example) and `<body>` the page's visible content.\nWhere HTML is applied\nHTML is used everywhere in web development, from simple static sites to complex web applications. It is the foundation on which styles (CSS) and interactivity (JavaScript) are laid. The modern internet is unimaginable without HTML.\nConclusion: HTML is a mandatory tool for every web developer. It lets you create structured, accessible web pages that browsers and search engines can read easily.",
    codeExample: "<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html>\n<head>\n    <title>My first page</title>\n</head>\n<body>\n    <h1>Hello, world!</h1>\n    <p>This is a paragraph of text.</p>\n    <a href=\"https://example.com\">A link</a>\n</body>\n</html>",
  },
  234: {
    source: "Что такое адаптивная верстка?",
    question: "What is responsive markup?",
    shortAnswer: "Responsive markup is an approach in which a site's design adapts to the size of the device's screen. CSS media queries are used to change the styles according to the width of the browser window. This lets a site look equally good on desktops, tablets and smartphones. The main goal is to make reading and navigation comfortable without horizontal scrolling.",
    longAnswer: "What is responsive markup?\nResponsive web design is a method of creating web pages that adapt automatically to the size of the user's device screen. Unlike fixed markup, where the width is set in pixels, responsive markup uses relative units (percentages, em, rem) and media queries to change the layout.\nThe main principles\n\n- A flexible grid: elements are positioned as percentages of the parent container's width.\n- Flexible images: pictures scale with max-width: 100%.\n- Media queries: CSS rules applied under particular conditions (when the screen is narrower than 768px, for example).\nA code example\n\nWhere it is applied\nResponsive markup is used in most modern sites, especially in online shops, blogs and news portals. It is critically important for SEO, since Google gives preference to mobile-friendly sites.\nConclusion: responsive markup is the standard of modern web design. It is worth applying whenever a site has to be available on different devices, in order to ensure a good user experience and a high position in search results.",
    codeExample: "/* The basic grid */\n.container {\n  width: 100%;\n  max-width: 1200px;\n  margin: 0 auto;\n}\n\n/* A media query for tablets */\n@media (max-width: 768px) {\n  .container {\n    padding: 0 15px;\n  }\n  .sidebar {\n    display: none;\n  }\n}\n\n/* A media query for phones */\n@media (max-width: 480px) {\n  .header {\n    font-size: 18px;\n  }\n}",
  },
  235: {
    source: "Чем DOM отличается от HTML?",
    question: "How does the DOM differ from HTML?",
    shortAnswer: "HTML is the textual markup you write in a file. The DOM (Document Object Model) is the object model of the document that the browser creates from the HTML. The DOM is a living, dynamic tree of nodes that can be changed with JavaScript, whereas the HTML stays unchanged. Changes to the DOM are not saved back into the HTML file.",
    longAnswer: "What are HTML and the DOM?\nHTML (HyperText Markup Language) is a markup language that describes a web page's structure as text with tags. When the browser loads an HTML file, it parses it and builds the DOM (Document Object Model) — an object representation of the document in memory. The DOM is a tree structure in which every HTML tag becomes a node and the text inside the tags becomes text nodes.\nThe key differences\n\n- Static vs dynamic: HTML is static — it is just text. The DOM is dynamic; it can be changed through JavaScript (adding, removing and changing nodes).\n- The representation: HTML is a string. The DOM is an object model you can interact with programmatically.\n- The effect on the page: changes to the DOM are reflected on the page immediately (the browser repaints it). Changes to the HTML file require the page to be reloaded.\nAn example\nImagine you have this HTML:\n\nAfter parsing it, the browser creates a DOM node for the `<div>` and a text node \"Hello\". With JavaScript you can change the text:\n\nThe DOM has now changed and the page displays \"World\", but the original HTML file has stayed the same.\nConclusion\nThe DOM is the bridge between static HTML and dynamic JavaScript. Understanding this difference is necessary for working with interactive web pages where the content has to change without a reload.",
    codeExample: "<div id=\"app\">Hello</div>\n\nconst div = document.getElementById('app');\ndiv.textContent = 'World';",
  },
  236: {
    source: "Как получить элемент по идентификатору?",
    question: "How do you get an element by its identifier?",
    shortAnswer: "The method document.getElementById('id') returns the element with the given id. If the element is not found, it returns null. This is the fastest way of finding a single element by its unique identifier.",
    longAnswer: "The basics of the getElementById method\nThe `document.getElementById()` method is one of the simplest and fastest ways of reaching a DOM element in JavaScript. It takes a string — the value of an element's `id` attribute — and returns the element itself if it exists, or `null` if the element is not found.\nAn example of use\n\nWhere it is applied\nThis method is used in any web application where you need to get hold of a particular element quickly in order to change its content or styles or handle its events. It is especially useful when working with forms, modal windows and dynamic interfaces.\nConclusion\nUse `getElementById` for quick access to a page's unique elements, when the id is known and guaranteed to be unique.",
    codeExample: "<div id=\"header\">A heading</div>\n<script>\n  const header = document.getElementById('header');\n  if (header) {\n    header.style.color = 'blue';\n  }\n</script>",
  },
  237: {
    source: "Как очищать содержимое DOM-элемента?",
    question: "How do you clear the contents of a DOM element?",
    shortAnswer: "The simplest way is to assign an empty string to the innerHTML property: element.innerHTML = ''. This is inefficient, however, and can cause memory leaks. It is better to use element.textContent = '' or a loop with removeChild. The modern method is element.replaceChildren(), which removes every child node in a single call.",
    longAnswer: "Clearing the contents of a DOM element\nClearing a DOM element is a frequent task when working with a dynamic interface. The wrong approach can lead to memory leaks or slow performance. Let us look at the main methods.\nThe methods of clearing\n\n- innerHTML = '' — the simplest, but slow and potentially dangerous: it removes every child element, event handlers included, which can cause leaks.\n- textContent = '' — faster than innerHTML, since it does not parse HTML. It removes only the text nodes and does not touch the child elements.\n- removeChild in a loop — a reliable way; it removes every child node one at a time. For example:\n\n- replaceChildren() — the modern method (ES2021); it removes every child node in a single call. For example:\n\nConclusion\nFor most cases use `replaceChildren()` — it is fast, safe and readable. If support for older browsers is needed, a loop with `removeChild` will do. Avoid `innerHTML = ''` because of the risk of leaks and its performance.",
    codeExample: "const parent = document.getElementById('container');\nwhile (parent.firstChild) {\n  parent.removeChild(parent.firstChild);\n}\n\nconst parent = document.getElementById('container');\nparent.replaceChildren();",
  },
  238: {
    source: "Как загрузка шрифтов влияет на рендеринг страницы?",
    question: "How does the loading of fonts affect a page's rendering?",
    shortAnswer: "The loading of fonts can block the rendering of text, causing delays. Browsers use different strategies: FOIT (Flash of Invisible Text), where the text is invisible until the font has loaded, or FOUT (Flash of Unstyled Text), where the text is displayed in a fallback font. This affects the perceived loading speed and can degrade the user experience.",
    longAnswer: "The effect of font loading on rendering\nWhen a web page uses custom fonts, the browser has to load them before it can display the text. This process can delay rendering and affect performance. There are two main behaviours: FOIT (Flash of Invisible Text) and FOUT (Flash of Unstyled Text).\nFOIT and FOUT\n\n- FOIT: the browser hides the text until the font has loaded. This can leave empty space on the page.\n- FOUT: the browser displays the text in a fallback font first and replaces it with the custom one once it has loaded. This causes a visible change of style.\nHow to optimize the loading of fonts\nUse `font-display` in your CSS to control the behaviour. For example:\n\nThe value `swap` enables FOUT, which improves the perceived speed. You can also preload fonts with `<link rel=\"preload\">`.\nConclusion\nConfiguring font loading properly is critical for performance and UX. Use `font-display: swap` and preloading in order to minimize delays and avoid invisible text.",
    codeExample: "@font-face {\n  font-family: 'MyFont';\n  src: url('myfont.woff2') format('woff2');\n  font-display: swap; /* Uses FOUT */\n}",
  },
  239: {
    source: "Чем отличаются свойства onload и onerror?",
    question: "How do the onload and onerror properties differ?",
    shortAnswer: "The onload and onerror properties are event handlers called when a resource (an image or a script, for example) loads or fails to load. onload fires when the resource has loaded successfully, while onerror fires when an error has occurred (the file was not found, or the network is unavailable, for example). They let you run code once loading has finished or when a problem arises.",
    longAnswer: "The difference between onload and onerror\nThe `onload` and `onerror` properties are event handlers assigned to HTML elements that load external resources, such as `<img>`, `<script>`, `<link>` and others. They let you respond to the successful loading of a resource or to an error during it.\nWhen onload fires\nThe `load` event (the `onload` handler) happens when a resource has loaded fully and is ready for use. For an image, for example, this means it has been downloaded completely and can be displayed; for a script, that it has been executed.\nWhen onerror fires\nThe `error` event (the `onerror` handler) arises if an error occurred while loading the resource. The causes may vary: an incorrect URL, a missing file on the server, network problems, CORS blocking and so on. It is important to note that `onerror` does not fire on syntax errors in a script — only on loading errors.\nAn example of use\n\nConclusion\nUse `onload` to perform actions once a resource has loaded successfully, and `onerror` to handle errors and provide fault tolerance (substituting a fallback image, for example). These handlers are especially useful when loading resources dynamically and in situations where correct display of the content has to be guaranteed.",
    codeExample: "const img = new Image();\nimg.onload = function() {\n  console.log('The image loaded successfully');\n  document.body.appendChild(img);\n};\nimg.onerror = function() {\n  console.error('Error loading the image');\n  // Show a fallback image\n  img.src = 'fallback.png';\n};\nimg.src = 'photo.jpg';",
  },
  240: {
    source: "Почему изображения не блокируют построение страницы?",
    question: "Why do images not block the building of a page?",
    shortAnswer: "Images do not block the building of the DOM and the CSSOM, since they are loaded asynchronously. The browser carries on parsing the HTML and building the DOM without waiting for the images to load. This lets a page display its content faster, while the images load later without delaying interactivity.",
    longAnswer: "How the browser handles images\nWhen the browser meets an `<img>` tag in the HTML, it does not stop parsing the document. Instead it sends a request to load the image asynchronously and carries on building the DOM. This is the key difference from blocking resources such as synchronous scripts or CSS files, which can delay rendering.\nThe role of the preload scanner\nModern browsers use a preload scanner that scans the HTML even before the main parser has built the DOM. It finds links to images, scripts and styles and starts loading them in advance. This speeds up the page's overall loading, since the requests are sent in parallel with the parsing.\nThe effect on the page's events\nImages do not block the `DOMContentLoaded` event, which fires once the DOM has been built fully. They can delay the `load` event, however, which waits for every resource to load, images included. This matters for analytics and interactivity.\nA code example\n\nIn this example the heading will appear at once while the image loads asynchronously. The `DOMContentLoaded` event will fire before the image has finished loading.\nConclusion\nUnderstanding the asynchronous loading of images helps you optimize a page's performance, especially for content sites. Use the `loading=\"lazy\"` attribute to defer the loading of off-screen images and speed the initial render up.",
    codeExample: "<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html>\n<head>\n  <title>An example</title>\n</head>\n<body>\n  <h1>The content is displayed at once</h1>\n  <img src=\"large-image.jpg\" alt=\"A large image\">\n  <script>\n    document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => {\n      console.log('The DOM is ready; the image may still be loading');\n    });\n  </script>\n</body>\n</html>",
  },
  241: {
    source: "Чем граф отличается от графика данных?",
    question: "How does a graph differ from a data chart?",
    shortAnswer: "A graph is a mathematical structure consisting of nodes and edges, used to model relationships. A data chart is a visual representation of numerical data — a line chart or a bar chart, for example. A graph describes relationships, while a chart shows trends and distributions.",
    longAnswer: "The main difference\nA graph in computer science and mathematics is an abstract data structure consisting of vertices (nodes) and edges (the links between them). It is used to model complex relationships such as social networks, routes or dependencies. A data chart (or plot) is a visual element that displays numerical data on coordinate axes — for analysing trends or comparing values, for example.\nExamples and uses\nGraphs are applied in pathfinding algorithms (Dijkstra, for example), in databases (graph databases) and in network models. Data charts are used in analytics, in BI systems and in reports to present statistics clearly.\nA code example\n\nConclusion\nA graph is a structure for modelling relationships, while a chart is a tool for visualizing data. The choice depends on the task: graphs suit the analysis of relationships, charts the presentation of numerical series.",
    codeExample: "// An example of a graph (JavaScript)\nconst graph = {\n  A: ['B', 'C'],\n  B: ['A', 'D'],\n  C: ['A'],\n  D: ['B']\n};\n\n// An example of a data chart (Python with matplotlib)\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nx = [1, 2, 3, 4]\ny = [10, 20, 15, 25]\nplt.plot(x, y)\nplt.show()",
  },
  242: {
    source: "Для чего используется библиотека Cytoscape?",
    question: "What is the Cytoscape library used for?",
    shortAnswer: "Cytoscape is a JavaScript library for visualizing and analysing graphs. It lets you display complex network structures such as social connections or routes. The library supports interactive capabilities: zooming, dragging nodes and updating the data dynamically. Cytoscape.js is widely used in bioinformatics, data analysis and web development.",
    longAnswer: "What is Cytoscape?\nCytoscape.js is a powerful open-source JavaScript library for visualizing and analysing graphs (networks). It lets developers create interactive web applications that work with data represented as nodes and links. The library provides flexible capabilities for styling, layout and interacting with graphs.\nThe main capabilities\n\n- Support for various layout algorithms (circular, hierarchical, force-directed, for example).\n- Interactivity: selecting nodes, zooming, dragging.\n- Updating a graph dynamically: adding and removing nodes and edges in real time.\n- Extensibility through plugins and custom styles.\nAn example of use\n\nWhere it is applied\nCytoscape.js is often used in bioinformatics to visualize molecular interactions, in the analysis of social networks, to display routes in logistics, and in any web application that needs a clear presentation of the relationships between objects.\nConclusion: Cytoscape.js is a versatile tool for working with graphs in the browser. It is worth applying when you need to visualize complex network structures with the ability to interact with them and analyse them.",
    codeExample: "// Creating a graph with two nodes and one edge\nvar cy = cytoscape({\n  container: document.getElementById('cy'),\n  elements: [\n    { data: { id: 'a' } },\n    { data: { id: 'b' } },\n    { data: { id: 'ab', source: 'a', target: 'b' } }\n  ],\n  style: [\n    { selector: 'node', style: { 'background-color': '#666', 'label': 'data(id)' } },\n    { selector: 'edge', style: { 'width': 3, 'line-color': '#ccc' } }\n  ],\n  layout: { name: 'grid' }\n});",
  },
  243: {
    source: "Какие существуют способы хранения токенов на клиенте?",
    question: "What ways are there of storing tokens on the client?",
    shortAnswer: "Tokens can be stored in localStorage, sessionStorage or cookies. localStorage and sessionStorage are vulnerable to XSS attacks, since JavaScript can reach them. Cookies with the HttpOnly and Secure flags are safer, since JS cannot reach them and they are transmitted only over HTTPS. For better security it is recommended to use httpOnly cookies and to keep tokens in the application's memory.",
    longAnswer: "Ways of storing tokens on the client\nStoring authentication tokens (a JWT, for example) on the client is a critically important task for the security of web applications. The main ways include using localStorage, sessionStorage and cookies. Each has its own advantages and drawbacks, especially in the context of protection against XSS and CSRF attacks.\nlocalStorage and sessionStorage\nlocalStorage and sessionStorage are browser stores reachable through JavaScript. They are simple to use but vulnerable to XSS attacks: if an attacker injects a script, they will be able to read the token. sessionStorage is cleared when the tab closes, which reduces the risk but does not eliminate it entirely.\n\nCookies\nCookies are a safer option if the HttpOnly (unreachable by JavaScript) and Secure (HTTPS only) flags are set. This protects against XSS but requires additional measures against CSRF attacks, such as using the SameSite attribute or CSRF tokens.\n\nStoring in memory\nA token can be kept in a JavaScript variable (in the application's memory). This is the safest way, since the token is not stored in the browser, but it is lost when the page reloads and the user has to authenticate again.\nConclusion\nFor maximum security it is recommended to use httpOnly cookies with the Secure and SameSite flags. If access to the token from JavaScript is required (to send it in headers, for example), consider keeping it in memory with a short lifetime and using refresh tokens in cookies.",
    codeExample: "// Saving a token in localStorage\nlocalStorage.setItem('token', 'my-jwt-token');\n\n// Reading the token\nconst token = localStorage.getItem('token');\n\n// Deleting the token\nlocalStorage.removeItem('token');\n\n// Setting a cookie with flags (on the server)\nSet-Cookie: token=my-jwt-token; HttpOnly; Secure; SameSite=Strict",
  },
  244: {
    source: "Для чего используется библиотека D3?",
    question: "What is the D3 library used for?",
    shortAnswer: "D3.js (Data-Driven Documents) is a JavaScript library for creating dynamic, interactive data visualizations in the browser. It lets you bind data to DOM elements and apply transformations to them. D3 provides powerful tools for working with scales, axes and animations. It is not a library of ready-made charts but a tool for building custom visualizations.",
    longAnswer: "What is D3.js?\nD3.js (Data-Driven Documents) is a JavaScript library for creating dynamic, interactive data visualizations in a web browser. Unlike ready-made solutions such as Chart.js, D3 provides low-level control over the DOM, letting a developer build any kind of chart, diagram or map using HTML, SVG and CSS.\nThe main concepts\n\n- Data binding: D3 lets you bind data to DOM elements. An array of numbers can be bound to a set of SVG circles, for example.\n- Scales: functions for converting data into visual attributes (coordinates, colours, for example).\n- Transitions: animations for updating a visualization smoothly when the data changes.\n- SVG: D3 makes heavy use of SVG for drawing shapes, lines and text.\nA code example\n\nWhere it is applied\nD3 is used in analytics, dashboards, scientific publications and any project that needs custom data visualization. It is ideal for creating unique charts that standard libraries do not support.\nConclusion\nD3.js is a powerful tool for developers who need complete flexibility in visualizing data. It is worth applying when you need to create non-standard, interactive, animated charts with deep control over every element.",
    codeExample: "// Creating a simple bar chart with D3\nconst data = [10, 20, 30, 40, 50];\nconst svg = d3.select('body').append('svg')\n    .attr('width', 400)\n    .attr('height', 200);\n\nsvg.selectAll('rect')\n    .data(data)\n    .enter()\n    .append('rect')\n    .attr('x', (d, i) => i * 40)\n    .attr('y', d => 200 - d * 3)\n    .attr('width', 30)\n    .attr('height', d => d * 3)\n    .attr('fill', 'steelblue');",
  },
  245: {
    source: "В чём разница между success и non-success статус-кодами в HTTP? Какие коды относятся к каждой группе?",
    question: "What is the difference between success and non-success status codes in HTTP? Which codes belong to each group?",
    shortAnswer: "HTTP status codes fall into successful (2xx) and unsuccessful (4xx, 5xx). Successful codes, 200 OK for example, mean the request was processed correctly. Unsuccessful codes signal an error: 4xx is a client error (404 Not Found, for example), 5xx a server error (500 Internal Server Error, for example).",
    longAnswer: "The classification of HTTP status codes\nHTTP status codes are three-digit numbers that the server sends in response to a client's request. They fall into five classes, each of which begins with a particular digit. The main difference between successful and unsuccessful codes is that the successful ones (2xx) indicate the request was processed correctly, while the unsuccessful ones (4xx and 5xx) signal an error.\nSuccessful codes (2xx)\nThese codes mean the request was accepted, understood and processed successfully. The most widespread are:\n\n- 200 OK — the standard response for successful GET requests.\n- 201 Created — a resource was created successfully (after a POST request, for example).\n- 204 No Content — the request was fulfilled but the response body is empty (on a DELETE, for example).\nUnsuccessful codes (4xx and 5xx)\nThe 4xx codes indicate an error on the client's side, while 5xx indicate an error on the server's. Examples:\n\n- 400 Bad Request — incorrect request syntax.\n- 401 Unauthorized — authentication is required.\n- 403 Forbidden — access is denied.\n- 404 Not Found — the resource was not found.\n- 500 Internal Server Error — a general server error.\n- 502 Bad Gateway — an invalid response from an upstream server.\n- 503 Service Unavailable — the server is temporarily unavailable.\nAn example of code handling status codes\n\nIn this example `response.ok` returns true for every successful code (2xx). To handle errors we check the specific statuses.\nConclusion\nUnderstanding the difference between successful and unsuccessful status codes is necessary for handling the server's responses properly, for debugging and for building reliable web applications. Use successful codes to confirm operations and unsuccessful ones to inform the client of errors.",
    codeExample: "fetch('/api/data')\n  .then(response => {\n    if (response.ok) { // status 200-299\n      return response.json();\n    } else if (response.status === 404) {\n      throw new Error('The resource was not found');\n    } else if (response.status === 500) {\n      throw new Error('A server error');\n    } else {\n      throw new Error('An unknown error');\n    }\n  })\n  .catch(error => console.error(error));",
  },
  246: {
    source: "Почему отправка HTML-формы по умолчанию вызывает перезагрузку страницы?",
    question: "Why does submitting an HTML form cause the page to reload by default?",
    shortAnswer: "When a form is submitted, the browser performs navigation by default to the URL given in the action attribute, sending an HTTP request (GET or POST). This causes the page to reload, since the browser loads a new document from the server. This behaviour is written into the HTML specification for the synchronous submission of data.",
    longAnswer: "Why does submitting an HTML form cause the page to reload?\nBy default, when the submit button is pressed, an HTML form initiates the browser's standard behaviour: it gathers the data from the form's fields, forms an HTTP request (GET or POST) and sends it to the server at the URL given in the `action` attribute. Once the response arrives, the browser loads a new document, which is what causes the page to reload. This is a historically established mechanism intended for the synchronous exchange of data between client and server.\nHow does it work?\nWhen the user presses the submit button, the browser performs the following steps:\n\n- It gathers the values of every form field (input, select, textarea).\n- It forms a query string (for GET) or a request body (for POST).\n- It sends the HTTP request to the server.\n- It loads the server's response as a new page, replacing the current document.\nAn example of a simple form:\n\nOn submission the browser will navigate to the URL `/search?q=value` and load a new page.\nHow do you prevent the reload?\nTo create modern single-page applications (SPAs) or to submit data asynchronously, JavaScript is used. The `preventDefault()` method cancels the form's standard behaviour, letting you handle the data through AJAX/the Fetch API without a reload.\nAn example with JavaScript:\n\nConclusion\nUnderstanding this mechanism matters for developing both traditional multi-page sites and modern SPAs. If you need to avoid a reload, use JavaScript and `preventDefault()`.",
    codeExample: "<form action=\"/search\" method=\"GET\">\n  <input type=\"text\" name=\"q\" />\n  <button type=\"submit\">Search</button>\n</form>\n\ndocument.querySelector('form').addEventListener('submit', function(event) {\n  event.preventDefault();\n  const formData = new FormData(this);\n  fetch('/api/submit', { method: 'POST', body: formData });\n});",
  },
  247: {
    source: "Что такое CustomEvent? Когда его используют?",
    question: "What is a CustomEvent? When is it used?",
    shortAnswer: "CustomEvent is a constructor built into JavaScript that lets you create events with an arbitrary name and pass additional data with them. It is used to organize the exchange of messages between independent parts of an application — between components or modules, for example. The event is created with new CustomEvent('name', { detail: data }) and then dispatched with element.dispatchEvent(event).",
    longAnswer: "What is a CustomEvent?\nCustomEvent is a standard browser interface that extends the basic Event and lets a developer create events with an arbitrary name and pass arbitrary data with them through the detail property. It is a powerful tool for implementing the Observer pattern in web applications, when one component wants to notify others of some action without creating tight coupling between them.\nHow does it work?\nTo create a custom event the constructor new CustomEvent(type, options) is used, where type is a string with the event's name and options is an object that may contain the fields bubbles, cancelable and detail. The detail field is whatever data you want to pass along with the event. The event is then dispatched to the target element with the dispatchEvent() method. Any listener subscribed to that event name on the element or its ancestors (if bubbles: true) will be notified.\nA code example\n\nWhen is this applied?\nCustomEvent is especially useful in architectures where components have to communicate without depending on one another directly. In micro-frontends, for example, when different parts of an application run in isolated containers, or in simple SPAs without a global state manager. It is also the standard way for web components to report their internal changes to the outside world.\nConclusion: CustomEvent is a lightweight, native way of implementing loosely coupled communication between the parts of an application. It is worth applying when you need to pass an event between independent modules or components while avoiding direct calls and dependencies, especially in the context of web components or micro-frontends.",
    codeExample: "// We create a custom event with data\nconst event = new CustomEvent('userLogin', {\n  detail: { username: 'john_doe', timestamp: Date.now() },\n  bubbles: true,\n  cancelable: true\n});\n\n// We dispatch the event to the document\ndocument.dispatchEvent(event);\n\n// Somewhere else we subscribe to it\ndocument.addEventListener('userLogin', (e) => {\n  console.log('The user logged in:', e.detail.username);\n});",
  },
  248: {
    source: "Из чего состоит HTTP-запрос?",
    question: "What does an HTTP request consist of?",
    shortAnswer: "An HTTP request consists of three main parts: the start line, the headers and the body. The start line contains the method, the URI and the protocol version. The headers carry metadata such as Content-Type or User-Agent. The body is optional and is used to pass data — in POST requests, for example.",
    longAnswer: "The structure of an HTTP request\nAn HTTP request is a text message the client sends to the server in order to receive or send data. It consists of three key components: the start line, the headers and the body. Understanding this structure helps developers debug network interaction and configure APIs properly.\nThe start line\nThe request's first line contains the method (GET, POST, for example), the URI (the path to the resource) and the HTTP version (HTTP/1.1, for example). For example:\n\nThe headers\nThe headers carry additional information about the request, such as the content type, authorization or caching. Every header has the form \"Key: Value\". For example:\n\nThe request body\nThe body is not always present and is used to pass data — in POST or PUT requests, for example. It may be in JSON, XML or form-data format. An example body:\n\nA practical example\nConsider a complete HTTP request for creating a user:\n\nHere the start line is POST /api/users HTTP/1.1, the headers are Host, Content-Type and Content-Length, and the body is a JSON object.\nConclusion\nKnowing the structure of an HTTP request is necessary for working with web servers, debugging network requests and building client-server applications. It is the foundation for understanding REST APIs and data-transfer protocols.",
    codeExample: "GET /api/users HTTP/1.1\n\nHost: example.com\nContent-Type: application/json\nAuthorization: Bearer token123\n\n{\"name\": \"John\", \"age\": 30}\n\nPOST /api/users HTTP/1.1\nHost: example.com\nContent-Type: application/json\nContent-Length: 27\n\n{\"name\": \"Alice\", \"age\": 25}",
  },
  249: {
    source: "Какие HTTP-заголовки могут передаваться в запросе?",
    question: "Which HTTP headers can be sent in a request?",
    shortAnswer: "A request's HTTP headers carry additional information about the request, the client or the message body. The main headers are: Host (states the domain), User-Agent (identifies the client), Accept (the response formats), Content-Type (the type of the request body), Authorization (authentication), Cookie (cookies) and Referer (the source of the request). They help the server process the request correctly.",
    longAnswer: "What are a request's HTTP headers?\nA request's HTTP headers are fields that the client (a browser, an application) sends to the server along with the request itself. They contain metadata: information about the client, preferences as to the data format, authentication, caching and so on. The headers are sent as key-value pairs, each on its own line, after the request line.\nThe main request headers\n\n- Host — a mandatory header stating the server's domain and port (`Host: example.com:8080`, for example).\n- User-Agent — a string identifying the client application (the browser, its version, the OS).\n- Accept — states which MIME types of response the client is prepared to accept (`Accept: application/json`, for example).\n- Content-Type — the type of the content in the request body (`application/x-www-form-urlencoded` or `multipart/form-data`, for example).\n- Authorization — passes the credentials for authentication (`Bearer <token>`, for example).\n- Cookie — sends previously stored cookies to the server.\n- Referer — the address of the page from which the request was made (used for analytics and protection against CSRF).\nAn example of use in code\n\nIn this example the client states explicitly that it is sending JSON, expects JSON in the response and is passing a token for authentication.\nConclusion\nA request's HTTP headers are necessary for proper communication between client and server: they determine the data format, authentication, caching and other aspects. Knowing the main headers helps when debugging network requests and developing APIs.",
    codeExample: "fetch('https://api.example.com/data', {\n  method: 'POST',\n  headers: {\n    'Content-Type': 'application/json',\n    'Authorization': 'Bearer my-token',\n    'Accept': 'application/json'\n  },\n  body: JSON.stringify({ key: 'value' })\n});",
  },
  250: {
    source: "Как браузер определяет тип полученного контента — HTML это или JSON?",
    question: "How does the browser determine the type of the content it has received — whether it is HTML or JSON?",
    shortAnswer: "The browser determines the content's type from the Content-Type header in the server's HTTP response. That header contains a MIME type — text/html for HTML or application/json for JSON, for example. If the header is absent or incorrect, the browser may try to guess the type (MIME sniffing), but that is unsafe and is often switched off with the header X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff.",
    longAnswer: "How the browser determines the type of content\nWhen the browser sends a request to the server, the server states the `Content-Type` header in its response. That header contains a MIME type telling the browser how to interpret the data it has received. For an HTML page, for example, `text/html` is used, and for JSON `application/json`.\nThe role of MIME types\nMIME types (Multipurpose Internet Mail Extensions) are standardized strings describing a data format. The main types are:\n\n- `text/html` — an HTML document\n- `application/json` — JSON data\n- `text/plain` — plain text\n- `application/octet-stream` — binary data\nAn example of how it works\nConsider a simple example in Node.js using Express:\n\nIn the first case the browser will receive JSON and will most likely offer to download the file or display it as text (depending on its settings). In the second it will render the HTML.\nMIME sniffing and security\nIf the `Content-Type` header is absent or stated incorrectly, the browser may try to guess the content's type by analysing the response's first bytes. This is called MIME sniffing. It can lead to vulnerabilities, however — if an attacker uploads JavaScript disguised as an image, for example. The header `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff` is used as protection, forbidding the browser from guessing the type.\nConclusion\nSetting the `Content-Type` header properly is critical for the browser to handle the data correctly. Always state the exact MIME type and use `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff` for better security.",
    codeExample: "const express = require('express');\nconst app = express();\n\napp.get('/data', (req, res) => {\n  res.setHeader('Content-Type', 'application/json');\n  res.json({ message: 'Hello' });\n});\n\napp.get('/page', (req, res) => {\n  res.setHeader('Content-Type', 'text/html');\n  res.send('<h1>Hello</h1>');\n});\n\napp.listen(3000);",
  },
  251: {
    source: "Как браузер строит DOM-дерево при парсинге HTML?",
    question: "How does the browser build the DOM tree while parsing HTML?",
    shortAnswer: "The browser receives the HTML document and starts parsing it. It breaks the raw HTML into tokens (tags, attributes, text) and then builds nodes from them. The nodes are linked into a tree structure — the DOM (Document Object Model). This process runs in sequence but can be paused when scripts or CSS are met.",
    longAnswer: "How the browser builds the DOM tree\nWhen the browser loads an HTML document, it begins the parsing process, which turns the raw text into a structured DOM tree. The DOM (Document Object Model) is an object representation of an HTML page that JavaScript can interact with.\nThe stages of building the DOM\n\n- Tokenization: the browser breaks the HTML string into tokens — opening tags, closing tags, attributes and text nodes. `<div class=\"container\">`, for example, becomes an opening-tag token with an attribute.\n- Node construction: node objects are created from the tokens. Every node has a type (Element, Text, Comment and so on) and properties.\n- Tree construction: the nodes are linked into a hierarchical structure. An opening tag becomes the parent of the nested elements. A closing tag completes the current branch.\nA code example\n\nAfter parsing this HTML the browser will create a DOM tree: the root node `document`, inside it `html`, then `head` with `title`, and `body` with `div` and `p`. Every element becomes an object with methods and properties.\nThe particulars of the process\n\n- Blocking resources: on meeting a `<script>` tag without the `async` or `defer` attributes, the browser pauses the parsing of the HTML, loads and executes the script, and then carries on. CSS can also block rendering, but not the parsing of the DOM.\n- Incremental processing: the browser does not wait for the whole HTML to load — it starts building the DOM as the data arrives, which speeds up the display of the page.\n- Errors in the HTML: browsers are tolerant of errors (missing closing tags, for example) and try to recover a correct tree.\nConclusion\nUnderstanding the process of building the DOM helps you optimize a page's loading: put scripts at the bottom, use the async/defer attributes, and minimize the number of blocking resources. This is the foundation for creating fast, responsive web applications.",
    codeExample: "<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html>\n<head>\n  <title>An example</title>\n</head>\n<body>\n  <div id=\"app\">\n    <p>Hello, world!</p>\n  </div>\n</body>\n</html>",
  },
  252: {
    source: "Чем плох вариант с прослушиванием события window scroll для определения достижения конца страницы?",
    question: "What is wrong with listening for the window scroll event in order to detect reaching the end of a page?",
    shortAnswer: "Listening for the scroll event fires very often, causing recalculations and potential lag. This can lead to performance problems, especially on mobile devices. The Intersection Observer API is recommended instead, since it is more efficient and does not cause unnecessary repaints.",
    longAnswer: "The problems with the scroll event\nThe scroll event fires on every pixel of scrolling, which can mean up to 60-120 calls a second. If the handler performs heavy operations (computing an element's position or querying the DOM, for example), this can lead to noticeable stuttering in the interface.\nThe alternative: Intersection Observer\nThe Intersection Observer API lets you track an element's visibility relative to an ancestor or the viewport asynchronously. It does not block the main thread and fires only when the visibility state changes.\n\nWhen to use it\nIntersection Observer is ideal for infinite scrolling, the lazy loading of images and animations on scroll. It reduces the load on the main thread and simplifies the code.\nConclusion: to detect reaching the end of a page, use Intersection Observer rather than the scroll event — it improves performance and makes the code easier to maintain.",
    codeExample: "// The bad approach: the scroll event\nwindow.addEventListener('scroll', () => {\n  const scrollTop = window.scrollY;\n  const docHeight = document.documentElement.scrollHeight;\n  const winHeight = window.innerHeight;\n  if (scrollTop + winHeight >= docHeight - 100) {\n    loadMore();\n  }\n});\n\n// The good approach: Intersection Observer\nconst sentinel = document.getElementById('sentinel');\nconst observer = new IntersectionObserver((entries) => {\n  if (entries[0].isIntersecting) {\n    loadMore();\n  }\n});\nobserver.observe(sentinel);",
  },
  253: {
    source: "Как использовать IntersectionObserver для реализации бесконечного скролла?",
    question: "How do you use IntersectionObserver to implement infinite scroll?",
    shortAnswer: "IntersectionObserver lets you track asynchronously when an element intersects the viewport. For infinite scroll, create an observer that loads new data when it intersects a trigger element and updates the trigger's position. This is more efficient than listening for the scroll event, since it works at the browser level without constant computation.",
    longAnswer: "What IntersectionObserver is and why it is needed\nIntersectionObserver is a browser API that lets you observe asynchronously when a target element intersects its parent or the viewport. In the context of infinite scroll it replaces expensive scroll-event handlers, which are called hundreds of times a second and can slow the interface down.\nHow infinite scroll works with IntersectionObserver\nThe core idea is to place an invisible trigger element (a sentinel) at the end of the list. When the user scrolls to it, the observer's callback fires and you load the next batch of data. Once it has loaded, the trigger ends up at the bottom again and the process repeats.\nAn example implementation\n\nImportant details\n\n- Configure the firing threshold through the `threshold` option (0.1 for early loading, for example).\n- Use `rootMargin` in order to start loading before the trigger appears on the screen.\n- Do not forget to disconnect the observer once the data has run out, in order to avoid unnecessary requests.\nConclusion\nIntersectionObserver is the modern, performant way of implementing infinite scroll. It suits news feeds, product lists and any dynamic list where smooth scrolling and economy of resources matter.",
    codeExample: "// HTML: <div id=\"sentinel\"></div>\n\nconst sentinel = document.getElementById('sentinel');\nlet page = 1;\n\nconst observer = new IntersectionObserver(async (entries) => {\n  if (entries[0].isIntersecting) {\n    const data = await fetch(`/api/items?page=${page}`);\n    const items = await data.json();\n    // We add the items to the list\n    items.forEach(item => {\n      const div = document.createElement('div');\n      div.textContent = item.name;\n      document.body.insertBefore(div, sentinel);\n    });\n    page++;\n  }\n});\n\nobserver.observe(sentinel);",
  },
  254: {
    source: "Как очищать старые DOM-узлы при бесконечном скролле, чтобы не накапливать элементы в памяти?",
    question: "How do you clear away old DOM nodes during infinite scroll so as not to accumulate elements in memory?",
    shortAnswer: "During infinite scroll the old DOM nodes have to be removed in order to avoid accumulating elements in memory. Use virtual scrolling, which renders only the visible elements and reuses the nodes. Alternatively you can remove elements outside the visible area by hand — deleting the old ones every time new ones are added, while keeping a buffer, for example.",
    longAnswer: "The problem of accumulating DOM nodes\nDuring infinite scroll every newly loaded item is added to the DOM. If the old nodes are not removed, the number of elements grows without limit, which increases memory consumption and slows the page down. The browser keeps every DOM node even when they are not visible, which can cause freezes and crashes.\nThe solution: virtual scrolling\nVirtual scrolling is a technique in which only a small number of elements visible in the viewport are rendered, plus a small buffer above and below. As the user scrolls, the old elements are removed and new ones added, reusing the DOM nodes. This lets you work with thousands of records without a loss of performance.\nAn example implementation in JavaScript:\n\nIn this example, as the user scrolls, it is computed which elements should be visible and only a small number of them are rendered. The old nodes are removed through `innerHTML = ''`.\nAn alternative approach: manual removal\nIf virtual scrolling is not suitable, you can remove by hand the elements that are far outside the visible area. When adding new elements, for example, delete the first N old ones, keeping a fixed limit (100 elements, for example).\n\nThis approach is simpler but less efficient for very large lists, since the DOM still contains up to 100 nodes.\nConclusion\nFor infinite scroll with a large amount of data, use virtual scrolling — it is the standard way of avoiding memory leaks and ensuring smooth operation. For small lists (up to a few hundred items) removing the old nodes by hand will do.",
    codeExample: "const container = document.getElementById('scroll-container');\nconst itemHeight = 50;\nconst buffer = 5;\nlet items = []; // the array of data\n\nfunction render(startIndex) {\n  container.innerHTML = '';\n  const endIndex = Math.min(startIndex + buffer * 2, items.length);\n  for (let i = startIndex; i < endIndex; i++) {\n    const div = document.createElement('div');\n    div.textContent = items[i];\n    div.style.height = itemHeight + 'px';\n    container.appendChild(div);\n  }\n}\n\ncontainer.addEventListener('scroll', () => {\n  const scrollTop = container.scrollTop;\n  const startIndex = Math.floor(scrollTop / itemHeight) - buffer;\n  render(Math.max(0, startIndex));\n});\n\nconst maxItems = 100;\nconst list = document.getElementById('list');\n\nfunction addItem(text) {\n  const li = document.createElement('li');\n  li.textContent = text;\n  list.appendChild(li);\n  if (list.children.length > maxItems) {\n    list.removeChild(list.firstChild);\n  }\n}",
  },
  255: {
    source: "Какой атрибут HTML-тега обязателен для корректной работы скринридеров применительно к изображениям?",
    question: "Which attribute of an HTML tag is mandatory for screen readers to work properly with images?",
    shortAnswer: "For screen readers to work properly with images, the alt attribute is mandatory. It provides a textual description of the image, which is read out to users with visual impairments. If the image is decorative, alt should be empty (alt=\"\") so that the screen reader skips it.",
    longAnswer: "Why is the alt attribute needed?\nThe alt attribute (alternative text) on an `<img>` tag provides a textual alternative to the image. Screen readers read this text out so that users with visual impairments can understand the image's content. Without alt, a screen reader may read out the file's name or simply say \"image\", which is uninformative.\nThe rules of use\n\n- For informative images: describe the gist of the image briefly but meaningfully. For example: `alt=\"A chart of sales growth in 2023\"`.\n- For decorative images: use an empty alt (`alt=\"\"`) so that the screen reader skips the element entirely.\n- Do not use phrases such as \"image\" or \"picture\" — the screen reader will announce that it is an image itself.\nA code example\n\nConclusion\nThe alt attribute is mandatory for every image on the web. It makes content accessible to people using screen readers and improves SEO. Always add alt, even if it is empty for decorative elements.",
    codeExample: "<!-- An informative image -->\n<img src=\"chart.png\" alt=\"A chart of sales growth by month\">\n\n<!-- A decorative image -->\n<img src=\"divider.png\" alt=\"\">\n\n<!-- An error: alt is missing -->\n<img src=\"photo.jpg\"> <!-- The screen reader will read out the file's name -->",
  },
  256: {
    source: "Какие инструменты Chrome DevTools используются для профилирования производительности фронтенд-приложения?",
    question: "Which Chrome DevTools tools are used to profile the performance of a frontend application?",
    shortAnswer: "Chrome DevTools provides several tabs for profiling: Performance for recording and analysing a page's activity, Network for analysing the loading of resources, Memory for investigating memory leaks and taking heap snapshots, and Lighthouse for auditing performance and getting recommendations. These tools help you uncover bottlenecks such as long-running scripts, slow loading or excessive memory consumption.",
    longAnswer: "The main Chrome DevTools tools for profiling performance\nChrome DevTools is a built-in set of tools that lets developers analyse and optimize the performance of web applications. Several key tabs are used for profiling, each solving its own task.\nThe Performance tab\nIt lets you record a session of interaction with the application and then study the timeline of events in detail: the page's loading, the execution of JavaScript, rendering, layout and paint. You can see which functions take the most time and find the bottlenecks.\n\nThe Network tab\nIt shows every network request, its size, its loading time and its order. It is useful for uncovering slow requests, a large number of requests or blocking resources. You can filter by type (XHR, JS, CSS) and sort by time.\nThe Memory tab\nIt is used to hunt for memory leaks. It lets you take heap snapshots, compare them and find objects that the garbage collector is not freeing. There is also the Allocation instrumentation on timeline tool for tracking memory allocation in real time.\nThe Lighthouse tab\nAn automatic audit of performance, accessibility, SEO and best practices. It generates a report with scores and specific recommendations for improvement — optimizing images, reducing the JavaScript bundle or using caching, for example.\nConclusion\nThese tools should be applied during development and testing in order to uncover and eliminate performance problems such as slow loading, a stuttering interface or excessive memory consumption, all of which directly affect the user experience.",
    codeExample: "// An example: recording Performance in order to find long tasks\n// 1. Open DevTools (F12)\n// 2. Go to the Performance tab\n// 3. Press the record button (the round icon)\n// 4. Perform the actions on the page\n// 5. Stop the recording and analyse the flame chart",
  },
  257: {
    source: "На каком этапе Event Loop происходит перерисовка страницы?",
    question: "At which stage of the event loop does the page get repainted?",
    shortAnswer: "The page is repainted after every microtask and macrotask in the current cycle of the event loop has run. The browser schedules rendering before the next macrotask, but it does not guarantee it every cycle. requestAnimationFrame is used to synchronize with rendering.",
    longAnswer: "How the event loop relates to rendering\nIn the browser the event loop manages the execution of JavaScript, the handling of events and the rendering of the page. Repaints and reflows happen at a special stage that follows the execution of every microtask and macrotask in the current cycle. The browser may combine several changes into a single render in order to optimize performance.\nThe order of execution\n\n- First every macrotask runs (setTimeout, input events, for example).\n- Then every microtask is processed (Promise.then, MutationObserver).\n- After that the browser checks whether the rendering needs updating (normally at 60 FPS).\n- If it does, the rendering stage begins: styles, layout, paint, compositing.\nA code example\n\nIn this example requestAnimationFrame runs before the macrotask, since it is tied to the rendering stage.\nConclusion\nUnderstanding this mechanism helps you avoid delays in the UI and use requestAnimationFrame for animations properly, guaranteeing synchronization with the screen's refresh.",
    codeExample: "console.log('start');\n\nsetTimeout(() => console.log('macro'), 0);\n\nPromise.resolve().then(() => console.log('micro'));\n\nrequestAnimationFrame(() => console.log('render'));\n\nconsole.log('end');\n// The output: start, end, micro, render, macro",
  },
  258: {
    source: "Чем CSS блокирует рендеринг страницы?",
    question: "How does CSS block a page's rendering?",
    shortAnswer: "CSS blocks rendering because the browser does not start painting the page until it has loaded and processed every CSS file. This is necessary in order to avoid displaying the page without styles (a Flash of Unstyled Content). The browser builds the DOM and the CSSOM, and rendering begins only after they have been combined into the render tree. To speed loading up you can use media queries or inline critical styles.",
    longAnswer: "How CSS blocks rendering\nWhen the browser loads a web page, it goes through the critical rendering path. That path involves several stages: building the DOM (Document Object Model) from the HTML, building the CSSOM (CSS Object Model) from the CSS, combining them into the render tree, computing the elements' geometry (layout) and finally painting.\nCSS is considered a render-blocking resource because the browser does not start painting the page until it has loaded and processed every CSS file named in a `<link>` in the `<head>`. This is done to prevent the page appearing without styles (a Flash of Unstyled Content, FOUC). If the browser started rendering before the CSS had loaded, the user would first see unstyled content and then a sudden application of styles, which degrades their experience.\nAn example of the effect on performance\nImagine you have an external CSS file that takes 2 seconds to load. During those 2 seconds the page will stay blank, even if the HTML has already loaded fully. This increases the time to the first paint and worsens Core Web Vitals metrics such as First Contentful Paint (FCP).\n\nHow to reduce the blocking\n\n- Use media queries: if the CSS applies only under particular conditions (for printing, for example), add a `media` attribute. The browser will load such CSS but will not block rendering while the condition is not met.\n- Inline critical styles: extract the styles needed to display the top of the page (above the fold) and insert them directly into the `<head>` inside a `<style>` tag. Load the rest asynchronously.\n- Asynchronous loading: use JavaScript to load CSS dynamically after the initial render — with `rel=\"preload\"` and the `onload` attribute, for example.\n\nConclusion\nUnderstanding render-blocking CSS is critically important for optimizing the performance of web pages. By applying techniques such as inline critical styles and the asynchronous loading of non-critical CSS, you can improve loading speed and the user experience considerably, especially on mobile devices and slow connections.",
    codeExample: "<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html>\n<head>\n  <link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"styles.css\"> <!-- Blocks rendering -->\n</head>\n<body>\n  <h1>Hello, world!</h1>\n</body>\n</html>\n\n<link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"print.css\" media=\"print\"> <!-- Does not block rendering on screen -->\n<link rel=\"preload\" href=\"non-critical.css\" as=\"style\" onload=\"this.rel='stylesheet'\">",
  },
  259: {
    source: "В чём разница между тегами <b> и <strong>?",
    question: "What is the difference between the <b> and <strong> tags?",
    shortAnswer: "The `<b>` tag is used to pick text out visually in bold without changing its meaning. The `<strong>` tag denotes the importance of the content, adding semantic weight. For screen readers `<strong>` marks the text out with intonation, while `<b>` is ignored. The difference is in the semantics, not only in the styling.",
    longAnswer: "The semantic difference between `<b>` and `<strong>`\nIn HTML the `<b>` and `<strong>` tags look identical — the text becomes bold. Their semantic meaning differs, however. The `<b>` tag (bold) is intended for stylistic emphasis that carries no additional importance. The `<strong>` tag indicates that the content is highly important, which affects how screen readers and search engines perceive it.\nExamples of use\nUse `<b>` for keywords in a text where no emphasis on importance is needed. Use `<strong>` for warnings or critical messages.\n\nThe effect on accessibility\nScreen readers may change their intonation when reading `<strong>`, underlining its importance. The `<b>` tag causes no such change. This makes `<strong>` preferable for ensuring accessibility.\nConclusion\nUse `<b>` for visual emphasis without semantics, and `<strong>` to denote important content. This improves accessibility and the document's semantic structure.",
    codeExample: "<p>Press <b>Submit</b> to finish.</p>\n<p><strong>Attention!</strong> The data will be deleted.</p>",
  },
  260: {
    source: "Строится ли DOM-дерево независимо от CSS, или они зависят друг от друга?",
    question: "Is the DOM tree built independently of the CSS, or do they depend on one another?",
    shortAnswer: "The DOM and the CSSOM are built independently of one another, but they are combined in order to build the render tree. The browser parses the HTML and the CSS in parallel; JavaScript can block the building of the DOM, however, if it is waiting for the CSS to load. The render tree is built only once both trees are ready.",
    longAnswer: "The independent building of the DOM and the CSSOM\nThe browser processes the HTML and the CSS in parallel, using different threads. The HTML parser builds the DOM (Document Object Model) while the CSS parser builds the CSSOM (CSS Object Model). These processes do not depend on one another and can run at the same time.\nThe effect of CSS on the DOM through JavaScript\nAlthough CSS does not block the building of the DOM directly, it can affect it indirectly through JavaScript. If a script tries to reach the styles (through `getComputedStyle`, for example), the browser pauses the execution of the JavaScript until the CSS has loaded and been parsed fully. This can delay the building of the DOM, since scripts block the parsing of the HTML.\nThe render tree — combining the DOM and the CSSOM\nOnce the DOM and the CSSOM have been built, the browser creates the render tree, which contains only the visible elements and their computed styles. The render tree is used to compute the geometry (layout) and to paint.\nAn example\n\nIn this example the script blocks the building of the DOM until the CSS has loaded, even though the CSS itself does not block the DOM.\nConclusion\nThe DOM and the CSSOM are built independently, but combining them into the render tree is necessary in order to render the page. Understanding this process helps you optimize a page's loading by putting the CSS in the `<head>` and avoiding blocking scripts.",
    codeExample: "<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html>\n<head>\n  <link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"styles.css\">\n</head>\n<body>\n  <div>Hello</div>\n  <script>\n    // This script will run only once the CSS has loaded\n    console.log('CSS loaded');\n  </script>\n</body>\n</html>",
  },
  261: {
    source: "Что такое Content Security Policy (CSP) и как он связан с безопасностью?",
    question: "What is a Content Security Policy (CSP) and how does it relate to security?",
    shortAnswer: "Content Security Policy (CSP) is an HTTP header that lets you control which resources (scripts, styles, images) may be loaded on a web page. It prevents XSS attacks by blocking the execution of unauthorized JavaScript. CSP is set through directives — script-src 'self', for example, permits only scripts from the same domain. It is an important layer of security for web applications.",
    longAnswer: "What is a Content Security Policy (CSP)?\nContent Security Policy (CSP) is a security standard implemented through the `Content-Security-Policy` HTTP header. It lets a developer tell the browser which sources of content (scripts, styles, fonts and so on) are considered trusted. CSP protects effectively against cross-site scripting (XSS) and other injections by restricting the execution of unauthorized code.\nHow does it work?\nOn receiving the CSP header, the browser checks every resource it loads against the rules that have been set. If a resource does not match the policy, it is blocked. The directive `script-src 'self'`, for example, permits the execution only of scripts from the same domain as the page. This prevents the execution of inline scripts or scripts from external sites, which may be malicious.\nAn example of use\nConsider a simple example of configuring CSP in HTML through a meta tag:\n\nHere `default-src 'self'` sets the base rule — only resources from the current domain are permitted. `script-src` additionally permits scripts from `https://apis.example.com`, while `style-src` allows inline styles (`'unsafe-inline'`).\nWhere is it applied?\nCSP is used in web applications to strengthen security, especially in systems with user-generated content (forums, blogs, CRMs). It is also mandatory for conformity with security standards such as PCI DSS.\nConclusion\nCSP is a powerful tool for preventing XSS attacks and controlling the loading of resources. It is worth applying in any web application where security matters, especially when working with dynamic content or third-party libraries.",
    codeExample: "<meta http-equiv=\"Content-Security-Policy\" content=\"default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' https://apis.example.com; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline';\">",
  },
  262: {
    source: "Зачем использовать черновое сохранение формы и как его реализовать?",
    question: "Why use draft saving for a form, and how do you implement it?",
    shortAnswer: "Draft saving for a form lets the user avoid losing the data they have entered if the page is closed by accident or something fails. It is implemented by saving the form's state in localStorage or sessionStorage every time a field changes. When the page loads, the data is restored from storage and fills the form in. This improves the user experience and reduces the number of abandoned forms.",
    longAnswer: "Why draft saving for a form is needed\nDraft saving solves the problem of losing data when a form has not been filled in completely. The user may close a tab by accident, reload the page or run into a network error. Without a saved draft, everything they entered is lost, which causes irritation and lowers conversion. This is especially critical for long forms: registration, placing an order, creating complex objects.\nHow it works\nThe core idea is to save the form's state in the browser's local storage (localStorage) on every change. When the page loads, we check whether any saved data exists and restore it. To do this we subscribe to the fields' change events (input, change) and periodically write the form's current state as a JSON string.\nAn example implementation in JavaScript\n\nWhere it is applied\n\n- Registration and login forms\n- Content editors (blogs, CMSs)\n- Multi-step forms (wizards)\n- An online shop's basket\nConclusion: draft saving is a simple, effective way of improving UX, especially for long or critical forms. An implementation through localStorage requires no server-side support and is easy to integrate into any project.",
    codeExample: "const FORM_KEY = 'draft_form_data';\n\nfunction saveDraft(formData) {\n  localStorage.setItem(FORM_KEY, JSON.stringify(formData));\n}\n\nfunction loadDraft() {\n  const data = localStorage.getItem(FORM_KEY);\n  return data ? JSON.parse(data) : null;\n}\n\nfunction clearDraft() {\n  localStorage.removeItem(FORM_KEY);\n}\n\n// Saving when the fields change\ndocument.querySelectorAll('input, textarea, select').forEach(el => {\n  el.addEventListener('input', () => {\n    const formData = new FormData(document.getElementById('myForm'));\n    saveDraft(Object.fromEntries(formData));\n  });\n});\n\n// Restoring on load\nwindow.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => {\n  const draft = loadDraft();\n  if (draft) {\n    Object.entries(draft).forEach(([name, value]) => {\n      const field = document.querySelector(`[name=\"${name}\"]`);\n      if (field) field.value = value;\n    });\n  }\n});\n\n// Clearing it after a successful submission\ndocument.getElementById('myForm').addEventListener('submit', clearDraft);",
  },
  263: {
    source: "Какие причины могут вызывать обрезание выпадающего списка внутри таблицы?",
    question: "What can cause a dropdown list inside a table to be clipped?",
    shortAnswer: "A dropdown list can be clipped because of the CSS property overflow: hidden on the table or on its parent container. The problem also arises if the list's element has a low z-index or sits inside a container with a fixed height. The solution is to set overflow: visible on the table, or to use position: fixed for the dropdown list.",
    longAnswer: "The causes of a dropdown list being clipped\nA dropdown list inside a table is often clipped because of the CSS property `overflow: hidden` applied to the table, to its cell or to a parent container. That property hides any content extending beyond the element's boundaries. The problem can also be caused by a low `z-index` value on the dropdown list, or by its sitting inside a container with a fixed height.\nHow it works\nTables in HTML have a strict structure, and their cells (`td` or `th`) do not by default let content extend beyond their boundaries unless `overflow: visible` is set. When the dropdown list opens it may extend beyond the cell, but if `overflow: hidden` is set on the table or its parent, part of the list will be hidden.\nA code example\n\nThe solutions\n\n- Set `overflow: visible` on the table and its cells.\n- Use `position: fixed` for the dropdown list, so that it is positioned relative to the browser window.\n- Place the dropdown list outside the table with JavaScript and absolute positioning.\nConclusion: the problem of a dropdown list being clipped inside a table is solved by configuring the overflow and positioning CSS properties properly. This matters for creating convenient interfaces, especially in forms and tables of data.",
    codeExample: "<table style=\"overflow: hidden;\">\n  <tr>\n    <td>\n      <div class=\"dropdown\">\n        <button>Open</button>\n        <div class=\"dropdown-menu\">\n          <!-- the dropdown list -->\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </td>\n  </tr>\n</table>",
  },
  264: {
    source: "Что такое CSRF и как от него защититься на уровне фронтенда?",
    question: "What is CSRF and how do you protect against it at the frontend level?",
    shortAnswer: "CSRF (Cross-Site Request Forgery) is an attack in which an attacker makes the victim's browser perform an unwanted action on a trusted site, using its cookies. On the frontend the defence involves using CSRF tokens, which the server generates and checks on every request. Setting the SameSite attribute on cookies (Strict or Lax) and checking the Origin or Referer header also help. It is important not to rely on cookies alone for authentication and to use additional mechanisms such as custom headers.",
    longAnswer: "What is CSRF?\nCSRF (Cross-Site Request Forgery) is a kind of attack in which an attacker makes an authenticated user's browser send a request to the target site without their knowledge. For example, the user visits a malicious site containing a hidden form or image that sends a POST request to a bank's server. If the user is already authenticated on that server (the cookies are stored), the request will be performed with their privileges.\nHow do you protect against it on the frontend?\nThe main methods of protection on the client side include:\n\n- CSRF tokens: the server generates a unique token, which is embedded in the HTML form or passed through a header (X-CSRF-Token, for example). On every request the frontend sends that token, and the server checks it. The token has to be random and tied to the session.\n- SameSite cookies: setting the attribute SameSite=Strict or Lax on cookies prevents them being sent in cross-site requests. SameSite=Lax, for example, permits them to be sent only for navigational GET requests, which blocks most CSRF attacks.\n- Checking headers: the server can check the Origin or Referer headers in order to be sure the request came from a trusted source. On the frontend this requires no additional action, but the server has to be configured.\n- Custom headers: using custom headers (X-Requested-With: XMLHttpRequest, for example) for AJAX requests, since browsers do not let such headers be set from cross-domain scripts without CORS.\nAn example implementation of a CSRF token on the frontend\n\nIn this example the token is read out of a meta tag that the server inserted into the HTML. It is then added to the request's header. The server checks the token and rejects the request if it does not match.\nConclusion\nCSRF protection on the frontend is a combination of using CSRF tokens, configuring cookies properly (SameSite) and checking headers. These methods are effective against most attacks but require coordinated work with the server. They are worth applying in any web application that has cookie-based authentication and performs state-changing requests (POST, PUT, DELETE).",
    codeExample: "// We get the CSRF token out of a meta tag or a cookie\nconst csrfToken = document.querySelector('meta[name=\"csrf-token\"]').getAttribute('content');\n\n// We send a POST request with the token in a header\nfetch('/api/transfer', {\n  method: 'POST',\n  headers: {\n    'Content-Type': 'application/json',\n    'X-CSRF-Token': csrfToken\n  },\n  body: JSON.stringify({ amount: 100, to: 'attacker' })\n});",
  },
  265: {
    source: "Как querySelector со звёздочкой позволяет получить все элементы страницы?",
    question: "How does querySelector with an asterisk let you get all the elements on a page?",
    shortAnswer: "The method querySelector('*') returns the first element matching the selector '*' — that is, any element. To get every element you have to use querySelectorAll('*'), which returns a NodeList with every element on the page. This is useful for iterating over or analysing the whole DOM.",
    longAnswer: "What is querySelector with an asterisk?\nThe `querySelector` method takes a CSS selector and returns the first element matching it. The selector `*` (an asterisk) in CSS means \"any element\". So `document.querySelector('*')` will return the very first element in the DOM (normally `<html>`).\nHow do you get every element?\nTo get every element on a page you have to use `querySelectorAll('*')`. This method returns a static NodeList containing every DOM element in the order they appear. For example:\n\nPractical use\nThis can be useful for:\n\n- Counting the number of elements on a page.\n- Applying styles or attributes to every element.\n- Debugging or analysing the DOM's structure.\nBe careful, though: iterating over every element can be expensive in terms of performance on large pages.\nConclusion\nUse `querySelectorAll('*')` when you need a complete list of DOM elements for analysis or bulk operations, but bear the possible performance problems in mind.",
    codeExample: "const allElements = document.querySelectorAll('*');\nconsole.log(allElements.length); // the number of all the elements",
  },
  266: {
    source: "Что возвращает getElementsByTagName — один элемент или коллекцию?",
    question: "Does getElementsByTagName return a single element or a collection?",
    shortAnswer: "The getElementsByTagName method returns a live HTMLCollection, not a single element. It updates automatically when the DOM changes. To get a particular element you have to index into it or use the item() method.",
    longAnswer: "What does getElementsByTagName return?\nThe `getElementsByTagName` method returns a live collection of elements — an `HTMLCollection`. This is not a single element but a list of every element in the document with the given tag. The collection is live, which means it updates automatically when elements are added to or removed from the DOM.\nHow do you work with the result?\nTo get a particular element, use an index or the `item()` method. For example:\n\nIf you need to iterate over every element, use a `for` loop or convert it into an array with `Array.from()`.\nThe difference from the other methods\n\n- `getElementById` — returns a single element (or null).\n- `querySelector` — returns the first matching element.\n- `querySelectorAll` — returns a static NodeList collection (not a live one).\nAn example of use\n\nConclusion: `getElementsByTagName` returns a live HTMLCollection, which is convenient for dynamic pages where the DOM changes often. For static lists, however, `querySelectorAll` is better, in order to avoid unexpected changes.",
    codeExample: "const divs = document.getElementsByTagName('div');\nconst firstDiv = divs[0]; // the first div\nconst secondDiv = divs.item(1); // the second div\n\n// We get every paragraph\nconst paragraphs = document.getElementsByTagName('p');\nconsole.log(paragraphs.length); // the number of paragraphs\n\n// We add a new paragraph\nconst newP = document.createElement('p');\ndocument.body.appendChild(newP);\nconsole.log(paragraphs.length); // it will increase automatically",
  },
  267: {
    source: "Какой заголовок HTTP регулирует разрешённые домены в контексте CORS?",
    question: "Which HTTP header governs the permitted domains in the context of CORS?",
    shortAnswer: "The Access-Control-Allow-Origin header states which domains may reach a resource. If the server does not send this header, the browser blocks requests from other origins. The value '*' permits every domain but is not supported together with cookies.",
    longAnswer: "What CORS is and why Access-Control-Allow-Origin is needed\nCORS (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing) is a browser security mechanism that controls access to resources on other domains. When a web page tries to make a request to a server on another domain, the browser checks the headers of the server's response. The main header governing the permitted domains is `Access-Control-Allow-Origin`.\nHow it works\nIn its response the server sends the `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` header naming the domain that is permitted access. If the server sends `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: https://example.com`, for example, then only pages on that domain can read the response. If every domain has to be permitted, the value `*` is used, but that does not work with requests that carry cookies or authorization.\nAn example of the configuration on the server (Node.js with Express)\n\nPractical use\nThis header is used in any API called from the frontend on a different domain. If you have a React application on `app.example.com` and an API on `api.example.com`, for example, the API's server has to send `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: https://app.example.com`.\nConclusion: Access-Control-Allow-Origin is the key header for safe cross-domain interaction. Configuring it properly prevents data leaks and keeps modern web applications working.",
    codeExample: "const express = require('express');\nconst app = express();\n\napp.use((req, res, next) => {\n  res.setHeader('Access-Control-Allow-Origin', 'https://myfrontend.com');\n  res.setHeader('Access-Control-Allow-Methods', 'GET, POST');\n  res.setHeader('Access-Control-Allow-Headers', 'Content-Type');\n  next();\n});\n\napp.get('/data', (req, res) => {\n  res.json({ message: 'Access permitted' });\n});\n\napp.listen(3000);",
  },
  268: {
    source: "Когда события обычно отлавливаются — на всплытии или на погружении?",
    question: "At which phase are events normally caught — bubbling or capturing?",
    shortAnswer: "Events are normally caught in the bubbling phase. This is the default behaviour for most events, such as click or keydown. The capturing phase is used more rarely, mainly to intercept events before they reach the target element. Bubbling is more intuitive and convenient for event delegation.",
    longAnswer: "The phases of event propagation\nWhen an event occurs in the DOM, it goes through three phases: capturing, target and bubbling. In the capturing phase the event travels from the root element (document) down to the target element. In the bubbling phase it travels the other way, from the target element up to the root.\nWhy bubbling is used more often\nBy default the `addEventListener` method attaches a handler in the bubbling phase (the third argument `false`, or omitted). This is done for convenience: bubbling lets you implement event delegation, where a single handler on a parent handles events from a great many child elements. For a `<ul>` list with a hundred `<li>` items, for example, you can attach one handler to the `<ul>` and catch clicks on any `<li>` through bubbling.\nA code example\n\nWhen capturing is used\nThe capturing phase is used more rarely — to intercept events before they reach the target element, for example, or for debugging. It is also useful when you need to handle an event before a child element handles it.\nConclusion: bubbling is the standard and most practical way of handling events in web development, especially for delegation. Capturing is applied in specific scenarios where early interception is required.",
    codeExample: "// A handler in the bubbling phase (the default)\ndocument.querySelector('ul').addEventListener('click', (event) => {\n  console.log('A click on', event.target.tagName);\n});\n\n// A handler in the capturing phase (the third argument true)\ndocument.querySelector('ul').addEventListener('click', (event) => {\n  console.log('Capturing: a click on', event.target.tagName);\n}, true);",
  },
  269: {
    source: "Какие существуют методы поиска элементов в DOM — querySelector, getElementBy*? Чем они отличаются?",
    question: "What methods are there for finding elements in the DOM — querySelector, getElementBy*? How do they differ?",
    shortAnswer: "The main methods for finding elements in the DOM are: getElementById (by id), getElementsByClassName (by class), getElementsByTagName (by tag) and querySelector/querySelectorAll (by CSS selector). The first three return live collections (HTMLCollection), while querySelectorAll returns a static NodeList. querySelector returns the first matching element, querySelectorAll every one of them.",
    longAnswer: "Methods for finding elements in the DOM\nJavaScript has several ways of finding elements in the DOM tree. The main ones are: `getElementById`, `getElementsByClassName`, `getElementsByTagName`, plus `querySelector` and `querySelectorAll`. They differ in the types they return, in performance and in flexibility.\ngetElementById\nIt returns a single element by its unique `id`. This is the fastest method, since the browser optimizes lookups by id.\n\ngetElementsByClassName and getElementsByTagName\nThey return a live `HTMLCollection`, which updates automatically when the DOM changes. `getElementsByClassName` searches by class, `getElementsByTagName` by tag name.\n\nquerySelector and querySelectorAll\nThese methods take a CSS selector. `querySelector` returns the first matching element, `querySelectorAll` a static `NodeList`, which does not update when the DOM changes. They are more flexible but slower than the specialized methods.\n\nThe main differences\n\n- The type of the return value: `getElementById` returns a single element, `getElementsBy*` a live collection, `querySelectorAll` a static NodeList.\n- Performance: `getElementById` is the fastest; `querySelector` is slower because the CSS selector has to be parsed.\n- Flexibility: `querySelector` lets you use any CSS selector, combinators and pseudo-classes included.\nConclusion: to search by id use `getElementById` — it is fast and simple. For complex selectors, or for searching by class or tag with a live collection, `getElementsBy*` will do. If you need a static collection or a complex CSS selector, choose `querySelectorAll`.",
    codeExample: "const element = document.getElementById('myId');\n\nconst items = document.getElementsByClassName('item');\nconst divs = document.getElementsByTagName('div');\n\nconst firstButton = document.querySelector('button.primary');\nconst allButtons = document.querySelectorAll('button');",
  },
  270: {
    source: "Как организовать взаимодействие между основной страницей и страницей внутри iFrame, если прямой доступ к функциям друг друга невозможен?",
    question: "How do you organize interaction between the main page and the page inside an iframe when direct access to one another's functions is impossible?",
    shortAnswer: "For interaction between a page and an iframe the window.postMessage method is used. The parent page sends a message through iframe.contentWindow.postMessage, and the iframe through window.parent.postMessage. On the receiving side the message is caught through the message event. The sender's origin must always be checked for security. This is the standard way of exchanging data between windows on different domains.",
    longAnswer: "The basics of interacting with an iframe through postMessage\nWhen a page and an iframe are on different domains, direct access to one another's DOM or functions is blocked by the same-origin policy. The postMessage API built into browsers is used for the safe exchange of data.\nHow it works\nA message is sent through the target window's postMessage method. The parent reaches the iframe through iframe.contentWindow, and the iframe reaches the parent through window.parent. The second argument is the expected origin, for security.\n\nPractical use\nThis approach is used for widgets, embedded payment forms, support chats and any micro-service interfaces where parts of the UI live on different domains. It is important always to check event.origin in order not to let data leak.\nConclusion\npostMessage is the only standard way of cross-domain communication with an iframe. It is safe when the origin is validated properly and suits any scenario that requires the exchange of data between windows from different origins.",
    codeExample: "// The parent page sends a message into the iframe\nconst iframe = document.getElementById('myIframe');\niframe.contentWindow.postMessage({ action: 'update', data: 'hello' }, 'https://child-domain.com');\n\n// In the iframe we listen for the message\nwindow.addEventListener('message', (event) => {\n  if (event.origin !== 'https://parent-domain.com') return;\n  console.log('Received:', event.data);\n  // We send a reply\n  event.source.postMessage({ response: 'ok' }, event.origin);\n});",
  },
  271: {
    source: "Как использовать метод showModal() и close() у нативного элемента <dialog> через реф?",
    question: "How do you use the showModal() and close() methods of the native <dialog> element through a ref?",
    shortAnswer: "The `<dialog>` element provides the methods showModal() and close() for controlling its display. In React you have to reach the DOM element through useRef and then call those methods in your event handlers. This lets you create modal windows without third-party libraries.",
    longAnswer: "What the `<dialog>` element is\nThe `<dialog>` element is a native HTML element intended for creating modal windows and dialogs. It provides the built-in methods showModal() for opening the window in modal mode (with the background blocked) and close() for closing it. This simplifies development, since no additional libraries are required.\nUse in React through refs\nIn React the useRef hook is used to reach a DOM element. A ref is created and attached to the `<dialog>` element. The showModal() and close() methods are then called in event handlers (onClick, for example) through the ref's current value.\n\nParticulars and uses\nThe showModal() method opens the dialog on top of the rest of the content and blocks interaction with the background. To close it you can use close() or press the Escape key. The `<dialog>` element also supports the 'close' event, which can be handled for additional logic. This approach is useful for simple modal windows, notifications or forms, when no complex animation or customization is required.\nConclusion: use `<dialog>` with refs in React to create modal windows quickly without external dependencies, if your project does not require complex state management or styling.",
    codeExample: "import { useRef } from 'react';\n\nfunction Modal() {\n  const dialogRef = useRef(null);\n\n  const openModal = () => {\n    dialogRef.current?.showModal();\n  };\n\n  const closeModal = () => {\n    dialogRef.current?.close();\n  };\n\n  return (\n    <>\n      <button onClick={openModal}>Open Modal</button>\n      <dialog ref={dialogRef}>\n        <p>Modal content</p>\n        <button onClick={closeModal}>Close</button>\n      </dialog>\n    </>\n  );\n}",
  },
  272: {
    source: "Какие группы HTTP-кодов ответов существуют и что они означают?",
    question: "What groups of HTTP response codes are there and what do they mean?",
    shortAnswer: "HTTP response codes fall into five groups. 1xx are informational, 2xx successful, 3xx redirections, 4xx client errors and 5xx server errors. Each group has its own meaning and helps you understand the result of a request.",
    longAnswer: "The groups of HTTP response codes\nHTTP response codes are three-digit numbers that the server sends to the client in response to a request. They fall into five groups, each with its own purpose. Understanding these codes helps developers diagnose problems in web applications quickly.\n1xx: Informational\nThe codes in this group report that the request has been received and is being processed. They are rarely used in everyday practice but matter for protocols such as WebSocket. An example: `100 Continue` — the client may carry on sending the request body.\n2xx: Successful\nThese codes indicate that the request was processed successfully. The best known is `200 OK`, which is returned on a successful GET request. `201 Created` is used after a resource has been created (on a POST, for example). `204 No Content` means success but with no response body.\n3xx: Redirections\nThe codes in this group say that additional actions have to be performed in order to fulfil the request. `301 Moved Permanently` means the resource has moved permanently; `302 Found` is a temporary redirect. `304 Not Modified` is used for caching.\n4xx: Client errors\nThese codes indicate problems on the client's side. `400 Bad Request` — incorrect request syntax. `401 Unauthorized` — authentication is required. `403 Forbidden` — access is denied. `404 Not Found` — the resource was not found. `429 Too Many Requests` — the request limit has been exceeded.\n5xx: Server errors\nThe codes in this group signal problems on the server's side. `500 Internal Server Error` — a general server error. `502 Bad Gateway` — an invalid response from an upstream server. `503 Service Unavailable` — the server is temporarily unavailable.\nAn example of use\n\nIn this example we handle the different response codes in order to react properly to success or to an error.\nConclusion\nKnowing the groups of HTTP codes is necessary for debugging network requests, configuring servers and building reliable client-server applications. It is a basic skill for every web developer.",
    codeExample: "fetch('/api/data')\n  .then(response => {\n    if (response.status === 200) {\n      return response.json();\n    } else if (response.status === 404) {\n      console.error('Resource not found');\n    } else if (response.status === 500) {\n      console.error('Server error');\n    }\n  });",
  },
  273: {
    source: "Как работает IntersectionObserver под капотом на уровне браузера?",
    question: "How does IntersectionObserver work under the hood at the browser level?",
    shortAnswer: "IntersectionObserver uses the browser's internal mechanism to track asynchronously when an element intersects its parent or the viewport. The browser optimizes the checks by running them within the event loop, normally before rendering. This avoids expensive computation on the main thread and keeps performance smooth.",
    longAnswer: "How IntersectionObserver works under the hood\nIntersectionObserver is a browser API that lets you track asynchronously when an element intersects its parent container or the viewport. Unlike listening for scroll events by hand, which can cause a great deal of computation on the main thread, IntersectionObserver uses the browser's internal optimizations.\nThe internal mechanism\nThe browser implements IntersectionObserver at the level of the rendering engine. When you create an observer, the browser registers it in a special data structure linked to the DOM tree. The intersection checks are performed not on every animation frame but within the event loop, normally before the rendering stage. This lets the browser batch the changes and minimize the load.\nA code example\n\nThe optimizations\n\n- The browser uses hardware acceleration to determine visibility, without loading the main thread.\n- The checks are performed only when the scroll position, the window's size or the DOM changes, not on every scroll event.\n- IntersectionObserver supports thresholds, which lets you control precisely when it fires.\nConclusion\nIntersectionObserver is an efficient tool for implementing lazy loading, infinite scrolling and animations, since it uses the browser's internal mechanisms to minimize the performance cost.",
    codeExample: "const observer = new IntersectionObserver((entries) => {\n  entries.forEach(entry => {\n    if (entry.isIntersecting) {\n      console.log('The element is visible');\n    }\n  });\n}, { threshold: 0.5 });\n\nconst target = document.getElementById('myElement');\nobserver.observe(target);",
  },
  274: {
    source: "Что такое fetch и что возвращает вызов response.json()?",
    question: "What is fetch and what does a call to response.json() return?",
    shortAnswer: "fetch is a method built into the browser for performing HTTP requests. It returns a Promise that resolves to a Response object. A call to response.json() also returns a Promise, which reads the response body and parses it as JSON. This is an asynchronous operation, so you have to use await or .then().",
    longAnswer: "What is fetch?\nfetch is an API built into the browser for performing HTTP requests. It replaces the obsolete XMLHttpRequest and provides a simpler, more flexible Promise-based interface. fetch takes a URL and an optional options object (the method, the headers, the request body) and returns a Promise that resolves to a Response object.\nWhat does response.json() return?\nThe response.json() method is called on a Response object and returns a Promise that resolves to the result of parsing the response body as JSON. This is an asynchronous operation, since the response body may be large and is read as a stream. If the response body is not valid JSON, the Promise is rejected with an error.\nAn example of use\n\nIn this example fetch sends a GET request, then the response's status is checked, and if all is well response.json() is called in order to extract the data. Note that response.json() is itself asynchronous, so it has to be handled through .then() or await.\nConclusion\nfetch and response.json() are the foundation of working with HTTP in modern web applications. They let you receive and send data in JSON format easily, which is especially useful when interacting with REST APIs. Use them to load data asynchronously on the client.",
    codeExample: "fetch('https://api.example.com/data')\n  .then(response => {\n    if (!response.ok) {\n      throw new Error('Network response was not ok');\n    }\n    return response.json(); // returns a Promise\n  })\n  .then(data => {\n    console.log(data); // we handle the data\n  })\n  .catch(error => {\n    console.error('Fetch error:', error);\n  });",
  },
  275: {
    source: "Какие браузерные хранилища подходят для кэширования данных и в чём их отличия?",
    question: "Which of the browser's storage mechanisms suit the caching of data, and how do they differ?",
    shortAnswer: "The main browser storage mechanisms for caching are: localStorage, sessionStorage, cookies, IndexedDB and Cache Storage. localStorage stores up to 5-10 MB with no expiry, sessionStorage until the tab is closed, cookies up to 4 KB with an expiry, IndexedDB large volumes of structured data, and Cache Storage HTTP responses. The choice depends on the volume and on how long the data has to live.",
    longAnswer: "An overview of the browser's storage mechanisms\nSeveral mechanisms are available for caching data on the client side in the browser: localStorage, sessionStorage, cookies, IndexedDB and Cache Storage. Each has its own characteristics as to volume, lifetime and the way it is accessed, which determines where it is applied.\nlocalStorage\nIt stores data as key-value pairs (strings) with a limit of around 5-10 MB. The data is preserved after the browser closes and has no expiry. It suits caching small volumes of static information — a user's settings, for example.\n\nsessionStorage\nThe same as localStorage, but the data lives only within the current tab and is deleted when it closes. The limit is also around 5-10 MB. It is used for temporary data — the state of a form on one page, for example.\n\nCookies\nSmall pieces of text data, up to 4 KB, which are sent to the server automatically with every HTTP request. They have an expiry, set through Expires or Max-Age. They are used for authentication and session tracking, but not for caching large volumes.\n\nIndexedDB\nAn object-oriented database in the browser that supports large volumes (hundreds of MB and more). It lets you store structured data, files and Blobs included. Access is asynchronous, through transactions. It is ideal for caching complex data — for offline applications, for example.\n\nCache Storage\nPart of the Service Worker API, intended for caching HTTP responses. It lets you store requests and responses in order to work offline. The limit depends on the browser but is normally substantial. It is used in Progressive Web Apps (PWAs).\n\nConclusion\nThe choice of storage depends on the task: for small string data that has to live a long time use localStorage; for temporary data, sessionStorage; for authentication, cookies; for large volumes of structured data, IndexedDB; for caching HTTP requests in a PWA, Cache Storage. Applying these mechanisms properly improves performance and the user experience.",
    codeExample: "// Saving in localStorage\nlocalStorage.setItem('theme', 'dark');\n// Reading it\nconst theme = localStorage.getItem('theme');\nconsole.log(theme); // 'dark'\n\n// Saving in sessionStorage\nsessionStorage.setItem('formData', JSON.stringify({name: 'John'}));\n// Reading it\nconst data = JSON.parse(sessionStorage.getItem('formData'));\n\n// Setting a cookie\ndocument.cookie = 'sessionId=abc123; max-age=3600; path=/';\n// Reading every cookie\nconsole.log(document.cookie);\n\n// Opening the database\nconst request = indexedDB.open('myDB', 1);\nrequest.onsuccess = (event) => {\n  const db = event.target.result;\n  const transaction = db.transaction(['store'], 'readonly');\n  const store = transaction.objectStore('store');\n  const getRequest = store.get('key');\n  getRequest.onsuccess = () => console.log(getRequest.result);\n};\n\n// Opening the cache and adding a response\ncaches.open('v1').then(cache => {\n  cache.add('/api/data');\n});\n// Reading from the cache\ncaches.match('/api/data').then(response => {\n  if (response) console.log('The data came from the cache');\n});",
  },
  276: {
    source: "Какой тип по умолчанию имеет элемент <button> в HTML?",
    question: "What type does the <button> element have by default in HTML?",
    shortAnswer: "By default the `<button>` element has the type \"submit\". This means that pressing a button inside a form sends its data to the server. If the button is outside a form, it performs no action. To change the behaviour you have to state the attribute type=\"button\" explicitly.",
    longAnswer: "The default type for the `<button>` element\nIn HTML the `<button>` element has the type `submit` by default. This is an important characteristic that often leads to unexpected behaviour if a developer does not state the `type` attribute explicitly. When a button is inside a form, pressing it causes the form's data to be sent to the server. If the button is outside a form, it performs no action.\nExamples of use\nConsider a simple form:\n\nIn this case the button will submit the form. To prevent the submission you have to state `type=\"button\"` explicitly:\n\nThere are also the types `reset` (which resets the form) and `submit` (which submits it).\nConclusion\nKnowing the default type helps you avoid errors when working with forms. Always state the `type` attribute on buttons in order to control their behaviour.",
    codeExample: "<form action=\"/submit\" method=\"post\">\n  <input type=\"text\" name=\"username\">\n  <button>Submit</button>\n</form>\n\n<button type=\"button\">Click me</button>",
  },
  277: {
    source: "Какие дочерние теги используются внутри <details> для создания аккордеона?",
    question: "Which child tags are used inside <details> to create an accordion?",
    shortAnswer: "Inside `<details>` the `<summary>` tag is used for the heading of the visible part, plus arbitrary content (`<p>`, `<ul>`, for example) for the block that is hidden. `<summary>` is always the first child element; otherwise the browser creates one automatically. The open attribute makes the accordion expanded by default.",
    longAnswer: "The main child tags\nThe `<details>` element is intended for creating an expandable block (an accordion) without JavaScript. It supports two main child tags:\n\n- `<summary>` — a mandatory tag that sets the visible heading. If you do not provide one, the browser adds the text \"Details\" automatically.\n- Any other HTML tags — the content that will be hidden or shown when `<summary>` is clicked. These may be `<p>`, `<ul>`, `<div>`, `<img>` and so on.\nA code example\n\nThe open attribute\nTo have the accordion expanded initially, add the `open` attribute to `<details>`:\n\nConclusion\nUse `<details>` and `<summary>` for simple accordions without JavaScript, when you need to hide additional information — an FAQ or spoilers, for example. This improves accessibility and simplifies the code.",
    codeExample: "<details>\n  <summary>Click to find out more</summary>\n  <p>This is the hidden text that appears on a click.</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li>Item 1</li>\n    <li>Item 2</li>\n  </ul>\n</details>\n\n<details open>\n  <summary>Already open</summary>\n  <p>This block is visible straight away.</p>\n</details>",
  },
  278: {
    source: "Какой HTML-тег использовать для кнопки, открывающей модальное окно?",
    question: "Which HTML tag should you use for a button that opens a modal window?",
    shortAnswer: "For a button that opens a modal window, use the `<button>` tag. It is semantically correct and accessible to the keyboard and to screen readers. Do not use a `<div>` or a `<span>` with click handlers, since they have no built-in accessibility.",
    longAnswer: "The right HTML tag for a modal-window button\nTo create a button that opens a modal window you should use the `<button>` tag. This tag semantically denotes an interactive element that performs an action when pressed. Unlike a `<div>` or a `<span>`, `<button>` is focusable by keyboard by default and is handled correctly by screen readers, which improves accessibility.\nA code example\n\nWhy you should not use other tags\n\n- A `<div>` or a `<span>` has no built-in button role, which requires additional ARIA attributes (`role=\"button\"`, for example) and the manual handling of keyboard events.\n- Using an `<a>` with href=\"#\" can lead to unwanted navigation or a changed URL unless the default behaviour is prevented.\nConclusion\nUse `<button>` for any action, opening modal windows included. This ensures semantic correctness, accessibility and ease of implementation.",
    codeExample: "<button id=\"openModalBtn\">Open the modal window</button>\n\n<div id=\"myModal\" class=\"modal\">\n  <div class=\"modal-content\">\n    <span class=\"close\">&times;</span>\n    <p>The modal window's content.</p>\n  </div>\n</div>\n\n<script>\n  document.getElementById('openModalBtn').addEventListener('click', function() {\n    document.getElementById('myModal').style.display = 'block';\n  });\n</script>",
  },
  279: {
    source: "Какой HTML-тег использовать для ссылки-кнопки, выполняющей навигацию по странице?",
    question: "Which HTML tag should you use for a link-button that performs navigation on a page?",
    shortAnswer: "For a link-button that performs navigation on a page, use the `<a>` tag with an href attribute. It is the semantically correct element for moving between pages or to anchors. Do not use `<button>` for navigation, since it is intended for actions within a page.",
    longAnswer: "The core concept\nTo create a link that takes the user to another page or to an anchor on the current page, you should use the `<a>` (anchor) tag. This tag is the semantically correct HTML element for navigation. The `href` attribute states the target URL or the anchor's identifier (`#section`, for example).\nWhy not `<button>`?\nThe `<button>` tag is intended for performing actions within a page, such as submitting a form or calling a JavaScript function. Using `<button>` for navigation breaks HTML's semantics and can degrade accessibility for screen-reader users and for search engines.\nA code example\n\nStyling it as a button\nIf you need the link to look like a button, use CSS to style the `<a>` tag. This preserves the semantics and ensures the correct behaviour.\n\nConclusion\nUse the `<a>` tag with an `href` attribute for any navigation on a page or between pages. This ensures semantic correctness, accessibility and correct indexing by search engines.",
    codeExample: "<!-- Correct: a link for navigation -->\n<a href=\"/about\">About us</a>\n\n<!-- A link to an anchor on the same page -->\n<a href=\"#features\">Go to the features</a>\n\n<!-- Incorrect: a button for navigation -->\n<button onclick=\"location.href='/about'\">About us</button>\n\n<a href=\"/register\" class=\"button\">Register</a>",
  },
  280: {
    source: "Почему тег <details> не всегда подходит для реализации аккордеона с анимацией?",
    question: "Why is the <details> tag not always suitable for implementing an accordion with animation?",
    shortAnswer: "The `<details>` tag does not support animating its expansion and collapse through a CSS transition or animation, since its height changes instantly. For smooth animation you have to use JavaScript, which complicates the code. `<details>` also does not let you manage the state of several elements at once, which is necessary for an accordion.",
    longAnswer: "Why `<details>` is not suitable for an animated accordion\nThe `<details>` tag is a native HTML element for creating expandable content. Its behaviour does not meet the requirements of an animated accordion, however, for several reasons.\nNo CSS animation\nBrowsers do not support animating the `height` or `max-height` property on `<details>`, since its expansion happens instantly. A CSS transition does not fire, because the change of state is not gradual.\n\nManaging the state\nAn accordion requires the other elements to close when one is opened. `<details>` provides no built-in mechanism for this — you have to use JavaScript to manage the `open` attribute on all the elements.\n\nStyling limitations\nThe styling of `<details>` is limited: you cannot easily change the icon or animate the marker without additional CSS tricks. This makes a consistent design harder to achieve.\nConclusion\nUse `<details>` for simple expandable blocks without animation. For an accordion with smooth opening and closing and synchronized states, it is better to implement a custom component in JavaScript with a CSS transition.",
    codeExample: "/* The animation does not work */\ndetails {\n  transition: height 0.3s ease;\n}\ndetails[open] {\n  height: auto;\n}\n\n// An example of managing it by hand\nconst details = document.querySelectorAll('details');\ndetails.forEach(d => {\n  d.addEventListener('toggle', () => {\n    details.forEach(other => {\n      if (other !== d) other.removeAttribute('open');\n    });\n  });\n});",
  },
  281: {
    source: "Какие семантические HTML-теги можно использовать для реализации аккордеона без JavaScript?",
    question: "Which semantic HTML tags can you use to implement an accordion without JavaScript?",
    shortAnswer: "To implement an accordion without JavaScript you can use the `<details>` and `<summary>` tags. Every block of the accordion is wrapped in `<details>` and its heading in `<summary>`. When `<summary>` is clicked, the browser expands or hides the content of `<details>` automatically. This is the semantically correct, accessible way of creating simple accordions.",
    longAnswer: "Using semantic tags for an accordion\nFor creating an accordion without JavaScript the `<details>` and `<summary>` tags are ideally suited. They are part of HTML5 and provide built-in functionality for expanding and hiding content. This is semantically correct, since browsers and assistive technologies (screen readers, for example) interpret these elements properly.\nHow it works\nEvery section of the accordion is wrapped in `<details>`. Inside it the first child element is `<summary>`, which serves as the heading. The rest of the content stays hidden until a click. When `<summary>` is clicked, the browser toggles the `open` attribute on `<details>`, showing or hiding the content.\nA code example\n\nParticulars and styling\nBy default `<details>` is displayed as a block with a marker (a triangle) to the left of `<summary>`. You can style it with CSS — removing the marker or adding an animation, for example. For complex accordions where only one section may be open at a time, however, JavaScript will be needed, since HTML does not provide that functionality out of the box.\nConclusion\nThe `<details>` and `<summary>` tags are a simple, accessible way of creating accordions without JavaScript. They suit cases where no complex behaviour is required — an FAQ or documentation, for example. If finer control is needed, JavaScript solutions are worth considering.",
    codeExample: "<details>\n  <summary>Section 1</summary>\n  <p>The content of the first section.</p>\n</details>\n<details>\n  <summary>Section 2</summary>\n  <p>The content of the second section.</p>\n</details>\n<details>\n  <summary>Section 3</summary>\n  <p>The content of the third section.</p>\n</details>",
  },
  282: {
    source: "Какие диапазоны HTTP-статус-кодов относятся к ошибкам клиента, а какие — к ошибкам сервера?",
    question: "Which ranges of HTTP status codes are client errors and which are server errors?",
    shortAnswer: "HTTP status codes fall into five classes. The 4xx codes (400–499) denote client errors — 404 Not Found or 400 Bad Request, for example. The 5xx codes (500–599) indicate server errors, such as 500 Internal Server Error or 503 Service Unavailable. Understanding these ranges helps you determine quickly who is to blame for a problem — the client or the server.",
    longAnswer: "The classification of HTTP status codes\nHTTP status codes fall into five classes, each of which begins with a particular digit. The code's first digit indicates the category of the response. For diagnosing errors the two most important ranges are 4xx (client errors) and 5xx (server errors).\nClient errors (4xx)\nThe range 400–499 means the request contains incorrect data or cannot be processed because of the client's actions. Examples:\n\n- 400 Bad Request — incorrect request syntax.\n- 401 Unauthorized — authentication is required.\n- 403 Forbidden — access is denied, even with authentication.\n- 404 Not Found — the resource was not found.\n- 429 Too Many Requests — the request limit has been exceeded.\nServer errors (5xx)\nThe range 500–599 indicates that the server could not fulfil the request because of an internal problem. Examples:\n\n- 500 Internal Server Error — a general server error.\n- 502 Bad Gateway — an invalid response from an upstream server.\n- 503 Service Unavailable — the server is temporarily unavailable (because of overload, for example).\n- 504 Gateway Timeout — the wait for an upstream server timed out.\nAn example of code handling statuses\n\nIn this example the code checks the status's range and prints the appropriate message. This helps a developer understand quickly where to look for the problem.\nConclusion: knowing the ranges of HTTP status codes is necessary for debugging web applications effectively, for writing correct error handling on the client and the server, and for analysing logs and monitoring.",
    codeExample: "fetch('/api/data')\n  .then(response => {\n    if (response.status >= 400 && response.status < 500) {\n      console.error('A client error:', response.status);\n    } else if (response.status >= 500) {\n      console.error('A server error:', response.status);\n    }\n    return response.json();\n  })\n  .catch(error => console.error('A network error:', error));",
  },
  283: {
    source: "Как реализовать анимированный аккордеон без JavaScript, используя HTML-элемент <label>, <input type=\"checkbox\"> и CSS-переход?",
    question: "How do you implement an animated accordion without JavaScript, using the HTML <label> element, <input type=\"checkbox\"> and a CSS transition?",
    shortAnswer: "An accordion without JavaScript is implemented with a hidden checkbox and a label. The checkbox holds the open/closed state, while the CSS selector :checked controls the display of the content. A CSS transition on the max-height or transform property is used for the animation. This is a simple, accessible way of creating interactive elements without scripts.",
    longAnswer: "The principle of a CSS accordion\nAn accordion without JavaScript is built on the \"checkbox hack\": a hidden `<input type=\"checkbox\">` holds the state (open or closed), while the `<label>` associated with it serves as the visible button. When the user clicks the label, the checkbox toggles, and the CSS selector `:checked` applies styles to the neighbouring elements, showing or hiding the content.\nThe basic HTML structure\n\nThe CSS for the animation\nFor smooth opening, the `max-height` property with a transition is used. Initially the content is hidden (`max-height: 0; overflow: hidden`), and when the checkbox is activated it expands to a large value.\n\nUses and limitations\nThis approach suits simple interfaces where no complex logic or multiple open panels are required. For an accordion where only one section may be open, you can use `<input type=\"radio\">` elements with the same name. The method does not support animating the height precisely, however (because of the fixed max-height) and is awkward for dynamic content.\nConclusion\nA pure CSS accordion is an excellent solution for static pages where performance and the absence of JavaScript dependencies matter. It is simple to implement but limited in flexibility and in the precision of its animation.",
    codeExample: "<div class=\"accordion\">\n  <input type=\"checkbox\" id=\"item1\" class=\"accordion-input\">\n  <label for=\"item1\" class=\"accordion-label\">Heading 1</label>\n  <div class=\"accordion-content\">\n    <p>The panel's content.</p>\n  </div>\n</div>\n\n.accordion-content {\n  max-height: 0;\n  overflow: hidden;\n  transition: max-height 0.3s ease;\n}\n\n.accordion-input:checked ~ .accordion-content {\n  max-height: 200px; /* a sufficiently large value */\n}",
  },
  284: {
    source: "Как работает onSubmit на форме и почему может не срабатывать в некоторых случаях?",
    question: "How does onSubmit work on a form, and why might it not fire in some cases?",
    shortAnswer: "onSubmit is the event that fires when a form is submitted — by pressing a submit button or the Enter key, for example. If it does not fire, the possible causes are: the button has no type=\"submit\", the form is nested inside another form, or there is a JavaScript error in the handler. It is also worth checking that the event has not been cancelled with preventDefault unnecessarily.",
    longAnswer: "How onSubmit works\nThe onSubmit event on an HTML form arises when the user tries to send the data — by pressing a button with type=\"submit\" or the Enter key inside an input field, for example. A handler for this event lets you intercept the submission, perform validation or an asynchronous request, and then either allow the submission or cancel it with the event.preventDefault() method.\nWhy it might not fire\n\n- The submit button has no type=\"submit\" attribute (the default is type=\"button\" in some browsers).\n- The form is nested inside another form — nested forms are not supported in HTML.\n- An error in the handler's JavaScript code that interrupts execution.\n- The use of event.preventDefault() without a subsequent submission.\n- The input field is not inside the `<form>` tag.\nA code example\n\nConclusion\nonSubmit is a key tool for controlling the submission of forms in web applications. It is worth applying to validate data on the client, to submit asynchronously without reloading the page and to improve the user experience. Always check the form's structure and that the button has type=\"submit\", in order to avoid problems.",
    codeExample: "document.getElementById('myForm').addEventListener('submit', function(event) {\n  event.preventDefault(); // we cancel the standard submission\n  const data = new FormData(event.target);\n  console.log('The form data:', Object.fromEntries(data));\n  // here the data could be sent through fetch\n});",
  },
  285: {
    source: "Как реализовать закрытие модального окна по клику на фон (click outside)?",
    question: "How do you implement closing a modal window by clicking the background (click outside)?",
    shortAnswer: "To close a modal window by clicking the background you need to add a click handler to the overlay (the darkened background). Inside the handler you check that the click was on the overlay itself rather than on the modal window's content. This can be done by comparing event.target with the overlay element, or by using event.stopPropagation() on the modal window's content.",
    longAnswer: "The concept of closing a modal window by clicking the background\nWhen a user clicks the darkened background (the overlay) of a modal window, they expect the window to close. This is a standard UX pattern that improves interaction with an interface. The implementation is based on handling click events and the bubbling of events in the DOM.\nHow it works\nThe overlay is the parent element, which takes up the whole screen. The modal window sits inside the overlay. When the overlay is clicked, the event bubbles, and we can intercept it at the overlay's level. A click on the modal window's content also bubbles up to the overlay, however, so we have to distinguish whether the click was on the overlay itself rather than on its child elements.\nAn example implementation\n\nWhere it is applied\n\n- Web applications with modal windows, dialogs and pop-ups.\n- UI libraries (React, Vue) often provide built-in solutions.\n- Custom components for improving UX.\nConclusion\nUse a check of event.target, or stopPropagation, for reliably closing a modal window on a click on the background. It is a simple, effective way of improving the user's interaction with an interface.",
    codeExample: "// HTML: <div id=\"overlay\"><div id=\"modal\">...</div></div>\n\nconst overlay = document.getElementById('overlay');\nconst modal = document.getElementById('modal');\n\noverlay.addEventListener('click', (event) => {\n  // If the click was on the overlay itself rather than on the modal\n  if (event.target === overlay) {\n    closeModal();\n  }\n});\n\n// The alternative approach: stop the bubbling inside the modal\nmodal.addEventListener('click', (event) => {\n  event.stopPropagation();\n});\n\noverlay.addEventListener('click', () => {\n  closeModal();\n});",
  },
  286: {
    source: "Чем отличается событие click от события input при обработке пользовательского ввода?",
    question: "How does the click event differ from the input event when handling user input?",
    shortAnswer: "The click event fires when an element is clicked with the mouse, while the input event fires when the value of an input element (a text field, for example) changes. Click is used for actions such as submitting a form, while input is for tracking every change to the input in real time.",
    longAnswer: "The difference between the click and input events\nThe `click` and `input` events belong to different kinds of interaction between the user and a web page. `click` arises when a mouse button is pressed and released on an element, while `input` fires when the value of a form element such as a text field or a slider changes.\nWhen and how they are applied\nThe `click` event is often used for buttons, links and other interactive elements, where a response to a completed action is required. The `input` event is applied to input fields in order to respond to every change in the text — for autocompletion or real-time validation, for example.\nCode examples\n\nIn the first example the message appears only after a click, while in the second every change to the text is printed to the console.\nConclusion\nUse `click` for actions that require explicit confirmation, and `input` for an immediate response to the data being entered — in search suggestions or forms with dynamic validation, for example.",
    codeExample: "// A click handler for a button\ndocument.getElementById('myButton').addEventListener('click', function() {\n  alert('The button was pressed!');\n});\n\n// An input handler for a text field\ndocument.getElementById('myInput').addEventListener('input', function() {\n  console.log('The current value:', this.value);\n});",
  },
  287: {
    source: "Какое событие нужно слушать, чтобы реагировать на каждое изменение значения в текстовом поле?",
    question: "Which event do you have to listen for in order to respond to every change of the value in a text field?",
    shortAnswer: "The 'input' event fires every time a text field's value changes, including pasting, deleting or a change through autofill. Unlike 'change', it does not wait for focus to be lost. It is the main event for updating data reactively in real time.",
    longAnswer: "What is the input event?\nThe `input` event is a standard DOM event that arises on every change to the value of an `<input>` or `<textarea>` element. It fires immediately after the user has typed, deleted or pasted a character, including actions through the context menu or the browser's autofill.\nThe difference from the other events\n\n- `change` — fires only after focus has been lost and only if the value changed. It is not suitable for an immediate response.\n- `keydown` / `keyup` — they respond to key presses but do not account for pasting with the mouse or autofill.\n- `input` — universal and reliable for tracking any change to the value.\nAn example of use\n\nIn this example every change in the field prints the new value to the console. This is useful for search-as-you-type, real-time validation or counting characters.\nConclusion\nThe `input` event is the main tool for responding to every change in a text field. It should be applied wherever immediate feedback from the user's input is needed — in autocompletes, filters or forms with dynamic validation, for example.",
    codeExample: "const input = document.getElementById('myInput');\ninput.addEventListener('input', function(event) {\n  console.log('The current value:', event.target.value);\n});",
  },
  288: {
    source: "Что такое контроллер в паттерне MVC?",
    question: "What is the controller in the MVC pattern?",
    shortAnswer: "The controller in MVC is the component that handles the user's input, interacts with the model to update the data and passes the result to the view. It acts as an intermediary between the model and the view, managing the application's logic. When a button is pressed, for example, the controller calls a method on the model and updates the interface.",
    longAnswer: "What is the controller in MVC?\nThe controller is the central element of the Model-View-Controller pattern, responsible for handling the user's actions and coordinating between the model and the view. It receives requests (clicks or keyboard input, for example), interprets them and decides which logic to perform. The controller does not contain business logic directly but delegates it to the model, after which it updates the view.\nHow does it work?\nWhen the user interacts with the interface, the controller receives the event, calls the appropriate method on the model to change the data, and then notifies the view that it needs repainting. This ensures a separation of concerns: the model manages the data, the view the display, and the controller the connection between them.\nA code example\n\nWhere is it applied?\nMVC is widely used in web frameworks (Ruby on Rails, Django, Spring MVC, for example) and in developing desktop applications. The controller simplifies the testing and maintenance of code, since the input logic is separated from the data and the interface.\nConclusion: the controller in MVC is necessary for organizing a clean separation of concerns, which makes an application more modular and easier to extend. It is worth applying in projects where structure and scalability matter.",
    codeExample: "// A controller for managing users\nclass UserController {\n  constructor(model, view) {\n    this.model = model;\n    this.view = view;\n  }\n\n  // Handling the addition of a user\n  addUser(name) {\n    this.model.createUser(name);\n    this.view.renderUserList(this.model.getUsers());\n  }\n\n  // Handling the deletion of a user\n  deleteUser(id) {\n    this.model.removeUser(id);\n    this.view.renderUserList(this.model.getUsers());\n  }\n}",
  },
  289: {
    source: "Из каких логических частей состоит URL/URI запроса?",
    question: "Which logical parts does a request's URL/URI consist of?",
    shortAnswer: "A URL consists of several logical parts: the protocol (https, for example), the domain (or IP address), the port (optional), the path to the resource, the query string (the query parameters) and the fragment (the anchor). These parts let you state exactly where and how to get a resource on the network.",
    longAnswer: "The structure of a URL/URI\nA URL (Uniform Resource Locator) is a resource's address on the network. It consists of several logical parts, each of which performs its own function. Understanding this structure is necessary for working properly with HTTP requests, APIs and web development in general.\nThe main components\n\n- The protocol (scheme) — it determines the way the data is transmitted: `http`, `https`, `ftp`, for example.\n- The domain (host) — the server's name or IP address: `example.com`, for example.\n- The port — an optional element stating the port number on the server (80 by default for HTTP, 443 for HTTPS).\n- The path — it states the resource's location on the server: `/api/users`, for example.\n- The query string — it begins with `?` and contains parameters in the form `key=value`, separated by `&`.\n- The fragment — it begins with `#` and points to a particular part of the resource (a section on a page, for example).\nAn example\n\nIn this example the protocol is `https`, the domain `api.example.com`, the port `8080`, the path `/users`, the query string `page=1&limit=10` and the fragment `section2`.\nUse\nKnowing a URL's structure helps you form requests to an API properly, handle the parameters on the server and the client, and understand how a browser or HTTP client interprets an address. When developing a REST API, for example, it is important to divide the path and the query parameters correctly.\nConclusion: understanding a URL's components is necessary for any work with web technologies, from simply following links to the complex integration of microservices.",
    codeExample: "https://api.example.com:8080/users?page=1&limit=10#section2",
  },
  290: {
    source: "Что такое присоединяемые свойства (Attached Property) в WPF?",
    question: "What are attached properties in WPF?",
    shortAnswer: "An attached property is a special kind of dependency property that is defined in one class but can be set on any other element. It lets child elements set the values of properties affecting their position or behaviour within a parent container. The Grid.Row property, for example, sets the grid row for an element, even though the property itself is defined in the Grid class.",
    longAnswer: "What are attached properties?\nAttached properties are a mechanism in WPF that lets one element set the values of properties defined in another class. It is an extension of the dependency property system, in which a property is declared in one type but can be set on any object that supports dependency properties.\nHow does it work?\nAttached properties are implemented through static Set and Get methods, which call the internal DependencyObject.SetValue and GetValue methods. In XAML they are written in the form `OwnerClass.PropertyName`. The `Grid.Row` property, for example, lets you set the grid row for any child element.\n\nAn example of use in XAML\n\nHere `Grid.Row` and `Grid.Column` are attached properties that determine the button's position in the grid.\nWhere is it applied?\nAttached properties are widely used in layout controls (Grid, Canvas, DockPanel) and also in styles and triggers. They let you create flexible, reusable components without breaking the class hierarchy.\nConclusion: attached properties are a powerful WPF tool for dividing logic between parent and child elements. They are worth applying when you need to set an element's behaviour or position in the context of another container without changing the element itself.",
    codeExample: "// Defining an attached property\npublic static readonly DependencyProperty RowProperty =\n    DependencyProperty.RegisterAttached(\n        \"Row\",\n        typeof(int),\n        typeof(Grid),\n        new FrameworkPropertyMetadata(0));\n\npublic static void SetRow(UIElement element, int value)\n{\n    element.SetValue(RowProperty, value);\n}\n\npublic static int GetRow(UIElement element)\n{\n    return (int)element.GetValue(RowProperty);\n}\n\n<Grid>\n    <Button Grid.Row=\"1\" Grid.Column=\"2\" Content=\"Click\" />\n</Grid>",
  },
  291: {
    source: "Вызовет ли Reflow изменение свойств transform/translate у элемента?",
    question: "Will changing the transform/translate properties on an element cause a reflow?",
    shortAnswer: "No, changing the transform or translate properties does not cause a reflow. These properties are handled at the compositing stage, bypassing the layout and paint stages. This makes them ideal for animations, since they do not recompute the geometry of other elements and do not cause expensive repaints.",
    longAnswer: "What a reflow is and how it works\nA reflow (or layout) is the rendering stage at which the browser computes the geometry of elements: their dimensions, their position and their placement relative to other elements. A reflow is triggered by changes that affect the page's layout — a change to the width, the height, the margin, the padding, display, positioning (top, left) and so on. It is an expensive operation, since it can affect both child and parent elements.\nWhy do transform/translate not cause a reflow?\nThe transform and translate properties (and also opacity, filter and will-change) are handled at the compositing stage. The browser creates a separate layer for the element (a compositing layer) and moves it with the GPU, without recomputing layout and paint. This means a change to transform does not affect the position of the other elements in the document's flow — the element \"floats\" above the page, as it were.\nA code example\n\nWhen does this matter?\nWhen creating animations, especially at 60fps, using transform rather than left/top is critically important for performance. It is standard practice in CSS animations and in JavaScript libraries (GreenSock, Framer Motion, for example).\nConclusion: use transform/translate for animations of movement in order to avoid a reflow and keep the interface running smoothly, especially on mobile devices and with complex layouts.",
    codeExample: "// Bad: it causes a reflow on every frame of the animation\nconst el = document.getElementById('box');\nel.style.left = '100px'; // A reflow!\n\n// Good: it causes no reflow and uses compositing\nel.style.transform = 'translateX(100px)'; // No reflow",
  },
  292: {
    source: "Как можно кастомизировать тему графика при использовании библиотеки Chart.js?",
    question: "How can you customize a chart's theme when using the Chart.js library?",
    shortAnswer: "A chart's theme in Chart.js can be customized through the global settings in Chart.defaults, overriding colours, fonts and styles. You can also use built-in plugins such as Chart.plugins.register, or create your own. For individual charts the settings are passed in the options object when the instance is created.",
    longAnswer: "The main ways of customizing a theme in Chart.js\nChart.js provides flexible capabilities for changing a chart's appearance, which lets you adapt it to a corporate style or to specific design requirements. Customization can be done at several levels: globally, for a particular chart, or through plugins.\nConfiguring the theme globally\nThe global settings are set through the `Chart.defaults` object. This lets you change the colours, fonts and other parameters for every chart on a page. For example:\n\nConfiguring a particular chart\nWhen creating a chart you can pass an `options` object with individual settings. This overrides the global values. An example:\n\nUsing plugins\nChart.js supports plugins that can change the theme. The `chartjs-plugin-datalabels` plugin, for example, lets you configure data labels. You can also create your own plugin for unique styling:\n\nConclusion\nCustomizing a theme in Chart.js is done through the global settings, the parameters of a particular chart, or plugins. This lets you control the appearance of your visualizations flexibly, making them match your design and brand requirements.",
    codeExample: "Chart.defaults.color = '#333';\nChart.defaults.font.family = 'Arial';\nChart.defaults.borderColor = '#ccc';\n\nconst ctx = document.getElementById('myChart').getContext('2d');\nnew Chart(ctx, {\n  type: 'bar',\n  data: { ... },\n  options: {\n    plugins: {\n      legend: {\n        labels: {\n          color: '#ff0000'\n        }\n      }\n    },\n    scales: {\n      y: {\n        grid: {\n          color: '#e0e0e0'\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n});\n\nChart.register({\n  id: 'customTheme',\n  beforeDraw: function(chart) {\n    const ctx = chart.ctx;\n    ctx.fillStyle = '#f5f5f5';\n    ctx.fillRect(0, 0, chart.width, chart.height);\n  }\n});",
  },
  293: {
    source: "Какие способы оптимизации Reflow/Repaint ты знаешь?",
    question: "What ways of optimizing reflow/repaint do you know?",
    shortAnswer: "Reflow and repaint are stages of the browser's rendering. A reflow happens when elements' geometry changes, a repaint when styles that do not affect dimensions change. Optimization involves minimizing changes to the DOM, using classes rather than inline styles, applying `will-change`, `transform` and `opacity` for animations, and batching the reading and writing of properties.",
    longAnswer: "What are reflow and repaint?\nA reflow (or layout) is the process of computing the dimensions and positions of the elements on a page. It is triggered by any change that affects the geometry: a change to the width or height, adding or removing elements, changing the font and so on. A repaint is the process of drawing the pixels, which happens after a reflow or when visual properties that do not affect the geometry change (a background colour or a shadow, for example).\nThe main ways of optimizing\n\n- Minimizing reflows: avoid frequent changes that cause the layout to be recomputed. Instead of changing styles one at a time, for example, use classes or `cssText`.\n- Batching reads and writes: reading properties that cause a reflow (`offsetHeight`, for example) after a write can lead to a forced reflow. Group your reads and writes separately.\n- Using `transform` and `opacity`: these properties are handled at the compositing level and cause neither a reflow nor a repaint, which is ideal for animations.\n- Applying `will-change`: it hints to the browser that an element is going to change, letting it create a separate layer in advance.\n- Avoiding complex selectors: although this affects repaint less, complex selectors can slow the recomputation of styles down.\nA code example\n\nConclusion\nOptimizing reflow and repaint is critical for the performance of web applications, especially with animations and dynamic content. Use modern CSS properties, group your operations and avoid forced layouts, for a smooth interface.",
    codeExample: "// Bad: it causes a reflow on every iteration\nfor (let i = 0; i < 100; i++) {\n  const el = document.getElementById('item');\n  el.style.width = i + 'px';\n  console.log(el.offsetWidth); // a forced reflow\n}\n\n// Good: batched reading and writing\nconst el = document.getElementById('item');\nconst widths = [];\nfor (let i = 0; i < 100; i++) {\n  widths.push(i);\n}\nfor (let w of widths) {\n  el.style.width = w + 'px';\n}\n// Reading after all the writes\nconsole.log(el.offsetWidth);",
  },
  294: {
    source: "Что обозначает квадратная скобка в JSON?",
    question: "What do square brackets denote in JSON?",
    shortAnswer: "Square brackets in JSON denote an array — an ordered list of values. An array may contain any data type: strings, numbers, objects, other arrays. The elements are separated by commas.",
    longAnswer: "What are square brackets in JSON?\nIn JSON the square brackets `[]` are used to denote an array. An array is an ordered collection of values that may contain elements of any type: strings, numbers, objects, booleans, `null` or other arrays.\nThe syntax and examples\nAn array begins with `[` and ends with `]`. The elements are separated by commas. An example of a simple array of strings:\n\nAn array may contain different data types:\n\nArrays can also be nested:\n\nWhere it is applied\nArrays in JSON are widely used to pass lists of data — a list of users, products or messages, for example. They are the basis for structuring data in APIs and configuration files.\nConclusion\nSquare brackets in JSON denote an array, which lets you store ordered collections of data. It is a key element of JSON's syntax, necessary for working with any list in web development and data exchange.",
    codeExample: "[\"apple\", \"banana\", \"cherry\"]\n\n[42, \"hello\", true, null, {\"key\": \"value\"}]\n\n[[1, 2], [3, 4], [5, 6]]",
  },
  295: {
    source: "На какой вкладке DevTools можно увидеть отправляемые и получаемые сетевые запросы?",
    question: "On which DevTools tab can you see the network requests being sent and received?",
    shortAnswer: "The Network tab in the browser's developer tools shows every network request being sent and received. You can see the headers, the request and response bodies, the status codes, the durations and the size of the data. This helps you debug problems with the loading of resources, with API requests and with performance.",
    longAnswer: "Where to look at network requests in DevTools\nIn the browser's developer tools (DevTools) the Network tab is responsible for analysing network activity. It displays every HTTP request the page sends: the loading of HTML, CSS, JavaScript, images and fonts, plus XHR/Fetch requests to APIs.\nHow to use it\nOpen DevTools (F12 or Ctrl+Shift+I), go to the Network tab and reload the page. You will see a table of requests, where you can:\n\n- Filter by type (XHR, JS, CSS, Img, Doc and so on)\n- Inspect the request's and response's headers\n- Look at the response body and the data that was sent (the payload)\n- Assess the duration (Timing) and the size\nAn example of debugging\nSuppose you expect a POST request to be sent when a button is pressed. Open Network and press the button — a new request will appear. Select it and go to the Payload tab in order to see the data that was sent, and to Response in order to check the server's reply.\n\nIn Network you will see this request, its status (200, 404, 500 and so on), its duration and its content.\nConclusion\nThe Network tab is an indispensable tool for debugging network interaction, checking that API requests are correct, analysing the loading performance of resources and hunting for errors on the client's or the server's side.",
    codeExample: "// An example: sending a POST request through fetch\nfetch('/api/data', {\n  method: 'POST',\n  headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },\n  body: JSON.stringify({ key: 'value' })\n})\n.then(res => res.json())\n.then(data => console.log(data));",
  },
  296: {
    source: "Что такое нативное мобильное приложение?",
    question: "What is a native mobile application?",
    shortAnswer: "A native mobile application is built specifically for one platform, such as iOS or Android, using that platform's own languages and tools (Swift/Objective-C for iOS, Kotlin/Java for Android). It has direct access to the device's hardware capabilities, providing maximum performance and a smooth user interface. Such applications are installed through the official app stores and can use every function of the OS.",
    longAnswer: "What is a native mobile application?\nA native mobile application is a program developed to run on a particular operating system, such as iOS or Android, using the official programming languages and tools the platform provides. For iOS that means Swift or Objective-C and the Xcode environment; for Android, Kotlin or Java and Android Studio. The main advantage of the native approach is complete integration with the device and its capabilities.\nThe key characteristics\n\n- Direct access to hardware components: the camera, GPS, the accelerometer, the sensors.\n- High performance, since the code is compiled for the particular architecture.\n- The use of every capability of the OS: push notifications, widgets, background tasks.\n- Conformity with the platform's guidelines (the Human Interface Guidelines for iOS, Material Design for Android).\nA code example (iOS, Swift)\n\nA code example (Android, Kotlin)\n\nWhen should you choose native development?\nThe native approach is ideal for applications that require high performance (games, graphics editors), deep integration with the device (fitness trackers, cameras) or the use of specific platform features. It is also preferable when the flawless operation of the interface and conformity with the platform's standards matter. Native development requires separate codebases for each platform, however, which increases the time and cost of development.\nConclusion: native applications provide the best user experience and performance, but they require more resources than cross-platform solutions.",
    codeExample: "import UIKit\n\nclass ViewController: UIViewController {\n    override func viewDidLoad() {\n        super.viewDidLoad()\n        // Configuring the interface\n        let label = UILabel()\n        label.text = \"Hello, world!\"\n        label.textAlignment = .center\n        view.addSubview(label)\n    }\n}\n\nimport android.os.Bundle\nimport androidx.appcompat.app.AppCompatActivity\nimport android.widget.TextView\n\nclass MainActivity : AppCompatActivity() {\n    override fun onCreate(savedInstanceState: Bundle?) {\n        super.onCreate(savedInstanceState)\n        setContentView(R.layout.activity_main)\n        val textView = findViewById<TextView>(R.id.textView)\n        textView.text = \"Hello, world!\"\n    }\n}",
  },
  297: {
    source: "Какие типы значений могут быть в JSON?",
    question: "Which types of value can there be in JSON?",
    shortAnswer: "JSON supports six types of value: string, number, boolean, null, object and array. Strings are always in double quotes; numbers may be integers or floating-point. Objects and arrays can be nested.",
    longAnswer: "The types of value in JSON\nJSON (JavaScript Object Notation) is a lightweight data-interchange format based on a subset of JavaScript's syntax. It supports a strictly limited set of data types, which makes it universal and simple to parse in any programming language.\nThe list of supported types\n\n- String — a sequence of Unicode characters enclosed in double quotes. Example: `\"hello\"`. Special characters such as `\\\"` or `\\n` must be escaped.\n- Number — an integer or a floating-point number. It uses no quotes. Examples: `42`, `-3.14`, `1.5e10`. It does not support NaN, Infinity or leading zeros.\n- Boolean — `true` or `false`, without quotes.\n- null — a special value denoting the absence of data. It is written as `null`.\n- Object — an unordered set of key-value pairs enclosed in curly braces `{}`. The keys are always strings in double quotes.\n- Array — an ordered list of values enclosed in square brackets `[]`. The elements may be of any type, other arrays or objects included.\nAn example of valid JSON\n\nWhat is not supported\nJSON does not support dates, functions, undefined, symbols or comments. Dates are normally passed as strings in ISO 8601 format. This limitation makes JSON safe to pass between systems.\nConclusion: knowing JSON's types is necessary for serializing data correctly and for writing APIs and configuration files. Always check that your data conforms to these six types, in order to avoid parsing errors.",
    codeExample: "{\n  \"name\": \"Alice\",\n  \"age\": 30,\n  \"isStudent\": false,\n  \"address\": null,\n  \"hobbies\": [\"reading\", 42, true],\n  \"metadata\": {\n    \"created\": \"2023-01-01\"\n  }\n}",
  },
  298: {
    source: "Как работать с документацией API в формате Swagger?",
    question: "How do you work with API documentation in Swagger format?",
    shortAnswer: "Swagger is a tool for describing REST APIs in the OpenAPI format. It lets you generate interactive documentation automatically, where you can send requests and see the responses. The documentation describes the endpoints, the parameters, the headers, the request bodies and the response formats. Swagger makes interaction between developers and testers easier and also lets you automate testing.",
    longAnswer: "What Swagger is and why it is needed\nSwagger (now part of the OpenAPI specification) is a standard for describing REST APIs. It is a YAML or JSON file containing complete information about all of an API's endpoints: the methods, the paths, the parameters, the headers, the request bodies, the response codes and the data schemas. Swagger UI is a tool that visualizes that description as an interactive web page where you can send requests to the API and see the responses right in the browser.\nHow do you use Swagger?\nThe main scenarios of use:\n\n- Development: Swagger serves as the single source of truth for the frontend and the backend. A frontend developer can see what data the API expects without waiting for the backend to be ready.\n- Testing: Swagger UI lets you check the endpoints quickly without writing code. There are also tools for generating tests automatically from a Swagger specification.\n- Documentation: Swagger generates readable documentation automatically, which is always up to date as long as the specification is kept current.\nA code example (a fragment of a Swagger specification in YAML)\n\nConclusion\nSwagger is an indispensable tool for developing REST APIs as a team. It improves communication, speeds up development and testing, and provides up-to-date documentation. It is worth applying in any project that has an API, especially when working in a distributed team or when integration with external services is required.",
    codeExample: "openapi: 3.0.0\ninfo:\n  title: User API\n  version: 1.0.0\npaths:\n  /users/{id}:\n    get:\n      summary: Get user by ID\n      parameters:\n        - name: id\n          in: path\n          required: true\n          schema:\n            type: integer\n      responses:\n        '200':\n          description: Successful response\n          content:\n            application/json:\n              schema:\n                type: object\n                properties:\n                  id:\n                    type: integer\n                  name:\n                    type: string",
  },
  299: {
    source: "Как определить, какие данные нужно хранить для сущности \"комментарий\"?",
    question: "How do you determine which data has to be stored for a \"comment\" entity?",
    shortAnswer: "For a comment entity you have to store a unique identifier, the comment's text, the date and time it was created, the author's identifier and the identifier of the parent entity (a post, for example). You can also add an edited flag, likes and the identifier of a parent comment for nesting. This data provides the basic functionality and the links to the other entities.",
    longAnswer: "Determining the data for a comment entity\nWhen designing the data structure for a comment you have to consider its role in the system: a comment links a user to some content and may support nesting. The main fields include a unique identifier (id), the text (body), the creation date (created_at), the author's identifier (author_id) and the parent entity's identifier (post_id). In addition you can store the date it was edited (updated_at), the number of likes (likes_count) and the identifier of a parent comment (parent_id) for a tree structure.\nAn example structure in SQL\n\nUse\nSuch a structure is used in blogs, forums and social networks. The parent_id field lets you implement nested comments without additional tables. Indexes on author_id and post_id speed queries up. Conclusion: store the minimum fields necessary for the functionality, adding optional ones as the need arises.",
    codeExample: "CREATE TABLE comments (\n  id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,\n  body TEXT NOT NULL,\n  created_at TIMESTAMP DEFAULT NOW(),\n  updated_at TIMESTAMP,\n  author_id INTEGER REFERENCES users(id),\n  post_id INTEGER REFERENCES posts(id),\n  parent_id INTEGER REFERENCES comments(id),\n  likes_count INTEGER DEFAULT 0\n);",
  },
  300: {
    source: "Как использование S3/CDN помогает решить проблему географической удалённости пользователей от сервера?",
    question: "How does using S3/a CDN help solve the problem of users being geographically far from the server?",
    shortAnswer: "S3 stores static files in the cloud, while a CDN caches them on servers located closer to the users. When a user requests a file, the CDN serves it from the nearest edge server rather than from the central store. This reduces latency and speeds up the loading of a site or application.",
    longAnswer: "How S3 and a CDN solve the problem of geographical distance\nWhen the server is far from the user, every request passes through a great many network nodes, which increases latency. This is especially critical for static content: images, CSS, JavaScript, video. S3 (Simple Storage Service) is object storage that keeps files in a particular region. If all the users are far from that region, loading will be slow.\nThe role of a CDN\nA CDN (Content Delivery Network) is a network of servers distributed all over the world. It caches content from S3 on edge servers located closer to the end users. When a user requests a file, the CDN routes the request to the nearest server that has a copy automatically. If there is no copy, the CDN fetches it from S3 and stores it for later requests.\nAn example configuration\n\nThe advantages\n\n- Lower latency: the content is delivered from the nearest server.\n- Less load on S3: the requests are handled by the CDN.\n- Saved bandwidth: a CDN can compress data and use HTTP/2.\nConclusion: using S3 together with a CDN is the standard approach for global applications where the loading speed of static assets matters. It is especially useful for sites with an audience in different countries, for streaming services and for large online shops.",
    codeExample: "// An example: attaching CloudFront (a CDN) to an S3 bucket\n// 1. You create an S3 bucket and upload the files\n// 2. You create a CloudFront distribution\n// 3. You name the origin — your S3 bucket\n// 4. CloudFront serves the content through its edge servers automatically\n\n// An example URL after the configuration:\n// https://d123.cloudfront.net/images/logo.png\n// Instead of the direct S3 URL:\n// https://my-bucket.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/images/logo.png",
  },
  301: {
    source: "Что такое медиа-запрос prefers-reduced-motion и для чего он используется?",
    question: "What is the prefers-reduced-motion media query and what is it used for?",
    shortAnswer: "The prefers-reduced-motion media query lets you detect whether the user has switched on the system setting for reduced motion. It is used to disable or replace animations, in order to avoid discomfort for people with vestibular disorders. This improves the accessibility of web applications.",
    longAnswer: "What is prefers-reduced-motion?\nThe `prefers-reduced-motion` media query is a CSS feature that checks the user's system setting for reduced animation. It returns one of two values: `no-preference` (the default) or `reduce` (the user has asked for motion to be minimized). This query is part of the CSS Media Queries Level 5 specification and is aimed at improving the accessibility of web content.\nWhy is this needed?\nMany people experience discomfort, dizziness or nausea when watching intense animations, especially those with vestibular disorders or migraines. By using `prefers-reduced-motion`, developers can disable or replace animations, making a site more comfortable for such users. It also conforms to the WCAG (Web Content Accessibility Guidelines) recommendations.\nHow do you use it?\nThe query is applied in CSS or JavaScript. In CSS you can override animations for users who prefer reduced motion. An example:\n\nIn JavaScript you can check the setting through `window.matchMedia`:\n\nWhere should you apply it?\nThis media query is useful on any site with animations: parallax effects, rotating elements, flashing banners, transitions between pages. It is especially important for sites with a lot of movement — portfolios or landing pages, for example.\nConclusion: use `prefers-reduced-motion` to create an inclusive interface that respects the needs of users sensitive to motion. It increases accessibility and improves the user experience.",
    codeExample: "/* The ordinary animation */\n.element {\n  animation: spin 2s infinite;\n}\n\n/* We disable the animation for users with the reduce setting */\n@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {\n  .element {\n    animation: none;\n  }\n}\n\nconst motionQuery = window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)');\nif (motionQuery.matches) {\n  // We disable the animations\n}",
  },
  302: {
    source: "Для чего нужен и как настраивается Prettier в проекте?",
    question: "What is Prettier for and how is it configured in a project?",
    shortAnswer: "Prettier is a tool for formatting code automatically that ensures a single style throughout a project. It is configured through a configuration file — .prettierrc, for example — where you can set parameters such as the line width and the use of quotes and semicolons. Prettier integrates with code editors and can be run automatically when a file is saved or through pre-commit hooks.",
    longAnswer: "What is Prettier and why is it needed?\nPrettier is an opinionated code formatter that brings code to a single style automatically, putting an end to arguments about formatting within a team. It supports many languages: JavaScript, TypeScript, CSS, HTML, JSON, Markdown and others. Its main purpose is to spare developers from formatting by hand and to make code readable and predictable.\nHow do you configure Prettier in a project?\nPrettier is installed through npm or yarn:\n\nCreate a `.prettierrc` configuration file in the project's root. An example:\n\nYou can also add a `.prettierignore` file in order to exclude particular files or folders from formatting — `node_modules` or `dist`, for example.\nIntegration with the editor and automation\nFor automatic formatting on save in VS Code, install the Prettier extension and configure it in `settings.json`:\n\nTo run it in CI/CD or in pre-commit hooks, use `pretty-quick` or `lint-staged`. An example with husky and lint-staged:\n\nConclusion\nPrettier is worth applying in any project where the consistency of the code and the automation of routine tasks matter. It is especially useful in teams, since it puts an end to discussions about formatting and speeds code review up.",
    codeExample: "npm install --save-dev prettier\n\n{\n  \"semi\": true,\n  \"singleQuote\": true,\n  \"tabWidth\": 2,\n  \"trailingComma\": \"es5\"\n}\n\n{\n  \"editor.formatOnSave\": true,\n  \"editor.defaultFormatter\": \"esbenp.prettier-vscode\"\n}\n\n// package.json\n{\n  \"husky\": {\n    \"hooks\": {\n      \"pre-commit\": \"lint-staged\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"lint-staged\": {\n    \"*.{js,jsx,ts,tsx,css,md}\": [\"prettier --write\"]\n  }\n}",
  },
  303: {
    source: "Как инлайн-стиль соотносится по приоритету с обычными CSS-классами?",
    question: "How does an inline style compare in priority with ordinary CSS classes?",
    shortAnswer: "Inline styles have a higher priority than ordinary CSS classes, because of the rules of CSS specificity. An inline style's specificity is counted as the highest among the simple selectors, so it overrides the properties set through a class. The exception is the use of !important, which can beat an inline style if it is applied in a class.",
    longAnswer: "What is CSS specificity?\nSpecificity is the mechanism that determines which styles will be applied to an element when several rules conflict. It is calculated from the types of selector: inline styles have the highest weight, then come ID selectors, then classes and attributes, and finally tags and pseudo-elements.\nThe priority of inline styles\nInline styles, set through the `style` attribute in the HTML, have a specificity of 1000 (notionally), which is higher than a class (10). This means that any property stated inline will override the same property from a class, unless the class uses `!important`.\nAn example\n\nThe exception: !important\nIf you add `!important` in the class, it will override the inline style, since `!important` has the highest priority of all the rules, inline ones included.\n\nConclusion\nInline styles are convenient for quick overrides, but abusing them makes code harder to maintain. It is better to use classes and to avoid `!important`, in order to keep the cascade predictable.",
    codeExample: "<div class=\"box\" style=\"color: red;\">Text</div>\n\n.box {\n  color: blue;\n}\n// The result: the text will be red, since the inline style has priority\n\n.box {\n  color: blue !important;\n}\n// Now the text will be blue, despite the inline style",
  },
  304: {
    source: "Как можно переопределить стиль, заданный через инлайн-атрибут style?",
    question: "How can you override a style set through the inline style attribute?",
    shortAnswer: "Inline styles have high specificity, but they can be overridden with the !important rule in CSS. You can also change the style attribute through JavaScript. Another way is to use the CSS property all: revert, which resets inline styles.",
    longAnswer: "What inline styles are and why they are hard to override\nInline styles are set directly in an HTML element's style attribute. They have very high specificity (1000 points), so ordinary CSS rules cannot override them without special techniques.\nThe main ways of overriding them\n\n- !important — added to a CSS property in order to raise its priority above inline styles. Example:\n\nthis will override `style=\"color: red\"`.\n- JavaScript — you can change or remove the style attribute directly:\n\n- CSS all: revert — it resets every inline style to its inherited or initial value:\n\nA practical example\n\nIn this example the colour will become blue because of !important, while the font size will stay 20px, since an ordinary rule cannot override the inline one.\nConclusion\nUse !important only as a last resort, when inline styles have to be overridden with certainty — in component libraries, for example. For flexible control over styles it is better to avoid inline attributes and to use CSS classes.",
    codeExample: "div { color: blue !important; }\n\nelement.style.color = 'green'; // or element.removeAttribute('style');\n\ndiv { all: revert; }\n\n<div style=\"color: red; font-size: 20px;\">Text</div>\n\n<style>\n  div {\n    color: blue !important; /* will override the red */\n    font-size: 16px; /* will not work, since the inline one is higher */\n  }\n</style>",
  },
  305: {
    source: "Какие есть способы избавиться от !important в проекте, если нужно его убрать?",
    question: "What ways are there of getting rid of !important in a project, if it has to go?",
    shortAnswer: "To get rid of !important you have to raise the selector's specificity, rearrange the order in which the styles are attached, or use more nested selectors. You can also apply the BEM methodology to isolate styles, or CSS modules. It is important to review your CSS architecture and to avoid conflicts through the proper organization of your code.",
    longAnswer: "Understanding the problem with !important\nThe !important directive in CSS breaks the natural cascade of styles, making a rule take priority regardless of specificity. This often leads to difficulties in maintaining and overriding styles. The main goal is to eliminate such dependencies and restore the cascade's predictability.\nWays of eliminating !important\n\n- Raising the selector's specificity: use more specific selectors — add a class to the parent element or use an ID, for example. Instead of `.button { color: red !important; }`, write `.container .button { color: red; }`.\n- Changing the order in which the styles are attached: if two rules have the same specificity, the last one wins. Move the rule you want lower in the file, or attach it later.\n- Using the BEM methodology: BEM (Block Element Modifier) creates unique class names, which rules out conflicts. For example: `.block__element--modifier { color: red; }`.\n- CSS modules or CSS-in-JS: these approaches generate unique class names automatically, isolating the styles and removing the need for !important.\nA practical example\nSuppose there is a button with !important:\n\nThe solution is to remove !important and raise the specificity:\n\nOr to use BEM:\n\nConclusion\nEliminating !important improves the maintainability and predictability of your CSS. Apply raised specificity, naming methodologies or style-isolation tools for clean, scalable code.",
    codeExample: ".btn { color: blue !important; }\n.special .btn { color: red; } /* will not work */\n\n.btn { color: blue; }\n.special .btn { color: red; } /* now it works */\n\n.btn { color: blue; }\n.btn--special { color: red; }",
  },
  306: {
    source: "Какие проблемы безопасности связаны с использованием innerHTML для вставки пользовательского ввода?",
    question: "What security problems are associated with using innerHTML to insert user input?",
    shortAnswer: "Using innerHTML to insert user input can lead to XSS attacks. An attacker can inject a malicious script that will run in the victim's browser. This lets them steal data, redirect the user or change the page's content. To insert text safely, use textContent or create elements through DOM methods.",
    longAnswer: "The security problems with innerHTML\nThe innerHTML method lets you insert HTML code into an element, but if the code being inserted contains user input, that opens the way to XSS attacks. XSS (Cross-Site Scripting) is a vulnerability in which an attacker injects a malicious script into a web page, which is then executed in other users' browsers.\nHow it works\nWhen you use innerHTML, the browser parses the string as HTML and processes every tag in it, including `<script>` and tags with event-handler attributes such as `<img onerror=\"...\">`. Even though a `<script>` inserted through innerHTML is not executed by modern browsers, an attribute like `onerror` or `onload` on an `<img>` or an `<svg>` will run, which is enough to steal cookies, read the DOM or send the user's data to someone else's server.\nThe safe alternatives\n\n- textContent — it inserts a string as plain text, so any markup in it is displayed rather than interpreted.\n- Creating elements through DOM methods (document.createElement, appendChild) and filling them through textContent or by setting attributes individually.\n- Sanitizing the HTML with a proven library (DOMPurify, for example), if you genuinely need to insert markup.\n- A Content Security Policy, as an additional layer that restricts the sources of scripts.\n\nConclusion: never pass user input into innerHTML without sanitizing it. Use textContent for text, DOM methods for structure, and a sanitizing library for the rare cases where markup really does have to be inserted.",
    codeExample: null,
  },
  // ---- end HTML ----
};
